are typically characterised by:","options":["little to no communication between headquarters and subsidiaries","complete subsidiary autonomy over communication","two-way communication between all units equally","high volume of orders, commands, and advice sent to subsidiaries"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"f21a1242-ced4-549d-8993-908a949f1ece","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"12454920-17a9-4c5d-8eed-726c4ecac4fb","sourceKey":"quiz:102","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under a Polycentric approach, evaluation and control standards are:","options":["determined by a third-party country","applied uniformly from home-country standards","determined locally","entirely absent"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"76e04f8a-4fae-5350-8cc4-570146e23685","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b4897138-d17b-4d4f-8f38-af66638577c2","sourceKey":"quiz:103","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which philosophy recruits and develops people of the home country for key positions everywhere in the world?","options":["Polycentric Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 11","Universalistic","Geocentric","Ethnocentric"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"e2f50cb4-66da-5ff3-a761-95edade139d7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"33ae3c57-66db-4fa1-8c13-f96108326f1a","sourceKey":"quiz:104","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is a key implication of Hofstede's study for multinational managers, according to the slides?","options":["Management practices are identical in every country","Management practices effective in one country may be troublesome in another","Culture has no bearing on business outcomes","Only economic indicators matter for management decisions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"2e5cb8e1-cc88-531f-9c88-d87c15b2368e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"202369db-b8ec-49b3-98e5-23950ea4e6c7","sourceKey":"quiz:105","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the slides, a limitation of Hofstede's research is that it:","options":["does not attempt to prescribe how to apply management techniques in different cultures","has never been criticised by any scholar","only applies to one country","ignores all quantitative data"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"a57ad271-e86d-5935-b749-8dd4ab4b52f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aaf09cc8-25e0-4cd2-baaf-7d25a59903c0","sourceKey":"quiz:106","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The slides state that a culturally diverse management team can be:","options":["illegal under international law Organisational Culture","irrelevant to organisational performance","a strength for international business organisations","a guaranteed source of conflict with no benefits"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"4693342b-7e91-562b-86fa-836fee4c8e6b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"196e985c-d895-4a37-84a5-8afbb756fa5d","sourceKey":"quiz:107","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Organisational culture (OC) is best defined as:","options":["the marketing budget of a company","the legal registration documents of a firm","the collection of values, norms, beliefs, attitudes, and policies of people in an organisation","the financial statements of an organisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"65fe6c0e-f6da-59f8-977b-bcb1cafec517","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90db816b-1abc-4b37-9dcd-677500400f51","sourceKey":"quiz:108","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Rituals\" as a form of OC refer to:","options":["legal contracts signed by employees","financial audits conducted annually","ceremonial behaviour in an organisation","office furniture arrangements"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"b3a5a9af-a377-5493-999a-c5f1c33d6974","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57ae7b2f-5513-4625-88d8-533d2eeb845f","sourceKey":"quiz:109","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is classified as a \"Symbol\" under forms of OC?","options":["A performance appraisal score","A company's logo or flag","An employee's job title only","A conflict resolution strategy"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"1002d298-f03d-5038-b67d-6829eefc4b09","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e84aafda-cbe3-43aa-a39d-fb5c1ef74b9a","sourceKey":"quiz:11","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The abbreviation \"C/R\" in the biological model of behaviour stands for: Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 2","options":["Conflict / Resolution","Control / Regulation","Consequences / Response Perspectives of OB","Culture / Religion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Foundations of OB"}},{"itemId":"87e36545-60bf-5bfb-95e2-d970a10bb493","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ec9cddc0-f9dc-4db3-be50-3ac7e2a49509","sourceKey":"quiz:110","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Artifacts\" in organisational culture refer to:","options":["unwritten unconscious beliefs","observable physical objects, such as technology","verbal agreements only","abstract values with no physical form"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"c81d93f3-d959-5804-96b5-519f47580242","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5de2593-d6e0-4520-a795-b10cd128fc6c","sourceKey":"quiz:111","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first level in the classification of organisational culture, \"Observable Artefacts,\" includes:","options":["physical, tangible aspects like website design, building layout, and logo","oral history passed down for generations","unconscious assumptions only","financial projections"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"bd7d1582-9971-5fc7-aa7b-a54df68ef66c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45a2e93a-3a2c-4071-b82a-22cc9df267c5","sourceKey":"quiz:112","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Exposed Values,\" the second level of OC classification, are described as:","options":["entirely invisible and undocumented","purely financial in nature","unrelated to organisational identity","tangible but documented, such as mission/vision statements, campaigns, and handbooks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"da97d487-3841-5b66-8a1c-ca2cf0d934f7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fbe60d1b-c00a-4699-b6db-90092098201a","sourceKey":"quiz:113","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Basic Assumptions,\" the third level of OC classification, refer to:","options":["publicly published financial reports","observable office layouts","formal legal documents only Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 12","beliefs and perceptions that members hold, consciously or unconsciously"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"c7667ba8-6145-5ff2-a98a-eab8458f1338","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"13db02f8-571b-4a64-8514-d93e7ae270aa","sourceKey":"quiz:114","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Clan Culture, as a category of OC, is best characterised by:","options":["strict hierarchy and centralised control","intense internal competition","complete absence of structure","collaboration and teamwork"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"b913cce2-6220-5b77-a266-a46d036d0859","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8fd39d1b-48c5-43dc-87be-e6cc47e2c173","sourceKey":"quiz:115","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Adhocracy Culture emphasises:","options":["strict internal competition","rigid rule-following and structured hierarchy","risk-taking, flexibility, and innovation/invention","avoidance of all change"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"deec4621-5fd0-511f-a5d4-f9e1e53bc4d1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8f98954-1205-433c-9b91-f610580e859a","sourceKey":"quiz:116","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Hierarchy Culture is based on:","options":["total absence of rules","unregulated competition","a structured system with clearly defined goals, activities, and functions","constant experimentation and risk-taking"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"0d34c516-8531-5d64-9ab6-6fee24a08e7d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4edd060a-fec4-4ca1-b4c3-6d655c227c62","sourceKey":"quiz:117","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Market Culture is primarily based on:","options":["competition, with a strong focus on external values","internal collaboration only","risk aversion","strict adherence to tradition"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"090c1fee-c20a-5a8d-80e9-ea07fb5d0e7b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"26ed8100-b267-46b4-8973-6e347fc65ea2","sourceKey":"quiz:118","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Strong Organisational Culture, as exemplified by Japanese organisational culture in the notes, is characterised by:","options":["total absence of shared norms","a high level of teamwork and cohesiveness, with members working within expected norms and rules","constant conflict and resistance to change","high employee turnover with no cohesion"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"f6471d13-875b-5020-b293-0dc8f3a578ef","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a181368-7c38-4026-907c-e15aff14bc3c","sourceKey":"quiz:119","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Weak Organisational Culture is characterised by:","options":["high cohesiveness and shared accountability","hostility, resistance to change, and conflict, with people working as a group rather than a team","strong teamwork and complementary skills","universal agreement on all decisions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"dda209df-afd6-52a1-a68d-477462a92a95","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2f52775c-e5dc-4be5-910b-d5f3b25f8d83","sourceKey":"quiz:12","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following belongs to the Individual (Micro) Perspective of OB?","options":["Org structure","Personality","Group dynamics","Reward management system"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Perspectives of OB"}},{"itemId":"7beedc82-8534-559c-8ae2-93c361cec25b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"769bd53e-6d9d-47f3-9917-0420321d47d0","sourceKey":"quiz:120","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the notes, what distinguishes a \"team\" from a mere \"group\" in a weak OC context?","options":["A team never communicates internally","A team has no leader","A group always outperforms a team","A team has shared accountability, complementary skills, and works cohesively"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"276e16fd-53b2-5f55-b2e8-22785a94fc35","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2783a718-970a-42ca-aa31-8aba83936170","sourceKey":"quiz:121","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following would most likely lead to Organisational Conflict, according to the notes?","options":["A strong, cohesive Organisational Culture","Effective performance appraisal systems","A bad or weak Organisational Culture","The presence of observable artefacts"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"796c3c05-5c29-5224-8152-a2fbdedb1af5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6261e89c-eef3-471f-9896-aacca0692cd3","sourceKey":"quiz:122","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company where employees are told the dress code, greeting rituals, and preferred communication style is demonstrating which forms of OC?","options":["Legal artefacts","Financial reports","Basic Assumptions only","Rituals and Symbols"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"09e94195-a000-538c-90c2-6a830796ce63","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb6d6d9e-dab6-46c7-bb4d-65ac8e453401","sourceKey":"quiz:123","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which OC category would best describe a fast-growing tech startup that rewards employees for experimenting with unproven ideas?","options":["Clan Culture only","Hierarchy Culture","Market Culture","Adhocracy Culture"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"bf89172d-bf20-51a2-b70d-8d7799726fd4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e3688809-9350-489f-871a-79189b5c6cd5","sourceKey":"quiz:124","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A government agency with clearly defined chains of command, standard procedures, and strict rules exemplifies which OC category? Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 13","options":["Hierarchy Culture","Clan Culture Organisational Conflict","Market Culture","Adhocracy Culture"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Organisational Culture"}},{"itemId":"e853e2b8-9dac-5efa-8a24-e410381d8c82","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"be9d18e9-a733-484e-87d2-5f3e1e7b4415","sourceKey":"quiz:125","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Organisational conflict is defined in the notes as:","options":["disagreement in the organisation","the absence of communication","a form of organisational culture","agreement in the organisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"1685bf65-e3a2-5169-84e6-f73f8b9e77c9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4139837c-ed31-4ca8-aec3-7985602e687f","sourceKey":"quiz:126","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Conflict between the Finance department and the Marketing department over budget allocation is an example of:","options":["Interpersonal conflict only","Conflict within an individual","Conflict between a group and the organisation","Conflict between groups in the organisation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"fbf97f4c-c63a-5756-9ce6-2f6e8a19a70a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d65d64ef-7271-4997-b38e-e852597d0b72","sourceKey":"quiz:127","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Line departments are described in the notes as being:","options":["solely responsible for legal compliance","directly responsible for achieving the organisation's goals (e.g., Production, Marketing)","only responsible for advising other departments","irrelevant to organisational goals"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"aae2b05c-c7aa-544f-a4f9-73a411a046dd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c1fb1446-a973-4f16-8568-60b217948dc2","sourceKey":"quiz:128","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Staff departments, such as HR, Finance, Accounting, and Procurement, are described as:","options":["entirely unrelated to organisational objectives","indirectly responsible for achieving organisational goals, providing advice and assistance","directly responsible for production output","the only departments responsible for company profit"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"2593943d-82ea-5781-9eb5-22b6ce2de428","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"403b6aa3-101b-4e6c-9ac7-00757a5c3f9b","sourceKey":"quiz:129","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first stage of the conflict process, \"Latent Conflict,\" refers to:","options":["conflict that has been fully resolved","open aggressive behaviour","the existence of conditions that may lead to conflict","the awareness that conflict exists"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"a637cdb3-f3f3-59c5-a706-c7faabe041be","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99313b67-6c6a-4ea5-a462-f39987cf4700","sourceKey":"quiz:13","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Leadership, group dynamics, power & politics, conflict, and communication\" belong to which perspective of OB?","options":["Individual (Micro) Perspective","Group (Meso) Perspective","International Perspective","Organisational (Macro) Perspective"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Perspectives of OB"}},{"itemId":"ef8e6ebc-2c9b-5918-b0d7-9d5615b24998","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90ce34cc-6c0e-4297-bd34-8fdcb40980a1","sourceKey":"quiz:130","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Perceived Conflict\" refers to:","options":["the awareness that conflict exists, nurturing the disagreement in one's mind","conflict that has been publicly announced","the physical resolution of a dispute","a conflict that never actually happened"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"401f8441-ab1d-51fc-be8d-7e30397ed6bc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11bc2dbe-666f-4895-960c-14619b287b0f","sourceKey":"quiz:131","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The conflict stage described as \"personalized\" is known as:","options":["Latent Conflict","Manifest Conflict","Felt Conflict","Conflict Aftermath"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"cbf0de0c-19cd-5dbf-809a-62b2b70d5083","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c2323042-f1c0-486b-bc98-6a855d23b30f","sourceKey":"quiz:132","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Manifest Conflict\" is best described as:","options":["conflict that has been completely forgotten","conflict that exists only in someone's imagination","conflict expressed openly in behaviour, such as aggressiveness","conflict that has not yet occurred"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"47933594-868e-550a-bac5-8944e99ed1dd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c6a233bf-0d7f-4b36-91b4-596769ea5f17","sourceKey":"quiz:133","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The final stage of the conflict process, \"Conflict Aftermath,\" can result in:","options":["guaranteed increases in productivity","low productivity, low job satisfaction, and high organisational tension","automatic promotion for all parties involved","the total elimination of future conflict"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"79d66bab-e696-50fa-98f6-482069a80db8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"77ef7bb4-ded3-4eba-a653-67a1275a5362","sourceKey":"quiz:134","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of conflict due to competition over limited resources?","options":["Role clarity","Task specialization","Effective communication Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 14","Scarcity of resources"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"51a58317-eea2-5ca3-8535-e5bfe54b0070","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b4f8d919-5a17-41eb-a538-e931e90807da","sourceKey":"quiz:135","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Task dependency\" as a cause of conflict refers to situations where:","options":["groups depend on each other, creating a tendency for conflict","groups operate in complete isolation from each other","tasks require no interaction between departments","all departments have identical objectives"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"0334e6bc-b7f8-5a73-8959-c2dc12e6c2a8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"73750a40-02c2-424c-bc3d-5cb5b82a9c1f","sourceKey":"quiz:136","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Conflict arising because \"objectives of different groups may clash\" is best described as being caused by:","options":["Communication breakdown","Personality clashes","Role ambiguity","Task specialization"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"f64da418-a44c-5882-bff2-9342dde60351","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9cf1aef4-7910-4ec9-9b91-6770ee7b22c2","sourceKey":"quiz:137","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When roles are not clearly defined, or the same role is assigned to two or more people, this is a cause of conflict known as:","options":["Incompatibility with reward system","Task dependency","Role ambiguity/Role conflict","Scarcity of resources"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"b58f5a1e-2d72-5dd6-b48b-009b6ef5fd12","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"349960f6-6cec-4639-9983-497f8e498108","sourceKey":"quiz:138","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Conflict caused by \"communication breakdown\" is described in the notes as:","options":["unrelated to organisational performance","something that only affects senior management","purely a random and unrelated occurrence","both a cause and an effect — a lack of communication causes conflict"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"3bac4adb-4fa5-5873-a716-5df33a8b128f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27b1bc4b-23c8-4f9a-a1f8-4b9d1f256ac2","sourceKey":"quiz:139","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Structural Approach to conflict management includes:","options":["adjusting structure, changing the order of work, adding more resources, and minimising bureaucracy","punishing all parties involved regardless of fault","eliminating all departments involved","ignoring the conflict entirely"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"09890f8f-aa16-584d-981c-865e7edce102","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d6555665-f170-48e4-b6a4-ff925f47a277","sourceKey":"quiz:14","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Org structure, org culture, org climate, reward system, and performance appraisal belong to which level of analysis?","options":["Interpersonal Perspective","Organisational (Macro) Perspective","Group (Meso) Perspective","Individual (Micro) Perspective"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Perspectives of OB"}},{"itemId":"5af82f91-ea64-525f-ba9e-0bf67703726a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"40265388-cbc0-4bd3-946f-950221753602","sourceKey":"quiz:140","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Interpersonal Approach to conflict management is used when conflict results from:","options":["national government policy","changes in currency exchange rates","the behaviour of people within the organisation","a shortage of office equipment only"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"57796c07-5359-5051-886b-719157cfe1c1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"54fad73e-cf0e-47b2-ae46-f5209b3f5d6c","sourceKey":"quiz:141","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Smoothing,\" as an interpersonal conflict management strategy, involves:","options":["eliminating one party from the organisation","appeasing the conflicting party","escalating the dispute publicly","ignoring the conflict indefinitely"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"5a9aa879-f5e2-58c1-bdc8-b566992eb056","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"24e5b482-31c6-4a4d-b652-1c84febccee9","sourceKey":"quiz:142","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A \"Win-Lose\" approach to conflict management is characterised by:","options":["an outcome where some parties win and others lose","the complete avoidance of any decision","a mutually beneficial outcome for all parties","automatic reconciliation without negotiation Perception & Attribution"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Organisational Conflict"}},{"itemId":"250a181d-0a92-52a0-bd9b-a3b7bc3c496d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a2e89480-dece-47ba-96dc-38a70904c7ee","sourceKey":"quiz:143","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Perception is defined as the process by which individuals:","options":["select, organize, and interpret their sensory impressions to give meaning to their environment","measure organisational profit margins","calculate financial ratios for decision-making","design physical office layouts"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"50fca90a-262d-5771-84f8-55b10fe5d5d7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"659ad34c-49a6-4c9d-ae0b-c790fec538fc","sourceKey":"quiz:144","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why does perception matter significantly in organisational behaviour?","options":["Because perception has no influence on workplace behaviour","Because perception is identical to reality","Because people's behaviour is based on their perception of reality, not on objective reality itself Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 15","Because only objective facts influence behaviour"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"f39e5b21-6850-52fa-9ccd-ebe9aa6d583b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3969ab72-8c7f-4c8e-a4da-145921fdf182","sourceKey":"quiz:145","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three broad factors influencing perception?","options":["The Situation","The Target","The Perceiver","The weather"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"6d71ece8-f31a-538c-b8a3-ebbc4641f354","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3515c644-8f23-4db3-8512-c624b8880d67","sourceKey":"quiz:146","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Factors such as a perceiver's attitudes, motives, interests, and past experience fall under which category influencing perception?","options":["The Situation","The Perceiver","The Target","The Organisation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"4eb7951e-453c-555a-af2c-1456c3ab8a5f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ebeea7f0-541c-4bd1-b357-e3372e7f0bc7","sourceKey":"quiz:147","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Novelty, motion, sound, size, and background\" are characteristics of which factor influencing perception?","options":["The Situation","The Attribution","The Target","The Perceiver"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"3dd6c4dd-32d5-5fbd-876a-3f5704d0b692","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff506cd0-67ce-4aa2-a423-64cc0b1a734d","sourceKey":"quiz:148","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Objects or events that are close together in space or time tend to be perceived as related due to the principle of:","options":["Proximity","Consensus","Projection","Contrast"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"bb24025e-5817-5659-829a-1797a606f9a6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0cb2b6c-359b-4bf1-8d43-ab93261f6ae3","sourceKey":"quiz:149","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The tendency to group similar people or objects together, which forms a basis for stereotyping, is known as:","options":["Proximity","Consistency","Distinctiveness","Similarity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"52f7cbb9-2450-5fbc-ac87-7b9e8c71bb42","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6cd04e17-0d97-4b6c-8dbf-d4a8382301ac","sourceKey":"quiz:15","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Micro/Meso/Macro framework, \"Meso Analysis\" corresponds to:","options":["Individual perspectives","Organisational perspectives","National perspectives","Group perspectives"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Perspectives of OB"}},{"itemId":"ac09ca29-5395-541b-a089-ade551b41ad5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0c6379e0-7deb-4ecc-ac58-96651a6aa0c9","sourceKey":"quiz:150","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Attribution theory concerns how observers determine whether another person's behaviour is:","options":["legal or illegal","internally or externally caused","permanent or temporary","profitable or unprofitable"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"93ab36e4-0c1a-58e5-be61-e8bed17fbfce","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"024821b9-6705-4c79-a9e2-4b2c35c36b79","sourceKey":"quiz:151","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If an employee's unusual behaviour occurs across many different situations, distinctiveness is considered:","options":["Impossible to determine","High, leading to an external attribution","Irrelevant to the attribution process","Low, leading to an internal attribution"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"602c7ab1-b303-5d9c-bb45-fe3514a497fa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ea183d31-5d69-488d-8052-c05c984e023f","sourceKey":"quiz:152","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If most people in the same situation would behave the same way, this reflects high:","options":["Projection","Distinctiveness, leading to an internal attribution","Consistency, leading to an internal attribution","Consensus, leading to an external attribution"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"584b2c5d-05ba-59af-bde7-dae0176139e0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dfac289d-bbe6-4891-9c0d-bb7f6b62477d","sourceKey":"quiz:153","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If a person acts the same way consistently over time, this reflects high:","options":["Consensus, leading to an external attribution","Distinctiveness, leading to an external attribution","Consistency, leading to an internal attribution","Selective perception"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"42dbd25f-6861-5ba8-9a25-f4cd8d07896a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e88cab1-219a-4c4f-869c-270c2e1527f6","sourceKey":"quiz:154","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The tendency to underestimate external factors and overestimate internal factors when judging others' behaviour is known as:","options":["Contrast Effect","Self-Serving Bias","Halo Effect","Fundamental Attribution Error"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"26607db2-396f-5cd8-920d-a48f11772b19","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0367b15-1dd6-4458-a698-32b6394ff22a","sourceKey":"quiz:155","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Attributing one's own success to ability and effort, while blaming failure on external factors like luck, is known as:","options":["Stereotyping Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 16","Projection","Fundamental Attribution Error","Self-Serving Bias"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"54f07fec-31d9-50d2-86c6-4ef0d7931844","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"52281fd9-6e2d-40da-ad73-403419c47a76","sourceKey":"quiz:156","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Interpreting an ambiguous situation based on one's own interests, background, and attitudes describes:","options":["Prejudice","Contrast Effects","Selective Perception","Consensus"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"7a5f6dac-8784-59fc-b4b9-d2dcb1780ddd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4690a3c1-af34-43ca-95c5-28a0a9c8aab8","sourceKey":"quiz:157","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Drawing a general impression of someone based on a single characteristic, such as intelligence or appearance, is known as:","options":["Contrast Effect","Projection","Halo Effect","Stereotyping"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"7b9cebda-50cf-5a51-a775-f58580acfdd2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eca6fc26-93d8-47a4-8124-40164ade20c6","sourceKey":"quiz:158","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a job candidate is rated more favourably after a weak applicant and less favourably after a strong one, this illustrates:","options":["Fundamental Attribution Error","Contrast Effects","Selective Perception","Halo Effect"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"297f447a-deb4-5586-adc8-53a18945767a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"75f8e6cf-6fd1-4daf-8bda-264054fe533f","sourceKey":"quiz:159","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Attributing one's own personal characteristics to other people is known as:","options":["Stereotyping","Projection","Self-serving bias","Prejudice"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"4e93ec34-fedb-5aa3-a4a6-21ef1f5a681e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45cec46a-db7b-4b79-a2be-3623f551acae","sourceKey":"quiz:16","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is classified under the Individual Perspective in the notes?","options":["Attitude","Power politics","Group decision making","Communication"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Perspectives of OB"}},{"itemId":"c48acff2-7575-5a68-8e12-a38a38963989","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0879cbcb-ba98-4c9d-a8df-8c76ea38541f","sourceKey":"quiz:160","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Judging an individual based on one's perception of the group to which they belong is known as:","options":["Halo Effect","Projection","Attribution","Stereotyping"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"819a4b6a-83f6-552b-b04f-8930c87d6b34","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93054a46-74ec-4161-a851-46ee2970e08a","sourceKey":"quiz:161","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What distinguishes Stereotyping from the Halo Effect?","options":["Halo Effect is always negative while Stereotyping is always positive","Halo Effect is based on a single individual characteristic, while Stereotyping is based on group membership","Stereotyping only applies to organisations, not individuals","They are identical concepts with different names"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"af4a677f-8bd9-5e00-9dd0-5d1a23da8457","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18670bd6-6b56-470a-a370-0115a1674fd8","sourceKey":"quiz:162","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An unfounded dislike of a person or group based on their belonging to a stereotyped group is known as:","options":["Selective Perception","Consensus","Prejudice","Contrast Effect"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"a02abb76-8355-5c70-90b9-86fb8329ef9f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4890a5ec-d53c-4e5e-8493-ebcd43555a26","sourceKey":"quiz:163","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A person will behave in ways consistent with how they are perceived by others — this describes the:","options":["Stereotyping","Halo Effect","Self-Fulfilling Prophecy (Pygmalion Effect)","Fundamental Attribution Error"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"1b3e7e4a-2660-5598-960f-d807d57fb95a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa410d3a-e9c6-4371-b3d5-1d0f66e0bf1e","sourceKey":"quiz:164","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Managers giving certain trainees \"high potential\" labels, after which those trainees actually performed better, illustrates:","options":["The Self-Fulfilling Prophecy Personality","Task Specialization","The Fundamental Attribution Error","Contrast Effects"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Perception & Attribution"}},{"itemId":"67b0ac63-b8a1-5773-b200-6fe27e8d78db","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb6d8ea8-e1a8-42d2-a59d-79f81afc8f91","sourceKey":"quiz:165","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Personality is defined as: Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 17","options":["the stable patterns of behaviour and consistent internal states that determine how an individual reacts to and interacts with others","a person's financial status within an organisation","a fixed set of physical characteristics only","the formal job title assigned to an employee"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"68b5f669-1e03-5d3c-aa06-2e0cccfbdce2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"143fcd2d-4718-48bc-96d9-58c223a92360","sourceKey":"quiz:166","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the notes, what determines personality?","options":["Salary, Education, and Nationality","Religion, Politics, and Diet","Age, Height, and Weight","Heredity, Environment, and Situation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"7941b382-6d3e-5912-bdb0-5c005fe8d22a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8628c408-bf52-45a9-87c1-c91e23cc281d","sourceKey":"quiz:167","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Heredity\" as a determinant of personality refers to characteristics that are:","options":["learned entirely from the workplace","unrelated to genetics","determined solely by current mood","acquired from one's parents"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"6e62c251-2a2f-5118-a8ec-be94aa26e711","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aac07338-4242-4dc8-b981-4318b59dcdbe","sourceKey":"quiz:168","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Environment\" as a determinant of personality refers to the idea that:","options":["the environment has no effect on behaviour","personality is entirely fixed at birth with no external influence","the environment shapes the person","only genetics matter"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"2fa1c3f4-fa64-559b-ba5e-b72e8a52561b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"46e56b61-6fe7-4451-8033-ec3d89ba107f","sourceKey":"quiz:169","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Personality characteristics that surface only under specific situations, and are mostly recessive otherwise, describe the:","options":["Cultural determinant of personality","Situational determinant of personality","Hereditary determinant of personality","Environmental determinant of personality"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"ec4f94aa-5d43-5103-bc4c-2f804342cd1f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"19169edd-4438-4489-a9ec-b22968667c0b","sourceKey":"quiz:17","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the notes, Organisation Behaviour is the study of interaction of people within:","options":["the context of work organisation","the context of family life","the context of religious institutions","the context of national politics"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Perspectives of OB"}},{"itemId":"31cd3a58-0084-5d8f-b7b9-fb2d7e66dd7f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2ab0cf02-5a8a-4e59-b6cb-358f8e5dc520","sourceKey":"quiz:170","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) classifies individuals into personality types based on how many dimensions?","options":["Ten","Six","Two","Four"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"db59218b-e5e9-5129-8910-7c2099714ddb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"16a86c67-c4e7-494e-a9b2-7221858ebb94","sourceKey":"quiz:171","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the MBTI, the Extraversion/Introversion dimension measures whether an individual's focus is directed:","options":["outside themselves (Extraversion) or inside themselves (Introversion)","toward risk-taking or risk-avoidance","toward the past or the future","toward material or spiritual concerns"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"f91d32d8-118e-5802-9ace-8e46f3c3b83a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ee564a39-1d72-4861-92b2-9b8e33651896","sourceKey":"quiz:172","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Sensing/Intuiting dimension of MBTI concerns how a person:","options":["makes final decisions","manages conflict","gathers information — systematically (Sensing) or via intuition (Intuiting)","expresses emotion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"69c11dd7-dd3d-5287-b92e-4a59136c6bc0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f2747c99-435e-40c3-a24c-8e8121ad671f","sourceKey":"quiz:173","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Thinking/Feeling dimension of MBTI concerns whether decisions are made:","options":["with or without data","publicly or privately","quickly or slowly","objectively and impersonally (Thinking) or subjectively and interpersonally (Feeling)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"dfe34f25-2774-518b-94b5-bcd1d69d7d47","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dfd8fbf8-f242-4328-9cc9-f70a84ba849e","sourceKey":"quiz:174","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Judging/Perceiving dimension of MBTI distinguishes between individuals who are:","options":["decisive and planned (Judging) versus spontaneous and flexible (Perceiving)","internal versus external in locus of control","masculine versus feminine","extroverted versus introverted"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"e04dfcec-e382-558d-b61e-e0636e751c92","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"04b4d510-49d4-4e62-8bd8-988c6e4cfd69","sourceKey":"quiz:175","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A key criticism of the MBTI noted in the textbook is that:","options":["it was banned in most countries","it is based entirely on physical characteristics","there is no hard evidence it is a valid measure of personality Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 18","it has never been used by any organisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"9fa0c24b-8b1e-5ecb-be57-188020450b00","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0c19262-2aa3-4095-aefd-a4aef4f5243d","sourceKey":"quiz:176","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Big Five personality model includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Machiavellianism","Conscientiousness","Extraversion","Openness to Experience"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"87f27f98-353d-535b-a6ae-9691315e5b40","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"08e00717-3937-4fb6-902a-637a5f8e6250","sourceKey":"quiz:177","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which Big Five trait is most consistently linked to higher motivation and organisational citizenship behaviour (OCB)?","options":["Extraversion","Agreeableness","Conscientiousness","Openness to Experience"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"04ea3c6c-5aa4-5275-8cec-b9a3283c368f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5952f4de-d906-44ac-ad67-0a842efc0469","sourceKey":"quiz:178","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Emotional Stability\" in the Big Five model refers to a person's ability to:","options":["reject any form of teamwork","avoid all social interaction","remain calm, self-confident, and secure, withstanding stress","constantly seek out risky and unpredictable situations"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"7416d5b6-4239-5612-bdda-f7b3e57438cb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27ca46b4-e9e8-4b70-a86b-d5cf8b274cfe","sourceKey":"quiz:179","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Locus of Control refers to:","options":["the extent of a person's professional network","a person's physical location within an office","an individual's position in the organisational hierarchy","the degree to which people believe they are in control of their own fate"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"74ed3d64-e885-51e0-bb00-94d69429e94b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a27390a7-5fac-4369-88a9-6e9cdcbfa8fd","sourceKey":"quiz:18","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which pair correctly matches perspective to example?","options":["Organisational Perspective — Personality","Group Perspective — Org Climate","Group Perspective — Leadership","Individual Perspective — Reward Management System"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Perspectives of OB"}},{"itemId":"a416fa9a-7e0e-55a0-b20b-3fc6f1f63120","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6dac3689-356a-43cc-a259-16857f1c45f3","sourceKey":"quiz:180","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Internals,\" in terms of locus of control, believe that:","options":["their lives are entirely controlled by luck and outside forces","the organisation is solely responsible for their success","they control their own destinies","personality has no effect on performance"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"f71ef80a-fb5d-5c4e-a51a-8833121b5453","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b2c9e531-539a-4bd4-b5a3-2fd9c1d39e4c","sourceKey":"quiz:181","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Externals,\" in terms of locus of control, tend to show:","options":["total control over organisational outcomes","greater well-being and higher motivation than internals in all tasks","less job satisfaction and higher absenteeism, believing outside forces control their lives","complete indifference to their environment"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"03a30027-ed46-53a7-9003-6f93ec5785f1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2ea9ddd8-7970-45fe-9310-c7dbb83210a8","sourceKey":"quiz:182","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Machiavellianism describes a personality trait involving:","options":["total avoidance of persuasion tactics","complete honesty in all negotiations","high emotional sensitivity and closeness to others","pragmatism, emotional distance, and the belief that ends justify means"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"c98ac480-b4ad-52bb-9565-e87498a99803","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e485426f-9fe4-44f0-91a3-06ec83446fb4","sourceKey":"quiz:183","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"High Machs (individuals high in Machiavellianism) are best suited to jobs requiring:","options":["purely manual labour","complete social isolation","bargaining skill, such as negotiation or commissioned sales","strict rule-following with no negotiation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"6a28f2a4-34b4-5c87-b08b-f139a7dff8bc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c6eb78c7-7f47-4ad4-bc73-990a165251ec","sourceKey":"quiz:184","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Self-Esteem, as a personality attribute, refers to:","options":["a person's physical strength","an individual's total years of work experience","the degree to which individuals like or dislike themselves","the size of an individual's professional network"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"fe290355-6faf-5933-92d9-305779267e36","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"82f42a86-ff6d-4196-a0a0-7982d1ec0b86","sourceKey":"quiz:185","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Individuals high in Self-Monitoring are described as:","options":["highly adaptable to different situations, sometimes showing contradictions between public and private selves","unable to hold any leadership position","indifferent to social cues","completely consistent in behaviour across all situations, with no adaptability"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"77ca2d94-bfee-5805-8666-79e2de9dd795","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"06eb14fd-5af6-409e-b422-f63187ce328e","sourceKey":"quiz:186","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"High risk-takers, as a personality attribute, tend to: Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 19","options":["always make slower decisions than low risk-takers","refuse to make any decisions at all","make faster decisions with less information","require more information than low risk-takers before deciding"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"1640fadc-ea7b-5a92-bbcc-aee3ea8d5d5b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b952695e-2aaf-43a4-8e6f-eccfb607d399","sourceKey":"quiz:187","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Type A personalities are described as being:","options":["entirely relaxed with no sense of time urgency","indifferent to achievement or competition","aggressively involved in a chronic struggle to achieve more in less time","incapable of working under pressure"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"8831e81b-2a35-575d-b55a-5d69f13e65ff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d71ea8e9-b2cd-4310-85e6-9b31d012eca3","sourceKey":"quiz:188","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Type B personalities are described as:","options":["constantly trying to do two things at once","having no sense of time urgency and able to relax without guilt","obsessed with quantity and numbers","impatient in all situations"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"5aa3908c-dc1c-526f-b268-ef0546ca1d96","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"071744e0-f5d9-4e9a-ab69-c520b5999786","sourceKey":"quiz:189","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Proactive Personality refers to individuals who:","options":["avoid all forms of change within the organisation","wait passively for instructions before acting","reject leadership roles entirely","identify opportunities, show initiative, and persevere until meaningful change occurs"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"6be59f4e-ca0f-5e56-b92e-2f261e77bb86","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"853c9161-ca8e-49f3-9916-8a9ad6624763","sourceKey":"quiz:19","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Performance Management/Stress\" is classified under which analysis level?","options":["Micro Analysis (Individual perspectives)","Meso Analysis (Group perspectives)","Macro Analysis (Organizational perspectives)","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Perspectives of OB"}},{"itemId":"d376830f-54f8-51d6-9108-0762b57ed350","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"04ddd96d-946e-4e05-a919-116218392328","sourceKey":"quiz:190","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Personality and National Culture,\" per the textbook, cautions that:","options":["personality tests are always universally valid","national culture has no relationship to personality","personality tests can carry cultural bias when applied outside the population they were designed for","personality is identical across all cultures with no variation Emotions"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Personality"}},{"itemId":"675181da-b5fe-5533-b160-12dfbe77a315","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"43543c46-dad2-4874-be9d-51aab72f226d","sourceKey":"quiz:191","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Emotions are defined in the textbook as:","options":["intense feelings that are directed at someone or something","identical in meaning to moods","feelings with no object or trigger","purely physical sensations unrelated to psychology"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"849428f8-5510-5a45-b845-afad87fe1ed4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8023b336-29d0-4962-8bc3-3931ae0796b0","sourceKey":"quiz:192","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Moods, as distinct from emotions, are described as:","options":["always directed at a specific object","feelings that are less intense and lack a contextual stimulus","identical to attitudes","always more intense than emotions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"93691c9b-41fe-5d40-81c4-5e8db9a29202","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a215454-8441-4bb4-ba36-6963b48b467a","sourceKey":"quiz:193","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Emotional Labour refers to:","options":["an employee expressing organisationally desired emotions during interpersonal interactions","a legal requirement for overtime pay","the physical effort required to perform manual labour","the total number of hours an employee works"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"afd74652-cf22-5531-9dd6-d39c1a60b54b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fb1b7de1-0f8c-466b-b745-7ad0adfc9201","sourceKey":"quiz:194","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A flight attendant who must appear cheerful regardless of personal feelings is demonstrating:","options":["Emotional Labour","Self-Serving Bias","Machiavellianism","Locus of Control"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"0e9fac49-17e0-5c54-9bc7-5bb08f1a1b8a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ec5348a5-9e51-40bb-ad6b-7c8cde23d209","sourceKey":"quiz:195","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Emotional Intelligence (EI) is best described as:","options":["noncognitive skills and competencies that influence a person's ability to cope with environmental demands and pressures","a measure of physical fitness","an individual's years of formal education","a person's IQ score on a standardised academic test"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"13371b11-5b57-5fa5-96c7-75c42340ad84","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8b86778a-c7cb-4e2c-a2e6-a4c0510cf333","sourceKey":"quiz:196","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the five dimensions of Emotional Intelligence?","options":["Power Distance","Empathy","Uncertainty Avoidance Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 20","Task Specialization"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"360ab3a2-9f42-5a32-9ae9-19e30e6e086c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c0e3d623-fa27-4358-90e4-6fdb6d79d7de","sourceKey":"quiz:197","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How does Emotional Intelligence (EI) differ from Emotional Labour?","options":["Neither concept relates to the workplace","They are identical concepts","EI is an internal personality capability, while Emotional Labour is an externally imposed job requirement","EI is a job requirement while Emotional Labour is a personality trait"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"0a505d40-1c0a-5d12-8901-4b686ab6cf39","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"78146b4d-783c-4111-ad4f-a69b271b66c2","sourceKey":"quiz:198","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Research on Bell Labs engineers and US Air Force recruiters found that top performers were distinguished mainly by:","options":["Machiavellianism","Emotional Intelligence rather than academic IQ","Physical strength","Years of formal education alone"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"b978b1b3-1ce6-590e-854c-0e97ea6bdc60","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"79b7ac38-f233-4585-87fd-efa3f69e2365","sourceKey":"quiz:199","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Employee deviance is defined as:","options":["a synonym for emotional labour","actions that always benefit the organisation financially","mandatory actions required by company policy","voluntary actions that violate established norms and threaten the organisation or its members"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"197336c3-1465-5c4e-93d2-f8d90e1893af","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ce86f3e5-90f8-42a4-bbe5-b3a820b9fc5d","sourceKey":"quiz:2","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the S→O→B→C/R model of behaviour, what does \"O\" represent?","options":["Observation","Objective","Outcome","Organism"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Foundations of OB"}},{"itemId":"56a805c0-7ae8-58f2-a441-d75a6264f699","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6d758e9a-172d-4e6b-9fc7-c0b812135640","sourceKey":"quiz:20","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an example of a Macro-level OB concern?","options":["Reward Management System","Motivation Concept of IOB Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 3","Learning","Perception"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Perspectives of OB"}},{"itemId":"23c01347-1fc0-5ac9-9091-3fd2953db201","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"92ef5847-5241-4320-8279-f87c11cfd980","sourceKey":"quiz:200","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Envy in the workplace, as discussed in the textbook, is linked to:","options":["increased organisational citizenship behaviour","improved teamwork with no downside Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 21","higher levels of trust among colleagues","hostility, backstabbing, and political behaviour"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Emotions"}},{"itemId":"86c7e5e8-0d0d-56e6-b3a0-b7ddf27f5dd7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3705dae-67c1-4fe9-b74c-7f8c16bf38d8","sourceKey":"quiz:21","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"International Organisational Behaviour (IOB) refers to:","options":["the study of organisational behaviour strictly within a single domestic firm","the study and application of organisational behaviour principles in organisations operating across different countries, cultures, and institutional environments","the study of international trade tariffs","the study of foreign exchange rate fluctuations"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"11123b74-a7c7-547d-97b7-df3fe1569169","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"316444c4-314f-49dc-bad8-625490d5d314","sourceKey":"quiz:22","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"IOB adopts a global perspective (unlike traditional/domestic OB) because:","options":["it assumes all countries share identical institutions","it ignores cultural differences entirely","it focuses only on multinational profits","management practices effective in one country may not produce similar outcomes in another"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"50f5201c-0eb3-5716-8dca-2b6b8cd431aa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b98f7b07-96b0-4b39-981d-224df95d0b5d","sourceKey":"quiz:23","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Luthans and Doh (2021), which of these is NOT one of the differences shaping workplace behaviour across national boundaries?","options":["Institutional differences","Astrological differences","Cultural differences","Political differences"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"208a520c-b6ab-55f4-ab80-f8632b2a672d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0f76e9ad-9212-437b-82e6-41a5d711e3f8","sourceKey":"quiz:24","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Management practices may fail to transfer across countries because of two broad categories of difference. These are:","options":["Military and Legal differences","Religious and Culinary differences","Cultural and Institutional differences","Financial and Technological differences"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"32425e8e-b1f9-5e03-be69-976dce22b5cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c218496e-e838-4d1c-9775-7e9bb4de6123","sourceKey":"quiz:25","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why has IOB become increasingly relevant, according to the notes?","options":["Because national cultures have become identical worldwide","Because international travel has become illegal in most countries","Because domestic businesses have stopped operating","Due to globalisation and the rise of multinational corporations, international partnerships, and virtual teams"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"88934fc7-c14c-58b5-887e-ab07b5c07ad8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a8c5be99-9ae8-4ce0-ae87-768ba4e6f201","sourceKey":"quiz:26","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company recruits employees from several countries who then collaborate remotely across time zones. This illustrates:","options":["The rise of virtual teams and cross-cultural collaboration due to globalisation","The end of outsourcing","The decline of multinational corporations","The elimination of cultural diversity"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"f06b99a5-ba7e-510c-8896-8fc86bd74391","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a351d9ca-4481-49a5-843a-db53f0e2cd95","sourceKey":"quiz:27","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is explicitly listed as an issue addressed by the IOB discipline in the notes?","options":["Personal tax filing","Expatriate management","Weather forecasting","Traffic law enforcement"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"fe193b11-2546-5fc7-87ca-7167f52a5382","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"547815d5-e452-44ce-a3f8-a0564df1a15c","sourceKey":"quiz:28","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Managers working internationally must develop cross-cultural competence\" mainly in order to:","options":["avoid paying local taxes","lead a diverse team effectively and achieve organisational objectives","reduce the number of expatriates hired","standardise all employee salaries globally"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"4fd1db6d-494c-5c0f-b0bb-e1386646c975","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e4c97e80-991a-402b-ab9a-d950e2dc9e97","sourceKey":"quiz:29","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Cross-cultural communication, global leadership development, and international HRM are all described in the notes as:","options":["Issues addressed by the discipline of IOB","Subfields of financial accounting","Branches of engineering management","Elements of national constitutional law"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"fd1f67cf-7415-59ef-b36e-3e08c52a95db","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"04379ba5-e745-4723-82d4-7a8cdf565c78","sourceKey":"quiz:3","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Organisational Behaviour is defined in the notes as the study of:","options":["the marketing strategies used by firms","the financial performance of an organisation","the structure of organisational hierarchies only","the behaviour of people within the context of work organisation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Foundations of OB"}},{"itemId":"7e95e38c-3548-54e5-9825-d254fcbce274","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8284151b-5ece-4c2c-a4bf-4242e4e8e772","sourceKey":"quiz:30","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A management technique that boosted morale in a US branch actually lowered morale in a branch located in a high-power-distance country. This best illustrates:","options":["why IOB argues that management practices effective in one country may not transfer to another","why culture has no impact on management Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 4","why morale cannot be measured across cultures","why domestic OB and IOB are identical fields"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"5d0d8aee-af02-5d9a-a29a-f6e9ca717759","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"294f6fb9-b71e-4b2f-bb61-140e58c76aa8","sourceKey":"quiz:31","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these organisational arrangements is cited in the notes as a driver of IOB's growing relevance?","options":["Nationalisation of private firms","Prohibition of partnerships","Outsourcing arrangements","Elimination of supply chains"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"d9727e42-6a55-5c60-a8c3-515c1a6d5c9f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bec90efa-d1da-4044-949e-bcad4be48697","sourceKey":"quiz:32","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Diversity, equity, and inclusion in multinational organisations\" is best classified as:","options":["A type of perceptual error","An issue the IOB discipline addresses","A dimension of Hofstede's culture model","A stage of the conflict process"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"8d8caaff-1f0e-5c8a-ad60-60d232cff9f5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2dfcf119-e196-44b8-92db-4d94850b9aee","sourceKey":"quiz:33","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The core distinguishing feature of IOB compared to domestic OB, per the notes, is its:","options":["exclusive focus on financial statements","focus only on government agencies","global perspective that accounts for cross-national cultural and institutional variation","rejection of all OB theory"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"af29e9f4-d1a0-5626-85da-1b896689e41e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4ab11f15-7666-4ba1-9355-49f160dbcb95","sourceKey":"quiz:34","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The notes state that employees in multinational firms \"frequently collaborate with colleagues located in different countries,\" making cross-cultural understanding:","options":["a purely academic concern","an optional bonus with no real business impact","irrelevant to team performance","a strategic necessity rather than merely a desirable skill"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"f0345474-162c-5417-9113-70606fb9a0b7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1e4624ef-45c0-4ac2-b2a7-f5f6b317eb90","sourceKey":"quiz:35","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which best describes the relationship between globalisation and IOB, according to the notes?","options":["Globalisation is a key driver making IOB increasingly relevant Convergence vs Divergence","Globalisation and IOB are unrelated","Globalisation has made IOB obsolete","Globalisation only affects IOB in Europe"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Concept of IOB"}},{"itemId":"f12691f2-e9df-53b2-a06e-08f7ce64e9bb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb0ba2c8-c208-4895-983f-2a39af1ee993","sourceKey":"quiz:36","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Universalistic theories of management and organisation predict that:","options":["cross-national differences will grow permanently stronger","no two organisations can ever be compared","management practices are irrelevant to culture","cross-national differences in management will disappear over time due to globalisation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Convergence vs Divergence"}},{"itemId":"de3c2448-cb3c-58a9-be2d-559432186da5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"245424e1-4b10-4860-9409-1406a948db8e","sourceKey":"quiz:37","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Particularistic theories argue that cross-national differences in management:","options":["will persist because management reflects country-specific history and path-dependent institutions","never existed in the first place","will vanish within a decade","are caused only by language barriers"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Convergence vs Divergence"}},{"itemId":"847109af-617b-5ade-893c-76a5bdf7524d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1a75a37d-d787-4e0c-becf-016c59cda904","sourceKey":"quiz:38","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The idea that \"best practices\" will eventually dominate regardless of a company's nationality is associated with:","options":["Universalistic theories (the Convergence thesis)","Particularistic theories (the Divergence thesis)","Hofstede's uncertainty avoidance dimension","The Emic approach"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Convergence vs Divergence"}},{"itemId":"1f98fdc0-49ac-584f-be0c-ca0ba0f1e239","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2476b9e5-8c9d-42cc-9da0-c10a21fe640b","sourceKey":"quiz:39","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Unique Japanese management and organisation being attributed to Japan's specific history and culture is an example supporting:","options":["Nomothetic research","Particularistic theories","Ethnocentric management","Universalistic theories"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Convergence vs Divergence"}},{"itemId":"1835b6dd-8186-5e69-8f66-5a6a6c5a8a62","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0dccfbb3-7651-4b0d-90f8-83673b9aafa6","sourceKey":"quiz:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Behaviour is defined as the way an individual responds to his or her environment.\" This emphasises which relationship?","options":["The interaction between a person and their environment","The interaction between a person and their salary","The interaction between government and business","The interaction between culture and religion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Foundations of OB"}},{"itemId":"fed244d5-7dd7-5ccd-b21f-051989cba89d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6ec3a580-74fe-4be6-98e8-4468b213347d","sourceKey":"quiz:40","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Path-dependent institutions\" is a concept most associated with which theoretical camp?","options":["Particularistic theories","Universalistic theories Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 5","Emic research only","The EPRG model"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Convergence vs Divergence"}},{"itemId":"8f680d8c-2ffb-5438-acd7-8ae6d9c7b31c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b9ff9a24-efa2-4585-828b-9c3628e6ed28","sourceKey":"quiz:41","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Universalistic theorists, existing cross-national management differences may be seen as:","options":["permanent and unchangeable features of the global economy","proof that culture does not exist","temporary disequilibria that will disappear as market obstacles are removed","evidence that globalisation has failed"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Convergence vs Divergence"}},{"itemId":"c98c07dc-645f-58d7-8e29-250e48d03c30","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"07b6327b-d3db-422d-b071-099aa62e7148","sourceKey":"quiz:42","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement is most consistent with the Divergence thesis?","options":["All countries will eventually adopt identical management systems","National management systems will retain distinct characteristics despite globalisation","Culture has no bearing on organisational practice","Only economic factors influence management style"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Convergence vs Divergence"}},{"itemId":"04875ab2-ea1b-5499-8a5e-771595902166","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5bab4c36-a59e-4526-93cb-7d1f426d2be0","sourceKey":"quiz:43","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A driving force for the homogenisation of management practices, according to Universalistic theory, is:","options":["reduced international travel","increased trade tariffs","the decline of multinational corporations National Culture","globalisation and worldwide competitive pressure"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Convergence vs Divergence"}},{"itemId":"d37d6b21-abe4-5746-b996-76b945501c74","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"73449b40-fd68-4c8a-ada7-f033cea6c70e","sourceKey":"quiz:44","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"National culture is best defined in the notes as:","options":["the shared beliefs, values, knowledge, and pattern of behaviour common to people within a society","the GDP per capita of a nation","the constitution of a country","the official language spoken in a country"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"c8efda68-f083-5fcb-9fa8-69d2689085f0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a2daea39-b150-40de-8cd9-d04d97e9582e","sourceKey":"quiz:45","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A \"sub-culture\" is best described as:","options":["a culture found only in rural areas","the opposite of national culture","a culture that no longer exists","a group of people with a unique way of life within a larger dominant culture"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"a9c1c08d-5842-5f92-8ab6-6531dcaf1d16","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"81e86561-ffc3-4a74-9acb-c8018a32e224","sourceKey":"quiz:46","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Cultural diversity is defined as:","options":["a term used only in biology","a situation where a nation has only one ethnic group","a situation where the people of a nation have a variety/diversity of culture","the absence of any cultural variation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"a6e76995-f6ca-5bb0-a9cb-a09682eb336e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3734e645-7458-4813-a0b7-1d2de4a950fe","sourceKey":"quiz:47","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is listed among the components of culture in the slides?","options":["Aesthetics","Stock market indices","Weather patterns","Currency exchange rates"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"a5b6ee87-1730-5435-961f-0de41dab3918","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4091a11a-b070-45f0-8241-534b4f2d8146","sourceKey":"quiz:48","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the slides, which of these is a component of culture?","options":["Interest rates","Manners and Customs","Gross Domestic Product","Population density"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"23034360-0832-5ea3-bbb0-1a25d501eda1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"02b1661b-d3cb-481f-a9bc-6705b25af6e8","sourceKey":"quiz:49","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Personal Communication,\" \"Education,\" and \"Religion\" are grouped in the course material under:","options":["Types of Organisational Structure","Components of Culture","Dimensions of Conflict","Stages of Perception"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"d258048f-7673-5365-8ca8-8cc2e65b8d68","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7271459a-f8d7-4db5-bca4-e17886d6502e","sourceKey":"quiz:5","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT listed among the organisational specialisations in the notes?","options":["Financial Accounting Specialisation","Marketing Specialisation","Organisational Behaviour Specialisation","Management Specialisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Foundations of OB"}},{"itemId":"fb56eead-4ce8-5ec4-8484-3aede524b582","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9922f923-a013-45ca-be7b-4d21ca8d93ab","sourceKey":"quiz:50","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The notes state that management must adapt its practices because:","options":["culture has no effect on management","only economic factors matter in management","components of culture vary across societies and affect behaviour","all cultures are identical"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"ca085248-0f30-5453-9939-57e091c86b61","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1e9890f8-d929-4b99-aaa2-d5d0e9623f52","sourceKey":"quiz:51","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Cultures are described in the slides as being learned, shared, transgenerational, symbolic, patterned, and:","options":["static","adaptive","irrelevant","illegal Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 6"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"03afccd3-12ca-5383-811f-391b18ec6c54","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1681f348-70c8-4e9a-adf1-96ace85e9cac","sourceKey":"quiz:52","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Behavioural differences between peoples of different countries arise primarily from, according to the slides:","options":["random chance","differences in physical appearance","differences in climate alone","differences in culture rather than differences in people"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"f417ba5d-b0d4-58ba-a1ba-fd9966e09dfe","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6e4aa0db-a518-42a6-a01c-1a5790123add","sourceKey":"quiz:53","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"View of work\" and \"perception of time\" are cited in the slides as being shaped by:","options":["Weather forecasting","Currency valuation","Cultural diversity","Legal tender laws"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"e6ec7853-995e-52d9-830e-50b9ea574fcf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6970166a-4873-492b-9746-95976b4c2c8b","sourceKey":"quiz:54","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A subculture, as distinct from the dominant national culture, is characterised mainly by:","options":["complete isolation from the rest of society","legal independence from the state","identical values to the national culture","a unique way of life within a larger, dominant culture Emic vs Etic Research"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: National Culture"}},{"itemId":"88c0c38d-a410-58a5-a909-b3e751116b34","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53647310-478c-4e21-9023-c9ceb465006d","sourceKey":"quiz:55","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Emic approach to studying culture emphasises:","options":["predicting stock market trends","the unique aspects of a specific culture, understood from the inside","comparing many cultures using standardised questionnaires","ignoring cultural context entirely"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"f4cd45f6-f5ef-5bfe-ad69-17252abc7ef0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b08d3df1-50b4-4098-949d-fabafe9e35b4","sourceKey":"quiz:56","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Etic approach to studying culture is best described as:","options":["a purely historical method","quantitative research using questionnaires to compare cultures (nomothetic)","qualitative research using participant observation only","a method that rejects all comparison"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"f6ede4c6-a721-50c3-ba1c-506fb41d4e9d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1c979db-2396-443e-9e42-135504bc187a","sourceKey":"quiz:57","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Nomothetic\" research is most closely associated with:","options":["the EPRG model","the Etic approach","the Emic approach","the Trait vs Situation debate"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"716624ec-64ed-5344-960c-dcaa337e075b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4e098cb7-d623-480a-a21f-819ae7261631","sourceKey":"quiz:58","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Idiographic\" research, based on qualitative data such as participant observation and interviews, is associated with:","options":["the Nomothetic approach","the Emic approach","Hofstede's dimensions","the Etic approach"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"63ecc754-e77f-5d54-b454-371788be3aa4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e26b47e3-4035-4dc7-845c-973f54988e76","sourceKey":"quiz:59","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A researcher who integrates into a specific community for a year to understand its unique rituals from the inside is using:","options":["an Emic approach","a Universalistic approach","a Nomothetic approach","an Etic approach"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"e6a99d89-aa33-5a8a-af96-7d3af29fafe8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"26c71b61-8d6d-4d64-871f-84bfd1e42acc","sourceKey":"quiz:6","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the biological model of behaviour (S→O→B→C/R), what does \"S\" stand for?","options":["Structure","Situation","Stimulus","System"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Foundations of OB"}},{"itemId":"e089027d-4cd8-5e36-9748-830833427b22","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c18c4687-8e75-464d-bc34-441249a7e0eb","sourceKey":"quiz:60","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A researcher who distributes the same standardised questionnaire in 40 different countries to compare cultural values is using:","options":["an Etic approach","a particularistic approach","an idiographic approach","an Emic approach"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"89637df9-1394-5527-9c2c-318bb880b881","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0758ce16-7ea3-486a-a42b-50de0d24d411","sourceKey":"quiz:61","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"One major limitation of studies from an Emic perspective, according to the slides, is:","options":["an excessive reliance on large sample sizes","total absence of researcher involvement Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 7","the impossibility of gathering any qualitative data","potential bias from the observer being embedded within the culture studied"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"647ae381-c914-55cb-bf0f-178bbd21db61","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"633da99d-2862-4feb-99fc-72800e0c6f32","sourceKey":"quiz:62","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"One key challenge with the Etic/Nomothetic approach, per the slides, is that:","options":["it can never be applied to more than one country at a time","it cannot be replicated","describing culture in a few dimensions cannot do justice to its full complexity","it does not use questionnaires"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"71be22fb-5dcf-5e29-8439-5997c3f65e21","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c9505e54-d981-4f4a-8d7f-0ced03e9bdb0","sourceKey":"quiz:63","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Geert Hofstede's research, surveying IBM employees across 40+ countries using standardised questionnaires, is a classic example of:","options":["Particularistic theory","Emic (Idiographic) research","The EPRG model","Etic (Nomothetic) research"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"ac01b354-953c-520d-a8f9-1a07384921cc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0e249024-0cc3-476d-8b23-f566cc5ca254","sourceKey":"quiz:64","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For an Etic/Nomothetic comparison to be valid across countries, the slides emphasise that:","options":["questionnaires must be translated loosely without consistency","the same question asked in country A must be asked in the same way in country B","each country must be asked completely different questions","only one country needs to be surveyed"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"e89e399c-ab0f-56aa-aa8b-84655f37f825","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6a686c23-440f-4608-b780-48e7a3c94bef","sourceKey":"quiz:65","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes Emic from Etic research?","options":["Etic never uses data collection","Emic and Etic are two names for the same method","Emic is always quantitative","Emic stresses the unique aspects of a culture; Etic emphasises comparable aspects across cultures"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"118dcbae-dce7-5e98-85b2-c7b5ee96c8f5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a9d1d65-2026-4b38-8a13-fe94e753530b","sourceKey":"quiz:66","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A key criticism of cross-cultural (Etic) research noted in the slides is:","options":["total agreement among scholars on every definition","complete absence of academic interest in the topic","the lack of a widely accepted definition of culture, both conceptually and operationally","total elimination of the need for further study"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"50685141-ff0e-50b0-932a-c9c11f2c9510","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b1382a9c-accf-4408-b8d3-122f7928725d","sourceKey":"quiz:67","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The \"outside perspective of comparativist researchers\" described in the slides refers to:","options":["the Emic approach Hofstede's Dimensions","the trait theory of personality","the EPRG model","the Etic approach"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Emic vs Etic Research"}},{"itemId":"31c850d8-3815-5509-a913-6a46afb00d28","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b2e5c96-9e76-44fb-9ae8-164c83bbb499","sourceKey":"quiz:68","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Hofstede's research initially involved employees from which company?","options":["General Electric","Samsung","Toyota","IBM"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"e1c972cc-bea7-5485-9fd9-891271eab7e4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b4f60d18-1b40-47ca-8fbb-168515b6a47f","sourceKey":"quiz:69","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Hofstede's original large-scale study surveyed employees across how many countries?","options":["Over 40 countries","Over 200 countries","Only 2 countries","Exactly 5 countries"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"378687e8-1486-5933-9640-27313558fd40","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0d4286f0-19bf-4b03-8b91-010c36059d15","sourceKey":"quiz:7","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which best completes the model: Stimulus → Organism → Behaviour → ___?","options":["Culture/Reward","Consequence/Response","Cognition/Reasoning","Conflict/Resolution"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Foundations of OB"}},{"itemId":"1277bc23-cba4-5bc5-9c23-c4df6cca6d06","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50af33a9-f7a6-438b-a849-83fae1e9191e","sourceKey":"quiz:70","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How many people were initially surveyed in Hofstede's IBM-based research, according to the notes?","options":["Over 116,000","Around 50","Over 5 million","Exactly 1,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"101c9ef0-beae-5f14-9bc0-5980e67d8aa5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cef1172f-318d-4cea-bc14-f06b0f640684","sourceKey":"quiz:71","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Hofstede's framework initially identified four dimensions and was later expanded to:","options":["Six dimensions","Three dimensions","Twelve dimensions","Ten dimensions Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 8"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"aa49964b-0254-5892-a934-26779eec03ed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3655c304-10ce-44ed-b6e9-d81acaef659f","sourceKey":"quiz:72","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a high Power Distance culture, subordinates typically:","options":["expect to be told what to do and show strong respect for authority","refuse to follow instructions","openly challenge their superiors as equals","reject all forms of hierarchy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"6e7b2f58-43ea-57a4-966d-92be6be32947","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2637069b-71f0-4e77-b92e-23470d3d91f9","sourceKey":"quiz:73","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A small Power Distance culture is characterised by:","options":["decentralisation and a consultative relationship between employees and superiors","total absence of any management structure","strict centralisation and unquestioning obedience to titles and ranks","complete rejection of organisational rules"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"9d798574-2673-5edc-a0da-d562837c325d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7ac72f64-892d-4be7-970f-e5c7f1b1b980","sourceKey":"quiz:74","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the slides, which region is cited as an example of high Power Distance?","options":["Canadian culture","Australian culture","Asian/Arabian cultures","Scandinavian cultures"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"88719f56-21af-5492-a8b7-df271051fb6b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"03fab532-c2ae-433a-9e3d-d041675ef93b","sourceKey":"quiz:75","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In an Individualist culture, people tend to:","options":["reject personal achievement entirely","avoid all forms of risk-taking","prioritise group support and relationships over individual tasks","focus on their own interests and the people close to them, valuing hard work and entrepreneurial risk-taking"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"c9cd044c-160a-5b93-bef8-d5086912e587","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a771e2b3-8c02-4ae8-b307-d68d06fa6ca3","sourceKey":"quiz:76","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a Collectivist culture, the emphasis is on:","options":["the complete absence of social groups","personal wealth accumulation only","individual competition above all else","the group, with group support expected and relationships prioritised over task orientation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"a225f165-93ce-54f6-b587-e8c7273ce9f6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bc694e70-4a81-46b5-9380-75fc0029611e","sourceKey":"quiz:77","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which country is used as an example of a highly individualist culture?","options":["China","United States","Japan","Guatemala"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"56a15f94-195f-5752-b86e-02399717e20f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bbf1b7d7-96a0-4fb2-96d2-989f9aab01a9","sourceKey":"quiz:78","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which country's collectivism is traced in the slides to the fusion of Confucian, Buddhist, and Shinto ideas?","options":["United Kingdom","Germany","Japan","United States"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"455a76dc-19a4-5861-b8fe-952a0f0edcf4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da675df6-1e3b-40bb-b883-f731a2c18cc5","sourceKey":"quiz:79","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"High Uncertainty Avoidance cultures are described as:","options":["open to risk-taking and comfortable with ambiguity","indifferent to rules and structure","afraid of ambiguity and uncertainty, preferring structure and formal rules","unable to make any decisions"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"96354062-defc-55b4-bfcd-f8845e2019e0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b5d1e6e1-adc1-4586-a513-4b055ee9e9bc","sourceKey":"quiz:8","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An employee reacts differently to the same instruction depending on whether it is given privately or in a team meeting. This best illustrates:","options":["Behaviour is unrelated to environment","Behaviour depends solely on personality traits","Behaviour is fixed regardless of context","Behaviour is a function of the person and the environment, B = f(P, E)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Foundations of OB"}},{"itemId":"55cbe3b8-548c-5098-af8d-ff452d9a2125","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"94886298-33a8-4de1-928e-025796e011cf","sourceKey":"quiz:80","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Low Uncertainty Avoidance (uncertainty tolerance) cultures are described as:","options":["accepting of uncertainty and open to risk-taking","opposed to all forms of change","obsessed with formal rules and structure","incapable of tolerating any ambiguity"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"3ca89088-b310-5702-82bd-b653bc8cf726","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"69f05ba5-0b2c-4bbc-9101-56cc80237c3f","sourceKey":"quiz:81","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the slides, which country is cited as having HIGH uncertainty avoidance?","options":["Hong Kong","Japan","Jamaica","United States"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"d5564eee-3eea-55e9-972a-e7bfd17967c4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7c8074a3-a3d4-4f21-bede-0e9d412aff7a","sourceKey":"quiz:82","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the notes, which country is cited as having LOW uncertainty avoidance?","options":["United States","Greece","Japan Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 9","France"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"68da67a0-c21f-5363-84ed-b038a5a5a5e9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0feabbbb-0442-4a24-ba11-ff56ceee8b92","sourceKey":"quiz:83","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Masculine culture, per the dimension, places emphasis on:","options":["aggressive and assertive behaviour, material success, and money","total rejection of achievement","quality of life, caring, and modesty","equal distribution of household labour only"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"fd1b7571-0221-58d5-864f-5879f24bbde8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7b58b9b0-27d0-44ed-8674-15185c309e1d","sourceKey":"quiz:84","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Feminine culture, per the dimension, is best characterised by:","options":["complete rejection of relationships","obsession with status symbols","relationship-orientation, concern for others' welfare, and modesty","aggressive competition for material wealth"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"07275851-3bd1-523f-b3ed-1975842549fc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e15d6ac8-1e87-4e86-9dc3-2448e3b6e40c","sourceKey":"quiz:85","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Long-Term Orientation cultures are characterised by:","options":["rejection of hard work","hard work, thrift, perseverance, and respect for tradition, fostering virtue oriented toward the future","total disregard for the future","consumption-driven behaviour focused only on the present"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"ff90883d-eca7-5ecf-bec6-044ff4065039","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b54a8c1a-678b-4928-8ee0-34ff8ce29a90","sourceKey":"quiz:86","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Short-Term Orientation cultures place relatively more emphasis on:","options":["values related to past and present, individual stability, reputation, and consumption","extreme thrift above all else","total rejection of tradition","multi-generational planning exclusively"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"e7d13dc0-b59b-53ea-b289-c3214daf9ff0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4796766c-2183-4200-98cf-bf5fcae408f7","sourceKey":"quiz:87","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the slides, which countries are cited as having a LONG-TERM orientation?","options":["United Kingdom and Germany","Taiwan and Hong Kong","United States and France","Nigeria and Ghana"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"4c784c09-d5e0-512c-b6c5-c58d459e4255","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"feea79bd-591c-49e9-ba8e-4a9636d9f088","sourceKey":"quiz:88","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the slides, which countries are cited as having a SHORT-TERM orientation?","options":["United States and France","India and Iran","Taiwan and Hong Kong","Japan and China"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"cfc2ecff-9bc0-580c-8e9b-519f840ad58c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6559a885-1cd0-4bef-b742-8298bb770d56","sourceKey":"quiz:89","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An Indulgent culture allows people to:","options":["strictly monitor every aspect of daily life","freely satisfy human needs related to enjoying life and having fun, valuing leisure and personal freedom","avoid any form of leisure","suppress all personal desires"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"fdc83b24-4531-5b93-bddf-47c2c06bda44","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1995ba03-4971-4a33-9d1c-475d354ef1ef","sourceKey":"quiz:9","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Operation Research Management\" as listed in the notes is an example of:","options":["An organisational specialisation","A conflict management style","A perceptual error","A cultural dimension"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: Foundations of OB"}},{"itemId":"1f9fc89c-3413-5f63-8d53-bdc46039f2c5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a7d6935-42f4-45bb-9736-2249dff3c80b","sourceKey":"quiz:90","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Restrained culture is described as:","options":["entirely focused on leisure","less concerned with satisfying leisure-related needs, with employees more likely to quit an unsatisfying job","indifferent to employee opinions","extremely permissive about personal freedom"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"b8781593-7e31-5555-a826-fc1c094e74ff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bc811089-0af3-467d-9937-61e95cd7999d","sourceKey":"quiz:91","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which countries are cited in the notes as examples of Restraint societies, contrasted with an indulgent society like the US?","options":["Japan and South Korea","Germany and Sweden","Canada and Australia","Iran and India"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"d1b72464-a9d8-51b4-83a8-5bcf91de9353","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d69c5e0a-3ea2-4c1b-ae3c-d961a1af5a17","sourceKey":"quiz:92","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Economically poorer Latin American countries like Guatemala and Panama are cited in the slides as having:","options":["long-term orientation and high indulgence","high uncertainty avoidance and low power distance","low power distance and high individualism","high power distance and low individualism"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"6781897e-df29-536f-977c-e176b901d448","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45c1a26e-3969-4750-9eee-aa76fe8a6ef9","sourceKey":"quiz:93","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Advanced Western countries such as the US, Germany, Netherlands, and UK are cited as having: Compiled by CampusIntell | Page 10","options":["extremely high power distance","the lowest individualism in the world","relatively low power distance","the highest uncertainty avoidance in the world"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"7ea5b491-51d3-50f8-b973-4a83170d91c8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"91020e0f-ee6a-43f6-ada8-428b2653e3c2","sourceKey":"quiz:94","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of Hofstede's six cultural dimensions?","options":["Uncertainty Avoidance EPRG / Implications","Emotional Intelligence","Long-Term vs Short-Term Orientation","Power Distance"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: Hofstede's Dimensions"}},{"itemId":"2622eaf5-c78d-5e2b-8d20-79803b3dfc90","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c375db03-14b2-4a53-8cc7-e316d653fbc7","sourceKey":"quiz:95","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Ethnocentric management philosophy is characterised by:","options":["sharing authority equally between all countries","corporate management implementing the same values and policies as the parent company regardless of environmental differences","adapting fully to local conditions in every subsidiary","ignoring the home country's practices entirely"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"dbefe175-e02b-5037-9da3-2a48d94f8ee4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1956aa15-e0ab-4220-b1a6-3a9020ddbddb","sourceKey":"quiz:96","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In an Ethnocentric organisation design, identification is based on:","options":["local subsidiary preference","the nationality of the owner","a truly international identity","the nationality of the host country"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"cf49dbe3-882c-5ca2-8a5d-d84be4d8fc81","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cac24fe5-9077-4aed-a838-6d377b258384","sourceKey":"quiz:97","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a Polycentric organisation, authority and decision-making are:","options":["entirely centralised at headquarters","relatively low in headquarters, with local subsidiaries given more autonomy","shared equally in a global collaborative approach","nonexistent"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"27de7ffc-e305-5979-9c29-1896c48da390","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e489e250-371e-4b88-a988-1e098ac8d136","sourceKey":"quiz:98","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Polycentric management philosophy reflects:","options":["complete disregard for local conditions","rejection of all local staff","strict imposition of home-country standards everywhere","awareness of environmental differences, with foreign operations managed as locally as possible"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}},{"itemId":"bccb417b-3bca-521a-a9fd-46f4f6e6ed02","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18048d2b-60b8-4dd3-910d-f87680fa721c","sourceKey":"quiz:99","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a Geocentric organisation, the approach to staffing key positions is to:","options":["develop the best people from anywhere in the world for key positions everywhere in the world","recruit only from the host country","avoid staffing key positions altogether","recruit only from the home country"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Topic: EPRG / Implications"}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
