select ok((select coalesce(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_object(
  'itemId', item_id, 'courseId', course_id, 'kind', kind,
  'questionId', question_id, 'sourceKey', source_key,
  'parentItemId', parent_item_id, 'revision', revision,
  'payload', payload) order by source_key collate "C"), '[]'::jsonb)
  from public.read_published_managed_content('58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed')
  where kind = 'note') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"a6e2c271-306d-5de6-9480-6bc557208ee7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:1","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","body":"What banking is and how it evolved; the functions, income, risks and regulation of commercial banks; credit creation and the money multiplier (with computations); the financial system, its components, functions and objectives; financial intermediation; non-bank financial institutions; financial markets; financial inclusion, innovation and globalisation.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":1,"mustKnow":[{"text":"What banking is and how it evolved; the functions, income, risks and regulation of commercial banks; credit creation and the money multiplier (with computations); the financial system, its components, functions and objectives; financial intermediation; non-bank financial institutions; financial markets; financial inclusion, innovation and globalisation."}],"lessons":[{"number":"1.1","title":"Banking, its origin and the types of banks","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Banking:","bold":true},{"text":" the business of accepting deposits from the public and lending or investing those funds (and providing payment and other financial services). Banks are intermediaries between savers (surplus units) and borrowers (deficit units)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank:","bold":true},{"text":" an institution whose main business is accepting deposits from the public and using them to grant loans and advances. Not a stock exchange, insurer or pension fund administrator."}]},{"text":[{"text":"\"Bank\"","bold":true},{"text":" comes from the Italian "},{"text":"banco","bold":true},{"text":": the "},{"text":"bench or table","bold":true},{"text":" on which money changers did their business. Italians were the first bankers."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Stages in the evolution of banking:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Valuables (gold) kept for safety in "},{"text":"temples and places of worship","bold":true},{"text":" (ancient Rome, Greece, Mesopotamia, China, Japan)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Money lending by merchants and money changers","bold":true},{"text":" (exchanged coins, later took deposits and gave loans)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Goldsmiths","bold":true},{"text":" (17th-century England) accepting gold on deposit and issuing receipts."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Commercial (joint-stock) banking."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Central banking","bold":true},{"text":" (issue of notes centralised, e.g. the Bank of England)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Barter replacing money is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a stage (money replaced barter)."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Goldsmiths:","bold":true},{"text":" charged fees and gave receipts. They noticed that "},{"text":"depositors rarely withdrew all their gold at the same time","bold":true},{"text":", so idle balances could be lent out at interest. They then paid interest on deposits to attract more. Their receipts circulated as payment and became "},{"text":"goldsmiths' notes","bold":true},{"text":", the forerunners of "},{"text":"bank notes","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"IOU (\"I owe you\"):","bold":true},{"text":" an "},{"text":"informal written acknowledgement of a debt","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"not negotiable","bold":true},{"text":" (cannot be assigned, sold or traded). A "},{"text":"promissory note","bold":true},{"text":" is a "},{"text":"negotiable","bold":true},{"text":" instrument. Contents of an IOU: parties, payment information, signatures, termination date, (ideally) a lawyer's involvement."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Types of banks","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Type"}],[{"text":"Key feature"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Central bank"}],[{"text":"Government-owned; regulates the banking system; does not serve the public (CBN)"}]],[[{"text":"Commercial bank"}],[{"text":"Accepts deposits, grants loans; income mainly the interest spread"}]],[[{"text":"Investment (merchant) bank"}],[{"text":"Capital-market work: underwriting, M&A advice, asset management, trading, research; income from "},{"text":"fees, commissions and profits on own investments","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Credit union"}],[{"text":"Member-owned, not-for-profit cooperative","bold":true},{"text":"; members are owners and customers; better savings rates, lower fees"}]],[[{"text":"Savings and loan association"}],[{"text":"Savings deposits and mortgage loans"}]],[[{"text":"Development bank"}],[{"text":"Government-sponsored long-term finance for development"}]],[[{"text":"Microfinance bank"}],[{"text":"Small loans and savings for "},{"text":"low-income people and small businesses","bold":true}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Lending existed before goldsmiths; goldsmiths did not invent lending, but lending out part of deposited gold laid the foundation of modern banking. So it is fair to say goldsmiths \"brought lending into banking\"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Underwriting:","bold":true},{"text":" the investment bank buys a whole new issue at an agreed price and resells it, bearing the risk of unsold shares."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mama Ngozi, an unbanked Onitsha trader who opens a small savings account and later borrows ₦50,000, is a typical microfinance bank customer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank of Industry (BOI) was formed in "},{"text":"2001","bold":true},{"text":" by merging the "},{"text":"Nigerian Industrial Development Bank (NIDB)","bold":true},{"text":", the Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry (NBCI) and NERFUND."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Today banking in Nigeria is governed by the "},{"text":"Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act (BOFIA) 2020","bold":true},{"text":", which replaced BOFIA 1991, and the "},{"text":"CBN Act 2007","bold":true},{"text":". The CBN Governor is "},{"text":"Olayemi Cardoso","bold":true},{"text":" (since 2023). The 2024 recapitalisation set minimum capital of ₦500 billion (international), ₦200 billion (national), ₦50 billion (regional and merchant) and ₦20 billion / ₦10 billion (non-interest national / regional), with a deadline of "},{"text":"31 March 2026","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"1.2","title":"What commercial banks do, how they earn, and their risks","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Two principal functions:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) "},{"text":"accepting deposits","bold":true},{"text":" from the public; (2) "},{"text":"granting loans and advances","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Deposits:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"current (demand)","bold":true},{"text":" (withdraw any time by cheque, little or no interest); "},{"text":"savings","bold":true},{"text":" (interest, limited withdrawals); "},{"text":"fixed (time)","bold":true},{"text":" (fixed period and rate agreed at the start; early withdrawal loses interest; higher rate the longer the term)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Advances:","bold":true},{"text":" loans, "},{"text":"overdrafts","bold":true},{"text":" (draw beyond your balance up to a limit; interest only on the overdrawn amount) and "},{"text":"bills discounting","bold":true},{"text":" (buying a bill before maturity for less than face value, collecting the full value at maturity)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Ancillary functions:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Agency:","bold":true},{"text":" collecting cheques, "},{"text":"dividends and interest","bold":true},{"text":"; paying insurance premiums and standing orders; buying and selling shares; remittances."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Acting as "},{"text":"executor, trustee or attorney","bold":true},{"text":" for customers' estates."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Safe custody","bold":true},{"text":" of wills, jewellery and documents."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Other credit services: "},{"text":"debt factoring","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"loan syndication","bold":true},{"text":", equipment leasing, direct credit, foreign exchange, advisory services."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Not commercial-bank functions: "},{"text":"issuing legal tender currency","bold":true},{"text":", lender of last resort (both CBN), "},{"text":"writing wills","bold":true},{"text":" (lawyers write wills; banks only keep them or act as executors)."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Debt factoring:","bold":true},{"text":" the bank "},{"text":"buys a customer's book debts at an agreed discount","bold":true},{"text":" and collects from the debtors."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loan syndication:","bold":true},{"text":" a "},{"text":"lead bank","bold":true},{"text":" brings several banks together to finance a large project no single bank could conveniently fund."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Income:","bold":true},{"text":" mainly "},{"text":"net interest income","bold":true},{"text":" = interest on loans minus interest on deposits (the spread); plus fees and investment income."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Goals of deposit money banks:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"profitability, liquidity, solvency","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Risks","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Risk"}],[{"text":"Meaning"}],[{"text":"Example"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Credit"}],[{"text":"Borrowers fail to repay"}],[{"text":"Floods ruin farmers who borrowed"}]],[[{"text":"Liquidity"}],[{"text":"Not enough cash for withdrawals"}],[{"text":"Funds locked in long mortgages; many withdraw at once"}]],[[{"text":"Interest rate"}],[{"text":"Rate changes hurt profit"}],[]],[[{"text":"Operational"}],[{"text":"Failed processes, people, fraud, systems"}],[{"text":"Cashier and fraudsters; computer fails to flag transfers"}]],[[{"text":"Market"}],[{"text":"Losses from price, exchange-rate moves"}],[]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Regulation:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"prudential","bold":true},{"text":" (safety of each bank: capital, asset quality, liquidity); "},{"text":"consumer protection","bold":true},{"text":" (disclosure of charges, complaints resolution); "},{"text":"monetary policy regulation","bold":true},{"text":" (reserve ratios, rates). "},{"text":"Capital adequacy","bold":true},{"text":" = minimum capital "},{"text":"relative to risk-weighted assets","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"Deposit insurance","bold":true},{"text":" (NDIC) protects depositors up to a limit if a bank fails, preventing runs and keeping confidence."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (net interest income):","bold":true},{"text":" loans ₦800m at 20%, deposits ₦1,000m at 8%. Interest earned = 20% × ₦800m = ₦160m. Interest paid = 8% × ₦1,000m = ₦80m. Net interest income = ₦160m − ₦80m = "},{"text":"₦80m","bold":true},{"text":". (₦96m and ₦240m are traps.)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (overdraft):","bold":true},{"text":" Bisi has ₦100,000 credit and a ₦500,000 overdraft limit; she withdraws ₦400,000. Overdrawn amount = ₦400,000 − ₦100,000 = "},{"text":"₦300,000","bold":true},{"text":"; interest is charged only on this. At 24% a year simple interest for 3 months: ₦300,000 × 0.24 × 3/12 = "},{"text":"₦18,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (bill discounting):","bold":true},{"text":" a ₦1,000,000 three-month bill is bought for ₦950,000. The bank's discount (income) = ₦1,000,000 − ₦950,000 = "},{"text":"₦50,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Spread:","bold":true},{"text":" paying 3% on savings and charging 8% on loans gives a margin of 5%."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NDIC cover now (since 2024):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"₦5 million","bold":true},{"text":" per depositor in deposit money banks (also payment service banks and mobile-money pass-through), "},{"text":"₦2 million","bold":true},{"text":" in microfinance and primary mortgage banks (formerly ₦500,000 and ₦200,000)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CBN core functions","bold":true},{"text":" (so you can reject them as commercial-bank work): issuing legal tender, banker and adviser to government, banker to banks and "},{"text":"lender of last resort","bold":true},{"text":" (emergency cash to a sound bank facing a run), managing external reserves, monetary policy, supervision. The CBN does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" take deposits from or lend to the public."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Direct monetary controls","bold":true},{"text":" (used before Nigeria moved to market-based management in 1993): credit ceilings, administered (fixed) interest rates, selective credit controls. "},{"text":"Open market operations","bold":true},{"text":" in treasury bills are "},{"text":"indirect","bold":true},{"text":" (market-based)."}]}]}]},{"number":"1.3","title":"Credit creation and the money multiplier","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Banks create money "},{"text":"by lending","bold":true},{"text":": each new loan creates a new "},{"text":"demand deposit","bold":true},{"text":". They do "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" print notes or coins (only the CBN issues currency)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Borrowing from banks abroad does not create money either: it only moves existing money from one holder to another. New money (deposits) arises only when a bank lends."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Assumptions:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) an initial "},{"text":"primary deposit in cash","bold":true},{"text":"; (2) only a "},{"text":"fraction","bold":true},{"text":" of each deposit is needed by depositors soon; (3) a fraction must by law be kept as "},{"text":"reserve","bold":true},{"text":"; (4) the rest, the "},{"text":"excess reserve","bold":true},{"text":", is lent out; (5) credit can be created to a multiple of the initial deposit; (6) the multiple depends on the reserve ratio. Also: loans are redeposited in banks and there is no cash drain."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Formulas","bold":true},{"text":" (r = cash reserve ratio as a decimal):"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Required reserve = r × deposit"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Excess reserve (amount lent at first stage) = deposit − required reserve = deposit × (1 − r)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Money (deposit, credit) multiplier: k = 1 / r"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Total deposits created (including the initial deposit) = initial deposit × k = initial deposit / r"}]},{"text":[{"text":"New (derived) deposits = total deposits − initial deposit"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Reserve ratio from totals: r = initial deposit / total deposits; multiplier k = total / initial"}]},{"text":[{"text":"The multiplier is the "},{"text":"inverse","bold":true},{"text":" (reciprocal) of the reserve ratio. Reserve ratio and total credit (and the multiplier) are "},{"text":"inversely","bold":true},{"text":" related."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Limits to credit creation:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) "},{"text":"size of the initial cash deposit","bold":true},{"text":"; (2) "},{"text":"reserve ratio","bold":true},{"text":"; (3) "},{"text":"leakages","bold":true},{"text":" (cash kept outside banks). Also: shortage of creditworthy borrowers or lack of collateral, the public's preference for holding cash, fear of cash drain, and "},{"text":"central bank contractionary policy","bold":true},{"text":". Borrowers "},{"text":"redepositing","bold":true},{"text":" loans in other banks does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" limit creation; it keeps it going."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Process, ₦1,000m at 25%:","bold":true},{"text":" Bank X keeps ₦250m, lends "},{"text":"₦750m","bold":true},{"text":". Bank Y receives ₦750m, keeps ₦187.5m, lends ₦562.5m. Bank Z receives ₦562.5m, keeps ₦140.625m, lends ₦421.875m. Total deposits = ₦1,000m × 1/0.25 = "},{"text":"₦4,000m","bold":true},{"text":". At 40%: ₦1,000m ÷ 0.40 = "},{"text":"₦2,500m","bold":true},{"text":". At 10%: ₦10,000m."}]},{"text":[{"text":"₦2,000,000 at 20%:","bold":true},{"text":" total = 2,000,000 ÷ 0.20 = "},{"text":"₦10,000,000","bold":true},{"text":" (new deposits ₦8,000,000 is the trap)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"₦5,000 at 25%:","bold":true},{"text":" k = 1 ÷ 0.25 = "},{"text":"4","bold":true},{"text":"; total = 5,000 × 4 = "},{"text":"₦20,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"₦500 at 15%:","bold":true},{"text":" k = 1 ÷ 0.15 = "},{"text":"6.67","bold":true},{"text":" (2 d.p.); total = 500 ÷ 0.15 = "},{"text":"₦3,333.33","bold":true},{"text":"; required reserve = 0.15 × 500 = ₦75; excess reserve = 500 − 75 = "},{"text":"₦425","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"₦1,000 at 15%:","bold":true},{"text":" 1,000 ÷ 0.15 = ₦6,666.67, about "},{"text":"₦6,667","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Finding r:","bold":true},{"text":" ₦15m creates ₦240m: k = 240 ÷ 15 = 16, r = 1 ÷ 16 = "},{"text":"6.25%","bold":true},{"text":". ₦35m creates ₦280m: r = 35 ÷ 280 = "},{"text":"12.5%","bold":true},{"text":". ₦50m creates ₦500m: k = "},{"text":"10","bold":true},{"text":", r = 10%."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Percentage changes (trap):","bold":true},{"text":" \"increased by 100%\" means "},{"text":"doubled","bold":true},{"text":"; \"increased by 50%\" means × 1.5 (10% becomes 15%, not 60%); \"reduced by 50%\" means halved; \"decreased by 25%\" means × 0.75; \"increased by 25%\" means × 1.25; \"increased by 40%\" means × 1.4."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Case"}],[{"text":"New r"}],[{"text":"New initial deposit"}],[{"text":"Total created"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"₦15m, 6.25% doubled"}],[{"text":"12.5%"}],[{"text":"₦15m"}],[{"text":"15 ÷ 0.125 = ₦120m"}]],[[{"text":"₦15m down 25%, 6.25%"}],[{"text":"6.25%"}],[{"text":"₦11.25m"}],[{"text":"11.25 ÷ 0.0625 = ₦180m"}]],[[{"text":"₦15m down 25%, r doubled"}],[{"text":"12.5%"}],[{"text":"₦11.25m"}],[{"text":"11.25 ÷ 0.125 = ₦90m"}]],[[{"text":"₦35m, 12.5% doubled"}],[{"text":"25%"}],[{"text":"₦35m"}],[{"text":"35 ÷ 0.25 = ₦140m"}]],[[{"text":"₦35m up 25%, 12.5%"}],[{"text":"12.5%"}],[{"text":"₦43.75m"}],[{"text":"43.75 ÷ 0.125 = ₦350m"}]],[[{"text":"₦35m down 25%, r doubled"}],[{"text":"25%"}],[{"text":"₦26.25m"}],[{"text":"26.25 ÷ 0.25 = ₦105m"}]],[[{"text":"₦10m at 10%"}],[{"text":"10%"}],[{"text":"₦10m"}],[{"text":"10 ÷ 0.10 = ₦100m"}]],[[{"text":"₦10m, 10% up 50%"}],[{"text":"15%"}],[{"text":"₦10m"}],[{"text":"10 ÷ 0.15 = ₦66.67m"}]],[[{"text":"₦10m, 10% down 50%"}],[{"text":"5%"}],[{"text":"₦10m"}],[{"text":"10 ÷ 0.05 = ₦200m"}]],[[{"text":"₦50m up 40%, 10% doubled"}],[{"text":"20%"}],[{"text":"₦70m"}],[{"text":"70 ÷ 0.20 = ₦350m"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"To create more money banks must "},{"text":"lend out more","bold":true},{"text":" of their deposits; raising their cash ratio with the CBN reduces creation."}]}]}]},{"number":"1.4","title":"The financial system and financial intermediation","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Financial system:","bold":true},{"text":" the network of "},{"text":"financial institutions, markets, instruments and regulations","bold":true},{"text":" that "},{"text":"channels funds from surplus units (savers) to deficit units (borrowers)","bold":true},{"text":". The 1976 Committee on the Nigerian Financial System called it \"the congeries of financial institutions and arrangements which serve the needs of an economy\". It operates at firm, national and global levels."}]},{"text":[{"text":"4 components:","bold":true},{"text":" financial "},{"text":"institutions","bold":true},{"text":", financial "},{"text":"markets","bold":true},{"text":", financial "},{"text":"instruments","bold":true},{"text":", financial "},{"text":"services","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Instruments:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"equity","bold":true},{"text":" (shares), "},{"text":"debt","bold":true},{"text":" (bonds, debentures, loans), "},{"text":"derivative","bold":true},{"text":" (options, futures, forwards, swaps), "},{"text":"hybrid","bold":true},{"text":" ("},{"text":"convertible bonds","bold":true},{"text":", preference shares)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"3 basic types of institutions:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"bank financial institutions","bold":true},{"text":" (BFIs), "},{"text":"non-bank financial institutions","bold":true},{"text":" (NBFIs), "},{"text":"regulatory and supervisory institutions","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Regulators:","bold":true},{"text":" CBN, Federal Ministry of Finance, "},{"text":"NDIC","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"SEC","bold":true},{"text":" (capital market), "},{"text":"NAICOM","bold":true},{"text":" (insurance), "},{"text":"PenCom","bold":true},{"text":" (pensions), FMBN (formerly mortgage institutions), FSRCC (coordinates regulators). NDIC and the "},{"text":"Debt Management Office","bold":true},{"text":" are "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" NBFIs."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Functions of the financial system (8):","bold":true},{"text":" mobilisation of savings; efficient capital allocation; "},{"text":"liquidity provision","bold":true},{"text":" (turning assets into cash quickly without significant loss of value); risk management and transfer; price discovery and information; payment and settlement; monetary policy transmission; promoting financial inclusion. The seven functions of financial markets (Lesson 1.6: savings mobilisation, storage of wealth, liquidity, credit, payments, protection against risk, monetary policy) are also functions of the financial system. Fixing the prices of goods and services is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a function."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Objectives of the Nigerian financial system (8):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Provide "},{"text":"non-inflationary","bold":true},{"text":" support to the economy."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Facilitate effective management of the economy."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Assist in transforming the "},{"text":"rural community","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Achieve greater "},{"text":"mobilisation of savings","bold":true},{"text":" and channel them efficiently."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Ensure that "},{"text":"no viable project is frustrated simply for lack of funds","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Protect the economy from the vicissitudes of the international economy."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Sustain indigenisation and management of the economy."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Achieve greater integration and linkage in agriculture."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Services:","bold":true},{"text":" intermediation, resource mobilisation, resource allocation, efficient payments, foreign exchange for trade."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Direct finance:","bold":true},{"text":" borrower raises funds straight from savers in the financial market (e.g. a company selling ₦5 billion of bonds to households). "},{"text":"Indirect finance (financial intermediation):","bold":true},{"text":" through an intermediary; the main route."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Financial intermediaries:","bold":true},{"text":" the general name for institutions that move funds from surplus to deficit units (BFIs and NBFIs both do this)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Weaknesses of direct financing:","bold":true},{"text":" much "},{"text":"time and effort","bold":true},{"text":" to find a lender; "},{"text":"high cost","bold":true},{"text":" (price) of funds; "},{"text":"short period","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"high risk","bold":true},{"text":" to the lender."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Advantages of intermediaries:","bold":true},{"text":" access to "},{"text":"bulky sums","bold":true},{"text":" (consortium if needed); "},{"text":"lower risk","bold":true},{"text":" per saver (pooling); "},{"text":"lower cost","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"longer term","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"How intermediaries promote savings, investment and capital formation:","bold":true},{"text":" by "},{"text":"pooling funds from surplus units and channelling them into productive investment","bold":true},{"text":" by deficit units; reducing risk, mobilising idle funds, giving liquidity and income to savers, and allocating funds expertly."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Why savers trust intermediaries:","bold":true},{"text":" they "},{"text":"pay interest","bold":true},{"text":", have a "},{"text":"reputation to protect","bold":true},{"text":", and let savers "},{"text":"withdraw when needed","bold":true},{"text":" (safety plus liquidity)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Success factors:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) "},{"text":"trust and confidence","bold":true},{"text":"; (2) "},{"text":"convenience","bold":true},{"text":" (little bureaucracy, nearness); (3) "},{"text":"price","bold":true},{"text":" (interest, fees). Loss of trust leads to "},{"text":"financial disintermediation","bold":true},{"text":" (mass withdrawal of funds)."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Maturity (securities) transformation:","bold":true},{"text":" banks lend for five years though most depositors leave money for months; they "},{"text":"bridge the different maturity (capital lock-up) periods","bold":true},{"text":". NBFIs do it by buying long-term primary securities and issuing their own indirect securities with different maturities. Other NBFI services: "},{"text":"aggregation","bold":true},{"text":", accessibility, specialisation, risk reduction."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank vs NBFI:","bold":true},{"text":" bank "},{"text":"liabilities (demand deposits withdrawable by cheque) are spendable as money","bold":true},{"text":", so banks can create credit; NBFI liabilities are not. Both mobilise surplus funds for deficit units."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Demand-following hypothesis:","bold":true},{"text":" finance grows "},{"text":"in response to demand","bold":true},{"text":" from a growing real economy (colonial Nigeria, dominated by expatriate banks financing export trade). "},{"text":"Supply-leading:","bold":true},{"text":" institutions are "},{"text":"set up ahead of demand","bold":true},{"text":" to mobilise savings and stimulate growth (Patrick, 1966). Do not confuse either with financial repression (rates fixed below market, credit rationed)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Factors in the development of Nigeria's system:","bold":true},{"text":" independence, the CBN (1958), civil war, oil boom, government financing needs, monetary policy changes, regulation and deregulation, the 1976 Financial System Review Committee, the 1972 Indigenisation Decree, SAP (1986), NDIC, EFCC, 2004 bank consolidation, 2009 reform."}]}]}]},{"number":"1.5","title":"Non-bank financial institutions","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"NBFIs are "},{"text":"heterogeneous","bold":true},{"text":": many different kinds, each with its own functions: insurers, pension funds and PFAs, finance companies, primary mortgage institutions, bureaux de change, discount houses, unit trusts, cooperatives."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Insurance:","bold":true},{"text":" two major types, "},{"text":"life","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"non-life","bold":true},{"text":". Two functions: "},{"text":"risk transfer","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"financial intermediation","bold":true},{"text":". Life covers loss from "},{"text":"death, disability or old age","bold":true},{"text":"; long-term; variants: "},{"text":"whole life","bold":true},{"text":" (paid on death) and "},{"text":"endowment assurance","bold":true},{"text":" (paid at the end of the term if alive, or to beneficiaries on earlier death). Non-life ("},{"text":"property","bold":true},{"text":": fire, theft, marine; "},{"text":"liability","bold":true},{"text":": negligence) is usually "},{"text":"yearly","bold":true},{"text":", so non-life funds go into short-term assets and life funds into long-term assets. Regulator: "},{"text":"NAICOM","bold":true},{"text":" (since 1997)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NPF/NSITF:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"National Provident Fund (1961)","bold":true},{"text":", for private-sector workers, was replaced by the "},{"text":"Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund (NSITF) in 1993 by Decree No. 73","bold":true},{"text":", because of "},{"text":"poor performance","bold":true},{"text":" (poor records, employers not remitting contributions). NSITF's "},{"text":"7 areas:","bold":true},{"text":" retirement pension, retirement grant, invalidity pension, invalidity grant, survivors' pension, survivors' grant, funeral grant."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Pension Reform Act 2004:","bold":true},{"text":" created the "},{"text":"National Pension Commission (PenCom)","bold":true},{"text":", plus "},{"text":"Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs)","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"Pension Fund Custodians (PFCs)","bold":true},{"text":". Each worker opens a "},{"text":"Retirement Savings Account (RSA) with a PFA of his choice","bold":true},{"text":"; the PFA invests, the "},{"text":"PFC keeps the assets in custody","bold":true},{"text":". PenCom "},{"text":"licenses and supervises PFAs and PFCs","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Defined contribution:","bold":true},{"text":" benefit depends on "},{"text":"contributions plus investment returns","bold":true},{"text":" in the RSA (worker bears investment risk). "},{"text":"Defined benefit:","bold":true},{"text":" benefit "},{"text":"fixed in advance by formula","bold":true},{"text":" (final salary, years of service, rank)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Primary mortgage institutions:","bold":true},{"text":" set up under "},{"text":"Decree No. 53 of 1989","bold":true},{"text":" to mobilise savings and give housing loans; first regulated by the "},{"text":"Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN)","bold":true},{"text":"; the CBN took over in the late 1990s. Now called primary mortgage banks, regulated by the CBN."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Finance houses/companies:","bold":true},{"text":" borrow "},{"text":"placements","bold":true},{"text":" and lend at higher rates; services include "},{"text":"LPO financing","bold":true},{"text":", leasing, project financing, debt factoring; under CBN oversight since 1991 (CBN Decree 24, BOFI Decree 25); may not take public deposits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bureaux de change (1989):","bold":true},{"text":" deal in foreign exchange for "},{"text":"small users","bold":true},{"text":". Purposes: cater for small users; bring the "},{"text":"parallel (black) market","bold":true},{"text":" under official purview and regulate it; eliminate the parallel-market premium by merging the rates. Also: give legal recognition to small dealers, free access for small buyers, better forex statistics, less pressure on the official market. Not for big corporate allocations, deposits or overdrafts. CBN oversight since 1997."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discount houses (1993):","bold":true},{"text":" dealt mainly in "},{"text":"short-term government securities","bold":true},{"text":" in the money market, between banks and the CBN."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Unit trusts:","bold":true},{"text":" pool many small savers' funds into a "},{"text":"diversified portfolio","bold":true},{"text":", with an independent trustee."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Current position:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"Pension Reform Act 2014","bold":true},{"text":" replaced the 2004 Act (minimum contributions 10% employer, 8% employee; the 2004 Act had 7.5% each). NSITF now runs the employees' compensation scheme. Discount houses were phased out in the mid-2010s, several becoming merchant banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Finance houses failed in the mid-1990s from speculation, rates up to 60%, loans without collateral and fund mismatch (short placements in fixed assets)."}]}]}]},{"number":"1.6","title":"Financial markets; inclusion, innovation and globalisation","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Financial market:","bold":true},{"text":" institutional arrangements that "},{"text":"mobilise funds from surplus units and channel them to deficit units","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"7 functions:","bold":true},{"text":" savings mobilisation, storage of wealth, liquidity, credit, payments system, "},{"text":"protection against risk","bold":true},{"text":", executing monetary policy. Not: fixing prices or issuing currency."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Money market"}],[{"text":"Capital market"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Short-term funds (up to one year)"}],[{"text":"Medium- to long-term funds"}]],[[{"text":"Treasury bills, commercial paper, bankers' acceptances, call money"}],[{"text":"Shares (equities), bonds, "},{"text":"debentures","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Liquidity, working capital"}],[{"text":"Expansion, factories (e.g. 15-year funds)"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Primary market:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"new issues","bold":true},{"text":"; the "},{"text":"only segment where capital formation occurs","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"Secondary market:","bold":true},{"text":" trading of "},{"text":"existing","bold":true},{"text":" securities."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Capital market:","bold":true},{"text":" market for raising medium- to long-term funds for investment. Principal institutions: "},{"text":"SEC","bold":true},{"text":" (apex regulator) and the stock exchange. Participants include issuing houses, stockbrokers, "},{"text":"underwriters","bold":true},{"text":", registrars; finance houses are "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" capital-market participants."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Benefit of a capital market to Nigeria:","bold":true},{"text":" it "},{"text":"channels medium- to long-term funds to firms and governments","bold":true},{"text":" for expansion and development projects."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Financial dualism:","bold":true},{"text":" organised and unorganised (informal: esusu, ajo, moneylenders) markets existing side by side."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bond prices move "},{"text":"inversely","bold":true},{"text":" with interest rates: a rise in the price of fixed-interest securities means the market rate has "},{"text":"fallen","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Current names:","bold":true},{"text":" the Nigerian Stock Exchange (NSE) demutualised in "},{"text":"2021","bold":true},{"text":" into "},{"text":"Nigerian Exchange Group","bold":true},{"text":", with "},{"text":"Nigerian Exchange Limited (NGX)","bold":true},{"text":" as the exchange. SEC now operates under the "},{"text":"Investments and Securities Act 2025","bold":true},{"text":" (replaced ISA 2007)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Financial inclusion:","bold":true},{"text":" all sections of society, especially low-income and vulnerable groups, have "},{"text":"access to appropriate, affordable financial services","bold":true},{"text":". Helped by "},{"text":"agent banking, mobile money and simple low-cost accounts","bold":true},{"text":" in underserved areas (Nigeria's National Financial Inclusion Strategy dates from 2012). Collateral for savings accounts or closing rural branches does the opposite."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Financial innovation:","bold":true},{"text":" new financial products, services or processes. "},{"text":"Globalisation:","bold":true},{"text":" growing interconnection of countries through flows of goods, services, finance, people and ideas."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Banking = taking deposits and lending; \"bank\" from Italian banco (bench); goldsmiths brought lending in; receipts became bank notes."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Principal functions: deposits and advances; agency (collect dividends); safe custody; no currency issue, no will writing."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Net interest income = interest earned − interest paid; overdraft interest only on the overdrawn amount."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Risks: credit, liquidity, interest rate, operational, market."}]},{"text":[{"text":"k = 1/r; total deposits = initial ÷ r; excess reserve = deposit × (1 − r); \"increased by 100%\" = doubled."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Limits: initial deposit, reserve ratio, leakages."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Financial system = institutions, markets, instruments, services; 3 institution types: BFIs, NBFIs, regulators."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Intermediation succeeds on trust, convenience, price; loss of trust = disintermediation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NPF 1961 → NSITF 1993 (Decree 73); PRA 2004 → PenCom, PFAs, PFCs; PMIs 1989 under FMBN."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Money market short-term, capital market long-term; primary market = capital formation; SEC is the capital-market apex regulator."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"dbe954f3-3729-5b02-be2c-8358c1436612","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"A bank receives a fresh cash deposit of ₦4,000,000. The cash reserve ratio is 20%. Find (a) the excess reserve of the first bank, (b) the money multiplier, (c) the total deposits the banking system can create, and (d) the total if the reserve ratio is increased by 25%."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"f4410e43-476b-545e-a3e0-76a6ef42b2bc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:10","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Bank-Customer Relationship","body":"Who a banker and a customer are (definitions and cases); the relationships between them; the banker's rights (charges and COT, lien, set-off, appropriation and Clayton's Rule) with their computations; the banker's duties, secrecy and when authority to pay ends; the customer's duties and rights; garnishee and bankruptcy; types of account, KYC, opening, mandates, powers of attorney and closing; special services.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":10,"mustKnow":[{"text":"Who a banker and a customer are (definitions and cases); the relationships between them; the banker's rights (charges and COT, lien, set-off, appropriation and Clayton's Rule) with their computations; the banker's duties, secrecy and when authority to pay ends; the customer's duties and rights; garnishee and bankruptcy; types of account, KYC, opening, mandates, powers of attorney and closing; special services."}],"lessons":[{"number":"10.1","title":"Who is a banker and who is a customer?","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"J.W. Gilbert:","bold":true},{"text":" a banker is \"a dealer in capital, or more properly a dealer in money. He is an intermediate party between the borrower and the lender. He borrows from one party and lends to another.\""}]},{"text":[{"text":"Hart:","bold":true},{"text":" a banker receives money and collects cheques for customers and honours cheques drawn on him by them, up to their current account balances."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Sir John Paget:","bold":true},{"text":" no one can be a banker who does not (1) take deposit accounts, (2) take current accounts, (3) issue and pay cheques, (4) collect cheques, crossed and uncrossed, for customers."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Halsbury's Laws of England:","bold":true},{"text":" a banker is an individual, partnership or corporation whose sole or predominant business is banking, that is, "},{"text":"the receipt of money on current or deposit account and the payment of cheques drawn by, and collection of cheques paid in by, a customer","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Criticism of Hart, Paget and Halsbury:","bold":true},{"text":" they describe "},{"text":"deposit banking","bold":true},{"text":" well but are "},{"text":"not wide enough","bold":true},{"text":" to cover the other businesses banks now do."}]},{"text":[{"text":"United Dominions Trust v Kirkwood (1966):","bold":true},{"text":" the marks of a banker: accepting money from and collecting cheques for customers, and "},{"text":"honouring cheques or orders drawn on the bank","bold":true},{"text":", and keeping current (or similar) accounts."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Akwule and Others v The Queen (1963), Supreme Court of Nigeria:","bold":true},{"text":" \"banker\" means "},{"text":"the company carrying on banking business","bold":true},{"text":", not an employee of the bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nigerian law today:","bold":true},{"text":" a bank is a company licensed by the CBN to carry on banking business under the "},{"text":"Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act (BOFIA) 2020","bold":true},{"text":" (which replaced BOFIA 1991)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Customer:","bold":true},{"text":" there is "},{"text":"no statutory definition","bold":true},{"text":"; the courts supply the principles. A person, firm, society or company becomes a customer when he "},{"text":"offers","bold":true},{"text":" to open an account and the bank "},{"text":"accepts","bold":true},{"text":" (offer and acceptance, as in any contract)."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Case"}],[{"text":"Rule"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Great Western Railway v London and County Banking Co. (1901)"}],[{"text":"A man who "},{"text":"merely cashed cheques","bold":true},{"text":" for his employer for many years was "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a customer"}]],[[{"text":"Commissioners of Taxation v English, Scottish and Australian Bank (1920)"}],[{"text":"Duration is not of the essence","bold":true},{"text":"; a person is a customer as soon as an account is opened"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Akwule in practice: if Mr Eze's funded cheque is wrongly returned at a Yaba branch, he sues "},{"text":"the bank","bold":true},{"text":", his banker and debtor, not the cashier, the branch manager or the CBN."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Duration and frequency of dealings are not decisive. Adaeze, whose account was opened yesterday with one ₦50,000 deposit, is already a customer. Tunde the messenger who cashed his employer's cheques weekly for ten years is not (GWR case)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trap: a customer does not need a loan, a cheque book or six months' operation. The relationship needs "},{"text":"both parties' intention","bold":true},{"text":", so it is "},{"text":"contractual","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"10.2","title":"The relationships between banker and customer","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Debtor and creditor (the primary relationship):","bold":true},{"text":" customer in credit = "},{"text":"creditor","bold":true},{"text":", bank = "},{"text":"debtor","bold":true},{"text":". Customer overdrawn = "},{"text":"debtor","bold":true},{"text":", bank = "},{"text":"creditor","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Principal and agent:","bold":true},{"text":" bank is "},{"text":"agent","bold":true},{"text":" when it collects cheques, makes payments (standing orders, premiums), buys or sells shares and securities, or does foreign exchange for the customer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bailor and bailee:","bold":true},{"text":" items left for "},{"text":"safe custody","bold":true},{"text":"; customer = "},{"text":"bailor","bold":true},{"text":", bank = "},{"text":"bailee","bold":true},{"text":". The bank charges a fee, so it is a "},{"text":"bailee for reward","bold":true},{"text":" and its standard of care is "},{"text":"high","bold":true},{"text":"; lost items are paid for."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mortgagor and mortgagee:","bold":true},{"text":" on a loan secured by property, customer = "},{"text":"mortgagor","bold":true},{"text":", bank = "},{"text":"mortgagee","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trustee and beneficiary:","bold":true},{"text":" where money is given to the bank for a "},{"text":"specific purpose","bold":true},{"text":" (held in a fiduciary capacity)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Contractual:","bold":true},{"text":" the whole relationship rests on contract (offer and acceptance at account opening, plus terms and mandate)."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Foley v Hill (1848):","bold":true},{"text":" money paid in "},{"text":"becomes the bank's money","bold":true},{"text":", to use as it pleases; the bank only owes an "},{"text":"equivalent sum","bold":true},{"text":". It is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" held on trust and need not be kept separately."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Joachimson v Swiss Bank Corporation (1921):","bold":true},{"text":" the bank need not repay a current account balance "},{"text":"until the customer demands it","bold":true},{"text":". Repayment is on "},{"text":"written demand, at the branch where the account is kept, during banking hours","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"General relationship:","bold":true},{"text":" from ordinary business: deposits, withdrawals, cheques, statements. "},{"text":"Special relationship:","bold":true},{"text":" from extra services: safe custody, advice, guarantees, letters of credit, powers of attorney, standing orders, direct debits, trustee services. Special services may carry "},{"text":"higher","bold":true},{"text":" duties."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Reversal of the ordinary rule: in law a debtor must seek out his creditor. In banking the "},{"text":"customer (creditor) must seek out the bank (debtor)","bold":true},{"text":" and demand payment."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Because the bank is a debtor, not a trustee, it may lend depositors' money to a manufacturer in Ikeja "},{"text":"without asking them","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Matching scenarios: Halima with ₦300,000 in credit is creditor; Sola overdrawn by ₦150,000 is debtor. Kemi asking the bank to buy ₦500,000 of FGN bonds and collect a Kano cheque makes the bank her "},{"text":"agent","bold":true},{"text":". Chief Okonkwo leaving his C of O and jewellery for a fee is "},{"text":"bailor","bold":true},{"text":". Musa mortgaging his Lekki house for a ₦10 million loan is "},{"text":"mortgagor","bold":true},{"text":", the bank "},{"text":"mortgagee","bold":true},{"text":". Mrs Ade's ₦5 million held only for her late brother's children's school fees makes the bank a "},{"text":"trustee","bold":true},{"text":", the children "},{"text":"beneficiaries","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Employer and employee","bold":true},{"text":" is never a banker-customer relationship."}]}]}]},{"number":"10.3","title":"Rights of the banker","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"To charge "},{"text":"reasonable commission","bold":true},{"text":" for services and "},{"text":"interest","bold":true},{"text":" on advances."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banker's lien.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Set-off","bold":true},{"text":" (combination of accounts)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Appropriation","bold":true},{"text":" of payments."}]},{"text":[{"text":"That the "},{"text":"customer seeks out the bank","bold":true},{"text":" for repayment."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To "},{"text":"return irregularly drawn","bold":true},{"text":" cheques."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To "},{"text":"return cheques that would create an unauthorised overdraft","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To "},{"text":"recall an overdraft","bold":true},{"text":", which is repayable on demand."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To be "},{"text":"reimbursed","bold":true},{"text":" for expenses incurred on the customer's behalf."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Banker's lien:","bold":true},{"text":" a "},{"text":"general lien","bold":true},{"text":": the right to "},{"text":"retain","bold":true},{"text":" a customer's securities that come into the bank's hands "},{"text":"in the ordinary course of business","bold":true},{"text":" until his debt is paid. It carries an "},{"text":"implied power of sale","bold":true},{"text":" and is "},{"text":"lost if the bank voluntarily parts with possession","bold":true},{"text":". It does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" cover items left for safe custody or for a specific purpose, and does not apply where the parties agreed no lien."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Set-off:","bold":true},{"text":" combining a "},{"text":"credit balance on one account","bold":true},{"text":" with a "},{"text":"debit balance on another","bold":true},{"text":" of the same customer, using the credit to reduce the debt. Conditions: (1) accounts in the "},{"text":"same name and same right","bold":true},{"text":"; (2) the debt "},{"text":"due and payable","bold":true},{"text":"; (3) "},{"text":"no agreement excluding","bold":true},{"text":" set-off; (4) notice usually given as good practice. Personal and "},{"text":"trust","bold":true},{"text":" accounts cannot be combined."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Appropriation order:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"customer","bold":true},{"text":" may appropriate first; if he does not, the "},{"text":"bank","bold":true},{"text":" may; if neither does, "},{"text":"Clayton's Rule","bold":true},{"text":" applies to a running account."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clayton's Rule (Devaynes v Noble, 1816):","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"first item on the debit side is discharged by the first item on the credit side","bold":true},{"text":" (first in, first out)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Commission on turnover (COT):","bold":true},{"text":" a charge on "},{"text":"current account","bold":true},{"text":" customers' "},{"text":"debit","bold":true},{"text":" entries for a period. "},{"text":"COT = periodic rate × total debit entries.","bold":true},{"text":" Savings accounts do "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" attract COT."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"COT (₦), bank-style.","bold":true},{"text":" Tola had ₦40,000 credit on 1 January; withdrew ₦50,000 (15 Jan), ₦30,000 (20 Feb), ₦10,000 (8 Mar). COT 15% per annum."}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Quarterly rate = 15% ÷ 4 = 3.75% = 0.0375."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Total debits = 50,000 + 30,000 + 10,000 = ₦90,000. (The opening balance is not a debit entry.)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"COT = 0.0375 × 90,000 = "},{"text":"₦3,375","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Current position: the CBN phased COT down from 2013, abolished it from 2016 and replaced it with a capped "},{"text":"current account maintenance fee","bold":true},{"text":" charged "},{"text":"per mille","bold":true},{"text":" (per ₦1,000) of customer-induced debits (CBN Guide to Charges). Exam questions still compute COT as above."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lien example.","bold":true},{"text":" Mr Bello owes ₦2 million. The bank holds share certificates received to collect his dividends (ordinary course of business) and a sealed jewellery box left for safe custody. Lien covers the "},{"text":"share certificates only","bold":true},{"text":". No court order or consent is needed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Set-off example.","bold":true},{"text":" Chidi: savings ₦450,000 Cr; current ₦620,000 Dr (due); trustee account for his father's estate ₦300,000 Cr. The trust account is in a different right, so leave it out. ₦620,000 − ₦450,000 = "},{"text":"₦170,000","bold":true},{"text":" still owed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clayton's Rule, partner's death.","bold":true},{"text":" Firm overdrawn ₦800,000 when Bayo dies; account not stopped; ₦500,000 paid in, ₦300,000 withdrawn."}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"The ₦500,000 credit discharges the oldest debit first: ₦800,000 − ₦500,000 = "},{"text":"₦300,000","bold":true},{"text":" of the old debt left (Bayo's estate remains liable for this)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The ₦300,000 withdrawal is a "},{"text":"new","bold":true},{"text":" debt, for which the estate is not liable."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clayton's Rule, guarantee.","bold":true},{"text":" Mrs Ojo ends her guarantee at ₦1,200,000; Dapo pays in ₦700,000 and withdraws ₦900,000. Her liability = 1,200,000 − 700,000 = "},{"text":"₦500,000","bold":true},{"text":". The ₦900,000 is new borrowing she never guaranteed. To protect itself, a bank "},{"text":"stops (rules off) the account","bold":true},{"text":" and opens a new one when a guarantee ends or a partner dies."}]}]}]},{"number":"10.4","title":"Duties of the banker, secrecy, and when authority to pay ends","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Duties of the banker:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"To "},{"text":"honour cheques","bold":true},{"text":" when (a) there are sufficient funds or an agreed overdraft, (b) the cheque is properly drawn and presented, (c) there is no legal bar."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To "},{"text":"receive","bold":true},{"text":" money and "},{"text":"collect","bold":true},{"text":" cheques for the customer with care."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To give "},{"text":"reasonable notice before closing","bold":true},{"text":" an account in credit."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To "},{"text":"furnish statements","bold":true},{"text":" of account periodically."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Duty of care","bold":true},{"text":" (act in good faith, without negligence)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To "},{"text":"pay agreed interest","bold":true},{"text":" on deposits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To "},{"text":"alert the customer","bold":true},{"text":" to irregularities on his account."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Secrecy.","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Wrongful dishonour:","bold":true},{"text":" a bank that wrongly returns a funded cheque is "},{"text":"liable in damages for breach of contract, including injury to the customer's credit","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Tournier v National Provincial and Union Bank of England (1924):","bold":true},{"text":" secrecy is a "},{"text":"legal duty arising from contract","bold":true},{"text":", but "},{"text":"qualified","bold":true},{"text":" by "},{"text":"4 exceptions","bold":true},{"text":":"}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Compulsion by law","bold":true},{"text":" (court order, subpoena, statute)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Duty to the public.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Interest of the bank.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Express or implied consent of the customer.","bold":true}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Secrecy "},{"text":"continues after the account is closed","bold":true},{"text":". A spouse has no right to the other's account details without authority."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"When the bank's authority to pay ends (12 cases):"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Lack of funds. 2. Countermand (stop payment). 3. Notice of the customer's "},{"text":"death","bold":true},{"text":". 4. Notice of defect in the presenter's title (lost or stolen cheque). 5. Notice of the customer's "},{"text":"mental incapacity","bold":true},{"text":". 6. Assignment of the balance. 7. "},{"text":"Garnishee order","bold":true},{"text":" or court injunction. 8. "},{"text":"Bankruptcy","bold":true},{"text":" of the customer. 9. "},{"text":"Forged","bold":true},{"text":" drawer's signature. 10. Irregularly drawn cheque. 11. Demand outside banking hours or at the wrong branch. 12. A lawful order freezing the account."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Duties of the customer:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Draw cheques with "},{"text":"reasonable care","bold":true},{"text":" so as not to facilitate fraud."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Seek out","bold":true},{"text":" the bank and demand repayment."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Issue cheques only against "},{"text":"available funds","bold":true},{"text":" or an agreed overdraft."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Pay bank charges","bold":true},{"text":" as agreed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Inform the bank promptly","bold":true},{"text":" of forgery, a lost cheque book or other suspicious dealings."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Rights of the customer: to know his balance; to "},{"text":"withdraw without notice","bold":true},{"text":" by cheque if the current account is in funds; to appropriate his payments; to receive statements; to confidentiality; to reasonable notice of closure; to "},{"text":"close the account","bold":true},{"text":" if not indebted."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Secrecy scenarios: a Federal High Court order for a fraud suspect's statements = "},{"text":"compulsion by law","bold":true},{"text":" (so are suspicious transaction reports to the NFIU under the Money Laundering (Prevention and Prohibition) Act 2022). Suing a guarantor and showing the principal debtor's balance, or briefing the bank's solicitor on a debt = "},{"text":"interest of the bank","bold":true},{"text":". Reporting a customer trading with an enemy in wartime, or a public officer looting public funds = "},{"text":"duty to the public","bold":true},{"text":". Kunle writing to let his auditors have his statements, or authorising a status report = "},{"text":"express consent","bold":true},{"text":". Funke closed her account in 2023; in 2026 the bank must still refuse a journalist."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Statements let the customer "},{"text":"reconcile","bold":true},{"text":" his records and detect errors."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Countermand","bold":true},{"text":" must be "},{"text":"in writing, clear and unambiguous, signed per the mandate, and received before the cheque is paid","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A "},{"text":"forged","bold":true},{"text":" drawer's signature makes the cheque "},{"text":"wholly inoperative","bold":true},{"text":"; it carries no mandate, so the bank cannot debit the customer, however skilful the forgery."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Death","bold":true},{"text":" cancels all mandates: Mr Danjuma's cheques signed the week before must "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" be paid after notice of death. A customer travelling abroad on holiday does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" end authority."}]},{"text":[{"text":"London Joint Stock Bank v Macmillan and Arthur (1918):","bold":true},{"text":" partners signed a cheque with blank spaces; a clerk raised the amount. The customer owes a duty to "},{"text":"draw cheques carefully so as not to facilitate fraud","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"bears the loss","bold":true},{"text":" of his carelessness."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Garnishee order:","bold":true},{"text":" a court order "},{"text":"attaching","bold":true},{"text":" funds in the customer's account to satisfy a "},{"text":"judgment debt","bold":true},{"text":" he owes a third party. Parties: judgment creditor (Mr Adamu), judgment debtor (Mrs Lawal), "},{"text":"garnishee = the bank","bold":true},{"text":" (Union Bank). On service of the "},{"text":"order nisi","bold":true},{"text":" the bank "},{"text":"freezes","bold":true},{"text":" the account and pays only when the order is made "},{"text":"absolute","bold":true},{"text":" or as the court directs."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bankruptcy","bold":true},{"text":" (Bankruptcy Act): on notice of a bankruptcy order the customer's "},{"text":"authority to operate the account ceases","bold":true},{"text":", the bank freezes it, and the "},{"text":"trustee in bankruptcy steps into his shoes","bold":true},{"text":". For an unsecured debt the bank "},{"text":"proves in the bankruptcy","bold":true},{"text":" and shares with other creditors; a secured creditor may realise its security."}]}]}]},{"number":"10.5","title":"Accounts: types, opening, mandates and closing","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"The 3 main types of deposit account (a "},{"text":"loan account","bold":true},{"text":" is not one):"}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Feature"}],[{"text":"Current"}],[{"text":"Savings"}],[{"text":"Fixed (time)"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Deposit type"}],[{"text":"Demand","bold":true},{"text":" deposit"}],[{"text":"Savings deposit"}],[{"text":"Time","bold":true},{"text":" deposit"}]],[[{"text":"Withdrawal"}],[{"text":"By "},{"text":"cheque","bold":true},{"text":", on demand, no notice"}],[{"text":"Withdrawal slip, by holder in person"}],[{"text":"Only at "},{"text":"maturity","bold":true},{"text":"; penalty if early"}]],[[{"text":"Interest"}],[{"text":"Little or none"}],[{"text":"Earns interest"}],[{"text":"Highest","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"References"}],[{"text":"Required (traditionally)"}],[{"text":"Not required"}],[{"text":"Not required"}]],[[{"text":"COT / overdraft"}],[{"text":"COT; overdraft possible"}],[{"text":"No COT; no overdraft"}],[{"text":"Deposit receipt issued"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Savings rule (traditional):","bold":true},{"text":" more than "},{"text":"3 withdrawals in a month","bold":true},{"text":" forfeits that month's interest."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Domiciliary account:","bold":true},{"text":" a "},{"text":"foreign-currency","bold":true},{"text":" account (e.g. US dollars) for receiving remittances and holding foreign exchange, subject to CBN rules."}]},{"text":[{"text":"KYC (Know Your Customer):","bold":true},{"text":" verifying a customer's "},{"text":"identity, address and beneficial ownership","bold":true},{"text":" before and during the relationship. Documents: photograph, ID (NIN, passport, driver's licence, voter's card), proof of address, "},{"text":"BVN","bold":true},{"text":"; companies add CAC documents, TIN, board resolution. "},{"text":"Enhanced due diligence","bold":true},{"text":" for high-risk customers: "},{"text":"PEPs, non-residents","bold":true},{"text":", complex structures."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mandate:","bold":true},{"text":" the customer's formal authority stating "},{"text":"who may sign cheques and operate the account","bold":true},{"text":". The bank must follow it strictly. Joint mandates: \"EITHER TO SIGN\", \"BOTH TO SIGN\", \"ANY TWO OF THE THREE TO SIGN\"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Power of attorney:","bold":true},{"text":" a formal written authority, "},{"text":"signed and sealed","bold":true},{"text":", by which the "},{"text":"donor","bold":true},{"text":" empowers the "},{"text":"donee (attorney)","bold":true},{"text":" to act for him generally or in specified matters. "},{"text":"Specific","bold":true},{"text":" (one purpose) or "},{"text":"General","bold":true},{"text":" (anything the donor may lawfully do by an attorney)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"On receiving a power of attorney the bank must:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Sight the original."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Keep a certified true copy in the customer's file."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Ensure it is stamped."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Operate strictly within the listed powers."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Follow the donor's instructions on revocation."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"A power of attorney ends by:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Completion of its purpose. 2. Expiry of its time. 3. Death of donor or donee. 4. Mental incapacity of either. 5. Bankruptcy of either. 6. Express revocation by the donor. 7. Implication (the donor resumes operating the account)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Documents for opening:"}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Customer"}],[{"text":"Key documents"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Limited company"}],[{"text":"Board resolution and extract naming signatories, memorandum and articles, certificate of incorporation (plus CAC returns)"}]],[[{"text":"Partnership"}],[{"text":"Firm's name; application signed by all partners; business-name search; deed of partnership; mandate with "},{"text":"joint and several liability","bold":true},{"text":" clause"}]],[[{"text":"Club, union, association"}],[{"text":"Rules; resolution; "},{"text":"extract of minutes certified by Chairman/President and Secretary","bold":true},{"text":"; specimen signatures and photographs"}]],[[{"text":"Executor"}],[{"text":"Grant of probate","bold":true},{"text":" (administrator: letters of administration)"}]],[[{"text":"Government or agency"}],[{"text":"Gazette establishing it; authority from Accountant-General (federal or state) or council Chairman and Secretary"}]],[[{"text":"Minor"}],[{"text":"Titled guardian's name "},{"text":"\"IN RE\"","bold":true},{"text":" minor's name and date of birth; guardian operates until "},{"text":"18","bold":true}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Tope and Yemi's mandate says \"BOTH TO SIGN\"; a ₦80,000 cheque signed only by Tope must be "},{"text":"refused","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"On a joint account, the balance passes to the "},{"text":"survivor","bold":true},{"text":" (rule of survivorship) unless the terms say otherwise."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Delegation of mandate","bold":true},{"text":" rules: no delegation on "},{"text":"trust accounts","bold":true},{"text":" (all trustees must sign), so the Ibadan orphanage trustee cannot delegate to his secretary; minors are not accepted as agents; an agent who does not sign as agent is personally liable; joint holders must all authorise any delegation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Alhaji Sani's power letting his son only withdraw for school fees is "},{"text":"specific","bold":true},{"text":". A salary credit never revokes a power of attorney."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Closing accounts.","bold":true},{"text":" A bank may close a "},{"text":"credit","bold":true},{"text":" account, after reasonable notice, for unreasonableness, frequent negligent operation, "},{"text":"habitual drawing on uncleared effects","bold":true},{"text":", fraud links or image risk. General procedure: (1) closing form completed, (2) unused cheques returned, (3) closing charges determined, (4) closing entries posted, (5) closing balance determined, (6) balance paid, (7) ledger marked "},{"text":"\"CLOSED\"","bold":true},{"text":", (8) entry in the opened and closed register. A new cheque book is never part of closing."}]}]}]},{"number":"10.6","title":"Special services that create special relationships","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Safe custody:","bold":true},{"text":" items (title deeds, C of O, share certificates, wills, jewellery) taken in a sealed envelope or locked box "},{"text":"\"contents unknown\"","bold":true},{"text":" (or listed and signed for); the customer gets a "},{"text":"receipt that must be surrendered","bold":true},{"text":" to withdraw them. The bank does not open, value, lend or pay interest on them."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Status enquiry (banker's opinion):","bold":true},{"text":" a reply on a customer's creditworthiness, given "},{"text":"briefly, in general terms, in confidence and \"without responsibility\"","bold":true},{"text":", without disclosing balances; the bank must still take care it is "},{"text":"not misleading","bold":true},{"text":". It never makes the bank a guarantor."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Reply"}],[{"text":"Meaning"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"\"Undoubted for your figures\""}],[{"text":"Strongest","bold":true},{"text":": considerable strength"}]],[[{"text":"\"Considered good for your figures\""}],[{"text":"Satisfactory"}]],[[{"text":"\"Should be good for your figures\""}],[{"text":"Some doubt"}]],[[{"text":"\"We cannot speak for your figures\""}],[{"text":"Unfavourable"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Standing order:","bold":true},{"text":" customer instructs his bank to pay a "},{"text":"fixed","bold":true},{"text":" amount regularly to a named beneficiary. "},{"text":"Direct debit:","bold":true},{"text":" customer authorises a "},{"text":"third party (originator)","bold":true},{"text":" to collect "},{"text":"variable","bold":true},{"text":" amounts from his account."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Agency services:","bold":true},{"text":" collecting cheques and dividend warrants, paying premiums, rent and bills, buying and selling shares, acting as trustee or executor. Accepting fixed deposits is "},{"text":"deposit-taking","bold":true},{"text":", not agency."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Bisi's phone company pulling a different amount monthly = "},{"text":"direct debit","bold":true},{"text":"; her bank paying a fixed ₦20,000 to her landlord = "},{"text":"standing order","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"No statutory definition of customer: offer and acceptance; duration not essential (Taxation Commissioners 1920); mere cheque-cashing is not enough (GWR 1901)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Akwule (1963): the banker is the bank, not its staff."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Customer in credit = creditor; overdrawn = debtor. Foley v Hill: deposit is the bank's money. Joachimson: written demand, at the branch, in banking hours."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bailor-bailee (safe custody, bailee for reward), agent, mortgagor-mortgagee, trustee; employer-employee never."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lien is general, with implied power of sale, not over safe custody. Set-off needs same name and same right."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clayton's Rule: first debit cleared by first credit; stop the account on death or end of guarantee."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Tournier: 4 exceptions: law, public duty, bank's interest, consent; secrecy survives closure."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Death, countermand, garnishee, bankruptcy, forgery and mental incapacity end authority to pay; travel does not."}]},{"text":[{"text":"COT = periodic rate × total debits; current accounts only."}]},{"text":[{"text":"KYC with EDD for PEPs; executors need probate; minors \"IN RE\"; no delegation on trust accounts."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"93b3314f-d916-555a-8263-c86999e9cc3f","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Ngozi guaranteed Ade's overdraft and gave notice ending her guarantee when the balance was ₦950,000 Dr. The bank did not stop the account. Ade then paid in ₦400,000 and ₦250,000 and withdrew ₦600,000. Using Clayton's Rule, find (a) Ngozi's remaining liability and (b) the total overdraft."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Bank-Customer Relationship","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"ec17bbbb-a980-543d-b948-0b20447f2e84","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:11","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","body":"How banks help foreign traders; the methods of receiving payment for exports and who bears the risk; documentary letters of credit (parties, UCP 600, types); collections (clean, documentary, D/P, D/A, URC 522); shipping documents; nostro, vostro and domiciliary accounts; Form M, Form NXP and Form A; spot and forward exchange; export finance (bill negotiation, pre- and post-shipment finance, forfaiting, factoring, export credit insurance); NEXIM; the trade roles of merchant and commercial banks.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":11,"mustKnow":[{"text":"How banks help foreign traders; the methods of receiving payment for exports and who bears the risk; documentary letters of credit (parties, UCP 600, types); collections (clean, documentary, D/P, D/A, URC 522); shipping documents; nostro, vostro and domiciliary accounts; Form M, Form NXP and Form A; spot and forward exchange; export finance (bill negotiation, pre- and post-shipment finance, forfaiting, factoring, export credit insurance); NEXIM; the trade roles of merchant and commercial banks."}],"lessons":[{"number":"11.1","title":"Banks in foreign trade and the methods of payment","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Role of banks in international trade:","bold":true},{"text":" banks lend to traders for their overseas deals (overdraft and other lending facilities) and act as the "},{"text":"link between customers and their overseas trade partners","bold":true},{"text":", especially in collecting payment for exports."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Methods banks use to obtain payment for exports:","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Open account:","bold":true},{"text":" the exporter sends the "},{"text":"documents of title direct to the importer","bold":true},{"text":" abroad, has "},{"text":"no control","bold":true},{"text":" of the goods and "},{"text":"relies completely on the importer's honesty","bold":true},{"text":" to remit the proceeds after receiving (and selling) the goods. Most risky; discouraged."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Payment by collection:","bold":true},{"text":" the exporter ships the goods and sends the bill of exchange (with or without documents) to "},{"text":"his banker for collection","bold":true},{"text":"."}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Clean collection:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"only the bill","bold":true},{"text":" of exchange is sent, no shipping documents."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Documentary collection:","bold":true},{"text":" the bill is "},{"text":"accompanied by commercial documents","bold":true},{"text":" (bill of lading, insurance certificate or policy, and others)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Not fully safe: the exporter "},{"text":"cannot be 100% sure","bold":true},{"text":" the importer will pay."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Documentary letter of credit:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"surest","bold":true},{"text":" method and the one most advised to customers; it gives a "},{"text":"guarantee of payment to the exporter provided he complies with the terms of the credit","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bill negotiation:","bold":true},{"text":" an exporter who needs funds and cannot wait "},{"text":"negotiates the bill to his bank by endorsement and delivery","bold":true},{"text":"; the bank pays "},{"text":"face value less a discount for commission and interest","bold":true},{"text":", then collects the full amount at maturity."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Payment in advance (cash in advance):","bold":true},{"text":" the importer pays before shipment. Safest for the exporter; "},{"text":"greatest risk for the importer","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Exporter's risk, lowest to highest:","bold":true},{"text":" payment in advance, letter of credit, documentary collection, clean collection, "},{"text":"open account","bold":true},{"text":". The importer's risk runs the other way."}]},{"text":[{"text":"How the money actually moves:","bold":true},{"text":" by "},{"text":"mail transfer","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"telegraphic (wire) transfer","bold":true},{"text":" or "},{"text":"bank draft","bold":true},{"text":". Banks actually remit abroad by "},{"text":"telegraphic (wire) transfer","bold":true},{"text":", sent over "},{"text":"SWIFT","bold":true},{"text":"; standing orders and direct debits are domestic."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A Kano sesame exporter who hands his bank a bill drawn on a German buyer "},{"text":"with no shipping documents","bold":true},{"text":" is asking for a "},{"text":"clean collection","bold":true},{"text":". Add the bill of lading and insurance certificate and it becomes a "},{"text":"documentary collection","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Open account = documents go "},{"text":"directly","bold":true},{"text":" to the buyer. Collection = documents go "},{"text":"through the banks","bold":true},{"text":". Do not confuse \"the importer's bank guarantees payment\" (a credit) with a collection, where no bank guarantees anything."}]}]}]},{"number":"11.2","title":"Documentary letters of credit","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Documentary letter of credit (LC):","bold":true},{"text":" a written undertaking by a bank (the issuing bank), given at the importer's request, to pay the exporter a stated sum "},{"text":"against documents that comply exactly","bold":true},{"text":" with the terms of the credit."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Party"}],[{"text":"Who it is"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Applicant","bold":true}],[{"text":"the "},{"text":"importer","bold":true},{"text":", who asks his bank to open the credit"}]],[[{"text":"Beneficiary","bold":true}],[{"text":"the "},{"text":"exporter","bold":true},{"text":", in whose favour the credit is opened"}]],[[{"text":"Issuing (opening) bank","bold":true}],[{"text":"the "},{"text":"importer's bank","bold":true},{"text":", which opens the credit in favour of the exporter"}]],[[{"text":"Advising bank","bold":true}],[{"text":"a bank in the "},{"text":"exporter's country","bold":true},{"text":" (usually his bank) that "},{"text":"tells the exporter","bold":true},{"text":" the credit has been opened, without necessarily adding its own promise to pay"}]],[[{"text":"Confirming bank","bold":true}],[{"text":"a bank (usually in the exporter's country) that "},{"text":"adds its own definite undertaking to pay","bold":true}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Process:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) exporter and importer sign a sales agreement with specific terms; (2) the "},{"text":"importer's bank (issuing bank) opens the credit","bold":true},{"text":" in favour of the exporter; (3) the "},{"text":"exporter's bank (advising bank)","bold":true},{"text":" is advised of it; (4) goods are shipped; (5) the exporter presents the documents; (6) "},{"text":"the documents of title are released to the importer only against payment (D/P) or acceptance (D/A) of the bill of exchange","bold":true},{"text":", so the exporter keeps control of the goods until then."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Rules:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"UCP 600","bold":true},{"text":" (Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits), published by the "},{"text":"International Chamber of Commerce (ICC)","bold":true},{"text":", in force since 1 July 2007."}]},{"text":[{"text":"3 key UCP 600 principles:","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A credit is "},{"text":"irrevocable even if it does not say so","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Irrevocable","bold":true},{"text":" = it "},{"text":"cannot be amended or cancelled without the agreement of the issuing bank, any confirming bank and the beneficiary","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banks deal with documents, not with goods.","bold":true},{"text":" Complying documents must be paid; "},{"text":"non-complying documents may be refused","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Types of credit:","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Type"}],[{"text":"Feature"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Confirmed","bold":true}],[{"text":"a second bank adds its own undertaking to pay"}]],[[{"text":"Sight","bold":true}],[{"text":"paid "},{"text":"when compliant documents are presented","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Usance (term, deferred)","bold":true}],[{"text":"paid at a "},{"text":"stated future date","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Transferable","bold":true}],[{"text":"the beneficiary (a middleman) "},{"text":"transfers part or all of the credit to a second beneficiary","bold":true},{"text":", his supplier"}]],[[{"text":"Back-to-back","bold":true}],[{"text":"a middleman uses the credit from his buyer "},{"text":"as security for a second, new credit","bold":true},{"text":" opened in favour of his supplier"}]],[[{"text":"Red clause","bold":true}],[{"text":"lets the exporter "},{"text":"draw an advance before shipment","bold":true},{"text":" to buy and pack the goods"}]],[[{"text":"Revolving","bold":true}],[{"text":"the amount is "},{"text":"restored after each drawing","bold":true},{"text":" for an agreed period"}]],[[{"text":"Standby","bold":true}],[{"text":"works as a "},{"text":"guarantee","bold":true},{"text":", paid only if the applicant "},{"text":"fails to perform or pay","bold":true}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"An Aba shoe exporter who doubts the overseas issuing bank asks a reputable Nigerian bank to add its promise: the credit is now "},{"text":"confirmed","bold":true},{"text":". Confirmation is optional; an irrevocable credit does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" have to carry a second bank's guarantee."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A Port Harcourt importer whose documents comply cannot stop payment by complaining about goods still at sea. The bank "},{"text":"pays","bold":true},{"text":", because banks deal in documents. His remedy is against the seller under the sales contract."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Transferable vs back-to-back:","bold":true},{"text":" transferable = the "},{"text":"same","bold":true},{"text":" credit passed on (Mrs Ezenwa passing part of her Ghanaian buyer's credit to the Kano factory). Back-to-back = a "},{"text":"second, separate","bold":true},{"text":" credit backed by the first."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A Lagos manufacturer importing the same raw material from India every month uses a "},{"text":"revolving","bold":true},{"text":" credit instead of opening a new one each time."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Standby credits follow "},{"text":"ISP98","bold":true},{"text":" (or UCP 600); collections follow URC 522 (Lesson 11.3). Incoterms (CIF, FOB and so on) are delivery terms, not LC rules."}]}]}]},{"number":"11.3","title":"Documentary collections and shipping documents","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Documentary collection:","bold":true},{"text":" the exporter's bank sends the bill and shipping documents to a bank in the importer's country, which releases them to the importer on his paying or accepting. "},{"text":"Banks act only as agents and give no undertaking to pay.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Rules:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"URC 522","bold":true},{"text":" (Uniform Rules for Collections, ICC)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Parties:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"principal","bold":true},{"text":" (exporter, drawer); "},{"text":"remitting bank","bold":true},{"text":" (the "},{"text":"exporter's bank","bold":true},{"text":", which receives the documents and sends them abroad); "},{"text":"collecting bank","bold":true},{"text":" (any bank in the process other than the remitting bank); "},{"text":"presenting bank","bold":true},{"text":" (the collecting bank that presents the documents to the importer); "},{"text":"drawee","bold":true},{"text":" (importer)."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[],[{"text":"D/P (documents against payment)"}],[{"text":"D/A (documents against acceptance)"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Importer gets documents"}],[{"text":"only "},{"text":"on paying","bold":true}],[{"text":"on "},{"text":"accepting a bill payable later","bold":true},{"text":" (e.g. 90 days)"}]],[[{"text":"Bill"}],[{"text":"at sight"}],[{"text":"usance (term)"}]],[[{"text":"Exporter's risk"}],[{"text":"lower: no payment, no documents"}],[{"text":"higher: importer has the goods before he pays"}]]]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Collection"}],[{"text":"Letter of credit"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"URC 522"}],[{"text":"UCP 600"}]],[[{"text":"Banks are agents only; "},{"text":"no bank undertaking","bold":true}],[{"text":"Issuing bank undertakes to pay","bold":true},{"text":" against compliant documents"}]],[[{"text":"Less safe, cheaper"}],[{"text":"Surest, dearer"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Shipping documents:","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Bill of lading (B/L):","bold":true},{"text":" issued by the shipping company. "},{"text":"3 functions:","bold":true},{"text":" (i) "},{"text":"receipt","bold":true},{"text":" for the goods, (ii) "},{"text":"evidence of the contract of carriage","bold":true},{"text":", (iii) "},{"text":"document of title","bold":true},{"text":", transferable by "},{"text":"endorsement","bold":true},{"text":"; the holder can take delivery."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Commercial invoice:","bold":true},{"text":" prepared by the "},{"text":"seller","bold":true},{"text":"; shows description, quantity, unit price and total value; used for "},{"text":"payment and customs valuation","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Certificate of origin:","bold":true},{"text":" states the "},{"text":"country where the goods were produced or manufactured","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Insurance policy or certificate:","bold":true},{"text":" proves the goods are covered against loss in transit; under "},{"text":"CIF","bold":true},{"text":" terms the buyer claims on it if goods are damaged at sea."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Air waybill:","bold":true},{"text":" the carrier's receipt for goods sent by air; "},{"text":"not a document of title","bold":true},{"text":", so goods are released to the "},{"text":"named consignee","bold":true},{"text":" without it."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Why prefer a collection to open account even though neither has a bank guarantee? Because in a collection the "},{"text":"shipping documents stay under bank control until the buyer pays or accepts","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The Onitsha importer who accepted a 90-day bill, took the documents, cleared the goods and then refused to pay: the exporter lost because the collection was on "},{"text":"D/A","bold":true},{"text":" terms."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trap: a bill of lading does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" certify the country of origin; that is the certificate of origin's job."}]}]}]},{"number":"11.4","title":"Foreign currency accounts, Nigerian trade forms and foreign exchange","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"International trade needs international settlements; two accounts are principally kept: "},{"text":"nostro","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"vostro","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nostro","bold":true},{"text":" (Latin \"our\"): accounts "},{"text":"maintained by Nigerian banks abroad","bold":true},{"text":", usually in foreign currency with a "},{"text":"correspondent bank","bold":true},{"text":", to "},{"text":"receive export proceeds","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"settle payments requiring foreign currency","bold":true},{"text":" overseas."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Vostro","bold":true},{"text":" (Latin \"your\"): accounts "},{"text":"kept by foreign commercial banks with Nigerian banks","bold":true},{"text":", into which payments in local currency are made and from which those banks "},{"text":"settle their obligations in Nigeria in naira","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loro","bold":true},{"text":" (\"their\"): an account of a third bank, referred to by two other banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"One account, two names: a Nigerian bank's dollar account in New York is its "},{"text":"nostro","bold":true},{"text":"; the New York bank calls it a "},{"text":"vostro","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Domiciliary account:","bold":true},{"text":" an account held in a "},{"text":"foreign currency","bold":true},{"text":" (USD, GBP, EUR and so on) in a "},{"text":"bank in Nigeria","bold":true},{"text":", by residents or non-residents, for foreign remittances, trade payments and holding forex. Since deregulation, exporters may keep their forex earnings in it. "},{"text":"2 types:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Export Domiciliary Account","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"Ordinary Domiciliary Account","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nigerian trade forms","bold":true},{"text":" (all processed through an "},{"text":"authorised dealer bank","bold":true},{"text":"):"}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Form"}],[{"text":"Use"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Form M","bold":true}],[{"text":"statutory "},{"text":"import","bold":true},{"text":" document, completed and "},{"text":"registered electronically","bold":true},{"text":" through the authorised dealer bank, supported by a "},{"text":"proforma invoice","bold":true},{"text":", before importing"}]],[[{"text":"Form NXP","bold":true},{"text":" (Nigerian Export Proceeds Form)"}],[{"text":"statutory "},{"text":"export","bold":true},{"text":" form, so the export and the "},{"text":"repatriation of its proceeds","bold":true},{"text":" are documented and tracked"}]],[[{"text":"Form A","bold":true}],[{"text":"foreign exchange for "},{"text":"invisible","bold":true},{"text":" (service) payments, e.g. school fees, medical bills, travel"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Spot transaction:","bold":true},{"text":" settled almost immediately (normally within "},{"text":"2 business days","bold":true},{"text":") at "},{"text":"today's rate","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Forward exchange contract:","bold":true},{"text":" fixes a rate "},{"text":"today","bold":true},{"text":" for exchange at a "},{"text":"future date","bold":true},{"text":"; removes exchange rate risk."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Formula:","bold":true},{"text":" Saving from forward = Foreign amount x (Spot rate on settlement day - Forward rate). A negative answer is an extra cost."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (forward saving):","bold":true},{"text":" an importer books a forward contract to buy USD 25,000 in 3 months at ₦1,520 per US dollar. On settlement day the spot rate is ₦1,560."}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Cost at spot: 25,000 x 1,560 = ₦39,000,000."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cost under forward: 25,000 x 1,520 = ₦38,000,000."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Saving: ₦39,000,000 - ₦38,000,000 = "},{"text":"₦1,000,000","bold":true},{"text":" (check: 25,000 x 40 = ₦1,000,000)."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"If the naira had instead strengthened to ₦1,500, he would still pay ₦1,520: 25,000 x (1,500 - 1,520) = -₦500,000, an extra cost of ₦500,000 compared with spot. A forward removes uncertainty both ways; it is cover, not a bet."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A Lagos importer who owes USD 40,000 in three months and fears naira weakness should take a "},{"text":"forward exchange contract","bold":true},{"text":" now, not wait for a spot purchase in three months."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Traps: the account every bank keeps with the "},{"text":"CBN","bold":true},{"text":" for clearing is not a nostro; a customer's dollar account at a local branch is "},{"text":"domiciliary","bold":true},{"text":", not nostro. Form M is for "},{"text":"imports","bold":true},{"text":", NXP for "},{"text":"exports","bold":true},{"text":". Nostro and vostro work is done in a bank's "},{"text":"Reconciliation Unit","bold":true},{"text":"; LCs and collections in "},{"text":"Trade Services","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"11.5","title":"Export finance, NEXIM and the trade roles of banks","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Pre-shipment finance:","bold":true},{"text":" money to "},{"text":"buy, process and pack","bold":true},{"text":" goods "},{"text":"before shipment","bold":true},{"text":" (e.g. packing credit, red clause credit)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Post-shipment finance:","bold":true},{"text":" money "},{"text":"after shipment","bold":true},{"text":", e.g. "},{"text":"negotiating or discounting the export bill","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bill negotiation formula:","bold":true},{"text":" Amount credited = Face value - Interest - Commission, where Interest = Face value x Rate x (Months / 12) and Commission = Face value x Commission rate."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Factoring:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"sale of short-term book debts","bold":true},{"text":" (receivables) to a factor, who pays an agreed portion (often about 60% of book value) at once."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Forfaiting:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"purchase, without recourse, of medium-term export receivables","bold":true},{"text":" such as "},{"text":"avalised","bold":true},{"text":" (bank-guaranteed) bills or promissory notes."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Export credit insurance:","bold":true},{"text":" protects the exporter against "},{"text":"commercial risk","bold":true},{"text":" (buyer's default) "},{"text":"and political risk","bold":true},{"text":" (war, transfer bans) in the buyer's country."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NEXIM (Nigerian Export-Import Bank):","bold":true},{"text":" established by the "},{"text":"NEXIM Act No. 38 of 1991","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"jointly owned by the Federal Government and the CBN","bold":true},{"text":", as Nigeria's "},{"text":"Export Credit Agency","bold":true},{"text":", with a share capital of ₦50 billion under the 1991 Act. "},{"text":"5 functions:","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Export "},{"text":"credit guarantee and credit insurance","bold":true},{"text":" facilities to Nigerian exporters."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Domestic credit insurance","bold":true},{"text":" that assists exports."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A "},{"text":"foreign exchange revolving fund","bold":true},{"text":" lent to exporters to "},{"text":"import foreign inputs","bold":true},{"text":" for producing exportable goods."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Investment guarantee and investment insurance","bold":true},{"text":" facilities to exporters."}]},{"text":[{"text":"An adequate "},{"text":"information system","bold":true},{"text":" to support export business."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"NEXIM also gives "},{"text":"short- and medium-term loans","bold":true},{"text":" to exporters, short-term "},{"text":"guarantees for bank loans","bold":true},{"text":" to exporters, and cover against "},{"text":"political and commercial risks","bold":true},{"text":" of non-payment by foreign buyers. It does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" grant mortgage loans."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Merchant banks' foreign exchange services","bold":true},{"text":" (wholesale, to corporates): "},{"text":"correspondent banking","bold":true},{"text":", where they "},{"text":"arrange the confirmation of letters of credit","bold":true},{"text":" for clients; and "},{"text":"operations","bold":true},{"text":": opening LCs, remitting export proceeds to clients' beneficiaries abroad, getting forex for clients' payments abroad. Their "},{"text":"traditional first function is acceptance","bold":true},{"text":": adding their name to bills of exchange to finance others' trade (hence \"accepting houses\")."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Commercial banks in international trade:","bold":true},{"text":" accepting and collecting foreign bills; discounting bills and documentary credits so sellers are paid at once; "},{"text":"confirming letters of credit","bold":true},{"text":"; buying and selling foreign currency; acting as "},{"text":"referees","bold":true},{"text":" (status opinions) on traders' creditworthiness. They do "},{"text":"not fix the official exchange rate","bold":true},{"text":"; that is the CBN's role."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (bill negotiation):","bold":true},{"text":" Chief Adebayo, a Lagos cocoa exporter, negotiates a ₦4,000,000 bill 3 months before maturity. Interest 12% p.a., commission 0.5% of face value."}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Interest: 4,000,000 x 0.12 x 3/12 = ₦120,000."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Commission: 4,000,000 x 0.005 = ₦20,000."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Credited: 4,000,000 - 120,000 - 20,000 = "},{"text":"₦3,860,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Traps: ₦3,880,000 forgets the commission; ₦3,980,000 forgets the interest."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"A Sapele timber exporter financed to process the timber and later having his bill discounted has received "},{"text":"pre-shipment then post-shipment","bold":true},{"text":" finance, in that order."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A cashew exporter fearing war or a currency transfer ban in the buyer's country needs "},{"text":"export credit insurance","bold":true},{"text":" such as NEXIM offers. Paying school fees abroad is a Form A matter, not a NEXIM fund."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Status opinion:","bold":true},{"text":" when a Ghanaian importer asks about a Nigerian exporter, the exporter's bank gives a confidential reply on his creditworthiness, acting as a "},{"text":"referee","bold":true},{"text":". It is given in confidence and without responsibility, but must not be misleading."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Exporter's risk rises: advance payment, LC, documentary collection, clean collection, "},{"text":"open account","bold":true},{"text":" (riskiest). Advance payment is riskiest for the importer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clean collection = bill only; documentary = bill plus documents; banks are agents only, under "},{"text":"URC 522","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"LC: applicant = importer; beneficiary = exporter; issuing = importer's bank; advising = exporter's bank; confirming adds a promise. "},{"text":"UCP 600","bold":true},{"text":": irrevocable even if silent; documents, not goods."}]},{"text":[{"text":"D/P = documents on payment; D/A = documents on acceptance (riskier)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Transferable, back-to-back, red clause (advance), revolving (reinstated), standby (guarantee)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"B/L = receipt, contract of carriage, document of title; air waybill is not a title document."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nostro = ours abroad; vostro = theirs here in naira; domiciliary = forex account in Nigeria (export and ordinary)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Form M imports, Form NXP exports, Form A invisibles. Forward fixes today's rate for a future date."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bill negotiation: face value - interest - commission. NEXIM: Act 38 of 1991, FGN and CBN, export credit agency."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Merchant banks: acceptance, LC confirmation. Commercial banks act as referees; the CBN, not they, sets the official rate."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"9437fe54-c183-5fea-93dc-a733022fb320","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"An exporter negotiates a ₦6,000,000 export bill with his bank 2 months before maturity. The bank charges interest at 15% per annum and a commission of 0.25% of face value. How much does he receive?"}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"34973020-0754-559b-b52e-67d797deaced","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:12","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Bank Lending","body":"Lending, borrowing, interest and risk assets; principles and objectives of lending; the 5 Cs and canons; security, its qualities and forms; loan procedure and agreement terms; loan versus overdraft; bad debts and recovery; loan computations.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":12,"mustKnow":[{"text":"Lending, borrowing, interest and risk assets; principles and objectives of lending; the 5 Cs and canons; security, its qualities and forms; loan procedure and agreement terms; loan versus overdraft; bad debts and recovery; loan computations."}],"lessons":[{"number":"12.1","title":"Meaning of lending and its principles","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Bank lending:","bold":true},{"text":" the extension of a credit facility to an intending borrower on the understanding that the principal and the interest on it will be repaid at an agreed future date. Lending is the "},{"text":"most profitable","bold":true},{"text":" business of banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Borrowing:","bold":true},{"text":" the ability of a willing and creditworthy customer to obtain a credit facility from a bank or other loan provider, with a view to repaying the sum at an agreed future date (with or without interest)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Interest:","bold":true},{"text":" the return paid by the borrower to the provider of funds, usually stated as a "},{"text":"percentage of the principal","bold":true},{"text":" per month or per annum. Banks charge a "},{"text":"higher rate","bold":true},{"text":" to compensate for the "},{"text":"risk that the borrower may default","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Risk asset:","bold":true},{"text":" loans and advances are called risk assets because the "},{"text":"recovery of all the credit extended is uncertain","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Three principles of lending (Mather, 1972):","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Safety:","bold":true},{"text":" of "},{"text":"paramount importance","bold":true},{"text":". The borrower's character, integrity and reliability must be sound; the source of repayment must be probed and certain."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Suitability:","bold":true},{"text":" the purpose must not conflict with the government's economic and monetary policies. No lending for gambling, betting or other speculative activities."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Profitability:","bold":true},{"text":" banks are not charities; every advance must earn profit (interest)."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Objectives banks seek when granting credit (4):","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Objective"}],[{"text":"Meaning"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Profitability"}],[{"text":"loans, advances and investments yield good returns to shareholders"}]],[[{"text":"Liquidity"}],[{"text":"assets can quickly become cash to meet depositors' withdrawals and CBN requirements"}]],[[{"text":"Safety"}],[{"text":"depositors' funds are safeguarded so they can withdraw on demand without delay"}]],[[{"text":"Suitability"}],[{"text":"lending fits sound policy and regulators' rules; not all loans committed to long-term projects"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Trap: "},{"text":"speculation","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"growth","bold":true},{"text":" are not lending objectives."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A bank that lends out almost all its deposits fails on "},{"text":"liquidity","bold":true},{"text":"; a bank that keeps everything as cash and lends nothing sacrifices "},{"text":"profitability","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A customer with a good record and good security who wants money for football betting or a gambling business is refused on "},{"text":"suitability","bold":true},{"text":", not safety."}]},{"text":[{"text":"BOFIA 2020","bold":true},{"text":" (which replaced BOFIA 1991) requires that a bank manager or officer may grant an advance, loan or credit facility only if it is "},{"text":"authorised in accordance with the rules and regulations of the bank","bold":true},{"text":" and in line with the CBN's regulations on collateralisation (section 17)."}]}]}]},{"number":"12.2","title":"The 5 Cs and the canons of lending","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"The 5 Cs of lending:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Character:","bold":true},{"text":" honesty, integrity, willingness to repay. The "},{"text":"first and best security","bold":true},{"text":"; \"the real security for any advance is the character of the borrower\". Checked by a "},{"text":"status enquiry","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Capacity:","bold":true},{"text":" ability to repay; track record, technical and managerial know-how."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Capital:","bold":true},{"text":" the borrower's own contribution to the project."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Collateral:","bold":true},{"text":" security to fall back on. The "},{"text":"last and least","bold":true},{"text":" consideration, a residual matter not crucial to the decision."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Conditions:","bold":true},{"text":" the economic environment and the sector's prospects."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Status enquiry:","bold":true},{"text":" obtaining background information on a prospective borrower (from other banks and sources) to find out whether he is "},{"text":"prudent or prodigal","bold":true},{"text":" with borrowed funds."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"The canons of lending: 7 questions on receiving a loan request","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"How much does the customer require? ("},{"text":"Amount","bold":true},{"text":")"}]},{"text":[{"text":"What does he want it for? ("},{"text":"Purpose","bold":true},{"text":")"}]},{"text":[{"text":"For how long? ("},{"text":"Duration","bold":true},{"text":")"}]},{"text":[{"text":"What is the source of repayment? ("},{"text":"Repayment","bold":true},{"text":")"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Is the business strong enough to keep going if plans suffer a setback? ("},{"text":"Strength of the business","bold":true},{"text":")"}]},{"text":[{"text":"What security can he offer? ("},{"text":"Security","bold":true},{"text":")"}]},{"text":[{"text":"What is your assessment of the customer? ("},{"text":"The borrower","bold":true},{"text":")"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Checklist for assessing proposals (9):","bold":true},{"text":" character, capacity, capital, purpose, amount, repayment, stability, security, monitoring."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Capital:","bold":true},{"text":" before a ₦50 million cassava-processing loan, the bank insists the promoters put in substantial money of their own. A borrower with little at stake may not be committed, leaving the risk mostly on the bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Conditions:","bold":true},{"text":" in a recession, a Lagos bank cuts new loans to hotels and event centres even for good customers."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Collateral","bold":true},{"text":" never replaces assessing ability to repay. A highly creditworthy borrower with a proven track record may get an advance "},{"text":"without collateral","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Amount:","bold":true},{"text":" lend too little and the project may be "},{"text":"abandoned uncompleted","bold":true},{"text":", so repayment never comes; lend too much and the bank takes excess risk."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Purpose:","bold":true},{"text":" must be legal and outside banks' no-go areas. An illegal activity is the purpose a Nigerian banker is least willing to finance. Borrowing "},{"text":"short","bold":true},{"text":" to buy fixed assets such as a factory building means \"borrowing short and investing long\", which is risky because fixed assets are not quickly saleable."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Duration:","bold":true},{"text":" deposits are mostly "},{"text":"short-term","bold":true},{"text":", so commercial banks prefer short-term working-capital finance. Medium and long-term requests go to merchant banks and development institutions such as the "},{"text":"Bank of Industry (BOI)","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Repayment:","bold":true},{"text":" a personal customer shows earnings and outgoings; a company submits a "},{"text":"cash budget, projected profit and loss account and projected balance sheet","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Strength of the business:","bold":true},{"text":" balance sheets and profit and loss accounts for about "},{"text":"three years","bold":true},{"text":" show whether it can remain a going concern."}]}]}]},{"number":"12.3","title":"Security and its qualities","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Security (collateral):","bold":true},{"text":" anything of value deposited or pledged as a guarantee of repayment of a loan, to be forfeited on default; an asset the bank can fall back on if the borrower fails to repay."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Security interest:","bold":true},{"text":" an enforceable legal claim on collateral pledged for a loan, giving the lender the right to repossess the property if the borrower fails to repay."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Margin:","bold":true},{"text":" the difference between the market value of the security and the loan granted against it."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Why banks take security:","bold":true},{"text":" a safety net if repayment fails; a customer who has deposited his property or title documents is more likely to "},{"text":"use the funds judiciously","bold":true},{"text":" for the agreed purpose."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Rule:","bold":true},{"text":" obtain and "},{"text":"perfect","bold":true},{"text":" the security "},{"text":"before disbursing","bold":true},{"text":" funds."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Qualities of good security (9):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Non-encumbrance:","bold":true},{"text":" free of disputes and of prior charges to other lenders."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Stability of price","bold":true},{"text":" and ease of valuation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Legal acceptability.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Adequacy:","bold":true},{"text":" covers the full loan plus a reasonable margin."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Proper documentation","bold":true},{"text":" (mortgage deed, pledge agreement)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Ownership and possession","bold":true},{"text":" (undoubted title)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Income generation / low maintenance cost.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Marketability:","bold":true},{"text":" a ready, broad market."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liquidity:","bold":true},{"text":" quickly convertible to cash at a known price."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Mrs Bello's shop already charged to another bank lacks "},{"text":"non-encumbrance","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bankers dislike "},{"text":"luxury goods","bold":true},{"text":" as security because their prices swing with fashion and taste; necessities are more stable."}]},{"text":[{"text":"An illegal asset is hard to dispose of and exposes the bank to legal consequences."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A block of flats paying rent beats a vacant plot: "},{"text":"income generation","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mr Ojo's late father's house, not in his name and disputed by family, lacks "},{"text":"undoubted title","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"High maintenance cost","bold":true},{"text":" is never a quality of good security."}]}]}]},{"number":"12.4","title":"Forms of security","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Personal security:","bold":true},{"text":" a guarantee by the borrower or a third party instead of a tangible asset (personal guarantee, life policy)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Non-personal (tangible) security:","bold":true},{"text":" movable and immovable property: land (mortgage), debentures, shares, goods (lien, pledge, hypothecation). "},{"text":"Safer","bold":true},{"text":", because the property can be sold to recover the unpaid amount."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Freehold","bold":true},{"text":" land: absolute title, lasts indefinitely, higher value. "},{"text":"Leasehold:","bold":true},{"text":" title expires; value "},{"text":"falls","bold":true},{"text":" as the unexpired years reduce."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mortgage:","bold":true},{"text":" a security created by contract giving the lender an interest in the borrower's property until a sum is repaid; written agreement registered at the land registry. Borrower = "},{"text":"mortgagor","bold":true},{"text":"; bank = "},{"text":"mortgagee","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Point"}],[{"text":"Legal mortgage"}],[{"text":"Equitable mortgage"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Legal estate"}],[{"text":"vests in the mortgagee"}],[{"text":"stays with the mortgagor"}]],[[{"text":"Creation"}],[{"text":"mortgage deed, under seal"}],[{"text":"deposit of title deeds"}]],[[{"text":"Possession"}],[{"text":"immediate right for mortgagee"}],[{"text":"mortgagor keeps the property"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Guarantee:","bold":true},{"text":" a promise by one person ("},{"text":"guarantor / surety","bold":true},{"text":") to answer for the present or future debt of another ("},{"text":"principal debtor","bold":true},{"text":"), made to the creditor (the "},{"text":"bank","bold":true},{"text":"). "},{"text":"Specific","bold":true},{"text":" guarantee: one debt (e.g. a term loan). "},{"text":"Continuing","bold":true},{"text":" guarantee: a series of transactions, e.g. an overdraft, present and future balances."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Advantages of a guarantee:","bold":true},{"text":" easy to take (just sign); useful where the borrower has no property. "},{"text":"Disadvantages:","bold":true},{"text":" value depends "},{"text":"wholly on the guarantor's financial standing","bold":true},{"text":"; suing him is costly and slow; technical defects may free him."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Charge over goods"}],[{"text":"Ownership"}],[{"text":"Possession"}],[{"text":"Note"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Lien"}],[{"text":"borrower"}],[{"text":"lender"}],[{"text":"ordinary lien: retain only; "},{"text":"banker's lien: may sell after proper notice","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Pledge"}],[{"text":"borrower"}],[{"text":"bank (actual or constructive delivery)"}],[{"text":"bailment of goods"}]],[[{"text":"Hypothecation"}],[{"text":"borrower"}],[{"text":"borrower"}],[{"text":"equitable charge; riskiest, because the goods stay in the borrower's possession"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Fixed charge:","bold":true},{"text":" over specific assets (a particular machine or property). "},{"text":"Floating charge:","bold":true},{"text":" over a class of assets (stock, receivables) the company may deal in until "},{"text":"crystallisation","bold":true},{"text":" on default or liquidation. Created by a "},{"text":"debenture","bold":true},{"text":" and registered with the "},{"text":"Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC)","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Constructive delivery:","bold":true},{"text":" the bank's padlock on the warehouse door holding a trader's rice, key kept by the bank, is a "},{"text":"pledge","bold":true},{"text":" although the rice never moves."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Hypothecation suits raw materials a business must keep using; the bank should inspect the goods regularly."}]},{"text":[{"text":"In Nigeria, since the "},{"text":"Land Use Act 1978","bold":true},{"text":", land in each state is vested in the state Governor and held under a "},{"text":"right of occupancy","bold":true},{"text":" (evidenced by a certificate of occupancy, usually for up to 99 years), so the leasehold point above (value falls as the unexpired term shortens) applies to Nigerian titles."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Procedure for taking security","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Savings account:","bold":true},{"text":" letter of authority to transfer; surrender the "},{"text":"passbook","bold":true},{"text":"; a signed blank withdrawal form (no date or amount). A certificate of occupancy is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" needed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Current account:","bold":true},{"text":" letter of authority; signed blank cheque."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fixed deposit:","bold":true},{"text":" letter of authority; surrender the receipt or certificate; renew and endorse it if it expires first."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Life policy:","bold":true},{"text":" check the insurer, beneficiary, surrender value and premiums; complete a "},{"text":"deed of assignment","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"give notice to the insurance company","bold":true},{"text":", which acknowledges it."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Stocks and shares:","bold":true},{"text":" customer signs a "},{"text":"memorandum of deposit","bold":true},{"text":" (rights including disposal on default); transfer by deed at the stock exchange (the traditional procedure; quoted shares are now held electronically in the Central Securities Clearing System, CSCS, where the bank's interest is recorded)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Land:","bold":true},{"text":" deposit the title deed or "},{"text":"certificate of occupancy","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"search at the land registry (ministry of lands)","bold":true},{"text":" to verify authenticity and ownership; physical inspection; then a legal mortgage (Governor's consent under the Land Use Act)."}]}]}]},{"number":"12.5","title":"Procedure, facilities, agreements, bad debts and recovery","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Individual applicant:","bold":true},{"text":" current account; application; "},{"text":"statement of affairs","bold":true},{"text":"; securities; agreement."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Corporate applicant","bold":true},{"text":" adds: audited accounts and tax clearance for 3 years; Memorandum and Articles; "},{"text":"certificate of incorporation","bold":true},{"text":"; cash-flow projection."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Ways banks extend credit:","bold":true},{"text":" loans, overdrafts, discounting of bills of exchange (a "},{"text":"standing order","bold":true},{"text":" is a payment service, not credit)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Overdraft:","bold":true},{"text":" an arrangement allowing a current account holder to withdraw more than his balance up to an agreed limit. "},{"text":"Conditions:","bold":true},{"text":" current account holder; evidence of honesty; account activity for at least "},{"text":"six months","bold":true},{"text":"; express approval of the manager."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Point"}],[{"text":"Loan"}],[{"text":"Overdraft"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Account"}],[{"text":"separate loan account"}],[{"text":"runs on the current account"}]],[[{"text":"Interest"}],[{"text":"on the whole amount granted"}],[{"text":"only on the amount actually overdrawn, daily"}]],[[{"text":"Term"}],[{"text":"specific purpose, fixed repayment; ends at maturity"}],[{"text":"short-term, repayable "},{"text":"on demand","bold":true},{"text":", renewable"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Loan account entries:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"debit","bold":true},{"text":" a new loan account, "},{"text":"credit","bold":true},{"text":" the current account. Repayment and interest: "},{"text":"debit","bold":true},{"text":" current account, "},{"text":"credit","bold":true},{"text":" loan account."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Terms of loan agreements (5):","bold":true},{"text":" prohibition of further charges; bank approval of major management changes; rendering of financial returns; restriction of benefits to shareholders (dividends after repayments); insurance cover on mortgaged property kept in good repair. A share of the borrower's profits is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a usual term."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Monitoring (3):","bold":true},{"text":" regular demand for records; "},{"text":"physical inspection","bold":true},{"text":" of the project; watching the account for hard-core borrowing and missed reductions."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bad debt:","bold":true},{"text":" a debt whose full or partial recovery is considered not possible."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Causes of bad debts (10):","bold":true},{"text":" lack of in-depth knowledge of the customer's operations; excessive dependence on the balance sheet; "},{"text":"connected lending","bold":true},{"text":" (loans to relations or related businesses without the basic principles); lending on "},{"text":"overvalued securities","bold":true},{"text":"; inadequate project monitoring; lack of appraisal skills; manipulation of account records; borrowers' misconception of loans as windfalls; "},{"text":"natural hazards","bold":true},{"text":" (drought, flood, war: worst for "},{"text":"agricultural loans","bold":true},{"text":"); changes in economic policies."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Recovery:","bold":true},{"text":" amicable methods first (restructuring, rescheduling, negotiated settlement), which "},{"text":"preserve the relationship and avoid costly litigation","bold":true},{"text":"; then demand letters, writ of summons, garnishee, winding-up or bankruptcy; "},{"text":"realisation of security","bold":true},{"text":" (power of sale under a mortgage, a receiver under a debenture, enforcing a pledge or guarantee); reporting defaulters to licensed "},{"text":"credit bureaus","bold":true},{"text":" such as CRC Credit Bureau, which "},{"text":"affects their future access to credit","bold":true},{"text":"; and, for eligible assets, "},{"text":"AMCON","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Amortisation:","bold":true},{"text":" retiring a loan gradually by periodic payments rather than all at maturity. For a specific asset such as a delivery truck, repayments should match the asset's "},{"text":"productive life","bold":true},{"text":" and come from its cash flows."}]}]}]},{"number":"12.6","title":"Loan computations","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Flat (simple) interest = P x R x T (T in years; months / 12; days / 365)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Total repayable = P + interest; equal instalment = total repayable / number of instalments"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Annuity payment = loan / PVIFA, where PVIFA = [1 - (1 + i)^-n] / i; interest each period = rate x opening balance; principal repaid = payment - interest"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Overdraft interest = amount overdrawn x rate x days / 365, summed for each balance"}]},{"text":[{"text":"LTV = loan / value of security x 100; margin = value - loan; margin % = (value - loan) / value x 100"}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"1. Mr Eze's vehicle loan:","bold":true},{"text":" ₦200,000 on 1 January 1996, 2 years, 4 equal half-yearly instalments, 10% flat; current account ₦40,000 credit."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Interest = 200,000 x 0.10 x 2 = ₦40,000; per half-year ₦10,000."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Principal per half-year = 200,000 / 4 = ₦50,000."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Debit each half-year = 50,000 + 10,000 = "},{"text":"₦60,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Current account: 40,000 + 200,000 = 240,000; then 180,000; 120,000; 60,000; "},{"text":"nil","bold":true},{"text":" at 31/12/97."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"2. Flat interest and instalments","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"₦500,000, 3 years, 18%: 500,000 x 0.18 x 3 = "},{"text":"₦270,000","bold":true},{"text":" (not ₦90,000, one year only)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"₦300,000, 9 months, 16%: 300,000 x 0.16 x 9/12 = "},{"text":"₦36,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"₦1,200,000, 12 months, 15%, 12 monthly instalments: interest 180,000; total 1,380,000; / 12 = "},{"text":"₦115,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"₦2,000,000, 2 years, 14%, 8 quarterly instalments: interest 560,000; total 2,560,000; / 8 = "},{"text":"₦320,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"3. Balances","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Mr Uche: ₦800,000 in 4 half-yearly principal instalments of 200,000. After the third: 800,000 - 600,000 = "},{"text":"₦200,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bisi: 150,000 + 600,000 = 750,000 credit. Year 1 debit = 600,000 / 3 + 600,000 x 0.10 = 200,000 + 60,000 = 260,000. Balance = "},{"text":"₦490,000 credit","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"4. Amortisation:","bold":true},{"text":" ₦1,000,000, 10%, 3 equal year-end payments."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"PVIFA = [1 - 1.1^-3] / 0.10 = 2.486852; payment = 1,000,000 / 2.486852 = "},{"text":"₦402,114.80","bold":true},{"text":" (to the nearest kobo)."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Year"}],[{"text":"Opening"}],[{"text":"Interest 10%"}],[{"text":"Principal"}],[{"text":"Closing"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"1"}],[{"text":"1,000,000.00"}],[{"text":"100,000.00"}],[{"text":"302,114.80"}],[{"text":"697,885.20"}]],[[{"text":"2"}],[{"text":"697,885.20"}],[{"text":"69,788.52"}],[{"text":"332,326.28"}],[{"text":"365,558.92"}]],[[{"text":"3"}],[{"text":"365,558.92"}],[{"text":"36,555.89"}],[{"text":"365,558.91"}],[{"text":"0.01 (rounding)"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"First year's principal reduction = 402,114.80 - 100,000 = "},{"text":"₦302,114.80","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"5. Overdraft interest","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Kola: 400,000 x 0.20 x 73/365 = "},{"text":"₦16,000.00","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Femi Stores (limit ₦1m, used ₦600,000): 600,000 x 0.24 x 30/365 = "},{"text":"₦11,835.62","bold":true},{"text":" (on the amount used, never the limit)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Changing balance: 250,000 x 0.18 x 20/365 = 2,465.75; 450,000 x 0.18 x 10/365 = 2,219.18; total = "},{"text":"₦4,684.93","bold":true},{"text":" (rounded to kobo)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"6. LTV and margin","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"₦12m against ₦20m: 12 / 20 x 100 = "},{"text":"60%","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Maximum LTV 70% on ₦15m: maximum loan 10,500,000; margin = 15,000,000 - 10,500,000 = "},{"text":"₦4,500,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"₦1.8m against shares worth ₦2.5m: (2,500,000 - 1,800,000) / 2,500,000 x 100 = "},{"text":"28%","bold":true},{"text":" (LTV is 72%)."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Lending = extending credit to be repaid with interest; loans are risk assets."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mather's principles: safety (paramount), suitability, profitability; objectives add liquidity."}]},{"text":[{"text":"5 Cs: character (first and best security), capacity, capital, collateral (last and least), conditions."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Canons: amount, purpose, duration, repayment, strength of business, security, the borrower."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Legal mortgage vests the legal estate; equitable mortgage is by deposit of deeds. Pledge = bank holds; hypothecation = borrower holds."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loan: separate account, interest on full amount. Overdraft: current account, interest on amount used, repayable on demand."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Flat interest = P x R x T; annuity payment = loan / PVIFA; LTV = loan / value."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"18d4be41-705b-541c-a2ea-ecd36219fe1f","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Ada borrows ₦900,000 for 18 months at 12% per annum flat interest on the original principal, repaying principal and interest in 18 equal monthly instalments. Find the total interest and each instalment."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Bank Lending","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"00f013a5-ed76-555c-b667-452f9a34679e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:13","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","body":"Why bank accounts must be uniform and what SAS 10 covered; the bank's financial statements; every asset and liability item in order; statutory reserve rules under BOFIA 2020; deposit and non-deposit sources of funds and the uses of funds; bank ratios (liquidity, cash reserve, excess reserve, loan-to-deposit); and how a lending banker reads a customer's accounts: liquidity, gearing, profitability and activity ratios, window dressing, break-up value and overtrading.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":13,"mustKnow":[{"text":"Why bank accounts must be uniform and what SAS 10 covered; the bank's financial statements; every asset and liability item in order; statutory reserve rules under BOFIA 2020; deposit and non-deposit sources of funds and the uses of funds; bank ratios (liquidity, cash reserve, excess reserve, loan-to-deposit); and how a lending banker reads a customer's accounts: liquidity, gearing, profitability and activity ratios, window dressing, break-up value and overtrading."}],"lessons":[{"number":"13.1","title":"Bank financial statements and SAS 10","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Why uniform bank accounts matter:","bold":true},{"text":" banks hold the public's savings, provide the means of payment and finance business and trade, so they must keep public confidence. Their statements must be "},{"text":"uniformly presented, reliable, factual and comparable","bold":true},{"text":", so users can judge performance and regulators (CBN, NDIC) can classify and supervise them."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Statement of Accounting Standard (SAS) 10","bold":true},{"text":" (accounting by banks) focused on "},{"text":"3 main areas:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Income recognition","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loss recognition","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Balance sheet classification","bold":true}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"6 reasons a special standard was needed:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Importance of the banking sector to industrial and commercial development."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Allegedly overstated profits","bold":true},{"text":" reported by banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Inconsistent accounting policies","bold":true},{"text":" and reporting practices that made comparison of performance difficult."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The need to sustain public confidence in the banking sector."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Survival problems of troubled banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A probable \"shake-out\" ahead from increased competition."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"2 major financial statements:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"balance sheet","bold":true},{"text":" and the "},{"text":"income statement","bold":true},{"text":". Others: source and application of funds, notes to the accounts, five-year financial summary, statement of value added."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Balance sheet rule:","bold":true},{"text":" group assets and liabilities by "},{"text":"nature","bold":true},{"text":" and list them in "},{"text":"order of liquidity and maturity","bold":true},{"text":", starting with "},{"text":"cash","bold":true},{"text":". It shows the sources of funds and how much has gone into loans, securities and other uses "},{"text":"at one date","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Income statement:","bold":true},{"text":" the cost of acquiring deposits and other funds (interest to depositors, staff, overheads, taxes) against revenue (interest on loans and advances, leases, fees), ending with "},{"text":"net earnings","bold":true},{"text":", part reinvested and part paid as dividends."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Foreign exchange rate determination, staff matters, branch hours and fixing savings rates were "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" SAS 10 areas."}]},{"text":[{"text":"SAS 10 is now history: Nigerian banks have reported under "},{"text":"IFRS","bold":true},{"text":" since 2012, where the balance sheet is called the "},{"text":"statement of financial position","bold":true},{"text":". The ordering and the items you learn here still describe a bank's position."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank versus trading company:","bold":true},{"text":" a trading company's balance sheet is mainly stock and fixed assets funded largely by owners' capital. A bank's is "},{"text":"mainly financial assets","bold":true},{"text":" (cash, bills, loans) "},{"text":"funded largely by deposits","bold":true},{"text":", with fixed assets a "},{"text":"small share","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Off-balance-sheet (contingent) items:","bold":true},{"text":" guarantees (e.g. a performance guarantee for a contractor building a road for a state government), acceptances and letters of credit are "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" assets or liabilities until the customer defaults. They are "},{"text":"disclosed in the notes to the accounts","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"13.2","title":"The asset side of a bank's balance sheet","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Assets, in order of liquidity (most liquid first):"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Cash and short-term funds:","bold":true},{"text":" notes and coins in the strong rooms of all branches, regional offices and head office; the most liquid asset. The bank fixes its cash ratio and keeps vault cash to the barest minimum. "},{"text":"Short-term funds","bold":true},{"text":" = sums due from other banks in Nigeria or abroad, e.g. cheques on other banks credited to customers before clearing. Balances with the CBN and with other banks are also assets."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Short-term investment:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Treasury bills","bold":true},{"text":", maturity within "},{"text":"91 days","bold":true},{"text":" (the shortest tenor; Nigerian Treasury bills are also issued for 182 and 364 days). Held because they are "},{"text":"safe (less risky), highly liquid and can be rediscounted with the CBN before maturity","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Long-term investment:","bold":true},{"text":" longer maturities but solid, e.g. "},{"text":"development stocks and bonds","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bills discounted:","bold":true},{"text":" FGN Treasury bills, special bills, trade bills, bankers' acceptances, commercial papers, less provision for discount."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loans and advances:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"risk assets","bold":true},{"text":"; credit facilities to customers. "},{"text":"About 70% or more of revenue","bold":true},{"text":" comes from them. Except term loans, all are technically "},{"text":"repayable on demand","bold":true},{"text":". They earn "},{"text":"more interest than government securities","bold":true},{"text":" because they are riskier, so banks take "},{"text":"security (collateral)","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Other assets:","bold":true},{"text":" accrued interest, prepayments, "},{"text":"accrued interest receivable","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fixed assets:","bold":true},{"text":" land and buildings, machinery, vehicles, fixtures and fittings, computers and other equipment, shown at "},{"text":"cost less depreciation","bold":true},{"text":" (net value) to give a true and fair view."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Equipment on lease:","bold":true},{"text":" machinery, vehicles and other fixed assets leased out."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Traps: "},{"text":"deposits are never an asset","bold":true},{"text":" of the bank. Treasury bills do "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" earn the highest rate and are "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" secured on land; they mature, so they give no permanent income."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Least liquid items sit at the bottom (fixed assets). Most liquid: cash and short-term funds."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Accrued interest "},{"text":"receivable","bold":true},{"text":" is an asset (money owed to the bank); accrued interest "},{"text":"payable","bold":true},{"text":" to depositors is a liability."}]}]}]},{"number":"13.3","title":"The liability and capital side; statutory reserve","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Deposits:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"largest liability","bold":true},{"text":" of any deposit money bank. Depositors are "},{"text":"creditors","bold":true},{"text":" of the bank who can call for most of their money "},{"text":"on demand or at short notice","bold":true},{"text":". They include personal and business customers and other banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Other liabilities:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"amount due to subsidiary companies"}]},{"text":[{"text":"provision for taxation","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"proposed dividends","bold":true},{"text":" to shareholders"}]},{"text":[{"text":"cash security for letters of credit"}]},{"text":[{"text":"cash security for collection"}]},{"text":[{"text":"remittance deposits"}]},{"text":[{"text":"interest reserves: interest on deposits accrued but not yet paid"}]},{"text":[{"text":"bank drafts and certified cheques","bold":true},{"text":" (issued, not yet paid)"}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Capital:","bold":true},{"text":" owners' contribution. "},{"text":"Paid-up capital","bold":true},{"text":" = the amount actually injected by shareholders. "},{"text":"Authorised capital","bold":true},{"text":" = the "},{"text":"maximum","bold":true},{"text":" shareholders may inject."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Statutory reserves:","bold":true},{"text":" a reserve fund every bank must keep by law, built from "},{"text":"net profit after tax, before any dividend is declared","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Capital reserves:","bold":true},{"text":" not mandatory but a prop for growth."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Retained profits (retained earnings):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"undivided profits","bold":true},{"text":" left in the business "},{"text":"after dividends and all other appropriations","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Statutory reserve transfer (BOFIA 2020, section 15; formerly BOFIA 1991, section 16):","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Reserve fund compared with paid-up share capital"}],[{"text":"Minimum transfer"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Less than paid-up capital"}],[{"text":"30% of net profit after tax"}]],[[{"text":"Equal to or more than paid-up capital"}],[{"text":"15% of net profit after tax"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"No transfer is made until all identifiable and accumulated losses have been made good."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Customer balances:","bold":true},{"text":" Mr Eze's ₦2,000,000 credit balance is a "},{"text":"liability","bold":true},{"text":" (the bank owes him). Mrs Musa's ₦500,000 overdraft is an "},{"text":"asset","bold":true},{"text":" (she owes the bank). Loans, advances and overdrafts are always assets."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example:","bold":true},{"text":" a bank earns net profit after tax of ₦10bn."}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Reserve fund below paid-up capital: 30% × ₦10bn = "},{"text":"₦3bn","bold":true},{"text":" to transfer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Reserve fund already equal to or above paid-up capital: 15% × ₦10bn = "},{"text":"₦1.5bn","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Watch the word "},{"text":"nominal","bold":true},{"text":": in this course it is used for the amount already invested by owners, though in company law nominal capital usually means authorised capital."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Not","bold":true},{"text":" liabilities: loans and advances, Treasury bills, bills discounted, accrued interest receivable."}]}]}]},{"number":"13.4","title":"Sources and uses of a bank's funds","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Deposit funds (4 types):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Current account:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"non-interest-bearing demand deposits","bold":true},{"text":", withdrawn at any time by cheque without notice, as long as the balance covers it."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Savings account:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"lowest interest rate","bold":true},{"text":" offered; a "},{"text":"minimal amount","bold":true},{"text":" opens it."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Time or fixed deposit:","bold":true},{"text":" a "},{"text":"fixed maturity (term)","bold":true},{"text":" and a "},{"text":"stipulated interest rate","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Short-term investors:","bold":true},{"text":" investors seeking money market yields."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Non-deposit funds:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Investment income:","bold":true},{"text":" from investments in other banks, government securities, subsidiaries, real estate."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Rental income:","bold":true},{"text":" from bank property let to others."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Borrowing:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"overnight call deposit","bold":true},{"text":" (arranged in minutes but at a "},{"text":"very high interest rate","bold":true},{"text":"), "},{"text":"interbank borrowing","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"long-term debenture","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Owners' capital:","bold":true},{"text":" founders' contribution or shares sold in the capital market."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Retained earnings:","bold":true},{"text":" accumulated net income left after dividends."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Interest income:","bold":true},{"text":" interest and fees on loans (the bulk of revenue), interest on placements and securities."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fees earned:","bold":true},{"text":" trust services, issuing houses work, account maintenance, bank drafts, "},{"text":"letters of credit","bold":true},{"text":", cheque books, excess clearing charges."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Sale of assets:","bold":true},{"text":" real estate, a subsidiary, collateral property. "},{"text":"Extraordinary income","bold":true},{"text":" because it is "},{"text":"usually non-recurring","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Uses of funds:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Repayment of deposits on demand (current, savings, time)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Payment of interest to depositors."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Salaries and wages."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loan repayment (principal and interest to other banks or debenture holders)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Investment in government securities and other approved investments."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Reserves required by regulators."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fees and capital budget: new branches and information technology."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Trap:","bold":true},{"text":" fees earned from letters of credit are a "},{"text":"source","bold":true},{"text":", not a use."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mrs Adeyemi's ₦5,000,000 for 180 days at 12% a year with an early-withdrawal penalty is a "},{"text":"fixed (time) deposit","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Capital history:","bold":true},{"text":" under the 2010 licensing regime a bank needed paid-up capital of ₦10bn (regional), ₦15bn (merchant), ₦25bn (national) or ₦50bn (international). The "},{"text":"2024 recapitalisation","bold":true},{"text":" (deadline "},{"text":"31 March 2026","bold":true},{"text":") raised this to "},{"text":"₦500bn","bold":true},{"text":" (international commercial), "},{"text":"₦200bn","bold":true},{"text":" (national), "},{"text":"₦50bn","bold":true},{"text":" (regional and merchant), "},{"text":"₦20bn","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"₦10bn","bold":true},{"text":" (national and regional non-interest)."}]}]}]},{"number":"13.5","title":"Reading a bank's balance sheet: ratios","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Liquidity ratio = Liquid assets ÷ Total deposit liabilities × 100"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Required liquid assets = Minimum liquidity ratio × Deposits; Shortfall = Required − Held"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Required cash reserve = Cash reserve ratio × Eligible deposits"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Excess reserve = Reserves actually held − Required reserve"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loan-to-deposit ratio = Loans and advances ÷ Total deposits × 100; Maximum loans = Limit × Deposits"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Liquid assets here: cash, balances with the CBN, Treasury bills (and net interbank placements). "},{"text":"Never","bold":true},{"text":" loans or fixed assets. Current CBN position: liquidity ratio "},{"text":"30%","bold":true},{"text":"; cash reserve ratio "},{"text":"45%","bold":true},{"text":" for deposit money banks. Exam questions usually state an assumed figure: use the one given."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Surulere Bank: cash ₦6bn, CBN balances ₦15bn, Treasury bills ₦24bn, loans and advances ₦110bn, fixed assets ₦12bn; deposits ₦150bn."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Liquidity ratio:","bold":true},{"text":" liquid assets = 6 + 15 + 24 = ₦45bn. Ratio = 45 ÷ 150 × 100 = "},{"text":"30%","bold":true},{"text":". (Loans and fixed assets are left out.)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loan-to-deposit ratio:","bold":true},{"text":" 110 ÷ 150 × 100 = "},{"text":"73.33%","bold":true},{"text":" (2 d.p.)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Maximum loans","bold":true},{"text":" under a 65% internal limit: 0.65 × ₦150bn = "},{"text":"₦97.5bn","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Shortfall:","bold":true},{"text":" minimum 30%, liquid assets held ₦40bn. Required = 0.30 × ₦150bn = ₦45bn. Shortfall = 45 − 40 = "},{"text":"₦5bn","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cash reserve and excess:","bold":true},{"text":" assume CRR 20% on ₦150bn: required = 0.20 × 150 = ₦30bn. If the bank holds ₦33bn with the CBN, excess reserve = 33 − 30 = "},{"text":"₦3bn","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Traps: liquidity ratio divides by "},{"text":"deposits","bold":true},{"text":", not total assets; excess reserve is held minus required, not the required amount itself."}]}]},{"number":"13.6","title":"Interpreting a customer's accounts","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Purpose:","bold":true},{"text":" the banker analyses the customer's balance sheet and profit and loss account (usually for three years) to judge whether the business "},{"text":"can repay the advance and interest from its operations and assets","bold":true},{"text":", not to audit, set prices or compute tax."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Question"}],[{"text":"Ratios"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Can it meet short-term debts? (liquidity)"}],[{"text":"Current ratio, quick ratio, working capital"}]],[[{"text":"How much is borrowed? (gearing, solvency)"}],[{"text":"Gearing, debt-to-equity, interest cover"}]],[[{"text":"Is it profitable?"}],[{"text":"Net profit margin, ROCE"}]],[[{"text":"How efficiently does it use assets? (activity)"}],[{"text":"Stock days, stock turnover, debtor days"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Formulas:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Current ratio = Current assets ÷ Current liabilities"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Quick (acid-test) ratio = (Current assets − Stock) ÷ Current liabilities"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Working capital = Current assets − Current liabilities"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Gearing = Long-term debt ÷ (Long-term debt + Shareholders' funds) × 100"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Debt-to-equity = Long-term debt ÷ Shareholders' equity"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Interest cover = Profit before interest and tax ÷ Interest (times)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Net profit margin = Net profit ÷ Sales × 100"}]},{"text":[{"text":"ROCE = Profit before interest and tax ÷ Capital employed × 100; Capital employed = Shareholders' funds + Long-term loans"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Stock days = Stock ÷ Cost of sales × 365"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Stock turnover = Cost of sales ÷ Average stock (times)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Debtor collection period = Trade debtors ÷ Credit sales × 365"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Shareholders' funds = Share capital + Reserves (including retained profits)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Key ideas:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Stock","bold":true},{"text":" is left out of the quick ratio because it is the "},{"text":"least liquid","bold":true},{"text":" current asset and may not turn into cash quickly at book value."}]},{"text":[{"text":"High gearing","bold":true},{"text":" = heavy reliance on borrowed funds relative to owners' funds: more risk to a new lender."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Break-up (forced-sale) basis:","bold":true},{"text":" a prudent banker values assets at what they would fetch if the business failed, not on a going-concern basis."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Tangible net worth","bold":true},{"text":" = Shareholders' funds − Intangibles; "},{"text":"goodwill is deducted","bold":true},{"text":" because it has little or no value if the business is wound up."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Window dressing:","bold":true},{"text":" arranging transactions just before the balance sheet date so the accounts look stronger than normal."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Overtrading:","bold":true},{"text":" sales growing faster than the working capital to support them: working capital falls, creditors and overdraft rise sharply, current ratio falls below 1."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Limitations of the balance sheet:","bold":true},{"text":" historical (possibly out-of-date) figures; position at "},{"text":"one date only","bold":true},{"text":"; open to window dressing. (Listing assets and liabilities is its purpose, not a limitation.)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cash flow forecast:","bold":true},{"text":" loans are repaid out of "},{"text":"cash","bold":true},{"text":"; a profitable business can still fail if it runs out of cash."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Ikeja Traders Ltd: stock ₦3,000,000, debtors ₦2,000,000, cash ₦1,000,000; trade creditors ₦2,500,000, overdraft ₦1,500,000; long-term loan ₦8,000,000; share capital ₦10,000,000, retained profits ₦6,000,000, goodwill ₦1,000,000; sales (all on credit) ₦36,500,000, cost of sales ₦21,900,000, net profit ₦2,920,000; profit before interest and tax ₦4,800,000, interest ₦800,000."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Current assets = 3,000,000 + 2,000,000 + 1,000,000 = ₦6,000,000. Current liabilities = 2,500,000 + 1,500,000 = ₦4,000,000 (the overdraft is a current liability)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Current ratio","bold":true},{"text":" = 6,000,000 ÷ 4,000,000 = "},{"text":"1.5 : 1","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Quick ratio","bold":true},{"text":" = (6,000,000 − 3,000,000) ÷ 4,000,000 = "},{"text":"0.75 : 1","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Working capital","bold":true},{"text":" = 6,000,000 − 4,000,000 = "},{"text":"₦2,000,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Shareholders' funds = 10,000,000 + 6,000,000 = ₦16,000,000. "},{"text":"Gearing","bold":true},{"text":" = 8,000,000 ÷ (8,000,000 + 16,000,000) × 100 = "},{"text":"33.3%","bold":true},{"text":" (1 d.p.). "},{"text":"Debt-to-equity","bold":true},{"text":" = 8,000,000 ÷ 16,000,000 = "},{"text":"0.5 : 1","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Interest cover","bold":true},{"text":" = 4,800,000 ÷ 800,000 = "},{"text":"6 times","bold":true},{"text":". If you are given profit "},{"text":"before tax","bold":true},{"text":" after interest (say ₦4,000,000), add the interest back first: 4,000,000 + 800,000 = 4,800,000, then divide."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Net profit margin","bold":true},{"text":" = 2,920,000 ÷ 36,500,000 × 100 = "},{"text":"8%","bold":true},{"text":". If given gross profit and expenses (sales ₦50m, gross profit ₦15m, expenses ₦11m): net profit = 15 − 11 = ₦4m; margin = 4 ÷ 50 × 100 = "},{"text":"8%","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"ROCE","bold":true},{"text":" = 4,800,000 ÷ (16,000,000 + 8,000,000) × 100 = "},{"text":"20%","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Stock days","bold":true},{"text":" = 3,000,000 ÷ 21,900,000 × 365 = "},{"text":"50 days","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"Stock turnover","bold":true},{"text":" = 21,900,000 ÷ 3,000,000 = "},{"text":"7.3 times","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Debtor days","bold":true},{"text":" = 2,000,000 ÷ 36,500,000 × 365 = "},{"text":"20 days","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Tangible net worth","bold":true},{"text":" = 16,000,000 − 1,000,000 = "},{"text":"₦15,000,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Working backwards:","bold":true},{"text":" current ratio 1.5 : 1 with current liabilities ₦4,000,000 gives current assets = 1.5 × 4,000,000 = ₦6,000,000, so working capital = ₦2,000,000."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Reading the signs:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Current ratio 2.4 : 1 but quick ratio 0.4 : 1: most current assets are "},{"text":"tied up in stock","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Debtor days rising from 35 to 80 with sales unchanged: "},{"text":"credit control is weakening","bold":true},{"text":", some debts may go bad, cash is squeezed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bisi Trading borrows ₦10m from a director on 30 December to clear its overdraft and redraws the overdraft on 3 January to repay him: "},{"text":"window dressing","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Sales double, working capital falls, creditors and overdraft jump, current ratio below 1: "},{"text":"overtrading","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Traps: use "},{"text":"cost of sales","bold":true},{"text":" (not sales) for stock days and stock turnover; use "},{"text":"credit sales","bold":true},{"text":" for debtor days; gearing divides by debt "},{"text":"plus","bold":true},{"text":" equity, debt-to-equity by equity alone."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"SAS 10 covered income recognition, loss recognition and balance sheet classification; reasons include overstated profits and inconsistent policies. Banks now use IFRS."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank assets run in order of liquidity: cash and short-term funds, Treasury bills, long-term investments, bills discounted, loans and advances, other assets, fixed assets, equipment on lease."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loans and advances are the risk assets earning about 70% or more of revenue; Treasury bills are safe, liquid and rediscountable."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Deposits are the largest liability; overdrafts and loans are assets; guarantees are off-balance-sheet."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Statutory reserve (BOFIA 2020): 30% of net profit after tax if the reserve is below paid-up capital, 15% if equal or above, before any dividend."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Deposit funds: current, savings, fixed, short-term investors. Non-deposit: investment and rental income, borrowing, capital, retained earnings, interest, fees, sale of assets (extraordinary)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liquidity ratio = liquid assets ÷ deposits; excess reserve = held − required; LDR = loans ÷ deposits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Customer analysis: current, quick, working capital, gearing, interest cover, margin, ROCE, stock and debtor days."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Prudent bankers value on a break-up basis, deduct goodwill, watch for window dressing and overtrading, and check cash flow."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"f637abb5-ecf7-5f70-8423-69c4aab9d5c0","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Oshodi Ventures Ltd asks for an overdraft. Current assets ₦9,000,000 (including stock ₦4,000,000); current liabilities ₦6,000,000; long-term loan ₦5,000,000; shareholders' funds ₦15,000,000; profit before interest and tax ₦3,000,000; loan interest ₦500,000. Compute the current ratio, quick ratio, working capital, gearing and interest cover, and comment."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"1b7ae81f-9a5d-5cef-9085-5e49ce5db46c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:14","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management 'Problem' of Banks","body":"The three goals (liquidity, profitability, solvency) and why they conflict; reasons for liquidity and for profitability; priorities in allocating funds; the four liquidity theories; asset, liability and asset-liability management and ALCO; liquidity ratio, cash reserve ratio, excess reserves and loans-to-deposits computations; maturity mismatch and gap analysis; capital adequacy, the Basel accords and CAMEL.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":14,"mustKnow":[{"text":"The three goals (liquidity, profitability, solvency) and why they conflict; reasons for liquidity and for profitability; priorities in allocating funds; the four liquidity theories; asset, liability and asset-liability management and ALCO; liquidity ratio, cash reserve ratio, excess reserves and loans-to-deposits computations; maturity mismatch and gap analysis; capital adequacy, the Basel accords and CAMEL."}],"lessons":[{"number":"14.1","title":"The three goals and the 'problem'","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"3 major goals of a deposit money bank (DMB):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"liquidity, profitability and solvency","bold":true},{"text":" (not longevity or endurability)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liquidity:","bold":true},{"text":" the conversion of current assets into cash within a short period of time without appreciable loss of value. In banking: the ability of a bank to meet its customers' demands for cash and its other obligations as they fall due (to pay depositors on demand)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Profitability:","bold":true},{"text":" earning a reasonable margin of income (interest on loans and investments) over costs (interest on deposits and other expenses)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Solvency:","bold":true},{"text":" the ability to meet all obligations in the long run, with assets exceeding liabilities. To be solvent a bank must be both liquid and profitable."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The 'problem':","bold":true},{"text":" balancing the competing goals of liquidity, profitability and solvency. Loans earn interest but tie up cash; idle cash keeps the bank liquid but earns no income. "},{"text":"The more liquid a bank is, the less profitable it tends to be.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cash","bold":true},{"text":" (notes and coins) is the "},{"text":"most liquid asset","bold":true},{"text":" but earns "},{"text":"no income","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"4 reasons a bank keeps adequate liquidity:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"To meet the cash demands (withdrawals) of customers; current account holders need give no notice."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To maintain the unshaken confidence of the public."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To meet the statutory liquidity requirements of the CBN (liquidity ratio, cash ratio)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To meet cash expenses, such as petty cash expenses."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"6 reasons a bank pursues profitability:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"To satisfy the interest of shareholders (dividends)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Evidence of strength and progress (radiates public confidence)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To meet the obligations of lenders and creditors."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Index of performance and stability."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Source of capital: retained (ploughed-back) profit strengthens the bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Buffer for unexpected losses."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Paying dividends is a reason for "},{"text":"profitability","bold":true},{"text":", not liquidity; maximising interest by lending out as much as possible is the opposite of a liquidity reason. Exams swap these."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The main cost of holding more cash than needed is the "},{"text":"opportunity cost","bold":true},{"text":": the interest or investment income the bank could have earned (from other banks or government securities). There is no CBN penalty for excess cash."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Solvent but illiquid:","bold":true},{"text":" assets comfortably exceed liabilities, but they are mostly long-term loans, so the bank cannot pay all the depositors who come today. Illiquidity can trigger a run that grounds even a solvent bank. "},{"text":"Insolvent:","bold":true},{"text":" liabilities exceed assets."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nigerians have a high propensity to hold cash, so a Nigerian bank cannot neglect liquidity whatever the cost."}]}]}]},{"number":"14.2","title":"The balance sheet and the priorities for funds","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Assets","bold":true},{"text":" (from most to least liquid): cash; balances with the CBN and other banks; money at call and short notice; Treasury bills and other government securities; loans and advances; long-term investments; premises and equipment."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liabilities:","bold":true},{"text":" capital (shareholders' funds: share capital and reserves); deposits (current, savings, fixed); borrowings from the CBN and other banks; balances held for other banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loans and advances:","bold":true},{"text":" the bank's "},{"text":"risk assets","bold":true},{"text":"; they generate most of its revenue and earn more than government securities."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Traditional priorities in allocating a commercial bank's funds","bold":true},{"text":" (in order):"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Primary reserves","bold":true},{"text":" (first claim): cash in vault, balances with the CBN (including the cash reserve requirement), balances with other banks, cheques in course of collection. For liquidity, not income."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Secondary reserves:","bold":true},{"text":" short-term, marketable, interest-earning assets, mainly "},{"text":"Treasury bills","bold":true},{"text":", Treasury certificates and call money. Some income, quickly turned into cash."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loans and advances","bold":true},{"text":" to customers: the main source of profit."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Investments for income:","bold":true},{"text":" longer-term securities bought with what is left."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Moving ₦20 billion from Treasury bills into three-year corporate loans makes a bank "},{"text":"more profitable but less liquid and more exposed to credit risk","bold":true},{"text":". Moving the other way makes it more liquid and less profitable. The rule: higher return comes with lower liquidity and higher risk."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Land and buildings and long-term mortgages are "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" secondary reserves; vault cash is a "},{"text":"primary","bold":true},{"text":" reserve."}]}]}]},{"number":"14.3","title":"Liquidity theories and management approaches","mustKnow":[{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Theory"}],[{"text":"Key idea"}],[{"text":"Weakness"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Commercial loan (real bills) theory","bold":true}],[{"text":"Grant only short-term, self-liquidating loans that finance goods in production or trade and are repaid when the goods are sold. Linked to Adam Smith (1776)."}],[{"text":"Ignores that a stable core of deposits can safely support some longer-term lending; goods may not sell in a slump."}]],[[{"text":"Shiftability theory","bold":true},{"text":" (H. G. Moulton, 1918)"}],[{"text":"Stay liquid by holding assets that can be sold or shifted to other investors or to the central bank before maturity without significant loss (e.g. Treasury bills)."}],[{"text":"In a general crisis all banks sell at once, so assets may not be shiftable without heavy loss."}]],[[{"text":"Anticipated income theory","bold":true},{"text":" (H. V. Prochnow)"}],[{"text":"A loan is liquid if it is repaid from the borrower's expected future income, usually in regular instalments."}],[{"text":"Depends on the borrower's income actually arriving."}]],[[{"text":"Liability management theory","bold":true},{"text":" (1960s)"}],[{"text":"Liquidity comes from the "},{"text":"liability side","bold":true},{"text":": borrow (interbank, certificates of deposit, central bank) when cash is needed."}],[{"text":"Borrowing costs rise and funds may dry up in a crisis."}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Asset management:","bold":true},{"text":" treats deposits and other liabilities as largely given and concentrates on choosing the mix of cash, securities and loans to meet liquidity and profit goals."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liability management:","bold":true},{"text":" actively raising funds (interbank borrowing, certificates of deposit) to meet loan demand or withdrawals instead of selling assets."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Asset-liability management (ALM):","bold":true},{"text":" coordinated management of both sides of the balance sheet so that the mix, maturity and pricing of assets and liabilities together achieve liquidity, profitability and risk targets."}]},{"text":[{"text":"ALCO (Asset and Liability Committee):","bold":true},{"text":" a committee of senior management (executive directors) that sets and monitors balance sheet strategy: liquidity, maturity profile, interest rate gaps and the pricing of loans and deposits. Not a CBN or NDIC body, not a shareholders' committee."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Sources of liquidity at short notice:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Asset side:","bold":true},{"text":" sell or rediscount Treasury bills; let maturing Treasury bills run off; collect maturing loans."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liability side:","bold":true},{"text":" borrow in the interbank market; issue certificates of deposit; borrow from the CBN through its Standing Lending Facility; attract new deposits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Granting a new five-year loan "},{"text":"uses","bold":true},{"text":" liquidity; it is never a source."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A Balogun Market textile trader borrowing ₦3 million for 90 days to buy stock, repaid when the fabric is sold, is the classic "},{"text":"commercial loan","bold":true},{"text":" case."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A civil servant's three-year car loan repaid by monthly salary deductions is the "},{"text":"anticipated income","bold":true},{"text":" case."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A bank that borrows ₦15 billion overnight in the interbank market and issues certificates of deposit, instead of selling its investments, is practising "},{"text":"liability management","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A monthly meeting of executive directors that reviews the deposit maturity profile, sets fixed deposit rates and decides to cut the negative gap is the "},{"text":"ALCO","bold":true},{"text":", not the audit, credit or remuneration committee."}]}]}]},{"number":"14.4","title":"Regulatory liquidity ratios and computations","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Liquidity ratio:","bold":true},{"text":" the minimum percentage of deposit liabilities a DMB must hold in specified liquid assets (cash, balances with the CBN, Treasury bills, net interbank placements). Current minimum for DMBs: "},{"text":"30%","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cash reserve ratio (CRR):","bold":true},{"text":" the percentage of deposits a bank is legally required to keep as cash reserves with the CBN. Since September 2025 (retained at the September 2026 MPC): "},{"text":"45%","bold":true},{"text":" for DMBs, 16% for merchant banks, 75% on non-TSA public sector deposits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loans-to-deposits ratio (LDR):","bold":true},{"text":" CBN minimum "},{"text":"50%","bold":true},{"text":" since April 2024 (was 65%)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Formulas:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Liquidity ratio = Liquid assets ÷ Deposit liabilities × 100"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Required liquid assets = Required ratio × Deposits; shortfall = Required − Actual"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liquid assets switchable to loans = Actual liquid assets − Required liquid assets"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Required reserves = Reserve ratio × Deposits"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Excess reserves = Total reserves − Required reserves"}]},{"text":[{"text":"New loans one bank can safely grant from a fresh deposit = Deposit × (1 − Reserve ratio)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"LDR = Loans and advances ÷ Total deposits × 100"}]}]}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Liquidity ratio.","bold":true},{"text":" Deposits ₦120bn; cash ₦8bn, Treasury bills ₦25bn, net interbank placements ₦5bn. Liquid assets = 8 + 25 + 5 = ₦38bn. Ratio = 38 ÷ 120 × 100 = 31.67% (2 d.p.)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Switchable surplus.","bold":true},{"text":" Required = 30% × 120 = ₦36bn. Surplus = 38 − 36 = ₦2bn can go into new loans."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Shortfall.","bold":true},{"text":" Same bank with liquid assets of only ₦30bn: shortfall = 36 − 30 = ₦6bn."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CRR.","bold":true},{"text":" Assume a 45% CRR on eligible deposits of ₦60bn: reserves = 0.45 × 60 = ₦27bn."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Excess reserves.","bold":true},{"text":" Deposits ₦800m, reserves ₦200m, ratio 20%. Required = 0.20 × 800 = ₦160m. Excess = 200 − 160 = ₦40m."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Single-bank lending.","bold":true},{"text":" Fresh deposit ₦20m, ratio 20%, no prior excess: keep 0.20 × 20 = ₦4m; lend 20 − 4 = ₦16m. Do not multiply by 1 ÷ 0.20; that is the whole system's money multiplier, not one bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Reserve deficiency after a withdrawal.","bold":true},{"text":" Deposits ₦500m, reserves exactly ₦50m (10%). A ₦30m cash withdrawal cuts both: deposits = ₦470m, reserves = ₦20m. Required = 0.10 × 470 = ₦47m. Deficiency = 47 − 20 = ₦27m. Required reserves fall only by 10% of the withdrawal, so a deficiency appears."}]},{"text":[{"text":"LDR.","bold":true},{"text":" Loans ₦330bn, deposits ₦500bn: 330 ÷ 500 × 100 = 66%. Do not divide deposits by loans."}]}]}]},{"number":"14.5","title":"Maturity mismatch and gap analysis","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Maturity mismatch:","bold":true},{"text":" financing long-term loans with deposits that can be withdrawn on demand or at short notice. Creates liquidity risk and interest rate risk."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Rate-sensitive assets (RSA) and liabilities (RSL):","bold":true},{"text":" those that reprice within a given period (e.g. 12 months)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Repricing (funding) gap","bold":true},{"text":" = RSA − RSL."}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Positive gap","bold":true},{"text":" (RSA > RSL): "},{"text":"asset-sensitive","bold":true},{"text":"; net interest income (NII) "},{"text":"rises when rates rise","bold":true},{"text":", falls when rates fall."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Negative gap","bold":true},{"text":" (RSL > RSA): "},{"text":"liability-sensitive","bold":true},{"text":"; NII falls when rates rise, rises when rates fall."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Change in NII = Gap × Change in interest rate.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Expect rates to fall: a "},{"text":"negative","bold":true},{"text":" gap benefits most. Expect rates to rise: a "},{"text":"positive","bold":true},{"text":" gap."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Positive gap.","bold":true},{"text":" RSA ₦450bn, RSL ₦400bn: gap = 450 − 400 = +₦50bn (asset-sensitive). Rates fall 3 percentage points: ΔNII = 50bn × (−0.03) = −₦1.5bn."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Negative gap.","bold":true},{"text":" RSA ₦200bn, RSL ₦260bn: gap = 200 − 260 = −₦60bn (liability-sensitive). Rates rise 2 points: ΔNII = −60bn × 0.02 = −₦1.2bn."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Convert percentage points to decimals (2 points = 0.02). ₦50bn × 0.02 = ₦1bn, not ₦100m or ₦10bn."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mismatch trap:","bold":true},{"text":" 10-year fixed-rate mortgages funded by 90-day fixed deposits. When rates rise sharply, deposit costs reprice upward quickly while mortgage income stays fixed, so the bank suffers."}]}]}]},{"number":"14.6","title":"Capital adequacy, Basel and CAMEL","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Capital adequacy ratio (CAR)","bold":true},{"text":" = Capital ÷ Risk-weighted assets × 100. RWA = sum of (each asset × its risk weight)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Basel Committee on Banking Supervision (BCBS):","bold":true},{"text":" set up by the G10 central banks in Basel, Switzerland, after banking crises of the 1970s; works under the "},{"text":"Bank for International Settlements (BIS)","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Basel I (July 1988):","bold":true},{"text":" capital of at least "},{"text":"8% of risk-weighted assets","bold":true},{"text":"; credit risk only. Risk weights: "},{"text":"0%","bold":true},{"text":" claims on government; "},{"text":"20%","bold":true},{"text":" claims on banks; "},{"text":"50%","bold":true},{"text":" residential mortgages; "},{"text":"100%","bold":true},{"text":" corporate and consumer loans."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Basel II (2004)","bold":true},{"text":", 3 pillars:"}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Minimum capital requirements (credit, market and operational risk)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Supervisory review process."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Market discipline through effective public disclosure."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Deposit insurance is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a pillar."}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Basel III (December 2010, finalised 2017):","bold":true},{"text":" stronger common equity (4.5% of RWA), 2.5% capital conservation buffer, countercyclical buffer, leverage ratio, and two liquidity ratios: "},{"text":"Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR):","bold":true},{"text":" enough high-quality liquid assets to cover net cash outflows over a "},{"text":"30-day stress period","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"Net Stable Funding Ratio (NSFR):","bold":true},{"text":" stable long-term funding for the whole balance sheet."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CAMEL","bold":true},{"text":" (rating used by CBN and NDIC): "},{"text":"C","bold":true},{"text":"apital adequacy, "},{"text":"A","bold":true},{"text":"sset quality, "},{"text":"M","bold":true},{"text":"anagement, "},{"text":"E","bold":true},{"text":"arnings, "},{"text":"L","bold":true},{"text":"iquidity. (CAMELS adds "},{"text":"S","bold":true},{"text":"ensitivity to market risk.)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nigerian CAR minimums: "},{"text":"15%","bold":true},{"text":" for banks with international authorisation, "},{"text":"10%","bold":true},{"text":" for national and regional banks."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"CAR worked.","bold":true},{"text":" Capital ₦18bn. Government securities ₦50bn (0%), interbank claims ₦30bn (20%), residential mortgages ₦40bn (50%), corporate loans ₦100bn (100%)."}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"RWA = 0 + (30 × 0.2) + (40 × 0.5) + (100 × 1) = 0 + 6 + 20 + 100 = ₦126bn."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CAR = 18 ÷ 126 × 100 = 14.29% (2 d.p.)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Against 15%: required capital = 0.15 × 126 = ₦18.9bn; shortfall = ₦0.9bn."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"A bank at exactly its minimum CAR that wants many more corporate loans must "},{"text":"raise more capital or retain more profit","bold":true},{"text":" first. More deposits do not help (they are not capital), and cutting zero-weight government securities does not reduce RWA."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Basel I's weakness: one measure (credit risk capital) using book values, ignoring operational risk; Basel II followed, then the 2007-2008 crisis led to Basel III."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank recapitalisation (₦500bn international, ₦200bn national, ₦50bn regional; deadline 31 March 2026) is about minimum capital, a separate rule from the CAR ratio."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Three goals: liquidity, profitability, solvency; balancing them is the portfolio 'problem'."}]},{"text":[{"text":"More liquid means less profitable; cash is the most liquid asset and earns nothing."}]},{"text":[{"text":"4 liquidity reasons (withdrawals, confidence, CBN rules, cash expenses); 6 profitability reasons (dividends, strength, creditors, performance, capital, buffer)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fund priorities: primary reserves, secondary reserves (Treasury bills), loans and advances, investments for income."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Theories: commercial loan (self-liquidating), shiftability (sell before maturity), anticipated income (instalments), liability management (borrow)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"ALCO sets balance sheet strategy; ALM manages both sides together."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liquidity ratio 30%; CRR 45% for DMBs; LDR 50%; excess reserves = actual − required."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Gap = RSA − RSL; ΔNII = gap × Δrate; positive gap gains when rates rise."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CAR = capital ÷ RWA; Basel I 8% (1988); Basel II three pillars; Basel III LCR (30 days) and NSFR; under BIS."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CAMEL: capital, asset quality, management, earnings, liquidity."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"f20cc198-5ebe-5c43-b2da-99b9171910d2","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Agege Bank has deposit liabilities of ₦300bn and qualifying liquid assets of ₦100bn. The minimum liquidity ratio is 30%. (a) What is its liquidity ratio? (b) What is the largest amount of liquid assets it can switch into loans and still comply? (c) What happens to its liquidity and profitability after the switch?"}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"2e9a2b1f-a7ee-595f-94d3-cb027a593b41","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:15","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","body":"What the CIBN is and how it grew from a 1963 local centre to a chartered body; the laws behind it (Decree No. 12 of 1990, now the CIBN Act, 2007); its objectives and functions, and what it does NOT do; its Governing Council, Registrar and disciplinary system; membership categories, grades and letters; the offence of practising without membership; its link with the Bankers' Committee.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":15,"mustKnow":[{"text":"What the CIBN is and how it grew from a 1963 local centre to a chartered body; the laws behind it (Decree No. 12 of 1990, now the CIBN Act, 2007); its objectives and functions, and what it does NOT do; its Governing Council, Registrar and disciplinary system; membership categories, grades and letters; the offence of practising without membership; its link with the Bankers' Committee."}],"lessons":[{"number":"15.1","title":"What the CIBN is and how it began","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"CIBN:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"professional body for banking practitioners","bold":true},{"text":" in Nigeria. It sets the standards of knowledge and skill for persons entering the banking profession, conducts professional banking examinations and promotes ethics among bankers. It is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a regulator, a deposit insurer or a bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"It was "},{"text":"primarily established by its own members","bold":true},{"text":", then backed by law."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Path of development (learn the order):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"1963:","bold":true},{"text":" bankers in Lagos set up a "},{"text":"local centre of the Institute of Bankers, London","bold":true},{"text":" (the Lagos Local Centre, resolved by 124 bankers on 28 November 1963)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"1973:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"Nigerian Institute of Bankers","bold":true},{"text":" was established; on 15 December "},{"text":"1976","bold":true},{"text":" it was incorporated as a "},{"text":"company limited by guarantee","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"1990:","bold":true},{"text":" it obtained "},{"text":"chartered status","bold":true},{"text":" by "},{"text":"Act (Decree) No. 12 of 1990","bold":true},{"text":" and became the "},{"text":"Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"2007:","bold":true},{"text":" the 1990 law was "},{"text":"repealed and replaced","bold":true},{"text":" by the "},{"text":"Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria Act, 2007","bold":true},{"text":" (signed 11 April 2007). This is the law that governs the CIBN today."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Short form: "},{"text":"Local centre of the Institute of Bankers, London -> Nigerian Institute of Bankers -> CIBN.","bold":true}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Traps: chartered status did "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" come from the CBN Act 1958, the Banking Ordinance 1952 or BOFIA 1991; it came from "},{"text":"Decree No. 12 of 1990","bold":true},{"text":". The current law is the "},{"text":"CIBN Act, 2007","bold":true},{"text":", not the NDIC Act 2023, CAMA 2020 or BOFIA 2020 (BOFIA 2020 governs the licensing and regulation of banks, which is the CBN's job)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Before 1990 the body was the "},{"text":"Nigerian Institute of Bankers","bold":true},{"text":", not the \"Institute of Banking and Finance\", the \"Nigerian Bankers' Association\" or a \"Banking Council\". It was a "},{"text":"company limited by guarantee","bold":true},{"text":", not a public limited company, a cooperative or a CBN corporation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The 1963 body was a "},{"text":"local centre of the London Institute","bold":true},{"text":", not a chapter of the American Bankers Association, a CBN branch or the clearing house."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The Institute's home is "},{"text":"Bankers House, Victoria Island, Lagos","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"15.2","title":"Objectives, functions and what the CIBN does not do","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"3 objectives","bold":true},{"text":" for which the CIBN was set up and backed by the 1990 Act:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Promote banking education","bold":true},{"text":" in Nigeria."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Uphold and ensure the observance of professional ethics and tradition","bold":true},{"text":" in the banking industry."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Promote the general advancement of banking practice","bold":true},{"text":" in its branches, facilitate good rapport between bankers on matters of common interest, keep abreast of the latest developments in banking, and "},{"text":"liaise with government in promoting and regulating the banking industry","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"4 functions under the CIBN Act, 2007 (section 3):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Determine the standards of knowledge and skill","bold":true},{"text":" to be attained by persons seeking to become members of the banking profession."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Maintain registers","bold":true},{"text":" of members and of corporate members."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Conduct professional examinations","bold":true},{"text":" and award certificates."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Promote and ensure the observance of ethical standards and professionalism","bold":true},{"text":" among banking practitioners."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"4 features","bold":true},{"text":" through which it promotes professionalism among its members:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Professional qualifications","bold":true},{"text":" through curriculum-based, rigorous training and skills development (examinations)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"High ethical standards","bold":true},{"text":", integrity and objectivity."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Codes of conduct","bold":true},{"text":" (rules and regulations) designed to "},{"text":"enforce self-regulation and compliance","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A system of recognition for merit:","bold":true},{"text":" honorary awards for distinguished service or outstanding contributions to banking knowledge."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"What the CIBN does NOT do","bold":true},{"text":" (each belongs to another body):"}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Activity"}],[{"text":"Who does it"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Granting banking licences; supervising banks"}],[{"text":"CBN (under BOFIA 2020)"}]],[[{"text":"Issuing currency; fixing the Monetary Policy Rate"}],[{"text":"CBN"}]],[[{"text":"Open market operations; dealing in treasury bills"}],[{"text":"CBN"}]],[[{"text":"Insuring bank deposits"}],[{"text":"NDIC"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"\"NOT a role\" questions: the odd one out is always a "},{"text":"regulatory or central-bank power","bold":true},{"text":" (licensing banks, open market operations, MPR, currency) or "},{"text":"deposit insurance","bold":true},{"text":". Liaising with government on regulation "},{"text":"is","bold":true},{"text":" an objective; actually licensing banks is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"How does the CIBN mainly promote professionalism? Through "},{"text":"rigorous professional examinations and training, codes of conduct, and recognition of distinguished members","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Codes of conduct do not guarantee profits, fix interest rates or replace CBN supervision; their benefit is "},{"text":"self-regulation and compliance","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Example: Chiamaka, a graduate trainee in a Lagos bank, who wants professional banking examinations leading to a recognised qualification registers with the "},{"text":"CIBN","bold":true},{"text":", not the Nigerian Exchange, SEC or NDIC."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bankers' Committee:","bold":true},{"text":" the regular meeting of the CBN with the chief executives of banks, "},{"text":"presided over (chaired) by the Governor of the CBN","bold":true},{"text":". It is the apex of the banking profession. The CIBN and the "},{"text":"Financial Institutions Training Centre (FITC)","bold":true},{"text":" are its \"twin institutions\"; the CBN contributes "},{"text":"50%","bold":true},{"text":" of their budgets. The FITC is a training centre; it does not conduct the professional examinations."}]}]}]},{"number":"15.3","title":"Governance and discipline","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Governing Council:","bold":true},{"text":" responsible for the "},{"text":"administration and general management","bold":true},{"text":" of the Institute's affairs and its policy. Chaired by the "},{"text":"President","bold":true},{"text":". Members include the CBN Governor or his representative, bank chief executives, elected members, past Presidents and government and education representatives; the "},{"text":"Registrar is its Secretary","bold":true},{"text":". Members serve two-year terms."}]},{"text":[{"text":"President:","bold":true},{"text":" a Fellow elected at the annual general meeting for "},{"text":"two years","bold":true},{"text":"; chairs the Council; deputised by the First Vice-President."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Registrar:","bold":true},{"text":" appointed by the Council; the "},{"text":"Chief Executive","bold":true},{"text":" of the Institute; "},{"text":"keeps the registers","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"Secretary to the Governing Council","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Disciplinary system (3 steps):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Investigating Panel:","bold":true},{"text":" conducts the "},{"text":"preliminary investigation","bold":true},{"text":" of an alleged breach of the code of conduct and decides whether the case should go to the Tribunal. It has 5 members."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Disciplinary Tribunal:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"hears the case","bold":true},{"text":" and decides whether the member is guilty of unprofessional conduct. Chaired by the "},{"text":"Second Vice-President","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Council confirmation:","bold":true},{"text":" the Tribunal's direction takes effect only when "},{"text":"confirmed by the Council","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Order: "},{"text":"Investigating Panel -> Disciplinary Tribunal -> confirmation by the Council.","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Sanctions:","bold":true},{"text":" a "},{"text":"reprimand","bold":true},{"text":", or an order to the Registrar to "},{"text":"strike the member's name off the register","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"Striking off is the maximum sanction.","bold":true}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A member may be sanctioned for unprofessional conduct, for a conviction the Tribunal finds incompatible with being a banker, or for registration obtained by fraud."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The member may appeal to the "},{"text":"Federal High Court","bold":true},{"text":" within 28 days. The court is an appeal route, not the first step."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Traps: the Tribunal cannot imprison anyone or revoke a bank's licence (licences are the CBN's). The Bankers' Committee and the CBN board play no part in CIBN discipline. The Registrar (not the President or Governor) is the Chief Executive."}]}]}]},{"number":"15.4","title":"Membership, grades and the law on practising","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"2 broad categories of membership:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"corporate members","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"individual members","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Corporate members:","bold":true},{"text":" institutions such as the "},{"text":"CBN, NDIC, banks","bold":true},{"text":" (including "},{"text":"microfinance banks","bold":true},{"text":", mortgage banks and development banks) and discount houses. Corporate members must get their staff registered."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Individual grades:","bold":true},{"text":" Ordinary, Student, Graduate, "},{"text":"Associate","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"Honorary Senior Member","bold":true},{"text":", Honorary Fellow, "},{"text":"Fellow","bold":true},{"text":". There is "},{"text":"no \"Principal Member\"","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Letters"}],[{"text":"Grade"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"ACIB"}],[{"text":"Associate"}]],[[{"text":"FCIB"}],[{"text":"Fellow"}]],[[{"text":"HCIB"}],[{"text":"Honorary Senior Member"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Chartered banker:","bold":true},{"text":" a person who has "},{"text":"passed the professional examinations and been elected an Associate or Fellow","bold":true},{"text":" of the Institute."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Technical heads:","bold":true},{"text":" nobody may be employed or appointed to "},{"text":"head a technical department of a bank","bold":true},{"text":" (e.g. Treasury) unless he is a "},{"text":"member of the CIBN with valid, current registration","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Offence:","bold":true},{"text":" a non-member who "},{"text":"practises or holds himself out as a professional banker for reward commits an offence","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A microfinance bank can be a "},{"text":"corporate","bold":true},{"text":" member; a retired manager, a new cashier or a banking student would be an "},{"text":"individual","bold":true},{"text":" member, never a corporate one."}]},{"text":[{"text":"ACIB is the Associate (the professional qualification earned by examination); FCIB is the senior Fellow grade; HCIB is honorary, not a \"Holder of a Certificate in Banking\"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Being a chartered banker depends on "},{"text":"examinations plus election","bold":true},{"text":", not on years of service, a directorship or a degree alone."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Penalties: an individual offender faces up to "},{"text":"two years' imprisonment","bold":true},{"text":", a fine of not less than "},{"text":"₦20,000","bold":true},{"text":", or both; a body corporate faces a fine of not less than "},{"text":"₦200,000","bold":true},{"text":". Making false statements to obtain registration is also an offence."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The Registrar may remove a member who has not paid subscriptions for over one year."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"CIBN = professional body for bankers: standards, examinations, ethics. Not a regulator, insurer or bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"History: 1963 local centre of the Institute of Bankers, London -> Nigerian Institute of Bankers (company limited by guarantee, 1976) -> chartered by Decree No. 12 of 1990 -> now the CIBN Act, 2007."}]},{"text":[{"text":"3 objectives: banking education; professional ethics and tradition; advancement of banking practice and liaison with government."}]},{"text":[{"text":"4 Act functions: standards of knowledge and skill; registers; examinations and certificates; ethics and professionalism."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Licensing banks, OMO, MPR, currency = CBN; deposit insurance = NDIC."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Governing Council manages; Registrar = Chief Executive and Council Secretary."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discipline: Investigating Panel -> Disciplinary Tribunal -> Council confirmation; maximum sanction = striking off."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Members: corporate and individual; ACIB Associate, FCIB Fellow, HCIB Honorary Senior Member."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Technical department heads must be registered members; holding out as a banker without membership is an offence."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bankers' Committee is chaired by the CBN Governor."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"b628bd1f-2f6b-5a85-9381-1c61cfc36600","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Discuss the role of the Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria in promoting professionalism in the Nigerian banking sector."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"4be90ce0-943d-57d4-8f48-184bc229b1bc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:2","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"The Development of Money","body":"Barter, its features, advantages and limitations; definitions of money and the schools of thought; the functions and characteristics of money; the types of money and how money evolved from barter to the eNaira; the money stock (M1, M2, M3), near money and the value of money; how banks create credit and what limits it.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":2,"mustKnow":[{"text":"Barter, its features, advantages and limitations; definitions of money and the schools of thought; the functions and characteristics of money; the types of money and how money evolved from barter to the eNaira; the money stock (M1, M2, M3), near money and the value of money; how banks create credit and what limits it."}],"lessons":[{"number":"2.1","title":"Barter and why money came","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Barter (trade by barter):","bold":true},{"text":" the direct exchange of goods and services for other goods and services without the use of money. In short, exchange of commodity for commodity (goods for goods)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Barter economy:","bold":true},{"text":" a simple economic system where production is limited, so surplus output is limited, and trade takes place only by exchanging commodities for commodities. It is an economy where money is not in use. Barter existed before the money economy."}]},{"text":[{"text":"3 basic features of a barter economy:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) a producer produces almost all his needs himself; (2) the level of surplus is limited; (3) a producer directly exchanges his products for other people's products."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Barter vs money economy:","bold":true},{"text":" barter = goods exchanged directly for goods; money economy = goods and services exchanged for money."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Limitations of barter:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Double coincidence of wants","bold":true},{"text":" (the main limitation): each party must have what the other wants "},{"text":"and","bold":true},{"text":" want what the other has, at the same time."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lack of a common measure of value","bold":true},{"text":" (no unit of account): too many relative prices to know; the problem of fixing exchange rates between goods."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Indivisibility","bold":true},{"text":" of goods (a cow cannot be split for a few bags of salt)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Difficulty of storing value","bold":true},{"text":" (no means of storing wealth; goods perish)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Difficulty of deferred payment","bold":true},{"text":" (no standard for debts and credit)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Transport difficulties","bold":true},{"text":" (bulky goods carried long distances in search of a partner)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lack of specialisation","bold":true},{"text":" (everyone a \"jack of all trades, master of none\")."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Waste of time and cost of information","bold":true},{"text":" (failure to expedite transactions)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Waiting cost and storage cost:","bold":true},{"text":" stockpiling output while waiting for a partner creates two fresh costs: the "},{"text":"waiting (opportunity) cost","bold":true},{"text":" of satisfaction forgone, and the "},{"text":"storage cost","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Relative prices under barter:","bold":true},{"text":" with n goods, number of relative prices = n(n − 1) / 2. A money economy needs only one money price per good."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Two major advantages of a money economy over barter:","bold":true},{"text":" money is a good "},{"text":"store of value","bold":true},{"text":" (barter goods are often perishable), and money is "},{"text":"divisible","bold":true},{"text":" into small units, so small purchases are possible."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Advantages of barter (5):","bold":true},{"text":" simplicity and directness; no need for money; flexibility in valuing goods; "},{"text":"freedom from inflation, currency devaluation and over-issue of money","bold":true},{"text":"; promotion of self-sufficiency."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Barter is traced to about 6000 BC in Mesopotamia (modern Iraq)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trap: \"direct exchange of goods for goods\" is what barter "},{"text":"is","bold":true},{"text":", not a drawback; \"inflation from over-issue of currency\" is a problem of money, not of barter."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Double coincidence example: a yam farmer wants cloth, but the only weaver wants fish, not yams. No trade. Indivisibility example: a cattle owner wants salt worth far less than one cow."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (relative prices): with 1,000 goods, 1,000 × 999 / 2 = 499,500, about "},{"text":"500,000","bold":true},{"text":" relative prices. With 50 goods: 50 × 49 / 2 = 1,225."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Modern barter","bold":true},{"text":" survives as "},{"text":"countertrade","bold":true},{"text":" (two countries swap, say, crude oil for machinery with no money changing hands), barter exchanges, and in crises such as Zimbabwe's hyperinflation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Money is a "},{"text":"social invention that evolved gradually","bold":true},{"text":" because barter's shortcomings made a "},{"text":"generally acceptable","bold":true},{"text":" medium of exchange necessary. It was not created by one decree, and it came long before commercial banks."}]}]}]},{"number":"2.2","title":"Meaning and definitions of money","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Money (general definition):","bold":true},{"text":" anything that is generally acceptable as a means of payment for goods and services and in settlement of debts. Put simply, money is a "},{"text":"medium of exchange","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Coulborn:","bold":true},{"text":" \"a means of valuation and of payment, as both the unit of account and the generally acceptable medium of exchange\"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Pierce and Shaw (1974):","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Abstract definition:","bold":true},{"text":" money is a "},{"text":"unit of account","bold":true},{"text":", the common denominator in which the exchange value of all goods and services is expressed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Tangible (concrete) definition:","bold":true},{"text":" money is a medium of exchange whose ownership is capable of changing hands."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"3 modern classifications:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) general, (2) legal, (3) functional."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"3 schools of thought under the general definition:","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"School"}],[{"text":"View"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Currency school"}],[{"text":"Money = legal tender currency (naira and kobo, pounds, dollars)"}]],[[{"text":"Banking school"}],[{"text":"Agrees, but says cheques must count: \"cheque is money and money is cheque\", since most bank transactions use cheques"}]],[[{"text":"Monetarist (Chicago) view, "},{"text":"Milton Friedman","bold":true}],[{"text":"Money is "},{"text":"anything that serves as a temporary abode of purchasing power","bold":true},{"text":" (includes cheques, drafts, promissory notes, bills of exchange)"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Legal definition:","bold":true},{"text":" money is what the law says it is."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Functional definition:","bold":true},{"text":" money is measured by aggregates "},{"text":"M1, M2, M3","bold":true},{"text":" set by each central bank (Lesson 2.5)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Hicks (1967):","bold":true},{"text":" \"money is defined by its functions; anything is money which is used as money; "},{"text":"money is what money does","bold":true},{"text":".\""}]},{"text":[{"text":"Numismatics:","bold":true},{"text":" the study of money, especially coins and paper currency."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A law declaring something legal tender does not on its own make it money; people must actually accept it."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Without money, the "},{"text":"price mechanism","bold":true},{"text":" and the "},{"text":"resource allocation system","bold":true},{"text":" would both be inoperative: in a market economy, money prices signal where resources should move and who gets what."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Money promotes "},{"text":"specialisation and division of labour","bold":true},{"text":", removes the need for a double coincidence of wants, and encourages "},{"text":"savings and investment","bold":true},{"text":". It does the opposite of forcing each household to produce all it needs."}]}]}]},{"number":"2.3","title":"Functions and characteristics of money","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Functions of money","bold":true},{"text":" (Jevons, 1875, gave four; a fifth is often added):"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Medium of exchange:","bold":true},{"text":" the intermediary in trade; removes the double coincidence of wants. The most fundamental function."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Unit of account (measure of value, standard of value, common denominator of value):","bold":true},{"text":" all prices expressed in one unit (the naira), so for each good you know how many units of money it costs, as the metre measures distance."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Store of value (store of wealth):","bold":true},{"text":" holding purchasing power for future use, e.g. saving for old age or a \"rainy day\"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Standard of deferred payment:","bold":true},{"text":" debts contracted now and settled in money later (loans, bonds, credit sales)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Transfer of value:","bold":true},{"text":" moving value from person to person and place to place."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Primary functions:","bold":true},{"text":" medium of exchange and measure of value (unit of account). "},{"text":"Secondary:","bold":true},{"text":" store of value and standard of deferred payment."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Not functions:","bold":true},{"text":" settling disputes, creating employment, earning interest, keeping prices constant at all times."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Characteristics (qualities) of good money (8):","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Quality"}],[{"text":"Meaning"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"General acceptability"}],[{"text":"Accepted by all, by custom or by law. "},{"text":"The most important","bold":true},{"text":"; the one quality near money lacks"}]],[[{"text":"Scarcity"}],[{"text":"Relatively scarce, not as abundant as sand, but not too rare"}]],[[{"text":"Durability"}],[{"text":"Lasts through repeated use; needed for store of value"}]],[[{"text":"Homogeneity (uniformity)"}],[{"text":"Every unit identical: ₦1 in Abuja = ₦1 in Ibadan"}]],[[{"text":"Divisibility"}],[{"text":"Divisible into small units so transactions of any size can be made (₦1 = 100 kobo)"}]],[[{"text":"Portability"}],[{"text":"Easy to carry about"}]],[[{"text":"Recognisability (cognisability)"}],[{"text":"Recognised as genuine at once, without technical aid"}]],[[{"text":"Stability"}],[{"text":"Stable in value over time"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Match the example to the function. Prices of goods quoted in naira = "},{"text":"unit of account","bold":true},{"text":". Repaying a loan in fixed naira over five years, borrowing ₦500,000 now to repay ₦550,000 in two years, buying now and paying in three months, or a bond paying naira interest and principal at set dates = "},{"text":"standard of deferred payment","bold":true},{"text":". Saving for old age = "},{"text":"store of value","bold":true},{"text":". Emeka in Lagos sending ₦30,000 monthly to his parents in Kano = "},{"text":"transfer of value","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"To serve as a "},{"text":"store of value and standard of deferred payment","bold":true},{"text":", money must be "},{"text":"stable in value","bold":true},{"text":". Money is a poor store of value during "},{"text":"inflation","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"Inflation","bold":true},{"text":" = a persistent rise in the general price level over a period; "},{"text":"deflation","bold":true},{"text":" = a persistent fall; "},{"text":"stagflation","bold":true},{"text":" = inflation with stagnant output and high unemployment."}]},{"text":[{"text":"\"Measure of value\" is a function, not a quality. \"Durable money\" is not a type of money. \"Abundance\" is not a quality (money must be scarce)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Money with legal tender status is "},{"text":"legal money","bold":true},{"text":"; money accepted only by custom is "},{"text":"customary money","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"2.4","title":"Types of money and how money evolved","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Order of evolution:","bold":true},{"text":" barter → commodity money → metallic money (coins) → paper money → bank deposit money → electronic and digital money."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Commodity money:","bold":true},{"text":" has intrinsic value of its own apart from its use as money (cowries, salt, cattle, grain, gold, tobacco). It may be used "},{"text":"without a distinguishing mark","bold":true},{"text":" or "},{"text":"with a design or symbol","bold":true},{"text":" (minted coins). Commodity money has largely gone out of use."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Standard (full-bodied) money:","bold":true},{"text":" money whose intrinsic (metal) value "},{"text":"equals","bold":true},{"text":" its face value."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Token money:","bold":true},{"text":" has no commodity value; its value comes entirely from being generally acceptable (paper money, bank deposits). Includes:"}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Representative (convertible) token money:","bold":true},{"text":" paper money convertible into a commodity such as gold "},{"text":"on demand","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Inconvertible token money (fiduciary issue):","bold":true},{"text":" not backed by gold or any commodity; its supply is limited only by "},{"text":"law","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fiduciary issue:","bold":true},{"text":" bank notes not backed by gold but by government securities. "},{"text":"Fiduciary money","bold":true},{"text":" is accepted because of "},{"text":"trust","bold":true},{"text":" in the issuer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fiat money:","bold":true},{"text":" money declared "},{"text":"legal tender","bold":true},{"text":" by government decree; no intrinsic value, not backed by a commodity. \"Fiat\" is Latin for "},{"text":"\"let it be done\"","bold":true},{"text":". Today's naira is fiat money."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Legal tender:","bold":true},{"text":" money that the law says must be accepted in settlement of debts. In Nigeria: "},{"text":"naira notes and coins issued by the CBN","bold":true},{"text":". Cheques, postal orders and treasury bills are not legal tender."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank deposit money:","bold":true},{"text":" deposits withdrawable by cheque or transfer. A cheque is not money itself; it transfers the deposit, which is money."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Limitations of commodity money (5):","bold":true},{"text":" perishability; "},{"text":"variability in quality","bold":true},{"text":" (two cows of one breed, one fat, one thin); transport difficulty; "},{"text":"supply fluctuations","bold":true},{"text":" (a bumper grain harvest crashes grain's value); difficulty in making small transactions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Why gold and silver were preferred (7):","bold":true},{"text":" durability (no rust or decay), uniformity, divisibility, portability (high value for weight), scarcity, intrinsic value, universal acceptability."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Lydia","bold":true},{"text":" (Asia Minor, modern Turkey) minted the first standardised coins about 600 BC from "},{"text":"electrum","bold":true},{"text":", a natural alloy of gold and silver. Before coins, Rome used "},{"text":"aes rude","italic":true},{"text":" (rough bronze), then "},{"text":"aes signatum","italic":true},{"text":" (marked bars)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Debasement:","bold":true},{"text":" a ruler cuts the gold or silver content of coins (e.g. mixing in copper) but keeps the face value, earning a profit called "},{"text":"seigniorage","bold":true},{"text":". Results: inflation, loss of public trust, rejection of debased coins for full-value or foreign coins. It "},{"text":"lowers","bold":true},{"text":", never raises, each coin's purchasing power."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example: a ruler holds 60 kg of silver. Full coins hold 25 g. 60 kg = 60,000 g. Full coins: 60,000 / 25 = 2,400. Debased to 20 g: 60,000 / 20 = 3,000. Extra coins = 3,000 − 2,400 = "},{"text":"600","bold":true},{"text":". (Same method: 120 kg at 20 g gives 6,000 coins; at 15 g gives 8,000; so 2,000 more.)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Paper money:","bold":true},{"text":" began in China (Tang dynasty, about 807 AD; Song dynasty "},{"text":"jiaozi","italic":true},{"text":"). Europe's first banknotes: Stockholms Banco, 1661; Bank of England founded 1694. Early problems: "},{"text":"counterfeiting","bold":true},{"text":" (answered with watermarks, intricate designs, special paper, several signatures), trust, overissue, wear."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Goldsmiths (17th-century England):","bold":true},{"text":" their receipts for gold deposited circulated as "},{"text":"goldsmiths' notes","bold":true},{"text":", forerunners of bank notes. Since depositors did not all collect at once, they issued notes worth more than the gold held: the start of "},{"text":"fractional reserve banking","bold":true},{"text":". Early notes had to be "},{"text":"convertible","bold":true},{"text":" because people accepted them only believing they could get gold or silver on demand. The "},{"text":"Bank Charter Act 1844","bold":true},{"text":" let the Bank of England make a fiduciary issue of £14 million."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Gold standard:","bold":true},{"text":" currencies defined as a fixed weight of gold; notes exchangeable for gold; fixed exchange rates; money supply limited by gold reserves (no free expansion). The link ended in "},{"text":"1971","bold":true},{"text":" when the USA suspended convertibility of the dollar into gold."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Electronic money:","bold":true},{"text":" cards, online banking, mobile money such as "},{"text":"M-Pesa","bold":true},{"text":" in Kenya (promotes "},{"text":"financial inclusion","bold":true},{"text":" of the unbanked)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cryptocurrency (e.g. Bitcoin, 2009):","bold":true},{"text":" decentralised digital currency on "},{"text":"blockchain","bold":true},{"text":", not issued or controlled by any government or central bank; very volatile; "},{"text":"not legal tender","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CBDC:","bold":true},{"text":" electronic central bank money for payments and storing value; legal tender. Nigeria's "},{"text":"eNaira","bold":true},{"text":" was launched on "},{"text":"25 October 2021","bold":true},{"text":", the first CBDC in Africa (the world's first nationwide CBDC was the Bahamas' Sand Dollar, 2020). It has the "},{"text":"same value as the cash naira","bold":true},{"text":" (1:1), "},{"text":"complements, not replaces","bold":true},{"text":", cash, is open to people without bank accounts, and pays "},{"text":"no interest","bold":true},{"text":". Expected benefits: lower cost of printing and handling cash, financial inclusion, better monetary policy data, safer payments, cheaper cross-border trade. Uptake has been low; the CBN now positions it more as payments infrastructure."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Point"}],[{"text":"CBDC (eNaira)"}],[{"text":"Cryptocurrency"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Issuer"}],[{"text":"Central bank"}],[{"text":"Users / private network"}]],[[{"text":"Legal tender"}],[{"text":"Yes"}],[{"text":"No"}]],[[{"text":"Volatility"}],[{"text":"Low"}],[{"text":"High"}]],[[{"text":"Backing"}],[{"text":"State-backed by law"}],[{"text":"Not backed by law"}]]]}]},{"number":"2.5","title":"The money stock, near money and the value of money","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Money supply (money stock, stock of money):","bold":true},{"text":" the total stock of money held by the public in an economy at a point in time: currency (notes and coins) held by the public "},{"text":"plus","bold":true},{"text":" bank deposits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Demand (sight) deposits:","bold":true},{"text":" current account, withdrawable on demand by cheque. "},{"text":"Time deposits:","bold":true},{"text":" fixed period; "},{"text":"savings deposits:","bold":true},{"text":" withdrawal conditions; both earn interest."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Quasi-money (near money) in Nigeria's aggregates:","bold":true},{"text":" time deposits + savings deposits held by the public with banks."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Formulas (Nigeria):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"M1 (narrow money) = currency outside banks + demand deposits"}]},{"text":[{"text":"QM (quasi-money) = savings deposits + time deposits"}]},{"text":[{"text":"M2 (broad money) = M1 + QM = currency + demand + savings + time deposits"}]},{"text":[{"text":"M3 = M2 + deposits with non-bank financial institutions (NBFIs)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Near money (quasi-money):","bold":true},{"text":" financial assets that can be converted into cash quickly with little or no loss, but are "},{"text":"not generally accepted as a medium of exchange","bold":true},{"text":". They store value but must first be converted before spending. Examples: "},{"text":"treasury bills","bold":true},{"text":", treasury certificates, time and savings (fixed) deposits, bills of exchange, bonds, debentures, insurance policies, postal orders, stocks and shares."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Demand deposits on current account are money (part of M1), not near money.","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Money vs near money (6 differences):","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Basis"}],[{"text":"Money"}],[{"text":"Near money"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Legality"}],[{"text":"Legal tender"}],[{"text":"No legal tender status"}]],[[{"text":"Liquidity"}],[{"text":"Spent directly"}],[{"text":"Must be converted first"}]],[[{"text":"Acceptability"}],[{"text":"Generally accepted"}],[{"text":"Not generally accepted"}]],[[{"text":"Yield"}],[{"text":"Cash earns no yield"}],[{"text":"Earns a yield"}]],[[{"text":"Conversion"}],[{"text":"None needed"}],[{"text":"Short delay and some cost"}]],[[{"text":"Safety"}],[{"text":"Cash can be lost or stolen"}],[{"text":"Safer, so often preferred"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Value of money:","bold":true},{"text":" its "},{"text":"purchasing power","bold":true},{"text":", the quantity of goods and services a given amount of money can buy. It varies "},{"text":"inversely","bold":true},{"text":" with the general price level and is measured with a "},{"text":"price index","bold":true},{"text":" (index number method)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Value of money = 1 / P. New value as % of old = (old price index / new price index) × 100."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (M aggregates):","bold":true},{"text":" currency ₦600bn, demand deposits ₦1,400bn, savings and time ₦1,100bn. M1 = 600 + 1,400 = "},{"text":"₦2,000bn","bold":true},{"text":" (savings and time are left out of M1)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (table, ₦ billion):","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Year"}],[{"text":"Currency"}],[{"text":"Demand"}],[{"text":"Savings"}],[{"text":"Time"}],[{"text":"M1"}],[{"text":"QM"}],[{"text":"M2"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"2007"}],[{"text":"500"}],[{"text":"1,000"}],[{"text":"780"}],[{"text":"680"}],[{"text":"1,500"}],[{"text":"1,460"}],[{"text":"2,960"}]],[[{"text":"2008"}],[{"text":"750"}],[{"text":"1,550"}],[{"text":"950"}],[{"text":"800"}],[{"text":"2,300"}],[{"text":"1,750"}],[{"text":"4,050"}]],[[{"text":"2009"}],[{"text":"1,000"}],[{"text":"2,000"}],[{"text":"1,450"}],[{"text":"1,250"}],[{"text":"3,000"}],[{"text":"2,700"}],[{"text":"5,700"}]],[[{"text":"2010"}],[{"text":"1,125"}],[{"text":"2,500"}],[{"text":"1,800"}],[{"text":"1,600"}],[{"text":"3,625"}],[{"text":"3,400"}],[{"text":"7,025"}]],[[{"text":"2011"}],[{"text":"1,125"}],[{"text":"2,500"}],[{"text":"1,800"}],[{"text":"1,600"}],[{"text":"3,625"}],[{"text":"3,400"}],[{"text":"7,025"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Check 2010 M2: 1,125 + 2,500 + 1,800 + 1,600 = ₦7,025bn."}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (M1, M2, M3):","bold":true},{"text":" currency 80, demand 320, savings 300, time 50, NBFI deposits 400 (₦bn). M1 = 80 + 320 = 400. M2 = 400 + 300 + 50 = 750. M3 = 750 + 400 = "},{"text":"₦1,150bn","bold":true},{"text":". Another year: currency 150, demand 400 gives M1 = "},{"text":"₦550bn","bold":true},{"text":"; currency 100, demand 300, savings 280, time 80 gives M2 = "},{"text":"₦760bn","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Friedman's M2 = currency + demand + time deposits; the broadest M3 is linked with "},{"text":"Gurley and Shaw","bold":true},{"text":". M1 shows the medium of exchange role; M2 and M3 add store of value."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Each component of the money stock is money "},{"text":"to the extent that it performs some of the functions of money","bold":true},{"text":". Currency and demand deposits (M1) are the most liquid and most generally accepted, so they are \"most qualified\" to be called money (M1 is sometimes called high-powered money; strictly, high-powered money is currency plus banks' reserves with the central bank)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A "},{"text":"falling share of cash","bold":true},{"text":" in the money stock shows more public confidence in banks "},{"text":"and","bold":true},{"text":" a more sophisticated financial system (in Nigeria's early-1990s bank distress the cash share rose)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (value of money, one index):","bold":true},{"text":" price index rises from 100 to 250. New value = 100 / 250 × 100 = "},{"text":"40%","bold":true},{"text":" of the old value (a 60% fall)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (weighted basket):","bold":true},{"text":" weights gari 0.3, yam 0.3, rice 0.4 (weights sum to 1). First-year prices ₦500, ₦800, ₦1,000: P1 = 0.3×500 + 0.3×800 + 0.4×1,000 = 150 + 240 + 400 = 790. Second-year prices ₦700, ₦1,000, ₦1,300: P2 = 210 + 300 + 520 = 1,030. Value = 790 / 1,030 × 100 = "},{"text":"76.70%","bold":true},{"text":" (to 2 d.p.) of its first-year value. This assumes the income shares stay fixed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Velocity of money","bold":true},{"text":" = times a unit of money changes hands in a period (Fisher: "},{"text":"MV = PQ","bold":true},{"text":"). Demand for money: Cambridge "},{"text":"Md = kPY","bold":true},{"text":"; Keynes's three motives (transactions, precautionary, speculative); Friedman's modern quantity theory."}]}]}]},{"number":"2.6","title":"Credit creation and the supply of money","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Credit creation:","bold":true},{"text":" banks create money (deposits) by granting loans and overdrafts from deposits, keeping only a fraction as reserves (fractional reserve banking)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Assumptions:","bold":true},{"text":" a primary cash deposit starts it; a legal fraction is kept as reserve; the rest (excess reserve) is lent; loans are redeposited in the same or another bank; no cash drain."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Formulas:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Credit (money) multiplier k = 1 / r, where r = cash reserve ratio"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Total deposits created = initial deposit / r = initial deposit × k"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Credit creation multiplier = total deposits created / original deposit"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Total loans = total deposits − initial deposit"}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"higher","bold":true},{"text":" the reserve ratio, the "},{"text":"lower","bold":true},{"text":" the multiplier and the credit created."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Limitations to credit creation:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Size of the legal "},{"text":"cash reserve ratio","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Amount of the initial cash deposit (level of cash reserves)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Leakages","bold":true},{"text":": cash kept outside banks (\"under the pillow\")."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fear of "},{"text":"cash drain","bold":true},{"text":" to other banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lack of acceptable "},{"text":"collateral security","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Actions of the monetary authorities."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Determinants of money supply:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Exogenous","bold":true},{"text":" (set by the central bank): the "},{"text":"minimum cash reserve ratio","bold":true},{"text":", the level of bank reserves (open market operations)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Endogenous","bold":true},{"text":" (from the economy): the public's desire to hold cash rather than deposits, business activity, demand for loans, interest rates."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (10% ratio):","bold":true},{"text":" ₦1,000 deposited."}],"blocks":[{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Round"}],[{"text":"Deposit (₦)"}],[{"text":"Reserve 10% (₦)"}],[{"text":"Loan 90% (₦)"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Original"}],[{"text":"1,000"}],[{"text":"100"}],[{"text":"900"}]],[[{"text":"1st redeposit"}],[{"text":"900"}],[{"text":"90"}],[{"text":"810"}]],[[{"text":"2nd redeposit"}],[{"text":"810"}],[{"text":"81"}],[{"text":"729"}]],[[{"text":"All banks"}],[{"text":"10,000"}],[{"text":"1,000"}],[{"text":"9,000"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"k = 1 / 0.10 = 10; total deposits = 1,000 × 10 = ₦10,000; loans = 10,000 − 1,000 = ₦9,000."}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"At 20%: ₦2,000 / 0.20 = "},{"text":"₦10,000","bold":true},{"text":". At 12.5%: k = 1 / 0.125 = "},{"text":"8","bold":true},{"text":". At 25%: ₦1,000m × 4 = ₦4,000m. At 40%: k = 2.5, so ₦1,000m gives ₦2,500m."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Leakage: if the public also keeps cash equal to 10% of deposits with r = 10%, the deposit multiplier falls to 1 / (0.10 + 0.10) = 5."}]},{"text":[{"text":"More cash deposited by the public "},{"text":"expands","bold":true},{"text":", never limits, credit creation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Raises money supply:","bold":true},{"text":" a cut in the cash reserve ratio; CBN buying securities. "},{"text":"Reduces it:","bold":true},{"text":" a rise in the reserve ratio; CBN "},{"text":"selling","bold":true},{"text":" treasury bills to banks in open market operations; a greater public preference for cash."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Barter = goods for goods; main problem: double coincidence of wants; extra costs: waiting and storage; n goods need n(n − 1)/2 relative prices."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Money = anything generally acceptable in payment. Friedman: temporary abode of purchasing power. Hicks: money is what money does."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Functions: medium of exchange and unit of account (primary); store of value, deferred payment (secondary); transfer of value."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Qualities: acceptability (most important), scarcity, durability, homogeneity, divisibility, portability, recognisability, stability."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Evolution: barter → commodity → coins → paper → deposits → electronic. Fiat = \"let it be done\"; gold link ended 1971; eNaira launched 25 October 2021."}]},{"text":[{"text":"M1 = currency + demand deposits; M2 = M1 + savings + time; M3 = M2 + NBFI deposits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Value of money = purchasing power, inverse to the price level, measured by a price index."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Credit multiplier k = 1/r; total deposits = initial deposit / r."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"e3c86c07-6a9b-5074-9b35-8a144da2c912","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"An economy's banks create total deposits of ₦500 million from an initial deposit of ₦50 million. (a) Find the money multiplier and the reserve ratio. (b) The reserve requirement is then raised by 100% and the initial deposit by 40%. Find the new total deposits."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"The Development of Money","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"f270c43a-54d7-59dd-850f-77a22d2f7bdd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:3","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","body":"The colonial (expatriate) banks and the West African Currency Board; the indigenous banks, the boom and doom and why they failed; Paton, the 1952 Ordinance, the 1958 law, the CBN and the 1969 Decree; indigenisation, development banks and merchant banks; SAP, distress and its cures; the 2004-2005 consolidation, the 2009 crisis, AMCON, universal banking and the current (2024-2026) recapitalisation.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":3,"mustKnow":[{"text":"The colonial (expatriate) banks and the West African Currency Board; the indigenous banks, the boom and doom and why they failed; Paton, the 1952 Ordinance, the 1958 law, the CBN and the 1969 Decree; indigenisation, development banks and merchant banks; SAP, distress and its cures; the 2004-2005 consolidation, the 2009 crisis, AMCON, universal banking and the current (2024-2026) recapitalisation."}],"lessons":[{"number":"3.1","title":"The colonial banks and the West African Currency Board","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"First bank:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"African Banking Corporation (ABC), 1892","bold":true},{"text":", Lagos. It distributed British (Bank of England) currency. It marked the start of commercial banking in Nigeria."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank of British West Africa (BBWA), 1894:","bold":true},{"text":" took over ABC's Lagos business in 1894; first surviving bank; near-monopoly for over 30 years; banker to the colonial government. Today "},{"text":"First Bank of Nigeria","bold":true},{"text":" (name from 1979)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Colonial Bank","bold":true},{"text":" (1916/1917) merged with Barclays, the Anglo-Egyptian Bank and the National Bank of South Africa to form "},{"text":"Barclays Bank DCO","bold":true},{"text":" (1925). Today "},{"text":"Union Bank of Nigeria","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"British and French Bank (for Commerce and Industry), 1949.","bold":true},{"text":" Today "},{"text":"United Bank for Africa (UBA)","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"These "},{"text":"three expatriate banks","bold":true},{"text":" (BBWA, Barclays DCO, British and French) dominated banking before independence. They are called the "},{"text":"first-generation banks","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Old name"}],[{"text":"Present name"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Bank of British West Africa (later Standard Bank)"}],[{"text":"First Bank"}]],[[{"text":"Colonial Bank / Barclays DCO"}],[{"text":"Union Bank"}]],[[{"text":"British and French Bank"}],[{"text":"UBA"}]],[[{"text":"Agbonmagbe Bank"}],[{"text":"Wema Bank"}]],[[{"text":"International Bank for West Africa"}],[{"text":"Afribank (later Mainstreet, now part of Polaris)"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Characteristics of the colonial banks:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Foreign-owned and managed; "},{"text":"registered, headquartered and controlled from London","bold":true},{"text":", so they fell under "},{"text":"London's","bold":true},{"text":" regulatory jurisdiction."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Served colonial government and "},{"text":"British/foreign trade","bold":true},{"text":" (palm oil, cocoa, groundnuts)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discriminatory lending:","bold":true},{"text":" they took African deposits but refused Africans credit."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Conservative lending with heavy collateral."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Branches only in "},{"text":"cities and coastal towns","bold":true},{"text":" where British trade was, not rural credit for peasant farmers."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"West African Currency Board (WACB), 1912:","bold":true},{"text":" issued and distributed currency in the British West African colonies before central banks. The "},{"text":"West African pound","bold":true},{"text":" was fixed at par with sterling and "},{"text":"100% backed by sterling","bold":true},{"text":" reserves. "},{"text":"BBWA was its agent","bold":true},{"text":" for custody and distribution of the currency."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Advantages:","bold":true},{"text":" unified currency accepted by the people; relieved government of currency management; no inflation or foreign exchange crisis; eased acceptance of new currencies at independence."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Criticisms:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) money supply was "},{"text":"tied to the balance of trade","bold":true},{"text":" (export earnings); (2) "},{"text":"no independence or discretion","bold":true},{"text":" for Nigeria's own monetary policy; (3) "},{"text":"hindered the growth of Nigeria's own financial system","bold":true},{"text":" (reserves invested abroad, no local investment); (4) failed to "},{"text":"train Africans","bold":true},{"text":" in monetary management; (5) one currency for colonies gaining independence at different dates."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Convertibility into sterling was a "},{"text":"feature","bold":true},{"text":" of the WACB system, not one of the criticisms listed against it."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trap: in some older questions ABC is dated 1882. The correct year is "},{"text":"1892","bold":true},{"text":"; choose the \"1892, African Banking Corporation\" option."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The 1940 Lagos trader whose loan is refused while a British firm gets credit illustrates "},{"text":"discriminatory lending by the expatriate banks","bold":true},{"text":", not any later CBN rule."}]}]}]},{"number":"3.2","title":"Indigenous banks, the boom and the doom (free banking era)","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Free (laissez-faire) banking era (about 1891-1951):","bold":true},{"text":" anyone could open a bank with "},{"text":"no licence, no minimum capital and no regulation","bold":true},{"text":". It ended in 1952."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Why indigenous banks emerged (2 reasons):","bold":true},{"text":" (1) the "},{"text":"expansion of African businessmen","bold":true},{"text":"; (2) the "},{"text":"discriminatory lending of the expatriate banks","bold":true},{"text":". Nationalism added fuel."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Early indigenous banks:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Industrial and Commercial Bank, 1929","bold":true},{"text":": the first indigenous bank; failed in 1930."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nigerian Mercantile Bank, 1931","bold":true},{"text":": failed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"National Bank of Nigeria, 1933","bold":true},{"text":": first successful indigenous bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Agbonmagbe Bank, 1945","bold":true},{"text":" (founded by Okupe): today "},{"text":"Wema Bank","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"African Continental Bank (ACB), 1947","bold":true},{"text":": associated with Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banking boom and banking doom (1945-1955):","bold":true},{"text":" many banks registered (about 145 banking companies in 1947 by one count), then most collapsed. By end-1954 "},{"text":"only three","bold":true},{"text":" indigenous banks survived: "},{"text":"National Bank, ACB and Agbonmagbe","bold":true},{"text":", all rescued by their "},{"text":"regional governments","bold":true},{"text":". Afribank was "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" one of them."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Causes of the collapse (learn the list):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Inadequate capital (undercapitalisation, weak capital base)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Untrained, inexperienced staff and bad management."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fraud and stealing; insider abuse."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Indiscriminate branch expansion (over-expansion)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Crude accounting and poor record keeping."}]},{"text":[{"text":"No regulatory framework","bold":true},{"text":" or supervisor."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Competition from the stronger foreign banks and the discriminating attitude of the colonial authorities."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lack of government patronage; political and ethnic ties."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The 1952 Ordinance's capital rules, which many could not meet."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"\"NOT a cause\" traps: "},{"text":"excessive CBN regulation","bold":true},{"text":" or "},{"text":"strict CBN cash reserve rules","bold":true},{"text":" (the CBN did not exist yet); "},{"text":"holding too much capital","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"foreign merchants' patronage and encouragement","bold":true},{"text":"; margin loans to stockbrokers or failing ₦25 billion (those are 2000s events). In \"EXCEPT\" items where every option (poor management, aggressive competition, no regulator) is a real cause, choose "},{"text":"none of the above","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"\"All of the above\" is right when the options are weak capital, untrained managers and fraud."}]}]}]},{"number":"3.3","title":"Regulation begins: Paton, 1952, the CBN and the 1969 Decree","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Paton Commission (1948):","bold":true},{"text":" led by "},{"text":"G. D. Paton","bold":true},{"text":"; enquired into banking business in Nigeria and "},{"text":"recommended the form and extent of control","bold":true},{"text":" to introduce."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banking Ordinance 1952:","bold":true},{"text":" Nigeria's "},{"text":"first banking law","bold":true},{"text":", based on the Paton Report, passed because of the "},{"text":"widespread failure of indigenous banks","bold":true},{"text":" in the free banking era. Provisions:"}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A "},{"text":"licence","bold":true},{"text":" (from the Financial Secretary) before banking business."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Minimum paid-up capital","bold":true},{"text":": £12,500 (₦25,000) for banks incorporated in Nigeria; £100,000 (₦200,000) for banks incorporated abroad, so "},{"text":"lower for Nigerian banks","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Reserve fund","bold":true},{"text":" from profits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Examination","bold":true},{"text":" of banks and periodic returns."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Assistance to indigenous banks; "},{"text":"3-year grace period","bold":true},{"text":" to comply."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"It did "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" create deposit insurance (NDIC came in 1988). Its lasting significance: it "},{"text":"ended free banking and set up the principle of government regulation","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"After 1952 many more banks failed","bold":true},{"text":" because (all of these): they could not meet the minimum capital; there was "},{"text":"no supervisory agency","bold":true},{"text":" to give direction; they lacked government patronage."}]},{"text":[{"text":"1958 law","bold":true},{"text":" (Banking Ordinance/Act 1958, with the CBN Act): expatriate capital raised to ₦400,000; indigenous unchanged at ₦25,000; licensing power passed to the Minister of Finance after consulting the CBN."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Road to the CBN:","bold":true},{"text":" Mbadiwe's motion (1952); "},{"text":"Fisher Report (1953)","bold":true},{"text":" said it was too early; IBRD mission (1953); "},{"text":"J. B. Loynes report (1957)","bold":true},{"text":" favoured a central bank. "},{"text":"CBN Act 1958","bold":true},{"text":"; operations began "},{"text":"1 July 1959","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"First Governor:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Roy Pentelow Fenton","bold":true},{"text":" (British, 1958-1963). First Nigerian Governor: "},{"text":"Aliyu Mai-Bornu","bold":true},{"text":" (1963-1967). Current Governor (2026): "},{"text":"Olayemi Cardoso","bold":true},{"text":" (since September 2023)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Early CBN steps:","bold":true},{"text":" own currency (1959); "},{"text":"treasury bills (April 1960)","bold":true},{"text":", which began the money market; "},{"text":"Lagos Bankers' Clearing House (1961)","bold":true},{"text":" to settle cheques drawn on one bank and paid into another; support for the Lagos Stock Exchange (1961)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Significance:","bold":true},{"text":" Nigeria gained "},{"text":"control of its own currency and monetary policy","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banking Decree 1969:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"more comprehensive","bold":true},{"text":"; added to the CBN's control tools; extended monetary control to "},{"text":"most banking institutions other than commercial banks","bold":true},{"text":". Minimum paid-up capital "},{"text":"₦600,000 indigenous, ₦1.5 million expatriate","bold":true},{"text":". Repealed by "},{"text":"BOFID No. 25 of 1991","bold":true},{"text":" (with CBN Decree No. 24 of 1991)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Regulatory milestones in order:","bold":true},{"text":" free banking (to 1951) · Banking Ordinance 1952 · CBN Act 1958 · Banking Decree 1969 · NDIC Decree 1988 · BOFID 1991 · Failed Banks Decree 1994 · CBN Act 2007 · BOFIA 2020 (current)."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"1952 versus 1969: the 1952 Ordinance ended free banking under colonial rule; the 1969 Decree gave the CBN a much wider range of controls."}]},{"text":[{"text":"You may also see the 1952 requirement quoted as ₦50,000 authorised capital. Either way, the point is that many small banks could not meet it."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A tiny Lagos bank closing in 1953 within the grace period is a victim of the "},{"text":"1952 licensing and minimum capital rules","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"3.4","title":"After independence: indigenisation, development and merchant banks","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Indigenisation:","bold":true},{"text":" transferring ownership and control of enterprises, banks included, "},{"text":"from foreigners to Nigerians","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nigerian Enterprises Promotion (Indigenisation) Decree 1972:","bold":true},{"text":" the Federal Government took "},{"text":"40%","bold":true},{"text":" in the "},{"text":"three largest foreign banks","bold":true},{"text":" (First, Union, UBA). The 1976-1977 amendments raised it: banking had to be "},{"text":"at least 60% Nigerian-owned","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Outcomes:","bold":true},{"text":" Nigerians moved into "},{"text":"top management and directorships","bold":true},{"text":"; banking skills grew; new indigenous and state-owned banks were set up."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Development finance institutions:","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Institution"}],[{"text":"Purpose"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Nigerian Industrial Development Bank (NIDB)"}],[{"text":"long-term finance for industrial projects"}]],[[{"text":"Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry (NBCI)"}],[{"text":"lending to Nigerian businessmen, including to buy shares in foreign firms"}]],[[{"text":"Nigerian Agricultural and Cooperative Bank (NACB)"}],[{"text":"agriculture and rural cooperatives"}]],[[{"text":"Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria"}],[{"text":"long-term housing (mortgage) finance"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Merchant banking","bold":true},{"text":" began in "},{"text":"1960","bold":true},{"text":" with "},{"text":"Nigerian Acceptances Limited (NAL)","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"Philip Hill (Nigeria) Limited","bold":true},{"text":"; they merged in "},{"text":"1969","bold":true},{"text":" as "},{"text":"NAL Merchant Bank","bold":true},{"text":", Nigeria's first merchant bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Merchant banks vs commercial banks:","bold":true},{"text":" merchant banks are "},{"text":"wholesale","bold":true},{"text":" (large firms, institutions, medium- to long-term corporate finance, issuing house, leasing, acceptances, few branches); commercial banks are "},{"text":"retail","bold":true},{"text":" (current and savings accounts, short-term loans, wide branches)."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A 10-year loan for a textile factory in the 1970s points to "},{"text":"NIDB","bold":true},{"text":". NIDB and NBCI were merged into the "},{"text":"Bank of Industry","bold":true},{"text":" (2001); NACB became today's "},{"text":"Bank of Agriculture","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The rural banking scheme (1977) and community banks (1990, now microfinance banks) took banking beyond the cities."}]}]}]},{"number":"3.5","title":"SAP deregulation, distress and the response","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Deregulation era (1986 to the mid-1990s):","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP)","bold":true},{"text":" from 1986. Reforms:"}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Deregulation of "},{"text":"interest rates","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Removal of "},{"text":"credit ceilings","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liberalised bank licensing","bold":true},{"text":", so banks proliferated (about 40 commercial and 15 merchant banks before 1986 to about 125 commercial and merchant banks by 1994)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Foreign exchange liberalisation: "},{"text":"Second-tier Foreign Exchange Market (SFEM), 1986","bold":true},{"text":", rate set by market forces."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Privatisation of government stakes."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"SAP did "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" increase Federal Government deposits in banks: in 1989 public sector deposits were "},{"text":"withdrawn","bold":true},{"text":" from banks to the CBN. Raising capital to ₦25 billion is 2004, not SAP."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank distress:","bold":true},{"text":" a bank suffers severe "},{"text":"financial, operational and managerial weakness","bold":true},{"text":" that makes it "},{"text":"unable to meet its obligations to customers, owners and the economy as and when due","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CAMEL (5):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"C","bold":true},{"text":"apital adequacy, "},{"text":"A","bold":true},{"text":"sset quality, "},{"text":"M","bold":true},{"text":"anagement, "},{"text":"E","bold":true},{"text":"arnings, "},{"text":"L","bold":true},{"text":"iquidity. Market share is not one."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Symptoms (5):","bold":true},{"text":" boardroom squabbles; customer complaints of unpaid maturing obligations; "},{"text":"desperate borrowing","bold":true},{"text":" above market rates; window dressing; "},{"text":"persistent adverse clearing position and overdraft with the CBN","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Causes:","bold":true},{"text":" internal: weak management, inadequate capital, fraud and insider abuse, poor asset and liability management. External: "},{"text":"macroeconomic instability","bold":true},{"text":" (inflation, recession), weak legal framework, delayed supervisory action, external shocks and balance of payments problems, political interference."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Responses:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"NDIC (1988)","bold":true},{"text":", from a 1983 CBN committee proposal for a "},{"text":"depositors protection fund","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"Failed Banks (Recovery of Debts) and Financial Malpractices in Banks Decree 1994","bold":true},{"text":": tribunals that recovered debts owed to failed banks "},{"text":"and","bold":true},{"text":" tried their directors; liquidations, the largest being "},{"text":"26 banks on 16 January 1998","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A bank that is a regular "},{"text":"net placer","bold":true},{"text":" of surplus funds in the interbank market is healthy, not distressed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cures for distress: a stable economy, sound regulation, good management, liquidity and funds management, fraud control."}]}]}]},{"number":"3.6","title":"Consolidation, the 2009 crisis and banking today","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Consolidation:","bold":true},{"text":" reducing the number of banks and other deposit-taking institutions while "},{"text":"increasing the size and concentration","bold":true},{"text":" of those that remain."}]},{"text":[{"text":"2004 programme:","bold":true},{"text":" announced by "},{"text":"CBN Governor Charles Soludo","bold":true},{"text":" at a special Bankers' Committee meeting on "},{"text":"6 July 2004","bold":true},{"text":". Minimum capital base rose from "},{"text":"₦2 billion to ₦25 billion","bold":true},{"text":" by "},{"text":"31 December 2005","bold":true},{"text":" (18 months)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Definitions:","bold":true},{"text":" capital base = "},{"text":"paid-up capital and reserves unimpaired by losses","bold":true},{"text":"; paid-up capital = ordinary shares plus non-redeemable preference shares."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Allowed modes:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"only mergers and outright acquisitions","bold":true},{"text":". A mere "},{"text":"group arrangement","bold":true},{"text":" (a holding company keeping separate banks) was "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" accepted."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Incentives:","bold":true},{"text":" authority to deal in "},{"text":"foreign exchange","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"public sector deposits","bold":true},{"text":"; recommendation to collect public revenue; prospect of managing part of the "},{"text":"external reserves","bold":true},{"text":"; tax incentives; lower transaction costs. "},{"text":"Not","bold":true},{"text":" an incentive: exemption from CBN supervision."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Result:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"89 banks became 25","bold":true},{"text":" (75 banks merged into groups); 14 failed and were liquidated. Stanbic and IBTC then merged, leaving 24. "},{"text":"Wema Bank merged with National Bank of Nigeria.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"2009 crisis:","bold":true},{"text":" governor "},{"text":"Sanusi Lamido Sanusi","bold":true},{"text":"; a "},{"text":"joint CBN and NDIC","bold":true},{"text":" special examination. In "},{"text":"August 2009","bold":true},{"text":" the CEOs of "},{"text":"five","bold":true},{"text":" banks were removed: "},{"text":"Afribank, Finbank, Intercontinental, Oceanic, Union","bold":true},{"text":". Then three more: "},{"text":"Bank PHB, Equatorial Trust Bank, Spring Bank","bold":true},{"text":". Causes: "},{"text":"margin loans and oil and gas exposure, weak risk management and corporate governance","bold":true},{"text":", insider abuse. Constant borrowing at the "},{"text":"Expanded Discount Window","bold":true},{"text":" showed the banks were "},{"text":"illiquid","bold":true},{"text":". The CBN injected about ₦620 billion."}]},{"text":[{"text":"AMCON (2010):","bold":true},{"text":" buys "},{"text":"non-performing loans","bold":true},{"text":" from banks, takes over the collateral and helps recapitalise them."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Universal banking (2001):","bold":true},{"text":" one licence for commercial, merchant (investment) and other financial services. "},{"text":"Reviewed in 2010:","bold":true},{"text":" banks had to "},{"text":"divest non-core businesses or use a holding company","bold":true},{"text":", under "},{"text":"commercial, merchant or specialised","bold":true},{"text":" licences."}]},{"text":[{"text":"2010 commercial bank capital:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"regional ₦10 billion, national ₦25 billion, international ₦50 billion","bold":true},{"text":" (merchant ₦15 billion)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Current position:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"BOFIA 2020","bold":true},{"text":" replaced BOFIA 1991; the "},{"text":"CBN Act 2007","bold":true},{"text":" governs the CBN. In "},{"text":"March 2024","bold":true},{"text":" the CBN set new minimum capital: "},{"text":"international ₦500 billion, national ₦200 billion, regional ₦50 billion, merchant ₦50 billion, non-interest national ₦20 billion and regional ₦10 billion","bold":true},{"text":", deadline "},{"text":"31 March 2026","bold":true},{"text":". In April 2026 the CBN confirmed that "},{"text":"33 banks","bold":true},{"text":" had met it."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"The March 2004 review rated 62 banks sound, 14 marginal, 11 unsound, and 2 had not sent returns (62 + 14 + 11 + 2 = 89)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Why consolidate? The 89 banks were small and fragile; one South African bank (ABSA) had more assets than all of them together."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The 2004 governor was "},{"text":"Soludo","bold":true},{"text":", not Sanusi (2009) or Cardoso (2024)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Development of the financial system:","bold":true},{"text":" it grew through rising savings and investment, the establishment of the CBN, and technology such as electronic banking. A return to barter, closing banks or removing regulation would hold it back."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Periods to name:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Pre-independence free banking (before 1959; colonial era 1892-1960)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Regulated banking (1960-1985)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"SAP deregulation (1986 to 1993/95)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Consolidation (July 2004 to December 2005), followed by post-2009 reform and today's digital era."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"ABC 1892 first; BBWA 1894 (First Bank); Colonial Bank/Barclays DCO (Union); British and French 1949 (UBA)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"WACB 1912: sterling-backed West African pound, BBWA as agent; no monetary independence."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Indigenous: ICB 1929 (first), National Bank 1933, Agbonmagbe 1945 (Wema), ACB 1947; only National Bank, ACB and Agbonmagbe survived the 1945-1955 boom and doom."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Paton Report led to the Banking Ordinance 1952: licence, minimum capital, reserves, examination; ended free banking."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CBN Act 1958, operations 1 July 1959, Fenton first governor; Loynes 1957."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banking Decree 1969: ₦600,000 indigenous, ₦1.5 million expatriate; replaced by BOFID 1991, now BOFIA 2020."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Indigenisation 1972 (40%), 1976-1977 (60%); NAL plus Philip Hill gave NAL Merchant Bank (1969)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"SAP 1986: deregulation, SFEM; distress measured by CAMEL; NDIC 1988; 26 banks liquidated in 1998."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Soludo, 6 July 2004: ₦2 billion to ₦25 billion; 89 to 25 banks; mergers and acquisitions only."}]},{"text":[{"text":"2009: five plus three CEOs removed; AMCON 2010; 2024 recapitalisation (₦500 billion international) with a 31 March 2026 deadline."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"b51b3be2-8fb2-5fba-b04b-741e9d970e39","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"In the 2004 consolidation, a bank had a capital base of ₦2 billion. (a) How much extra capital did it need, how many times did the requirement rise, and by what percentage? (b) By what percentage did the number of banks fall when 89 became 25?"}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"9b891e1e-95e3-59f2-a764-32ecca5fcbe0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","body":"What a central bank is and its canons; the WACB and the road to the CBN (1958 Act, 1 July 1959); the CBN Act 2007, Board, Governor, Deputy Governors and MPC; traditional and developmental functions; monetary policy, its objectives and instruments with simple computations; the CBN-led reforms of 2004, 2009, 2010 and 2024.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":4,"mustKnow":[{"text":"What a central bank is and its canons; the WACB and the road to the CBN (1958 Act, 1 July 1959); the CBN Act 2007, Board, Governor, Deputy Governors and MPC; traditional and developmental functions; monetary policy, its objectives and instruments with simple computations; the CBN-led reforms of 2004, 2009, 2010 and 2024."}],"lessons":[{"number":"4.1","title":"Central banking, its canons and the birth of the CBN","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Central bank:","bold":true},{"text":" the apex monetary authority of a country, with the sole right to issue currency and responsibility for regulating the banking and monetary system. It is the government's bank and the bankers' bank; it deals mainly with government and banks, not the public."}]},{"text":[{"text":"In Nigeria the apex monetary authority is the "},{"text":"Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN)","bold":true},{"text":", headquartered in Abuja."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Canons (principles) of central banking:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"It must be the "},{"text":"sole bank of issue","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"It must "},{"text":"not do ordinary commercial banking or compete with commercial banks","bold":true},{"text":" (no profit-seeking deposits from the public); it is a bankers' bank and the government's bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"It controls financial institutions with "},{"text":"monetary tools","bold":true},{"text":" (bank rate, open market operations and others)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"It keeps "},{"text":"some assets in liquid form","bold":true},{"text":" so it can discount short-term commercial and government bills."}]},{"text":[{"text":"It should be "},{"text":"free from government interference","bold":true},{"text":" (independent)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Also accepted as central banking principles: acting as "},{"text":"lender of last resort","bold":true},{"text":" and being responsible for the "},{"text":"stability of the financial system","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"West African Currency Board (WACB):","bold":true},{"text":" set up in "},{"text":"1912","bold":true},{"text":" (after the "},{"text":"Lord Emmott","bold":true},{"text":" committee of 1911) to issue sterling-backed currency for four British colonies: Nigeria, Gold Coast (Ghana), Sierra Leone, The Gambia. Based in London; the "},{"text":"Bank of British West Africa","bold":true},{"text":" was its currency distributor in Nigeria. Before the CBN, the WACB issued Nigeria's legal tender."}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Advantages of the WACB"}],[{"text":"Disadvantages of the WACB"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"A unified currency standard accepted by the people"}],[{"text":"One currency for four colonies caused problems as they became independent at different dates"}]],[[{"text":"Relieved colonial governments of currency management"}],[{"text":"100% sterling backing invested abroad deprived the colonies of earning assets at home"}]],[[{"text":"Removed inflation and foreign exchange crises"}],[{"text":"Failed to train Africans in monetary management"}]],[[{"text":"Paved the way for easy acceptance of new currencies at independence"}],[{"text":"No independent monetary policy: Nigeria's money supply simply moved with its sterling (trade) earnings"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Road to the CBN (the three missions):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"1952:","bold":true},{"text":" K. O. Mbadiwe's motion in the House of Representatives asked for a central bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fisher Report, 1953","bold":true},{"text":" (J. L. Fisher, Bank of England adviser): against a central bank then. \"The financial environment hardly exists in Nigeria for the establishment of a Central Bank.\" Reasons: the financial system was "},{"text":"too underdeveloped/narrow","bold":true},{"text":" for a central bank to control the money supply, and "},{"text":"trained staff were lacking","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"World Bank (IBRD) Mission","bold":true},{"text":" (report 1955): too early for a full central bank; recommended a "},{"text":"State Bank of Nigeria","bold":true},{"text":" that could later become a central bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Loynes Report, 1957","bold":true},{"text":" (J. B. Loynes, Bank of England): "},{"text":"favoured","bold":true},{"text":" a central bank; its recommendations formed the draft bill presented to the House in "},{"text":"March 1958","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"There was "},{"text":"no \"Johnson's Commission 1954\"","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"CBN Act (No. 24) of 1958","bold":true},{"text":" established the CBN. It "},{"text":"commenced operations on 1 July 1959","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"First Governor: "},{"text":"Roy Pentelow Fenton","bold":true},{"text":" (1958-1963). First Nigerian Governor: "},{"text":"Aliyu Mai-Bornu","bold":true},{"text":" (1963-1967), followed by "},{"text":"Clement Isong","bold":true},{"text":" (1967-1975). Current Governor (2026): "},{"text":"Olayemi Cardoso","bold":true},{"text":", appointed 2023."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"By 1956 (self-rule) the government was persuaded a central bank was needed to "},{"text":"harness the financial resources of the economy, give leadership and direction to banks and other financial institutions, and create institutions, instruments and markets","bold":true},{"text":" for the growth of the financial system. If asked which of these, the answer is \"all of the above\"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Watch the dates: "},{"text":"Act 1958, operations 1959","bold":true},{"text":". Options like 1 October 1960 (independence) or 1 July 1958 are traps."}]}]}]},{"number":"4.2","title":"Legal framework, objects and organisational structure","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Laws:","bold":true},{"text":" CBN Act 1958, replaced by CBN Decree No. 24 of 1991, amended 1993, 1997, 1998, 1999, now the "},{"text":"CBN Act 2007","bold":true},{"text":" (in force). The CBN also administers the "},{"text":"Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act (BOFIA) 2020","bold":true},{"text":", which replaced BOFIA 1991."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Principal objects under the CBN Act 2007 (5):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Ensure "},{"text":"monetary and price stability","bold":true},{"text":" (the first and primary object)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Issue "},{"text":"legal tender currency","bold":true},{"text":" in Nigeria."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Maintain "},{"text":"external reserves","bold":true},{"text":" to safeguard the international value of the currency."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Promote a "},{"text":"sound financial system","bold":true},{"text":" in Nigeria."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Act as "},{"text":"banker and provide economic and financial advice to the Federal Government","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Board of Directors (apex of the structure):","bold":true},{"text":" chaired by the "},{"text":"Governor","bold":true},{"text":"; members are the Governor, the "},{"text":"4 Deputy Governors","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"5 non-executive directors","bold":true},{"text":", the "},{"text":"Permanent Secretary of the Federal Ministry of Finance","bold":true},{"text":" and the "},{"text":"Accountant-General of the Federation","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Governor","bold":true},{"text":" (and Deputy Governors): appointed by the "},{"text":"President, subject to Senate confirmation","bold":true},{"text":", for "},{"text":"5 years in the first instance, renewable once","bold":true},{"text":" for not more than 5 years."}]},{"text":[{"text":"4 Deputy Governors (2007 Act):","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Deputy Governor"}],[{"text":"Departments include"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Economic Policy"}],[{"text":"Monetary Policy, Research, Statistics, Financial Markets"}]],[[{"text":"Financial System Stability"}],[{"text":"Banking Supervision","bold":true},{"text":", Other Financial Institutions Supervision"}]],[[{"text":"Operations"}],[{"text":"Banking Services, Currency Operations, Reserve Management, Payments System Management"}]],[[{"text":"Corporate Services"}],[{"text":"Human Resources, Finance"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Monetary Policy Committee (MPC):","bold":true},{"text":" formulates "},{"text":"monetary and credit policy","bold":true},{"text":". Set up formally by the CBN Act 2007."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Members ("},{"text":"12","bold":true},{"text":"): Governor (Chairman) 1 + Deputy Governors 4 + Board members 2 + President's appointees 3 + Governor's appointees 2 = "},{"text":"12","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Must meet "},{"text":"at least four times a year","bold":true},{"text":"; in practice it meets about every two months."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Licensing under BOFIA:","bold":true},{"text":" to carry on banking business you must apply "},{"text":"in writing to the Governor of the CBN","bold":true},{"text":" for a licence. A bank may open or close a branch only with the "},{"text":"prior written consent of the CBN","bold":true},{"text":". Mergers and acquisitions also need the Governor's prior approval."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Why banks are heavily regulated (8):","bold":true},{"text":" protect depositors; protect customers from unfair dealing; promote monetary stability (control cost, volume and availability of credit); win public confidence; keep banks solvent; prevent crises that would damage the economy; safety of deposits; develop an integrated, efficient financial system. Regulation does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" guarantee every bank a profit."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"The 2007 Act gave the CBN greater autonomy."}]},{"text":[{"text":"You may see the MPC described as \"2 CBN appointees and 5 presidential appointees\"; that split is not the Act's, but the total is still 12. The 2007 Act wording is 2 Board members, 3 presidential and 2 Governor's appointees."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The NDIC (1988, operations 1989) shares bank oversight but is a separate body."}]}]}]},{"number":"4.3","title":"Traditional functions of the CBN","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"The CBN's functions fall into three groups: "},{"text":"traditional","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"regulatory (supervisory)","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"developmental","bold":true},{"text":" (Lesson 4.5)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Five major functions under the 1958 Act:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) issue currency notes and coins (legal tender); (2) banker to the government; (3) banker to other banks; (4) manage the country's external reserves; (5) manage domestic monetary policy. Today you also add "},{"text":"regulation and supervision","bold":true},{"text":" of banks and other financial institutions, and "},{"text":"financial adviser to government","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Issue of legal tender:","bold":true},{"text":" sole issuer of the naira. The CBN "},{"text":"issues","bold":true},{"text":" currency; "},{"text":"Nigerian Security Printing and Minting Plc prints and mints","bold":true},{"text":" it."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banker to government:","bold":true},{"text":" keeps federal, state and local government accounts; "},{"text":"manages the public debt","bold":true},{"text":" (issues Treasury Bills, Treasury Certificates, development stocks); grants "},{"text":"Ways and Means advances","bold":true},{"text":" (temporary direct advances to the FG to cover revenue shortfalls); runs the "},{"text":"Treasury Single Account (TSA)","bold":true},{"text":": all MDAs close separate bank accounts and pay receipts into one consolidated account at the CBN."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banker to banks:","bold":true},{"text":" keeps banks' current/reserve accounts, provides "},{"text":"clearing","bold":true},{"text":" facilities, rediscounts their bills, and is "},{"text":"lender of last resort","bold":true},{"text":": emergency funds to a "},{"text":"solvent but temporarily illiquid","bold":true},{"text":" bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"External reserves:","bold":true},{"text":" holds gold and convertible currencies to safeguard the naira's international value."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Monetary stability and a sound financial system:","bold":true},{"text":" monetary policy plus licensing, examination (on-site and off-site) and enforcement."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Things the CBN does NOT do:","bold":true},{"text":" accept deposits from or lend to individuals, households or businessmen; insure deposits or pay depositors of failed banks ("},{"text":"NDIC","bold":true},{"text":"); register stockbrokers or list shares ("},{"text":"SEC","bold":true},{"text":" and the Exchange); register companies ("},{"text":"CAC","bold":true},{"text":"); collect taxes; examine and qualify bankers ("},{"text":"CIBN","bold":true},{"text":"). A central bank is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a merchant bank."}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Central bank"}],[{"text":"Commercial bank"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"One per country; apex of the system"}],[{"text":"Many in one country"}]],[[{"text":"Monopoly of currency issue"}],[{"text":"Cannot issue currency"}]],[[{"text":"Not profit-driven"}],[{"text":"Main objective is profit"}]],[[{"text":"Deals with government and banks"}],[{"text":"Deals with the public"}]],[[{"text":"Controls","bold":true},{"text":" credit"}],[{"text":"Creates","bold":true},{"text":" credit"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Currency history:","bold":true},{"text":" Nigerian pound issued "},{"text":"1959","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"1 January 1973","bold":true},{"text":" decimalisation: the "},{"text":"naira and kobo","bold":true},{"text":", at "},{"text":"₦2 = £1","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"₦20 note 1977","bold":true},{"text":" honouring "},{"text":"General Murtala Mohammed","bold":true},{"text":"; ₦1,000 note 2005."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Reserves history:","bold":true},{"text":" sterling backing received from the WACB in 1959; from "},{"text":"1973","bold":true},{"text":" held in as many traded currencies as possible (not only US dollars and pounds) to "},{"text":"earn new income and reduce losses","bold":true},{"text":" from a fall in any one currency."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Domiciliary accounts:","bold":true},{"text":" since deregulation, exporters and others may keep foreign exchange in domiciliary accounts with banks. Two types: "},{"text":"Export Domiciliary Account","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"Ordinary Domiciliary Account","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Ways and Means limit:","bold":true},{"text":" the CBN Act 2007 caps outstanding advances at "},{"text":"5% of the FG's previous year's actual revenue","bold":true},{"text":" (the 1991 Decree said 12.5% of estimated recurrent revenue). Example: previous year's revenue ₦10 trillion; limit = 5% × ₦10,000 billion = ₦500 billion. Heavy deficit financing by the CBN pumps money into the economy and weakens its control of prices: this is "},{"text":"fiscal dominance","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"4.4","title":"Monetary policy and its instruments","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Monetary policy:","bold":true},{"text":" the use of the central bank's control over the "},{"text":"quantity, availability and cost of money and credit","bold":true},{"text":" to achieve general economic objectives. Not fiscal policy, which uses "},{"text":"taxation, government expenditure and public debt","bold":true},{"text":" (Federal Ministry of Finance)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Monetary and fiscal policy are both "},{"text":"economic stabilisation policies","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Four objectives:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) "},{"text":"stable prices","bold":true},{"text":", (2) "},{"text":"high growth rate of output","bold":true},{"text":", (3) "},{"text":"full employment","bold":true},{"text":", (4) "},{"text":"balance of payments equilibrium","bold":true},{"text":". Secondary: stable exchange rate, income redistribution. Population growth and bank profits are "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" objectives. In practice the CBN's primary focus is "},{"text":"price stability","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Stable prices:","bold":true},{"text":" the general price level avoids wide fluctuations and changes only gradually (not frozen prices)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Full employment:","bold":true},{"text":" all able and willing to work find jobs; some frictional and seasonal unemployment remains."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Types:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"expansionary","bold":true},{"text":" (more money and credit; for recession) and "},{"text":"contractionary","bold":true},{"text":" (less; for inflation)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Instruments","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Quantitative (general, traditional)"}],[{"text":"Qualitative (selective)"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Open market operations (OMO)"}],[{"text":"Moral suasion"}]],[[{"text":"Discount rate / bank rate (now MPR)"}],[{"text":"Selective credit control"}]],[[{"text":"Reserve requirements (cash reserve ratio, liquidity ratio)"}],[{"text":"Margin requirement"}]],[[{"text":"Special deposits"}],[{"text":"Loan (credit) ceiling"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"OMO:","bold":true},{"text":" buying and selling government (or CBN) securities to change bank reserves and money supply. "},{"text":"Sell","bold":true},{"text":" = withdraw money (contract). "},{"text":"Buy","bold":true},{"text":" = inject money (expand). Main "},{"text":"indirect, market-based","bold":true},{"text":" tool since "},{"text":"June 1993","bold":true},{"text":". Uses Nigerian Treasury Bills, bonds and "},{"text":"CBN Bills","bold":true},{"text":" (issued by the CBN itself to mop up liquidity)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Repo:","bold":true},{"text":" CBN sells and agrees to buy back = "},{"text":"temporarily withdraws","bold":true},{"text":" liquidity. "},{"text":"Reverse repo:","bold":true},{"text":" temporarily injects."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discount rate = bank rate:","bold":true},{"text":" the rate at which the central bank lends to banks or rediscounts their eligible bills. Lower it to expand credit; raise it to contract. In Nigeria the "},{"text":"Minimum Rediscount Rate (MRR)","bold":true},{"text":" was replaced in "},{"text":"December 2006","bold":true},{"text":" by the "},{"text":"Monetary Policy Rate (MPR)","bold":true},{"text":", the signalling rate."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Standing facilities corridor:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"SLF","bold":true},{"text":" (Standing Lending Facility) = overnight borrowing from the CBN against collateral, "},{"text":"above","bold":true},{"text":" MPR. "},{"text":"SDF","bold":true},{"text":" (Standing Deposit Facility) = overnight deposit with the CBN, "},{"text":"below","bold":true},{"text":" MPR. Formulas: SLF rate = MPR + upper band; SDF rate = MPR − lower band (100 basis points = 1%)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cash reserve ratio (CRR):","bold":true},{"text":" minimum % of deposits a bank must keep "},{"text":"with the CBN","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"Liquidity ratio:","bold":true},{"text":" minimum % of deposit liabilities held in "},{"text":"liquid assets","bold":true},{"text":" (cash in vault, balances with the CBN, Treasury Bills). Raise either: excess reserves fall, lending and money supply contract."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Simple deposit multiplier = 1 ÷ reserve ratio.","bold":true},{"text":" Maximum lendable from new deposits = deposits × (1 − CRR). Liquidity shortfall = (liquidity ratio × deposits) − liquid assets held."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Special deposits:","bold":true},{"text":" cash kept with the central bank "},{"text":"over and above","bold":true},{"text":" required reserves, "},{"text":"not counted","bold":true},{"text":" toward them."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Moral suasion:","bold":true},{"text":" persuasion or friendly appeal to banks to act in the national interest, "},{"text":"without legal compulsion","bold":true},{"text":". It is the classic "},{"text":"non-market","bold":true},{"text":" instrument."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Selective credit control:","bold":true},{"text":" administrative guidelines directing credit to sectors in set amounts at set rates (pre-"},{"text":"1986 SAP","bold":true},{"text":", to agriculture and manufacturing)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Margin requirement:","bold":true},{"text":" fixes how much security is needed for advances, to stop lending against commodities hoarded for speculation. "},{"text":"Loan ceiling:","bold":true},{"text":" total advances may not rise above the level reached on a date the CBN names."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Direct controls of bank liquidity:","bold":true},{"text":" supplementary reserves, variable cash ratio, variable liquidity ratio."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Current position (September 2026 MPC):","bold":true},{"text":" MPR "},{"text":"23%","bold":true},{"text":"; corridor "},{"text":"+50/−300","bold":true},{"text":" basis points; CRR "},{"text":"45%","bold":true},{"text":" for deposit money banks, "},{"text":"16%","bold":true},{"text":" merchant banks, "},{"text":"75%","bold":true},{"text":" on non-TSA public sector deposits; liquidity ratio "},{"text":"30%","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Interest rates and spending move oppositely: "},{"text":"higher rates lead to lower spending; lower rates to higher spending","bold":true},{"text":". To pump money in, the CBN "},{"text":"reduces","bold":true},{"text":" the discount rate or MPR, "},{"text":"buys","bold":true},{"text":" securities, or "},{"text":"cuts","bold":true},{"text":" reserve ratios. To fight inflation it "},{"text":"sells","bold":true},{"text":" securities (OMO), "},{"text":"raises","bold":true},{"text":" MPR or reserve ratios, uses selective credit control; a "},{"text":"surplus budget","bold":true},{"text":" is a fiscal anti-inflation tool. "},{"text":"Cutting the MPR is not","bold":true},{"text":" an anti-inflation tool. Taxation and budgeting are fiscal, not monetary."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Reserve requirements change the "},{"text":"multiplier","bold":true},{"text":"; OMO changes the "},{"text":"monetary base","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Direct controls (1959-1986):","bold":true},{"text":" credit ceilings, sectoral allocation, fixed interest rates. "},{"text":"Market-based era","bold":true},{"text":" from SAP (1986), OMO from 1993."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 1 (multiplier):","bold":true},{"text":" CRR 10%: multiplier = 1 ÷ 0.10 = 10, so ₦10,000 of new cash supports ₦10,000 × 10 = ₦100,000 of deposits. If the CRR rises to 20%: multiplier = 1 ÷ 0.20 = 5, supporting ₦10,000 × 5 = ₦50,000. Raising the CRR from 10% to 20% cuts the multiplier from 10 to 5."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 2 (CRR):","bold":true},{"text":" CRR 40%, new deposits ₦250 billion. Required reserve = 40% × ₦250bn = ₦100bn. Maximum lending = ₦250bn − ₦100bn = "},{"text":"₦150bn","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 3 (liquidity ratio):","bold":true},{"text":" Ekiti Trust Bank: deposits ₦600bn, liquid assets ₦150bn, liquidity ratio 30%. Required = 30% × ₦600bn = ₦180bn. Shortfall = ₦180bn − ₦150bn = "},{"text":"₦30bn","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 4 (corridor):","bold":true},{"text":" MPR 20%, corridor +500/−100 bps. SLF = 20% + 5% = "},{"text":"25%","bold":true},{"text":"; SDF = 20% − 1% = "},{"text":"19%","bold":true},{"text":". September 2026: SLF = 23% + 0.5% = 23.5%; SDF = 23% − 3% = 20%."}]}]},{"number":"4.5","title":"Developmental functions of the CBN","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Developmental functions:","bold":true},{"text":" roles beyond the traditional ones, suited to a developing economy, to build the financial system and finance key sectors. Example: special financing schemes for agriculture and industry."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Three major developmental functions:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Provision of financial infrastructure:","bold":true},{"text":" creating "},{"text":"markets and instruments","bold":true},{"text":": Treasury Bills (1960), call money fund scheme (1962), Treasury Certificates (1968), certificates of deposit, bankers' acceptances, discount houses (1993); encouraged the "},{"text":"Lagos Stock Exchange (1961)","bold":true},{"text":"; helped found NIDB, the NDIC and SEC."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Provision of special funds and schemes","bold":true},{"text":" (see below)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Provision of information and training:","bold":true},{"text":" publications ("},{"text":"Statistical Bulletin, Annual Report, CBN Briefs, Economic and Financial Review","bold":true},{"text":", Bullion), seminars, surveys and the "},{"text":"Monetary Policy Forum","bold":true},{"text":" (from 2000); manpower development."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Special funds and schemes:","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Scheme"}],[{"text":"Key facts"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Rural Banking Scheme","bold":true},{"text":" (1977, 4 phases to 1993)"}],[{"text":"Commercial banks "},{"text":"directed to open branches in rural areas","bold":true},{"text":" to mobilise rural savings"}]],[[{"text":"Agricultural Credit Guarantee Scheme Fund (ACGSF)","bold":true},{"text":" (1977)"}],[{"text":"Guarantees "},{"text":"75% of the net amount in default","bold":true},{"text":" on agricultural loans; share capital subscribed 60% Federal Government, 40% CBN"}]],[[{"text":"NERFUND","bold":true},{"text":" (National Economic "},{"text":"Reconstruction","bold":true},{"text":" Fund)"}],[{"text":"Medium/long-term funds, partly in foreign exchange; merged into Bank of Industry (2001)"}]],[[{"text":"SMIEIS","bold":true},{"text":" (Small and Medium Industries Equity Investment Scheme)"}],[{"text":"CBN initiative via the "},{"text":"Bankers' Committee","bold":true},{"text":": banks set aside "},{"text":"10% of profit before tax","bold":true},{"text":" for equity in small and medium industries"}]],[[{"text":"Rediscounting and Refinancing Facility (RRF)","bold":true},{"text":" (1987)"}],[{"text":"Lets banks rediscount or refinance "},{"text":"export","bold":true},{"text":" credit"}]],[[{"text":"Foreign Input Facility (FIF)","bold":true},{"text":" (1989)"}],[{"text":"Helps producers get foreign exchange to "},{"text":"import capital goods and raw materials","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Anchor Borrowers' Programme","bold":true},{"text":" (2015) and others (Real Sector Support Facility, MSMEDF)"}],[{"text":"Development finance interventions for farmers and the real sector"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"RRF and FIF passed to "},{"text":"NEXIM","bold":true},{"text":" in 1991. There is "},{"text":"no","bold":true},{"text":" \"National Economic Rejuvenation Fund\"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Community banking (1990)","bold":true},{"text":" replaced rural banking and later became "},{"text":"microfinance banking","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The CBN helped set up development banks: NIDB 1964 (now Bank of Industry), NACB 1973 (now Bank of Agriculture), Federal Mortgage Bank 1977."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Manpower:","bold":true},{"text":" the CBN chairs the Bankers' Committee and supports the "},{"text":"CIBN","bold":true},{"text":" and the "},{"text":"Financial Institutions Training Centre (FITC)","bold":true},{"text":", seconds staff and runs its own training programmes."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Payments:","bold":true},{"text":" cashless policy (2012); "},{"text":"Bank Verification Number (BVN)","bold":true},{"text":", launched with the Bankers' Committee in "},{"text":"February 2014","bold":true},{"text":", gives each customer one biometric identity across all banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Since 2023-2024 the CBN under Cardoso has scaled back its intervention lending to concentrate on price stability."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Paying insured depositors of failed banks is the "},{"text":"NDIC's","bold":true},{"text":" work, not a CBN achievement."}]}]}]},{"number":"4.6","title":"CBN-led banking reforms","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Consolidation:","bold":true},{"text":" a reduction in the number of banks, with the remaining banks becoming "},{"text":"larger and more concentrated","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"2004-2005 consolidation:","bold":true},{"text":" announced "},{"text":"6 July 2004","bold":true},{"text":" by Governor "},{"text":"Charles Soludo","bold":true},{"text":" to the Bankers' Committee. Minimum capital raised from "},{"text":"₦2 billion to ₦25 billion","bold":true},{"text":", deadline "},{"text":"31 December 2005","bold":true},{"text":" (18 months). Reason: banks were "},{"text":"small, fragile, poorly developed, with a weak capital base","bold":true},{"text":" compared with their expected role. Allowed modes: "},{"text":"mergers and outright acquisitions/takeovers","bold":true},{"text":" (plus fresh capital); a "},{"text":"mere group arrangement was not acceptable","bold":true},{"text":". Result: "},{"text":"89 banks fell to 25","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"14","bold":true},{"text":" failed and were liquidated."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"For consolidation"}],[{"text":"Against consolidation"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Revenue and cost efficiency gains"}],[{"text":"More risk-taking through leverage and off-balance-sheet activity"}]],[[{"text":"Removes weak banks"}],[{"text":"Very large banks are complex and costly to manage"}]],[[{"text":"Better diversification of risk"}],[{"text":"Scale economies are not unlimited"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"2009 crisis:","bold":true},{"text":" caused by "},{"text":"heavy lending concentrated in the capital market (margin loans) and oil and gas","bold":true},{"text":", plus "},{"text":"weak risk management and corporate governance","bold":true},{"text":". A "},{"text":"joint CBN-NDIC special examination","bold":true},{"text":" (Governor "},{"text":"Sanusi Lamido Sanusi","bold":true},{"text":", August 2009) found "},{"text":"very high non-performing loans","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"illiquid","bold":true},{"text":" banks depending on CBN support (Expanded Discount Window) and interbank borrowing. Five, then three more, banks had their managements removed and received CBN capital injections; "},{"text":"AMCON","bold":true},{"text":" (2010) bought their bad loans."}]},{"text":[{"text":"2010 licensing regime","bold":true},{"text":" (universal banking ended): commercial bank with "},{"text":"international","bold":true},{"text":" authorisation "},{"text":"₦50 billion","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"national","bold":true},{"text":" ₦25 billion; "},{"text":"regional","bold":true},{"text":" ₦10 billion; merchant bank ₦15 billion."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Current (2024 recapitalisation, deadline 31 March 2026):","bold":true},{"text":" international commercial "},{"text":"₦500 billion","bold":true},{"text":"; national ₦200 billion; regional ₦50 billion; merchant ₦50 billion; non-interest national ₦20 billion, regional ₦10 billion."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Before 2004, existing banks needed ₦1 billion and new banks ₦2 billion; one South African bank (ABSA) had more assets than all 89 Nigerian banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The five banks first examined in 2009 (Afribank, Finbank, Intercontinental, Oceanic, Union) had liquidity ratios "},{"text":"below","bold":true},{"text":" the 25% minimum then."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Central bank = apex monetary authority; sole issuer; bankers' bank; government's bank; does not compete with commercial banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"WACB 1912; Fisher 1953 (against), World Bank 1955 (State Bank), Loynes 1957 (for); CBN Act 1958; operations 1 July 1959."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CBN Act 2007: first object is monetary and price stability; Board chaired by the Governor; 4 Deputy Governors; Governor appointed by the President with Senate confirmation for 5 years, renewable once."}]},{"text":[{"text":"MPC: 12 members, meets at least 4 times a year; MPR replaced MRR (2006)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Quantitative tools: OMO, bank rate, reserve requirements, special deposits; qualitative: moral suasion, selective credit control, margin requirement, loan ceiling."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Sell securities or raise CRR to contract; buy securities or cut rates to expand. Multiplier = 1 ÷ reserve ratio."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Developmental: infrastructure, special funds and schemes (ACGSF 75%, SMIEIS 10% of PBT, RRF exports, FIF imports, rural banking), information and training."}]},{"text":[{"text":"2004: ₦25 billion, 89 to 25 banks; 2010: international ₦50 billion; 2024: international ₦500 billion by 31 March 2026."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"04394be6-b73b-50c1-b2b9-761e7cb01a71","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Lekki Bank has deposit liabilities of ₦800 billion. The CRR is 45% and the liquidity ratio 30%. (a) How much must it keep with the CBN as cash reserve? (b) What minimum liquid assets must it hold? (c) If the CBN cuts the CRR to 40%, how much is released for lending?"}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"9adc1855-7b73-5002-9fb1-8ed211865a89","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:5","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","body":"The parts of the financial system and how intermediaries turn savings into investment; commercial, merchant and non-interest banks, the licence regime and every recapitalisation; the development finance institutions and their mergers; Peoples Bank, community banks and microfinance banks; the NDIC and distress resolution; the other regulators and non-bank institutions; simple interest, real rate, NHF and NDIC computations.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":5,"mustKnow":[{"text":"The parts of the financial system and how intermediaries turn savings into investment; commercial, merchant and non-interest banks, the licence regime and every recapitalisation; the development finance institutions and their mergers; Peoples Bank, community banks and microfinance banks; the NDIC and distress resolution; the other regulators and non-bank institutions; simple interest, real rate, NHF and NDIC computations."}],"lessons":[{"number":"5.1","title":"The financial system, savings and investment","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"3 basic types of financial institutions:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) "},{"text":"bank financial institutions (BFIs)","bold":true},{"text":": deposit money banks and specialised banks; (2) "},{"text":"non-bank financial institutions (NBFIs)","bold":true},{"text":": insurance companies, pension funds, finance houses, unit trusts, mutual funds, savings and loans; (3) "},{"text":"regulatory and supervisory institutions","bold":true},{"text":". BFI liabilities (demand deposits) are spendable as money; NBFI liabilities are not."}]},{"text":[{"text":"4 components of the system:","bold":true},{"text":" institutions, markets (money and capital), instruments, regulators."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Formal sector:","bold":true},{"text":" licensed and regulated (banks, insurers, pension funds, capital market operators). "},{"text":"Informal sector:","bold":true},{"text":" unlicensed and unsupervised: "},{"text":"esusu, ajo, isusu","bold":true},{"text":" (rotating contributions), moneylenders, daily savings collectors."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Financial intermediation:","bold":true},{"text":" channelling funds from "},{"text":"surplus units","bold":true},{"text":" (savers) to "},{"text":"deficit units","bold":true},{"text":" (investors). Intermediaries promote the savings-investment process and so "},{"text":"capital formation","bold":true},{"text":" (acquiring capital assets to raise productivity) and growth."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Why intermediaries beat direct lending:","bold":true},{"text":" they pool small savings into bulky sums; spread risk (lower risk per saver, so lower cost of funds); give longer terms ("},{"text":"maturity transformation","bold":true},{"text":": short-term deposits funding long-term loans); assess borrowers expertly; cut transaction costs; give savers liquidity and income. Direct sourcing means high cost, short term and high risk."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Factors for successful intermediation:","bold":true},{"text":" trust and confidence; convenience; price (interest and fees); nearness to market. Loss of confidence causes "},{"text":"disintermediation","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"3 goals of a DMB:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"profitability, liquidity, solvency.","bold":true},{"text":" Loans earn most but reduce liquidity; cash is liquid but earns nothing."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Savings products:","bold":true},{"text":" savings account, target savings, fixed (time) deposits, digital savings. "},{"text":"Channels of bank investment:","bold":true},{"text":" direct lending, securities (Treasury Bills, bonds), equity (within limits), trade finance."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Challenges to saving:","bold":true},{"text":" low income, inflation, financial exclusion, distrust after bank failures, competing assets (property, dollars, crypto)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Formulas:","bold":true},{"text":" Simple interest = P × R × T (T in years). Amount = P + interest. Real interest rate ≈ nominal rate − inflation rate."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Twenty Oshodi traders each paying ₦5,000 weekly, one collecting the pool in turn, with no licence or regulator: "},{"text":"informal","bold":true},{"text":" sector."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A bank using thousands of withdrawable savings to fund a ten-year cement-factory loan shows "},{"text":"maturity transformation","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Fixed deposit:","bold":true},{"text":" ₦1,000,000 for 3 months at 12% simple. Interest = 1,000,000 × 0.12 × 3/12 = ₦30,000. Amount paid = 1,000,000 + 30,000 = "},{"text":"₦1,030,000","bold":true},{"text":". (₦30,000 alone is the interest, not the payment.)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Savings:","bold":true},{"text":" Chidinma's ₦800,000 at 6% for 9 months: 800,000 × 0.06 × 9/12 = "},{"text":"₦36,000","bold":true},{"text":" interest."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Real rate:","bold":true},{"text":" savings pay 12%, inflation is 21%: 12 − 21 = "},{"text":"−9%","bold":true},{"text":". The saver loses purchasing power; this is why inflation discourages saving."}]}]}]},{"number":"5.2","title":"Deposit money banks: commercial, merchant, non-interest","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Deposit money banks (DMBs)","bold":true},{"text":" = "},{"text":"commercial banks and merchant banks","bold":true},{"text":". Nigeria runs "},{"text":"branch banking","bold":true},{"text":": one bank, many offices nationwide, one serving as head office."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Commercial bank functions:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) accept deposits: current (demand), savings, fixed/time; (2) grant advances: loans, overdrafts, bill discounting; ancillary: agency (payments, remittances), executor and trustee, "},{"text":"safe-keeping of wills, jewellery and documents","bold":true},{"text":", payment services, foreign exchange."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Merchant bank:","bold":true},{"text":" engaged in wholesale banking, medium- and long-term finance, equipment leasing, debt factoring, investment management, issue and acceptance of bills, unit trusts. Started 1960 (Philip Hill Nigeria and Nigerian Acceptances Ltd, merged 1969 as NAL Merchant Bank)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Merchant bank services:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"banking:","italic":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"acceptances","bold":true},{"text":" (the "},{"text":"traditional","bold":true},{"text":" function that marks them out: accepting bills of exchange to finance the trade of others), loans and advances including "},{"text":"bridging loans","bold":true},{"text":", deposits (fixed/call, current for corporates, "},{"text":"NCDs","bold":true},{"text":"), "},{"text":"equipment leasing","bold":true},{"text":", foreign exchange (letters of credit). "},{"text":"Corporate finance:","italic":true},{"text":" project financing and loan syndication, "},{"text":"issuing house","bold":true},{"text":", investment and financial advisory (mergers, acquisitions)."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Feature"}],[{"text":"Merchant bank"}],[{"text":"Commercial bank"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Type"}],[{"text":"Wholesale banker"}],[{"text":"Retail banker"}]],[[{"text":"Customers"}],[{"text":"Firms and institutions, large sums"}],[{"text":"Everyone: individuals, households, firms, government"}]],[[{"text":"Deposits"}],[{"text":"Mainly fixed"}],[{"text":"Demand, savings and fixed"}]],[[{"text":"Branches"}],[{"text":"Few, urban"}],[{"text":"Many, urban and rural"}]],[[{"text":"Clearing house"}],[{"text":"Traditionally not members"}],[{"text":"Members"}]],[[{"text":"Loans"}],[{"text":"Bulkier, medium term"}],[{"text":"Mostly short term"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Universal banking (2001 to 2010):","bold":true},{"text":" one bank could do commercial and merchant banking plus insurance and stockbroking. Advantages: removed the commercial/merchant dichotomy, created a "},{"text":"level playing field","bold":true},{"text":", raised competition. Ended by the CBN's "},{"text":"Regulation on the Scope of Banking Activities (2010)","bold":true},{"text":", which created licence types and confined banks to core banking (no insurance underwriting, no real estate except own premises, no non-permitted equity investment, no issuing currency) without CBN authorisation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Licence categories:","bold":true},{"text":" commercial ("},{"text":"international, national, regional","bold":true},{"text":" authorisation), merchant, non-interest (national, regional). International = all states plus branches or subsidiaries abroad; national = all states, no offshore; regional = a limited set of states."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Non-interest (Islamic) banking:","bold":true},{"text":" no interest ("},{"text":"riba","bold":true},{"text":"); works on "},{"text":"profit-and-loss sharing","bold":true},{"text":". Demand deposits earn nothing; investment (time) deposits get a "},{"text":"share of profit","bold":true},{"text":" at a ratio agreed in advance. Contracts: "},{"text":"Murabaha","bold":true},{"text":" (cost-plus sale with disclosed mark-up), "},{"text":"Ijarah","bold":true},{"text":" (leasing), "},{"text":"Mudarabah","bold":true},{"text":" (profit-sharing). "},{"text":"Jaiz Bank","bold":true},{"text":" is the pioneer; TAJ Bank and Lotus Bank followed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Minimum capital history:","bold":true},{"text":" 1991 BOFID ₦50m commercial, ₦40m merchant; 1997 ₦500m both; 2001 universal ₦1bn (old) / ₦2bn (new); 2004 ₦2bn; "},{"text":"July 2004 consolidation: ₦25bn shareholders' funds by 31 December 2005","bold":true},{"text":"; 2010: regional ₦10bn, merchant ₦15bn, national ₦25bn, international ₦50bn."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Current (March 2024 programme, deadline 31 March 2026):","bold":true},{"text":" international commercial "},{"text":"₦500bn","bold":true},{"text":", national "},{"text":"₦200bn","bold":true},{"text":", regional "},{"text":"₦50bn","bold":true},{"text":", merchant "},{"text":"₦50bn","bold":true},{"text":", non-interest national "},{"text":"₦20bn","bold":true},{"text":", regional "},{"text":"₦10bn","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Present-day structure:","bold":true},{"text":" CBN at the apex; commercial, merchant and non-interest banks; MFBs; DFIs; primary mortgage institutions; other institutions. The "},{"text":"West African Currency Board","bold":true},{"text":" (Nigeria's currency issuer before the CBN, 1912 to 1959) is history, not part of today's structure."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Law:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"BOFIA 2020","bold":true},{"text":" (Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act) replaced BOFIA 1991."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Consolidation","bold":true},{"text":" = banks merging, acquiring one another and raising fresh capital to meet a higher minimum. In 2004-2005, 89 banks became 25. Recapitalisation gives banks a "},{"text":"very strong capital base","bold":true},{"text":". By the 2026 deadline the CBN reported 33 banks compliant, with a few still in regulatory or court processes."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Musa's ₦4m van sold to him at ₦4.6m with the ₦600,000 mark-up disclosed (600,000 ÷ 4,000,000 × 100 = 15%), paid in instalments: "},{"text":"Murabaha","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Delta Foods needs cash now, to be repaid from a share issue in four months: "},{"text":"bridging loan","bold":true},{"text":". A bank buying a machine and renting it to a firm: "},{"text":"equipment leasing","bold":true},{"text":". Selling a client's new shares to the public: "},{"text":"issuing house","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"NCDs","bold":true},{"text":": interbank instruments of "},{"text":"3 to 36 months","bold":true},{"text":" channelling commercial banks' surplus funds into merchant banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Merchant banks are wholesale because they mobilise large funds from firms and institutions, not because they sell goods. Examples today: FBN Merchant Bank, Coronation Merchant Bank. International-licence examples: Access, Zenith, First Bank, UBA, GTBank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Indigenisation:","bold":true},{"text":" in 1973 the Federal Government took "},{"text":"40%","bold":true},{"text":" of the three big expatriate banks (now First Bank, Union Bank, UBA), raised to "},{"text":"60% in 1976","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"5.3","title":"Development finance institutions","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Development banks (DFIs, specialised banks):","bold":true},{"text":" government-owned, set up "},{"text":"jointly by the Federal Government and the CBN","bold":true},{"text":", to give "},{"text":"long-term finance to priority sectors","bold":true},{"text":" whose projects are too large or slow-maturing for conventional banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"4 rationales:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) DMBs lend short because their deposits are short, leaving a "},{"text":"long-term finance gap","bold":true},{"text":"; (2) fund "},{"text":"socially desirable but commercially unprofitable","bold":true},{"text":" projects (housing, hence FMBN); (3) bridge the "},{"text":"information gap","bold":true},{"text":", on-lending funds from the World Bank and African Development Bank; (4) push catalyst sectors (agriculture, industry) with big long-run multiplier effects."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Institution"}],[{"text":"Year"}],[{"text":"Purpose"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"NIDB"}],[{"text":"1964 (from ICON, 1959)"}],[{"text":"Long-term loans and equity to "},{"text":"medium and large","bold":true},{"text":" industry"}]],[[{"text":"NBCI"}],[{"text":"1973"}],[{"text":"Help Nigerians buy shares under the "},{"text":"Indigenisation (Enterprises Promotion) Decree 1972","bold":true},{"text":"; SMEs"}]],[[{"text":"NACB"}],[{"text":"1973 as NAB, NACB 1978"}],[{"text":"Agriculture and cooperatives"}]],[[{"text":"FMBN"}],[{"text":"1977 (from Nigerian Building Society, 1956)"}],[{"text":"Housing finance; apex mortgage bank"}]],[[{"text":"NERFUND"}],[{"text":"1989"}],[{"text":"Foreign-currency funds for SMEs, on-lent through banks; a fund, not a bank"}]],[[{"text":"NEXIM (Nigerian Export-Import Bank)"}],[{"text":"1991 (Act 38)"}],[{"text":"Export credit, "},{"text":"export credit insurance and guarantees","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"UDBN"}],[{"text":"1992 (Decree 51)"}],[{"text":"Urban infrastructure","bold":true},{"text":": markets, motor parks, mass transit, drainage, public toilets; now the Infrastructure Bank"}]],[[{"text":"BOI"}],[{"text":"2001"}],[{"text":"NIDB + NBCI + NERFUND"}]],[[{"text":"NACRDB, now "},{"text":"Bank of Agriculture","bold":true}],[{"text":"2000-2001"}],[{"text":"NACB + Peoples Bank + FEAP"}]],[[{"text":"DBN"}],[{"text":"2017"}],[{"text":"Wholesale MSME funding through "},{"text":"participating financial institutions","bold":true}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"NIDB functions:","bold":true},{"text":" identify and promote high value-added projects; mobilise funds and skills at home and abroad; long-term loans and equity. Services: loans to medium and large firms, equity stakes, World Bank funds for on-lending, information to foreign investors, technical help. It took "},{"text":"no current-account deposits","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NBCI terms:","bold":true},{"text":" equity only if promoters provide "},{"text":"at least 30%","bold":true},{"text":"; loans repayable over "},{"text":"12 to 15 years","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NACB aims:","bold":true},{"text":" food self-sufficiency, raw materials for industry, export crops for foreign exchange (all three)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"BOI purposes:","bold":true},{"text":" empower SMEs, revitalise ailing industries with fresh funds and ideas, modernise and diversify enterprises not yet moribund. It is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a lender of last resort."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mortgage system:","bold":true},{"text":" Mortgage Institutions Decree "},{"text":"53 of 1989","bold":true},{"text":" created a "},{"text":"two-tier","bold":true},{"text":" market: "},{"text":"FMBN apex","bold":true},{"text":" (wholesale funds, oversight), "},{"text":"primary mortgage institutions","bold":true},{"text":" second tier (take deposits, lend to people to buy, build or improve homes). FMBN's "},{"text":"regulatory role passed to the CBN","bold":true},{"text":" (1998); CBN now licenses and supervises PMIs. FMBN still "},{"text":"manages the National Housing Fund","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NHF (Decree 3 of 1992):","bold":true},{"text":" workers contribute "},{"text":"2.5% of monthly basic salary","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mortgage problems:","bold":true},{"text":" title documents, weak capital, no long-term funds, low incomes, ignorance, poor manpower, poverty."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Why the mergers:","bold":true},{"text":" the 1990s crises brought loan defaults and distress. A "},{"text":"2000 World Bank review","bold":true},{"text":" blamed non-commercial governance, "},{"text":"politicised lending","bold":true},{"text":", poor credit discipline, poor internal direction, no diversification, "},{"text":"lack of quality staff","bold":true},{"text":", inappropriate funding vehicles; 78% of loans were non-performing."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NIDB = large industry; NBCI = small and medium firms and indigenisation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NHF:","bold":true},{"text":" basic salary ₦180,000. Monthly = 180,000 × 2.5% = ₦4,500. Yearly = 4,500 × 12 = "},{"text":"₦54,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A cashew exporter fearing a European buyer's default goes to "},{"text":"NEXIM","bold":true},{"text":". Funmi's bakery gets DBN money "},{"text":"through her commercial or microfinance bank","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"African Continental Bank was a regional-government commercial bank, not a development bank."}]}]}]},{"number":"5.4","title":"Peoples Bank, community banks and microfinance banks","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Specialised banks","bold":true},{"text":" for the urban poor and rural masses, giving funds in very small amounts."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Peoples Bank of Nigeria (1989):","bold":true},{"text":" loans "},{"text":"without collateral and without interest","bold":true},{"text":" (only a service charge), because the poor could not afford collateral or DMB interest rates. Repayment enforced by lending to members of "},{"text":"recognised trade groups","bold":true},{"text":" (peer pressure). "},{"text":"Challenges:","bold":true},{"text":" few skilled managers, high cost of many small loans, dependence on government funds, defaults, loss of funds placed with failed DMBs. Merged into NACRDB."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Community banks:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Decree No. 46 of 1992","bold":true},{"text":". Capital "},{"text":"₦250,000","bold":true},{"text":" (1992), raised to "},{"text":"₦5,000,000","bold":true},{"text":" (2003). Supervised by the "},{"text":"National Board for Community Banks (NBCB)","bold":true},{"text":", which kept a fund for them to borrow in crisis. Same functions as commercial banks (deposits: savings, time, target; debentures; collections; ancillary services; credit) "},{"text":"except","bold":true},{"text":" (1) limited to their "},{"text":"own community","bold":true},{"text":", (2) "},{"text":"no foreign exchange","bold":true},{"text":" without CBN approval, (3) "},{"text":"not in the clearing house","bold":true},{"text":". Collapse causes: inflation, high costs, loan defaults, funds lost in distressed DMBs (not too much capital)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Microfinance Policy, December 2005 (launched 15 December):","bold":true},{"text":" community banks to "},{"text":"convert to microfinance banks within 24 months","bold":true},{"text":" by meeting new capital. Elements: states and LGAs set aside "},{"text":"at least 1% of annual budgets","bold":true},{"text":"; Microfinance Development Fund; "},{"text":"deposit insurance for MFBs","bold":true},{"text":"; consultative committee; accreditation; MFI database."}]},{"text":[{"text":"2005 categories:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"unit MFB (₦20m)","bold":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"state MFB (₦1bn)","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Goals of MFBs:","bold":true},{"text":" diversified, affordable, dependable services to the "},{"text":"economically active poor","bold":true},{"text":"; mobilise savings; create jobs and raise household income; channel government micro-credit; pay salaries, gratuities and pensions for government."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Objectives of the policy:","bold":true},{"text":" access for the excluded; mainstream the informal sector; serve MSMEs; rural transformation; "},{"text":"linkages","bold":true},{"text":" between universal/development banks and MFBs. CBN supervision also "},{"text":"enhances monetary stability","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Revised tiers (2019):","bold":true},{"text":" Tier 1 unit ₦200m (urban), Tier 2 unit ₦50m (rural), state ₦1bn, national ₦5bn."}]},{"text":[{"text":"MFB prohibitions:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"foreign exchange","bold":true},{"text":", real estate except own office, public-sector deposits (except for micro-credit programmes and payments)."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"MFBs do not finance multinationals' large projects or deal in dollars and domiciliary accounts; an Ibadan MFB selling US dollars is "},{"text":"not allowed","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"History is tested: ₦250,000 (1992) to ₦5,000,000 (before conversion); December 2005; unit and state."}]}]}]},{"number":"5.5","title":"The NDIC and bank distress","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"NDIC:","bold":true},{"text":" set up by "},{"text":"Decree No. 22 of 1988","bold":true},{"text":", began operations "},{"text":"1989","bold":true},{"text":"; law now the NDIC Act 2023 (replacing the 2006 Act). Linked to "},{"text":"SAP deregulation","bold":true},{"text":", which swelled bank numbers (about 40 in 1986 to about 120 by the early 1990s)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Rationale:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) complement CBN supervision as banks multiplied; (2) protect depositors, "},{"text":"especially small ones","bold":true},{"text":"; (3) prevent bank failure and loss of confidence; (4) ensure "},{"text":"orderly exit","bold":true},{"text":" of failing banks, reducing disruption. "},{"text":"Not","bold":true},{"text":" to protect shareholders or government deposits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Functions:","bold":true},{"text":" insure deposit liabilities; assist banks in difficulty (loans, guarantees, take over management, arrange mergers); guarantee payment to depositors; act as "},{"text":"liquidator","bold":true},{"text":" (after the CBN revokes the licence: pays insured depositors, then shares asset proceeds among depositors and creditors); help monetary authorities with banking policy. It does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" issue currency or control money supply."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Premium:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"15/16 of 1%","bold":true},{"text":" of total deposit liabilities a year (flat rate, 1988; the 2006 Act capped it at 15/16 of 1%)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cover per depositor:","bold":true},{"text":" ₦50,000 (1989) → ₦200,000 (2006) → "},{"text":"₦500,000 (2010)","bold":true},{"text":" → "},{"text":"₦5,000,000 for DMBs (2024)","bold":true},{"text":"; ₦2,000,000 for MFBs and primary mortgage banks. Balance above the cover is paid "},{"text":"pro rata as liquidation dividends","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Subrogation:","bold":true},{"text":" once paid, the depositor's claim passes to the NDIC to that extent."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Sick bank:","bold":true},{"text":" cannot meet obligations to depositors/creditors as due, "},{"text":"or","bold":true},{"text":" losses have eroded or threaten shareholders' funds."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Supervision:","bold":true},{"text":" three concerns: "},{"text":"capital adequacy, loan concentration, liquidity ratio","bold":true},{"text":". Capital adequacy 1:10 = "},{"text":"₦1 of capital supports at most ₦10 of loans","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"Off-site","bold":true},{"text":" = analysing periodic returns; "},{"text":"on-site","bold":true},{"text":" = examining books at the bank. NDIC and CBN examine jointly and share reports; under the 1988 Decree the CBN Governor chaired the NDIC Board (today the Board has its own chairman)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Distress:","bold":true},{"text":" severe financial, operational and managerial weakness leaving a bank unable to meet obligations as due. "},{"text":"CAMEL:","bold":true},{"text":" Capital adequacy, Asset quality, Management, Earnings, Liquidity."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Symptoms:","bold":true},{"text":" boardroom squabbles, customer complaints, borrowing above market rates, window dressing, "},{"text":"persistent adverse clearing positions","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Causes:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"internal:","italic":true},{"text":" weak management, insider abuse, fraud, inadequate capital, poor asset-liability management. "},{"text":"External:","italic":true},{"text":" macroeconomic instability, political interference, weak laws, oil shocks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cures:","bold":true},{"text":" better management and internal controls, adequate capital, stable macro policy, good regulation, fraud reduction."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Resolution = rehabilitation or liquidation.","bold":true},{"text":" Options: (1) "},{"text":"moral suasion","bold":true},{"text":" (dialogue and persuasion); (2) "},{"text":"holding actions","bold":true},{"text":" (stop advertising for deposits, stop new loans, aggressive debt recovery, inject fresh capital, no new capital projects); (3) "},{"text":"change of management","bold":true},{"text":" (interim board); (4) "},{"text":"NDIC assumes control and management","bold":true},{"text":" (runs it through an interim board, restructures, sells or liquidates if a turnaround costs too much); (5) comprehensive supervisory intervention; (6) "},{"text":"purchase and assumption","bold":true},{"text":" (healthy bank buys assets and takes over deposits); (7) "},{"text":"bridge banks","bold":true},{"text":"; (8) "},{"text":"liquidation","bold":true},{"text":", the "},{"text":"last option","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Premium:","bold":true},{"text":" deposits ₦64bn. 64,000,000,000 × 15/16 × 1/100 = "},{"text":"₦600,000,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Payout:","bold":true},{"text":" deposit ₦7.2m, cover ₦5m. Insured payment = ₦5,000,000; remainder 7,200,000 − 5,000,000 = "},{"text":"₦2,200,000","bold":true},{"text":" claimed from liquidation dividends as assets are sold."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Capital adequacy:","bold":true},{"text":" ₦2bn capital at 1:10 supports at most 2bn × 10 = ₦20bn of loans."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bridge banks (August 2011):","bold":true},{"text":" Mainstreet, Enterprise and Keystone took over Afribank, Spring Bank and Bank PHB."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Criticisms and replies:","bold":true},{"text":" premium too heavy and should be "},{"text":"risk-based","bold":true},{"text":" (reply: higher rates would crush weak banks the scheme protects); ₦50,000 too small (reply: comparable abroad, plus pro-rata dividends); exclude government securities (reply: they are "},{"text":"assets","bold":true},{"text":", never insured deposits); scheme should be voluntary for big banks (reply: big banks fail too and their premiums "},{"text":"subsidise small banks","bold":true},{"text":"). Lack of offices in every state was "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a criticism."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Achievements:","bold":true},{"text":" orderly resolution through rescues, sales, mergers and insured payouts. It never ended bank failure."}]}]}]},{"number":"5.6","title":"Other regulators and non-bank institutions","mustKnow":[{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Body"}],[{"text":"Regulates"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"CBN"}],[{"text":"Banks, MFBs, PMIs, BDCs, finance companies"}]],[[{"text":"NDIC"}],[{"text":"Deposit insurance"}]],[[{"text":"SEC","bold":true}],[{"text":"Apex capital market regulator","bold":true},{"text":": registers securities and operators, supervises exchanges, protects investors"}]],[[{"text":"NAICOM","bold":true}],[{"text":"Insurance companies, brokers, loss adjusters"}]],[[{"text":"PenCom","bold":true}],[{"text":"Pensions: licenses and oversees PFAs and custodians"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Pensions:","bold":true},{"text":" before 2004, civil service pensions were an "},{"text":"unfunded defined benefit","bold":true},{"text":" scheme paid from annual revenue. "},{"text":"Pension Reform Act 2004","bold":true},{"text":" created the "},{"text":"contributory","bold":true},{"text":" scheme (7.5% + 7.5%); "},{"text":"2014 Act","bold":true},{"text":": "},{"text":"employer 10%, employee 8%","bold":true},{"text":". Features: contributory; individual "},{"text":"retirement savings account (RSA)","bold":true},{"text":"; fully funded; managed by "},{"text":"PFAs","bold":true},{"text":", assets held by "},{"text":"custodians (PFCs)","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Finance companies:","bold":true},{"text":" leasing, hire purchase, factoring, LPO finance; licensed by CBN."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bureaux de change (1989):","bold":true},{"text":" retail forex to small users; originally only "},{"text":"foreign bank notes, coins and buying travellers' cheques","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discount houses (from 1992, FSDH first):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"intermediaries between the CBN and banks","bold":true},{"text":", trading in and holding Treasury Bills, commercial bills and short-term securities. Most later became merchant banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Unit trusts:","bold":true},{"text":" pool small savers' funds for diversified investment."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Insurance:","bold":true},{"text":" risk transfer plus financial intermediation; life and non-life."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A Lagos PFA seeking approval goes to "},{"text":"PenCom","bold":true},{"text":", not SEC or NAICOM. FDI = "},{"text":"Foreign Direct Investment","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Intermediaries move savings to investment: pooling, maturity transformation, lower risk and cost."}]},{"text":[{"text":"DMBs = commercial (retail) + merchant (wholesale; acceptances are their traditional function)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Universal banking 2001-2010; then licence types. Capital: ₦25bn (2005); ₦500bn/₦200bn/₦50bn (2026). BOFIA 2020."}]},{"text":[{"text":"BOI = NIDB + NBCI + NERFUND; Bank of Agriculture = NACB + Peoples Bank + FEAP."}]},{"text":[{"text":"FMBN apex, PMIs second tier; NHF 2.5% of basic salary."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Community banks (Decree 46, 1992) became MFBs (December 2005)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NDIC 1988: premium 15/16 of 1%; cover ₦50,000 → ₦500,000 → ₦5m."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liquidation is the last resort; moral suasion is dialogue."}]},{"text":[{"text":"SEC, NAICOM, PenCom regulate capital market, insurance, pensions."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"b7457b9f-2007-595e-b06a-17c072779a6e","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"A deposit money bank had total deposit liabilities of ₦48 billion. (a) What annual premium did it pay at 15/16 of 1%? (b) It later fails. A depositor holds ₦9 million. Using the ₦5 million cover, how is she paid?"}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"8f86e2a0-f7a8-5bc9-b549-559655739f8a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:6","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"The Nigerian Money Market","body":"What a money market is and how it differs from the capital market; why Nigeria set one up; its structure and operators; every instrument with its date; discount houses and the lines of defence; growth factors, appraisal and challenges; Treasury bill, call money and repo computations.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":6,"mustKnow":[{"text":"What a money market is and how it differs from the capital market; why Nigeria set one up; its structure and operators; every instrument with its date; discount houses and the lines of defence; growth factors, appraisal and challenges; Treasury bill, call money and repo computations."}],"lessons":[{"number":"6.1","title":"Meaning and features of the money market","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Money market:","bold":true},{"text":" a market for buying and selling "},{"text":"short-term financial instruments or investible funds","bold":true},{"text":", usually with maturities of "},{"text":"up to one year","bold":true},{"text":". Money is bought and sold like any other commodity. What is traded are "},{"text":"money market instruments","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Capital market:","bold":true},{"text":" the market for buying and selling "},{"text":"medium- to long-term","bold":true},{"text":" instruments (2 years to 30 or more; equity is perpetual)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Features of the money market:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Short-term instruments (up to one year)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Highly liquid: quickly turned into cash."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Low risk: issuers are creditworthy (government, big banks, blue-chip firms)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Wholesale and professional:","bold":true},{"text":" large deals dominated by institutions with specialised knowledge."}]},{"text":[{"text":"No single trading floor: deals are done by telephone and electronic platforms."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Point"}],[{"text":"Money market"}],[{"text":"Capital market"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Term"}],[{"text":"Short (up to 1 year)"}],[{"text":"Medium to long"}]],[[{"text":"Main purpose"}],[{"text":"Short-term "},{"text":"liquidity adjustment","bold":true}],[{"text":"Turning savings into "},{"text":"long-term investment","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Apex regulator"}],[{"text":"CBN","bold":true}],[{"text":"SEC","bold":true},{"text":" (Securities and Exchange Commission)"}]],[[{"text":"Dominant institutions"}],[{"text":"Deposit money banks","bold":true}],[{"text":"Non-bank financial institutions","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Instruments"}],[{"text":"TBs, CPs, CDs, BAs, call money"}],[{"text":"Shares (equities), bonds, debentures, mortgages"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"abiding (most basic) distinction","bold":true},{"text":" is the "},{"text":"term and liquidity","bold":true},{"text":" of the instruments."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Both markets have a "},{"text":"primary market","bold":true},{"text":" (new issues, raising new funds) and a "},{"text":"secondary market","bold":true},{"text":" (resale of existing securities)."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Trap: shares, equities, debentures, bonds and mortgages are "},{"text":"capital market","bold":true},{"text":" instruments, never money market ones."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Today most Nigerian money market trading (Treasury bills, commercial paper) is done over the counter on the FMDQ Securities Exchange platform, not on a stock exchange floor."}]}]}]},{"number":"6.2","title":"Why Nigeria needed a money market","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Before the CBN (1958/59) Nigeria had no money market.","bold":true},{"text":" 4 consequences:"}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Businessmen and industrialists had no local market to raise funds."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Surplus funds were "},{"text":"sent abroad to the London money market","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Government had no machinery for mobilising resources as independence approached."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Monetary policy was futile while foreign banks, run on conditions abroad, dominated finance."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Rationale (reasons for establishing it):","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Government's need to "},{"text":"bridge the gap between expenditure needs and expected revenue","bold":true},{"text":" (Treasury bills even out seasonal revenue)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nigerianisation of the credit base:","bold":true},{"text":" keep funds in Nigeria for local credit needs instead of letting them flow overseas."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Firms' need for "},{"text":"working capital","bold":true},{"text":" and traders' need to "},{"text":"finance trade","bold":true},{"text":" (sometimes overnight)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Machinery for government's short-term financing."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A step to independent nationhood ("},{"text":"monetary autonomy","bold":true},{"text":")."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Perform all traditional money market functions. Banks also gain a way to "},{"text":"economise on cash and adjust liquidity","bold":true},{"text":", and the CBN a basis for "},{"text":"effective monetary policy","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"On the supply side, banks and multinationals hold "},{"text":"excess funds","bold":true},{"text":" they want to invest short-term and safely."}]},{"text":[{"text":"4 prerequisites for a money market:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Short-term funds whose owners are willing to invest them."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A "},{"text":"continuous and sufficient flow of assets of varying maturities","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Intermediaries","bold":true},{"text":" to pool funds and invest them in short-term bills."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A "},{"text":"lender of last resort","bold":true},{"text":" (the central bank) to guarantee the liquidity of the assets."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"4 functions:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A basis for effective "},{"text":"monetary policy","bold":true},{"text":" and macroeconomic stability."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Deficit institutions borrow and surplus institutions invest "},{"text":"temporarily","bold":true},{"text":"; less idle cash."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Institutions hold part of their funds in liquid assets convertible to cash quickly."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The CBN can "},{"text":"gauge scarcity or surplus of funds through movements in money market interest rates","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Benefits:","bold":true},{"text":" (a) reduces "},{"text":"idle, barren cash","bold":true},{"text":": people earn income yet stay liquid; (b) banks hold less idle cash and invest secondary reserves; (c) ready gauge of fund scarcity for the CBN; (d) efficient execution of monetary policy."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Price discovery:","bold":true},{"text":" the money market sets benchmark short-term interest rates that serve as reference rates for other instruments."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"\"All of the above\" is the answer when options list government's revenue gap, firms' working capital and traders' finance: all three create the need."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A requirement that instruments be listed on the stock exchange is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a prerequisite."}]},{"text":[{"text":"How the money market helps monetary policy: the CBN "},{"text":"buys or sells short-term government securities (open market operations)","bold":true},{"text":" to inject or withdraw liquidity. It does not remove reserve requirements or fix prices of goods."}]}]}]},{"number":"6.3","title":"Structure and operators","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Apex institution:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"CBN","bold":true},{"text":" (current Governor: Olayemi Cardoso, since 2023)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Structure:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Formal (organised) market:","bold":true},{"text":" CBN open market operations, CBN standing facilities, the "},{"text":"interbank market","bold":true},{"text":", the discount market, primary and secondary markets in Treasury bills and other instruments."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Informal market:","bold":true},{"text":" esusu, ajo, isusu and other rotating contribution clubs, thrift collectors and money lenders."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Interbank market:","bold":true},{"text":" banks "},{"text":"lend to and borrow from each other","bold":true},{"text":" (often overnight) to manage daily liquidity, at rates set by supply and demand."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Standing facilities (CBN):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Standing Lending Facility (SLF):","bold":true},{"text":" a bank short of funds borrows "},{"text":"overnight from the CBN","bold":true},{"text":" against eligible securities. "},{"text":"Standing Deposit Facility (SDF):","bold":true},{"text":" a bank with surplus places it overnight with the CBN."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Operators:","bold":true},{"text":" CBN; discount houses (historically); Federal and state governments; commercial, merchant, universal and (today) non-interest and other deposit money banks; specialised banks (community, now microfinance, banks; People's Bank); savings-type institutions; statutory boards and corporations; NSITF; mortgage and finance houses; insurance companies; pension funds; large corporations; individuals; local governments."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lines of defence for a bank short of cash:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"First line:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"call money / interbank borrowing","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Second line:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"discounting bills","bold":true},{"text":" (e.g. Treasury bills) for cash."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discount house:","bold":true},{"text":" a non-bank institution \"trading in and holding Treasury bills, commercial bills and other securities\", acting as "},{"text":"intermediary between the CBN and licensed banks","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Structure: "},{"text":"oligopolistic","bold":true},{"text":", dominated (apart from government) by a few large banks, long the \"big three\" (First Bank, Union Bank, UBA). It is not perfectly competitive or a CBN monopoly."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Stockbrokers, registrars, issuing houses, the stock exchange and the SEC are "},{"text":"capital market","bold":true},{"text":" operators. A group of \"CBN, deposit money banks, Federal Government, large corporations\" is wholly money market."}]},{"text":[{"text":"An ajo scheme in Balogun Market, where traders pay ₦2,000 a day and each collects the pool in turn, is the "},{"text":"informal","bold":true},{"text":" money market."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A merchant bank buying 182-day Treasury bills at the CBN auction is in the "},{"text":"primary","bold":true},{"text":" market; reselling them to a pension fund two weeks later is the "},{"text":"secondary","bold":true},{"text":" market."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discount houses history:","bold":true},{"text":" first suggested in the 1960s but dropped for lack of business. "},{"text":"First Securities Discount House (FSDH)","bold":true},{"text":" was set up in "},{"text":"1992","bold":true},{"text":", the first. Later came others such as Kakawa, Consolidated, Express and Associated Discount Houses. They bought government securities from the CBN, warehoused and sold them (so government got its full funds), took call loans from banks, and gave banks a place to "},{"text":"invest surplus short-term funds and get them back quickly","bold":true},{"text":". By about 2015 the leading ones had converted into "},{"text":"merchant banks","bold":true},{"text":" (FSDH Merchant Bank, Coronation Merchant Bank from Associated, FBNQuest Merchant Bank from Kakawa). Treasury bill auctions now run through banks acting as primary dealers."}]},{"text":[{"text":"OMO sale","bold":true},{"text":" of Treasury bills by the CBN "},{"text":"reduces banks' reserves and withdraws liquidity","bold":true},{"text":"; an OMO purchase injects liquidity."}]}]}]},{"number":"6.4","title":"Money market instruments","mustKnow":[{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Instrument"}],[{"text":"Key facts"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Treasury bill (TB)","bold":true}],[{"text":"Short-term "},{"text":"FG","bold":true},{"text":" debt issued by the "},{"text":"CBN on behalf of the Federal Government","bold":true},{"text":"; sold at a "},{"text":"discount","bold":true},{"text":", redeemed at "},{"text":"face value","bold":true},{"text":". First issued "},{"text":"1960","bold":true},{"text":", ₦18 million. Standard tenors "},{"text":"91, 182, 364 days","bold":true},{"text":" (91 is the shortest). Sold by "},{"text":"auction","bold":true},{"text":": authorised dealers (banks) tender for themselves and customers."}]],[[{"text":"Treasury certificate (TC)","bold":true}],[{"text":"1968","bold":true},{"text":", ₦20 million; "},{"text":"1 to 2 years","bold":true},{"text":", so higher return than TBs. Widened instruments and financed the "},{"text":"civil war and post-war reconstruction","bold":true},{"text":". Discontinued by 1996."}]],[[{"text":"CBN certificate","bold":true}],[{"text":"February "},{"text":"2001","bold":true},{"text":"; 180 and 360 days; minimum ₦250,000."}]],[[{"text":"CBN bills","bold":true}],[{"text":"September "},{"text":"2005","bold":true},{"text":"; issued by the CBN for "},{"text":"monetary policy (to mop up excess liquidity)","bold":true},{"text":" in OMO; originally 14 to 182 days."}]],[[{"text":"Certificate of deposit (CD)","bold":true}],[{"text":"Bank receipt for a time deposit, "},{"text":"3 months to 3 years","bold":true},{"text":". Introduced "},{"text":"1975","bold":true},{"text":" as an "},{"text":"interbank","bold":true},{"text":" instrument channelling "},{"text":"commercial banks' surplus funds to merchant banks","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"Negotiable","bold":true},{"text":" (rate negotiated; can be "},{"text":"sold in the secondary market","bold":true},{"text":" before maturity) or "},{"text":"non-negotiable","bold":true},{"text":" (rate fixed by CBN; held to maturity)."}]],[[{"text":"Commercial paper (CP)","bold":true}],[{"text":"Short-term unsecured promissory note","bold":true},{"text":" of a large, creditworthy (blue-chip) firm, sold at a discount; backed only by the "},{"text":"issuer's reputation","bold":true},{"text":"; up to 270 days."}]],[[{"text":"Bankers' acceptance (BA)","bold":true}],[{"text":"A "},{"text":"bill of exchange drawn on a bank and accepted by it","bold":true},{"text":": the bank writes "},{"text":"\"Accepted\"","bold":true},{"text":" with an authorised signature across the face; 30 to 270 days."}]],[[{"text":"Call money","bold":true}],[{"text":"Very short loans (overnight or a few days) "},{"text":"repayable on demand","bold":true},{"text":"."}]],[[{"text":"Repo","bold":true}],[{"text":"Sale of securities with an agreement to "},{"text":"buy them back","bold":true},{"text":" at a set price and date."}]],[[{"text":"Tenored deposits","bold":true}],[{"text":"Call deposit","bold":true},{"text":" (no fixed maturity; ended by notice of 24 hours, 7 days, 14 days, 1 month); "},{"text":"fixed deposit","bold":true},{"text":" (fixed period, fixed rate)."}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Discontinued schemes:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Call Money Fund","bold":true},{"text":" (1962 to 1974): banks lent surplus clearing balances to the fund at a CBN-fixed rate (between 2% and 2.5%) for investment in TBs; abolished because "},{"text":"government's financial position had improved","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Produce Bills Finance Scheme","bold":true},{"text":" (from 1962, in the 1960s): a consortium of financial houses "},{"text":"discounted marketing boards' export produce bills","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bankers' Unit Fund","bold":true},{"text":" (1976 to 1988): banks pooled surplus funds with the CBN for investment mainly in "},{"text":"Federal Government stocks","bold":true},{"text":"; counted as liquid assets."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Eligible development stocks","bold":true},{"text":" (1975): FG development loan stocks with "},{"text":"less than three years to maturity","bold":true},{"text":", counted as banks' "},{"text":"liquid assets","bold":true},{"text":" for the liquidity ratio."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Modern instruments:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Electronic Funds Transfer","bold":true},{"text":" (via "},{"text":"SWIFT","bold":true},{"text":") and the "},{"text":"smart card","bold":true},{"text":" (\"cashless wallet\" with a memory chip)."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"TBs and TCs are "},{"text":"primary sources of liquidity","bold":true},{"text":" for a bank. A TB investor's return is "},{"text":"face value minus price paid","bold":true},{"text":": no dividends, no coupons."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CP vs commercial (trade) bill:","bold":true},{"text":" a trade bill arises from "},{"text":"an actual sale of goods on credit","bold":true},{"text":"; CP is "},{"text":"not tied to any trade transaction","bold":true},{"text":". A highly rated food company needing ₦2 billion for 120 days, unsecured and cheaper than a bank loan, issues "},{"text":"CP","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A BA is "},{"text":"safer than CP","bold":true},{"text":" of the same maturity because a "},{"text":"bank has undertaken to pay","bold":true},{"text":". BAs mainly finance "},{"text":"international trade","bold":true},{"text":" (an importer's draft accepted by a bank)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CBN bills vs TBs:","bold":true},{"text":" CBN bills mop up liquidity; TBs raise short-term funds for the FG."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Call deposit example:","bold":true},{"text":" Mrs Adeyemi places ₦5 million with no fixed maturity, ended by seven days' notice: a "},{"text":"call deposit","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Under the SLF the bank "},{"text":"borrows","bold":true},{"text":"; under the SDF it "},{"text":"deposits","bold":true},{"text":". Don't swap them."}]}]}]},{"number":"6.5","title":"Money market computations","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Discount (bank discount method) = Face value x rate x days/365"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Price = Face value - Discount"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Simple yield = (Face value - Price) / Price x 365/days"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Interest (call money, interbank, deposits) = Principal x rate x days/365"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Repo rate = (Repurchase price - Sale price) / Sale price x 365/days"}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Example 1: Treasury bill price.","bold":true},{"text":" ₦20,000,000 face value, 182 days, 11% discount rate."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Discount = 20,000,000 x 0.11 x 182/365 = ₦1,096,986.30 (rounded to kobo)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Price = 20,000,000 - 1,096,986.30 = "},{"text":"₦18,903,013.70","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The same method prices commercial paper: the issuer receives the price."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Example 2: yield.","bold":true},{"text":" Holding the bill above to maturity:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Gain = 20,000,000 - 18,903,013.70 = ₦1,096,986.30."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Yield = 1,096,986.30 / 18,903,013.70 x 365/182 = 0.058032 x 2.005495 = "},{"text":"11.64%","bold":true},{"text":" (2 d.p.)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The yield is "},{"text":"above","bold":true},{"text":" the 11% discount rate, because the gain is measured on the smaller price. A ₦1,000,000 91-day bill bought for ₦975,000: 25,000/975,000 x 365/91 = "},{"text":"10.28%","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Example 3: interbank interest.","bold":true},{"text":" ₦30,000,000 lent for 3 days at 18%: 30,000,000 x 0.18 x 3/365 = "},{"text":"₦44,383.56","bold":true},{"text":". For overnight use days = 1."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Example 4: repo rate.","bold":true},{"text":" A bank sells TBs for ₦4,900,000 and buys them back for ₦5,000,000 after 30 days."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Gain to the buyer = ₦100,000."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Rate = 100,000 / 4,900,000 x 365/30 = 0.020408 x 12.166667 = "},{"text":"24.83%","bold":true},{"text":" (2 d.p.)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Traps: use 365, not 360 (360 gives wrong options); the discount is not the price; for a repo divide by the "},{"text":"amount received","bold":true},{"text":", not the buy-back price."}]}]},{"number":"6.6","title":"Growth, appraisal and challenges","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"5 factors that helped growth:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Establishment and "},{"text":"enthusiasm of the CBN","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Policy of "},{"text":"Nigerianising the credit base","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"teleprinter","bold":true},{"text":" service, which "},{"text":"speeded up transfers between CBN branches and its Lagos head office","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Permissive legislation","bold":true},{"text":" allowing new and varied instruments."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Government's financial requirements","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Appraisal (how well it performed):","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Economic function (no idle funds): "},{"text":"deficient","bold":true},{"text":": excess liquidity remains."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nigerianising credit: worked in the 1960s, weakened after the oil boom (capital flight, external borrowing)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"First line of defence: weak since the Call Money Fund ended in "},{"text":"1974","bold":true},{"text":"; the interbank market is smaller and dearer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Second line of defence (bill discounting): partly met since discount houses came."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Channel for CBN cash and basis for "},{"text":"OMO","bold":true},{"text":": partly met; OMO is now a key tool."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Short-term funds for government: "},{"text":"most effective","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Challenges:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Dominance by government securities","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Narrow range of instruments","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lack of "},{"text":"depth, breadth and resilience","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Low "},{"text":"public awareness","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Oligopolistic structure","bold":true},{"text":" dominated by big banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Weak "},{"text":"institutional framework for probity, integrity and accountability","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Information asymmetry","bold":true},{"text":": unequal access to market information, weakening pricing."}]}]}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"\"Listing of Treasury bills on foreign stock exchanges\" was "},{"text":"never","bold":true},{"text":" a growth factor."}]},{"text":[{"text":"\"Lack of market space in some cities\" is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a challenge: the market needs no physical location."}]},{"text":[{"text":"An unregulated market, too many instruments or too many small investors are all wrong options; the real problems are government dominance and too few instruments."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Money market = short-term (up to 1 year), liquid, low-risk, wholesale; CBN apex, banks dominate. Capital market = long-term; SEC apex, non-bank institutions dominate."}]},{"text":[{"text":"No money market before the CBN; surplus funds went to London. Key aim: Nigerianise the credit base."}]},{"text":[{"text":"4 prerequisites end with a lender of last resort."}]},{"text":[{"text":"First line of defence: call money/interbank; second: discounting bills."}]},{"text":[{"text":"TBs 1960 (91/182/364 days); TCs 1968; Call Money Fund 1962-74; Produce Bills from 1962; CDs and eligible stocks 1975; BUF to 1988; CBN certificate 2001; CBN bills 2005; FSDH 1992."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CP is unsecured, backed by reputation; BA is a bill accepted by a bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"OMO sale withdraws liquidity; SLF lends to banks, SDF takes their deposits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discount = FV x r x days/365; price = FV - discount."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"79757ac4-9d38-5934-b193-781b9f2373f8","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"A ₦10,000,000 91-day Treasury bill is issued at a 10% discount rate. Find the price and the investor's simple yield (365-day year)."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"The Nigerian Money Market","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"df6f8e7b-2c44-56c6-b389-8ec18be571e2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:7","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Organisational Structure of Clearing Banks","body":"What a clearing bank is and its six characteristics; the three levels of structure (governance, management, operational); board committees and executive officers; front, middle and back office and support functions; the branch network; line and staff, centralisation and decentralisation; unit, branch, group, chain and correspondent banking; the clearing process and the history of cheque clearing in Nigeria (manual, MICR, NACS, the 2004 settlement framework, electronic systems).","noteType":"guide_topic","number":7,"mustKnow":[{"text":"What a clearing bank is and its six characteristics; the three levels of structure (governance, management, operational); board committees and executive officers; front, middle and back office and support functions; the branch network; line and staff, centralisation and decentralisation; unit, branch, group, chain and correspondent banking; the clearing process and the history of cheque clearing in Nigeria (manual, MICR, NACS, the 2004 settlement framework, electronic systems)."}],"lessons":[{"number":"7.1","title":"Clearing banks and their characteristics","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Clearing banks:","bold":true},{"text":" financial institutions that are "},{"text":"members of a clearing house","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"maintain accounts with the Central Bank","bold":true},{"text":" and can "},{"text":"participate directly in the clearing and settlement","bold":true},{"text":" of cheques, payments and other payment instruments. In Nigeria they are "},{"text":"commercial banks licensed by the CBN","bold":true},{"text":" to take part in the national clearing and settlement system."}]},{"text":[{"text":"6 key characteristics:","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Membership of the clearing house","bold":true},{"text":" run by or under the CBN."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Settlement account with the CBN","bold":true},{"text":", used to settle inter-bank fund transfers and net clearing positions."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Wide branch network","bold":true},{"text":" across the country."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Large capital base","bold":true},{"text":" (must meet CBN minimum capital)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Advanced technology","bold":true},{"text":" for payment processing."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Regulatory compliance:","bold":true},{"text":" subject to strict CBN supervision."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Regulatory framework (6):","bold":true},{"text":" CBN (primary regulator: licences, examinations); "},{"text":"NDIC","bold":true},{"text":" ("},{"text":"insures deposits","bold":true},{"text":", supervises, "},{"text":"resolves failed banks","bold":true},{"text":"); "},{"text":"BOFIA 2020","bold":true},{"text":" (main banking law; replaced BOFIA 1991); CBN Prudential Guidelines (capital, liquidity, lending limits); AML/CFT regulations; Consumer Protection Framework."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A clearing bank is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" restricted to a single office in one locality; that describes a unit bank (Lesson 7.4). The settlement account at the CBN is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" for safe custody, deposit insurance premiums or issuing currency."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Commercial banks such as First Bank, Zenith, Access, UBA and GTBank are clearing banks; microfinance banks are not direct clearing house members."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Challenges (7):","bold":true},{"text":" cybersecurity; compliance costs; fintech disruption; cost of branch networks; credit risk (bad loans); customers' demand for 24/7 service; talent."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Future trends (7):","bold":true},{"text":" digital transformation; agility (flatter structures); "},{"text":"centralisation of operations","bold":true},{"text":" (back office consolidated into central processing centres and shared services, for "},{"text":"efficiency","bold":true},{"text":"); fintech partnerships and open banking; data analytics; ESG (green) banking; hybrid work."}]}]}]},{"number":"7.2","title":"Governance and management levels","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"The structure is hierarchical and examined at "},{"text":"3 levels:","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Governance level:","bold":true},{"text":" the Board of Directors."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Management level:","bold":true},{"text":" executive management."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Operational level:","bold":true},{"text":" departments and units."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Board of Directors:","bold":true},{"text":" sits at the "},{"text":"apex","bold":true},{"text":"; responsible for "},{"text":"overall strategic direction and governance","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Board composition:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Board Chairman (non-executive)","bold":true},{"text":"; MD/CEO; Executive Directors (typically 3 to 5); independent Non-Executive Directors; representative of major shareholders (where applicable)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"7 board functions:","bold":true},{"text":" formulating strategic policies and objectives; appointing and overseeing senior management; approving major capital expenditure and investments; ensuring regulatory compliance and corporate governance; risk oversight and approving the risk management framework; reviewing and approving annual budgets and financial statements; protecting shareholders' interests."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Board committee"}],[{"text":"Job"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Audit"}],[{"text":"Financial reporting, internal controls, external audit"}]],[[{"text":"Risk Management"}],[{"text":"Monitors and manages all categories of risk"}]],[[{"text":"Credit"}],[{"text":"Reviews and approves large credit facilities; sets credit policies"}]],[[{"text":"Governance and Nomination"}],[{"text":"Board appointments, succession planning, governance"}]],[[{"text":"Remuneration"}],[{"text":"Compensation packages for executive management"}]],[[{"text":"IT Governance"}],[{"text":"Technology strategy, cybersecurity, digital transformation"}]]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Board vs executive management:","bold":true},{"text":" the board "},{"text":"sets strategy and oversees","bold":true},{"text":"; executive management "},{"text":"runs the bank day to day","bold":true},{"text":" and implements board policies."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Executive"}],[{"text":"Responsibility"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"MD/CEO"}],[{"text":"Highest-ranking executive: implements board decisions, develops strategy, leads the management team, represents the bank externally, ensures profitability and growth"}]],[[{"text":"Deputy Managing Director (DMD)"}],[{"text":"Assists the MD/CEO and acts in his absence; often oversees strategic business units"}]],[[{"text":"ED, Commercial Banking"}],[{"text":"Commercial, corporate and SME banking"}]],[[{"text":"ED, Retail Banking"}],[{"text":"Retail operations, consumer lending, branch network"}]],[[{"text":"ED, Treasury and Financial Markets"}],[{"text":"Treasury, foreign exchange, investment portfolio, money market"}]],[[{"text":"Chief Financial Officer (CFO)"}],[{"text":"Financial planning, accounting, financial reporting, investor relations"}]],[[{"text":"Chief Risk Officer (CRO)"}],[{"text":"Enterprise risk management: credit, market, operational risk"}]],[[{"text":"Chief Compliance Officer (CCO)"}],[{"text":"Regulatory compliance, AML, KYC"}]],[[{"text":"CTO/CIO"}],[{"text":"IT infrastructure, digital banking platforms, technology strategy"}]],[[{"text":"Chief Operations Officer (COO)"}],[{"text":"Operations, service delivery, process optimisation, efficiency"}]],[[{"text":"Company Secretary/Legal Adviser"}],[{"text":"Legal counsel, corporate governance, regulatory filings"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Traps:","bold":true},{"text":" the Chairman is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" the MD/CEO; CBN governance rules keep the roles separate. The board does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" process account-opening forms. Shareholders, not depositors, meet at the AGM."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Scenario links: a ₦25 billion loan above management's limit goes to the "},{"text":"Credit Committee","bold":true},{"text":"; finding a successor to a retiring chairman is "},{"text":"Governance and Nomination","bold":true},{"text":"; overseeing external auditors is "},{"text":"Audit","bold":true},{"text":"; cybersecurity oversight is "},{"text":"IT Governance","bold":true},{"text":" (not Risk Management)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Kemi, who heads AML and KYC, is the "},{"text":"CCO","bold":true},{"text":", not the CRO. The CRO handles risk, not compliance."}]}]}]},{"number":"7.3","title":"Operational level, branch network and authority","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"A. Front office (revenue generating; deals directly with customers)","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Retail Banking:","bold":true},{"text":" personal accounts, consumer loans, cards."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Corporate Banking:","bold":true},{"text":" large corporates, project and trade finance."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Commercial Banking:","bold":true},{"text":" medium-sized enterprises, working capital."}]},{"text":[{"text":"SME Banking:","bold":true},{"text":" small businesses, micro-finance."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Treasury:","bold":true},{"text":" FX, money market, investment securities, asset-liability management."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Investment Banking:","bold":true},{"text":" mergers and acquisitions advisory, capital raising, underwriting."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Private Banking/Wealth Management:","bold":true},{"text":" high net-worth individuals, investment advisory, estate planning."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"B. Middle office (support and control)","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Risk Management:","bold":true},{"text":" credit, market and operational risk."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Compliance:","bold":true},{"text":" AML/CFT, sanctions, regulatory reporting."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Internal Audit:","bold":true},{"text":" independent review of controls."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Credit Administration:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"credit appraisal, loan documentation, collateral management, credit monitoring","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Finance and Planning:","bold":true},{"text":" budgeting, management reporting."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Legal:","bold":true},{"text":" contracts, litigation, debt recovery."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"C. Back office (processing and settlement)","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Operations:","bold":true},{"text":" transaction processing, account opening, cheque clearing, funds transfer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Payment and Settlement:","bold":true},{"text":" clearing house operations, RTGS, NEFT, inter-bank settlement."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Reconciliation Unit:","bold":true},{"text":" account reconciliation, "},{"text":"nostro/vostro","bold":true},{"text":" accounts, "},{"text":"suspense account","bold":true},{"text":" resolution."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Customer Service:","bold":true},{"text":" enquiries, complaints."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cards and Electronic Banking:","bold":true},{"text":" ATMs, POS, mobile banking."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trade Services:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"letters of credit","bold":true},{"text":", bills for collection, trade documents."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"D. Support functions:","bold":true},{"text":" Human Resources; Information Technology; Procurement/General Services; Marketing and Corporate Communications; Business Development; Internal Control."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Branch network (top down):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Head Office → Regional/Zonal Office → Area/Divisional Office → Branches","bold":true},{"text":", plus "},{"text":"Agency banking/agents","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Head office:","bold":true},{"text":" executive management and central departments. "},{"text":"Regional/Zonal office:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"oversees clusters of branches in a geographical zone","bold":true},{"text":" (e.g. Lagos Zone); reports to head office. "},{"text":"Area office:","bold":true},{"text":" smaller clusters within a region. "},{"text":"Branches:","bold":true},{"text":" outlets serving customers directly (flagship, commercial, retail, mini). The "},{"text":"branch manager","bold":true},{"text":" has overall responsibility: business development, customer relations, approving loans within delegated limits."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Agency banking:","bold":true},{"text":" third-party agents (e.g. a shop owner) offering basic deposit and withdrawal services in underserved areas."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Line authority:","bold":true},{"text":" direct authority passed down the chain of command over people who carry out the bank's main business (e.g. MD → regional manager → branch manager)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Staff authority (staff function):","bold":true},{"text":" specialists who "},{"text":"advise","bold":true},{"text":" line managers but "},{"text":"cannot command","bold":true},{"text":" them (legal, research, HR training)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Centralisation:","bold":true},{"text":" decision-making concentrated at head office; branches refer major decisions upward. "},{"text":"Decentralisation:","bold":true},{"text":" authority delegated to branch managers, who decide locally."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Traps:","bold":true},{"text":" Internal Audit is "},{"text":"middle office","bold":true},{"text":", not front office. Payment and Settlement is "},{"text":"back office","bold":true},{"text":", not a support function. Treasury and Private Banking "},{"text":"are","bold":true},{"text":" front office. Credit Administration is middle office, distinct from Corporate Banking which sells loans."}]},{"text":[{"text":"An importer in Kano wanting a letter of credit is served by "},{"text":"Trade Services","bold":true},{"text":" (back office). A unit matching nostro and vostro entries is the "},{"text":"Reconciliation Unit","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A branch manager is a "},{"text":"line","bold":true},{"text":" officer; the head of legal advice, a research analyst and an HR training officer are "},{"text":"staff","bold":true},{"text":". When the Legal Department reviews a loan contract but cannot order the branch to lend, it is a staff function."}]},{"text":[{"text":"An Ibadan branch manager who must send every small loan to Lagos shows "},{"text":"excessive centralisation","bold":true},{"text":" (delay, frustrated customers). "},{"text":"Decentralisation","bold":true},{"text":" gives "},{"text":"faster decisions that respond to local needs","bold":true},{"text":"; centralisation gives tighter uniform control."}]}]}]},{"number":"7.4","title":"Types of banking systems","mustKnow":[{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"System"}],[{"text":"Meaning"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Unit banking"}],[{"text":"Each bank is a separate entity, usually a single office, with its own management; operations restricted to one locality (USA tradition)"}]],[[{"text":"Branch banking"}],[{"text":"One bank operates many branches across the country under one head office (Nigeria, UK)"}]],[[{"text":"Group banking"}],[{"text":"Two or more banks, each keeping its separate name, controlled by a "},{"text":"holding company","bold":true},{"text":"; the controlled banks are "},{"text":"operating banks","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Chain banking"}],[{"text":"Control of several separate banks by an "},{"text":"individual or group of individuals","bold":true},{"text":" through share ownership, with "},{"text":"no holding company","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Correspondent banking"}],[{"text":"Small ("},{"text":"respondent","bold":true},{"text":") banks keep deposits with large city ("},{"text":"correspondent","bold":true},{"text":") banks, which give them services; it knits unit banks together"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Unit banking: merits","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Prevents monopoly banking."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Close personal relationship with, and knowledge of, local customers."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Quick local decisions; efficient management."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Funds are used locally for local development."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Unit banking: demerits","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Cannot grant large loans (small resources)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Risk is not spread geographically."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Difficulty meeting a sudden rush of withdrawals."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Dependence on correspondent banks for remittance of funds, which is costly."}]},{"text":[{"text":"No economies of scale; savings facilities limited to one locality."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Branch banking: merits","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Economies of large-scale operation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Geographical spreading of risk","bold":true},{"text":" (losses in one area covered by profits elsewhere)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Small cash reserves at each branch: "},{"text":"cash can be mobilised quickly from sister branches","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Wider mobilisation of savings, including rural areas."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cheap and easy remittance of funds."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Easier and more efficient "},{"text":"central bank control","bold":true},{"text":" of a few large banks."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Branch banking: demerits","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Can create "},{"text":"monopolistic or oligopolistic and bureaucratic conditions","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Delay and red tape: branch managers refer decisions to head office."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lack of personal touch with local customers."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Funds drained from rural to urban areas."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Group banking:","bold":true},{"text":" merits: economies of scale, central management, operating banks can "},{"text":"pool resources to finance large borrowings","bold":true},{"text":". Demerits: may lead to "},{"text":"monopoly","bold":true},{"text":"; holding company may "},{"text":"divert surplus reserves to its non-banking interests","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"failure of one operating bank can affect others","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Group vs chain:","bold":true},{"text":" group banking uses a "},{"text":"holding company","bold":true},{"text":"; chain banking is control by "},{"text":"individuals","bold":true},{"text":" through share ownership (Chief Okonkwo and his brothers owning controlling shares in three banks). In both, the banks keep separate names."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Correspondent vs respondent:","bold":true},{"text":" the large city bank is the correspondent; the small community bank that keeps deposits with it is the "},{"text":"respondent","bold":true},{"text":". The correspondent clears cheques, remits funds, gives investment advice and joins in large loans for the respondent. It does not replace the CBN as lender of last resort and does not put banks under one holding company."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nigerian commercial banks with hundreds of outlets across the 36 states and the FCT under one head office practise "},{"text":"branch banking","bold":true},{"text":"; some also sit under holding companies (e.g. Access Holdings, First HoldCo, formerly FBN Holdings)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"\"Close personal relationship with local customers\" is a "},{"text":"merit","bold":true},{"text":" of unit banking, not a demerit."}]}]}]},{"number":"7.5","title":"The clearing function and its history in Nigeria","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Clearing system:","bold":true},{"text":" the process of "},{"text":"obtaining values for non-in-house cheques among deposit money banks","bold":true},{"text":", usually carried out at the "},{"text":"clearing house of the CBN or its designated branches","bold":true},{"text":". Put simply: banks exchange cheques drawn on one another and settle the "},{"text":"net","bold":true},{"text":" amounts owed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Lagos Clearing House:","bold":true},{"text":" a "},{"text":"cheque sorting (exchange) centre","bold":true},{"text":", not a stock exchange or commodity market. The CBN set up the first bankers' clearing house in "},{"text":"1961","bold":true},{"text":" for the Lagos zone."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"7 steps of cheque clearing (in order):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Collection:","bold":true},{"text":" branches collect cheques deposited by customers."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Sorting:","bold":true},{"text":" by paying bank into "},{"text":"on-us","bold":true},{"text":" (drawn on the same bank, settled internally) and "},{"text":"other-bank","bold":true},{"text":" items."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Presentation:","bold":true},{"text":" other-bank cheques are presented to the clearing house."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clearing session:","bold":true},{"text":" the clearing house coordinates the exchange between banks."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Settlement:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"net positions settled through settlement accounts at the CBN","bold":true},{"text":" (debits and credits)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Return items:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"unpaid cheques sent back through the clearing system with reasons","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Customer notification","bold":true},{"text":" of cleared or returned items."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"History (dates):","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Period"}],[{"text":"Key facts"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Manual (from 1961)"}],[{"text":"Bank representatives met physically to exchange cheques and present claims; up to 8 hours to know net positions; up to 27 days for customers to get value"}]],[[{"text":"MICR (from 1988)"}],[{"text":"Magnetic Ink Character Recognition, to speed machine processing and shorten clearing; phased from CBN in 1988; Lagos zone stopped conventional cheque exchange on 1 July 1994; reject rate about 30% for lack of cheque standards"}]],[[{"text":"NACS (2002)"}],[{"text":"Nigeria Automated Clearing System: automated clearing began in 2002 with 22 banks; NACS is a department of "},{"text":"NIBSS","bold":true},{"text":" (Nigeria Inter-Bank Settlement System)"}]],[[{"text":"New settlement framework (1 April 2004)"}],[{"text":"Banks split into settlement and non-settlement banks"}]],[[{"text":"Cheque truncation (2012)"}],[{"text":"Image-based clearing; less physical movement of cheques"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"NACS: 4 core objectives","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Efficiently and cost-effectively process clearing items using electronic data presentation."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Significantly reduce the clearing float.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Maintain and enforce standards for clearing procedures."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Encourage development and integration of other electronic payment products."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"NACS participants (3):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Zonal Clearing House","bold":true},{"text":" (centre: registers participants, processes data, determines settlement positions, manages NACS infrastructure); "},{"text":"Direct participating bank","bold":true},{"text":" (through its Participating Bank Clearing Centre, PBCC, sends data straight to the ZCH; 21 at inception); "},{"text":"Indirect participating bank","bold":true},{"text":" (clears "},{"text":"through a direct participating bank","bold":true},{"text":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"2004 framework:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"settlement bank","bold":true},{"text":" = a bank that clears and settles cheques "},{"text":"on behalf of other banks","bold":true},{"text":"; only settlement banks keep "},{"text":"settlement accounts","bold":true},{"text":" with the CBN and deposit collateral with it. "},{"text":"Non-settlement banks","bold":true},{"text":" keep "},{"text":"only operations accounts","bold":true},{"text":" with the CBN (for FX, cash and cash reserve requirements, always in credit) and clear through an "},{"text":"agency arrangement","bold":true},{"text":" with a settlement bank, depositing at least ₦250 million collateral with it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Electronic systems:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"NEFT","bold":true},{"text":" (NIBSS Electronic Funds Transfer: "},{"text":"batch","bold":true},{"text":" processing); "},{"text":"RTGS","bold":true},{"text":" (Real-Time Gross Settlement: "},{"text":"individual large-value payments in real time","bold":true},{"text":"); "},{"text":"NIP","bold":true},{"text":" (NIBSS Instant Payment: "},{"text":"instant","bold":true},{"text":" transfer to another bank); "},{"text":"Cheque Truncation System","bold":true},{"text":" (electronic image-based clearing)."}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Why the 2004 framework:","bold":true},{"text":" by 1995 over thirty banks persistently overdrew their CBN accounts through clearing; suspending a bank from clearing was too damaging, so the framework imposed market discipline to end overdrawn accounts. Settlement banks needed ₦15 billion (later ₦16 billion) clearing collateral and branches in all 22 clearing zones; seven were named before the 2005 consolidation (including UBA, First Bank, Union Bank, GTBank and Zenith)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Benefits of automation:","bold":true},{"text":" shorter clearing cycle and "},{"text":"smaller clearing float","bold":true},{"text":", so customers get value faster (5 days cut to 3, now shorter); less rowdiness and late balancing; less fraud. Automation did "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" abolish cheques and did "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" remove banks' CBN accounts or allow unlimited overdrawing."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Today:","bold":true},{"text":" NIBSS (owned by the CBN and the banks) runs NACS, NIP and NEFT; the CBN runs RTGS and holds banks' settlement accounts. With cheque truncation, cheques now clear on the next working day (T+1)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Traps:","bold":true},{"text":" NACS is run by "},{"text":"NIBSS","bold":true},{"text":", not CIBN, SEC or NDIC; NDIC does not settle clearing balances. A cheque drawn on Bank B paid into Bank A is an "},{"text":"other-bank item","bold":true},{"text":" presented through the clearing house; a cheque drawn on Bank A itself is "},{"text":"on-us","bold":true},{"text":". RTGS is real-time and gross; NEFT is batch."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Clearing bank = clearing house member + CBN settlement account + direct clearing. Three levels: governance (board), management (executives), operational (departments)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Board at the apex; Chairman non-executive; six committees: Audit, Risk, Credit, Governance and Nomination, Remuneration, IT Governance."}]},{"text":[{"text":"MD/CEO leads; DMD acts in his absence; CRO risk; CCO compliance, AML, KYC."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Front office earns revenue; middle office controls (incl. Internal Audit, Credit Administration); back office processes (Operations, Payment and Settlement, Reconciliation, Trade Services)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Branch network: head office → region/zone → area → branch, plus agents."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Line commands; staff advises. Excessive centralisation causes delay; decentralisation speeds local decisions."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Unit, branch (Nigeria), group (holding company, operating banks), chain (individuals), correspondent (respondent banks)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clearing: collect → sort → present → session → settle at CBN → returns → notify."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Manual 1961, MICR 1988, NACS 2002 (NIBSS), settlement banks 1 April 2004, truncation 2012; NIP instant, RTGS real-time large value, NEFT batch."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"cf183a1a-405b-5437-b01b-151c2507c180","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Distinguish a settlement bank from a non-settlement bank under the CBN's April 2004 framework, and list the steps in clearing a cheque."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"7f9bf555-6a8e-5d21-9568-bac17ccfe251","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:8","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","body":"Negotiable instruments; bills of exchange, promissory notes and IOUs; acceptance, maturity, days of grace and discounting; holders, endorsement and dishonour; cheques, their parties, types and crossings; when a bank must not pay; and cheque clearing in Nigeria.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":8,"mustKnow":[{"text":"Negotiable instruments; bills of exchange, promissory notes and IOUs; acceptance, maturity, days of grace and discounting; holders, endorsement and dishonour; cheques, their parties, types and crossings; when a bank must not pay; and cheque clearing in Nigeria."}],"lessons":[{"number":"8.1","title":"Negotiable instruments, bills, notes and IOUs","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Law:","bold":true},{"text":" the Bills of Exchange Act (Cap B8, Laws of the Federation of Nigeria 2004), modelled on the English Bills of Exchange Act 1882."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Negotiable instrument:","bold":true},{"text":" a written document that promises or orders payment of a sum of money, is transferable by delivery or by endorsement and delivery, and gives the holder in due course a good title free from defects in the title of prior parties."}]},{"text":[{"text":"6 characteristics:","bold":true},{"text":" (1) writing, (2) signature, (3) unconditional promise or order, (4) fixed sum (sum certain in money), (5) payable on demand or at a fixed or determinable future time, (6) transferability."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bill of exchange:","bold":true},{"text":" an unconditional order in writing, addressed by one person to another, signed by the person giving it, requiring the person to whom it is addressed to pay on demand or at a fixed or determinable future time a sum certain in money to or to the order of a specified person or to bearer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cheque:","bold":true},{"text":" a bill of exchange drawn on a banker payable on demand."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Promissory note:","bold":true},{"text":" an unconditional promise in writing made by one person to another, signed by the maker, engaging to pay on demand or at a fixed or determinable future time a sum certain in money to, or to the order of, a specified person or to bearer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"IOU (\"I owe you\"):","bold":true},{"text":" an informal document acknowledging a debt owed by one person to another. It is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a negotiable instrument: it cannot be assigned, transferred or sold."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Parties","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Instrument"}],[{"text":"Parties"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Bill of exchange"}],[{"text":"Drawer","bold":true},{"text":" (gives the order), "},{"text":"drawee","bold":true},{"text":" (ordered to pay; becomes "},{"text":"acceptor","bold":true},{"text":" once he accepts), "},{"text":"payee","bold":true},{"text":" (to be paid)"}]],[[{"text":"Cheque"}],[{"text":"Drawer (customer who writes it), drawee (always a "},{"text":"bank","bold":true},{"text":"), payee"}]],[[{"text":"Promissory note"}],[{"text":"Maker","bold":true},{"text":" (signs and promises to pay), payee: only 2 parties"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Bill versus promissory note","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Bill of exchange"}],[{"text":"Promissory note"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"An "},{"text":"order","bold":true},{"text":" to pay"}],[{"text":"A "},{"text":"promise","bold":true},{"text":" to pay"}]],[[{"text":"3 parties"}],[{"text":"2 parties"}]],[[{"text":"A time bill needs acceptance"}],[{"text":"No acceptance"}]],[[{"text":"Drawer liable only if the drawee fails to pay"}],[{"text":"Maker is primarily liable"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Mr Bello, who banks with Zenith Bank, writes a ₦250,000 cheque to Mrs Adeyemi: Bello = drawer, "},{"text":"Zenith Bank = drawee","bold":true},{"text":", Adeyemi = payee. The Central Bank of Nigeria is never the drawee of an ordinary customer's cheque."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trap: a bill or a note must be in writing, so \"need not be in writing\" is always false. A promissory note can be payable on demand."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Contents of an IOU (5):","bold":true},{"text":" parties' details; payment information (amount, due date, interest, method); signatures of both parties; termination date; involvement of a legal practitioner."}]}]}]},{"number":"8.2","title":"Bills in operation: acceptance, maturity, days of grace and discounting","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Acceptance:","bold":true},{"text":" the signification by the drawee of his assent to the order of the drawer. It must be written on the bill and signed by the drawee. After accepting he is the "},{"text":"acceptor","bold":true},{"text":" and is primarily liable."}]},{"text":[{"text":"General acceptance:","bold":true},{"text":" assents without qualification. "},{"text":"Qualified acceptance:","bold":true},{"text":" varies the bill's effect, e.g. "},{"text":"partial","bold":true},{"text":" (\"Accepted for ₦300,000 only\" on a ₦500,000 bill), "},{"text":"conditional","bold":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"local","bold":true},{"text":" (one place only), qualified as to "},{"text":"time","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"When payable:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"On demand (sight bill):","bold":true},{"text":" expressed \"on demand\", \"at sight\" or \"on presentation\", or no time stated. A cheque is always on demand."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Usance (time) bill:","bold":true},{"text":" \"X days after date\", \"X days after sight\", or after an event "},{"text":"certain","bold":true},{"text":" to happen (e.g. someone's death)."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Presentment for acceptance is necessary","bold":true},{"text":" for a bill payable "},{"text":"after sight","bold":true},{"text":" (to fix maturity), a bill that says so, and a bill payable away from the drawee's residence or place of business."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Days of grace:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"3 days","bold":true},{"text":" are added to a bill not payable on demand, unless the bill says otherwise. Cheques and demand bills get none."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Counting:","bold":true},{"text":" exclude the day of the date (or of sight), include the day of payment."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Inland bill:","bold":true},{"text":" drawn and payable within Nigeria (or drawn in Nigeria on a resident). "},{"text":"Foreign bill:","bold":true},{"text":" any other."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Accommodation party:","bold":true},{"text":" signs as drawer, acceptor or endorser "},{"text":"without receiving value","bold":true},{"text":", to lend his name to another."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discounting:","bold":true},{"text":" a bank buys an "},{"text":"accepted usance bill","bold":true},{"text":" before maturity for less than its face value."}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Discount = Face value x Rate x Days / 365"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Proceeds = Face value - Discount"}]}]}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Contingency trap:","bold":true},{"text":" \"30 days after the death of Chief Ade\" is valid (death is certain; only its date is unknown). \"On the day she gets married\" is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" a bill: marriage may never happen. Any condition (\"provided the receipt is signed\") also destroys a bill or cheque."}]},{"text":[{"text":"\"Ninety days after sight pay Chika Nwosu or order ₦1,200,000\" is a "},{"text":"usance bill","bold":true},{"text":" and must be presented for acceptance to fix its maturity. A bill drawn in Accra and payable in Lagos is a "},{"text":"foreign bill","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 1 (maturity):","bold":true},{"text":" bill dated 15 January, payable 60 days after date (not a leap year)."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Exclude 15 January. Days left in January: 31 - 15 = 16."}]},{"text":[{"text":"February: 28 more = 44. March: 60 - 44 = 16, so 16 March."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Add 3 days of grace: due "},{"text":"19 March","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Same method: dated 1 March, 30 days after date: 30 days to 31 March, plus 3 = "},{"text":"3 April","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 2 (discounting):","bold":true},{"text":" Mrs Okon's accepted bill of ₦800,000 matures in 73 days; her bank discounts at 10% a year (365-day year)."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Discount = 800,000 x 0.10 x 73/365 = ₦16,000."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Proceeds = 800,000 - 16,000 = "},{"text":"₦784,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Another: ₦1,460,000 at 15% for 60 days: discount = 1,460,000 x 0.15 x 60/365 = ₦36,000; proceeds = "},{"text":"₦1,424,000","bold":true},{"text":". The bank collects the full face value at maturity; the difference is its income. It discounts only for credible customers, with at least two parties (e.g. acceptor and customer) of good standing. Cheques are not discounted: they are already payable on demand."}]}]},{"number":"8.3","title":"Holders, endorsement and dishonour","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Holder:","bold":true},{"text":" the payee or endorsee who is in possession of the bill, or the bearer of it."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Holder in due course:","bold":true},{"text":" a holder who took the bill:"}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"complete and regular on the face of it;"}]},{"text":[{"text":"before it was overdue, and without notice of any previous dishonour;"}]},{"text":[{"text":"in good faith and for value;"}]},{"text":[{"text":"without notice of any defect in the title of the person who negotiated it."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"He need "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" be the original payee. "},{"text":"Advantage:","bold":true},{"text":" he takes a good title free from defects in the title of prior parties."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Endorsement:","bold":true},{"text":" the signature of the holder on the instrument (usually on the back) made to transfer it. "},{"text":"Delivery","bold":true},{"text":" is transfer of possession; endorsement plus delivery transfers an order instrument; a bearer instrument passes by delivery alone."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Valid endorsement:","bold":true},{"text":" written on the bill itself and signed by the endorser; it must cover the "},{"text":"whole","bold":true},{"text":" bill (no partial endorsement)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Types of endorsement:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Blank:","bold":true},{"text":" signature only; makes the instrument payable to "},{"text":"bearer","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Special:","bold":true},{"text":" names the endorsee (\"Pay Chinedu Okeke or order\")."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Restrictive:","bold":true},{"text":" stops further negotiation (\"Pay Musa Ibrahim only\")."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Conditional:","bold":true},{"text":" adds a condition, which the payer may disregard."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Dishonour","bold":true},{"text":" happens in 2 ways: by "},{"text":"non-acceptance","bold":true},{"text":" and by "},{"text":"non-payment","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Notice of dishonour:","bold":true},{"text":" given to the drawer and endorsers to "},{"text":"preserve the holder's right of recourse","bold":true},{"text":" against them."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Noting:","bold":true},{"text":" a notary public's minute on the bill. "},{"text":"Protest:","bold":true},{"text":" the formal notarial certificate attesting dishonour."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Foreign bill","bold":true},{"text":" dishonoured: protest is "},{"text":"compulsory","bold":true},{"text":", or the drawer and endorsers are "},{"text":"discharged","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"Inland bill:","bold":true},{"text":" noting or protest is "},{"text":"optional","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Fictitious or non-existing payee:","bold":true},{"text":" the bill may be treated as payable to bearer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bill prohibiting transfer","bold":true},{"text":" (\"Pay Ade only\"): valid between the parties but not negotiable."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Material alteration","bold":true},{"text":" (date, sum, time or place of payment) without the assent of all parties liable: the bill is "},{"text":"avoided","bold":true},{"text":", except against a party who made, authorised or assented to it, and later endorsers."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Liability:","bold":true},{"text":" the acceptor is primarily liable; the drawer and endorsers are liable if it is dishonoured. Until a cheque is endorsed, the "},{"text":"drawer","bold":true},{"text":" alone is liable on it: the drawee bank never accepts a cheque, so it owes nothing to the holder (it answers to its customer under their contract)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Thief and holder in due course:","bold":true},{"text":" a thief steals an uncrossed bearer cheque and passes it to Ada for goods; she takes it in good faith, for value, before overdue, without notice, complete and regular. Ada is a "},{"text":"holder in due course with a good title","bold":true},{"text":". If the cheque was crossed "},{"text":"\"not negotiable\"","bold":true},{"text":", Ada gets "},{"text":"no better title than the thief had","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trap: noting, protest, crossing and countermand are not ways of dishonour."}]}]}]},{"number":"8.4","title":"Cheques: features, types, crossings and advantages","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Characteristics of a cheque (7):","bold":true},{"text":" (1) in writing; (2) unconditional order; (3) drawn on a specified banker; (4) signed by the drawer; (5) payable on demand; (6) sum certain in money (amount specified); (7) payable to a specified person or order, or to bearer. It needs "},{"text":"no acceptance","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Face of a completed cheque:","bold":true},{"text":" bank and branch, date, payee, amount in words and in figures, drawer's signature, cheque number and MICR code line. There is no acceptance signature."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Cheque versus time bill","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Cheque"}],[{"text":"Bill of exchange"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Drawee is always a banker"}],[{"text":"Drawee may be any person, including a banker"}]],[[{"text":"Always payable on demand"}],[{"text":"On demand or at a future time"}]],[[{"text":"No acceptance"}],[{"text":"Time bill is presented for acceptance"}]],[[{"text":"No days of grace"}],[{"text":"3 days of grace on time bills"}]],[[{"text":"Can be crossed"}],[{"text":"Not crossed"}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Similarities:","bold":true},{"text":" both are negotiable instruments, unconditional written orders, with three parties."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Types of cheque","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Open:","bold":true},{"text":" not crossed; can be cashed over the counter of the drawee bank."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Crossed:","bold":true},{"text":" two parallel transverse lines across the face; payable only through a bank account."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bearer:","bold":true},{"text":" payable to whoever presents it, without endorsement."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Order:","bold":true},{"text":" payable to the named payee or as he directs by endorsement."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Stale:","bold":true},{"text":" in circulation unreasonably long; Nigerian banks refuse a cheque more than "},{"text":"6 months","bold":true},{"text":" old."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Post-dated:","bold":true},{"text":" bears a future date; not paid before that date."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Undated:","bold":true},{"text":" not invalid in law, but bankers by custom refuse it until dated."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Certified:","bold":true},{"text":" marked by the drawee bank to confirm the drawer has sufficient funds."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Crossings","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"General:","bold":true},{"text":" two parallel lines, with or without \"& Co\"; pay only to a banker."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Special:","bold":true},{"text":" a bank's name between the lines (e.g. \"Guaranty Trust Bank Plc\"); pay "},{"text":"only to that bank","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"\"Not negotiable\":","bold":true},{"text":" a transferee gets no better title than his transferor."}]},{"text":[{"text":"\"Account payee only\":","bold":true},{"text":" a direction to the "},{"text":"collecting","bold":true},{"text":" bank to credit the proceeds only to the payee's account."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Who may cross:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"drawer or a holder","bold":true},{"text":"; a banker may also cross for collection. A crossing is a "},{"text":"material part","bold":true},{"text":" of the cheque and may not be unlawfully obliterated or altered."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Benefits of crossing:","bold":true},{"text":" payment goes through a bank account; protects the owner against theft; helps trace who received payment. It does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" let the payee cash faster over the counter; it stops that."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Advantages of cheques:","bold":true},{"text":" economise currency notes and coins; safety (no carrying cash); proof of payment; payment can be stopped against fraud; convenient for large and distant payments. A cheque does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" guarantee the drawer has funds; it can bounce. Issuing a cheque that bounces for lack of funds is a criminal offence under the Dishonoured Cheques (Offences) Act (Cap D11, LFN 2004)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Precautions in drawing a cheque:","bold":true},{"text":" write the amount in words and figures, and make them agree; leave no spaces; sign every alteration in full; date it; fill in the payee (never leave it blank); cross cheques sent by post; keep the cheque book safe."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Ngozi's cheque dated 2 January, presented 10 August: over 6 months, "},{"text":"refused as stale","bold":true},{"text":". A cheque dated 30 November presented on 15 November: "},{"text":"post-dated","bold":true},{"text":", refused until 30 November."}]}]}]},{"number":"8.5","title":"Paying, collecting and clearing cheques","mustKnow":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"When the bank's authority to pay ends (or it must not pay):","bold":true}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Insufficient funds (no balance or overdraft limit to cover it)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Countermand:","bold":true},{"text":" in writing, clear, signed in line with the mandate, "},{"text":"before","bold":true},{"text":" the cheque is paid."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Notice of the customer's "},{"text":"death","bold":true},{"text":" (express or constructive)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Suspicion that the cheque was lost or stolen (investigate first)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Assignment of the credit balance to a third party."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Court injunction or "},{"text":"garnishee order","bold":true},{"text":" (a court order attaching the balance to satisfy a judgment debt)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Notice of a "},{"text":"bankruptcy petition","bold":true},{"text":" (or a company's winding-up)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Forgery","bold":true},{"text":" of the drawer's signature."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Irregularly drawn cheque (e.g. words and figures differ)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Presentation outside banking hours or at the wrong branch."}]},{"text":[{"text":"A lawful order freezing the account."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Forgery:","bold":true},{"text":" the cheque is wholly inoperative; the bank has "},{"text":"no mandate","bold":true},{"text":" and cannot debit the customer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Wrongful dishonour:","bold":true},{"text":" the bank is liable to the customer in "},{"text":"damages for breach of contract","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Paying banker protection:","bold":true},{"text":" paying a crossed cheque according to the crossing, "},{"text":"in good faith and without negligence","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Collecting banker protection:","bold":true},{"text":" receiving payment for a "},{"text":"customer","bold":true},{"text":" in good faith and without negligence. Collecting for a non-customer, or without identity checks, loses protection; the bank becomes liable to the true owner."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clearing:","bold":true},{"text":" the procedure by which a bank collects the proceeds of a cheque drawn on another bank for its own customer. An "},{"text":"in-house (on-us)","bold":true},{"text":" cheque, drawn on and paid into the same bank, needs no interbank clearing."}]},{"text":[{"text":"MICR:","bold":true},{"text":" Magnetic Ink Character Recognition."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"A new cheque book does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" end authority to pay. Notice of death on Monday: a cheque presented Tuesday must "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" be paid, even if signed earlier. Proper return reasons: \"insufficient funds\", \"refer to drawer\", \"payment stopped\", \"account closed\"; a large amount or the payee banking elsewhere is no reason."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Before paying cash, a cashier checks:","bold":true},{"text":" regular and complete; drawn on that branch; not crossed; not post-dated; not stale; not stopped; properly endorsed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clearing history in Nigeria:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"1961: first bankers' clearing house, Lagos, run by the CBN (manual exchange)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"1988: MICR introduced in phases; conventional cheque exchange ended at the Lagos Clearing House on 1 July 1994."}]},{"text":[{"text":"2002: "},{"text":"Nigeria Automated Clearing System (NACS)","bold":true},{"text":", run by the Nigeria Inter-Bank Settlement System ("},{"text":"NIBSS","bold":true},{"text":")."}]},{"text":[{"text":"1 April 2004: new settlement framework (settlement and non-settlement banks)."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"NACS participants:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"Zonal Clearing House","bold":true},{"text":" (centre: registers participants, processes data, fixes settlement positions); "},{"text":"direct participating banks","bold":true},{"text":" (own clearing centre, the PBCC, with MICR reader/sorters, collateral in treasury bills); "},{"text":"indirect participating banks","bold":true},{"text":" (take part "},{"text":"through a direct participating bank","bold":true},{"text":")."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Current position:","bold":true},{"text":" the clearing cycle fell from 5 days to 3; cheque truncation (2012) then brought nationwide "},{"text":"T+1","bold":true},{"text":" (next-day) clearing from 2013, through NIBSS."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Bill = unconditional written "},{"text":"order","bold":true},{"text":"; note = "},{"text":"promise","bold":true},{"text":" (maker); cheque = bill drawn on a banker payable on demand; IOU = not negotiable."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Drawer writes, drawee pays (the bank for a cheque), payee receives; an accepting drawee becomes the acceptor."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Time bills: acceptance (general or qualified), "},{"text":"3 days of grace","bold":true},{"text":"; after-sight bills must be presented for acceptance."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Discount = Face x Rate x Days/365; proceeds = face - discount."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Holder in due course: regular, not overdue, good faith, value, no notice; gets a title free of defects."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Endorsements: blank (bearer), special, restrictive, conditional. Dishonour by non-acceptance or non-payment; foreign bills must be protested."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Crossings: general, special, \"not negotiable\", \"account payee\"; drawer or holder may cross."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Stale after 6 months; post-dated not paid early; undated not invalid."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Death, countermand, garnishee, bankruptcy and forgery end authority to pay; wrongful dishonour = damages."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clearing: Lagos 1961, MICR 1988, NACS (NIBSS) 2002, T+1 from 2013."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"b18a4814-169e-5a94-bd65-42e2358ab6f6","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"A bill of exchange for ₦3,650,000 dated 5 May is payable 90 days after date. The holder discounts it with her bank on 28 June at 10% per annum (365-day year). Find the due date and the proceeds."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","named":false}]}},{"itemId":"ede397d8-af01-5f95-a344-f31dc97cc099","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-topic:9","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"title":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","body":"Domestic methods; standing orders, direct debits and their computations; international methods and Nostro/Vostro; electronic banking, cards and Nigerian payment systems; the e-Naira.","noteType":"guide_topic","number":9,"mustKnow":[{"text":"Domestic methods; standing orders, direct debits and their computations; international methods and Nostro/Vostro; electronic banking, cards and Nigerian payment systems; the e-Naira."}],"lessons":[{"number":"9.1","title":"Domestic methods of payment","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Domestic methods to list:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Standing order","bold":true},{"text":" (Lesson 9.2)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Direct debit","bold":true},{"text":" (Lesson 9.2)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mail and telegraphic transfers","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank drafts","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank cheques","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Intra-bank transfers","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banker's payments","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Personal cheques","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Credit transfer (bank giro)","bold":true}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Personal cheque:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"simplest and most common","bold":true},{"text":" method of paying a debt within the economy. Its weakness: "},{"text":"fraud and dishonour","bold":true},{"text":", so many creditors prefer drafts or standing orders."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank draft:","bold":true},{"text":" a cheque "},{"text":"drawn by a branch of a bank on another branch or on the head office of the same bank","bold":true},{"text":", signed by the "},{"text":"bank's own authorised signatories","bold":true},{"text":". The issuing branch sends the paying branch an "},{"text":"advice","bold":true},{"text":" with the draft's particulars so the paying branch can "},{"text":"confirm the draft is genuine","bold":true},{"text":". Because it is a "},{"text":"bank's own instrument, it is not normally dishonoured","bold":true},{"text":". Used for large payments, locally and internationally."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank cheque","bold":true},{"text":" (also called a certified cheque): issued by the bank on the customer's written request (paid for in cash or by his own cheque) and cleared in the usual way. It is used "},{"text":"within the same clearing area only","bold":true},{"text":", while a "},{"text":"bank draft is used where other clearing areas are involved","bold":true},{"text":", so a bank cheque is of narrower use."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Mail transfer (MT) and telegraphic transfer (TT):","bold":true},{"text":" the customer gives written instructions stating the "},{"text":"beneficiary's name, the beneficiary's bank, the account number and the amount","bold":true},{"text":"; the bank debits him and pays. A "},{"text":"TT is faster","bold":true},{"text":" (cable, telex or electronic message) than an MT (post)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Intra-bank transfer:","bold":true},{"text":" a customer with "},{"text":"several accounts at the same bank","bold":true},{"text":" moves funds between them, e.g. from savings to current so that a cheque already issued can be paid."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banker's payment:","bold":true},{"text":" an instrument "},{"text":"banks use to pay each other","bold":true},{"text":", (1) for services between banks such as "},{"text":"cash supplied","bold":true},{"text":" by one bank to another, and (2) for the "},{"text":"proceeds of a cheque sent for special clearing","bold":true},{"text":". It passes through clearing: the issuing bank is debited, the receiving bank credited."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Credit transfer (bank giro):","bold":true},{"text":" the payer fills "},{"text":"credit (giro) slips","bold":true},{"text":" at his own bank, and the funds are credited directly to the payees' accounts at the same or other banks."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Trap: neither a bank cheque nor a draft is drawn by the customer on his own account or by the CBN; the tested difference is "},{"text":"same clearing area (bank cheque)","bold":true},{"text":" versus "},{"text":"other clearing areas (draft)","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Example: after heavy withdrawals, Bank X asks Bank Y for ₦50 million cash and settles with a banker's payment in favour of Bank Y; through clearing, Bank X is debited and Bank Y credited. In special clearing, the paying bank sends a banker's payment for the cheque's proceeds."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Domestic settlement between banks is "},{"text":"direct","bold":true},{"text":" (cheques, drafts or banker's payments) or "},{"text":"through the clearing house","bold":true},{"text":" (cheques, or credit and debit vouchers)."}]}]}]},{"number":"9.2","title":"Standing orders and direct debits","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Standing order:","bold":true},{"text":" an instruction by a customer to his bank to make "},{"text":"regular payments of a fixed amount","bold":true},{"text":" at weekly, monthly or quarterly intervals, from his account (current or savings), to a "},{"text":"named third party","bold":true},{"text":" at the same or another bank. It is signed "},{"text":"in line with the account mandate","bold":true},{"text":"; the bank acts as "},{"text":"agent","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Used for (6):","bold":true},{"text":" insurance premiums; regular subscriptions; house rents; hire purchase instalments; instalment loan repayments; school fees. (Not one-off payments.)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"Information the bank needs before opening one (11):","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"the customer's application"}]},{"text":[{"text":"name of the customer giving the order"}]},{"text":[{"text":"name of the beneficiary"}]},{"text":[{"text":"address of the beneficiary"}]},{"text":[{"text":"destination of the remittance"}]},{"text":[{"text":"mode of payment (air, sea, land, rail and so on)"}]},{"text":[{"text":"amount to be remitted"}]},{"text":[{"text":"intervals of payment"}]},{"text":[{"text":"customer's signature"}]},{"text":[{"text":"account number of the applicant"}]},{"text":[{"text":"account number of the beneficiary"}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Customer's duties (4):","bold":true},{"text":" give accurate information; keep sufficient funds; pay the bank's charges; "},{"text":"notify the bank promptly when the order is to be discontinued","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banker's duties (4):","bold":true},{"text":" handle the order with "},{"text":"diligence and care","bold":true},{"text":"; remit strictly according to the mandate, provided funds are available; warn the customer not to cause delay or non-payment (the bank is not responsible for these); send "},{"text":"periodic statements","bold":true},{"text":" to the customer."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banker's rights (2):","bold":true},{"text":" charge "},{"text":"commission","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"cancel","bold":true},{"text":" the order where the customer has caused non-payment on the due date "},{"text":"three consecutive times or more","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Advantages to the bank (2):","bold":true},{"text":" commission income; widens the range of services offered."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Advantages to the customer (4):","bold":true},{"text":" pays recurring items; provides a record of payment; cheap transmission charges; "},{"text":"the onus of paying on the due dates rests with the banker","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bank's considerations:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Insufficient funds:","bold":true},{"text":" the bank "},{"text":"is not obliged to pay and incurs no liability","bold":true},{"text":" for not paying."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Payee asks the bank to cancel:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"disregard it unless the customer confirms","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Payee refuses the payments:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"continue to make each payment as it falls due and notify the customer of each refusal","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Payee asks why a payment is late:","bold":true},{"text":" do not answer; "},{"text":"advise the payee to contact the customer","bold":true},{"text":" (secrecy)."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"The bank's obligation to pay stops when (4):","bold":true},{"text":" funds are insufficient; the balance is insufficient and there is "},{"text":"no credit (overdraft) arrangement","bold":true},{"text":"; the customer "},{"text":"countermands","bold":true},{"text":"; a "},{"text":"garnishee order","bold":true},{"text":" stops payments out of the account."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Direct debit:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"beneficiary (creditor) initiates","bold":true},{"text":" the payment. The paying customer endorses (authorises) the creditor's document, and the creditor then sends signed debit claims to the bank "},{"text":"from time to time","bold":true},{"text":". Used by "},{"text":"insurance companies and finance houses","bold":true},{"text":"; suits "},{"text":"variable amounts","bold":true},{"text":"; the customer can cancel the authority."}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Point"}],[{"text":"Standing order"}],[{"text":"Direct debit"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Started by"}],[{"text":"the customer (payer)"}],[{"text":"the beneficiary (creditor), with the payer's signed authority"}]],[[{"text":"Amount"}],[{"text":"fixed"}],[{"text":"fixed or variable"}]],[[{"text":"Typical user"}],[{"text":"individual paying rent, fees"}],[{"text":"insurers, finance houses, clubs"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Paying ₦45,000 rent to a landlord on the first of every month \"until further notice\" is a standing order. An insurer sending signed debit claims under a policyholder's endorsed authority uses a direct debit; so should a gym whose fees change monthly, because a standing order is for a fixed amount."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Traps: \"the beneficiary writes to cancel\" does "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" end the duty to pay (only the customer cancels); sending statements and taking care are the "},{"text":"bank's","bold":true},{"text":" duties, notifying discontinuance is the "},{"text":"customer's","bold":true},{"text":"; the beneficiary's income is never needed."}]}]}]},{"number":"9.3","title":"Standing order computations","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Total debit","bold":true},{"text":" = standing order amount + commission (rate × amount) + postage."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Balance before","bold":true},{"text":" = opening balance (CR +, DR −) + credits − debits. "},{"text":"Balance after","bold":true},{"text":" = balance before − total debit."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Decision rule:","bold":true},{"text":" pay if the balance after is CR or a DR "},{"text":"within the approved overdraft limit","bold":true},{"text":"; otherwise the bank "},{"text":"does not pay","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Annual commission","bold":true},{"text":" = rate × amount × number of payments in the year."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 1 (Mr Emeka).","bold":true},{"text":" Standing order ₦5,000 due at month end. Opening balance ₦1,000 DR; cheque paid in ₦8,000; cheque book ₦100; cheque issued to Ben ₦1,950; cash lodged ₦920. Charges: ½% commission and ₦50 postage. Overdraft limit ₦1,000."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Debits: ₦1,000 (opening DR) + ₦100 + ₦1,950 = ₦3,050. Credits: ₦8,000 + ₦920 = ₦8,920."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Balance before: ₦8,920 − ₦3,050 = "},{"text":"₦5,870 CR","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Commission 0.5% × ₦5,000 = ₦25; total debit ₦5,000 + ₦25 + ₦50 = ₦5,075."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Balance after: ₦5,870 − ₦5,075 = "},{"text":"₦795 CR","bold":true},{"text":", so the bank "},{"text":"pays","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 2 (total debit).","bold":true},{"text":" ₦12,000 at ½% plus ₦100 postage: commission 0.5% × ₦12,000 = ₦60; total debit ₦12,000 + ₦60 + ₦100 = "},{"text":"₦12,160","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 3 (within the limit).","bold":true},{"text":" Balance ₦4,000 CR; order ₦4,500; ½% and ₦50; limit ₦1,000. Charges ₦22.50 + ₦50 = ₦72.50; total debit ₦4,572.50; balance after ₦4,000 − ₦4,572.50 = "},{"text":"₦572.50 DR","bold":true},{"text":", inside the limit, so the bank "},{"text":"pays","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 4 (beyond the limit).","bold":true},{"text":" Balance ₦3,000 CR; order ₦5,000; ½% and ₦50; limit ₦1,500. Total debit ₦5,075; balance after ₦3,000 − ₦5,075 = "},{"text":"₦2,075 DR","bold":true},{"text":", beyond ₦1,500, so the bank "},{"text":"does not pay","bold":true},{"text":". (Forgetting charges gives ₦2,000 DR, the trap.)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 5 (1% commission).","bold":true},{"text":" Opening ₦2,500 CR; in ₦15,000 and ₦1,800; out ₦6,200 and ₦200. Balance before ₦12,900 CR; total debit ₦10,000 + ₦100 + ₦50 = ₦10,150; after "},{"text":"₦2,750 CR","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Worked example 6 (annual commission).","bold":true},{"text":" ₦25,000 monthly at ½%: ₦125 per payment × 12 = "},{"text":"₦1,500","bold":true},{"text":" (₦125 is one month only)."}]}]},{"number":"9.4","title":"International methods of payment and settlement","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"International methods (6):","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Payment in advance:","bold":true},{"text":" the importer pays before shipment (safest for the exporter)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Open account:","bold":true},{"text":" the exporter sends the "},{"text":"documents of title direct to the importer","bold":true},{"text":", loses control of the goods, and relies on the importer's honesty to remit after selling. "},{"text":"Riskiest","bold":true},{"text":" for the exporter; discouraged."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Clean collection:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"only the bill of exchange","bold":true},{"text":" is sent to the bank for collection."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Documentary collection:","bold":true},{"text":" the bill is sent "},{"text":"with commercial documents","bold":true},{"text":" (bill of lading, insurance certificate or policy). Still not certain, because the importer may not pay."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Documentary letter of credit (LC):","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"surest","bold":true},{"text":" method; a "},{"text":"bank's undertaking to pay","bold":true},{"text":" the exporter "},{"text":"provided he complies with the terms","bold":true},{"text":" of the credit."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bill negotiation:","bold":true},{"text":" the exporter needing cash now "},{"text":"endorses and delivers","bold":true},{"text":" the bill to his bank, which pays the "},{"text":"face value less a discount","bold":true},{"text":" for commission and interest and collects the full amount at maturity."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"Parties to an LC:","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"issuing bank","bold":true},{"text":" = the "},{"text":"importer's bank","bold":true},{"text":", which opens the credit in the exporter's favour; "},{"text":"advising bank","bold":true},{"text":" = the "},{"text":"exporter's bank","bold":true},{"text":", which tells the exporter the credit has been opened."}]},{"text":[{"text":"D/P (documents against payment):","bold":true},{"text":" documents of title released when the bill is "},{"text":"paid","bold":true},{"text":". "},{"text":"D/A (documents against acceptance):","bold":true},{"text":" released when the bill is "},{"text":"accepted","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"In all these methods, the actual money moves by "},{"text":"mail transfer, telegraphic transfer or bank draft","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nostro","bold":true},{"text":" (\"our\" in Latin): an account a "},{"text":"Nigerian bank keeps with a correspondent bank abroad","bold":true},{"text":", in foreign currency, to receive export proceeds and make foreign payments."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Vostro","bold":true},{"text":" (\"your\" in Latin): an account a "},{"text":"foreign bank keeps with a Nigerian bank","bold":true},{"text":", in naira, to settle its obligations in Nigeria."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Bill negotiation:","bold":true},{"text":" amount received = face value − (discount rate × face value)."}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Exporter's risk, least to most: payment in advance, letter of credit, documentary collection, clean collection, open account."}]},{"text":[{"text":"LC process: sale agreed; issuing bank opens the credit; advising bank informs the exporter; goods shipped; documents released only on payment or acceptance, so the exporter keeps control."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Point of view decides the name: a London bank's naira account with Zenith Bank in Lagos is a "},{"text":"Vostro","bold":true},{"text":" account to Zenith Bank (and a Nostro account to the London bank)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Worked example (bill negotiation, bank figures).","bold":true},{"text":" Face value ₦800,000; discount 2.5%. Discount = 0.025 × ₦800,000 = ₦20,000. Exporter receives ₦800,000 − ₦20,000 = "},{"text":"₦780,000","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"number":"9.5","title":"Electronic banking, cards and the Nigerian payment system","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Electronic banking:","bold":true},{"text":" delivering banking services through electronic channels: "},{"text":"payment cards","bold":true},{"text":" (credit, cheque, charge, debit and cash cards), "},{"text":"electronic funds transfer, ATMs, POS terminals, telephone and internet (home) banking","bold":true},{"text":". Over-the-counter cash withdrawal with a signed slip is "},{"text":"not","bold":true},{"text":" e-banking."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Problems with cash that pushed banks to alternatives (4):","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"the "},{"text":"falling value of the naira","bold":true},{"text":" means large sums must be carried"}]},{"text":[{"text":"single over-the-counter transactions can run into millions of naira"}]},{"text":[{"text":"carrying cash invites "},{"text":"robbery and loss of life","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"banks spend heavily on "},{"text":"staff and note-counting machines","bold":true}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"E-banking products in Nigeria (4):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"electronic smart card; electronic funds transfer; telephone banking; internet banking.","bold":true}]},{"text":[{"text":"Smart card:","bold":true},{"text":" looks like a credit card but has an "},{"text":"intelligent memory chip","bold":true},{"text":" that stores value and keeps an "},{"text":"up-to-date record of the transactions","bold":true},{"text":" made with it. Called a "},{"text":"\"cashless wallet\"","bold":true},{"text":". Value can be loaded and withdrawn."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Electronic funds transfer (EFT):","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"electronically recorded pre-authorised debits and credits","bold":true},{"text":" that "},{"text":"replace paper cheques","bold":true},{"text":". Three areas: between financial institutions; wholesale (corporate), e.g. the "},{"text":"direct deposit (payroll) programme","bold":true},{"text":" paying salaries straight into staff accounts; retail (consumer): electronic bill payment, "},{"text":"POS using a debit card","bold":true},{"text":", and cash-based money transfer (Western Union, MoneyGram)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"ATM services (5):","bold":true},{"text":" cash withdrawal; balance enquiry; statement request; cash deposit; "},{"text":"transfer between accounts","bold":true},{"text":". Operated with a card "},{"text":"plus a secret personal identification number (PIN)","bold":true},{"text":". Not done at an ATM: approving loans, opening fixed deposits, granting unapproved overdrafts."}]},{"text":[{"text":"ATM advantages:","bold":true},{"text":" cash outside banking hours; shorter queues in banking halls; time saved for customers' own business."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cards:","bold":true}],"blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Debit card:","bold":true},{"text":" payment taken "},{"text":"directly from the holder's own account","bold":true},{"text":" at the time of use."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Credit card:","bold":true},{"text":" a "},{"text":"revolving credit line","bold":true},{"text":"; pay a minimum and carry the rest with interest."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Charge card:","bold":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"full balance must be settled at the end of each billing period","bold":true},{"text":"."}]}]}]},{"text":[{"text":"SWIFT = Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication:","bold":true},{"text":" a non-profit, cooperative message network "},{"text":"based in Belgium","bold":true},{"text":". It carries "},{"text":"secure payment instructions, not the funds","bold":true},{"text":"; money settles through correspondent (Nostro/Vostro) accounts. Its premium is "},{"text":"security","bold":true},{"text":", not speed."}]},{"text":[{"text":"NIBSS (Nigeria Inter-Bank Settlement System):","bold":true},{"text":" runs "},{"text":"NIP (NIBSS Instant Payment)","bold":true},{"text":", which credits beneficiaries "},{"text":"in real time","bold":true},{"text":", and "},{"text":"NEFT (Nigeria Electronic Funds Transfer)","bold":true},{"text":", which works in "},{"text":"batches","bold":true},{"text":" with a longer delay. NIBSS also runs automated cheque clearing."}]},{"text":[{"text":"RTGS (Real-Time Gross Settlement):","bold":true},{"text":" run by the "},{"text":"CBN","bold":true},{"text":"; "},{"text":"each payment is settled individually and immediately","bold":true},{"text":" across banks' accounts, mainly for "},{"text":"large-value","bold":true},{"text":" transfers."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cashless policy:","bold":true},{"text":" the CBN policy to reduce cash in circulation. "},{"text":"First piloted in Lagos State from 1 January 2012.","bold":true}]}]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Internet banking:","bold":true},{"text":" the bank's website for balances, history and transfers from home or office. "},{"text":"USSD","bold":true},{"text":" (dial a short code such as *XYZ#) is popular in Nigeria because it "},{"text":"works on basic phones without internet data","bold":true},{"text":"; mobile apps need a smartphone."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Paying at a Shoprite till with a debit card and PIN uses a "},{"text":"POS terminal","bold":true},{"text":". The "},{"text":"BVN (Bank Verification Number)","bold":true},{"text":" identifies a customer across banks; it is not the PIN, and a "},{"text":"sort code","bold":true},{"text":" identifies a bank branch."}]},{"text":[{"text":"The cashless policy later went nationwide. "},{"text":"Current position (from 1 January 2026):","bold":true},{"text":" no cash-deposit limit; weekly cash withdrawals capped at "},{"text":"₦500,000 for individuals and ₦5 million for corporates","bold":true},{"text":", with "},{"text":"3% and 5%","bold":true},{"text":" charged on the excess."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trap: \"cash leaves an electronic record\" and \"cash has no handling cost\" are false, so they are never problems of cash."}]}]}]},{"number":"9.6","title":"The e-Naira (central bank digital currency)","mustKnow":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Central bank digital currency (CBDC):","bold":true},{"text":" an "},{"text":"electronic form of central bank money","bold":true},{"text":" that people use to make digital payments and which can also act as a "},{"text":"store of value","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"e-Naira:","bold":true},{"text":" Nigeria's CBDC, developed under "},{"text":"Project Giant","bold":true},{"text":"; the CBN announced its technical partner for the project on "},{"text":"30 August 2021","bold":true},{"text":"; officially "},{"text":"launched in October 2021","bold":true},{"text":" (25 October 2021), the first CBDC in Africa."}]},{"text":[{"text":"It is "},{"text":"legal tender","bold":true},{"text":", issued by the CBN, and "},{"text":"complements (does not replace) the cash naira","bold":true},{"text":" at "},{"text":"one e-Naira = one naira","bold":true},{"text":"."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Benefits:","bold":true},{"text":" digital-economy catalyst; better monetary policy (payment data); "},{"text":"financial inclusion","bold":true},{"text":"; safer payments; cheaper cross-border trade; banking stability; "},{"text":"lower naira printing cost","bold":true},{"text":"; less abuse of the naira, theft and disease from dirty notes."}]},{"text":[{"text":"CBDC versus cryptocurrency (4):","bold":true}]}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Point"}],[{"text":"CBDC (e-Naira)"}],[{"text":"Cryptocurrency (e.g. Bitcoin)"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Issuer"}],[{"text":"central bank"}],[{"text":"privately created by users"}]],[[{"text":"Legal tender"}],[{"text":"yes"}],[{"text":"no"}]],[[{"text":"Volatility"}],[{"text":"low (fixed 1:1 with the naira)"}],[{"text":"high"}]],[[{"text":"Legal backing"}],[{"text":"backed by law (state)"}],[{"text":"not backed by law"}]]]}],"explainMore":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Challenges: cyber fraud, a foreign technology provider, public enlightenment, teething problems."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Trap: making the naira "},{"text":"more volatile","bold":true},{"text":" for speculation is never a benefit; CBDCs are designed to avoid volatility."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Do not confuse dates: "},{"text":"January 2012","bold":true},{"text":" is the cashless pilot in Lagos; "},{"text":"October 2021","bold":true},{"text":" is the e-Naira launch."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Personal cheque = simplest domestic method; draft = branch on branch/head office, rarely dishonoured; bank cheque = same clearing area only."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Banker's payment = bank pays bank; intra-bank transfer = between a customer's own accounts."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Standing order = customer starts, fixed amount; direct debit = beneficiary starts under the payer's authority, can be variable."}]},{"text":[{"text":"No funds, no overdraft, countermand or garnishee order ends the duty to pay; a payee cannot cancel."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Total debit = amount + commission + postage; pay only if the balance after stays CR or within the overdraft limit."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Open account riskiest; letter of credit surest; issuing = importer's bank, advising = exporter's bank; D/P against payment, D/A against acceptance."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Nostro = \"our\" account abroad; Vostro = \"your\" account with us. SWIFT sends messages, not money."}]},{"text":[{"text":"ATM = card + PIN; debit card = own account now; charge card = settle in full. NIP real time, NEFT batch, RTGS large-value; cashless pilot Lagos, 1 January 2012."}]},{"text":[{"text":"e-Naira launched October 2021, legal tender, 1:1 with the naira, complements cash."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"2bb50407-c66f-50dc-bf77-5c088c5cd6e4","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Mrs Bello's account opened the month at ₦2,000 DR. She paid in a cheque of ₦9,500, issued a cheque of ₦4,800 and was charged ₦150 for a cheque book. Her standing order of ₦6,000 falls due at month end; the bank charges ½% commission and ₦50 postage, and her overdraft limit is ₦2,000. Will the bank pay?"}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","named":false}]}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'elements-banking: note published payload and identity match source');
select is((select count(*)::int from public.read_published_managed_content('58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed')
  where kind = 'cbt_question'), 0,
  'elements-banking: CBT questions are withheld from the student content read');
reset role;
select ok((select coalesce(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_object(
  'itemId', item_id, 'courseId', course_id, 'kind', kind,
  'questionId', question_id, 'sourceKey', source_key,
  'parentItemId', parent_item_id, 'revision', revision,
  'payload', payload) order by source_key collate "C"), '[]'::jsonb)
  from (select i.id item_id, i.course_id, i.kind, i.question_id, i.source_key, i.parent_item_id,
    i.published_revision revision, r.payload from public.managed_content_items i
    join public.managed_content_revisions r on r.item_id = i.id and r.revision = i.published_revision
    where i.course_id = '58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed' and i.published_revision is not null) published
  where kind = 'cbt_question') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"b75dc465-aed0-584b-a952-452b8810e29f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"84492da1-b4b5-551d-81ab-b259e8558f23","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ikeja Trust Bank has total deposit liabilities of N100 billion. Its qualifying liquid assets are cash N6 billion, Treasury bills N20 billion and net interbank placements N4 billion. What is its liquidity ratio?","options":["30%","20%","26%","3.33%"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The liquidity ratio is qualifying liquid assets divided by deposit liabilities: (6 + 20 + 4) / 100 = 30%. Option C leaves out the interbank placements, and option B counts only the Treasury bills. Option D turns the ratio upside down, dividing deposits by liquid assets."}},{"itemId":"43317b7c-4d66-59c3-8a10-fcd473dd961c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8af135a1-6ca4-5948-bd06-ab6db49a00a5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Lagos bank has deposit liabilities of N250 billion and holds N60 billion in qualifying liquid assets. If the required minimum liquidity ratio is 30%, by how much is the bank short of the requirement?","options":["N18 billion","N75 billion","N15 billion","N0, because it already meets the requirement"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The bank must hold 30% x N250 billion = N75 billion in liquid assets, but it holds only N60 billion, so it is N15 billion short. Option B is the total required, not the shortfall. Option A wrongly applies 30% to the N60 billion the bank already holds, and option D ignores that N60 billion is below N75 billion."}},{"itemId":"117782a6-fdde-587f-a8d2-89fc963c25fe","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7bfc7515-825b-518f-bffe-79417246eafe","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Assume the Central Bank of Nigeria requires commercial banks to keep a cash reserve ratio of 45% of their eligible deposits. A commercial bank with eligible deposits of N80 billion must keep how much as cash reserves with the CBN?","options":["N3.6 billion","N36 billion","N44 billion","N177.8 billion"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Required cash reserves equal the cash reserve ratio times eligible deposits: 45% x N80 billion = N36 billion. Option C is the 55% of deposits left after the reserve, and option A misplaces the decimal point. Option D divides deposits by the ratio instead of multiplying."}},{"itemId":"053aa8e0-373e-5ae8-b64c-53ee7c86b4e0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9a53f3cb-5b49-59e2-a535-81a232445481","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Oshodi Bank has deposits of N500 million and total reserves of N130 million. If the required reserve ratio is 20%, what are its excess reserves?","options":["N130 million","N100 million","N30 million","N26 million"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Excess reserves are total reserves minus required reserves: required reserves are 20% x N500 million = N100 million, so N130 million - N100 million = N30 million. Option B is the required reserve itself, and option A is total reserves. Option D wrongly takes 20% of the reserves instead of 20% of deposits."}},{"itemId":"e2a41fb6-b0cd-5c18-aeb1-3118f6d40b05","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c12ff0e4-4418-53fa-b84b-e98bf0c440d2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A single bank receives a fresh cash deposit of N10 million. The required reserve ratio is 25% and the bank had no excess reserves before. What is the maximum amount of new loans this one bank can safely grant out of the deposit?","options":["N7.5 million","N40 million","N10 million","N2.5 million"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"A single bank can lend only its excess reserves: it must keep 25% x N10 million = N2.5 million, leaving N7.5 million to lend. Option D is the amount it must keep, not lend, and option C ignores the reserve requirement. Option B is the money-multiplier figure (N10 million / 0.25) for the whole banking system, not what one bank can lend."}},{"itemId":"7580b145-4f75-53e0-8800-e7e2cbf64dc7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"302a4f59-93cb-5e36-9e1f-5ec9dce28762","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"At the end of the year, a deposit money bank reports loans and advances of N420 billion and total deposits of N600 billion. What is its loans-to-deposits ratio?","options":["30%","142.9%","70%","41.2%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The loans-to-deposits ratio is loans and advances divided by deposits: 420 / 600 = 70%. Option B divides deposits by loans, and option A is the share of deposits not lent out. Option D wrongly divides loans by the sum of loans and deposits (420 / 1,020)."}},{"itemId":"dfcca400-bbcf-5c01-bab8-07b4afb7d9e0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c377df4a-cf01-5f40-a202-19bd682d7177","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank has rate-sensitive assets of N300 billion and rate-sensitive liabilities of N380 billion for the next 12 months. What is its repricing gap, and how is the bank described?","options":["-N80 billion; asset-sensitive","-N80 billion; liability-sensitive","N680 billion; fully hedged","+N80 billion; asset-sensitive"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The repricing gap is rate-sensitive assets minus rate-sensitive liabilities: 300 - 380 = -N80 billion. A negative gap means more liabilities than assets reprice in the period, so the bank is liability-sensitive. Option D reverses the subtraction, option A pairs the right number with the wrong label, and option C adds the two figures together."}},{"itemId":"ca9c4112-c015-56d8-a854-09dc40f2dcb8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"436094bb-1218-5147-87b6-87ce93c69ec5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Yaba Bank has a positive repricing gap of N50 billion over one year. If market interest rates rise by 2 percentage points across the board, the expected change in its net interest income is:","options":["An increase of N100 million","An increase of N10 billion","An increase of N1 billion","A decrease of N1 billion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Change in net interest income = gap x change in rate = N50 billion x 0.02 = +N1 billion. With a positive gap, more assets than liabilities reprice, so rising rates raise interest income faster than interest expense. Option D has the right size but the wrong direction, while options B and A misplace the decimal point."}},{"itemId":"d5cbcf15-dc6f-5dc8-acaa-d20eb7a03f56","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a2625c8d-ba3a-5adb-ba24-730edcd6fcd3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank's one-year repricing gap is -N80 billion. If interest rates rise by 1.5 percentage points, its net interest income will change by about:","options":["-N120 million","-N12 billion","-N1.2 billion","+N1.2 billion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Multiply the gap by the change in rates: -N80 billion x 0.015 = -N1.2 billion. A negative gap means the bank's deposits and borrowings reprice faster than its loans, so a rate rise squeezes net interest income. Option D has the wrong sign, and options B and A misplace the decimal point."}},{"itemId":"c5a7e4fc-d88e-57c1-a813-aa522ffe222f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"001312cd-05a4-51c2-a739-0d3acca3f161","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank has capital of N12 billion. Its assets are government securities N40 billion (risk weight 0%), residential mortgages N20 billion (risk weight 50%) and corporate loans N70 billion (risk weight 100%). What is its capital to risk-weighted assets ratio?","options":["17.14%","13.33%","15%","9.23%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Risk-weighted assets are (40 x 0) + (20 x 0.5) + (70 x 1.0) = N80 billion, so the ratio is 12 / 80 = 15%. Option D divides capital by total unweighted assets of N130 billion. Option B wrongly gives the mortgages a full 100% weight (12 / 90), and option A counts only the corporate loans (12 / 70)."}},{"itemId":"cf4a702d-a6c0-5872-81d7-b72b0570d2d1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7651aeaf-dd69-5df0-b095-9973f5bc59e7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Surulere Bank has deposit liabilities of N200 billion and qualifying liquid assets of N70 billion. With a required minimum liquidity ratio of 30%, what is the largest amount of its liquid assets it can switch into new loans and still meet the requirement?","options":["N10 billion","N70 billion","N49 billion","N60 billion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The bank must keep 30% x N200 billion = N60 billion in liquid assets, so only the N10 billion above that can be lent out. Option B would leave it with no liquid assets at all, and option D is the amount it must keep, not the surplus. Option C wrongly takes 70% of its liquid assets."}},{"itemId":"81571fa6-b110-5802-9de5-ca0ee3e01774","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b50f325d-6466-5baf-a3e5-335f8247ca59","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank with deposits of N400 million holds exactly the required reserves of N40 million (reserve ratio 10%). A customer then withdraws N20 million in cash. What is the bank's reserve deficiency after the withdrawal?","options":["N2 million","Nil, because required reserves also fall","N20 million","N18 million"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"After the withdrawal deposits are N380 million, so required reserves are 10% x 380 = N38 million, but actual reserves have fallen to N40 - 20 = N20 million, a deficiency of N18 million. Option C ignores that the requirement itself falls by N2 million. Option A is only that fall in the requirement, and option B forgets that reserves fell by the full N20 million."}},{"itemId":"c45e77fd-c221-527b-a2b8-23b0ba66ebd6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"70c969c1-b63c-57c9-8699-e6e139e8e9dc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking, the conversion of current assets into cash within a short period of time without appreciable loss of value is known as:","options":["Liquidity","Profitability","Solvency","Capital adequacy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Liquidity is the ease of turning assets into cash quickly without losing much value; for a bank it means being able to pay depositors on demand. Option C, solvency, is about assets exceeding liabilities in the long run. Option B is about earning income, and option D is about holding enough capital against risk."}},{"itemId":"228a65de-feaa-56db-863f-8cb32dd62e9a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"789adda8-b959-5e91-a546-5e05ffd41409","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank's total assets exceed its total liabilities by a comfortable margin, but most of its assets are long-term loans, and it cannot pay all the depositors who come to withdraw today. The bank is best described as:","options":["Insolvent and illiquid","Solvent but illiquid","Liquid but insolvent","Both liquid and solvent"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Assets exceeding liabilities means the bank is solvent, but being unable to meet today's withdrawals means it is illiquid. Option C reverses the two ideas. Option D ignores the failed withdrawals, and option A ignores that its assets cover its liabilities."}},{"itemId":"fe5e6103-e4ae-5f16-a311-a32a0a5f4e17","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7134c69e-0a52-5a82-b519-f31c543c4e35","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a reason why a deposit money bank maintains an adequate level of liquidity?","options":["To meet the statutory liquidity requirements of the CBN","To meet the daily cash withdrawals of customers","To pay dividends that satisfy shareholders","To maintain the unshaken confidence of the public"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Satisfying shareholders with dividends is a reason for pursuing profitability, not liquidity. Options B, A and D are all genuine reasons for holding liquidity: meeting withdrawals on demand, obeying the CBN's liquidity ratio or cash ratio, and keeping public confidence."}},{"itemId":"8463fd99-a7a0-511d-a67c-fde3c51f243c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6176f93e-2c76-5b5f-9d16-08c2a3a8f6ea","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a reason why a bank pursues profitability?","options":["Retained profit serves as a source of capital that strengthens the bank","It keeps the bank within the CBN's cash reserve ratio","It enables the bank to meet petty cash expenses","It allows current account holders to withdraw without notice"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Profit ploughed back into the business builds up the bank's capital, which is one of the main reasons banks seek profit. Options D and C describe reasons for holding liquidity, not for earning profit. Option B is a regulatory reserve requirement, which actually reduces earning assets."}},{"itemId":"af675fa3-03fd-5a7f-9eca-f2c738be124c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5a3d7d79-62c0-5905-93bd-ca6fe5421b23","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a bank keeps more cash in its vaults than it needs, the main cost to the bank is:","options":["The higher interest it must pay on current accounts","The interest or investment income it could have earned on those funds","The rise in its capital adequacy requirement","The penalty charged by the CBN for holding excess cash"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Idle cash earns nothing, so its cost is the opportunity cost: the interest it would have earned if placed with other banks or invested in government securities. There is no CBN penalty for holding extra cash (option D), and cash carries no capital charge (option C). Current accounts are generally non-interest-bearing, so option A is wrong."}},{"itemId":"4ed0ccbc-fc81-5dc2-8cc2-a797c937eacf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2037d8b0-70b9-5472-b493-c0cc0d419799","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The basic problem of asset and liability portfolio management in a commercial bank is best stated as:","options":["Lending out every naira of deposits to maximise interest income","Balancing the competing goals of liquidity, profitability and solvency","Maximising the number of branches the bank operates","Keeping all deposits as cash so that no depositor is ever turned away"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"A bank must earn profit, yet remain liquid enough to pay depositors and solvent enough to absorb losses; striking a balance among these is its central problem. Option D would kill profitability, and option A would destroy liquidity and safety. Option C has nothing to do with the portfolio of assets and liabilities."}},{"itemId":"b966db9f-95e7-527f-bcda-bd6034a9be5c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"69c89233-bfcd-5ed1-bb47-e8fa44785e1e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bank A moves N20 billion from short-term Treasury bills into three-year corporate loans. Compared with before, the bank is most likely to be:","options":["Less profitable and less risky","More liquid and less profitable","Equally liquid, since both are earning assets","More profitable but less liquid and more exposed to credit risk"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Corporate loans usually earn more than Treasury bills but cannot be turned into cash quickly and carry default risk, so profit rises while liquidity and safety fall. Options B and A get the trade-off backwards. Option C is wrong because Treasury bills are highly liquid and can be rediscounted with the CBN, while term loans are not."}},{"itemId":"596f59ce-bb48-5261-961a-d62a3231e465","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf37aafc-4f69-553d-95bc-174338a8957f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The asset of a deposit money bank that represents its risk assets, generates most of its revenue and earns a higher rate than government securities is:","options":["Treasury bills","Loans and advances","Fixed assets","Cash and short-term funds"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Loans and advances are the credit facilities a bank grants to customers; they are its risk assets and produce the bulk of its income, which is why their interest rate is higher. Option A is a safe, liquid short-term investment with a lower yield. Option D earns little or nothing, and option C supports operations rather than generating interest."}},{"itemId":"c83e7556-557a-57d8-a96a-5ee9c7404b23","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2b2563b5-6219-581e-8417-b7c2b92bf303","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The approach in which a bank treats its deposits and other liabilities as largely given and concentrates on choosing the mix of cash, securities and loans to meet its liquidity and profit goals is known as:","options":["Credit rationing","Capital management","Asset management","Liability management"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Asset management stores liquidity on the asset side: the bank decides how much to keep in cash, near-cash securities and loans while taking its funding as given. Option D does the opposite, actively buying funds to meet liquidity needs. Option B is about the level of owners' funds, and option A is about limiting loans to borrowers."}},{"itemId":"39d80676-c531-5382-9fcc-d2a5a018e037","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"866f01f2-ed0d-5306-b271-22ecd923a3b5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Faced with a sudden surge in loan requests, a Nigerian bank borrows N15 billion overnight in the interbank market and issues certificates of deposit to companies instead of selling its investments. This practice is:","options":["Shiftability","The real bills doctrine","Liability management","Asset management"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Liability management means meeting liquidity needs by actively buying funds, for example interbank borrowing and issuing certificates of deposit. Option D would instead involve running down cash or selling securities. Option A relies on selling or shifting assets, and option B is about lending only short-term self-liquidating trade loans."}},{"itemId":"c1ef1d45-e311-5775-bbcc-54ca0107d92c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7c8d6ed0-f271-5a93-94e8-b9ad8a1dbc24","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The coordinated management of both sides of a bank's balance sheet, so that the mix, maturity and pricing of assets and liabilities together achieve the bank's liquidity, profitability and risk targets, is called:","options":["Asset-liability management","Treasury bill management","Credit administration","Branch expansion planning"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Asset-liability management (ALM) looks at assets and liabilities together, managing their mix, maturities and interest rates to balance liquidity, profit and risk. Option C deals only with granting and monitoring loans, and option B with one type of investment. Option D is a growth decision, not balance sheet risk management."}},{"itemId":"9b044141-fb26-5c3a-8344-83a13709c0e5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd4ada41-c7a9-5f0c-9f75-b9c6841ca9d5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a bank, the Asset and Liability Committee (ALCO) is best described as:","options":["A CBN department that inspects banks' liquidity returns","A unit of the NDIC that pays insured depositors of failed banks","A committee of shareholders that approves the annual dividend","A committee of senior management that sets and monitors the bank's balance sheet strategy, including liquidity, interest rate gaps and pricing of loans and deposits"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"ALCO is an internal committee of the bank's top managers that reviews the balance sheet and decides how to manage liquidity and interest rate risk and how to price deposits and loans. Option A is an external regulator, not part of the bank. Options C and B describe shareholder and deposit insurance functions that have nothing to do with ALCO."}},{"itemId":"cc0c87c9-4ec2-5c13-aeff-4eb1423dda0b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e287073d-f1ff-594d-ae2a-532f41a7f176","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"At its monthly meeting, a group of a bank's executive directors reviews the maturity profile of deposits, sets the rates the bank will offer on fixed deposits and decides to reduce the bank's negative repricing gap. This group is the bank's:","options":["Asset and Liability Committee","Audit Committee","Credit Committee","Board Remuneration Committee"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Reviewing the maturity mix, pricing deposits and managing the repricing gap are classic tasks of the Asset and Liability Committee (ALCO). Option B checks financial reporting and internal controls. Option C approves individual loans, and option D sets pay for directors."}},{"itemId":"ac480109-ef6b-581b-a3f2-8d537b3c4562","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f34b2ac8-f16f-51f3-93b3-b5a911cb6444","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the traditional priorities for allocating a commercial bank's funds, the FIRST claim on the funds goes to:","options":["Loans and advances to customers","Secondary reserves","Long-term investments for income","Primary reserves"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Primary reserves (cash in vault, balances with the central bank and cheques in course of collection) come first because the bank must meet legal reserve requirements and daily withdrawals before anything else. Option B comes second, protecting liquidity while earning some income. Options A and C come only after liquidity needs are met."}},{"itemId":"65d44fe8-b75a-5bbf-b42a-37bdc38909fe","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8e0cc978-fad8-5b24-8d29-c6ec10ac140d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which sequence correctly shows the traditional priorities in allocating a commercial bank's funds?","options":["Primary reserves, secondary reserves, loans and advances, investments for income","Secondary reserves, primary reserves, investments for income, loans and advances","Loans and advances, primary reserves, secondary reserves, investments for income","Investments for income, loans and advances, secondary reserves, primary reserves"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Funds go first to primary reserves for legal and day-to-day liquidity, then to secondary reserves as a liquid earning buffer, then to loans to meet customers' credit needs, and only the remainder to investments for income. Option C puts lending ahead of liquidity. Options D and B reverse or scramble the order so that liquidity is not protected first."}},{"itemId":"a441f32d-96f6-5bac-a0b0-b440d3d3a651","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b68bf989-8f38-5bc2-901a-1c89e2608e01","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a typical secondary reserve asset of a Nigerian deposit money bank?","options":["Treasury bills","Land and buildings","Cash in the bank's vault","Long-term mortgage loans"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Secondary reserves are short-term, highly marketable earning assets such as Treasury bills, which can be sold or rediscounted with the CBN when cash is needed. Option C is a primary reserve that earns nothing. Options D and B are illiquid assets that cannot be turned into cash quickly without loss."}},{"itemId":"da21b3c5-7258-5207-945a-5d2e1d1a148c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"31523f04-176b-5231-b77a-cd483cfce6ab","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The theory of bank liquidity which holds that banks should grant only short-term, self-liquidating loans, such as loans to finance goods in the course of production or trade that are repaid when the goods are sold, is the:","options":["Commercial loan (real bills) theory","Anticipated income theory","Liability management theory","Shiftability theory"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The commercial loan or real bills theory says a bank stays liquid by lending only for short-term trade purposes, so that each loan repays itself from the sale of the goods it financed. Option D relies on selling assets to other investors, and option B on the borrower's future income. Option C relies on borrowing funds when liquidity is needed."}},{"itemId":"a7f49eba-b152-5410-910c-28a64426772d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1c1a4dc6-d2f7-5038-9c4d-5a8f341b7785","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mama Nkechi, a textile trader at Balogun Market, borrows N3 million for 90 days to buy stock, and the loan is repaid from the proceeds when the fabric is sold. A bank that restricts itself to such loans is following the:","options":["Commercial loan theory","Anticipated income theory","Shiftability theory","Liability management theory"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"A short-term loan that finances stock and is repaid from the sale of that stock is a self-liquidating loan, the only kind the commercial loan (real bills) theory approves. Option B would justify loans repaid in instalments from future earnings, such as term loans. Option D is about borrowing funds, and option C about selling assets before maturity."}},{"itemId":"7f62e039-4cb1-5b84-b6e0-8ca1d92afdab","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df9617ab-6311-5a85-a8ad-567e9a1de2aa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A major criticism of the commercial loan (real bills) theory of bank liquidity is that it:","options":["Depends entirely on the bank's ability to borrow in the money market","Requires banks to hold all their deposits as cash","Allows banks to grant too many long-term mortgage loans","Ignores that a stable core of deposits can safely support some longer-term lending"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Not all depositors withdraw at once, so part of a bank's deposits is stable, and limiting banks to short self-liquidating loans wastes this and starves the economy of term credit. Option C is the opposite of what the theory says. Option A describes liability management, and option B exaggerates: the theory allows short-term lending."}},{"itemId":"d36d4aed-76a2-518b-a447-52ecafcaa20f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff2f115f-ae61-58f3-96b4-b779e61c3e28","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The theory which holds that a bank can remain liquid by holding assets that can be sold or transferred to other investors, or to the central bank, before maturity without significant loss is the:","options":["Commercial loan theory","Anticipated income theory","Shiftability theory","Liability management theory"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The shiftability theory says liquidity comes from the marketability of assets: as long as the bank holds assets it can shift to others, such as Treasury bills, it can raise cash when needed. Option A relies on loans repaying themselves, and option B on borrowers' future income. Option D relies on borrowing, not selling assets."}},{"itemId":"2a46e16e-c443-50ce-b96f-ad57821a6e17","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e21cb551-c11d-5bba-b483-0c042a5e2ceb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A key weakness of the shiftability theory of liquidity is that:","options":["It forbids banks from holding Treasury bills","It requires every loan to be repaid from the sale of goods","It assumes banks can borrow unlimited funds overnight","In a general crisis all banks try to sell assets at once, so assets may not be shiftable without heavy loss"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Shifting assets works for one bank at a time, but when the whole system needs cash together, buyers disappear and prices fall, unless the central bank steps in. Option A is false, because marketable securities like Treasury bills are the core of the theory. Option B describes the commercial loan theory, and option C the liability management theory."}},{"itemId":"129d6f2e-e97a-57a7-b0d5-d89b09b87a81","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bfb0efa9-9f41-57ba-9488-ae4dab53d08a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the anticipated income theory of bank liquidity, a loan is liquid if:","options":["It is repaid out of the borrower's expected future income, usually in regular instalments","It is funded by interbank borrowing","It finances goods that will be sold within 90 days","It can be sold to another bank before maturity"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The anticipated income theory links liquidity to the borrower's future earnings: a term loan repaid in scheduled instalments gives the bank a steady, predictable cash inflow. Option C describes the commercial loan theory, and option D the shiftability theory. Option B is about the liability side, not the loan itself."}},{"itemId":"10793a5c-ad64-5055-98a3-87138a81fc51","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"771bda62-ca3a-588b-b6ff-aadc131d5bc3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank grants a civil servant a three-year car loan repaid by equal monthly deductions from her salary, arguing that the predictable instalments keep the bank liquid. This reasoning reflects the:","options":["Liability management theory","Anticipated income theory","Shiftability theory","Commercial loan theory"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Basing liquidity on the borrower's expected income and a fixed repayment schedule is the anticipated income theory, which supports term and instalment lending. Option D would reject a three-year consumer loan as not self-liquidating. Option C depends on reselling assets, and option A on borrowing funds."}},{"itemId":"c5bc5bc8-079e-568f-9cb1-784f02f53c57","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5f7ad725-ea6b-560c-a81a-470602245425","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which liquidity theory focuses on the LIABILITY side of the balance sheet rather than on the assets a bank holds?","options":["Shiftability theory","Commercial loan theory","Anticipated income theory","Liability management theory"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The liability management theory says a bank need not store all its liquidity in assets because it can buy funds when needed, for example through interbank borrowing or certificates of deposit. Options A, C and B all look for liquidity in the bank's assets: marketable securities, instalment loans and self-liquidating trade loans respectively."}},{"itemId":"d8da3288-67da-5254-806f-3fc04867e5d2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"197e98d5-c7eb-5747-8921-b071cb16ce13","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a way a deposit money bank can obtain liquidity at short notice?","options":["Selling or rediscounting its Treasury bills","Borrowing in the interbank market","Borrowing from the CBN through its standing lending facility","Granting a new five-year loan to a manufacturer"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Granting a new long-term loan uses up cash rather than providing it, so it is not a way of obtaining liquidity. Option A converts liquid assets into cash, while options B and C raise cash by borrowing from other banks or from the CBN."}},{"itemId":"bdfea5c0-866a-5110-b832-f9ce9d4bb369","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a52d0bf5-d02c-5f5d-b9a0-41fcd300ddac","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank raises cash by (1) letting its maturing Treasury bills run off and (2) borrowing overnight from another bank. Which statement correctly classifies these sources of liquidity?","options":["Both are asset-side sources","Both are liability-side sources","(1) is a liability-side source and (2) is an asset-side source","(1) is an asset-side source and (2) is a liability-side source"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Maturing Treasury bills turn an asset into cash, so that is liquidity stored on the asset side, while overnight borrowing creates a new liability, so it is purchased liquidity. Options A and B lump the two together, and option C swaps them."}},{"itemId":"087a27d0-f6c2-55c5-9e8f-1891c4fcddba","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aec3628d-85a3-5c9c-8e53-07d1151bbf30","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The situation in which a bank finances long-term loans with deposits that can be withdrawn on demand or at short notice is known as:","options":["Maturity mismatch","Capital adequacy","Credit rationing","Statutory liquidity"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Borrowing short and lending long creates a maturity mismatch: the bank's liabilities fall due much sooner than its assets, which exposes it to liquidity and interest rate risk. Option B concerns the size of capital relative to risk assets. Option C is about restricting loans, and option D is not a recognised term for this gap in maturities."}},{"itemId":"8edde193-093c-5c90-904a-dd05fa18409d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1fb7b500-f19a-5c6c-8fd7-9754d44d08e1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank holds mainly 10-year fixed-rate mortgage loans funded by 90-day fixed deposits. If market interest rates rise sharply, the bank will most likely suffer because:","options":["Its capital will rise because of higher interest rates","Its mortgage income will rise faster than its deposit costs","Its deposit costs will reprice upward quickly while its mortgage income stays fixed","Its liquidity ratio will automatically increase"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"This is interest rate risk from a maturity mismatch: the 90-day deposits must be renewed at the new, higher rates while the fixed mortgage rates do not change, so net interest income shrinks. Option B gets the direction wrong because the mortgage rate is fixed. Options D and A have no link to a rate rise."}},{"itemId":"034b2d3e-ae17-5dde-9d13-68cb7ee7179e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"34211198-22b5-50e5-b698-2957c8ad47b7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The difference between a bank's rate-sensitive assets and its rate-sensitive liabilities over a given period is called the:","options":["Loans-to-deposits ratio","Repricing (funding) gap","Net interest margin","Liquidity ratio"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The repricing or funding gap measures how much more (or less) of a bank's assets than its liabilities will reprice within a period, and it shows how net interest income will react to rate changes. Option A compares loans with deposits, and option D compares liquid assets with deposits. Option C is net interest income relative to earning assets."}},{"itemId":"862a531f-a599-530c-9864-757a13ad58bd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"243b8029-bdd7-54cd-8fe3-a649c97965bf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank whose rate-sensitive assets exceed its rate-sensitive liabilities (a positive gap) will find that its net interest income:","options":["Falls when interest rates rise","Is unaffected by any change in interest rates","Rises when interest rates fall","Rises when interest rates rise"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"With a positive gap more assets than liabilities reprice, so a rate rise lifts interest income more than interest expense and net interest income goes up. Option A describes a negative gap. Option C is the reverse, since a positive-gap bank loses when rates fall, and option B fits only a zero gap."}},{"itemId":"85d7ab78-edf2-572a-8ef7-586442845258","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8f29230b-dbda-5521-a683-4bf1a6fe30eb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The ALCO of a Nigerian bank expects interest rates to fall over the coming year. Under simple gap analysis, which repricing position would benefit the bank most?","options":["A zero gap","A negative gap, with more rate-sensitive liabilities than rate-sensitive assets","A positive gap, with more rate-sensitive assets than rate-sensitive liabilities","A gap equal to the bank's paid-up capital"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"When rates fall, a negative-gap bank sees its deposit and borrowing costs drop faster than its asset income, so net interest income rises. Option C would lose, because its assets reprice down faster. Option A only protects income rather than gaining from the fall, and option D has no meaning in gap analysis."}},{"itemId":"78878411-34eb-53e0-84be-47e7b5d712cf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f07f070-a17a-5943-a17d-11e3783ba7ec","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three pillars of the Basel II Accord?","options":["Market discipline","Minimum capital requirements","Deposit insurance","Supervisory review process"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Basel II rests on three pillars: minimum capital requirements, supervisory review of capital adequacy and market discipline through disclosure. Deposit insurance protects depositors but is a separate safety-net arrangement, run in Nigeria by the NDIC, and not a Basel II pillar. Options B, D and A are the genuine pillars."}},{"itemId":"3f677714-1db4-5c88-a078-fb3a6e426d7d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4e8b527a-23d6-5821-913d-5bcfebde476c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Basel Committee on Banking Supervision, which issues the Basel capital accords, operates under the auspices of the:","options":["World Bank","Bank for International Settlements","African Development Bank","International Monetary Fund"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The Basel Committee was set up by the G10 central banks and works under the Bank for International Settlements (BIS) in Basel, Switzerland. Options D, A and C are international financial institutions with other mandates, and none of them hosts the Basel Committee."}},{"itemId":"b0593207-69e5-5143-9fed-e784ce0c7fb2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"12175a22-6ab3-5337-a216-e33b8250a5bf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under Basel III, the Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) is intended to ensure that a bank holds:","options":["Stable long-term funding for its whole balance sheet","Enough high-quality liquid assets to cover its net cash outflows over a 30-day stress period","Cash reserves with the central bank equal to a fixed share of deposits","Capital of at least 8% of its risk-weighted assets"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The LCR is a short-term liquidity standard: liquid assets must cover the cash a bank would lose over 30 days of stress. Option D is the Basel I capital rule. Option A describes the Net Stable Funding Ratio, which tackles longer-term maturity mismatch, and option C is a cash reserve ratio."}},{"itemId":"140d6c78-8d3f-596f-85cf-90989066e50c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a530de5c-7fa5-537c-a38b-5a6beb717f83","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank's capital is exactly at the minimum required ratio of capital to risk-weighted assets, and it wants to grant many more corporate loans. Without breaching the requirement, it must:","options":["Reduce its holdings of zero-risk-weight government securities","Raise more capital or retain more profit before expanding risk assets","Lower its cash reserve ratio","Simply attract more deposits"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Corporate loans add to risk-weighted assets, so at the minimum ratio the bank must raise capital (or retain profit) to support more lending; this is how capital adequacy limits balance sheet growth. Option D funds loans but adds no capital. Option A does not reduce risk-weighted assets because those securities carry no weight, and option C is set by the CBN, not the bank."}},{"itemId":"1430062f-4064-52a5-8257-9ef21d010ca5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4178efda-da70-5c60-bc39-303c793deb77","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ALM-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the CAMEL rating system used by Nigerian bank supervisors, the letter 'L' stands for:","options":["Licensing","Loan growth","Leverage","Liquidity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"CAMEL rates a bank on Capital adequacy, Asset quality, Management, Earnings and Liquidity, so 'L' is liquidity, the ability to meet withdrawals and other obligations. Options C, B and A are banking terms but not part of the CAMEL acronym."}},{"itemId":"2abec3b9-b58a-5bca-ad5e-d729555ba7d1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d3dfcc94-b97b-5cc9-b7c6-8678d89ef9a2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Adebayo Stores Ltd applies to its bank for an overdraft. Its balance sheet shows current assets of N4,800,000 and current liabilities of N3,200,000. What is its current ratio?","options":["1.5 : 1","1.6 : 1","0.67 : 1","2.5 : 1"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"The current ratio is current assets divided by current liabilities, so 4,800,000 / 3,200,000 = 1.5 : 1. Option C turns the ratio upside down (liabilities over assets), while B and D do not follow from the figures given."}},{"itemId":"99a272f9-10e3-5322-91ec-0e95d6b488f5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b27ca2b3-133d-53be-82c9-5a90baed5998","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Adebayo Stores Ltd has current assets of N4,800,000 (including stock of N1,600,000) and current liabilities of N3,200,000. What is its quick (acid-test) ratio?","options":["2.0 : 1","0.5 : 1","1.0 : 1","1.5 : 1"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"The quick ratio removes stock from current assets because stock is the least liquid current asset: (4,800,000 - 1,600,000) / 3,200,000 = 1.0 : 1. Option D is the current ratio, which forgets to deduct stock, and B divides stock alone by current liabilities."}},{"itemId":"b9705d6e-e61f-5a70-bfee-1c867b44b3ad","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b68f288f-46fd-578e-9045-b66d8100bf02","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chinedu Ventures has current assets of N7,500,000 and current liabilities of N5,250,000. What is its working capital?","options":["N2,250,000","N1,428,571","N12,750,000","-N2,250,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Working capital (net current assets) is current assets minus current liabilities: 7,500,000 - 5,250,000 = N2,250,000. Option C adds the two figures instead of subtracting, and D subtracts the wrong way round, giving a negative figure."}},{"itemId":"4d24e51c-068e-5b69-8d84-d70ef507e678","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c73cf52-d1f1-5827-8ac0-35ea34e98c22","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Gearing is defined here as long-term debt divided by (long-term debt + shareholders' funds). Okon Plastics Ltd has long-term loans of N6,000,000 and shareholders' funds of N14,000,000. Its gearing is","options":["30%","70%","23.1%","42.9%"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"With the stated definition, gearing = 6,000,000 / (6,000,000 + 14,000,000) = 30%. Option D (42.9%) is the debt-to-equity ratio, which divides debt by equity only, and B is the share of total long-term funds supplied by the owners rather than by lenders."}},{"itemId":"126d406a-f1a9-5ca6-93ab-f6f11048752b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e5a14aed-8616-57c0-9240-9a579c62ae95","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bello Agro Ltd has total long-term debt of N9,000,000 and shareholders' equity of N12,000,000. What is its debt-to-equity ratio?","options":["0.57 : 1","0.43 : 1","1.33 : 1","0.75 : 1"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Debt-to-equity is debt divided by equity: 9,000,000 / 12,000,000 = 0.75 : 1, meaning lenders supply 75 kobo for every naira the owners have put in. Option C divides equity by debt, and B is debt as a share of debt plus equity (a gearing-type measure)."}},{"itemId":"fece020e-9017-5fb8-9ca3-305d767069db","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"407b742f-e553-5599-b5a5-d95810dc804f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ifeoma Foods Ltd earned profit before interest and tax of N8,400,000 and paid loan interest of N1,200,000 for the year. What is its interest cover?","options":["8.4 times","0.14 times","6 times","7 times"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Interest cover is profit before interest and tax divided by interest charges: 8,400,000 / 1,200,000 = 7 times. Option C wrongly deducts the interest from profit first, and B inverts the ratio."}},{"itemId":"9f1e1cc6-d46c-5120-9f06-0dc86afdba5e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f8c55fd-8903-577a-90ac-27bb42d3fd82","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Lagos pharmacy recorded sales of N24,000,000 and a net profit of N1,800,000 for the year. Its net profit margin is","options":["7.5%","18%","75%","13.3%"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Net profit margin is net profit divided by sales, times 100: 1,800,000 / 24,000,000 x 100 = 7.5%. Option D divides sales by profit (13.3 times), and C misplaces the decimal point."}},{"itemId":"09293f8b-b03f-5b07-916b-3ea5d3156855","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"44f50101-c742-5798-be75-909d4e48a5cf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Emeka Motors Ltd has shareholders' funds of N15,000,000 and long-term loans of N5,000,000, and earned profit before interest and tax of N3,600,000. Taking capital employed as shareholders' funds plus long-term loans, its return on capital employed (ROCE) is","options":["24%","14.4%","18%","72%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"ROCE = PBIT / capital employed = 3,600,000 / (15,000,000 + 5,000,000) = 18%. Option A uses shareholders' funds alone as the denominator, and D uses only the long-term loans."}},{"itemId":"e4727d81-c067-56f8-9036-cd1cb75bd1b2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ee62ed0b-28ce-5c86-a61b-32e5b1c33ef2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ngozi Textiles holds closing stock of N2,400,000. Its cost of sales for the year was N14,600,000 and its sales were N21,900,000. Using a 365-day year and cost of sales, how many days' stock does it hold?","options":["6.1 days","60 days","90 days","40 days"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Stock days = stock / cost of sales x 365 = 2,400,000 / 14,600,000 x 365 = 60 days. Option D wrongly uses sales instead of cost of sales, and A is the stock turnover in times (about 6.1), not a number of days."}},{"itemId":"77335662-e8cc-522f-95cd-6f437f691f41","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b9236226-c0df-5423-9de7-e26209845a61","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kunle Distributors has trade debtors of N3,000,000 and annual credit sales of N18,250,000. Using a 365-day year, its debtor collection period is","options":["30 days","6.1 days","60 days","73 days"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Debtor days = debtors / credit sales x 365 = 3,000,000 / 18,250,000 x 365 = 60 days, so customers take about two months to pay. Option B is the debtor turnover in times (18,250,000 / 3,000,000), not days."}},{"itemId":"91cfe8cd-07bc-527e-9000-b5c288bbd9ce","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a26e8f4-954c-52d2-a700-826175c5c190","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Halima Supermarket's cost of sales was N18,000,000 and its average stock was N3,000,000. What is its stock turnover?","options":["21 times","0.17 times","3 times","6 times"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Stock turnover = cost of sales / average stock = 18,000,000 / 3,000,000 = 6 times a year, meaning stock is replaced roughly every two months. Option B inverts the ratio, and A wrongly adds the two figures before dividing."}},{"itemId":"b17f39fa-b42f-59c9-a8a3-733add3d2559","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7564182d-2d78-544d-b372-d90b377dc749","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Tunde Hardware's balance sheet shows: stock N2,000,000; debtors N1,500,000; cash N500,000; trade creditors N1,600,000; bank overdraft N900,000. What is its current ratio?","options":["0.8 : 1","1.6 : 1","2.5 : 1","0.63 : 1"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Current assets are 2,000,000 + 1,500,000 + 500,000 = 4,000,000 and current liabilities are 1,600,000 + 900,000 = 2,500,000, so the current ratio is 1.6 : 1. Option C forgets that the bank overdraft is a current liability, and A is the quick ratio because it leaves out stock."}},{"itemId":"670924c3-c3ef-5b45-bb7c-5a5fb6d25db6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae741fe0-d653-5459-b9d4-9d05b90a1ed7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A customer's current ratio is 1.2 : 1 and its current liabilities are N5,000,000. What is its working capital?","options":["N11,000,000","N1,000,000","N4,166,667","N6,000,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Current assets are 1.2 x 5,000,000 = 6,000,000, so working capital = 6,000,000 - 5,000,000 = N1,000,000. Option D is the current assets figure, not working capital, and C wrongly divides the liabilities by the ratio."}},{"itemId":"76d41ab1-763a-59d7-a39e-041d3a880a56","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1203d27a-9e32-51cc-8b9e-5c3400249ef4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ada Cosmetics had sales of N40,000,000, gross profit of N12,000,000 and operating expenses of N7,000,000 (ignore tax and interest). What is its net profit margin?","options":["12.5%","41.7%","17.5%","30%"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Net profit is gross profit minus expenses, 12,000,000 - 7,000,000 = 5,000,000, and 5,000,000 / 40,000,000 = 12.5%. Option D is the gross profit margin, and C is the expenses-to-sales ratio."}},{"itemId":"6f7ab1f4-cead-5adb-9284-20b3016e17cf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5ed7117a-07da-590c-be74-cdf6649342ef","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Taking the liquidity ratio as liquid assets divided by total deposit liabilities, a deposit money bank with liquid assets of N36 billion and total deposits of N120 billion has a liquidity ratio of","options":["70%","33.3%","30%","3.3%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Liquidity ratio = 36 / 120 x 100 = 30%, showing what share of deposits could be met from liquid assets. Option D inverts the ratio (3.33 times written as a percentage), and A is the share of deposits not covered by liquid assets."}},{"itemId":"5aeb3d26-4f50-5650-999c-8bda2ffac873","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9840e49e-fe78-5a0a-a571-66bb1b23300e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Assume the Central Bank sets a cash reserve ratio of 20% on deposits. A bank with eligible deposits of N250 billion must keep a cash reserve with the Central Bank of","options":["N50 billion","N12.5 billion","N200 billion","N1,250 billion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"The required cash reserve is deposits x cash reserve ratio = 250 x 0.20 = N50 billion. Option C is the amount left after the reserve, and D wrongly divides deposits by 0.20 instead of multiplying."}},{"itemId":"74c34977-0e22-53eb-b15b-2829ea662499","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c37283cb-0316-54eb-bd72-01da54f36fc6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank has loans and advances of N84 billion and total customer deposits of N120 billion. Its loan-to-deposit ratio is","options":["142.9%","36%","70%","30%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Loan-to-deposit ratio = loans / deposits x 100 = 84 / 120 = 70%, meaning 70 kobo of every naira deposited has been lent out. Option A divides deposits by loans, and D is the share of deposits not lent out."}},{"itemId":"752ad778-0338-5ff4-9471-8e4181171f05","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1906acf5-0d6d-5aff-be5d-4537ca4e77c3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Assume a cash reserve ratio of 25%. A bank has deposits of N200 billion and currently holds N56 billion with the Central Bank. Its excess reserve is","options":["N6 billion","N42 billion","N144 billion","N50 billion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Required reserve = 25% x 200 = N50 billion, so excess = 56 - 50 = N6 billion. Option D is the required reserve itself, and C is deposits minus the amount held."}},{"itemId":"4ded3561-84d7-5df7-a1bd-263cc5332925","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d30bbb42-6aa8-5ea3-88d6-562664f3a07c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank's assets are: cash N5bn, balances with the Central Bank N12bn, treasury bills N18bn, loans and advances N90bn, fixed assets N10bn. Its deposits are N140bn. Treating cash, Central Bank balances and treasury bills as liquid assets, its liquidity ratio is","options":["12.1%","32.1%","89.3%","25%"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Liquid assets = 5 + 12 + 18 = N35bn, and 35 / 140 = 25%. Option C wrongly counts loans and advances (which are risk assets, not liquid assets) and A counts only cash and Central Bank balances, leaving out treasury bills."}},{"itemId":"e3342a2f-7527-59b1-bc23-4f259e4c30e2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0098daf-0e0c-5cba-bd74-3f295b7e7f73","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under BOFIA 2020, a bank whose statutory reserve fund is less than its paid-up share capital reports net profit after tax of N8 billion. The minimum it must transfer to the statutory reserve before declaring a dividend is","options":["N0.8 billion","N1.2 billion","N4.0 billion","N2.4 billion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Where the reserve fund is below paid-up capital, at least 30% of net profit after tax goes to the reserve: 30% x 8 = N2.4 billion. Option B applies the 15% rate, which is used only once the reserve fund equals or exceeds paid-up capital."}},{"itemId":"a7ecbdc2-535b-50c8-8fc0-08c862165553","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"56f4b4d7-c50c-56b0-a364-d48766c722fa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under BOFIA 2020, a bank's statutory reserve fund already exceeds its paid-up share capital. If its net profit after tax is N12 billion, the minimum transfer to the statutory reserve is","options":["N3.6 billion","N1.8 billion","N6.0 billion","N1.2 billion"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Once the reserve fund equals or exceeds paid-up capital, the minimum transfer falls to 15% of net profit: 15% x 12 = N1.8 billion. Option A uses the 30% rate, which applies only while the reserve fund is still below paid-up capital."}},{"itemId":"be2a3f28-ee8f-5afe-b380-5016b7e4c940","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ad39ae6-8fac-5f50-b05c-b2998c353162","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Femi Bakeries reports profit before tax of N5,400,000 after charging loan interest of N600,000. What is its interest cover?","options":["9 times","0.1 times","10 times","11 times"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Interest cover uses profit BEFORE interest and tax, so first add the interest back: 5,400,000 + 600,000 = 6,000,000, then 6,000,000 / 600,000 = 10 times. Option A uses profit before tax without adding back the interest, and B inverts the ratio."}},{"itemId":"70b8c572-80c3-536b-9006-d74cf61fefbf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6812e5cc-6c2a-5745-84d4-2f58b7c0eccf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Gearing is defined here as long-term debt divided by (long-term debt + shareholders' funds). Uche Haulage Ltd has share capital of N15,000,000, retained profits of N6,000,000 and a long-term bank loan of N9,000,000. Its gearing is","options":["42.9%","30%","37.5%","60%"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Shareholders' funds are share capital plus retained profits, 15,000,000 + 6,000,000 = 21,000,000, so gearing = 9,000,000 / (9,000,000 + 21,000,000) = 30%. Option C forgets the retained profits and uses share capital alone, and A is the debt-to-equity ratio (9 / 21)."}},{"itemId":"c3408a6c-b545-5337-8ac6-3929af8f4cd4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"55f47bb9-7b43-5c05-93a8-591e8c900569","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank with customer deposits of N300 billion has an internal policy that its loan-to-deposit ratio must not exceed 65%. What is the maximum amount of loans and advances it can hold?","options":["N195 billion","N235 billion","N105 billion","N461.5 billion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Maximum loans = deposits x 65% = 300 x 0.65 = N195 billion. Option C is the 35% of deposits that must stay unlent, and D wrongly divides deposits by 0.65."}},{"itemId":"8ffc94fa-5c5e-564a-99b3-379ef23b4217","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"08e1b2c4-7d38-5b2a-ac8b-18631f468af6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Assume a minimum liquidity ratio of 30% of deposits. A bank with deposits of N500 billion holds liquid assets of N135 billion. By how much is it short of the minimum?","options":["N3 billion","N15 billion","N150 billion","N365 billion"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"The bank needs 30% x 500 = N150 billion of liquid assets but holds 135, so it is N15 billion short. Option C is the full requirement rather than the shortfall, and D is deposits minus liquid assets held."}},{"itemId":"010b754e-a4c1-53ea-ba2d-02c8a346d589","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"497b126f-e06f-536c-ad05-7221d16dc97c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the balance sheet of a deposit money bank, assets are normally arranged","options":["in order of liquidity, starting with cash","in alphabetical order of the asset names","in order of the dates on which they were acquired","in order of size, starting with the largest"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Banks group assets by nature and list them in order of liquidity and maturity, so the reader can see at a glance whether the bank holds enough cash and liquid assets to repay depositors at short notice. Options D, B and C would hide that liquidity picture."}},{"itemId":"03075935-849a-5a6a-9c29-d5ce3264a18c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4528a422-6628-58b5-8f26-2634e7056d62","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following assets of a deposit money bank is the MOST liquid?","options":["Loans and advances","Fixed assets such as land and buildings","Long-term investments in development stocks","Cash and short-term funds"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Cash and short-term funds (currency held in the strong rooms and cheques due from other banks) are already money or very close to it, so they head the asset side. Loans and advances in option A are risk assets tied up with borrowers, and fixed assets in B are the hardest to turn into cash."}},{"itemId":"cb0594d1-f62c-5495-91df-87dd4c738145","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"682b6c9f-5fcb-5906-ad59-17bc8eaf4253","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On a deposit money bank's balance sheet, the item described as the bank's 'risk assets', from which about seventy percent or more of its revenue comes, is","options":["bills discounted","loans and advances","cash and short-term funds","treasury bills"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Loans and advances are the credit facilities granted to customers; they earn the highest interest but carry default risk, which is why bankers take security. Treasury bills in option D are near-riskless government paper, and cash in C earns nothing."}},{"itemId":"f79b7490-4687-5d49-983b-8b32ca1b7688","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5fda1346-e906-5d4f-9951-889c2dcfcbb6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Banks hold treasury bills as a short-term investment mainly because they","options":["earn the highest rate of interest of all bank assets","never mature and so provide a permanent income","are secured on the borrower's land and buildings","are safe, highly liquid and can be rediscounted with the Central Bank before maturity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Treasury bills are short government securities (commonly 91 days) that are low-risk and can be rediscounted with the Central Bank of Nigeria if the bank needs cash early. Option A describes loans and advances, which pay more because they are riskier, and C describes a secured loan, not a government bill."}},{"itemId":"e1ef806e-1e90-55ee-a95b-f4e3a7c4d82a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"89d5ae98-fb27-5131-9c43-9c96f3111107","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an item under 'other liabilities' in a deposit money bank's balance sheet?","options":["Provision for taxation","Proposed dividends to shareholders","Accrued interest receivable","Bank drafts and certified cheques"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Accrued interest receivable is money owed TO the bank, so it is grouped under other assets. Provision for taxation, proposed dividends and outstanding bank drafts/certified cheques (options A, B, D) are all amounts the bank owes and appear under other liabilities."}},{"itemId":"92ac4aac-ca60-5b81-85ac-0c1c97a6869b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"62d8848f-01cf-5195-b37a-b545b67594ed","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Deposits are described as the largest liability of a deposit money bank because","options":["deposits are reserves the law requires the bank to set aside from profit","depositors are creditors of the bank who can call for most of their money on demand or at short notice","deposits are the bank's own capital contributed by shareholders","deposits are loans the bank has granted to its customers"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"A deposit is money the bank owes back to the customer, making the depositor a creditor, and deposits are by far the biggest part of the money a bank works with. Option C describes capital, D describes loans and advances (an asset), and A describes the statutory reserve."}},{"itemId":"dfa4c65c-68cf-523d-b616-b28ad122be96","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0a4ea1c4-14e3-5a32-b4a9-08609e86d3ad","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Eze has N2,000,000 in his current account and his neighbour Mrs Musa has an overdraft of N500,000 with the same bank. In the bank's balance sheet,","options":["both balances are liabilities of the bank","Mr Eze's balance is an asset and Mrs Musa's overdraft is a liability","both balances are assets of the bank","Mr Eze's balance is a liability and Mrs Musa's overdraft is an asset"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"A credit balance is money the bank owes the customer, so it is a deposit liability, while an overdraft is money the customer owes the bank, so it is part of loans and advances on the asset side. Option B reverses the two, which is the usual mistake of looking at it from the customer's side."}},{"itemId":"9408f898-49a3-59c7-8874-7c4ea1d0bce1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1fe51c40-4984-5198-a7d1-56a5f7455ecd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three main areas of bank accounting practice addressed by Statement of Accounting Standard (SAS) 10?","options":["Foreign exchange rate determination","Income recognition","Balance sheet classification","Loss recognition"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"SAS 10 on banks and similar institutions deals with income recognition, loss recognition and balance sheet classification, aiming to make bank accounts reliable and comparable. Setting foreign exchange rates is a monetary-policy and market matter, not an accounting standard area."}},{"itemId":"22ec6548-9b2a-5fdc-92bc-0b6676fb404f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"293936a1-1448-5dc2-a09d-b793fd5a18e9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A major reason for a specific accounting standard (SAS 10) for banks in Nigeria was","options":["inconsistent accounting policies and allegedly overstated bank profits that made comparison of performance difficult","the need to fix the interest rate banks pay on savings accounts","the need to stop banks from granting loans and advances","the need to replace the balance sheet with a cash book"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"SAS 10 was a response to inconsistent policies, overstated profits and the need to keep public confidence in banks, so that bank statements would be uniform and comparable. Options B, C and D are not accounting-standard purposes at all."}},{"itemId":"a60c99f8-d8d3-5a89-9412-69e4b004d3e6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"88d7b777-5591-50e1-8540-4f82f7b4ca0a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under BOFIA 2020, the statutory reserve fund of a bank is built up by transferring a percentage of","options":["gross income, before tax and expenses","total deposits, at the end of each month","net profit after tax, before any dividend is declared","paid-up capital, whenever new shares are issued"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"The Act requires every bank to transfer part of its net profits for the year, after provision for tax and before any dividend, into a statutory reserve fund (30% while the fund is below paid-up capital, 15% once it equals or exceeds it). Option B confuses this with the cash reserve held against deposits at the Central Bank."}},{"itemId":"a8072581-96f4-5856-bfb2-913bc815803f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7bd848d3-305a-54ee-a245-596015cd680c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a bank's balance sheet, 'retained profits' (retained earnings) are","options":["profits paid out to shareholders as dividends","interest paid to depositors during the year","undivided profits left in the business after dividends and all other appropriations","the maximum share capital the bank may issue"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Retained profits are accumulated net income kept in the bank after dividends and other appropriations, and they form part of shareholders' funds. Option A is the opposite (profit distributed), and D describes authorised capital."}},{"itemId":"28f414c8-f960-5272-b6e6-4ef4f7e89fe7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a09c9e1-2a4a-5c9c-881d-735395b99972","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following appears on the ASSETS side of a bank's balance sheet?","options":["Proposed dividends","Statutory reserves","Bills discounted","Customers' deposits"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Bills discounted (treasury bills, trade bills, bankers' acceptances and commercial papers bought by the bank) are claims the bank holds, so they are assets. Statutory reserves (option B) are part of shareholders' funds, while deposits and proposed dividends (D, A) are amounts the bank owes."}},{"itemId":"5274fe24-54c8-57d5-a20d-3b877269eb5d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"17cbac95-ed47-5871-b86a-800b0d9d4cc4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank's land and buildings, vehicles, computers and other equipment, shown in the balance sheet at cost less depreciation, are classified as","options":["other assets","equipment on lease","long-term investments","fixed assets"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Fixed assets are the bank's own premises and equipment, shown at their net value after periodic depreciation. Option A covers items such as prepayments and accrued interest, C covers stocks and bonds, and B covers assets the bank has leased out to customers."}},{"itemId":"44dacee9-b7cc-526e-8658-7142821f7344","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"575e2c82-08b8-57f4-afa0-172518305db9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank issues a performance guarantee for a customer who is building a road for a state government. Until the customer defaults, this guarantee is","options":["an off-balance-sheet (contingent) item disclosed in the notes to the accounts","a deposit liability shown in the balance sheet","part of the bank's statutory reserve","a loan and advance shown as an asset"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Guarantees, bonds, acceptances and letters of credit only become real debts if a stated event happens, so they are contingent liabilities disclosed alongside, but not in, the balance sheet. No money has been lent or deposited, so options B and D are wrong."}},{"itemId":"375aca2f-baf1-5c35-bf41-6168193a57c2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5130dcc8-b9a6-5d14-a5b1-17722776c988","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Compared with the balance sheet of a trading company, a commercial bank's balance sheet typically shows","options":["no current liabilities because deposits are permanent capital","mainly stock and fixed assets funded largely by shareholders' capital","mainly financial assets funded largely by deposits, with fixed assets forming a small share","loans and advances on the liabilities side"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"A bank deals in money: its assets are mostly cash, securities and loans, and most of its funds come from depositors, so it is very highly geared compared with a trading firm. Option B describes a trading or manufacturing company, and A is wrong because most deposits are repayable on demand or at short notice."}},{"itemId":"ad9fcfd7-8d38-5a2a-8246-a9be4690c065","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f18c9b57-746d-5743-98f6-bf2294e25969","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a NON-DEPOSIT source of funds for a deposit money bank?","options":["Savings account deposits","Fixed (time) deposits","Current account balances","Rental income from bank property let to others"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Non-deposit funds include investment income, rental income, borrowing, owners' capital, retained earnings, fees and sale of assets. Options A, C and B are all depositors' funds brought in by customers."}},{"itemId":"48458e9e-1897-5b12-886d-5dbf587e6640","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"141a24c0-0da3-5b37-939d-5d05b614162b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the classification of bank deposits, current account balances are best described as","options":["funds from investors seeking money market yields","deposits with a fixed maturity and stipulated rate of interest","deposits that bear the lowest rate of interest and need a minimal opening amount","non-interest-bearing demand deposits that can be withdrawn at any time by cheque"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Current account money can be drawn at any time without notice, usually by cheque, and normally earns no interest. Option B describes fixed (time) deposits, and C describes savings accounts."}},{"itemId":"51974317-6d6d-5f90-b21c-901ec1422edc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c5d06acc-a037-5215-a78e-046f24698091","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Adeyemi places N5,000,000 with her bank for 180 days at an agreed interest rate of 12% a year and cannot withdraw it early without penalty. This is a","options":["savings account deposit","call money borrowing","current account deposit","fixed (time) deposit"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"A fixed or time deposit carries a fixed maturity (term) and a stipulated interest rate. Option C is withdrawable on demand without interest, A is withdrawable savings with a low rate, and B is short-term money a bank borrows from other banks."}},{"itemId":"13fd9df7-49dd-50fa-90d4-5307beae26f2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"123a995f-d33f-5581-b4ab-9a40374f6890","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a bank raises money through an overnight call deposit or interbank borrowing, the funds can usually be arranged quickly but","options":["the interest rate is usually very high","they must be transferred to the statutory reserve","they carry no obligation to repay","they become part of the bank's paid-up capital"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Overnight and call borrowing can be arranged in minutes, which makes it useful for short liquidity gaps, but it is expensive. It is a debt the bank must repay, so it is not capital (option D) and certainly carries an obligation to repay (option C)."}},{"itemId":"536e07ec-af53-57e3-a4e1-1041001e7ad2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a402f157-f748-5b45-b107-57c83037c7fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a use of a deposit money bank's funds?","options":["Fees earned from issuing letters of credit","Payment of interest to depositors","Investment in government securities","Repayment of customers' deposits on demand"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Fees from letters of credit, bank drafts and similar services flow INTO the bank, so they bring money in rather than use it. Repaying deposits, paying interest and investing in government securities (options D, B, C) are all ways the bank uses its funds."}},{"itemId":"f8cc5e32-bfa5-5578-a4f3-b7769f596dd2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aa6dc184-c74b-5e92-9305-2d9c3039ca29","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Income from the sale of a bank's building or of a subsidiary company is regarded as extraordinary income because it is","options":["a type of customer deposit","usually non-recurring","guaranteed by the Central Bank","earned on loans and advances"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Sale of assets brings in funds but does not happen every year, so it is treated as extraordinary rather than as regular operating income. Interest on loans (option D) is the bank's main recurring income, not extraordinary income."}},{"itemId":"693cd09e-32f2-5fab-b1f6-718666053e8f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93d4f9eb-ae1a-5020-8796-3fbd8436b876","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main reason a banker analyses a customer's balance sheet and profit and loss account before granting an advance is to","options":["decide the price at which the customer should sell its goods","audit the accounts on behalf of the shareholders","judge whether the business can repay the advance and interest from its operations and assets","calculate the customer's personal income tax"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"A lending banker uses the accounts to assess liquidity, profitability, gearing and cash flow, and so the ability to repay and the margin of safety if things go wrong. Auditing (option B) is the auditor's job, and pricing or tax (options D, A) are not the banker's lending concern."}},{"itemId":"583190ae-dfa3-59d2-a36e-dec10e9739ad","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"886c2728-eb6f-5d55-8d14-f38227bc8e68","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A banker who wants to know whether a customer can meet its short-term debts as they fall due would look mainly at the","options":["return on capital employed and net profit margin","earnings per share and dividend cover","debt-to-equity ratio and gearing","current ratio and quick ratio"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Current and quick ratios compare liquid resources with short-term liabilities, so they measure short-term liquidity. Option A measures profitability, C measures long-term financial structure and risk, and B matters mainly to shareholders."}},{"itemId":"8614f57e-0c53-5fd2-a841-e71c2f0fefe5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f891a74-51a6-5e96-a3dd-958d673fad2d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Stock is excluded from current assets when calculating the quick (acid-test) ratio because","options":["it is always valued at more than its cost","it is the least liquid current asset and may not quickly turn into cash at book value","it is owed to trade creditors","it is a fixed asset, not a current asset"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Stock must first be sold and the debts collected before it becomes cash, and in a forced sale it may fetch less than book value. It is still a current asset, so option D is wrong, and option A is false because stock is normally valued at the lower of cost and net realisable value."}},{"itemId":"9db15cc8-6ba5-5f95-b4b1-8a1994680c0d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"97fbf089-bb24-5183-88ed-ec92ac1c71e3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A customer's current ratio is 2.4 : 1 but its quick ratio is only 0.4 : 1. The most likely explanation is that","options":["it has no current liabilities","most of its current assets are tied up in stock","it holds very large cash balances","its debtors pay immediately in cash"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"The gap between the two ratios is caused by stock, the only item the quick ratio leaves out, so a high current ratio with a very low quick ratio points to heavy (possibly slow-moving) stock. Large cash balances (option C) would raise the quick ratio, not lower it."}},{"itemId":"775a11ee-4647-5767-9238-cf4f8a14883b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6e560444-84c8-5287-aaf0-63bd905fe299","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"From a lending banker's point of view, a HIGHLY geared company is one that","options":["holds more cash than it needs","relies heavily on borrowed funds relative to owners' funds, increasing the risk to new lenders","relies mainly on shareholders' funds and retained profits","has no long-term borrowing at all"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"High gearing means interest-bearing debt is large compared with equity, so fixed interest charges are heavy and the owners have less of their own money at stake. Options D and C describe a low-geared company."}},{"itemId":"f43682bb-01af-5f02-a184-6ae487523271","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"22de2961-dde4-5dc2-abf1-a417c9385ecb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Interest cover tells the lending banker","options":["how many times the customer's profit before interest and tax covers its interest charges","what percentage of sales is left as net profit","how much of the business is financed by borrowing","how many days customers take to pay their debts"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Interest cover = profit before interest and tax / interest, so a low figure warns that a small fall in profit could leave the borrower unable to pay interest. Option B is net profit margin, D is debtor days, and C is gearing."}},{"itemId":"4b5bb334-0f72-5c37-91e1-26f7639fd4c6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4ad8df07-58f4-5148-bdd1-a69cd0869a8d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Over two years a customer's debtor collection period rises from 35 days to 80 days while sales stay the same. The banker should conclude that","options":["the business has repaid its overdraft","stock is selling more quickly","credit control is weakening and some debts may become bad, putting pressure on cash","the business is collecting its debts faster"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"A longer collection period means customers are taking more time to pay, which ties up cash and raises the risk of bad debts. Option D is the opposite of what the figures show, and B concerns stock days, not debtor days."}},{"itemId":"53700e30-895c-5c13-86dd-a04ccc031f53","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"281c7ac8-4c03-5f23-8c44-9a04f4a775a6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In assessing what could be recovered if a borrower failed, a prudent banker values the borrower's assets on a","options":["going-concern basis, assuming the business continues trading","break-up (forced-sale) basis rather than a going-concern basis","replacement-cost basis, at the cost of buying new assets","historical-cost basis, exactly as shown in the balance sheet"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Going-concern values assume the business keeps trading, but if it collapses the assets are sold quickly and usually fetch much less, so the banker looks at break-up values for safety. Option A suits a healthy, continuing business, and D ignores that book values may not be realisable."}},{"itemId":"69de05a3-4b14-5a83-a584-9eca16327398","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1d2bd184-cb8c-5d45-8703-b98612885857","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a banker calculates a customer's tangible net worth, goodwill shown in the balance sheet is usually","options":["treated as a long-term liability","deducted, because it is likely to have little or no value if the business is wound up","doubled, because it represents future profits","added to current assets"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Goodwill and similar intangibles depend on the business continuing and rarely realise anything on break-up, so bankers deduct them to see the real asset backing. It is not a current asset (option D) and not a liability (option A)."}},{"itemId":"119542c4-fbfd-5b35-ab84-60e6ce87c4d8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0fbd35c8-838c-5950-8e07-896b7714bb8f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Arranging transactions just before the balance sheet date so that the accounts look stronger than the business's normal position is called","options":["consolidation","window dressing","factoring","overtrading"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Window dressing makes year-end figures look better, for example by temporarily cutting the overdraft or delaying purchases, so the balance sheet gives a misleading picture. Option D is expanding sales too fast for the available working capital, and A is the combining of group accounts or of banks."}},{"itemId":"a7c61d0c-0ce1-53bd-ae41-7abd64affdae","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aae6527a-5d25-56e5-b8ec-38c618b773be","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On 30 December, Bisi Trading Ltd borrows N10 million from a director to clear its bank overdraft, then draws the overdraft again on 3 January to repay the director. This is an example of","options":["a statutory reserve transfer","a going-concern valuation","capital reserve creation","window dressing"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"The company makes its year-end balance sheet show no overdraft even though the borrowing returns a few days later, which is window dressing. Options C and A are reserve movements out of profit, and B is a basis of valuing assets."}},{"itemId":"13407cea-6378-5e76-8300-9af4cbc09910","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f8e4a8e-35e6-5a06-9462-7d903d72a4b5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a limitation of a customer's balance sheet as a basis for lending?","options":["It is based on historical figures that may be out of date","It can be affected by window dressing","It lists the assets and liabilities of the business","It shows the position at only one date"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Listing assets and liabilities is simply what a balance sheet does, so it is its purpose, not a weakness. Being a snapshot at one date, using historical figures and being open to window dressing (options D, A, B) are recognised limitations, which is why bankers also look at management accounts and cash flow."}},{"itemId":"8a382d8d-15d2-5b11-9c00-4fb5997e8e1d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3998b7b9-a3ff-5027-a990-bcd2a8c01d41","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A banker examines a customer's cash flow forecast in addition to its profit because","options":["profit figures already include all future loan repayments","cash flow forecasts are audited and so cannot be wrong","loans are repaid out of cash, and a profitable business can still fail if it runs out of cash","cash flow shows the break-up value of fixed assets"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Profit is an accounting figure, while repayment needs actual cash; heavy stock or slow debtors can leave a profitable firm short of cash. Forecasts are estimates, not audited facts (option B), and break-up values (D) come from valuing assets, not from cash flow."}},{"itemId":"9ec93512-f88f-5678-97cc-9dca2c4f0b18","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"639cb69e-62ea-5660-bc6c-3b9db05b8959","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BAL-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A customer's sales have doubled in one year, but its working capital has fallen, its creditors and overdraft have risen sharply and its current ratio has dropped below 1. The banker should suspect","options":["window dressing","low gearing","over-capitalisation","overtrading"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Overtrading is expanding sales faster than the firm's working capital can support, so it leans increasingly on creditors and the bank. Option A is cosmetic year-end manipulation, and C (too much idle capital) is the opposite problem."}},{"itemId":"9c861328-7571-5f7e-baca-4bf257284732","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3600c24-824d-5571-93ea-36da7f7780f8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking literature, which writer defined a banker as \"a dealer in capital, or more properly a dealer in money... an intermediate party between the borrower and the lender\"?","options":["Herbert D. Hart","Sir John Paget","J.W. Gilbert","Halsbury's Laws of England"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"This definition belongs to J.W. Gilbert, and it stresses the traditional intermediation role of borrowing from one party and lending to another. Options A and B are writers whose definitions focus on receiving money on current account and paying or collecting cheques. Option D is a legal encyclopaedia whose definition is built around the banker's sole or predominant business of receiving deposits and handling cheques."}},{"itemId":"4a0dc85d-d15e-50b4-a7b8-4239d0fe8a5f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9023e836-19c1-5cda-bc7f-3e799a0917b8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Halsbury's Laws of England defines a banker as an individual, partnership or corporation whose sole or predominant business is banking. In that definition, banking means:","options":["The receipt of money on current or deposit account and the payment of cheques drawn by, and collection of cheques paid in by, a customer","The issue of currency notes and the management of the government's accounts","The granting of long-term loans to industry and the underwriting of new share issues","The giving of financial advice and the safe custody of customers' valuables for a fee"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Halsbury's definition is a deposit-and-cheque definition: receiving money on current or deposit account, paying cheques drawn by customers and collecting cheques paid in. Option C describes development or investment banking activities, and option B describes central banking functions. Option D lists ancillary services that banks offer, but they are not what the definition treats as the core of banking."}},{"itemId":"d34a43be-aafb-5a20-950b-a4bb5bb7db24","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"40bc9c19-b856-59bf-bc60-b991bc22ebc5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The definitions of a banker given by Hart, Paget and Halsbury are generally criticised because they:","options":["Describe deposit banking well but are not wide enough to cover other banking businesses","Ignore the role of cheques in the operation of a current account","Define a bank only by reference to the licence issued by the Central Bank","Treat bank employees and managers as bankers in their own right"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"All three definitions centre on receiving deposits and paying or collecting cheques, which fits deposit banking but leaves out the many other businesses banks now carry on. Option B is wrong because cheques are exactly what these definitions emphasise. Option D is wrong because none of them treats employees as bankers, and option C describes a statutory licensing approach rather than these writers' definitions."}},{"itemId":"9f745e81-efcd-5ba4-9bc7-d8812c9e0885","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8255cdf4-71b5-500e-93c9-a71584fed170","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Akwule and Others v The Queen (1963), the Supreme Court of Nigeria held that the word \"banker\" refers to:","options":["The company carrying on banking business, not a person who is a mere employee of the bank","The directors and shareholders who own the bank","The branch manager who approves facilities for customers","Any person who works in a bank and handles customers' money"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The court held that a banker is the company licensed to carry on banking business, so a branch manager is only an officer carrying out the bank's instructions. Option D reflects the everyday usage the court rejected. Options B and C are also wrong because the case established that neither managers nor directors and shareholders are the banker; any action by a customer lies against the bank itself."}},{"itemId":"bba96f16-5be8-5869-9893-36ebe48746c3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"55193103-6d04-5402-aea2-c5858c9f4a14","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Eze's cheque for N2 million drawn on his account at a Yaba branch is wrongly refused although his account is well in funds. He wants to sue. Following the reasoning in Akwule v The Queen (1963), his action should be brought against:","options":["The bank, because the bank and not its staff is his banker and debtor","The cashier who returned the cheque, because she handled it","The Central Bank of Nigeria, because it licenses the bank","The branch manager personally, because he runs the branch"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The banker-customer contract is with the bank as a company, so the bank is the debtor holding Mr Eze's money and is the proper defendant. Options B and D are wrong because staff such as managers and cashiers are only officers carrying out the bank's business. Option C is wrong because the regulator is not a party to the customer's contract with his bank."}},{"itemId":"5d16a005-4827-52d0-aa36-1d7a493d48c4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0f4b8e2-1506-5fc1-a40c-756979dfe6b3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In United Dominions Trust Ltd v Kirkwood (1966), the functions that identify a person as a banker were stated to include:","options":["Accepting money from and collecting cheques for customers, and honouring cheques or orders drawn on the bank","Holding a licence and keeping cash reserves with the Central Bank","Granting loans and charging interest at commercial rates","Giving financial advice and acting as executor of customers' wills"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The case identified the essential characteristics of banking as accepting money and collecting cheques for customers and honouring cheques drawn on the bank. Option C describes lending, which many non-banks such as moneylenders also do. Option B is a regulatory requirement rather than a test laid down in the case, and option D lists ancillary services, not identifying functions."}},{"itemId":"8d7ca119-9f8a-5f44-a5f2-5647d0003f03","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"854081f4-ec34-5cd4-81d2-7002002243c5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement about the definition of a \"customer\" of a bank is correct?","options":["There is no statutory definition, so the courts' decisions supply the principles for deciding who is a customer","The Bills of Exchange Act defines a customer as anyone who visits a bank more than once","A customer is defined by the CBN as anyone holding a bank account for at least six months","BOFIA defines a customer as any person who has cashed a cheque at a bank"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"No statute defines a customer, so whether a person is a customer is worked out from principles laid down in court decisions, such as intention to contract and the bank's acceptance. Options B, C and D all invent statutory or regulatory definitions that do not exist, and the frequency, cheque-cashing and duration tests they describe have been rejected by the courts."}},{"itemId":"4c3febe6-098d-5cc2-81c2-ea225ef1a813","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"46122c0c-a536-5a73-bb8b-432804bc0ee9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Great Western Railway Co. v London and County Banking Co. (1901), a man had cashed cheques at the defendant bank on his employer's behalf for many years. The court held that he was:","options":["Not a customer, because merely cashing cheques does not create a banker-customer relationship","A customer, because of the long period over which he dealt with the bank","A customer, because he dealt with the bank almost every day","Not a customer, because he had never been granted an overdraft"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The court held that frequent dealings over many years do not make a person a customer if the dealings, like cashing cheques over the counter, are not the kind that establish the relationship. Options B and C rely on duration and frequency, which the court rejected as the test. Option D is wrong because having or not having an overdraft is irrelevant; the absence of an account was the point."}},{"itemId":"a4f7d220-0061-5b5e-b919-9b024c32e883","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"64277e75-c2c4-5bb6-bd8c-2b31b02d2ddb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The banker-customer relationship does not come into existence unless both parties intend to enter into it. This principle shows that the relationship is essentially:","options":["Statutory","Contractual","Fiduciary in every case","A bailment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Because the relationship needs the intention of both parties, built on an offer by the prospective customer and acceptance by the bank, it is contractual. Option C is wrong because a fiduciary or trust element arises only in special situations. Option A is wrong because no statute creates the relationship, and option D describes only the safe custody aspect."}},{"itemId":"706d8f4a-6a6a-504d-9487-e78e9a777d4c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"78d9d3e4-fd79-5186-8fd6-3737d7c0b62e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Adaeze filled the account-opening forms at a bank in Surulere yesterday; the bank accepted her and opened a current account with her first deposit of N50,000. She has made no other transaction. In law, Adaeze is:","options":["Only a casual visitor until she obtains a loan from the bank","Not yet a customer, because she has not operated the account for six months","A customer, because duration and frequency of dealings are not decisive once the bank has accepted her and opened an account","Not yet a customer, because she has not issued any cheque on the account"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Once the bank accepts a person and opens an account, the relationship exists; how long or how often the person has dealt with the bank does not matter. Options B and D wrongly add a time or cheque-issuing requirement. Option A is wrong because borrowing is not needed; a depositor is already a customer."}},{"itemId":"028e9332-d99f-591f-b161-fe1c123fa92f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1af629b8-7243-521d-9558-d7b5c6061209","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following persons is MOST clearly a customer of Zenith Bank in law?","options":["Femi, who paid his school fees into the university's account at Zenith Bank","Tunde, a messenger who has cashed his employer's cheques at Zenith Bank every week for ten years","Bola, whose application to open a savings account has been accepted by Zenith Bank, which has opened the account","Ngozi, who walked into Zenith Bank once to make an enquiry about its loan rates"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A person becomes a customer when the bank accepts his offer to open an account, so Bola is a customer. Option B matches the messenger in the Great Western Railway case, who was not a customer despite years of cheque cashing. Options A and D are one-off dealings that do not create a relationship with the person concerned; in Femi's case the customer is the university."}},{"itemId":"d0344029-56a0-5f02-8f34-70b99b5e7cf6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d4dd4d46-fe35-593d-99dd-9f9ca62f4216","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Commissioners of Taxation v English, Scottish and Australian Bank (1920) is usually cited for the rule that:","options":["A bank owes a duty of secrecy subject to four qualifications","The banker is a debtor and not a trustee of money deposited","Duration of dealings is not of the essence; a person can become a customer as soon as an account is opened","A customer must have dealt with the bank for a reasonable period before being regarded as such"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"This case established that it is the opening of an account, not the length of the dealings, that makes a person a customer. Option D states the very view the case rejected. Option A is the rule in Tournier's case, and option B is the rule in Foley v Hill."}},{"itemId":"30483a04-ba2a-5df3-b158-5af957437466","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93976faf-9d3e-5905-a1ff-f7526af9ffa4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A person, firm or company becomes a customer when he makes an offer to become a customer and the bank duly accepts it. This shows that the banker-customer relationship is formed by:","options":["Registration with the Central Bank","Offer and acceptance, as in any contract","The issue of a cheque book","Frequent visits to the banking hall"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The relationship begins like any contract: the prospective customer offers to open and operate an account and the bank accepts. Option A is wrong because customers do not register with the Central Bank. Option D reflects the frequency test the courts rejected, and option C is wrong because many customers, such as savings account holders, never receive cheque books."}},{"itemId":"a08bacd1-8044-5e83-8b5d-6b8c51314317","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"863cfbe0-87ee-50c4-824a-15a08769f295","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Halima has a credit balance of N300,000 on her current account. In respect of that balance, the relationship between Halima and her bank is that of:","options":["Creditor (Halima) and debtor (the bank)","Beneficiary (Halima) and trustee (the bank)","Bailor (Halima) and bailee (the bank)","Debtor (Halima) and creditor (the bank)"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"When a customer's account is in credit, the bank has borrowed the money and owes it, so the bank is the debtor and the customer the creditor. Option D reverses the roles, which is the position only when the account is overdrawn. Option B is wrong because deposited money is not held on trust, and option C applies to items left for safe custody, not money deposited."}},{"itemId":"4edeccf5-4892-57b6-850a-a0d01d26f567","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4bc19a4e-09af-5ddd-acd2-be0f6a95bc28","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Sola's current account is overdrawn by N150,000 under an approved overdraft. In respect of the overdrawn balance:","options":["Sola is the creditor and the bank is the debtor","Sola is the principal and the bank is the agent","Sola is the mortgagee and the bank is the mortgagor","Sola is the debtor and the bank is the creditor"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"An overdraft is money the bank has lent, so the usual roles are reversed: the customer becomes the debtor and the bank the creditor. Option A describes an account in credit. Option B describes the agency relationship, which arises when the bank performs services such as collecting cheques. Option C reverses mortgage roles; in a secured loan the customer is the mortgagor and the bank the mortgagee."}},{"itemId":"e1b35023-6c83-52b3-bd3b-03aa1b8b4c13","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d412e7d8-9049-542e-a5d2-2b6fc1618aa0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Foley v Hill (1848), the House of Lords held that money paid into a bank by a customer:","options":["Is held by the bank on trust, so the bank must account to the customer for profits made with it","Becomes the bank's money, which it may use as it pleases, subject only to its obligation to repay an equivalent sum","Remains the customer's property and must be kept separately in the bank's vault","Is held by the bank as a bailee for reward and must be returned in the same notes and coins"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Foley v Hill established that deposited money becomes the banker's own money; the bank is a debtor who must repay an equal amount, not a trustee who must account for how the money is used. Option A states the trust view the court rejected. Options C and D wrongly treat the deposit as the customer's specific property, which is the position only for articles left in safe custody."}},{"itemId":"4aecc597-e94e-5bf0-8d12-4c88c4c1c509","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"78bf4be6-2fb6-5ee8-8fc0-22dd86471ab6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank lends part of its depositors' money to a manufacturer in Ikeja without asking the depositors. This is lawful because:","options":["The bank is the agent of the depositors in making loans","The depositors have impliedly appointed the bank as their trustee for investment","The Central Bank must first approve each loan on behalf of depositors","The bank, as debtor, is entitled to use money deposited without the customer's prior approval"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Once money is deposited it belongs to the bank, which only owes the customer an equivalent sum, so the bank may use it without consulting the customer. Option B is wrong because the bank is not a trustee of deposits. Option C invents a CBN approval requirement, and option A is wrong because lending the bank's own funds is not done as the depositor's agent."}},{"itemId":"0282a620-b1da-57c0-9e4d-970333ffcdf3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"768bde4c-6d8a-5221-9ae3-3767728cef64","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Joachimson v Swiss Bank Corporation (1921) is best known for establishing that:","options":["Payments into a running account discharge the earliest debits first","A bank is not liable to repay a current account balance until the customer has made a demand for it","Cashing cheques over a long period does not make a person a customer","A bank owes a duty of secrecy that continues after the account is closed"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Joachimson held that the bank's promise is to repay against the customer's demand, so no cause of action arises until a demand is made. Option D is the secrecy rule from Tournier's case. Option C is the Great Western Railway rule on who is a customer, and option A is Clayton's Rule."}},{"itemId":"4cc7e265-9583-5b51-af13-63320ba7bc6a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"144208bf-9b60-57db-9fa9-5fa34a13a64f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the ordinary law of debt, a debtor must seek out his creditor and pay him. In the banker-customer relationship, this rule is:","options":["Applied strictly: the bank must deliver the balance to the customer's home","Reversed: the customer (creditor) must seek out the bank (debtor) and demand payment","Replaced by a rule that the bank must repay automatically at the end of each quarter","Applied only to savings accounts and not to current accounts"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Because the bank undertakes to repay only on the customer's demand, the customer, who is the creditor, must come to the bank and ask for his money. Option A states the ordinary debt rule that does not apply to bank deposits. Options C and D are invented distinctions; the reversal applies generally and there is no automatic quarterly repayment."}},{"itemId":"f72fd41b-bdf7-5fd0-a55f-c6fe73db9bb3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"101aed9d-a0de-5949-b74f-ba99d1a477a9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the debtor-creditor relationship as explained in Joachimson v Swiss Bank Corporation, a bank undertakes to repay its current account customer:","options":["On oral demand made by telephone to any staff of the bank at any time","At the end of each financial year, without any demand","Only after giving the customer seven days' notice","On written demand, at the branch where the account is kept, during banking hours"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The bank's promise is to repay against the customer's written order, such as a cheque, presented at the branch where the account is kept and during banking hours, unless other arrangements are made. Option A is wrong because an oral telephone request at any time is not the required written demand. Option B ignores the need for a demand, and option C describes notice deposits, not current accounts."}},{"itemId":"7159f892-8dfb-5053-9deb-5298cfc01e12","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4934ad8f-39a4-5e8e-888f-d118fcb375f3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kemi instructs her bank to buy N500,000 worth of Federal Government bonds for her and to collect a cheque paid to her by a client in Kano. In performing these services, the bank acts as her:","options":["Bailee","Mortgagee","Agent","Debtor"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"When a bank makes payments, buys securities or collects cheques on a customer's behalf, it acts as the customer's agent, bringing her into dealings with third parties. Option A applies when the bank keeps her valuables in safe custody. Option B applies when the bank holds a mortgage over her property, and option D describes the bank's position regarding her credit balance, not the services."}},{"itemId":"43d33e8b-ad9d-59b4-8861-c8c355821d32","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f315174-841d-5e8b-a703-8872b46fcdd1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chief Okonkwo deposits his Certificate of Occupancy and some gold jewellery with his bank for safe-keeping and pays a fee. The relationship created is that of:","options":["Bailor (Chief Okonkwo) and bailee (the bank)","Bailee (Chief Okonkwo) and bailor (the bank)","Creditor (Chief Okonkwo) and debtor (the bank)","Mortgagor (Chief Okonkwo) and mortgagee (the bank)"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Leaving goods with another for safe-keeping is a bailment; the owner who hands them over is the bailor and the bank that keeps them is the bailee. Option B reverses the roles. Option C applies to money deposited, which becomes the bank's own, while these items remain his property. Option D would apply only if the items were given as security for a loan."}},{"itemId":"99c7f7f0-2621-5462-bfb0-1b85eedddced","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fe1b2ddc-dad3-50c6-8096-1a91547a557c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why is the standard of care expected of a bank in keeping customers' items in safe custody regarded as high?","options":["Because the customer is a debtor to the bank for the value of the items","Because the bank becomes the owner of the items deposited","Because the bank is a trustee of every item kept in its vault","Because the bank charges for the service and is therefore a bailee for reward"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A bank that is paid for safe custody is a bailee for reward and must take a high degree of care of the items. Option B is wrong because items in safe custody remain the customer's property. Option C wrongly converts bailment into trust, and option A confuses the debtor-creditor relationship with bailment."}},{"itemId":"6d4b3ccb-1e53-5ff2-bbb9-cd1a635b65b6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3cb30e9f-02b5-576c-af6e-7058e1192b71","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Musa obtains a N10 million loan from his bank and gives the bank a legal mortgage over his house in Lekki as security. In this arrangement:","options":["Musa is the mortgagee and the bank is the mortgagor","Musa is the principal and the bank is the agent","Musa is the bailee and the bank is the bailor","Musa is the mortgagor and the bank is the mortgagee"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The person who gives property as security is the mortgagor, and the lender who takes the security is the mortgagee. Option A reverses the roles, a common slip because both words sound alike. Option C describes safe custody with the roles also reversed, and option B describes agency services, not a secured loan."}},{"itemId":"f484d2fd-032b-525b-bf02-4d480aaad56e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b96d488a-9ebc-599d-813d-84a58417c14d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes the general relationship from the special relationship between banker and customer?","options":["The general relationship applies only to companies; the special relationship applies only to individuals","The general relationship carries higher duties of care than the special relationship","The general relationship is created by statute; the special relationship is created by contract","The general relationship arises from ordinary deposit, withdrawal and cheque business; the special relationship arises from additional services such as safe custody, guarantees and standing orders"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The general relationship covers the ordinary business of keeping an account, while special relationships arise from extra services, often under separate agreements, and may carry higher duties. Option A invents a distinction based on the type of customer. Option C is wrong because both are essentially contractual, and option B reverses the position on duties of care."}},{"itemId":"e2622833-f78d-5a29-9c33-8c113b1c104b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b2d1598b-d04f-524c-8b69-5b4bed74f1ef","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the relationships that may exist between a banker and a customer?","options":["Employer and employee","Principal and agent","Debtor and creditor","Bailor and bailee"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A customer does not employ the bank or work for it, so employer and employee is not a banker-customer relationship. Option C is the primary relationship arising from deposits and loans. Option B arises when the bank collects cheques or makes payments for the customer, and option D arises from safe custody."}},{"itemId":"d003a6bd-623a-5ea9-b516-f793d7dfbaad","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c11e9e96-85ee-56c8-806e-7958c096e7ca","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank receives N5 million from Mrs Ade with clear instructions that it is to be held for her late brother's children and paid out only for their school fees. In this situation the bank may be regarded as:","options":["A trustee, with the children as beneficiaries","A bailee, with Mrs Ade as bailor","An ordinary debtor, free to use the money as its own","A mortgagee, with the children as mortgagors"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Where a bank holds funds in a fiduciary capacity for a stated purpose and for named beneficiaries, the relationship is one of trustee and beneficiary. Option B applies to physical items in safe custody. Option D concerns property given as loan security, and option C describes ordinary deposits, which are not earmarked for others in this way."}},{"itemId":"bd160f0c-daf4-5f1a-ba5c-87443d30bca8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"778633f8-92a3-5002-845b-42cd75c209f2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A banker's lien is best described as:","options":["A general lien giving the bank the right to retain a customer's securities that come into its hands in the ordinary course of business until the customer's debt is paid","A court order freezing a customer's account in favour of a judgment creditor","A particular lien that allows the bank to keep only the item on which it has done work","The bank's right to combine a credit balance on one account with a debit balance on another"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A banker's lien is a general lien: it extends to all the customer's securities and valuables received in the ordinary course of business, and the bank may retain them until the debt is paid. Option C describes a particular lien, which is narrower. Option D describes the right of set-off, and option B describes a garnishee order."}},{"itemId":"2bf3a2a8-ab8f-53dc-a287-3ae56bd82f5b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b75eaa24-95de-52ce-834f-26a08a3e784a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Bello owes his bank N2 million on an overdraft. The bank holds (i) share certificates it received to collect his dividends and (ii) a sealed box of jewellery he left purely for safe custody. Over which items can the bank exercise its banker's lien?","options":["The share certificates only","Both the share certificates and the jewellery","Neither, because a lien requires a court order","The jewellery only"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The banker's lien covers securities that reach the bank in the ordinary course of banking business, such as share certificates held for collecting dividends, but it does not normally extend to items left for safe custody or for a specific purpose. Option D and option B are wrong because safe custody items fall outside the lien. Option C is wrong because a lien arises by operation of law and needs no court order."}},{"itemId":"04f08ce5-6cf1-5b1d-a495-4bc943fb0dac","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f224b528-19e7-57a1-922e-0618fe642221","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following statements about a banker's lien is correct?","options":["It can be exercised only after the customer gives written consent","It applies even where the customer and bank have agreed that no lien shall arise","It applies to all items deposited for safe custody","It confers on the bank an implied power of sale and is lost if the bank voluntarily parts with possession"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The banker's lien carries an implied power of sale, and because it depends on possession it ends if the bank voluntarily gives up the items. Option C is wrong because safe custody items are normally outside the lien. Option A is wrong because no consent is required, and option B is wrong because an inconsistent agreement excludes the lien."}},{"itemId":"27d5c445-3fdb-5b07-bb40-b02dbc24488e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0f7d5e3-edb3-5f0e-859f-3914f092a9ed","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The right of a bank to combine a customer's credit balance on one account with a debit balance on another account of the same customer, and use the credit to reduce the debt, is called the right of:","options":["Appropriation","Set-off","Garnishee","Lien"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Set-off, also called combination of accounts, lets the bank merge balances on accounts held by the same customer in the same right. Option D is the right to retain the customer's securities. Option A concerns which debt a payment discharges, and option C is a court process attaching funds for a third party."}},{"itemId":"f9ff7e67-3fe2-5aca-8eaa-a858e1800e76","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"605c94da-9535-5eef-afdd-0e3382b035fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a condition for a bank to exercise its right of set-off?","options":["There must be no agreement excluding set-off","The debt must be due and payable","The customer must have given the bank a power of attorney","The accounts must be in the same name and same right"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A power of attorney concerns authority to operate an account and has nothing to do with set-off. Options A, B and D are genuine conditions: the accounts must be held in the same name and same capacity, the debt must be due, and set-off must not have been excluded by agreement."}},{"itemId":"cde55c0e-6060-5949-a501-3004a38e7ab7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8bcd5eee-1222-5fb8-9417-0f40d7432d2b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chidi has three accounts with his bank: a personal savings account in credit by N450,000, a personal current account overdrawn by N620,000 (due and payable), and an account he operates as trustee for his late father's estate in credit by N300,000. If the bank lawfully exercises its right of set-off, what is Chidi's position?","options":["He owes the bank N620,000","He owes the bank N170,000","He owes the bank N1,070,000","The bank owes him N130,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Only accounts held in the same right can be combined, so the trust account is excluded: N620,000 overdraft less N450,000 savings leaves N170,000 owed to the bank. Option D wrongly includes the trust account (N450,000 + N300,000 - N620,000 = N130,000). Option A ignores set-off entirely, and option C wrongly adds the balances instead of netting them."}},{"itemId":"808d1bfd-261f-5b1d-8c80-bda079070b82","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a84a9707-70c3-562a-a762-e7de1cf05247","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Clayton's Rule, from Devaynes v Noble (1816), states that in a running account, where neither party has appropriated a payment:","options":["The first item on the debit side is discharged by the first item on the credit side","The most recent debit is discharged first by any new credit","Credits are shared equally among all outstanding debits","Credits are applied to the largest debit first"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Clayton's Rule works on a first-in, first-out basis: the earliest debit is the first to be extinguished by credits paid in. Option B reverses the order to last-in, first-out. Options C and D describe allocation methods that the rule does not use."}},{"itemId":"821bc660-c0e4-505c-8793-cd8787fad58e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c8b001a3-b85f-59bf-81d2-fad5ece3d9ce","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a customer who owes a bank several debts makes a payment, the correct order for deciding which debt the payment settles is:","options":["The bank always decides, and the customer has no say","The customer may appropriate first; if he does not, the bank may; if neither does, Clayton's Rule applies on a running account","The court must be asked to decide each time","Clayton's Rule always applies, whatever the parties say"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The customer has the first right to direct which debt or cheque a payment should meet; if he is silent the bank may appropriate, and only where neither does so does Clayton's first-in, first-out rule apply to a running account. Option A ignores the customer's right. Option D ignores the parties' rights to appropriate, and option C is unnecessary because the rules settle the matter without going to court."}},{"itemId":"f6b90ba4-12f4-5040-8b90-9314e1c0dc77","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"71754df1-95cf-517d-b899-7475456be5ee","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Musa and Bayo are partners whose firm account is overdrawn by N800,000 when Bayo dies. The bank does not stop the account, and afterwards N500,000 is paid in and N300,000 is withdrawn. Applying Clayton's Rule, how much of the debt that existed at Bayo's death is still outstanding?","options":["N300,000","N600,000","N800,000","N500,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Under first-in, first-out, the N500,000 paid in after the death reduces the oldest debit, so N800,000 - N500,000 = N300,000 of the old debt remains; the later N300,000 withdrawal is a new debt. Option B is the total balance on the account (N800,000 - N500,000 + N300,000), which mixes old and new debt. Option C ignores the credits, and option D is simply the amount paid in."}},{"itemId":"dc8924a3-ff9e-5d43-9a79-e44c1e694121","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c1ac7c2-bff5-5076-b449-5faa515d0f55","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Ojo guaranteed Dapo's overdraft and gave notice ending her guarantee when the overdraft stood at N1,200,000. The bank continued the same account, and afterwards Dapo paid in N700,000 and withdrew N900,000. Under Clayton's Rule, what is Mrs Ojo's remaining liability for the guaranteed debt?","options":["N200,000","N1,200,000","N500,000","N1,400,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Because the bank did not break the account, the N700,000 paid in discharges the earliest debits, which are the guaranteed ones: N1,200,000 - N700,000 = N500,000 remains guaranteed, while the N900,000 withdrawal is new unguaranteed debt. Option D is the total overdraft (N1,200,000 - N700,000 + N900,000). Option B ignores the credits, and option A is only the net movement after the notice."}},{"itemId":"d0eb349a-0ed1-59f9-818a-f7f9c070e251","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d1c28ac5-030c-54c7-904f-b10a0e887ade","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a right of a banker?","options":["To close a customer's account without any notice whenever it wishes","To pay cheques drawn after receiving notice of the customer's death","To charge reasonable commission for services and interest on advances","To disclose a customer's balance to the customer's spouse on request"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A bank is entitled to charge reasonable commission and interest for its services, as agreed and within CBN guidelines on bank charges. Option A is wrong because reasonable notice must be given before closing an account. Option D breaches the duty of secrecy, and option B is wrong because death ends the bank's authority to pay."}},{"itemId":"58b81cea-fcbc-5978-b4b4-39f0c6164a60","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dda539cd-7e05-5da9-8b95-16b2c93a873e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a right of a banker?","options":["To obtain reimbursement for expenses incurred on the customer's behalf","To refuse to pay a properly drawn cheque on a funded account where there is no legal bar","To return cheques that would create an unauthorised overdraft","To recall an overdraft on current account and expect immediate repayment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Refusing a properly drawn cheque on an account in funds, with no legal bar, is wrongful dishonour and a breach of the bank's duty, not a right. Options A, C and D are genuine rights: an overdraft is repayable on demand, expenses on the customer's behalf can be recovered, and cheques that exceed the agreed limit may be returned."}},{"itemId":"2c092cb0-8a4d-533a-aca5-9cb0bb0acf25","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"15fc0616-936a-52ef-a6a8-05f26cb5cb8b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank is under a duty to honour its customer's cheque when:","options":["The cheque is presented at any bank, at any hour, even if the account is not in funds","There are sufficient funds or an agreed overdraft, the cheque is properly drawn, and there is no legal bar to payment","The payee is a well-known customer of the bank, regardless of the drawer's balance","The branch manager approves, even if the drawer's signature differs from the mandate"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The duty to pay arises only when the account is in funds or covered by an overdraft, the cheque is regularly drawn according to the mandate, and nothing such as a garnishee order or stop instruction prevents payment. Option A ignores funds and banking hours. Option C wrongly looks at the payee rather than the drawer's account, and option D ignores the mandate."}},{"itemId":"01a36ee9-f5b1-5a74-9b5d-a2390d319ee5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"95e2df9c-4b1a-5ee4-8c20-a550293fb325","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank carelessly returns Mrs Okoro's cheque marked \"insufficient funds\" although her account was well in credit. The bank:","options":["Can avoid liability by apologising to the payee","Has no liability, because a bank may dishonour any cheque at its discretion","Is liable to her in damages for breach of contract, including injury to her credit","Is liable only if she proves she lost a contract worth over N1 million"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Wrongful dishonour of a cheque when the customer is in funds is a breach of the bank's contractual duty, and the bank is liable in damages, which can include harm to the customer's credit and reputation. Option B wrongly gives the bank a free discretion. Option D invents a monetary threshold, and option A is wrong because an apology does not cancel the breach."}},{"itemId":"19eba7c5-91fe-5cd2-8135-206c1f9ccfad","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c3c5416d-c333-5d08-867c-c57b52555ad0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Tournier v National Provincial and Union Bank of England (1924), the Court of Appeal held that a bank's duty of secrecy:","options":["Ends as soon as the customer's account is closed","Is a legal duty arising from contract, but is qualified by four exceptions","Is absolute and admits no exceptions at all","Is only a moral duty that cannot be enforced in court"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Tournier established that confidentiality is an implied term of the banker-customer contract, enforceable at law, but subject to four qualifications. Option D is wrong because the duty is legal, not merely moral. Option C ignores the four exceptions, and option A is wrong because the duty continues after the account is closed."}},{"itemId":"f660d518-7f65-5f87-a2f6-9252be92d41e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2977a2e2-6bc3-5e92-bd9b-3a8cdae21a86","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four exceptions to the banker's duty of secrecy laid down in Tournier's case?","options":["Where the customer's spouse requests the information","Where there is a duty to the public to disclose","Where the interests of the bank require disclosure","Where disclosure is under compulsion by law"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The four Tournier exceptions are compulsion by law, duty to the public, the bank's own interest, and the express or implied consent of the customer; a spouse's request is not among them. Options B, C and D are genuine exceptions. A bank should not even discuss a wife's account with her husband, or the reverse, without authority."}},{"itemId":"c231abee-4da2-5123-86c3-521dbe456c2e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b581525b-fb4d-5dcd-9234-5bb7ad130f64","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Federal High Court in Lagos issues an order requiring First Bank to produce the statements of account of a customer standing trial for fraud. If the bank complies, its disclosure falls under which exception to the duty of secrecy?","options":["Interest of the bank","Compulsion by law","Consent of the customer","Duty to the public"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A court order or subpoena compels disclosure, so the bank discloses under compulsion by law. Option D covers cases where a wider public danger requires disclosure without a legal order. Option A applies when the bank must protect itself, for example when suing for a debt, and option C does not apply because the customer has not agreed."}},{"itemId":"df5df41f-3787-5325-afea-b943833bccb7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d2fb2f67-b0a1-5a45-8c9e-45681a7cfe05","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank sues a guarantor for the overdraft of the principal debtor, and in its claim it discloses the principal debtor's account balance. This disclosure is justified under which exception to the duty of secrecy?","options":["Duty to the public","Interest of the bank","Compulsion by law","Implied consent of the guarantor"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"When a bank demands payment from a guarantor or sues to recover a debt, it must reveal the account position to protect its own interest. Option C requires a court order or statute directing disclosure. Option A concerns danger to the public, and option D is wrong because the consent that matters is the customer's, not the guarantor's."}},{"itemId":"7b93fc1d-922e-5076-a31a-7f33ad819a78","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7e29382b-4aeb-5448-b269-321553215fb9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"During a war, a bank discovers that a customer is trading with an enemy country and reports this to the authorities. The bank's disclosure falls under the exception of:","options":["Interest of the bank","Compulsion by law","Express consent of the customer","Duty to the public"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Where the danger to the state or the public outweighs the customer's private interest, as with trading with the enemy in wartime, the bank may disclose under duty to the public. Option A concerns the bank protecting itself in recovering debts. Option C does not apply since the customer has not agreed, and option B would need a court order or statute."}},{"itemId":"38f6a1ff-0803-5b82-add9-c11e46efd240","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5913da5c-9c72-523c-bad1-2c51b89976e8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kunle writes to his bank authorising it to send details of his account to his auditors for preparing his company's final accounts. The bank's disclosure is permitted under which exception to secrecy?","options":["Duty to the public","Express consent of the customer","Compulsion by law","Interest of the bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The customer himself authorised the disclosure in writing, so it falls under express consent. Option A covers matters of public danger. Option C requires a court order or statute, and option D covers the bank protecting its own position, which is not the case here."}},{"itemId":"bc9fb123-edb2-5aa8-8898-3afc01303ba2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a96bebc8-45a1-5e67-b7d3-8c619dd23ce7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Funke closed her account with a bank in 2023. In 2026 a journalist asks the bank about her past transactions. The bank:","options":["May disclose freely, because the duty of secrecy ended when the account was closed","Must disclose because the journalist is acting in the public interest","May disclose because more than two years have passed since closure","Must still keep her affairs confidential, because the duty of secrecy continues after the account is closed"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The duty of secrecy does not end when the account is closed; information obtained during the relationship stays confidential. Options A and C wrongly treat closure or the passage of time as ending the duty. Option B is wrong because a journalist's curiosity is not the kind of public duty that justifies disclosure."}},{"itemId":"850c290f-bb87-5eaf-b150-c4bd102f3bfb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6383260b-e962-597a-b7ec-6bd292494d58","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a duty owed by a bank to its customer?","options":["To provide statements only when the customer pays a court fee","To repay the customer's balance at his home without any demand","To give reasonable notice before closing an account in credit","To guarantee every loan the customer takes from other lenders"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A bank must give reasonable notice before closing a credit account so the customer can make other arrangements and so cheques already issued are not returned. Option B is wrong because the customer must make a demand at the bank. Option D is no duty at all, and option A is wrong because providing regular statements is part of the bank's ordinary duty."}},{"itemId":"33a47dfb-0dff-528c-b0a4-c90483a3f986","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"352745fb-6ebe-5bbc-96c4-cf4d271d697b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why must a bank regularly provide its customer with a statement of account?","options":["So the bank can avoid its duty to honour cheques","So the bank can charge commission on turnover on savings accounts","So the customer can reconcile his own records with the bank's and detect errors","So the customer's balance can be published for creditors"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A statement shows all transactions passing through the account, letting the customer reconcile it with his own records and spot errors or fraud. Option A is wrong because statements do not affect the duty to honour cheques. Option D would breach the duty of secrecy, and option B is wrong because commission on turnover is charged on current accounts, not savings accounts."}},{"itemId":"69f728e6-508d-51be-bc9d-331b6c3b5864","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"414e7e3b-d5a8-532e-88ba-7a90011d2834","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank officer notices several unusual withdrawals on an elderly customer's account, made with cheques whose signatures look slightly different. The bank's correct duty is to:","options":["Say nothing, because the customer must examine his own account","Publish the details so that other banks can be warned","Close the account immediately without telling the customer","Draw the customer's attention to the suspicious activity to prevent forgery or unauthorised dealing"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A bank must alert the customer to suspicious or irregular events on the account so that forgery or unauthorised dealings can be stopped. Option A ignores this duty of care. Option C is wrong because closure needs reasonable notice and does not protect the customer, and option B would breach the duty of secrecy."}},{"itemId":"51608503-b66b-5ebb-9279-53bbf3e724a8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"328c8395-0517-50ba-aff4-02d8c81f34de","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following does NOT end a bank's authority to pay its customer's cheques?","options":["Service of a garnishee order on the account","The customer travelling abroad on holiday","Countermand (stop payment) by the customer","Notice of the customer's death"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A customer's travel does not affect the bank's mandate; cheques he signed remain payable. Options A, C and D all end or suspend the bank's authority to pay: death cancels all mandates, a valid stop instruction must be obeyed, and a garnishee order attaches the balance."}},{"itemId":"b44ab11d-44d3-54ad-8563-d23a16866be6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f44c41b-1e03-58c2-81a0-85640b521a23","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For a customer's instruction to stop payment of a cheque (countermand) to be effective, it should be:","options":["In writing, clear and unambiguous, signed according to the mandate, and received before the cheque is paid","Made orally to any staff of the bank, even after the cheque has been paid","Sent to the Central Bank of Nigeria for registration","Approved by the payee of the cheque"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The drawer may stop a cheque at any time before it is paid, but the instruction should be in writing, clear about which cheque it covers, and signed in line with the mandate. Option B fails on both form and timing, since a paid cheque cannot be stopped. Options C and D add parties who play no part in a countermand."}},{"itemId":"c522e8c3-e489-5d81-8434-56dd7af3404d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"961efb09-f4f5-51d1-969a-a095cf9dc630","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank pays a cheque on which the drawer's signature has been forged. The bank:","options":["Can debit the account if the payee is a regular customer","Can debit half the amount to the customer and bear the other half","Cannot debit the customer's account, because a forged signature makes the cheque wholly inoperative and it carries no mandate","Can debit the customer's account, provided the forgery was skilful"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A cheque with a forged drawer's signature is wholly inoperative and is not the customer's mandate, so the bank has no authority to debit his account. Option D wrongly suggests that a skilful forgery shifts the loss. Options A and B invent ways of sharing liability that do not exist. The position may differ only where the customer's own conduct, such as failing to report a known forgery, stops him from denying the signature."}},{"itemId":"43818352-8833-5370-9502-0b3aa01f4ca9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"596171aa-fe45-50bc-a3c7-a44f5e67c8f4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank receives reliable notice that its customer, Mr Danjuma, has died. Cheques he signed the week before are presented the next day. The bank should:","options":["Pay them only if they are for less than N100,000","Pay them, because they were signed while he was alive","Not pay them, because death cancels all mandates and authorities on the account","Pay them to his eldest son on request"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The death of a customer cancels the bank's authority to pay, so once the bank has notice of death no further cheques should be honoured, whenever they were signed. Option B is wrong because the date of signing does not keep the mandate alive. Option A invents a limit, and option D is wrong because the account must be dealt with by the personal representatives under probate or letters of administration."}},{"itemId":"3b2654f2-740e-573d-acdb-e32538ab76a4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2c0db66c-a2da-5e0b-840d-4e8af5ed2ea4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a duty of a bank customer?","options":["To accept any charge the bank imposes, even if it exceeds what was agreed","To keep the bank's affairs secret from the Central Bank","To give the bank seven days' notice before every cheque withdrawal from a current account","To inform the bank without delay of suspicious dealings such as a lost cheque book or forgery of his signature"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A customer must promptly tell the bank of things like lost cheques or forgeries; failing to do so may stop him from later disputing the loss. Option C is wrong because current account balances are withdrawable without notice. Option B is not a customer duty, and option A is wrong because the customer pays charges as agreed or as allowed by CBN guidelines, and may protest overcharges."}},{"itemId":"4b403a6b-d271-51f6-80af-165123cd6e02","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"12cb5325-104e-57c5-9fce-28086cc89624","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In London Joint Stock Bank v Macmillan and Arthur (1918), a clerk raised the amount of a cheque the partners had signed with blank spaces left around the figures. The House of Lords held that:","options":["The customer owes a duty to draw cheques with reasonable care so as not to facilitate fraud, and must bear the loss caused by his carelessness","The bank must always bear the loss on an altered cheque, whatever the customer's conduct","The bank's duty of secrecy prevented it from querying the cheque","The clerk became a customer of the bank by presenting the cheque"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The case established the customer's duty to take reasonable care in drawing cheques; because the customer left spaces that made alteration easy, the customer bore the loss. Option B states the general rule the case qualified. Option D confuses the facts with the rules on who is a customer, and option C has nothing to do with secrecy."}},{"itemId":"7d4d3a12-3b66-5368-8848-10d67ef7295f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"011de4b9-b663-51ef-a6d3-70c7bc3821b7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a right of a current account customer?","options":["To close the account and leave an unpaid overdraft behind","To withdraw his money by cheque without giving notice, provided the account is in funds","To have his cheques paid at any time of day or night at any branch","To overdraw the account without any arrangement with the bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A current account balance is payable on demand, so the customer may draw cheques without notice as long as there are funds or an agreed overdraft. Option D is wrong because an overdraft needs the bank's agreement. Option C ignores the rule on banking hours and the branch where the account is kept, and option A is wrong because a customer may close his account only if he is not indebted to the bank."}},{"itemId":"5a23184f-1905-5f9e-8b45-12d87ad8acc2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cef2df79-ee56-5a51-be4c-f343dde64581","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A court order attaching the funds in a customer's account to satisfy a judgment debt that the customer owes to a third party is called a:","options":["Power of attorney","Countermand","Garnishee order","Banker's lien"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A garnishee order attaches money that the bank owes the customer so that it can be used to pay the customer's judgment creditor. Option D is the bank's own right to retain securities. Option B is the customer's instruction to stop a cheque, and option A is an authority given to an agent to act for the customer."}},{"itemId":"d793aab8-17fa-59c8-9990-b4e7cf31fa04","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"520bfa75-74a3-5122-9eb4-0a408cbec13c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Adamu obtains a court judgment against Mrs Lawal for N3 million and applies to attach her account at Union Bank. In the garnishee proceedings, Union Bank is the:","options":["Judgment creditor","Garnishee","Trustee in bankruptcy","Judgment debtor"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The garnishee is the third party, here the bank, that owes money to the judgment debtor and is ordered to hold it for the judgment creditor. Option A is Mr Adamu, who won the judgment. Option D is Mrs Lawal, who owes the judgment debt, and option C is the officer who takes over a bankrupt's property."}},{"itemId":"48937359-b54d-5270-a252-9e52470be538","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"05b71be4-6c07-5110-810c-f269cc2eae97","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a garnishee order nisi is served on a bank in respect of a customer's account, the bank should:","options":["Ignore the order until the customer gives consent","Close the account and return the balance to the customer","Freeze the account and pay out only when the order is made absolute or as the court directs","Immediately pay the whole balance to the judgment creditor"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"An order nisi freezes the funds provisionally; the bank pays the judgment creditor only after the order is made absolute or as the court directs. Option D is premature at the nisi stage. Option A is wrong because the customer's consent is irrelevant to a court order, and option B would defeat the order and expose the bank to liability."}},{"itemId":"108df014-eb9c-5431-b475-fb4442e482bc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c1421726-a50b-56ea-9e8f-44a623632283","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Once a bank learns that a bankruptcy order has been made against its individual customer, which of the following is correct?","options":["The bank must immediately pay the balance to the customer's family","The customer's authority to operate the account ceases and the trustee in bankruptcy steps into his shoes","The customer may continue to issue cheques until his debts are fully paid","The bank loses its right of set-off for dealings before the bankruptcy"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"On bankruptcy the customer can no longer operate the account, the bank should stop paying his cheques, and the bankrupt's property vests in a trustee in bankruptcy. Option C ignores the end of the customer's authority. Option A is wrong because the family has no claim, and option D is wrong because set-off may still apply to mutual dealings before the bankruptcy."}},{"itemId":"89cbc9cf-6f2d-5149-b981-51fe67a7e210","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cea07d27-c20c-5f5f-b5ad-988afa165423","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the bankruptcy of a customer who owes it an unsecured overdraft, a bank's normal remedy is to:","options":["Seize any of the customer's property it chooses without court approval","Recover the debt from the customer's employer","Charge the debt to the customer's joint account holder's personal account","Prove in the bankruptcy for the debt and share in the distribution with other creditors"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"An unsecured creditor proves its debt in the bankruptcy and is paid from the estate according to statutory priorities. Option A is wrong because, without security, the bank has no right to seize property. Option B invents liability for the employer, and option C ignores the rule that only accounts in the same name and right may be combined."}},{"itemId":"db9ce9f4-8f19-583c-9bdc-912f8b3c1c38","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c04c94f5-391e-51be-ab15-36c241b03469","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking, a mandate is:","options":["The bank's right to retain securities until a debt is paid","A court order freezing a customer's account","A letter from a referee confirming the customer's character","The formal authority given by a customer specifying who may sign cheques and operate the account"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A mandate tells the bank who may operate the account and how, for example which signatories must sign cheques, and the bank must follow it strictly. Option B describes a garnishee or freezing order. Option A describes a lien, and option C describes a reference."}},{"itemId":"1c4e492d-ed18-5701-a9da-e0aba11158e5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"64b686ae-4caf-545a-a9bb-25e22754ca00","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-065","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The mandate on the joint account of Tope and Yemi reads \"BOTH TO SIGN\". A cheque for N80,000 is presented bearing only Tope's signature. The bank should:","options":["Pay it and ask Yemi to sign afterwards","Pay it, because Tope is one of the account holders","Pay it if the amount is below N100,000","Refuse payment, because the cheque does not comply with the mandate"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Under a \"both to sign\" mandate, a cheque signed by only one holder is not properly drawn, and a payment contrary to the mandate may not be debited to the account. Option B would fit an \"either to sign\" mandate. Options A and C invent exceptions that the mandate does not allow."}},{"itemId":"fadced98-9584-5dcb-92b4-7c9e88db2240","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"46a1ba10-3d7e-5156-a81e-450506c9e990","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-066","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"One of the two holders of a joint deposit account, Mr and Mrs Nwosu, dies. Unless the account terms say otherwise, the credit balance normally:","options":["Is shared equally between the survivor and the deceased's estate","Passes to the surviving account holder by the rule of survivorship","Is transferred to the bank's dormant accounts ledger","Is paid entirely to the deceased's estate"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Survivorship rules apply to joint accounts, so on the death of one holder the balance normally belongs to the survivor. Option A and option D ignore survivorship. Option C confuses death of a joint holder with dormancy, which arises only when an account is not operated for a long time."}},{"itemId":"6926c31c-ca17-51f3-8ffa-e5305d6f7895","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f0c480bb-0cbc-54a9-8971-de9c6fc9514a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-067","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A formal written authority, signed and sealed, by which one person (the donor) empowers another (the donee or attorney) to act for him generally or in specified matters is a:","options":["Bank reference","Power of attorney","Letter of indemnity","Standing order"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A power of attorney is the formal instrument by which the donor appoints an attorney (the donee) to act on his behalf. Option D is a customer's instruction to make regular fixed payments. Option C is a promise to compensate the bank for loss, and option A is a statement about a customer's character or standing."}},{"itemId":"4793cd81-800a-5542-886a-10413cd51735","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aa3b8dcb-0316-517b-abd2-0627e33294ff","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-068","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Alhaji Sani, who is travelling to Saudi Arabia for three months, signs a power of attorney authorising his son only to withdraw funds to pay the school fees of his younger children. This is a:","options":["Specific power of attorney","Direct debit authority","Joint and several mandate","General power of attorney"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A specific power of attorney gives the donee authority for a stated purpose only, here paying school fees. Option D would allow the son to do anything the donor could lawfully do through an attorney. Option C concerns liability of joint borrowers such as partners, and option B lets a third party collect variable amounts from the account."}},{"itemId":"1cb8e32c-d8cb-507a-b0b0-858fe7bd651e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a031c67-6265-56ca-8fd0-592b7889d31f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-069","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On receiving a power of attorney from a customer, which of the following should a bank NOT do?","options":["Allow the attorney to do anything he asks, even outside the powers listed in the instrument","Keep a certified true copy in the customer's file","Ensure that the power of attorney is stamped","Sight the original power of attorney"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The bank must conduct the account strictly within the powers listed in the instrument, so allowing acts outside them is wrong. Options B, C and D are genuine steps: the bank should see the original, keep a certified copy on file and confirm that the document has been stamped."}},{"itemId":"814a8c1a-9c83-52cf-8f77-655791dbf188","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18d6cf8e-e043-5173-95af-3c7a7cbbe536","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-070","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following does NOT revoke or end a power of attorney?","options":["The bankruptcy of the donor or donee","The death of the donor","The donor expressly revoking the power","The donor's account receiving a large salary credit"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A salary credit has no effect on the attorney's authority. Options A, B and C are recognised ways a power of attorney ends, along with completion of its purpose, expiry of its time, mental incapacity of either party and implied revocation, such as the donor returning to run his own account."}},{"itemId":"99cc1e37-6131-561b-856f-4d70e0c15b5b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc961f93-a951-568f-b526-e89ea0575866","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-071","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Three trustees operate a trust account for an orphanage in Ibadan. One trustee asks the bank to let him delegate his signing authority to his secretary. The bank should:","options":["Refuse, because delegation of authority is not allowed on trust accounts and all trustees must sign","Agree, as long as he signs a letter of authority","Agree, if the other two trustees are abroad","Agree, because any account holder may delegate his mandate"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Trustees must act personally, so delegation of authority is not permitted on trust accounts and all the trustees must sign cheques. Options B, C and D all assume that a trustee may hand his authority to someone else, which the rule forbids, even though ordinary account holders may delegate in writing."}},{"itemId":"a3201138-5874-58a1-943d-7eff8783bcce","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50c16a40-fe6f-5fa0-af3a-bf7eaf94e1e6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-072","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Thomas Ude opens an account as guardian for his 12-year-old son, John Ude. How is the account normally titled and operated?","options":["In the names of Thomas and John as a joint account, either to sign","In the bank's name, operated by the branch manager on John's behalf","In John's name alone, operated by John with his own signature","In the guardian's name with the words \"IN RE\" before the minor's name, operated by the guardian until the minor reaches 18"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A minor lacks contractual capacity, so the account is opened and operated by the parent or guardian, titled with the guardian's name followed by \"IN RE\" and the minor's name and date of birth, until the child is 18. Option C ignores the minor's lack of capacity. Option A wrongly lets a minor sign as a joint holder, and option B is wrong because the bank never operates a customer's account for him."}},{"itemId":"bfbb55d2-678f-5de7-a12f-ef9a142f9d2c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"40dd37d4-2e2c-586f-8ad7-649af99ff2f8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-073","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When opening an account for a partnership, which of the following should a bank do?","options":["Open the account in the firm's name and include a joint and several liability clause in the mandate","Require only one partner to sign the application, whatever the deed says","Open the account in the name of the senior partner alone","Obtain a certificate of incorporation and memorandum of association"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A partnership account is opened in the firm's name, with a mandate signed by all partners and a clause making them jointly and severally liable for its debts. Option C is wrong because the account belongs to the firm. Option D lists company documents, and a partnership is not incorporated. Option B is wrong because the application should be jointly signed by the partners."}},{"itemId":"e10022dd-44c7-568f-a6c0-aa02793d88dc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c3eebf7-010c-5330-92ce-e95a57cb3626","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-074","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT normally required by a bank when opening an account for a limited liability company?","options":["The certificate of incorporation","A board resolution authorising the account and naming the signatories","The memorandum and articles of association","A deed of partnership"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A deed of partnership is the governing document of a partnership, not a company. Options A, B and C are standard company account-opening documents, which together show that the company exists, what it may do, and who may operate its account."}},{"itemId":"3a95c5cc-844b-5ed1-b943-8ef4fc2deaad","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"792f06c1-68a3-5dfd-bfae-9d5452a433bb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-075","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Igbobi Old Boys Association wants to open an account. In addition to the rules of the association, which document best shows that the members authorised the account and its signatories?","options":["A certificate of incorporation from the Corporate Affairs Commission","A government gazette establishing the association","A certified extract of the minutes containing the resolution, signed by the Chairman (or President) and Secretary","A grant of probate from the High Court"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"For clubs and societies the bank needs a resolution to open the account, evidenced by an extract of the minutes certified by the Chairman or President and the Secretary, plus the signatories' specimen signatures. Option A is required of companies. Option D is required for executors' accounts, and option B is required for government ministries and agencies."}},{"itemId":"acfb5a28-611e-5495-b207-81da01e63ea2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a911d4b8-4747-57a0-a2b7-5297e18f8673","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-076","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Barrister Oke is named executor in the will of the late Chief Adeyemi and wants to open an account to administer the estate. The key document the bank will require is:","options":["A deed of partnership","A grant of probate","A certificate of incorporation","A board resolution"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Executors prove their authority to deal with a deceased person's estate by a grant of probate (or letters of administration where there is no executor). Option D is required of companies. Option A is for partnerships, and option C shows that a company exists."}},{"itemId":"09561741-a875-5208-b10f-3743f61b176b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"30c47ae9-7cea-5086-9e22-66f546d284f8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-077","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The regulatory process by which a bank verifies the identity, address and beneficial ownership of a customer before and during the relationship is called:","options":["Commission on turnover","Status enquiry","Bank reconciliation","Know Your Customer (KYC)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"KYC requires banks to confirm who a customer is, for example through means of identification, BVN and proof of address, and to keep monitoring the account. Option A is a charge on current account debits. Option C is agreeing account records, and option B is a request for an opinion on someone's creditworthiness."}},{"itemId":"db955357-ac3a-5d4d-a995-340984c6cbca","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"23dbf219-66ea-597d-b933-fe44ba282231","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-078","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under KYC rules, enhanced due diligence is applied to which group of customers?","options":["Every customer who requests a cheque book","All savings account holders below the age of 25","High-risk customers such as politically exposed persons (PEPs) and non-residents","Customers whose accounts have been dormant for six months"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Enhanced due diligence is extra scrutiny for higher-risk customers such as politically exposed persons, non-residents and those with complex ownership structures. Options A, B and D describe ordinary customers who receive standard KYC checks, not the enhanced checks applied for anti-money laundering purposes."}},{"itemId":"ed7d77ca-6e5e-5e41-a0a1-8b6393b54f87","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a8239343-3a0f-5ed1-a078-dde010e3fd3e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-079","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Traditionally, which account requires the prospective customer to provide references from referees before it is opened?","options":["Savings account","Fixed deposit account","Minor's savings account opened by a guardian","Current account"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"References are traditionally taken when opening a current account because the customer will be issued cheques, which can be misused; savings accounts do not need them. Options A and B carry no cheque facility and so do not traditionally require references. Option C is also a savings account and likewise has no cheque book, so references are not traditionally required."}},{"itemId":"2d912a5c-721c-5df4-8e44-e34c5edb7955","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"04beebcf-0943-5078-823c-c9c7a8befec9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-080","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes a current account from a savings account?","options":["Only companies may operate savings accounts","A savings account can be overdrawn but a current account cannot","A current account is operated by cheques and attracts commission on turnover; a savings account uses withdrawal slips and does not attract it","A current account always earns more interest than a savings account"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Current accounts are designed for frequent cheque transactions and carry charges such as commission on turnover, while savings accounts use withdrawal slips or cards and are not charged commission on turnover. Option D is wrong because current accounts earn little or no interest. Option B reverses the overdraft rule, and option A is wrong because savings accounts are mainly for individuals."}},{"itemId":"e344203e-4003-5b62-bb28-bc695594b4f1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5dd6e50b-8b05-5d1b-ba32-76995f209adc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-081","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A foreign-currency account, such as one held in US dollars, used by Nigerian residents to receive foreign remittances and hold foreign exchange, subject to CBN regulations, is a:","options":["Current account","Fixed deposit account","Dormant account","Domiciliary account"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A domiciliary account is held in a foreign currency and used for receiving remittances and trade payments. Option A and option B are naira accounts for transactions and term deposits respectively. Option C refers to any account that has not been operated for a long time, not a currency type."}},{"itemId":"e952eccd-a2fa-5442-b7b2-b3cf9a9b50e3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"713d0453-275b-52ff-b914-f4a555b58b60","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-082","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Her mobile network provider collects a different amount each month from Bisi's account for her post-paid phone bill under her signed authority, while her bank pays a fixed N20,000 monthly to her landlord on her instruction. These are respectively a:","options":["Standing order and a direct debit","Direct debit and a standing order","Direct debit and a cashing credit","Standing order and a bank draft"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A direct debit lets a third party (the originator) collect variable amounts from the account, while a standing order is the customer's instruction to the bank to pay a fixed amount regularly. Option A reverses the two. Options C and D name instruments that do not involve regular payments: a bank draft is a single payment instrument, and a cashing credit lets a customer cash cheques at another branch."}},{"itemId":"68f1d418-8aa4-546d-ae46-ff81087b3271","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"111d9c0a-7212-58fd-8fa8-76365f25ec37","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-083","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank gives a status report on a customer to a supplier who asked about the customer's creditworthiness. Which statement about such banker's opinions is correct?","options":["They make the bank a guarantor of the customer's debts","They must disclose the customer's exact balance and all transactions","They can be given to anyone who asks, without any authority","They are usually given in confidence and without responsibility, but the bank must take care that the opinion is not misleading"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Banker's opinions are brief, confidential assessments given without responsibility, but careless or misleading opinions can still expose the bank to liability. Option B would breach secrecy, since opinions are general, not detailed statements. Option C ignores the need for the customer's implied or express consent, and option A confuses an opinion with a guarantee."}},{"itemId":"6ccdd8e8-4b43-502f-9057-8553f8bbdd19","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e6ab2ea6-ea23-537a-8cae-b364fe6f6fc7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-084","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an agency function performed by a bank for its customer?","options":["Making periodic payments such as insurance premiums","Collection of cheques","Accepting fixed deposits","Buying and selling shares on the customer's behalf"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Accepting deposits is a primary banking function in which the bank borrows as debtor, not something it does as the customer's agent. Options A, B and D are agency services, because the bank acts on the customer's instructions in dealings with third parties."}},{"itemId":"65cecaf9-4980-576a-967e-3e37712bc850","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f159ef7-00e8-52fb-a8b2-53c38a73de61","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-085","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a valid reason for a bank to close a current account that is in credit, after giving reasonable notice?","options":["The customer has asked for a statement of account","The customer has refused to buy the bank's insurance products","The customer habitually draws on uncleared effects","The customer has kept a large credit balance for many years"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Habitually drawing against uncleared cheques is a recognised ground for closing a credit account, along with fraud-linked or negligent operation. Option D is a normal and desirable use of the account. Option A is a right of the customer, and option B is not a lawful ground for closure."}},{"itemId":"505a1404-bb45-537c-9ea9-33c1da5d8500","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df6ffd6c-5ec8-55e1-99e8-85a91a02fefc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BCR-086","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT part of the general procedure for closing a customer's current account?","options":["Determining closing charges and the closing balance","Marking the ledger conspicuously \"CLOSED\"","Issuing the customer a new cheque book","Asking the customer to return unused cheques"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A closing account needs no new cheque book; in fact unused cheques must be withdrawn. Options A, B and D are all steps in closing an account: unused cheques are returned, charges and the final balance are worked out and paid, and the ledger is marked closed and recorded in the register."}},{"itemId":"c2808bcc-966e-5ffb-9bdf-5bd9768e387b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e676b3e8-7692-5a77-b85e-45ea48774783","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"During a sudden wave of withdrawals, a sound Nigerian commercial bank runs short of cash and obtains emergency funds from the Central Bank of Nigeria to meet its depositors' demands. In doing this, the CBN is acting as","options":["manager of the country's external reserves","banker and financial adviser to the government","sole issuer of legal tender currency","lender of last resort to the banks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A central bank is the lender of last resort when it provides emergency funds to banks that cannot raise cash elsewhere, especially during a crisis, so that panic does not spread. Option B refers to the CBN's services to the government, not to banks. Options A and C are genuine central bank functions, but neither describes lending to a bank in distress."}},{"itemId":"53444b24-d9f9-55a1-b9eb-444631ea883a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a11b1c24-cdd2-52bd-b70e-d7a7226e5d65","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the traditional (core) functions of the Central Bank of Nigeria?","options":["Acting as banker and financial adviser to the federal government","Accepting savings deposits from and granting loans to members of the public","Issuing legal tender currency notes and coins in Nigeria","Maintaining external reserves to safeguard the international value of the naira"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A central bank does not deal with individual customers; taking deposits from and lending to the public is the work of deposit money banks. Options A, C and D are all part of the CBN's core mandate, together with promoting monetary stability and a sound financial system."}},{"itemId":"dde8d1a3-7dce-5638-9a75-98f3279e41fc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9dcf493d-6cb1-5d48-a9b2-989b512c0adf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Unlike a commercial bank, an investment bank earns its income mainly from","options":["fees for advisory and underwriting services, commissions on securities trading and profits on its own investments","the issue of currency notes and coins","the interest rate spread between loans and customer deposits","premiums collected from policyholders against stated risks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Investment banks focus on capital market work, so their income comes from fees, commissions and trading profits rather than from deposit-taking and lending. Option C is where most of a commercial bank's income comes from, not an investment bank. Option B belongs to the central bank and option D describes an insurance company."}},{"itemId":"6a04d684-9304-594d-9c81-e6c3c09c70dc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bf688fd3-e0c8-58d1-93fa-88331a5b294e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An investment bank agrees to buy the whole of a company's new issue of shares at an agreed price and then resells the shares to investors, bearing the risk of any unsold shares. This service is called","options":["asset management","debt factoring","underwriting","bill discounting"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Underwriting is the process by which an investment bank assesses and prices a new issue of securities, buys it from the issuer and resells it to investors, helping the company raise capital. Option D is a bank buying a bill before maturity at less than face value, and option B is buying a firm's trade debts. Option A means managing investment portfolios for clients, not taking up a new issue."}},{"itemId":"4dc99855-f8c5-5f53-aa2d-320c91e3a39c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0b86601f-1a98-50dc-8b65-b1ee1e341c29","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A member-owned, not-for-profit financial cooperative in which the members are both the owners and the customers, and which usually offers better savings rates and lower fees than commercial banks, is a","options":["development bank","discount house","credit union","merchant bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A credit union is a financial cooperative owned by its members, who also use its savings and loan services; because it does not aim at profit, it can pay better rates and charge lower fees. Option D is a profit-making bank serving mainly corporate clients, option A is a government-sponsored lender for long-term development projects, and option B deals mainly in short-term money market instruments."}},{"itemId":"15d284fa-ea5f-5a73-808b-b600db377637","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"86669fa7-3247-5dcf-bf67-5a1d4f6aba5d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mama Ngozi, a petty trader in Onitsha with no account in any commercial bank, opens a small savings account and later obtains a N50,000 loan to expand her stall from an institution set up to serve low-income people and small businesses. This institution is most likely a","options":["merchant bank","development bank","investment bank","microfinance bank"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Microfinance banks provide small loans, savings accounts and financial literacy to low-income people and small businesses who lack access to traditional banks, which is exactly Mama Ngozi's case. Option B provides long-term finance for large development projects such as infrastructure and industry. Options A and C serve corporations and big investors, not petty traders."}},{"itemId":"ce3cdfb0-7b00-5368-8f83-e4379c958dd9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dda3bb3e-44e7-516c-bede-525734bba183","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chidi places N2,000,000 with his bank for 12 months at an interest rate agreed at the start, and is told that he will lose part of the interest if he withdraws before the end of the period. Chidi has opened a","options":["fixed (time) deposit account","current (demand) deposit account","overdraft facility","savings deposit account"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A fixed or time deposit is kept for a predetermined period at a fixed rate, earns higher interest than a savings account, and attracts a penalty for early withdrawal. Option B can be withdrawn at any time without notice and earns little or no interest. Option D allows withdrawals with only some restrictions, and option C is a loan facility, not a deposit."}},{"itemId":"8dcb13dd-ac04-5091-874e-49ab532b6636","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d9e77072-4280-5e43-abed-c55f49406abc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bisi's current account has a credit balance of N100,000 and her bank has approved an overdraft limit of N500,000. She withdraws N400,000. On what amount will the bank charge overdraft interest?","options":["N500,000","N400,000","N300,000","N100,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Overdraft interest is charged only on the amount actually overdrawn: N400,000 withdrawn minus her own N100,000 balance leaves N300,000 overdrawn. Option B wrongly charges interest on her own money as well, and option A is the approved limit, not the amount used. Option D is her credit balance, which earns no overdraft charge at all."}},{"itemId":"07ff01e8-95ce-56e8-88e3-5413b7252100","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38f7097b-48f9-5169-8227-a93ddfecfa1e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In one year a Nigerian bank earns 20% interest on loans of N800 million and pays 8% interest on deposits of N1,000 million. What is its net interest income for the year?","options":["N120 million","N96 million","N240 million","N80 million"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Net interest income is interest earned minus interest paid: 20% of N800 million is N160 million, 8% of N1,000 million is N80 million, so the bank keeps N80 million. Option B applies the 12% rate gap to loans only and option A applies it to deposits only, ignoring that the two balances differ. Option C adds the two interest figures instead of subtracting."}},{"itemId":"ccf6835c-8f80-5aee-a0e5-6c6aaf93314f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"941612a1-d363-5bfd-8862-e23fdb3d185f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"After severe floods destroy crops in a farming area, many farmers who borrowed from a rural bank are unable to repay their loans. The risk the bank has suffered is","options":["credit risk","interest rate risk","operational risk","liquidity risk"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Credit risk is the risk that borrowers will default on their loans, and banks reduce it through careful credit assessment, collateral and spreading loans across many sectors. Option D is the risk of being unable to meet short-term obligations such as withdrawals. Option C arises from internal failures such as fraud or system breakdown, and option B comes from changes in interest rates."}},{"itemId":"7fbe4057-83e2-5ac9-bc7e-687e8b4ca213","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7856747d-304d-520c-9f65-3a9ada422f68","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A profitable bank has lent most of its funds on long-term mortgages and, when many depositors come to withdraw on the same day, it does not have enough cash to pay them. This situation illustrates","options":["market risk","liquidity risk","operational risk","credit risk"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Liquidity risk is the risk of being unable to meet short-term obligations because there are not enough liquid assets, which is why banks keep reserves and liquidity buffers. Option D would require the borrowers to default, which has not happened here. Option A concerns losses from changes in market prices, and option C concerns fraud, error or system failure."}},{"itemId":"5ce25ae4-1c7a-5e06-a76f-e8e78ad1b800","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bf077cd8-4d61-5297-9e33-b36d84510cc3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank loses N45 million when a cashier collaborates with fraudsters and its computer system fails to flag the suspicious transfers. This type of risk is","options":["interest rate risk","liquidity risk","operational risk","credit risk"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Operational risk arises from inadequate internal processes, human error, fraud or system failure, and is controlled through strong internal controls and technology. Option D is about borrowers failing to repay, option B is about shortage of cash to meet obligations, and option A is about adverse changes in interest rates; none of these caused this loss."}},{"itemId":"ba8d73c4-3efe-5e96-a607-430b4d2ab51f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3563d498-5939-54ff-a234-358d517d9e41","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking regulation, capital adequacy refers to the requirement that a bank must","options":["maintain a minimum level of capital relative to its risk-weighted assets","insure all its deposits with the deposit insurance corporation","keep a fixed percentage of its deposits with the central bank as cash reserves","lend a minimum share of its loans to priority sectors"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Capital adequacy rules, set out in the Basel Accords, require a bank to hold enough capital in proportion to its risk-weighted assets so that it can absorb losses and protect depositors. Option C describes reserve requirements, which deal with liquidity rather than capital. Option B describes deposit insurance and option D describes sectoral credit allocation."}},{"itemId":"fa3ee5b7-2711-5041-82df-8cdda62d1695","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf9cafc7-83c2-5090-a558-80ce2a828570","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A regulation requiring banks to disclose all their charges clearly to customers and to provide a system for resolving customer complaints is an example of","options":["monetary policy regulation","prudential regulation","capital adequacy regulation","consumer protection regulation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Consumer protection regulation ensures fair treatment of bank customers through transparent fees, truth in lending, disclosure and complaint resolution. Option B focuses on the safety and soundness of each bank through capital, asset quality and liquidity rules, and option C is one part of that prudential framework. Option A concerns reserve ratios and interest rate guidelines set for macroeconomic aims."}},{"itemId":"4fc790b5-8620-5332-85a9-b2b5b0b2cd8c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba59f638-9cf1-52c5-97e4-22a829353a9f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main purpose of a deposit insurance scheme such as the one run by the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) is to","options":["insure banks against losses on all the loans they grant","protect depositors up to a certain limit if a bank fails, so as to prevent bank runs and maintain confidence","guarantee that every bank makes a profit each year","provide long-term loans to the federal government"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Deposit insurance pays depositors up to a stated limit when an insured bank fails; knowing this, depositors are less likely to rush to withdraw, so bank runs are prevented and confidence is kept. Option C is wrong because insurance does not guarantee profits, and option A confuses deposit insurance with protection of the bank's loan book. Option D has nothing to do with deposit insurance."}},{"itemId":"aae34672-8052-5c51-a51f-fa8199140615","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8e64f162-037e-540e-b334-e7f6853a1aa2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial instruments are commonly grouped as equity, debt, derivative and hybrid instruments. Which of the following is a hybrid instrument?","options":["A treasury bill","An ordinary share","A futures contract","A convertible bond"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A hybrid instrument combines features of debt and equity; a convertible bond pays interest like debt but can later be converted into shares. Option B is a pure equity instrument and option A is a short-term debt instrument. Option C is a derivative, whose value depends on an underlying asset."}},{"itemId":"76f9b12c-5493-579e-a75b-c733055b50af","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"467fe460-2767-569d-9302-96bf8affc2b8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Nigerian manufacturing company raises N5 billion by selling its bonds directly to households and firms through the capital market, without borrowing from a bank. This method of moving funds from savers to the borrower is","options":["financial disintermediation","direct finance","indirect finance","credit creation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"In direct finance, borrowers obtain funds straight from lenders in the financial markets by selling them financial instruments such as bonds or shares. Option C would involve an intermediary such as a bank borrowing from savers and then lending to the company. Option A is the mass withdrawal of funds from financial institutions, and option D is the multiple expansion of deposits by banks."}},{"itemId":"6467353a-33db-50f5-b3ac-da8efe78ee8a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90784d62-efe4-5a5f-87da-380303003b98","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which body regulates insurance business in Nigeria?","options":["National Insurance Commission (NAICOM)","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)","National Pension Commission (PenCom)","Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"NAICOM is the financial authority that regulates and supervises insurance companies in Nigeria. Option D regulates the capital market and option C regulates pension schemes. Option B insures bank deposits and complements the CBN in supervising banks; despite its name, it does not regulate insurance companies."}},{"itemId":"edba193e-f698-5254-8881-67754fdcfe5b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"07aab088-04af-5746-89e8-f7f16fc16444","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the stated objectives of the Nigerian financial system?","options":["To cut the economy off completely from international trade","To guarantee a loan to every applicant whether or not the project is viable","To ensure that no viable project is frustrated simply for lack of funds","To replace private savings entirely with government funding"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A key objective of the financial system is to mobilise savings and channel them so that sound, viable projects are not abandoned merely because funds are lacking. Option B goes too far, since only viable projects are meant to be financed. Option A is wrong because the aim is to protect the economy from the ups and downs of the international economy, not to isolate it, and option D contradicts the aim of mobilising savings."}},{"itemId":"a650cf97-53be-5863-b625-82f1f6f41a3f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ef15a44-8cba-5be6-a73c-e56d2a78ab76","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes an IOU from a promissory note?","options":["An IOU must always carry interest, while a promissory note never carries interest","A promissory note is an informal acknowledgement of debt, while an IOU is an order to a bank to pay","An IOU is negotiable and can be traded in the open market, while a promissory note cannot be transferred","A promissory note is a negotiable instrument that can be transferred to another person, while an IOU is an informal acknowledgement of debt that is not negotiable"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The key difference is negotiability: a promissory note can be assigned, transferred or sold, while an IOU simply records that a debt exists and cannot be traded. Option C reverses the two. Option A is wrong because an IOU may be with or without interest, and option B wrongly describes an IOU as an order to a bank, which is closer to a cheque."}},{"itemId":"ba62d0d1-8ecc-593b-ad76-6bb91ad1c09a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58c9dde8-5a0a-537d-acb4-34924c7ed4b3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a non-bank financial intermediary buys long-term primary securities from borrowers and in turn offers savers its own indirect securities with different, often shorter, maturities, it is performing","options":["credit creation through deposit expansion","financial disintermediation","maturity (securities) transformation","an aggregation service"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Securities or maturity transformation means altering the maturities of primary securities by holding them and issuing indirect securities with various maturities that suit savers. Option D is a related but different service, the collecting of many small savings into one pool. Option B is the withdrawal of funds from intermediaries, and option A is associated mainly with banks, whose deposit liabilities can be spent as money."}},{"itemId":"b6e6949b-5f8d-5db5-9e52-897921b5bfa9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"de70912b-9947-5611-a1e9-5633a714b649","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why are the funds of non-life insurance business usually invested in short-term assets such as treasury bills and deposits, while life insurance funds are invested long-term?","options":["Non-life policies are usually for one year, so the liabilities are short, while life policies run for many years, so the liabilities are long","Non-life insurers earn no premium income and must stay fully liquid","Life insurance premiums are paid only once, at the start of the policy","Non-life insurers are forbidden by law to hold any long-term assets"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Insurers match their investments to the length of their liabilities: non-life covers such as motor and fire are usually renewed yearly, while life and endowment policies may run for twenty years or more. Option D overstates the position, since non-life insurers only rarely hold long-term investments. Options B and C are not true descriptions of how premiums are paid or earned."}},{"itemId":"298bbb5b-9a31-5771-95cb-b5efe9e86e5c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1acce601-d7ac-575e-9eff-f716762c6a32","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Bank of Industry (BOI) was formed in 2001 by merging three earlier institutions. Which of the following was one of them?","options":["Nigerian Industrial Development Bank (NIDB)","Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria","Urban Development Bank of Nigeria","People's Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The Bank of Industry was reconstructed from the Nigerian Industrial Development Bank, the Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry and the National Economic Reconstruction Fund (NERFUND). Option D was merged into the agricultural bank that later became the Bank of Agriculture. Option C became the Infrastructure Bank, and option B still exists as the mortgage bank."}},{"itemId":"3cffee62-3b7d-5521-b890-96fad9493427","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"22cd2e89-b6c4-5556-bf3a-2aeecb6225f5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before the shift to market-based monetary management in Nigeria, the CBN relied on direct control instruments. Which of the following is NOT a direct control instrument?","options":["Credit ceilings on bank lending","Open market operations in treasury bills","Administered (fixed) interest rates","Selective credit controls"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Open market operations are an indirect, market-based instrument: the central bank buys or sells securities and lets the market adjust bank reserves and interest rates. Options A, C and D are direct controls, because the authority itself fixes how much banks lend, to whom, and at what rate."}},{"itemId":"a67752e2-ca28-5689-bfab-2785ee18fc5e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d13cc5fd-9709-5e90-9ae3-a10811ff5a01","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BFSW-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Most of a bank's depositors keep their money with it for only a few months, yet the bank is able to grant a manufacturer a five-year loan for new machinery. This shows that deposit money banks","options":["remove the need for savers to be paid any interest","bridge the gap between the short periods for which savers commit funds and the longer periods investors need","lend only the bank's own share capital to investors","create legal tender currency for long-term investors"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Because deposits keep flowing in and out while the total stays fairly stable, a bank can turn many short-term deposits into longer-term loans, bridging differing capital blocking periods. Option D is wrong because only the central bank issues currency. Option A is false, since savers still earn interest, and option C ignores that loans are funded mainly from deposits."}},{"itemId":"1ad6897e-bff9-552b-ab8d-b2bcae389695","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7da56c73-0729-5925-b732-5570c5e82c78","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An unconditional order in writing, addressed by one person to another, signed by the person giving it, requiring the person to whom it is addressed to pay on demand or at a fixed or determinable future time a sum certain in money to or to the order of a specified person or to bearer, is a","options":["bill of exchange","letter of credit","promissory note","treasury bill"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"This is the statutory definition of a bill of exchange: an order from a drawer to a drawee to pay a sum certain to a payee or bearer. Option C is a promise by the maker himself, not an order to someone else. Options B and D are a bank undertaking in trade finance and a government short-term debt security, not orders of this kind."}},{"itemId":"1033150c-6254-5ae9-8863-b70db84cefed","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c95e54cf-dee7-5580-9b06-8725ec53cb84","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, a cheque is best defined as","options":["a written acknowledgement by a bank that a customer owes it money","a bill of exchange drawn on a banker payable on demand","a bill of exchange drawn on any person payable at a fixed future date","an unconditional promise by a banker to pay the bearer on demand"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A cheque is simply a special kind of bill of exchange: the drawee must be a banker and payment must be on demand. Option C describes a time bill, which may be drawn on anyone. Option D is a promise rather than an order, which is the mark of a promissory note or bank note, and A is not a payment instrument at all."}},{"itemId":"7304f4d6-0a6d-5aa1-8229-64541ade8365","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f2e04dbd-880f-5f17-8cd2-49fc6403b1de","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An unconditional promise in writing made by one person to another, signed by the maker, engaging to pay on demand or at a fixed or determinable future time a sum certain in money to a specified person or to bearer, is a","options":["promissory note","cheque","bill of exchange","banker's draft"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A promissory note is a promise by the maker to pay, so it has only two original parties, the maker and the payee. Options C and B are orders addressed to a drawee (a cheque is an order to a banker), not promises. A banker's draft, option D, is an order drawn by a bank on itself or its branch."}},{"itemId":"9d440dff-f395-5f92-bc45-cf57a7c7af62","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"10fe77f7-0542-5029-bc03-68642fe2e193","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The basic difference between a bill of exchange and a promissory note is that","options":["a bill is an order to pay, while a promissory note is a promise to pay","a bill has only two parties, while a promissory note has three parties","a bill need not be in writing, while a promissory note must be in writing","a bill must be payable on demand, while a promissory note is never payable on demand"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A bill of exchange orders a drawee to pay, whereas in a promissory note the maker himself promises to pay. Option D is wrong because bills can be payable at a future time and a promissory note can be payable on demand. Both instruments must be in writing, so C is wrong, and B reverses the parties: a bill has drawer, drawee and payee, a note has maker and payee."}},{"itemId":"90d204fb-bca0-508c-a9ef-bc29bcd44290","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8ae96fd9-d5cf-53fe-9191-306d424de093","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a promissory note, the person who signs the note and undertakes to pay is called the","options":["maker","endorsee","acceptor","drawee"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The maker is the party who makes the promise and signs the note, so he is primarily liable on it. A drawee, option D, and an acceptor, option C, belong to a bill of exchange, where an order is addressed to another person who may then accept it. An endorsee, option B, is someone to whom an instrument has been transferred by endorsement."}},{"itemId":"5fed825c-42f9-5326-b167-0059140cac68","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"94e13397-ee75-516c-83fc-ab138d1be871","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Bello, who keeps a current account with Zenith Bank, writes a cheque for N250,000 in favour of Mrs Adeyemi. In this transaction the drawee is","options":["the Central Bank of Nigeria","Mr Bello","Mrs Adeyemi","Zenith Bank"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The drawee is the bank on which the cheque is drawn, the bank that is ordered to pay, which here is Zenith Bank. Mr Bello, option B, signs the order and so is the drawer, while Mrs Adeyemi, option C, is the payee who will receive the money. The Central Bank, option A, is not a party to the cheque at all."}},{"itemId":"40e53e5d-73bd-580b-8d08-a61b7f7c2129","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7b86e57-ed08-59bd-b23e-8b432392f3c0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The person to whom a cheque is made payable, usually a creditor of the drawer, is the","options":["drawee","payee","drawer","acceptor"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The payee is the person named on the cheque to receive payment, and he presents it for settlement. The drawer, option C, writes and signs the cheque, while the drawee, option A, is the bank ordered to pay. An acceptor, option D, is a drawee of a bill who has signified assent to it, which does not arise with cheques."}},{"itemId":"16af8e7d-6dff-56df-a23c-0599c0fe8e0a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"517ea3b8-a89b-5e1f-810c-cd6784ead909","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On whom may a bill of exchange be drawn, compared with a cheque?","options":["A bill may be drawn only on a banker, while a cheque may be drawn on any person","A bill may be drawn only on a company, while a cheque may be drawn on any individual","Both a bill and a cheque may be drawn only on the Central Bank","A bill may be drawn on any person, including a banker, while a cheque may be drawn only on a banker"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A bill of exchange can be addressed to any person, a trader, a company or a bank, but a cheque by definition must be drawn on a banker. Option A reverses the rule. Options C and B invent restrictions that do not exist in law."}},{"itemId":"e87c4aca-0e76-5617-af83-e21662e489df","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7ae81b1a-bd27-547f-8a69-d438fbf59c23","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a SIMILARITY between a cheque and a bill of exchange?","options":["Both can be drawn on any person","Both are negotiable instruments","Both are entitled to days of grace","Both require formal acceptance by the drawee"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Cheques and bills of exchange are both negotiable instruments: written, signed orders to pay that can be transferred. Option D is wrong because a cheque needs no acceptance. Days of grace in option C apply only to time bills, and option A fails because a cheque can only be drawn on a banker."}},{"itemId":"ab7054a4-effa-5e2c-9e4f-efc154fea0e2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa04f138-ae48-537e-94c0-178688eb1bc3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly compares a cheque with a time (usance) bill of exchange?","options":["A cheque is entitled to days of grace, while a time bill is payable strictly on demand","A cheque is payable only after a fixed period, while a time bill is payable on demand","A cheque must be accepted by the paying bank, while a time bill needs no acceptance","A cheque needs no formal acceptance, while a time bill is presented to the drawee for acceptance"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A cheque is payable on demand and the bank simply pays or refuses, so no formal acceptance is needed, whereas a drawee of a time bill normally signs an acceptance. Options C, A and B all reverse the true positions: days of grace and fixed future payment belong to time bills, not cheques."}},{"itemId":"f31bb921-ca45-5117-aae4-ee7052fa95bd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c81b1f6-35fd-511c-992d-d5cfe9826166","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, where a bill payable at a fixed period after date does not otherwise provide, how many days of grace are added to the time of payment?","options":["Three days","Fourteen days","Seven days","No days at all"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The Act adds three days of grace to the time of payment fixed by a time bill, and the bill is due on the last day of grace. Options C and B are not periods used by the Act. Option D describes a bill payable on demand, which gets no days of grace."}},{"itemId":"97eebf5e-aaaf-591c-990b-1af21c7c194d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"55f2e337-f550-510c-a6d1-038090bfa7a9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bill of exchange dated 1 March is payable 30 days after date. Under the Bills of Exchange Act, three days of grace are added to such a bill. Counting the period by excluding the day of the date and including the day of payment, and assuming none of the relevant days is a weekend or public holiday, the bill falls due on","options":["31 March","4 April","3 April","2 April"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Thirty days after 1 March, excluding 1 March itself, is 31 March; adding the three days of grace gives a due date of 3 April. Option A forgets the days of grace. Option D wrongly counts 1 March as the first day, and option B adds one day too many."}},{"itemId":"9b0a5bbe-a413-526d-bf3c-9a1b81b0a821","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c9cc44a-765a-5e37-9f1c-70a0cf49db3e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Okon holds an accepted bill of exchange for N800,000 which matures in 73 days. Her bank discounts it at 10% per annum on a 365-day year. How much will she receive?","options":["N720,000","N784,000","N816,000","N768,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The discount is N800,000 x 10% x 73/365 = N16,000, so the bank pays her N800,000 - N16,000 = N784,000 now and collects the full face value at maturity. Option A charges a full year's discount, and option C wrongly adds the discount to the face value. Option D deducts the discount twice."}},{"itemId":"2e1e263a-8eae-5c87-bf5d-6fcfb3358391","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e23071cb-c8a2-5612-a3d7-87ee4562b1bc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following instruments can a holder discount with a bank to raise cash before its maturity date?","options":["An accepted usance bill of exchange","A certified cheque","An open cheque","A crossed cheque"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A usance bill is payable at a future date, so a bank can pay the holder now, less a discount, and collect the full amount at maturity. Options C, B and D are all cheques, which are payable on demand and have no future maturity date to discount against."}},{"itemId":"2347e97a-d458-5800-83e4-eb28d89191cb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a38c5593-9cae-5473-8461-525319da24f9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The signification by the drawee of a bill of his assent to the order of the drawer is called","options":["negotiation","acceptance","endorsement","noting"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Acceptance is the drawee's written assent to the drawer's order, usually by signing across the face of the bill, and it makes him the acceptor. Endorsement, option C, is the holder's signature for transfer. Noting, option D, records a dishonour, and negotiation, option A, is the transfer of the bill to a new holder."}},{"itemId":"8688d06d-4488-53eb-867f-eb226dfa188e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4de028cc-0509-5168-9bec-0462bec151ca","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Once the drawee of a bill of exchange has signed his acceptance on it, he becomes known as the","options":["acceptor","holder in due course","drawer","endorser"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"By accepting, the drawee takes on primary liability to pay the bill at maturity and is then called the acceptor. The drawer, option C, is the person who wrote the order. An endorser, option D, signs to transfer the bill, and a holder in due course, option B, is a protected transferee, not the person liable to pay."}},{"itemId":"eb33edcc-171b-56f3-af79-e4e03fd4855a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3736a2a-3544-5373-8a10-16c02838d452","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Alhaji Musa is the drawee of a bill for N500,000. He writes on it \"Accepted for N300,000 only\" and signs. This is","options":["a qualified acceptance","a restrictive endorsement","a noting of the bill","a general acceptance"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"An acceptance that varies the effect of the bill as drawn, here by agreeing to pay only part of the amount, is a qualified (partial) acceptance. A general acceptance, option D, assents to the order without any qualification. Options B and C are not acceptances at all: one limits transfer, the other records dishonour."}},{"itemId":"13d86fba-9947-501e-b98a-f9a0ee500675","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0f2a3465-605a-579f-ba6a-d1cd8108e9a6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A general acceptance of a bill of exchange is one which","options":["agrees to pay only part of the sum ordered","assents without qualification to the order of the drawer","is given by someone other than the drawee","agrees to pay only if a stated condition is fulfilled"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A general acceptance simply agrees to the drawer's order exactly as drawn. Options A and D describe partial and conditional acceptances, which are forms of qualified acceptance. Option C is wrong because acceptance is the act of the drawee."}},{"itemId":"857e5249-c91c-5c1b-864c-1c46de3e81e5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cdbf6cb5-4525-56a5-9fe2-8c5603a5ed10","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, a bill expressed to be payable \"at sight\" or \"on presentation\" is treated as","options":["a usance bill needing acceptance to fix maturity","a bill payable on demand","an invalid bill because no date of payment is stated","a bill payable three days after presentation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The Act treats a bill payable at sight or on presentation as a demand (sight) bill, payable as soon as it is presented. Option D confuses this with days of grace, which attach to time bills. Option A describes a bill payable after sight, and option C is wrong because the payment time is clear."}},{"itemId":"8fed6a9f-a22b-5e22-8f2e-00766f4a5515","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b89d5c9e-4c79-58d6-bb21-cb886f71cad7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bill reads \"Ninety days after sight pay Chika Nwosu or order the sum of N1,200,000.\" This instrument is","options":["a sight (demand) bill","a usance (time) bill","a promissory note","a cheque"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A bill payable a fixed period after sight is a usance or time bill, because payment falls at a determinable future time. A sight bill, option A, is payable immediately on presentation. Option C is a promise by a maker, not an order, and a cheque, option D, must be drawn on a banker and payable on demand."}},{"itemId":"44fa18b2-c4fd-5975-9cae-89db1f4bd0a3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e802c4aa-7f18-57a5-b702-1fad53052a40","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For which of the following bills is presentment for acceptance necessary in order to fix its maturity date?","options":["A bill payable on demand","A bill payable 60 days after sight","A bill payable at sight","A bill payable 60 days after its date"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"When a bill is payable a period after sight, the period can only start running once the drawee has seen and accepted it, so presentment for acceptance fixes the maturity. Options A and C are demand bills paid on presentation. In option D the maturity is already fixed by the date on the bill."}},{"itemId":"6a703eae-03d4-54be-bac6-6383c4503d41","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f26584e-ccb6-5de6-9b29-d00052368611","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following written orders CANNOT be a valid bill of exchange?","options":["\"Pay Kemi Ade N200,000 thirty days after the death of Chief Ade\"","\"Pay Kemi Ade N200,000 thirty days after date\"","\"Pay Kemi Ade N200,000 on the day she gets married\"","\"Pay Kemi Ade N200,000 on demand\""],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A bill must be payable on demand or at a fixed or determinable future time, and marriage may never happen, so an order payable on a contingency is not a bill. Options B and D are a time bill and a demand bill. Option A is valid because death is an event certain to happen, even though its date is unknown."}},{"itemId":"4f0e2b7e-402f-5af0-927c-0178b3b2700b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df19cae4-4cac-549e-a858-9cd6ae302b30","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A customer writes to her bank: \"Pay Ola Stores N90,000 provided the attached receipt is signed by the payee.\" The instruction is addressed to the bank. In law this document is","options":["not a cheque, because it is a conditional order","a promissory note made by the customer","a valid bearer cheque","a valid order cheque"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A cheque must be an unconditional order, and an order addressed to the bank that makes payment depend on a signed receipt is conditional. Options D and C are wrong because the condition stops it being a cheque of any kind. Option B is wrong because the document is an order to the bank, not a promise by the customer to pay."}},{"itemId":"b2509f74-e7be-5561-a90e-78427c395ca8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a6d48242-ea77-56b0-bec8-00d2459d25a2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bill of exchange is drawn in Accra by a Ghanaian exporter and is payable in Lagos. Under the Bills of Exchange Act it is","options":["an inland bill","a promissory note","a foreign bill","an accommodation bill"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"An inland bill is one both drawn and payable within Nigeria; this bill was drawn outside Nigeria, so it is a foreign bill. Option A would need both drawing and payment in Nigeria. Option D refers to a bill signed to lend credit without value, and option B is a promise rather than an order."}},{"itemId":"01d6003c-dbc6-5087-98f3-3f742a4d2579","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6401be14-7c4c-5d1c-a51b-d6876ff13b37","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A foreign bill of exchange, appearing on its face to be such, is dishonoured by non-payment and the holder fails to have it protested. The effect is that","options":["the bill becomes payable to bearer","the drawer and endorsers are discharged","the drawer remains liable but the acceptor is discharged","nothing happens, because protest is always optional"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"For a dishonoured foreign bill, protest is required, and if the bill is not protested the drawer and endorsers are discharged. Option A has nothing to do with dishonour. Option C is wrong because the acceptor's liability is not what protest preserves, and option D confuses foreign bills with inland bills, where noting is optional."}},{"itemId":"889fc5e7-5a1c-59e7-a921-ea546c88f1e2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"87884baf-6ef4-5351-bf21-de217a525ce2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The formal certificate drawn up by a notary public, attesting that a bill of exchange has been dishonoured, is called a","options":["notice of dishonour","qualified acceptance","protest","restrictive endorsement"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A protest is the notary's formal certificate of dishonour, usually built on an earlier noting of the bill. Notice of dishonour, option A, is the message the holder sends to the drawer and endorsers, which need not be made by a notary. Options B and D concern accepting and transferring a bill, not recording its dishonour."}},{"itemId":"b598dde7-8d8a-5e40-9c84-e38bf68ecf10","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9d87d1d9-5c30-5cca-99e1-eaf248a11ef8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, when an inland bill has been dishonoured, noting or protest of the bill is","options":["compulsory, or all prior parties are discharged","optional, at the holder's discretion","forbidden for inland bills","done only by the Central Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The Act says a dishonoured inland bill may be noted or protested if the holder thinks fit, so it is optional. Option A is the rule for foreign bills, not inland ones. Option C is wrong because noting is allowed, and option D is wrong because noting and protest are the work of a notary public, not the Central Bank."}},{"itemId":"3f292c81-4ae4-5901-9833-b5de7decbdf3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae2fc6ea-b992-5d8d-ad28-33cdd15c0cb3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bill of exchange may be dishonoured in which two ways?","options":["By non-acceptance and by non-payment","By crossing and by countermand","By endorsement and by delivery","By noting and by protest"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A bill is dishonoured when the drawee refuses to accept it or, after acceptance or at maturity, refuses to pay it. Option C lists the ways a bill is transferred. Option D lists ways of recording a dishonour, not the dishonour itself, and option B relates to cheques."}},{"itemId":"0d9a61c5-061b-54ec-8a83-8db5dfd3cf58","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"81bb0751-fc9e-5776-8e9e-3c6e91f33db8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a bill of exchange is dishonoured, the main purpose of giving notice of dishonour to the drawer and endorsers is to","options":["transfer ownership of the bill to the drawee","cancel the drawer's liability on the bill","preserve the holder's right of recourse against them","convert the bill into a promissory note"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Notice of dishonour tells prior parties that the bill was not accepted or paid, and it keeps their liability alive so the holder can claim from them. Option A is not what notice does. Option D is impossible, and option B is the opposite: it is failure to give notice that discharges them."}},{"itemId":"cefe75bb-1371-50b3-9951-13b76b208abd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9a1db2fa-6b58-5baa-8569-0ab3cf1cee97","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, the \"holder\" of a bill means","options":["only the drawee who has accepted the bill","the bank on which the bill is drawn","the payee or endorsee who is in possession of it, or the bearer of it","any person who has ever signed the bill"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A holder is the payee or endorsee in possession of the bill, or the bearer of a bearer bill. Option A describes the acceptor. Option D is too wide, since past signers who have parted with the bill are not holders, and option B describes the drawee bank."}},{"itemId":"372b0f7f-3c44-5f5c-9791-89f8cf67009e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"763415f5-6408-5137-b451-e8be87ceca36","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a requirement for a person to be a holder in due course of a bill?","options":["He took the bill in good faith and for value","He took the bill complete and regular on the face of it","He had no notice of any defect in the title of the person who negotiated it","He must be the original payee named on the bill"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A holder in due course is a transferee who took a complete and regular bill before it was overdue, in good faith, for value and without notice of any defect in title, so being the original payee is not one of the conditions. Options B, A and C are genuine requirements set by the Act."}},{"itemId":"829076a0-b32c-57f7-8f59-1cfbeb954546","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"82429335-0ded-5f0a-9a54-eb5d05010b0a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main advantage of being a holder in due course of a negotiable instrument is that he","options":["takes a good title free from defects in the title of prior parties","can never be sued by any later holder","is excused from presenting the instrument for payment","is entitled to three extra days of grace"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The special feature of negotiability is that a holder in due course gets a good title even if an earlier party's title was defective, for example where the instrument had been obtained by fraud. Options B, D and C are not privileges of a holder in due course; he must still present the instrument, and days of grace depend on the type of bill."}},{"itemId":"71cc0874-13df-5a37-8b68-60009667e5a2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"52b230f1-bbc4-58d8-9a65-2b71d135b4b8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A thief steals an uncrossed bearer cheque and passes it to Ada, a trader, in exchange for goods. Ada takes it in good faith, for value, before it is overdue and without any notice of the theft, and it is complete and regular. Ada's position is that she","options":["becomes the drawer of the cheque","has no title, because a thief cannot pass any title","must return the cheque because it was a bearer cheque","is a holder in due course with a good title"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Ada meets every condition for a holder in due course, and such a holder takes free of the defect in the thief's title, so she can enforce the cheque. Option B states the rule for ordinary goods and for instruments crossed \"not negotiable\", not for a negotiable bearer cheque. Option C has no legal basis, and option A is wrong because the drawer is the person who signed the order."}},{"itemId":"0570a414-bd09-527c-a178-a16d96245e41","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa1a84ba-1061-5e6c-9c56-2fa765edda44","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A thief steals a cheque crossed \"not negotiable\" and transfers it to Ada, who takes it in good faith and for value. Compared with an ordinary crossed cheque, the effect of the words \"not negotiable\" is that Ada","options":["gets no better title than the thief had","can cash the cheque over the counter","becomes liable as drawer of the cheque","becomes a holder in due course with a perfect title"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A person who takes a cheque crossed \"not negotiable\" cannot get or give a better title than his transferor had, so Ada takes the thief's defective title and the true owner can recover. Option D is what she would get without those words. Option B is wrong because any crossing stops over-the-counter payment, and option C confuses her with the drawer."}},{"itemId":"03bc71f9-a8b4-5bc0-83e1-21dee5b5eebf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9ee279d6-7c14-51ee-8042-c0d208c35d19","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The signature of the holder on a negotiable instrument, usually on the back, made for the purpose of transferring it, is called","options":["endorsement","crossing","countermand","acceptance"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Endorsement is the holder's signature that, with delivery, transfers title to the instrument. Acceptance, option D, is the drawee's assent to pay a bill. Crossing, option B, is the two parallel lines on a cheque, and countermand, option C, is the drawer's order to stop payment."}},{"itemId":"e5d6e497-15bd-51de-9ca2-ce68e9abcc22","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5f30e23f-10b0-590f-8c8c-f36230137c73","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kola, the payee of an order cheque, simply signs his name on the back without naming anyone. Under the Bills of Exchange Act, this is","options":["a restrictive endorsement, stopping further transfer","a conditional acceptance by the payee","a special endorsement, making the cheque payable only to Kola","a blank endorsement, making the cheque payable to bearer"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"An endorsement that names no endorsee is a blank endorsement, and the instrument then becomes payable to bearer. A special endorsement, option C, names the person to be paid. Option A requires words such as \"pay X only\", and option B is wrong because only a drawee can accept."}},{"itemId":"9db89b3c-16f4-538f-b960-7acd6269ac3c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc62eb80-ed56-5fb8-b67e-191b21ff5b92","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On the back of a cheque payable to her, Bisi writes \"Pay Chinedu Okeke or order\" and signs. This is","options":["a general crossing","a restrictive endorsement","a special endorsement","a blank endorsement"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A special endorsement names the person to whom, or to whose order, the instrument is to be paid, here Chinedu Okeke. A blank endorsement, option D, would be her bare signature. Option B would forbid further transfer, but \"or order\" allows Chinedu to transfer it again, and option A is made with lines on the front, not words on the back."}},{"itemId":"10e4eca1-bbb0-5d61-a2eb-0a1e328ff7fc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6b1dea33-5776-5015-bfde-d6af32b7b997","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following endorsements is a restrictive endorsement?","options":["\"Funke\" written alone on the back","\"Pay Musa Ibrahim only\" - signed Funke","\"Pay bearer\" - signed Funke","\"Pay Musa Ibrahim or order\" - signed Funke"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A restrictive endorsement prohibits further negotiation, and the word \"only\" means Musa can receive payment but cannot transfer the instrument. Option D is a special endorsement that allows further transfer. Option A is a blank endorsement, and option C makes the instrument payable to bearer, which makes transfer easier, not harder."}},{"itemId":"40292cad-75a1-5d85-9742-39881e785a87","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d198efeb-b929-56df-a586-0e0b0fd52d0e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, which of the following is a requirement for a valid endorsement?","options":["It must be witnessed by a notary public","It must be approved by the drawee bank in writing","It must be written on the bill itself and signed by the endorser","It must state the reason for the transfer"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The Act requires an endorsement to be written on the bill itself and signed by the endorser, and his bare signature is enough. Option A confuses endorsement with noting and protest. Options D and B add conditions the Act does not require."}},{"itemId":"bf89c650-aa13-5918-8b70-9fe1b14f42ed","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2491ecfc-21cb-561b-a0cd-f37406b0894d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How does a bearer cheque differ from an order cheque?","options":["A bearer cheque must be paid into an account, while an order cheque can only be cashed over the counter","A bearer cheque is always crossed, while an order cheque is always open","A bearer cheque is payable to whoever presents it without endorsement, while an order cheque is payable to the named payee or as he directs by endorsement","A bearer cheque needs acceptance by the bank, while an order cheque does not"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A bearer cheque can be cashed by anyone holding it and passes by mere delivery, while an order cheque is payable to the named payee or to his order, so it needs his endorsement to be transferred. Option A is about crossing, not bearer or order status. Options D and B are wrong because cheques need no acceptance and either type can be open or crossed."}},{"itemId":"bc4a3115-a47f-5be0-97e6-3993f5bd3155","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bb6b386b-ef15-5d03-ba4b-c05c2e6a2451","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, where the payee named on a bill or cheque is a fictitious or non-existing person, the instrument","options":["is void and cannot be paid at all","may be treated as payable to bearer","becomes payable only to the drawee","must first be protested by a notary public"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The Act provides that a bill whose payee is fictitious or non-existing may be treated as payable to bearer. Option A overstates the effect, since the instrument is not void. Options C and D have no basis in the Act."}},{"itemId":"c95bfd12-fa5b-5f2d-b839-c4cc2cf72893","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"62cd919d-8cdb-5b2b-b78b-761f155e0989","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A cheque that is not crossed and can therefore be presented and cashed over the counter of the bank on which it is drawn is","options":["an open cheque","a post-dated cheque","a stale cheque","a crossed cheque"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"An open cheque carries no crossing, so the paying bank can pay cash over the counter, which also makes it risky if it falls into the wrong hands. A crossed cheque, option D, must be paid through a bank account. Options C and B describe cheques that are too old or dated in the future, not their crossing status."}},{"itemId":"8d767088-978f-5507-ab4e-5e9ebe34e4f3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"407a9bb4-4c21-5527-8e60-e100adbd5552","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Crossing a cheque involves","options":["signing the back of the cheque before handing it to the payee","stamping the cheque \"paid\" after encashment","drawing two parallel transverse lines across the face of the cheque","writing the date on the cheque in red ink"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A crossing is made by drawing two parallel transverse lines across the face of the cheque, with or without words such as \"& Co\" between them. Option A describes an endorsement. Options D and B have nothing to do with crossing; a paid stamp is what the bank applies after payment."}},{"itemId":"109807f1-e138-52c3-bfe6-ceb49491578a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7209c8a0-6a54-5339-89f4-5cab9e97a752","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Eze draws two parallel lines across his cheque and writes \"& Co\" between them. No bank is named. This is","options":["an account payee crossing","a restrictive endorsement","a general crossing","a special crossing"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Two parallel lines, with or without \"& Co\" and with no bank named, form a general crossing, so the cheque can only be paid through a bank account. A special crossing, option D, must name a particular bank between the lines. Option A needs the words \"account payee\", and option B is written on the back."}},{"itemId":"bbbf1254-ee32-5dc6-8772-f184df52803c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"317dafeb-f0ae-5fa9-a376-525c445e5a71","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A cheque has two parallel lines on its face with \"Guaranty Trust Bank Plc\" written between them. This crossing means the cheque","options":["can be paid to any bank presenting it","can be cashed over the counter by the payee","is not negotiable and cannot be transferred","can be paid only to Guaranty Trust Bank"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Writing a bank's name in the crossing makes it a special crossing, so the paying bank may pay only to the bank named. Option A describes a general crossing. Option B is impossible for any crossed cheque, and option C is the effect of the words \"not negotiable\", not of naming a bank."}},{"itemId":"29fee9a3-7a62-5f3c-a805-4e12b962b9f5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b6ed1a96-7bf5-5186-836b-0711c8ad12ec","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A cheque crossed \"Account payee only\" operates mainly as","options":["a direction to the collecting bank to credit the proceeds only to the payee's account","a notice that the drawer has stopped payment","an instruction to the paying bank to pay cash only to the payee","proof that the paying bank has certified the drawer's funds"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"\"Account payee\" tells the collecting bank that only the named payee's account should be credited with the proceeds. Option C is wrong because a crossed cheque is never paid in cash over the counter. Option D describes a certified cheque, and option B describes a countermand."}},{"itemId":"732a8d24-423a-5b32-8acc-76c99355d69c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4622fb8e-ccef-5356-8c68-45bbd1e98ded","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, who may cross an uncrossed cheque?","options":["Only the Central Bank of Nigeria","The drawer, or a holder of the cheque","Only the collecting banker","Only the drawer"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The Act allows the drawer to cross a cheque, and also allows a holder to cross an uncrossed cheque generally or specially; a banker may add a special crossing for collection. Options D and C wrongly limit this right to one person, and option A has no role in crossing."}},{"itemId":"0235581c-dd28-560f-920b-e0389d6fddcc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"51b7cfe0-e0d0-511d-a2cd-84509473da50","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, a crossing on a cheque","options":["has no legal effect and is only a banking custom","becomes void after the cheque is endorsed","may be cancelled by any holder who wants cash","is a material part of the cheque and may not be unlawfully obliterated or altered"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The Act makes an authorised crossing an essential part of the cheque, and it is unlawful to obliterate it or add to or alter it except as the Act allows. Options C and B wrongly suggest it can simply disappear. Option A is wrong because crossings have statutory force."}},{"itemId":"f0b0e8d5-c1f5-579f-9acb-3822aaec4994","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ffcb03e-13b9-5056-8742-f3c4c09807bf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a benefit of crossing a cheque?","options":["It helps to trace the person who received payment if the cheque was stolen","It ensures payment is made through a bank account","It protects the owner against loss by theft","It allows the payee to cash the cheque faster over the counter"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Crossing does the opposite of option D: it prevents payment over the counter, so the money must pass through a bank account. Options C, A and B are genuine benefits, because a thief cannot easily get cash and the collecting bank and account can be traced."}},{"itemId":"db943907-725d-5aab-94eb-fbb82c697da8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4e3c2b39-4013-5734-a612-fcf6c61d073f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an advantage of payment by cheque?","options":["It guarantees that the drawer has enough funds in the account","It economises the use of currency notes and coins","It serves as proof of payment","Payment can easily be stopped to prevent fraud"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"An ordinary cheque carries no guarantee of funds, which is why cheques can bounce; only a certified cheque gives that assurance. Options C, B and D are genuine advantages of paying by cheque."}},{"itemId":"3f8352c4-0518-54dd-88d8-77e94f8da2e6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5ffaef1-afac-5971-894c-df02cee73392","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a proper precaution when drawing a cheque?","options":["The drawer should sign any alteration made on the cheque","The amount should be written in figures only","The payee's name should be left blank for the bank to fill in","The cheque should be folded before it is handed over"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The drawer must sign any alteration so the bank knows it was authorised. Options B, D and C are the reverse of good practice: the amount should be in words and figures, the cheque should not be folded, and the payee's name should be written properly."}},{"itemId":"2b24765f-52a0-5c38-b741-e4b7c143ff07","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5fd98504-38d0-579d-81ed-2969741b34d4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of a cheque?","options":["It is an unconditional order","It must be in writing","The amount must be specified","It must be accepted by the drawee bank before payment"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A cheque is payable on demand and needs no formal acceptance, so option D is not one of its characteristics. Options A, B and C are true: a cheque is an unconditional written order for a specified amount."}},{"itemId":"ede5197e-b9db-5f65-9ad2-0bf2e3d5a8ae","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7e204c4b-e0a1-547b-9f06-d987359b813a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A cheque that has been in circulation for an unreasonably long time, so that in Nigerian banking practice a bank will refuse it once it is more than six months old, is","options":["a post-dated cheque","a stale cheque","a certified cheque","a countermanded cheque"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A stale cheque is one presented too long after its date, and banks in Nigeria treat a cheque more than six months old as expired. A post-dated cheque, option A, carries a future date. A countermanded cheque, option D, has been stopped by the drawer, and option C has been confirmed by the bank as funded."}},{"itemId":"f8e14b58-3d60-5696-84bb-511ab66fd558","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6d3280be-be5c-51b4-b4ba-c1e0df500451","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ngozi receives a cheque dated 2 January but forgets it in her drawer and presents it on 10 August of the same year. Under Nigerian banking practice the paying bank will most likely","options":["refuse payment because the cheque is stale","refuse payment because the cheque is post-dated","pay it, since cheques never expire","pay it only after the drawer accepts it"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"More than six months have passed since 2 January, so the cheque is stale and the bank will return it, leaving Ngozi to ask the drawer for a fresh one. Option C ignores the six-month practice. Option D wrongly brings acceptance into cheques, and option B is wrong because the date is in the past, not the future."}},{"itemId":"b7db50c5-6e16-5a31-9c06-105014f39ffd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93d2a443-d388-5301-992f-a1e68518fc90","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On 15 November, Tunde is handed a cheque dated 30 November of the same year and presents it at the bank the same day. The bank will","options":["pay it at once because the date is irrelevant","pay it only if it is crossed","refuse to pay it until 30 November because it is post-dated","treat it as stale and cancel it"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A post-dated cheque bears a later date than the day it was issued, and it cannot be paid before that date, so the bank will return it if presented early. Option A ignores the date written on it. Option D confuses it with a stale cheque, and option B is wrong because crossing does not change when a cheque may be paid."}},{"itemId":"ef86544d-2221-5735-a749-b4815d180156","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"48dd7266-be16-52d4-9170-bdbcca238c9c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A cheque which the drawee bank has marked to confirm that the drawer has sufficient funds to meet it is","options":["a bearer cheque","a dishonoured cheque","a certified cheque","an open cheque"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A certified cheque has been confirmed by the bank as covered by the drawer's funds, which makes it much more acceptable to the payee. Options A and D describe who may be paid and whether the cheque is crossed, not any bank guarantee. Option B is a cheque the bank has refused to pay."}},{"itemId":"fdfff1d7-8741-5eee-8677-60488a572d9f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c10b8348-96aa-5876-9cf8-84fb5e78387b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under Nigerian banking practice, a customer who wishes to stop payment (countermand) of a cheque should give the bank an instruction that is","options":["endorsed on the back of the cheque by the payee","verbal, given to any staff member at any branch","in writing, clear and signed in line with his mandate, before the cheque is paid","by telephone, after the cheque has been paid"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A countermand must reach the bank before payment and should be written, unambiguous and signed as the account mandate requires, so the bank can act on it safely. Option D is too late, since a cheque already paid cannot be stopped. Option B lacks the written, signed form, and option A is wrong because the payee cannot stop the drawer's cheque."}},{"itemId":"204a3de7-12f9-5d19-a97b-51630dc558f2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"35679ee5-79c8-576f-8454-96c5774d2c01","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On Monday, Union Bank receives a death certificate showing that its customer, Mr Okafor, died on Saturday. On Tuesday, a cheque he signed last week is presented for payment. The bank should","options":["pay the cheque, because it was signed before he died","pay half the amount and keep the rest for the estate","pay the cheque if the payee endorses it","not pay the cheque, because notice of death ends its authority to pay"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Once a bank has notice of its customer's death, its authority to pay his cheques ends, even for cheques he signed while alive. Option A ignores this rule, and option C wrongly suggests endorsement overcomes it. Option B has no basis; the funds belong to the estate and are dealt with by the personal representatives."}},{"itemId":"2ced5365-14a4-5c8d-abf4-a3c371393ea0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b573ab76-d5ba-55f1-9bf4-8bc8ba236911","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A court order served on a bank attaching the credit balance in a customer's account to satisfy a judgment debt, so that the bank may not pay the customer's cheques out of that balance, is","options":["a notice of dishonour","a garnishee order","a countermand","a power of attorney"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A garnishee order attaches the money a bank owes its customer so that it goes to the judgment creditor, and the bank must not pay cheques against it. Option D gives someone authority to act for the customer. A countermand, option C, comes from the customer himself, and option A is notice that an instrument was not paid."}},{"itemId":"0bc658d1-6f00-53e5-a884-0633f6d30267","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a960bcde-b59c-51f9-8c0e-eed27dc9af39","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A cheque is paid by a bank, but it later turns out that the drawer's signature was forged by a dismissed employee. The general legal position is that","options":["the bank cannot debit the customer's account, because a forged signature gives it no mandate","the customer must bear the loss, because the cheque book was his","the bank may debit the customer, because the cheque looked regular","the payee must refund the bank before the customer can complain"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A forged drawer's signature is wholly inoperative, so the customer never gave the bank an order to pay and the bank cannot charge his account. Option C confuses this with the protection given for forged endorsements in some cases, not forged drawer's signatures. Options B and D shift the loss without any legal basis, unless the customer's own conduct caused it."}},{"itemId":"9d31d42e-37e1-5ec5-b59e-87c4481ea4b8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd50fafe-1f1c-55ff-9b65-9b728d87d132","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following does NOT end a bank's authority to pay its customer's cheques?","options":["A valid written countermand by the customer","Notice of a bankruptcy petition against the customer","Notice of the customer's death","The customer's issue of a new cheque book"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Issuing a new cheque book is routine and has no effect on the bank's authority to pay. Options C, B and A are recognised grounds on which the bank's authority to pay ends or the bank must not pay."}},{"itemId":"b194fb06-6fe9-5921-b804-2e73b411d13e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45cf9808-264e-5e07-9c93-ad781afa4426","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a proper reason for a bank to dishonour a customer's cheque?","options":["The payee is a customer of another bank","The drawer's account does not have enough funds to cover it","The cheque is presented by the payee in person","The cheque is drawn for a large amount"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A bank need not pay a cheque that is not covered by the balance or an agreed overdraft. Option A is normal, since clearing exists for cheques payable to customers of other banks. Options D and C are not grounds for refusing payment on their own."}},{"itemId":"467a942a-81cb-52af-865f-98579630e7ef","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"35758eeb-821f-5562-bbeb-80a0da382cf0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, where a bill or cheque is materially altered without the assent of all parties liable on it, the instrument is","options":["valid only if the drawee bank initials the change","automatically converted into a promissory note","still fully valid against every party","avoided, except against a party who made, authorised or assented to the alteration"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"An alteration of an essential term, such as the amount, date or payee, made without everyone's assent avoids the instrument, except against the person who made or agreed to the change; this is why banks return cheques with alterations not signed by the drawer. Option C ignores the alteration rule. Options B and A have no basis in the Act, because it is the drawer, not the bank, who must confirm changes."}},{"itemId":"0b87a24f-8279-5c12-9433-0a6f9be0b956","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e9217430-0f7c-5232-a699-3d1bb841d3be","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank wrongfully dishonours a customer's cheque even though his account was in credit and nothing barred payment. The bank is","options":["entitled to charge the customer a returned-cheque fee","liable only to the Central Bank of Nigeria","not liable, since a bank may refuse any cheque","liable to the customer in damages for breach of contract"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A bank's contract with its customer requires it to pay properly drawn cheques when funds are available, so dishonouring one without justification is a breach of contract for which damages can be claimed. Option C wrongly gives the bank a free choice. Options B and A ignore that the injured party is the customer."}},{"itemId":"13fc165d-acfb-5ebc-b693-689ab1b63168","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"97bdcb5e-8255-5857-8279-e170294a24ac","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-065","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Bills of Exchange Act, an undated cheque is","options":["void and of no effect from the start","treated as a promissory note","not invalid in law, though bankers by custom usually refuse to pay it until it is dated","payable only after six months"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The date is not an essential part of a bill, so an undated instrument is not invalid, but it is a general custom of bankers to refuse undated cheques. Option A overstates the law. Options D and B have no basis."}},{"itemId":"0f22fc7c-4f12-5c14-bd01-02e48eeb76ec","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8f241c63-8a50-5f7d-9049-d9738f09fec8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-066","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Until a cheque has been endorsed, the only party who is liable on it is the","options":["drawee bank","drawer","collecting bank","payee"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"No one is liable on a cheque until he signs it, and before any endorsement the only signature is the drawer's. The payee, option D, becomes liable only after he endorses it. The banks in options A and C do not sign the cheque and so are not liable on it as parties."}},{"itemId":"be4ed7a6-6f3d-564a-a7ac-153d4b704203","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"82d16726-4c1a-5906-bd31-48a0ea5f53a0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-067","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Bills of Exchange Act protects a paying banker who pays a crossed cheque in accordance with the crossing when he acts","options":["only when the drawer is present in the banking hall","only after obtaining a court order","only if the cheque is less than one month old","in good faith and without negligence"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Where a banker on whom a crossed cheque is drawn pays it in good faith, without negligence and in line with the crossing, the Act protects him. Options B, A and C are not conditions of that protection."}},{"itemId":"e1c23d50-160d-571e-81f7-c4497a26b816","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dafefa4f-fcef-58e6-afc4-829e7d1badfb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-068","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A collecting bank collects a stolen crossed cheque, but it does so for a person who is not its customer and without checking his identity. The bank is most likely to","options":["be fully protected because it acted only as an agent","be protected because the cheque was crossed","lose its statutory protection and be liable to the true owner","pass all liability to the paying bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The collecting banker's protection covers collection for a customer in good faith and without negligence, so collecting for a non-customer without proper checks is negligent and exposes the bank to the true owner. Options A and B forget that the protection depends on care, not just on agency or crossing. Option D has no basis."}},{"itemId":"2bfb630e-6937-54b1-b07d-d24b8b0b0326","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8851459e-b70d-5915-bcb5-a569a798660e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-069","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The procedure through which a bank collects the proceeds of a cheque drawn on another bank in favour of its own customer is called","options":["countermanding","discounting","certification","clearing"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Clearing is the exchange and settlement of cheques between banks so that a bank can obtain value for cheques drawn on other banks. Discounting, option B, is buying a bill before maturity. Countermanding, option A, is stopping payment, and certification, option C, is confirming the drawer's funds."}},{"itemId":"b32eb686-12e4-5c54-bf6b-f5e3d6c85ef4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27a6ddfc-8c39-50cd-8bfd-bf1d89ba4c5e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-070","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Nigerian cheque clearing system, MICR stands for","options":["Multiple Interbank Credit Reconciliation","Manual Interbank Cheque Register","Magnetic Ink Character Recognition","Monetary Instrument Clearing Regulation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"MICR is Magnetic Ink Character Recognition, a technology in which codes printed in magnetic ink on a cheque are read by machines to speed up sorting and processing. Options B, D and A are made-up names that sound like banking terms but are not what MICR means."}},{"itemId":"4a8be26e-8230-526e-b173-5f952c1618fc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"290b271e-34ed-516a-a82e-53b77ffc5ec9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-071","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Nigerian automated cheque clearing system, an indirect participating bank is one that","options":["exchanges cheques directly at the clearing house with its own equipment","does not accept cheques from its customers at all","manages the zonal clearing house for all banks","takes part in clearing through a direct participating bank"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"An indirect participant does not clear on its own account but clears its cheques through a direct participating bank. Option A describes a direct participant. Option C is the role of the clearing house itself, and option B is not a category of clearing participant."}},{"itemId":"15014f77-558a-5d08-be01-8af4fad70eaa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"05a273af-3613-5577-889c-64c64ba71015","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-072","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following cheques would NOT need to pass through the interbank clearing system?","options":["A cheque drawn on Bank C and paid into an account at Bank D","A cheque drawn on Bank A and paid into another account at Bank A","A cheque drawn on Bank B and paid into an account at Bank A","A cheque drawn on Bank A and paid into an account at Bank B"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Clearing is needed to obtain value for non-in-house cheques, those drawn on a different bank from the one collecting them. In option B the same bank is both paying and collecting, so it settles internally. Options D, C and A all involve two different banks and must go through clearing."}},{"itemId":"01f66f3f-3ada-5958-84e3-333ccda0c7bb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cfe5a8f4-fee2-5c00-9747-53b9bbb62175","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-073","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A person who signs a bill of exchange as drawer, acceptor or endorser without receiving value for it, in order to lend his name to another person, is","options":["a payee","a drawee in case of need","a holder in due course","an accommodation party"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"An accommodation party signs a bill without receiving value, purely to lend his name and credit to someone else, and he is liable to a holder for value. Option C is a transferee who took the bill for value. Option B is a person named to be approached if the drawee dishonours the bill, and option A is the person to whom the bill is payable."}},{"itemId":"89aa4ed9-f838-55be-97cd-6fa0e440f3be","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f8364346-4253-55e3-aa3e-eb12f2924daf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-074","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bill of exchange contains words prohibiting its transfer, such as \"Pay Ade only\". Under the Bills of Exchange Act, the bill is","options":["negotiable by delivery alone","payable to bearer","valid between the parties but not negotiable","void and unenforceable by anyone"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"Words prohibiting transfer do not destroy the bill; it remains valid between the original parties but cannot be negotiated to anyone else. Option D goes too far. Options A and B describe the opposite of what the words do."}},{"itemId":"0615056d-5dd0-5180-868a-734e1a49ee8a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fd779c2-e023-5046-bc2e-f6ed95b8e2dd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:BOE-075","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the features normally seen on the face of a completed cheque?","options":["The date","The amount in words and in figures","The drawee's acceptance signature","The drawer's signature"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"A completed cheque shows the payee, the drawee bank, the amount in words and figures, the date, the account number and the drawer's signature. Option C does not appear because a cheque is not accepted by the drawee. Options B, A and D are standard features of a cheque."}},{"itemId":"f49a9ca1-6493-56e2-a90b-0315308f2386","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5a70800b-81a9-5de0-959b-ebf17f47fe2a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the generally accepted principles (canons) of central banking?","options":["The central bank should grant loans directly to individuals who cannot borrow from commercial banks","The central bank should keep part of its assets in liquid form so that it can discount short-term commercial and government bills","The central bank should open retail branches to attract savings deposits from households","The central bank should aim to earn the highest possible profit on its lending"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A central bank must stay liquid so that it can discount short-term bills and support the banking system when needed, which is one of its recognised canons. Options C and A describe ordinary commercial banking with the public, which a central bank should avoid so that it does not compete with the banks it supervises. Option D is wrong because a central bank serves the economy, not profit maximisation."}},{"itemId":"d787efc0-fb8f-5f86-8e8f-b1e1f641767f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"47c34916-4a17-5438-b960-9f918aa0ca2b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was an ADVANTAGE of the West African Currency Board (WACB) system?","options":["It allowed Nigeria to run an independent monetary policy suited to its own needs","It established a unified currency standard that was widely accepted by the people","It let the colonies invest their currency backing in earning assets at home","It trained Africans in the art of monetary management"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The WACB gave the British West African colonies one common, generally accepted currency and relieved colonial governments of currency management. Option A is wrong because the WACB gave Nigeria no independent monetary policy. Options D and C are actually its known weaknesses: it failed to train Africans in monetary management, and its sterling backing deprived the colonies of earning assets."}},{"itemId":"3fa3b89f-e477-5aed-b2bb-3bf6b30f5656","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"31596faa-751e-5bd1-933f-cc0a129fe71a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a DISADVANTAGE of the West African Currency Board (WACB)?","options":["Using one currency for four colonies caused problems because the colonies became independent at different dates","It relieved the colonial governments of the duty of managing currency","It paved the way for easy acceptance of new currencies at independence","It established a single currency standard that people accepted"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Because Nigeria, Ghana, Sierra Leone and The Gambia shared one WACB currency but gained independence at different times, the common currency became hard to manage, which was a real weakness of the system. Options B, C and D are all advantages of the WACB, not disadvantages."}},{"itemId":"531b62f0-146e-5ffe-b18e-bf9de5cabeeb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d7573138-b012-5461-8eeb-862ffd33ba4c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN Act 2007, which of the following is the FIRST of the principal objects of the Central Bank of Nigeria, the one monetary policy is mainly built around?","options":["Achieving full employment of labour","Ensuring monetary and price stability","Maximising the rate of economic growth","Fixing the exchange rate of the naira permanently"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Section 2 of the CBN Act 2007 lists 'ensure monetary and price stability' first among the Bank's principal objects, and the Monetary Policy Committee exists to help achieve price stability. Options A and C are broad aims of economic policy, but they are not stated as the CBN's first object. Option D is not a CBN object at all; the Act speaks of safeguarding the international value of the currency, not fixing it permanently."}},{"itemId":"ba5e6bca-18d5-58dc-975b-dd3e7e538df7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8e808ec7-2ae4-5574-9841-7acb805253f0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN Act 2007, which of the following officials is a member of the Board of Directors of the Central Bank of Nigeria?","options":["The Managing Director of the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","The President of the Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria","The Permanent Secretary of the Federal Ministry of Finance","The Director-General of the Securities and Exchange Commission"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Section 6 of the CBN Act 2007 makes the Board consist of the Governor (chairman), the four Deputy Governors, the Permanent Secretary of the Federal Ministry of Finance, five Directors and the Accountant-General of the Federation. The heads of the NDIC (A) and SEC (D) run separate regulatory bodies and do not sit on the CBN Board, and the CIBN (B) is a professional body."}},{"itemId":"9ecb5e8b-8093-5669-977a-30ae3ca267cc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"83156baa-0c8f-5461-96bb-9af88b2d52cd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN Act 2007, the Governor of the Central Bank of Nigeria is appointed for","options":["a term of five years in the first instance, and may be reappointed for one more term of not more than five years","a term of four years, renewable as many times as the President wishes","life, until the Governor reaches the retirement age of the civil service","a single term of seven years that cannot be renewed"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Act appoints the Governor (and each Deputy Governor) for five years in the first instance, with eligibility for one further term of up to five years, so the longest normal tenure is ten years. Options D, B and C describe tenures the Act does not provide; a fixed but renewable term protects the Governor's independence while still allowing a change of leadership."}},{"itemId":"bda86a79-04ae-51ac-b845-57f83b77619d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"871c3d06-4630-59a5-9abe-f25c4928dbea","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN Act 2007, the Monetary Policy Committee consists of the Governor (as Chairman), the four Deputy Governors, two members of the Board of Directors, three members appointed by the President and two members appointed by the Governor. How many members does the MPC have in total?","options":["14","11","13","12"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Adding the groups gives 1 Governor + 4 Deputy Governors + 2 Board members + 3 presidential appointees + 2 Governor's appointees = 12 members. Option B forgets the Governor, who is himself a member and chairs the Committee, while C and A add members who are not in the Act."}},{"itemId":"1396a22f-b08e-587b-a122-0d7e4e1a443a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"535b720f-29d3-5446-b4c3-a1d828e490f3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN Act 2007, the Monetary Policy Committee of the Central Bank of Nigeria is required to meet","options":["whenever the Minister of Finance summons it","at least four times in a year","only once a year, at the end of the financial year","at least twice a year"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Act's schedule on the MPC says it 'shall meet at least four times in a year'; in practice it has often met more frequently, but four meetings is the legal minimum. Options C and D set lower minimums than the law requires, and A is wrong because the MPC is a committee of the Bank, not one called by the Minister."}},{"itemId":"c7b2cb74-a597-5f36-91ba-0f04f41c9e45","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93264102-554a-5be7-9119-9def4ecfdd5f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the organisational structure of the Central Bank of Nigeria, with four Deputy Governors each overseeing a group of departments, the Banking Supervision Department falls under the Deputy Governor responsible for","options":["Operations","Economic Policy","Corporate Services","Financial System Stability"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Supervising banks is about keeping the financial system safe and sound, so Banking Supervision sits with the Deputy Governor for Financial System Stability, along with departments such as Other Financial Institutions Supervision and Consumer Protection. The Economic Policy directorate (B) handles monetary policy, research and statistics; Operations (A) handles currency, banking services and reserves; and Corporate Services (C) handles staffing, finance and legal matters."}},{"itemId":"ece4eec9-ce7f-52a0-8a26-86472a745a98","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93d37a4c-0cf2-54ad-8ef5-20f370b506ed","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nigeria decimalised its currency on 1 January 1973, when the CBN introduced the naira and kobo. The naira replaced the Nigerian pound at the rate of","options":["N2 to one pound","N20 to one pound","N1 to one pound","N10 to one pound"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"When the naira was introduced on 1 January 1973, each Nigerian pound was exchanged for two naira, and the naira was divided into 100 kobo. Option C ignores the conversion, while D and B confuse the conversion rate with the denominations of the new currency, which went up to N10 at the time."}},{"itemId":"ab4a83c4-d2d4-5be8-9bd5-7c277faa2b48","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"07139deb-eb2a-55fc-85dc-c82d3fb61a24","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The N20 note issued by the CBN was introduced in 1977 to honour a late Head of State whose portrait it still carries. Who is he?","options":["Sir Ahmadu Bello","General Murtala Mohammed","Chief Obafemi Awolowo","Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The N20 note honours General Murtala Mohammed, the Head of State assassinated in 1976, and his portrait remains on the green N20 note. Chief Obafemi Awolowo (C) appears on the N100 note, Sir Ahmadu Bello (A) on the N200 note and Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe (D) on the N500 note."}},{"itemId":"2c9ca000-42a2-5534-b250-506627d05728","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5c4e704c-2664-58b5-8b49-eb1469e5919b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"From 1973 the CBN began to hold Nigeria's external reserves in as many traded currencies as possible, instead of only US dollars and British pounds. The main aim of this change was to","options":["make the naira fully convertible into gold","earn new income and reduce possible losses from a fall in the value of any single reserve currency","stop exporters from surrendering their foreign exchange earnings to the CBN","allow commercial banks to keep the country's reserves on behalf of the CBN"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Spreading reserves across several currencies is diversification: it opens up new earning opportunities and limits the loss if one reserve currency is devalued or depreciates. Option A has nothing to do with currency diversification, D is wrong because reserve holdings were centralised in the CBN, and C reverses the policy, which required exporters to surrender their proceeds to the Bank."}},{"itemId":"06daf101-0349-5019-b5fc-731c186349a1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7569dced-76e6-5718-828d-75c85fc4c71b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Federal Government directs all its ministries, departments and agencies to close their separate revenue accounts in commercial banks and pay all government receipts into one consolidated account kept at the CBN. This arrangement is known as the","options":["Treasury Single Account (TSA)","Ways and Means Advance","Federation Account Allocation Committee (FAAC)","Excess Crude Account"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Treasury Single Account pools government revenues into a single account at the CBN, improving transparency and cash management, and the CBN runs it as banker to the government. A Ways and Means Advance (B) is a temporary CBN loan to government, FAAC (C) is the committee that shares federally collected revenue among the tiers of government, and the Excess Crude Account (D) holds oil earnings above the budget benchmark."}},{"itemId":"81a6d61f-a079-5887-97b2-09554662be84","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7e948ff-2b8d-58e2-9f77-765d2c2a0520","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When large government deficits financed by CBN lending, such as Ways and Means advances, pump money into the economy and weaken the CBN's ability to keep prices stable, the problem is described as","options":["fiscal dominance","financial inclusion","regulatory forbearance","moral suasion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Fiscal dominance happens when the government's borrowing needs push the central bank into financing deficits, so monetary policy ends up serving fiscal needs instead of price stability. Moral suasion (D) is informal persuasion of banks, financial inclusion (B) is extending financial services to the unbanked, and regulatory forbearance (C) is a supervisor's decision to delay enforcing rules on a weak bank."}},{"itemId":"382fa19c-2415-5b2a-9ff9-5aeeb4aeb91a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90bb4545-1a36-5285-a1b3-2b4042e86eee","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"At the close of business, Bank A finds it is short of funds to settle its obligations and cannot raise money in the interbank market. It borrows overnight from the CBN against eligible collateral, at a rate set above the Monetary Policy Rate. Bank A has used the","options":["Treasury Single Account","Standing Deposit Facility (SDF)","Standing Lending Facility (SLF)","Cash Reserve Requirement"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Standing Lending Facility lets a bank borrow overnight from the CBN, and its rate is set above the MPR so that banks try the interbank market first. The SDF (B) works the other way round, letting banks with surplus funds place them with the CBN at a rate below the MPR. The CRR (D) is a reserve requirement, not a borrowing window, and the TSA (A) is the government's account."}},{"itemId":"1a563c75-e994-50b7-b8c3-42ae776638cd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5d600e5a-9dfc-5cf7-b6c9-d785903af51c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Suppose the CBN sets the Monetary Policy Rate at 20% with an asymmetric corridor of +500 basis points and -100 basis points around it. What are the Standing Lending Facility (SLF) and Standing Deposit Facility (SDF) rates?","options":["SLF 25%; SDF 15%","SLF 21%; SDF 15%","SLF 19%; SDF 25%","SLF 25%; SDF 19%"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"One hundred basis points equal one percentage point, so the SLF is 20% + 5% = 25% and the SDF is 20% - 1% = 19%. Option B applies the two margins to the wrong facilities, A uses 5 points on both sides as if the corridor were symmetric, and C wrongly puts the lending rate below the deposit rate."}},{"itemId":"4b7b8510-b2d2-56b9-b955-09899934d204","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5e6bc69e-e67b-5b9c-b74e-e3c9499aa272","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In an open market operation, the CBN sells government securities to banks under an agreement to buy them back at a stated price on a future date. What is the effect of this transaction on the banking system?","options":["It has no effect on bank reserves, because the securities will be bought back","It temporarily withdraws liquidity from the banking system","It temporarily injects liquidity into the banking system","It permanently injects liquidity into the banking system"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"When banks buy the securities they pay from their reserve accounts, so liquidity leaves the system, but only until the CBN buys the securities back and the money returns. Option D describes an outright purchase, and C describes the opposite deal in which the CBN buys securities and agrees to sell them back later. Option A is wrong because reserves really are reduced for the life of the agreement."}},{"itemId":"7a9fa492-6b40-5c0c-9da8-608a50d0813f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99fee798-bee5-58c7-a562-fd563be75c46","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Short-term securities issued by the Central Bank of Nigeria itself, rather than by the Federal Government, specifically to mop up or manage liquidity as part of open market operations, are known as","options":["Nigerian Treasury Bills","Federal Government Development Stocks","Bankers' acceptances","CBN Bills"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"CBN Bills are issued by the CBN for monetary policy purposes, so they are a liquidity-management tool, not a way of funding the government. Nigerian Treasury Bills (A) and development stocks (B) are Federal Government debt, even though the CBN manages their issue, and bankers' acceptances (C) are bills accepted by banks to finance trade."}},{"itemId":"b54c72b3-30e1-5947-87d3-98bf1c3bc9ba","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dab252db-cc9b-578d-9cf0-0bfde2480b59","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Suppose the CBN requires deposit money banks to keep a liquidity ratio of 30%. Ekiti Trust Bank has total deposit liabilities of N600 billion and liquid assets (cash, balances with the CBN and Treasury Bills) of N150 billion. By how much is the bank short of the required liquid assets?","options":["N45 billion","N150 billion","N30 billion","N180 billion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Required liquid assets are 30% of N600 billion = N180 billion, and the bank holds N150 billion, so the shortfall is N180 billion - N150 billion = N30 billion. Option D is the full requirement rather than the shortfall, A wrongly takes 30% of the liquid assets, and B is simply what the bank already holds."}},{"itemId":"bba1e35b-b522-5d20-886f-d3965f01f22e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fddd5258-f8ce-56ec-a714-c520e4b81e1d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Suppose the CBN sets the cash reserve ratio at 40%. A bank receives new deposits of N250 billion. Ignoring all other requirements, what is the most the bank can use for lending out of these deposits?","options":["N250 billion","N210 billion","N150 billion","N100 billion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The bank must keep 40% of N250 billion = N100 billion as reserves with the CBN, leaving N250 billion - N100 billion = N150 billion that it can lend. Option D is the reserve that must be kept, not the amount available, B wrongly subtracts 40 naira-billions instead of 40 percent, and A ignores the reserve requirement altogether."}},{"itemId":"abaefc54-b2aa-509d-9025-673aba685e32","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d29716a7-734e-5b9c-a041-f4e2f191c58e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the cash reserve ratio (CRR) from the liquidity ratio in Nigerian banking?","options":["The CRR is kept as balances with the CBN, while the liquidity ratio can also be met with liquid assets such as Treasury Bills and cash in the bank's vaults","The CRR is fixed by the Bankers' Committee, while the liquidity ratio is fixed by the NDIC","The CRR applies only to merchant banks, while the liquidity ratio applies only to microfinance banks","The CRR can be met by holding Treasury Bills, while the liquidity ratio must be met only with deposits at the CBN"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CRR is a percentage of deposits that must sit as reserves with the CBN, while the liquidity ratio is a wider requirement that a bank can meet with several liquid assets, including cash, CBN balances and Treasury Bills. Option D reverses the two, and C and B are wrong because both ratios are set by the CBN and apply to deposit-taking banks generally."}},{"itemId":"6fb646f3-d793-5cea-9c39-e8d231a0e09f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"384a5f51-dfe9-5be3-a97a-b16b060915f9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the CBN moved from direct monetary controls to a market-based approach in the 1990s, which of the following became the main indirect, market-based instrument of monetary management?","options":["Administratively fixed interest rates","Credit ceilings on bank lending","Sectoral credit allocation to priority sectors","Open market operations"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Open market operations work through the market: the CBN buys or sells securities, banks' reserves change, and banks remain free to make their own lending decisions. Options B, C and A are direct controls used mainly before the Structural Adjustment Programme, where the CBN dictated how much banks could lend, to whom, and at what rate."}},{"itemId":"44bd6a54-9e8c-5bbc-ba6a-8bcac804eb04","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e29d0d56-67e6-5609-ad12-2f0907b6564d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A selective (qualitative) control under which the central bank fixes how much security a borrower must provide for bank advances, so that banks do not lend too much against commodities likely to be hoarded for speculation, is called","options":["margin requirement","open market operation","loan ceiling","variable cash ratio"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A margin requirement controls the value of security a borrower must pledge relative to the loan, so raising the margin limits speculative borrowing against particular goods. A loan ceiling (C) caps the total level of advances, a variable cash ratio (D) changes the cash banks keep with the central bank, and an open market operation (B) is the buying and selling of securities."}},{"itemId":"0c63a61d-3dce-5758-8673-083a1e3d7590","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bcc46020-6c44-5272-8e61-b481cddc48be","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The CBN orders commercial banks not to let their total advances rise above the level they had reached on a date the CBN names. This instrument is","options":["supplementary reserves","a margin requirement","a loan ceiling","the bank rate"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A loan ceiling freezes or caps bank advances at the level reached on a date the central bank fixes, so it directly limits credit growth. A margin requirement (B) deals with the security needed for advances, supplementary reserves (A) require extra holdings of government securities, and the bank rate (D) works through the cost of borrowing from the central bank, not a quantity limit."}},{"itemId":"ff4af87b-27f8-5501-9aaa-3782e92af249","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae3db04c-ebd8-5cef-85c0-2be95ef730b6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under a scheme run in phases from the late 1970s, the CBN directed commercial banks to open a set number of branches in rural areas. This is an example of the CBN's","options":["function of issuing legal tender currency","role as lender of last resort to distressed banks","developmental function of spreading banking services and encouraging rural savings","function of managing the country's external reserves"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The rural banking scheme was a developmental step: by making banks open rural branches, the CBN sought to bring banking to unbanked areas and mobilise rural savings. Options D, A and B are traditional central banking functions with no direct link to where commercial banks place their branches."}},{"itemId":"89ad5de1-85f9-5894-8e0d-b2e141b97768","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"75588a7f-5abe-5dac-b5e2-5c9da3099248","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CBNW-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which payment-system reform was launched by the CBN with the Bankers' Committee in February 2014 to give every bank customer a single biometric identity that can be verified across all Nigerian banks?","options":["The Real-Time Gross Settlement (RTGS) system","The Treasury Single Account (TSA)","The cashless policy","The Bank Verification Number (BVN)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The BVN, launched on 14 February 2014, captures each customer's biometrics so that banks can confirm identity, meet know-your-customer rules and reduce fraud. The TSA (B) consolidates government accounts, RTGS (A) settles large interbank payments in real time, and the cashless policy (C), started in 2012, aimed to reduce cash transactions."}},{"itemId":"f9928cf4-5272-5f51-bbec-b7c450d0d13e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8230f5ab-51e8-552a-a6c7-18b25440737a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigeria, the professional body that sets standards of knowledge and skill for persons entering the banking profession, conducts professional banking examinations and promotes ethics among bankers is the","options":["Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria","Central Bank of Nigeria","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","Financial Institutions Training Centre"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The CIBN is the professional body for bankers: it sets entry standards, examines candidates and upholds ethics in the profession. Option B is the regulator of banks and the monetary authority, option C insures deposits, and option D is a training centre, not the body that awards professional banking qualifications."}},{"itemId":"a6243615-f909-5c40-b4ef-c6c8d665e326","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"61307de5-84bc-5ff5-82c0-17c6e155d533","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN) is best described as","options":["a professional body for banking practitioners","a deposit insurance agency","the regulator that licenses banks","a commercial bank owned by bankers"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The CIBN is a professional institute: it qualifies, registers and disciplines bankers as professionals. It does not license banks (option C, a CBN role), insure deposits (option B, an NDIC role) or carry on banking business itself (option D)."}},{"itemId":"c6522702-5b2c-5429-929c-1094395b9d0a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99cda720-209e-5e9c-9323-509b86651e59","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria first obtained chartered status by","options":["the CBN Act of 1958","the Banking Ordinance of 1952","the BOFI Act of 1991","Act (Decree) No. 12 of 1990"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"Chartered status came in 1990 through Act (Decree) No. 12 of 1990, which also gave the Institute its present name. Options A, B and C are banking laws dealing with the central bank and the regulation of banks, not with the professional institute."}},{"itemId":"e730aeba-a084-567e-b4ff-c4aaa0cafbab","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dbc6736a-63f0-5561-b717-5b22d7899df8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The 1990 law that chartered the CIBN has been repealed and replaced. The CIBN is now governed by","options":["the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation Act, 2023","the Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria Act, 2007","the Companies and Allied Matters Act, 2020","the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act, 2020"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The CIBN Act, 2007 repealed the 1990 Act and transferred the Institute's rights and liabilities to the Institute it re-established. Options D and A govern banks and deposit insurance, and option C governs companies generally, so none of them is the Institute's own establishing law."}},{"itemId":"1fa5d8d3-e350-5be2-9a4b-d95c99c2fc39","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"67394fdc-ee47-57db-b990-7a71ff38b004","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The CIBN traces its origin to 1963, when bankers in Lagos set up","options":["a chapter of the American Bankers Association","a branch of the Central Bank of Nigeria","a local centre of the Institute of Bankers, London","the Nigerian Bankers' Clearing House"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"In November 1963 bankers meeting in Surulere, Lagos resolved to form a local centre of the Institute of Bankers, London, and this centre became the nucleus of the CIBN. The CBN (option B) is a regulator, a clearing house (option D) settles cheques, and option A has no part in the Institute's history."}},{"itemId":"65d8ac68-b4eb-58c6-bb9e-7c43abf668ff","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0199e895-7abc-54b3-8e4a-8d6cfe67295a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before it became chartered in 1990, the body now known as the CIBN operated under the name","options":["Nigerian Institute of Bankers","Institute of Banking and Finance of Nigeria","Nigerian Bankers' Association","Nigerian Banking Council"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The body was registered as the Nigerian Institute of Bankers and changed its name to the Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria when it was chartered in 1990. Options C, B and D sound similar but were never the Institute's name."}},{"itemId":"938a61de-7257-5fe4-8f5a-252dd9b698d4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"74c83ca6-b2cf-5022-9308-516f8c11a879","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before its chartering, the Nigerian Institute of Bankers was registered as","options":["a public limited company","a statutory corporation of the CBN","a cooperative society","a company limited by guarantee"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"Professional bodies are commonly formed as companies limited by guarantee because they have members, not shareholders, and do not distribute profit; the Nigerian Institute of Bankers was registered this way. Option A is a profit-making company with share capital, option B would be a government body, and option C is a member-owned business form."}},{"itemId":"de85adf9-ea08-5d3e-8d5c-585fd1157fa3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"783c6eb9-a101-592a-8246-e4e9138b9be5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which sequence correctly traces the development of the CIBN?","options":["Bankers' Committee -> local centre of the Institute of Bankers, London -> CIBN","CIBN -> Nigerian Institute of Bankers -> local centre of the Institute of Bankers, London","Local centre of the Institute of Bankers, London -> Nigerian Institute of Bankers -> CIBN","Nigerian Institute of Bankers -> local centre of the Institute of Bankers, London -> CIBN"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The body began in 1963 as a local centre of the London institute, was then registered locally as the Nigerian Institute of Bankers, and became the CIBN on chartering in 1990. Options D and B reverse the order, and option A wrongly treats the Bankers' Committee, a forum of the CBN and bank chief executives, as the Institute's origin."}},{"itemId":"ffbe5475-4b23-56e4-bba6-b80df394e476","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f7715aa-1a7f-52e6-a213-18ccae6d9277","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CIBN Act, which of the following is NOT a function of the Institute?","options":["Granting licences to banks to accept deposits","Maintaining registers of members and corporate members","Determining the standards of knowledge and skill for persons seeking to become bankers","Conducting professional examinations leading to certificates"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"Licensing banks is a regulatory power of the Central Bank of Nigeria, not of the professional institute. Options C, B and D are all functions given to the CIBN by its Act: setting standards, keeping registers and conducting examinations."}},{"itemId":"1e75b795-f02a-5c1d-8024-3b52cb2b57f1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"97756f0d-a978-59a7-b7e1-b9347fae7e01","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a function of the CIBN under its establishing Act?","options":["Insuring the deposits of bank customers","Issuing the national currency","Ensuring the observance of ethical standards and professionalism among bankers","Fixing the Monetary Policy Rate"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"Section 3 of the CIBN Act charges the Institute to ensure the furtherance, maintenance and observance of ethical standards and professionalism among practitioners of the banking profession. Option A is a role of the NDIC, while options B and D are central bank functions."}},{"itemId":"a74b2310-2d91-5356-9629-18dcb5ec670c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5041557-1e4f-5784-b251-c6a7b3cb8f01","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chiamaka, a graduate trainee in a Lagos bank, wants to sit professional banking examinations that lead to a recognised professional qualification as a banker. The body she should register with is the","options":["Nigerian Exchange Limited","Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria","Securities and Exchange Commission","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"Conducting professional examinations leading to the award of certificates is a statutory function of the CIBN. The NDIC (option D) insures deposits, the SEC (option C) regulates the capital market, and option A runs the stock exchange; none of them examines bankers."}},{"itemId":"a9b9e705-f350-54ba-a1b1-11417198ce59","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"391c4b9a-f712-53b6-aa99-6ef92d643751","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The CIBN was set up to do all of the following EXCEPT","options":["conduct open market operations to control money supply","uphold professional ethics and tradition in the banking industry","promote banking education in Nigeria","liaise with government in promoting and regulating the banking industry"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"Open market operations are a monetary policy tool of the Central Bank of Nigeria. Options C, B and D are all objects of the CIBN: promoting banking education, upholding ethics and tradition, and liaising with government on the development of banking."}},{"itemId":"2fdefebf-4709-53d0-9207-4ca2a917653c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"830fe19c-ed52-5a54-b340-f6a6637c00fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A key benefit of the CIBN's codes of conduct for the banking profession is that they","options":["guarantee that every bank will make a profit","fix the interest rates banks charge customers","replace the supervisory role of the CBN","enforce self-regulation and compliance among members"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"A professional body develops rules and codes of conduct so that members police their own standards, which is self-regulation. Option A is not something any code can guarantee, option C is wrong because the CBN still supervises banks, and option B confuses ethics with pricing."}},{"itemId":"a08d736d-6803-5f4e-aae3-14635b4da765","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e6c620e6-62f8-5604-a2a7-00aea7802e3e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The CIBN conferring honorary awards on persons for distinguished service or outstanding contributions to banking knowledge is an example of its","options":["disciplinary procedure","register of corporate members","examination process","system of recognition for merit"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"Honorary awards for distinguished service are the Institute's way of recognising and rewarding merit in the profession. Option A deals with misconduct, option C tests candidates for qualifications, and option B is a list of member institutions."}},{"itemId":"b4abdc4b-e138-55d6-b841-3c490283a424","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bbbfcb08-6f42-5ae2-80c1-600c752aac27","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CIBN Act, the administration and general management of the Institute's affairs is the responsibility of the","options":["Disciplinary Tribunal","Governing Council","Bankers' Committee","Board of Directors of the CBN"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The CIBN is governed by a Governing Council chaired by the President, which handles its administration and general management. The Bankers' Committee (option C) is a forum of the CBN and bank chief executives, option D governs the central bank, and the Disciplinary Tribunal (option A) only decides misconduct cases."}},{"itemId":"2b827fb1-2052-5303-b41e-b78d528324d8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"46c182ee-0b67-559e-953e-c39cd224677f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The officer of the CIBN who is its Chief Executive, keeps its registers and serves as Secretary to the Governing Council is the","options":["Governor","Registrar","President","Second Vice-President"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Registrar/Chief Executive runs the Institute day to day, keeps the registers and acts as Secretary to the Council. The President (option C) chairs the Council, the Second Vice-President (option D) is one of the Council's elected officers, and a Governor (option A) heads the CBN, not the Institute."}},{"itemId":"deb3a9d3-2e6f-5c72-9ea5-aeb08fe69adb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1bd6870d-3b88-54b3-a777-9059d2459e64","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Within the CIBN's disciplinary system, the body that conducts the preliminary investigation of an alleged breach of the code of conduct and decides whether the case should go further is the","options":["Federal High Court","Body of Past Presidents","Disciplinary Tribunal","Investigating Panel"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Investigating Panel does the first-stage inquiry and decides whether there is a case to refer. The Disciplinary Tribunal (option C) hears cases only after referral, the Federal High Court (option A) hears appeals, and option B is an advisory body of former presidents."}},{"itemId":"cd89fcba-95cb-5c41-a49d-01c3433e2588","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b95f0b0f-86d7-55e7-9b95-f851e90656d9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A complaint against a CIBN member has been investigated and referred for hearing. The body that hears the case and decides whether the member is guilty of unprofessional conduct is the","options":["Investigating Panel","Bankers' Committee","Disciplinary Tribunal","Board of Fellows"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Disciplinary Tribunal determines cases referred to it and can find a member guilty of unprofessional conduct. The Investigating Panel (option A) only carries out the preliminary inquiry, while options B and D are not the statutory body for deciding misconduct cases under the Act."}},{"itemId":"3c11634e-ea49-5a94-8232-d1e72e5855dc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6487f923-b5d7-588e-8b49-68bcbde61c89","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CIBN Act, the maximum sanction for a member found guilty of unprofessional conduct by the Disciplinary Tribunal is","options":["a reprimand only","imprisonment for life","revocation of his bank's operating licence","having his name struck off the register of members"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Act makes striking a name off the register the maximum sanction, which effectively removes the person from the profession. A reprimand (option A) is the lighter sanction, option B is not a power of a professional tribunal, and option C is a CBN power over banks."}},{"itemId":"e8b4a2f3-47c6-58fe-8d4b-791f48e71751","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9af70c28-5c7d-5c69-bd01-d7a8060f7cc0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which order correctly describes how a misconduct complaint against a CIBN member is handled?","options":["Council -> Investigating Panel -> Bankers' Committee","Disciplinary Tribunal -> Investigating Panel -> confirmation by the Council","Investigating Panel -> Disciplinary Tribunal -> confirmation by the Council","Federal High Court -> Investigating Panel -> Disciplinary Tribunal"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Panel first investigates and refers, the Tribunal then hears and decides, and its direction takes effect after confirmation by the Council. Option B reverses the first two stages, option A brings in the Bankers' Committee which has no role here, and option D wrongly puts the court first, when it only hears appeals."}},{"itemId":"632fb3cb-d741-59e9-8579-c2eea35e81b4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5ceddde3-551c-54d1-bb4f-36f87a15d624","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CIBN Act, membership of the Institute is divided into two broad categories, namely","options":["corporate members and individual members","practising members and retired members","local members and foreign members","public members and private members"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Act recognises corporate members (institutions) and individual members (persons in the various grades). Options D, C and B are not the categories used by the Act."}},{"itemId":"4751ee71-5287-5bce-be0e-3d0f2ef4b9e9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"70e4f3b2-84ca-5ad6-8663-f223407874ce","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a grade of individual membership of the CIBN?","options":["Principal Member","Honorary Senior Member","Associate","Fellow"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"Individual grades include Ordinary, Student, Graduate, Associate, Honorary Senior Member, Honorary Fellow and Fellow, but there is no Principal Member grade. Options C, D and B are genuine grades."}},{"itemId":"0d0db106-db36-5a48-b399-a4e85e7087cf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3ace0a94-faa3-57dd-aa36-cd68681d1aec","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following can be a corporate member of the CIBN?","options":["A retired bank manager","A bank's newly employed cashier","A microfinance bank","A university student of banking"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"Corporate membership is for institutions such as the CBN, the NDIC and deposit money, development, mortgage and microfinance banks. Options B, D and A are individuals, who would join in one of the individual grades instead."}},{"itemId":"160636b1-6dec-54ae-b035-3fd4b4b6b8dd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0949dbd1-1ee1-5457-b7d8-1265b7dac6f4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A banker who writes the letters ACIB after her name is, in the CIBN,","options":["a Fellow","an Associate","a Student member","an Honorary Senior Member"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"ACIB stands for Associate of the Chartered Institute of Bankers. A Fellow (option A) uses FCIB, an Honorary Senior Member (option D) uses HCIB, and a Student member (option C) has no such designatory letters."}},{"itemId":"e307c0fd-fd12-59f0-adc1-c83c3e46c10d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1b4c3221-2ddf-58c6-bc55-1685006a7af2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CIBN Act, the letters FCIB and HCIB respectively denote","options":["a Fellow and a Holder of a Certificate in Banking","a Founder and an Honorary Chairman","a Fellow and an Honorary Senior Member","an Associate and an Honorary Fellow"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"FCIB is used by Fellows and HCIB by persons registered as Honorary Senior Members. Option D wrongly gives FCIB to Associates, who use ACIB, and options A and B invent meanings for the letters."}},{"itemId":"a6624133-6d94-5720-8b5d-40cb9384439d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5a1684e4-959d-5960-9d3b-4380bd004506","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CIBN Act, a 'chartered banker' is a person who","options":["has worked in any bank for at least ten years","has passed the professional examinations and been elected an Associate or Fellow of the Institute","is a director of a licensed bank","holds a university degree in banking and finance"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Act defines a chartered banker by professional qualification and election to the Associate or Fellow grade. Long service (option A), a university degree (option D) or a directorship (option C) do not on their own make anyone a chartered banker."}},{"itemId":"3ff48bec-2443-51d6-adb0-7c3f2b6f0bf3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba481b3c-1239-5a98-87af-474637d69e4a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank wants to appoint Mr Bello to head its Treasury, a technical department. Under the CIBN Act, a key condition is that Mr Bello must","options":["be a member of the CIBN with valid, current registration","be approved by the Bankers' Committee","hold shares in the bank","have been a CBN staff member"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Act provides that only persons with current registration as members of the Institute may be appointed to head a bank's technical departments. Options C, B and D are not conditions set by the Act."}},{"itemId":"d3d50702-9b75-51d8-8e73-862144d76892","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"710769a0-cd5e-55cf-bbf8-e911e4a1b6fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CIBN Act, a person who is not a member of the Institute but holds himself out as a professional banker for reward","options":["commits no wrong if his bank is licensed","commits an offence","is automatically made a Student member","can be sanctioned only by his employer"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Act makes it an offence to practise or hold oneself out as a banker without being a member, with penalties of a fine, imprisonment or both. Option A confuses the bank's licence with the individual's professional status, and options C and D are not consequences the Act provides."}},{"itemId":"7d2ceb31-0de0-55bf-a330-864ee34b5303","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9a422cf1-bcdb-5ab6-a4cf-4e7faf085fb3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:CIB-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Bankers' Committee in Nigeria, a regular meeting of the central bank with the chief executives of banks, is presided over by the","options":["Managing Director of the NDIC","Governor of the Central Bank of Nigeria","Minister of Finance","President of the CIBN"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The Bankers' Committee brings the CBN and bank chief executives together, and the CBN Governor presides over it. The CIBN President (option D) chairs the Institute's own Council, while options C and A do not chair the Committee."}},{"itemId":"b392c325-94c5-54c8-b36b-a465c3182920","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"312c122d-2f67-5062-839f-41f52d29885a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An export arrangement in which the exporter ships the goods and sends the documents of title directly to the overseas buyer, relying entirely on the buyer to remit the proceeds after receiving the goods, is known as","options":["documentary letter of credit","documentary collection","open account trading","bill negotiation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Under open account the exporter hands over both the goods and the documents of title and simply trusts the importer to pay later, so the exporter loses all control. Option B still keeps the documents in the banking chain until the buyer pays or accepts, and option A adds a bank's undertaking to pay. Option D is a way of getting early finance on a bill, not a method of sending documents to the buyer."}},{"itemId":"f19d3ea9-ba29-5fd4-bba8-059aae502963","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e2ac2374-2c9c-5f98-8be7-207061f4deaa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"From the exporter's point of view, which international method of payment carries the HIGHEST risk of non-payment?","options":["Open account","Documentary collection on D/P terms","Documentary letter of credit","Advance payment by the buyer"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Open account is the riskiest for an exporter because goods and documents of title go straight to the buyer and payment depends only on the buyer's honesty. Option C gives a bank's guarantee of payment, and option B keeps the documents out of the buyer's hands until he pays. Option D is the safest possible position for an exporter, since the money arrives before the goods leave."}},{"itemId":"ebdc0240-4ce9-562d-a59a-6efe2de154ff","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aca5cc3f-ada4-53f3-8ee0-e4442a3c5ccd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which method of payment in foreign trade places the GREATEST risk on the IMPORTER?","options":["Confirmed documentary credit","Documentary collection on D/A terms","Advance payment (cash in advance)","Open account"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"With advance payment the importer pays before the goods are shipped, so he bears the full risk that the exporter never ships or ships the wrong goods. Option D is the reverse case, where the importer receives goods first and pays later, and option B also lets the importer obtain documents before paying. Option A protects both sides, because the bank pays only against documents that comply with the credit."}},{"itemId":"2f5a3827-6c3f-5b8f-9133-f3f3da53d76c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4c294124-7eb0-5ef9-9efc-6fc45e8d19e5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Kano exporter of sesame seeds hands his bank a bill of exchange drawn on a buyer in Germany, WITHOUT any shipping documents attached, and asks the bank to obtain payment. This is a","options":["documentary credit","documentary collection","bill negotiation","clean collection"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"When only the financial document, such as a bill of exchange, is sent for collection with no commercial documents attached, it is a clean collection. Option B would require commercial documents like the bill of lading and insurance certificate to accompany the bill. Option A involves a bank undertaking opened by the buyer's bank, and option C means the bank buys the bill rather than merely collecting it."}},{"itemId":"a1bfbd10-dde9-5dba-a7b9-77a6f43c230d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"55220d96-1cca-56c8-b237-346ceae97b04","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In international trade, a documentary collection is best described as one in which","options":["the bill of exchange is accompanied by commercial documents such as the bill of lading and insurance certificate","only the bill of exchange is forwarded to the overseas bank for payment","the exporter's bank buys the bill outright at a discount before it matures","the importer's bank gives an irrevocable undertaking to pay the exporter against compliant documents"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A documentary collection is a collection where the bill travels with the commercial documents, such as the bill of lading and insurance documents, and the bank releases them to the buyer only on payment or acceptance. Option D describes a documentary credit, which carries a bank undertaking that a collection does not. Option B is a clean collection, and option C is bill negotiation or discounting."}},{"itemId":"388868f6-40d9-567e-ac55-76b17b21d625","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"017a8ee4-ccb6-55c1-b3b8-b2b2d23b89ab","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which international method of payment is regarded as the surest for an exporter who wants a guarantee of payment from a bank, provided he complies with its terms?","options":["Documentary letter of credit","Clean collection","Open account","Documentary collection"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A documentary letter of credit gives the exporter a bank's guarantee of payment as long as he presents documents that comply with the terms of the credit. Option D keeps documents under bank control but the bank does not promise to pay, so the buyer may still refuse. Options C and B offer even less protection, because they rely on the buyer's willingness to pay."}},{"itemId":"6d0c996a-7fac-533b-b5be-41e1f740d79c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50646ad1-5fcd-5fd4-8c07-116b5422c34a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chief Adebayo, a Lagos cocoa exporter, needs cash now and cannot wait for his German buyer to pay. He endorses and delivers the bill of exchange drawn on the buyer to his bank, which pays him the face value less a discount for interest and commission. This service is","options":["a standing order","bill negotiation","open account settlement","documentary collection"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"In bill negotiation the exporter transfers the bill to his bank by endorsement and delivery and receives the face value less a discount, and the bank collects the full amount at maturity. Option D would mean the bank merely collects the money as agent and pays the exporter only when the buyer pays. Option C involves no bill passing through the bank, and option A is a domestic instruction for regular payments."}},{"itemId":"a9b31c3f-0e9e-50b9-9bd6-001b135c0b0e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"63346f22-5958-5b89-aad7-145f40dfed2c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An exporter negotiates a N4,000,000 export bill with his bank 3 months before maturity. The bank deducts interest at 12% per annum on the face value for the 3 months plus a commission of 0.5% of the face value. How much is credited to the exporter's account?","options":["N3,980,000","N3,500,000","N3,880,000","N3,860,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Interest is N4,000,000 x 12% x 3/12 = N120,000 and commission is 0.5% x N4,000,000 = N20,000, so the exporter receives N4,000,000 - N140,000 = N3,860,000. Option C forgets to deduct the commission, and option B wrongly charges a full year's interest of N480,000. Option A deducts only the commission and ignores the interest."}},{"itemId":"8e8b7b27-7ebd-565d-bf25-3579d5d5aac8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a028ff8-7d50-54f8-bfd3-e18223bd5efa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A nostro account, from the point of view of a Nigerian bank, is","options":["an account the Nigerian bank keeps with a correspondent bank abroad, usually in foreign currency","an account a foreign bank keeps with the Nigerian bank in naira","the account each bank keeps with the Central Bank of Nigeria for clearing","a foreign currency account opened by a Nigerian customer at a local branch"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Nostro means 'our', so it is our account held abroad: Nigerian banks keep these accounts overseas to receive export proceeds and settle foreign currency payments. Option B is a vostro account, which is 'your' account held with us. Option D is a domiciliary account, and option C is a settlement account with the central bank, not a correspondent banking account."}},{"itemId":"a81787b9-0e6b-56d8-9535-ca00fe0f1eee","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9843c21c-9126-5ff6-97e6-e5b7536c06a8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Accounts kept by foreign commercial banks with Nigerian banks, into which local-currency payments are made and from which the foreign banks settle their naira obligations in Nigeria, are called","options":["domiciliary accounts","escrow accounts","nostro accounts","vostro accounts"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Vostro means 'your', so a vostro account is the foreign bank's account held with a Nigerian bank, used to settle obligations in naira. Option C is the opposite: a Nigerian bank's own account held abroad. Option A is a foreign currency account held by a customer in Nigeria, and option B is an account where funds are held pending fulfilment of a condition."}},{"itemId":"dcb4e91e-c1be-5a6b-ba72-8bccc4773ad4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"770ae4ea-a2fb-503f-b1b9-a27753a05f37","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Nigerian bank holds a US dollar account with its correspondent bank in New York and uses it to receive its customers' export proceeds. To the Nigerian bank, this account is its","options":["domiciliary account","vostro account","nostro account","loro account"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Because the account belongs to the Nigerian bank and sits with a bank abroad, the Nigerian bank calls it 'our account with you', that is, a nostro account. Option B is how the New York bank would describe the same account from its own side. Option A is a customer's foreign currency account in Nigeria, and option D refers to an account of a third bank, not the bank's own account."}},{"itemId":"a56593f7-9491-571f-96e7-42e79662367d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"20977685-4734-5115-9bca-e9cb66a635bd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a documentary letter of credit transaction, the issuing bank is","options":["the importer's bank, which opens the credit in favour of the exporter","the bank that buys the exporter's bill at a discount before maturity","the exporter's bank, which tells the exporter that a credit has been opened","a bank in the exporter's country that adds its own undertaking to pay"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The issuing bank is the buyer's (importer's) bank, which opens the credit at the importer's request and gives the undertaking to pay. Option C is the advising bank, and option D is the confirming bank. Option B describes a negotiating or discounting bank."}},{"itemId":"6fae2ee0-b3b7-5959-872b-a0bcb7658f67","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"088aa419-15b4-5585-b91a-e042fc39b1b1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a letter of credit transaction, the bank in the exporter's country that informs the exporter that a credit has been opened in his favour, without necessarily adding its own promise to pay, is the","options":["confirming bank","issuing bank","advising bank","collecting bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The advising bank is the exporter's bank that passes on (advises) the credit to the exporter and checks that it looks genuine. Option B is the importer's bank that opens the credit, and option A goes further than advising by adding its own undertaking to pay. Option D belongs to documentary collections, not documentary credits."}},{"itemId":"b2f08548-84aa-53c1-8692-4bb70556f003","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cbd230f2-edbb-5bb6-86b0-258e3565631a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a documentary credit, the 'beneficiary' is","options":["the shipping company that carries the goods","the bank that opens the credit on the importer's instructions","the exporter, in whose favour the credit is opened","the importer, at whose request the credit is opened"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The beneficiary is the seller or exporter who is entitled to be paid under the credit once he presents compliant documents. Option D is the applicant, option B is the issuing bank, and option A is the carrier, which issues the bill of lading but is not a party to the credit."}},{"itemId":"c0850e0a-6d21-5f47-95a7-bda6c3d4bebe","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"843c3229-c324-5dd0-8aa0-1cd81bdae69d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The party who asks his bank to open a documentary letter of credit is known as the","options":["advising bank","drawee bank","applicant","beneficiary"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The applicant is the buyer (importer) who requests the credit and agrees to reimburse the issuing bank. Option D is the exporter who receives payment under the credit. Options B and A are banks, not the trading party who requests the credit."}},{"itemId":"dfa32aaa-fa0a-5b01-a378-f908948e960f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e877b53b-5cc5-5b13-83d9-9edff23c4087","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An Aba shoe exporter is worried that the overseas bank that issued a credit in his favour may fail to pay. He asks a reputable bank in Nigeria to add its own definite undertaking to pay him against compliant documents. The credit has become a","options":["confirmed credit","red clause credit","transferable credit","revolving credit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A credit is confirmed when a second bank, usually in the beneficiary's country, adds its own undertaking to pay, so the exporter is protected even if the issuing bank or its country fails. Option C allows the credit to be passed to another supplier, option D restores itself for repeated shipments, and option B allows advances before shipment."}},{"itemId":"e13dbb77-2611-5a80-b720-e2b2f352ceaa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"42b5b390-c39d-5c5e-9bb9-17145d233409","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under UCP 600, a documentary credit is","options":["revocable by the issuing bank at any time before shipment","irrevocable, even if it does not say so","revocable unless it expressly states that it is irrevocable","irrevocable only if it has been confirmed by a second bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"UCP 600 states that a credit is irrevocable even if there is no indication to that effect, so it cannot be amended or cancelled without the agreement of the issuing bank, any confirming bank and the beneficiary. Options C and A reflect older thinking about revocable credits, which UCP 600 no longer treats as the default. Option D confuses irrevocability with confirmation, which is a separate, extra undertaking."}},{"itemId":"b7eb8ea3-5d0f-564c-88e7-762d562e2337","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"39171932-36b9-5474-8453-53c514668121","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An irrevocable documentary credit is one that","options":["can be cancelled by the importer once the goods have been shipped","allows the exporter to transfer it to another supplier","must always carry the guarantee of a second bank in the exporter's country","cannot be amended or cancelled without the agreement of the issuing bank, any confirming bank and the beneficiary"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Irrevocable means the issuing bank's promise cannot be withdrawn or changed on its own: the beneficiary (and any confirming bank) must agree. Option A would defeat the whole purpose of the credit. Option C describes a confirmed credit, and option B describes a transferable credit."}},{"itemId":"b0175abe-d3c5-5fbe-afb5-509781ba145b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e99ee25e-18dd-5e7b-8735-7c8cf690a210","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Port Harcourt importer's bank receives documents under a letter of credit that comply exactly with its terms. The importer then complains that the goods, still at sea, may be of poor quality, and asks the bank not to pay. Under UCP 600, the bank should","options":["return the documents to the exporter and cancel the credit","pay, because banks deal with documents and not with the goods","pay only half the value until the quality dispute is settled","refuse to pay until the importer has inspected the goods"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Under UCP 600 banks deal with documents, not with the goods or services to which the documents relate, so a complying presentation must be honoured; a quality dispute is a matter between buyer and seller under their sales contract. Options D and C would make the bank a judge of the goods, which it is not. Option A is impossible because an irrevocable credit cannot be cancelled without the beneficiary's consent."}},{"itemId":"2c4c93fe-6e0b-57ed-bfa7-a591ca948a78","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0a86e1a5-19d9-5449-8546-a4b8881d4e40","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The internationally accepted rules that govern documentary letters of credit, published by the International Chamber of Commerce, are known as","options":["Incoterms","The Bills of Exchange Act","URC 522 (Uniform Rules for Collections)","UCP 600 (Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"UCP 600 is the ICC's set of rules for documentary credits, the 2007 revision. Option C is the ICC set for documentary collections, not credits. Option A defines delivery terms such as FOB and CIF between buyer and seller, and option B is national legislation on negotiable instruments."}},{"itemId":"a1a46a43-61e2-53aa-98fb-1319aab92172","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0ff50383-115d-5a40-920f-31a20fbf9c7e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Nigerian bank handling a documentary collection for an exporter would normally process it under which set of ICC rules?","options":["ISP98","UCP 600","URC 522","Incoterms"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"URC 522, the Uniform Rules for Collections, is the ICC set that governs documentary and clean collections. Option B governs documentary credits, and option A is designed for standby letters of credit. Option D sets out delivery and cost-sharing terms between buyer and seller, not bank collection procedures."}},{"itemId":"dc0f0950-a440-519d-b0f9-49f3152efa1f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d4fec6be-4cb0-5158-abb7-cdac9d9eaecb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main difference between documents against payment (D/P) and documents against acceptance (D/A) in a documentary collection is that","options":["under D/P a bank guarantees payment, while under D/A no bank is involved","under D/P the buyer gets the documents only on paying, while under D/A he gets them on accepting a bill payable later","under D/P the bill is payable at a future date, while under D/A it is payable at sight","D/P is used only for imports, while D/A is used only for exports"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"With D/P the collecting bank releases the shipping documents only when the buyer pays, usually at sight; with D/A it releases them when the buyer accepts a usance (time) bill, so payment comes later. Option A is wrong because neither D/P nor D/A carries a bank guarantee, as both are collections. Option C reverses the usual pattern, and option D is not a real distinction."}},{"itemId":"f8f9ae92-b03d-59e6-9ef4-eb759c7551c7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fc2d7917-1fb9-5b56-bfcf-357ee96af76c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under a documentary collection, an importer in Onitsha accepts a 90-day bill of exchange, collects the shipping documents and clears the goods, but at maturity refuses to pay. The exporter suffered this loss mainly because the collection was on","options":["documents against payment (D/P) terms","documents against acceptance (D/A) terms","a confirmed letter of credit","advance payment terms"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Under D/A the documents, and so the goods, are released once the buyer accepts the bill, leaving the exporter holding only an accepted bill if the buyer later defaults. Under option A the buyer could not have obtained the documents without paying first. Option C would have given a bank's promise to pay, and option D means the money would have been received before shipment."}},{"itemId":"c38c1986-d414-5093-8cd6-edb6108a597f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fdcd8d6-9132-5763-a909-836deea14df5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a documentary collection from a documentary credit?","options":["A collection is governed by UCP 600, whereas a credit is governed by URC 522","In a collection the banks act only as agents and give no undertaking to pay, whereas in a credit the issuing bank undertakes to pay","In a collection the importer's bank guarantees payment, whereas in a credit only the exporter's bank is involved","In a collection the documents go directly to the importer, whereas in a credit they go through the banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"In a collection the banks merely handle documents on the exporter's instructions and do not promise to pay, which is why a collection is riskier for the exporter than a credit. Option C gets the guarantee backwards, and option A swaps the two sets of ICC rules. Option D is wrong because documents pass through the banks in both methods; it is open account where they go directly to the buyer."}},{"itemId":"dd9fdde6-352a-55cc-a976-9621d4d8814a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9e0a3284-8920-55c5-9d94-7d98fa6ae532","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a documentary collection, the exporter's bank, which receives the documents from the exporter and sends them to a bank in the importer's country, is called the","options":["confirming bank","remitting bank","issuing bank","presenting bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The remitting bank is the bank to which the principal (exporter) entrusts the collection, and it forwards the documents abroad. Option D is the bank in the buyer's country that actually presents the documents to the drawee. Options C and A are parties to documentary credits, not collections."}},{"itemId":"39d3b6ac-fcc9-57d8-b275-3e8fab1e0407","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2a930ec2-af25-54a2-8f8f-b3501583da94","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A sight letter of credit differs from a usance (term) letter of credit in that","options":["a sight credit needs no documents, while a usance credit requires shipping documents","a sight credit is revocable, while a usance credit is always irrevocable","a sight credit can be transferred to a second beneficiary, while a usance credit cannot","under a sight credit payment is made when compliant documents are presented, while under a usance credit payment is made at a stated future date"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Sight means payment on presentation of complying documents; usance means the exporter is paid later, for example 90 days after sight or after the bill of lading date. Option B confuses timing with revocability, and option C confuses it with transferability. Option A is wrong because both are documentary credits that require documents."}},{"itemId":"546aea12-b0aa-5a45-ae0e-974c8cee9b13","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f2776f16-6284-5ee6-8bc7-9b46c5dda52d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Ezenwa, a Lagos middleman, holds a letter of credit from a Ghanaian buyer for 500 cartons of textiles. She has no stock, so she asks her bank to pass part of the credit to the Kano factory that will actually supply the goods. The type of credit that allows this is a","options":["transferable credit","red clause credit","standby credit","revolving credit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A transferable credit allows the first beneficiary, usually a middleman, to transfer all or part of it to one or more second beneficiaries such as the real supplier. Option D renews itself for repeated shipments, option B lets the beneficiary draw an advance before shipment, and option C acts as a guarantee that is drawn only on default."}},{"itemId":"2dcf3f3e-996f-5a29-aaa4-92977911682b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bb72a039-9cc3-5a46-8866-8cab0c575f24","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A back-to-back letter of credit is one in which","options":["a middleman uses a credit received from his buyer as security to have a second credit opened in favour of his own supplier","the beneficiary transfers part of the original credit directly to a second beneficiary","the beneficiary can draw an advance before shipping the goods","the amount of the credit is automatically reinstated after each shipment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"In a back-to-back arrangement the middleman's bank opens a separate, new credit for the supplier, backed by the original credit the middleman received. Option B describes a transferable credit, where the original credit itself is passed on. Option D is a revolving credit and option C is a red clause credit."}},{"itemId":"e0505719-04f1-5673-9fea-56a08c1f4ea2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99615b4b-eaf7-59fa-bcb0-7694fd398db8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A letter of credit contains a special clause allowing the exporter to draw an advance from the advising bank before shipment, to buy and pack the goods. This is a","options":["standby credit","back-to-back credit","revolving credit","red clause credit"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A red clause credit, so named because the clause was traditionally written in red ink, allows the beneficiary to obtain pre-shipment advances, making it a form of pre-shipment finance. Option C renews itself for repeated shipments, option A is drawn only if the applicant defaults, and option B is a second credit backed by an original one."}},{"itemId":"9360de61-a720-5e35-8c32-4a9d3801ea78","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c0ad980a-0fa0-5f90-9436-b05509528eed","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Lagos manufacturer imports the same raw material from India every month. Instead of opening a new credit for each shipment, his bank opens one credit whose amount is restored after each drawing for an agreed period. This is a","options":["revolving credit","transferable credit","red clause credit","back-to-back credit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A revolving credit is reinstated to its original amount after use, so it suits regular, repeated shipments between the same parties. Option B allows a middleman to pass the credit to a supplier, option C provides advances before shipment, and option D uses one credit to support another."}},{"itemId":"8d299e62-0bc4-5d13-bb60-a95ca5df1094","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d8f9aa4b-16f3-5222-943d-da91de431188","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A standby letter of credit is mainly used","options":["as the normal channel through which every shipment is paid for","to give the exporter an advance before the goods are shipped","to allow a middleman to transfer the credit to his supplier","as a guarantee, paid out only if the applicant fails to perform or pay as agreed"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A standby credit works like a bank guarantee: the beneficiary draws on it only when the applicant defaults, so in a smooth transaction it is never used. Option A describes an ordinary commercial credit, option B a red clause credit, and option C a transferable credit."}},{"itemId":"9fe04486-a202-5408-b114-7b0496cf03b8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"defa3fb3-6032-570e-a58b-a854705f635d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of a bill of lading?","options":["It certifies the country in which the goods were produced","It is a document of title to the goods","It is a receipt for the goods issued by the shipping company","It is evidence of the contract of carriage"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A bill of lading performs three classic functions: it is a receipt for goods shipped, evidence of the contract of carriage and a document of title that allows the goods to be transferred by endorsement. Stating the country of manufacture is the job of a certificate of origin. Options C, D and B are all genuine functions of a bill of lading."}},{"itemId":"753a6c2c-7dba-56b9-bcdd-702d21f7c4bb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c6803316-83ab-5034-b9a3-4218c5481f9e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which shipping document gives its holder the right to take delivery of the goods and can be transferred by endorsement, making it a document of title?","options":["Insurance certificate","Certificate of origin","Bill of lading","Commercial invoice"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The bill of lading is a document of title, so whoever lawfully holds it can claim the goods, which is why banks keep it until the buyer pays or accepts. Option D shows the price and description of the goods, option B states where they were made, and option A proves that the goods are insured; none of them gives control over the goods."}},{"itemId":"b065219b-4089-5240-b3fb-b37d36923e58","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"617e10f0-733e-5f6c-81eb-3f25496b73fa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A certificate of origin presented with export documents serves mainly to","options":["evidence the contract between the exporter and the shipping line","prove that the goods are covered against loss in transit","state the country in which the goods were produced or manufactured","show the price, quantity and description of the goods sold"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A certificate of origin confirms where the goods were made, which matters for customs duties, trade agreements and import rules. Option A is a function of the bill of lading, option D is the role of the commercial invoice, and option B is the role of the insurance certificate or policy."}},{"itemId":"aebd4566-d545-5e38-8f46-86a732890d5f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"03ede486-8a10-5f40-9eb8-6fbbffb88518","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The shipping document prepared by the seller that shows the description, quantity, unit price and total value of the goods, and is used for payment and customs valuation, is the","options":["bill of lading","certificate of origin","commercial invoice","insurance policy"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The commercial invoice is the seller's bill to the buyer, setting out what was sold and for how much. Option A is issued by the carrier as a receipt and document of title, option B states the country of manufacture, and option D covers the goods against loss or damage in transit."}},{"itemId":"82c4f9b2-1915-5efe-8db8-0ac61225cc61","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"41072ef9-6c66-59a9-a45f-03b4e7feedd1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Goods shipped from Apapa on CIF terms are damaged by sea water during the voyage. To claim compensation, the buyer will rely mainly on the","options":["certificate of origin","commercial invoice","Form NXP","insurance policy or certificate"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The insurance policy or certificate is evidence that the goods are covered against loss or damage in transit, and it is the basis of any claim against the insurer. Option A only states where the goods were made, and option B shows their price and description. Option C is a Nigerian export proceeds form, not an insurance document."}},{"itemId":"feae7894-6ced-55c2-a957-5ee0bcf6454c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b32bba1-d6e3-5771-b77a-70ec611f3b3b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Okonkwo, a Lagos trader, wants to import machinery from China through his bank. Under Nigerian import procedures, the statutory document he must first complete and register electronically through his authorised dealer bank, supported by a proforma invoice, is","options":["Form M","Form NXP","Form A","a bill of lading"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Form M is the statutory document required for physical imports into Nigeria, processed electronically through an authorised dealer bank and supported by a proforma invoice and other documents. Option B is for export proceeds, and option C is used for invisible transactions such as school fees and travel, not imports of goods. Option D is issued by the shipping company after goods are shipped."}},{"itemId":"01af0a0f-d36e-5f13-8808-9255dcda6259","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9e976cc3-117a-5c0d-955a-6d35b7a8caed","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigeria, the statutory form completed by an exporter through his bank so that the export and the repatriation of its proceeds can be documented and tracked is the","options":["Form NXP (Nigerian Export Proceeds Form)","Certificate of Origin","Form M","Form A"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Form NXP, the Nigerian Export Proceeds Form, is completed through the exporter's bank to document an export and the proceeds that must come back to Nigeria. Option C is the import counterpart, used before goods are brought in. Option D relates to invisible payments, and option B only states where the goods were made."}},{"itemId":"ba654708-c02a-5662-aa41-cace1bb3f179","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"acd00995-1092-5010-bf4a-ed724161918a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A domiciliary account in a Nigerian bank is","options":["an account a Nigerian bank keeps with a correspondent bank abroad","an account a foreign bank keeps with a Nigerian bank in naira","an account held in a foreign currency, such as US dollars, in a bank in Nigeria","a naira account opened only for customers who live near the branch"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A domiciliary account lets a customer hold, receive and pay out foreign currency through a bank in Nigeria, which is useful for exporters receiving dollar proceeds and importers making foreign payments. Option A is a nostro account and option B is a vostro account, both kept between banks. Option D confuses 'domiciliary' with where the customer lives."}},{"itemId":"0f4774a1-ffa6-56b2-8580-b927d9003bcf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"119707eb-b566-5dd4-aa87-6a38512373f2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The key difference between a spot foreign exchange transaction and a forward foreign exchange contract is that","options":["a spot deal is used only by banks, while a forward contract is used only by importers","a spot deal involves two currencies, while a forward contract involves only one","a spot deal fixes a rate for a future date, while a forward contract is settled immediately","a spot deal is settled almost immediately at today's rate, while a forward contract fixes a rate today for exchange at a future date"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A spot transaction is settled within a very short period, normally two business days, at the current rate, whereas a forward contract locks in an agreed rate now for delivery of the currency later. Option B reverses the two. Options A and C are not real distinctions, since both deals are used by many market participants and both always involve two currencies."}},{"itemId":"8f222b9d-adf1-59e0-a87e-f32ea3d7919a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3ab52aa5-869b-5ea8-b736-91e2ae21538e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Lagos importer must pay a US supplier $40,000 in three months' time and fears the naira may weaken before then. To remove this exchange rate risk, his bank can best offer him","options":["a clean collection","a spot purchase of dollars in three months' time","a standing order","a forward exchange contract"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"A forward contract fixes today the rate at which the importer will buy dollars in three months, so a later fall in the naira will not raise his naira cost. Option B leaves him fully exposed, because he would buy at whatever rate prevails then. Option A is a way of collecting payment, and option C is a domestic payment instruction; neither manages exchange risk."}},{"itemId":"e5762a62-092e-5b37-ae22-99f3d5aa2544","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f7ade07-7346-5512-892d-30e3e006d36e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An importer books a forward contract with his bank to buy $25,000 in three months at N1,520 per dollar. On the settlement date the spot rate is N1,560 per dollar. How much naira does the forward contract save him compared with buying at the spot rate on that day?","options":["N1,000,000","N38,000,000","N40,000","N39,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"At spot he would pay $25,000 x N1,560 = N39,000,000, but under the forward contract he pays $25,000 x N1,520 = N38,000,000, saving N1,000,000. Options B and D are the total costs under each rate rather than the saving. Option C is just the rate difference of N40 multiplied by 1,000 instead of by 25,000."}},{"itemId":"c9bce7ec-bd0c-5950-9d69-fc446002724d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5b7a9fd-f6e6-5594-b558-cbce063f4c99","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank finances a Sapele timber exporter to buy, process and pack the timber before it is shipped, and later discounts the export bill after shipment. These two facilities are, respectively,","options":["factoring and forfaiting","pre-shipment finance and post-shipment finance","post-shipment finance and pre-shipment finance","import finance and export credit insurance"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Pre-shipment (packing) finance helps the exporter produce or buy the goods before shipping, while post-shipment finance, such as negotiating or discounting the export bill, bridges the gap between shipment and receipt of the proceeds. Option C reverses the order. Option D is wrong because both facilities are export finance, and option A names other specialised receivables techniques."}},{"itemId":"eb3d5b01-2d1e-5dba-ac8e-88d8ae29e92a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d303f2a1-825a-5120-a87f-c2bb4bdf424d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian Export-Import Bank (NEXIM) was established by","options":["the Central Bank of Nigeria Act of 1958, as a department of the CBN","the NEXIM Act No. 38 of 1991, owned jointly by the Federal Government and the Central Bank of Nigeria","a 2001 CBN circular introducing universal banking","the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act of 1991, as a commercial bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"NEXIM was set up by the Nigerian Export-Import Bank Act No. 38 of 1991 as Nigeria's export credit agency, with its capital subscribed equally by the Federal Government and the CBN. Option D is the general banking law, not NEXIM's establishing Act. Options A and C refer to the central bank's own statute and to the universal banking era, which have nothing to do with NEXIM's creation."}},{"itemId":"81ba0e84-4ca1-5eca-8455-0de95cf6a246","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2f8614e8-32e2-5b35-b0b8-9f2ae431804e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the Nigerian Export-Import Bank (NEXIM)?","options":["Maintaining a foreign exchange revolving fund lent to exporters to import inputs","Providing investment guarantee and investment insurance facilities to exporters","Providing export credit guarantee and credit insurance facilities to Nigerian exporters","Granting long-term mortgage loans to individuals for home ownership"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"NEXIM is an export credit agency, so its work centres on export guarantees, credit insurance, a foreign exchange revolving fund and investment guarantees for exporters. Housing finance is the business of mortgage institutions such as the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria. Options C, A and B are all listed NEXIM functions."}},{"itemId":"3ad51788-44cd-5d76-b430-33578ede90ba","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd25465d-447a-52fb-9712-8e73f25da422","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Nigerian exporter of processed cashew fears that his buyer in another country may fail to pay because of war or a ban on foreign currency transfers in that country. The facility that protects him against both such political risks and the buyer's commercial default is","options":["a domiciliary account in Nigeria","export credit insurance, such as that offered by NEXIM","a clean collection through his bank","a nostro account with a foreign bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Export credit insurance compensates the exporter when a foreign buyer fails to pay for commercial reasons (such as insolvency) or political reasons (such as war or transfer restrictions); NEXIM provides this cover for Nigerian exporters. Options D and A are simply accounts for holding currency and offer no protection against default. Option C is a payment method in which the bank gives no guarantee at all."}},{"itemId":"19065c94-a731-5571-8a87-984160573951","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a112b57-b1e1-57ca-86a5-f072b1242eb9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the operation of the Nigerian Export-Import Bank, the foreign exchange revolving fund is meant to","options":["settle the clearing balances of deposit money banks","lend foreign exchange to exporters to import inputs needed to produce exportable goods","finance government imports of petroleum products","pay school fees and medical bills of Nigerians abroad"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"NEXIM maintains a foreign exchange revolving fund from which exporters borrow to import raw inputs and other items used in producing goods for export. Option D describes invisible personal payments, and option C is not an export promotion activity. Option A relates to the clearing system, which is outside NEXIM's role."}},{"itemId":"2d9515ee-6b73-5484-a4cb-f9d1949b3fc6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2a5f6182-c711-5175-a46f-0baf2414cb34","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best distinguishes forfaiting from factoring?","options":["Forfaiting is the purchase, without recourse, of medium-term export receivables such as avalised bills, while factoring is the sale of short-term book debts","Forfaiting is a type of letter of credit, while factoring is a type of documentary collection","Forfaiting is lending against landed property, while factoring is lending against shares","Forfaiting is the sale of short-term book debts, while factoring buys medium-term export bills without recourse"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Factoring is essentially the sale of book debts, usually short-term trade receivables, whereas forfaiting is the without-recourse purchase of medium-term export receivables, typically bills of exchange or similar negotiable instruments guaranteed (avalised) by a bank. Option D swaps the two. Options B and C confuse them with payment methods and with secured lending."}},{"itemId":"79c4c609-1991-526e-9d50-a3d4876c517c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c5038e1f-6368-5a48-8c43-c52230405387","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In providing foreign exchange services under correspondent banking, Nigerian merchant banks","options":["participate directly in the cheque clearing house","accept savings deposits from individual customers with passbooks","arrange the confirmation of letters of credit for their clients","issue travellers cheques to retail customers in rural branches"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Merchant banks provide foreign exchange services to corporate clients on a wholesale basis, and under correspondent banking they arrange for clients' letters of credit to be confirmed. Option A is wrong because merchant banks are not members of the clearing house. Options D and B are retail services more typical of commercial banks."}},{"itemId":"e190a72f-2ebf-50e1-9796-033cd7d36a96","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"22b72a0d-03d7-5707-9f56-2fa95cc00e56","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The traditional first function of merchant banks, in which they add their name to bills of exchange to finance the trade of others, is","options":["equipment leasing","acceptance","issuing house services","project financing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Acceptance is the classic merchant banking function: the bank accepts bills of exchange so that its customers' trade can be financed on the strength of the bank's name. Option A involves hiring out assets, option D funds large new projects through a special entity, and option C helps companies raise capital from the public."}},{"itemId":"9354619c-d90c-57fc-80b8-1259e178d515","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9e6a4ac4-1033-5c1c-a5c7-d0be5c24be3e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a role of commercial banks in international trade?","options":["Discounting documentary credits so that sellers are paid at once","Fixing the official exchange rate of the naira","Acting as referees on the creditworthiness of exporters","Confirming letters of credit to reduce default in payment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Commercial banks help foreign trade by providing documentary credits, discounting them, confirming letters of credit, acting as referees and dealing in foreign exchange. Managing the exchange rate is a monetary authority matter for the Central Bank of Nigeria, not a service a commercial bank sells to traders. Options A, C and D are all genuine roles."}},{"itemId":"690ef2b8-bc9c-53da-b7c8-d69ee7952784","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a35f4b61-0c5d-5ecc-a6ac-4326ae80e606","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before signing a large supply contract, a Ghanaian importer asks for information on the financial standing of a Nigerian exporter. The exporter's bank provides a confidential reply on his creditworthiness. In this role the bank is","options":["operating a vostro account for the importer","acting as a referee by giving a status opinion","providing post-shipment finance","acting as an issuing bank under a letter of credit"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Banks act as referees for their customers by giving status opinions or bankers' references on creditworthiness, usually in general and confidential terms. Option D involves opening a credit, not reporting on a customer, and option C involves lending to the exporter after shipment. Option A is a correspondent account between banks."}},{"itemId":"ec7b31eb-c9f3-5a70-9200-0840f2141db2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5316e4e0-c183-59d8-ab0a-8b0d67cd4d94","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a Nigerian bank actually remits payment abroad for a customer's international transaction, which of the following channels is commonly used?","options":["Standing order card","Intra-bank transfer between the customer's own accounts","Direct debit mandate","Telegraphic (wire) transfer"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"In international payments the actual remittance is made by mail transfer, telegraphic transfer or bank draft, with telegraphic or wire transfer being the fast option. Options A and C are domestic methods for regular or creditor-initiated payments. Option B only moves money between the same customer's accounts within one bank."}},{"itemId":"328a7569-a2b3-5aeb-942b-1706ea6b0aa4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0f4e65b-4f53-5b94-a89c-9a4b24317e4a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why do exporters generally prefer documentary collection to open account, even though neither gives a bank guarantee of payment?","options":["Because a collection is cheaper than open account, which carries high bank charges","Because a collection allows the exporter to receive payment before shipping the goods","Because in a collection the shipping documents stay under bank control until the buyer pays or accepts the bill","Because in a collection the importer's bank must pay even if the importer refuses"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"In a documentary collection the exporter's bank and the collecting bank hold the documents of title and release them only against payment or acceptance, so the buyer cannot simply take the goods without paying or accepting. Option D describes a letter of credit, not a collection. Option B describes advance payment, and option A is not the reason, since open account involves the least bank handling."}},{"itemId":"121ba061-5b94-52ab-8b82-90efcb339f51","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1fc54ee5-578b-5df4-85b1-b0393bfefdd6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under a documentary credit, if the documents presented by the exporter do NOT match the terms of the credit, the issuing bank","options":["must still pay, since it has already issued an irrevocable credit","must inspect the goods to decide whether to pay","is entitled to refuse payment because the presentation is not complying","must pay half and hold the rest until the importer agrees"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The bank's undertaking is to pay against a complying presentation, so documents with discrepancies give it the right to refuse, although it may approach the applicant for a waiver. Option A ignores the condition that documents must comply. Options B and D are wrong because banks deal in documents, not goods, and there is no rule of part payment."}},{"itemId":"d4ee1b6c-eda0-556a-bb57-23020495e255","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53990c4d-d0e3-5c7f-9e71-9e991af4f030","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:EXP-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Unlike a bill of lading, an air waybill used for goods sent by air from Lagos","options":["states the country in which the goods were made","must be endorsed to transfer ownership of the goods","is issued by the exporter rather than the carrier","is not a document of title, so the goods can be released to the named consignee without it"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"An air waybill is a receipt and evidence of the contract of carriage, but it is not a document of title; the airline delivers the goods to the named consignee. Option B describes a negotiable bill of lading, which is a document of title. Option C is wrong because the carrier or its agent issues it, and option A is the job of a certificate of origin."}},{"itemId":"3d5efc40-b71a-508f-a925-3a7cee06b0d5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b04bcd97-33e0-56c7-ac3d-225219e8527e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best defines the financial system?","options":["The set of government agencies that collect taxes and spend public revenue","The network of financial institutions, markets, instruments and regulations that channels funds from surplus units to deficit units in an economy","The group of commercial banks that accept deposits and grant loans to the public","The market in which long-term securities such as shares and bonds are traded"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The financial system is the whole network of institutions, markets, instruments and regulators that moves funds from savers (surplus units) to borrowers (deficit units), helping to mobilise savings and allocate capital. Option C is too narrow, because commercial banks are only one type of financial institution within the system. Option D describes the capital market, which is just one of the financial markets, while option A describes the government's fiscal machinery rather than the financial system."}},{"itemId":"bc698f8b-7490-5f6f-8016-22f779a54410","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9c04e009-5e2c-5854-aa8e-f8b793f8dc5d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0001-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a financial system, funds are mainly channelled","options":["from importers to exporters through the foreign exchange market","from deficit units (borrowers) to surplus units (savers)","from surplus units (savers) to deficit units (borrowers)","from the central bank directly to households for their daily spending"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Surplus units are people and organisations that earn more than they spend, and the financial system moves their idle savings to deficit units who need funds for consumption or investment. Option B reverses this flow, since borrowers receive funds rather than supply them. Options D and A describe narrow or incorrect flows that do not capture the basic intermediation role of the financial system."}},{"itemId":"55541101-f7fb-5950-8972-fa42decdbff7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"021abbf5-513e-5fb7-b055-174dac57b02a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an objective of the financial system?","options":["Fixing the prices of all goods and services in the economy","Provision of liquidity to investors and businesses","Mobilisation of savings from individuals and organisations","Facilitation of payment and settlement of transactions"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The financial system does not set the prices of goods and services; prices are mostly determined by the market forces of demand and supply. Mobilising savings (option C), providing liquidity (option B) and facilitating payments and settlements (option D) are all genuine objectives of the financial system, alongside efficient allocation of funds and risk management."}},{"itemId":"88c7e424-fb24-5a2e-b152-a510b619609c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e4c8cad-c780-52b8-a087-962046469c36","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0002-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The ability of investors to convert their financial assets into cash quickly without significant loss of value reflects which function of the financial system?","options":["Price discovery","Monetary policy transmission","Liquidity provision","Risk transfer"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Liquidity provision means the financial system allows investors to turn their investments into cash quickly and at little cost, for example by selling securities in a market. Option D refers to shifting risk to others through insurance or diversification, and option A refers to markets setting fair prices for assets through supply and demand. Option B is the channel through which central bank actions affect the economy, not the ease of converting assets to cash."}},{"itemId":"ecd8f722-ffb6-5a13-8c42-f7fab2170fb2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0c166b7-50ba-5cf6-b6f0-b518e187a584","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An IOU ('I owe you') is best described as","options":["a written acknowledgement of a debt owed by one party to another","a document of title to goods shipped by sea","a certificate showing part-ownership of a company","an instruction by a customer to his bank to make regular fixed payments to a third party"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"An IOU is simply a written acknowledgement that one person owes another a sum of money; it usually shows the parties, the amount and the date, and serves as evidence of the obligation. Option C describes a share certificate, which represents ownership rather than debt. Option B is a bill of lading used in foreign trade, and option D describes a standing order."}},{"itemId":"d0b5b45e-5f22-56e4-ba9f-bf2298dd408b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8c0c627-a640-586b-a35c-a208bb368195","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes the Nigerian banking system before independence?","options":["It was dominated by indigenous banks closely supervised by the Central Bank of Nigeria","Colonial banks channelled most of their credit to Nigerian farmers and small traders","It was built around a well-developed stock exchange that financed most Nigerian businesses","It was dominated by foreign-owned banks that served mainly colonial trade and gave little credit to indigenous entrepreneurs"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Before independence, expatriate banks controlled Nigerian banking, concentrating on financing trade between Nigeria and Britain and lending mainly to foreign firms, while regulation was weak. Option A is wrong because indigenous banks were few and many of them failed, and the Central Bank of Nigeria did not begin operations until 1959. Option B is the opposite of what happened, since colonial banks were known for discriminating against indigenous borrowers, and option C is wrong because organised stock market activity only began around independence."}},{"itemId":"c7816292-fb15-5302-a6c5-66d53c003f32","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"158349ab-1a2d-532d-9ff8-bf9171a33a83","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0004-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank of Nigeria, established under the CBN Act of 1958, commenced operations on","options":["1 October 1960","1 July 1958","1 July 1959","1 January 1962"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN Act was passed in 1958, but the Bank formally opened for business on 1 July 1959, taking over currency issue from the West African Currency Board. Option B picks the year the Act was passed, not the year operations started. Option A is Nigeria's independence day, which came more than a year after the CBN began operating, and option D has no connection with the start of the CBN."}},{"itemId":"a727e0fa-0ea0-513f-a93e-0e02fa0b0bd5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7ba42e89-6af5-53e7-b656-6a49eef0b5a3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0004-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before the Central Bank of Nigeria was set up, currency in Nigeria was issued by the","options":["Federal Ministry of Finance","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","Bank of British West Africa","West African Currency Board"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The West African Currency Board, set up by the British colonial government in 1912, issued sterling-backed currency for Nigeria and other British West African colonies until the CBN took over this role. Option C is tempting because that bank acted as the Board's agent in distributing currency, but it was not the issuing authority. Option B is a deposit insurer created long after independence, and option A has never been a currency-issuing authority in Nigeria."}},{"itemId":"4c13162e-efab-56ec-af12-67d94f9db2b9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7503d2ee-efd7-53ec-8474-b539165abe7a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a factor that contributed to the development of the Nigerian financial system?","options":["Growth in savings and investment activities","The establishment of the Central Bank of Nigeria","Technological advancement such as electronic banking","A return to barter as the main means of exchange"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"A return to barter would shrink, not develop, the financial system, because barter removes the need for money, banks and financial markets. The establishment of the CBN (option B) provided leadership and regulation, technology such as electronic banking (option C) widened access to financial services, and rising savings and investment (option A) increased the funds flowing through financial institutions."}},{"itemId":"7898c2e6-4906-5024-885f-78c4e0f3643c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fa809dd-94d6-56e5-b600-1f37aa4d2539","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The view that financial institutions and services expand in response to the demand created by growth in the real economy is known as","options":["the financial repression hypothesis","the demand-following hypothesis","the supply-leading hypothesis","the theory of credit creation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Under the demand-following view, economic growth comes first and creates demand for financial services, so banks and other institutions develop to meet that demand. Option C is the reverse view, where financial institutions are set up ahead of demand to stimulate growth. Option A refers to government controls such as interest rate ceilings that hold back financial development, and option D explains how banks expand deposits through lending, not how financial development relates to growth."}},{"itemId":"5d135cac-1dbb-5389-95c1-1dc912c19dfa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8af5cee-4ab0-5d55-9345-5d8731b9afb5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0006-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the supply-leading approach to financial development,","options":["banks lend only to the government and refuse private sector borrowers","the central bank fixes interest rates below market levels and rations credit to favoured sectors","financial institutions appear only after the real economy has grown and created a demand for their services","financial institutions are deliberately set up ahead of demand to mobilise savings and stimulate economic growth"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The supply-leading approach holds that creating financial institutions first can drive growth, because they mobilise savings and provide funds for investment before the economy demands them. Option C describes the demand-following approach, where finance responds to growth instead of leading it. Options B and A describe credit controls and restrictions rather than either view of financial development."}},{"itemId":"f0d988d1-6e8e-546b-a3aa-1fa0647d87ca","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae0cca2b-e137-5fc2-a92b-2db801f5b5c7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the stages in the evolution of banking?","options":["The development of central banking systems","The replacement of money by barter as the main means of exchange","Goldsmiths accepting deposits of gold and issuing receipts","Money lending by merchants and money changers"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Banking evolved forward from money changers and merchants to goldsmith bankers, commercial banks and then central banks, so a move back to barter is not a stage in its evolution. Option D describes the earliest money-lending activities from which banking grew. Option C describes goldsmith banking, which led to fractional reserve lending, and option A is a later stage in which central banks came to regulate the banking system."}},{"itemId":"3fcd9f55-f766-576b-b529-a4adcbb6a396","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"abdfbca1-74f2-5cd1-a759-0591cd655e2e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Goldsmiths began lending out part of the gold deposited with them mainly because they observed that","options":["the central bank had guaranteed repayment of their loans","depositors rarely withdrew all their gold at the same time","gold kept in their vaults steadily lost its value","the law required them to lend out all deposits received"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Goldsmiths noticed that only a small part of the gold left with them was withdrawn at any one time, so they could safely lend out the rest and earn interest. This idea of keeping only a fraction of deposits as reserves is the basis of modern fractional reserve banking. Option C is wrong because gold holds its value well, option D is wrong because no law forced them to lend, and option A is wrong because no central bank guaranteed goldsmiths' loans."}},{"itemId":"56086f25-40f6-572e-902d-4916506ee3c1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"75d405f7-687e-5617-9b1e-63eef2eb6c02","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0008-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement about goldsmiths and lending is most accurate?","options":["Goldsmiths' receipts were never accepted by anyone as a means of payment","Goldsmiths were the first people in history to lend money at interest","Goldsmiths only stored gold for safekeeping and never lent any of it","Lending existed before goldsmiths, but their practice of lending out part of deposited gold helped lay the foundation of modern banking"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Money lending is far older than goldsmith banking, but goldsmiths formalised the practice of lending part of the deposits entrusted to them, which is why they are seen as forerunners of modern banks. Option B overstates their role, since merchants and money lenders lent at interest long before them. Options C and A are wrong because goldsmiths did lend part of the deposits, and their receipts came to circulate as a means of payment."}},{"itemId":"3e4db3bf-95d1-54f9-bcc7-b3b2cf6b31d3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"484d1718-87cf-58a8-8dd2-845c4e56007e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a limitation of the barter system?","options":["Indivisibility of some goods","Difficulty in making deferred payments","Inflation caused by over-issue of currency","Lack of double coincidence of wants"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Barter involves no currency at all, so it cannot suffer from inflation caused by issuing too much money; that problem belongs to money economies. Option D is the most serious weakness of barter, since each trader must find someone who has what he wants and wants what he has. Indivisibility (option A) and the difficulty of arranging future payments (option B) are also genuine limitations of barter."}},{"itemId":"893c02a4-83f7-5132-bbdd-1f4c00c3536b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"55acf16a-0c2a-5b9b-9b7d-7aebff907670","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0009-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A cattle owner wants to obtain a few bags of salt worth far less than one cow, but the cow cannot be split without destroying its value. This illustrates which limitation of barter?","options":["Lack of double coincidence of wants","Indivisibility of goods","Lack of a standard of deferred payment","Difficulty in storing value"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Indivisibility means some goods cannot be broken into smaller units without losing their value, so they cannot be used for smaller exchanges; a live cow is the classic example. Option A would apply if the salt seller did not want a cow, which is a different problem. Option D concerns perishable goods losing value over time, and option C concerns arranging payments in the future, neither of which is the issue here."}},{"itemId":"b436cb8f-1ef1-54dc-8320-4aa0b7f9aa87","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"67ab6c38-7688-50b8-b012-282a6b1c52fa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of money?","options":["Keeping the general price level constant at all times","Medium of exchange","Store of value","Unit of account"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Keeping prices constant is not something money does; prices can rise and erode the purchasing power of money, and price stability is a goal of monetary policy rather than a function of money. Medium of exchange (option B), unit of account (option D) and store of value (option C) are all recognised functions of money, together with standard of deferred payment."}},{"itemId":"ec44318c-c212-564c-b27e-aff1da5b09a3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b11de3cf-4fcc-5a36-945e-f413e364c90f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0010-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a borrower agrees to repay a bank loan in fixed naira amounts over the next five years, money is serving as a","options":["standard of deferred payment","store of value","unit of account","medium of exchange"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money acts as a standard of deferred payment when debts and contracts to be settled in the future are expressed and paid in money, as in a loan repaid in naira over five years. Option C is related, since the loan is stated in naira, but unit of account refers to pricing and comparing values, while the function that covers future settlement of debts is the standard of deferred payment. Option D applies to buying and selling now, and option B applies to holding money to spend later."}},{"itemId":"a358706c-d1bd-5653-8af6-16598b8cec42","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"837def69-55e3-51cb-a2de-e94e921147bd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an example of near money?","options":["Treasury bills","Demand deposits on current accounts","Currency notes held by the public","Coins in circulation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Treasury bills are near money because they can be converted into cash quickly and with little loss, yet they are not themselves generally accepted as a means of payment. Options C and D are cash, which is money proper. Option B, current account deposits, can be spent directly by cheque or transfer, so they are counted as money rather than near money."}},{"itemId":"a38c6e40-fa82-5055-a1cc-c3d2e7ef1a9f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bc83ff28-8315-5156-a48f-6fed5ce68592","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0011-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Near money (quasi-money) is best described as","options":["financial assets that can be converted into cash quickly with little or no loss but are not generally accepted as a medium of exchange","foreign currencies held by the central bank as external reserves","assets such as currency and coins that are generally accepted in payment for goods and services","money issued by the government that has no intrinsic value"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Near money includes assets such as savings and time deposits, treasury bills and bills of exchange: they are highly liquid stores of value, but people do not normally use them directly to pay for goods. Option C describes money proper, which is generally accepted as a medium of exchange. Option D describes fiat money, and option B describes external reserves, which are a different concept."}},{"itemId":"e0320984-5f94-5619-967e-d2fb999214d6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4767a830-d0ad-5cb3-9d71-ad2eff984003","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a way in which money facilitates economic activity?","options":["Promoting specialisation and division of labour","Making it necessary for each household to produce all the goods it needs","Eliminating the need for a double coincidence of wants","Encouraging savings and investment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money does the opposite of forcing self-sufficiency: because goods can be sold for money and the money used to buy other goods, people can specialise in what they do best. Removing the need for a double coincidence of wants (option C), encouraging savings and investment (option D) and promoting specialisation and division of labour (option A) are all ways money makes the economy work better."}},{"itemId":"5c98a55c-31c4-5cb0-ad3c-0f0c6b12b394","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a2c5e93-8bc7-5346-8bdc-fbbe25732048","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Other things being equal, which of the following would most likely INCREASE the money supply?","options":["A greater public preference for holding cash rather than bank deposits","A reduction in the cash reserve ratio by the central bank","An increase in the cash reserve ratio by the central bank","Sale of treasury bills by the central bank to banks through open market operations"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"A lower cash reserve ratio frees up reserves that banks can lend, and as loans are redeposited the banking system creates more deposits, so the money supply rises. Option C has the opposite effect because banks must lock up more reserves. Option D withdraws reserves from banks when they pay for the securities, and option A leaves less cash in the banking system to support new lending, so both reduce the money supply."}},{"itemId":"9add9f10-74bf-5954-826c-dc56027643fc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ce48ff0c-5e8b-56f9-b024-419cbfa9d31a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best describes the origin of money?","options":["Money was created at a single point in time by one government decree and has not changed form since","Money arose because barter made it too easy to store wealth","Money is a social invention that evolved gradually to overcome the inefficiencies of barter","Money developed only after commercial banks had been established"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money was not invented in one moment; societies gradually adopted commonly accepted commodities, then metals, paper and bank money, all to escape the problems of barter such as the double coincidence of wants. Option A ignores this long evolution through many forms. Option D reverses the order, since money in forms such as cowries and metal coins existed long before modern banks, and option B is wrong because barter made storing wealth difficult, not easy."}},{"itemId":"fa8c11a2-81d4-53be-9214-76b77f2fae39","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7979f092-183b-5c02-8472-fd840f4a71bf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank receives a fresh cash deposit of N2,000,000. If the cash reserve ratio is 20% and banks lend out all excess reserves with no cash leakage, what is the maximum total amount of deposits the banking system can create, including the initial deposit?","options":["N10,000,000","N400,000","N2,500,000","N8,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The credit multiplier is 1 divided by the reserve ratio, so 1/0.20 = 5, and maximum total deposits are N2,000,000 x 5 = N10,000,000. Option D is only the new deposits created through lending, which leaves out the initial N2,000,000 the question asks you to include. Option C wrongly divides by 0.80 instead of 0.20, and option B is just the reserve the first bank keeps on the initial deposit."}},{"itemId":"89eb259a-9a81-5e0f-971b-d65d77dc040a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0f8971a2-b9fd-51a0-b806-c7644734e8f2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0016-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following limits the ability of commercial banks to create credit?","options":["A steady inflow of fresh cash deposits into the banks","A low public preference for holding cash","A shortage of creditworthy borrowers","A low cash reserve ratio"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Banks can only create credit by making loans, so if there are few borrowers who are able and willing to repay, lending and therefore credit creation are held back. Option D actually helps credit creation, because banks keep less in reserve and can lend more. Options B and A also support credit creation, since more cash stays in the banks to be lent out again."}},{"itemId":"d7a1bdcc-90ae-58a8-8568-5c318a6c4cbd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"85072c63-a93c-5f29-bbd7-cd7a5a32c629","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a developmental, rather than a traditional, function of a central bank in a developing economy?","options":["Providing special financing schemes for agriculture and industry","Issuing the national currency","Acting as lender of last resort to banks","Acting as banker to the government"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"In developing countries central banks often go beyond their classic duties and promote growth directly, for example by running intervention funds and credit schemes for agriculture, industry and small businesses. Options B, D and C are traditional central banking functions that central banks perform in both developed and developing economies."}},{"itemId":"439c4d02-4ecf-5ac3-9be6-9f5fcb7318d4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"43245a6a-9265-50ca-87e7-0f2cbda17f4f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following has been one of the CBN's development finance initiatives?","options":["The Anchor Borrowers' Programme","Open market operations","The cash reserve requirement","The Monetary Policy Rate"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Anchor Borrowers' Programme was a CBN intervention that provided loans to smallholder farmers and linked them with processors, which makes it a development finance initiative. Options B, D and C are monetary policy instruments used to influence liquidity, interest rates and credit across the economy, not schemes aimed at financing particular sectors."}},{"itemId":"f6c55635-def9-5284-aa8c-73dd3502c15d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4bdd1606-8d7d-59a4-8725-5381fb11f473","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The achievements of the CBN include all of the following EXCEPT","options":["paying insured depositors when a bank fails","issuing and managing the national currency","regulating and supervising banks","developing the national payment system"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Paying insured depositors of a failed bank is the work of the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation, not the CBN. Issuing and managing the naira (option B), regulating and supervising banks (option C) and modernising the payment system (option D) are all achievements credited to the CBN, along with implementing monetary policy and supporting development programmes."}},{"itemId":"76cbd326-2c7d-5fd7-b943-0ff45cf3e34d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e2bc871d-aae0-551a-897c-ed75f4166a29","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a principle (canon) of central banking?","options":["Responsibility for the stability of the financial system","Acting as lender of last resort to the banking system","Independence of the central bank in conducting monetary policy","Competing with commercial banks for public deposits in order to maximise profit"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A central bank is meant to serve the public interest and lead the banking system, so it should not compete with commercial banks for ordinary customers or be driven by profit. Independence in monetary policy (option C), acting as lender of last resort (option B) and responsibility for financial stability (option A) are all accepted principles of sound central banking."}},{"itemId":"b84aab51-0f7c-5e63-a4ab-559877b99094","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e0858bac-4777-5bdd-92e5-b0b88c17742c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN Act 2007, the Board of Directors of the Central Bank of Nigeria is chaired by the","options":["Minister of Finance","Senate President","President of Nigeria","Governor of the CBN"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN Act 2007 makes the Governor the chairman of the Board of Directors, which also includes the Deputy Governors and other directors. Option A is wrong because the Minister of Finance does not head the Bank's board, and options C and B are wrong because the President and the Senate take part in appointing the Governor but do not chair the board."}},{"itemId":"3fbd9c26-76a7-5002-a11d-f319c860c77f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"476a83c8-1af8-5b1a-8c20-4d11fb820c4d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0021-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN Act 2007, the Governor of the CBN is appointed by","options":["the Board of Directors of the CBN from among its members","the President, subject to confirmation by the Senate","the Bankers' Committee","the Minister of Finance, without reference to the National Assembly"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Governor, like the Deputy Governors, is appointed by the President of Nigeria and must be confirmed by the Senate before taking office. Option D is wrong because the Minister of Finance does not appoint the Governor, and option A is wrong because the Board is chaired by the Governor rather than choosing the Governor. Option C is a forum of bankers with no power to appoint the head of the CBN."}},{"itemId":"004c0f1b-40c3-519c-993c-ac398863534b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b7d1b976-e754-5b10-b0a4-4349a59b1e45","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0021-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN Act 2007, which body within the CBN is responsible for formulating monetary and credit policy?","options":["The Federal Executive Council","The Monetary Policy Committee","The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","The Bankers' Committee"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Monetary Policy Committee, chaired by the Governor, is the body within the CBN that formulates monetary and credit policy and decides on the Monetary Policy Rate. Option A is the federal cabinet, which handles government policy generally, not CBN monetary policy. Option D is a forum of bankers, and option C is the deposit insurer, neither of which sets monetary policy."}},{"itemId":"2a434abb-cff6-5c14-b471-9a3c51970d40","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8ccac31f-2532-5052-b4ad-08f628c45d19","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Commercial banking in Nigeria began during the colonial era with the establishment of","options":["foreign-owned (expatriate) banks set up mainly to serve colonial trade","microfinance banks serving rural communities","the Central Bank of Nigeria","indigenous banks founded by Nigerian nationalists"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The first banks in Nigeria were expatriate institutions, such as the forerunner of today's First Bank, which were set up mainly to finance trade between Nigeria and Britain. Option D is wrong because indigenous banks came later, partly as a reaction to the discrimination Nigerians faced in foreign banks. Option C was not established until 1958, and option B is a much more recent category of bank."}},{"itemId":"bb301dda-db38-5f88-b13c-e6b21065afe1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c89b36d-d9da-5f91-bff0-3fbda23ad0ca","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0022-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Bank of British West Africa, one of the earliest banks in Nigeria, eventually became","options":["First Bank of Nigeria","United Bank for Africa","Wema Bank","Union Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Bank of British West Africa went through several name changes, including Standard Bank of Nigeria, before becoming First Bank of Nigeria. Option D grew out of Barclays Bank DCO, and option B grew out of the British and French Bank for Commerce and Industry. Option C grew out of the indigenous Agbonmagbe Bank, not a colonial bank."}},{"itemId":"31b2e1be-17f0-59ed-b5e1-d3b94121737a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0216685-8399-59f7-a662-05e18eb9ad05","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0022-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the banking consolidation programme announced by the CBN in July 2004, the minimum capital base of Nigerian banks was raised to","options":["N25 billion","N10 billion","N2 billion","N500 billion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"In July 2004 the CBN raised the minimum capital base of banks from N2 billion to N25 billion, with a deadline at the end of 2005, and the number of banks fell from 89 to 25 through mergers and acquisitions. Option C was the old requirement in force before the reform. Options B and D do not match the 2004 programme."}},{"itemId":"facc4ae4-22d7-5c50-95b0-64b02a62556c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"98150f0e-018a-5835-9e80-2c3b53b66e68","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT part of the present-day Nigerian banking structure?","options":["Deposit money (commercial) banks","The West African Currency Board","Microfinance banks","Merchant banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The West African Currency Board was a colonial currency authority whose role in Nigeria passed to the CBN in 1959, so it is not part of today's banking structure. Deposit money banks (option A), merchant banks (option D) and microfinance banks (option C) all operate in Nigeria today under the regulation of the CBN, alongside development finance institutions and non-interest banks."}},{"itemId":"061d640e-c7c2-5f4b-83c6-79b55a6f4f16","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"716e95e6-b7f0-5b62-9008-70713bb11fa3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0023-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Microfinance banks in Nigeria are mainly intended to","options":["underwrite share issues for large corporations","provide financial services to low-income individuals and micro and small enterprises","provide long-term finance for large industrial projects","act as lender of last resort to other banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Microfinance banks exist to promote financial inclusion by offering small loans, savings and other services to poor households and micro and small businesses that conventional banks often neglect. Option C describes development finance institutions such as the Bank of Industry. Option D is a central bank function, and option A is the work of investment or merchant banks."}},{"itemId":"e3da788d-973f-582a-8393-f25fc1615de6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8d476371-3d17-5a8b-9cb2-3d4e33cfe6f7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a reason for the collapse of many early indigenous banks in Nigeria?","options":["Poor management practices","Excessive regulation and strict supervision by the Central Bank of Nigeria","Lack of a proper regulatory framework","Inadequate capital"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Most early indigenous banks failed in the 1940s and early 1950s, when there was little or no banking regulation and the CBN did not yet exist, so excessive CBN supervision cannot have caused their collapse. Inadequate capital (option D), poor management (option A) and the absence of proper regulation (option C) are the main reasons usually given for those failures."}},{"itemId":"a79a2ade-ca80-5289-90d2-299e0b0604b6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8e9efd2c-ee7d-5f35-ba1e-1be3af2c7617","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0024-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The wave of indigenous bank failures in Nigeria in the late 1940s and early 1950s led the colonial government to","options":["nationalise all foreign-owned banks","create the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation to compensate depositors","introduce banking legislation that required licensing and minimum capital for banks","ban Nigerians from owning banks"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Following an enquiry into the failures, the colonial government passed Nigeria's first banking law, which introduced licensing and minimum paid-up capital requirements for banks. Option B is wrong because the NDIC was set up decades later, long after independence. Options A and D did not happen; the response was regulation, not nationalisation or a ban on indigenous ownership."}},{"itemId":"aacc5dbe-3211-5c54-b0dc-46c53f3c6bf4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4acc2dc4-2c8b-5ce5-bfa3-b678c089956f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a lending banker carries out a status enquiry into a prospective borrower's background and integrity, which of the 5 Cs of lending is being assessed?","options":["Collateral","Conditions","Capital","Character"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Character concerns the borrower's honesty, reputation and willingness to repay, and banks check it through background or status enquiries before granting a loan. Option C looks at how much of the borrower's own money is committed to the business, option A is the security the bank can fall back on if the loan is not repaid, and option B refers to the state of the economy and other external circumstances."}},{"itemId":"64dabb61-82d5-535e-ba97-d5f377826a41","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df250303-5cc6-5292-8321-49a6f296d07f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0025-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In bank lending, collateral (security) mainly serves to","options":["replace the need to assess the borrower's ability to repay","give the bank something to fall back on if the borrower fails to repay","prove that the borrower has the legal capacity to contract","fix the interest rate on the loan"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Collateral is a safeguard: if the borrower defaults, the bank can realise the security to recover what it is owed. Option A is a common misconception, because sound lending still depends mainly on the borrower's ability and willingness to repay, with security only as a fallback. Option C relates to capacity, not security, and option D is wrong because security does not by itself determine the interest rate."}},{"itemId":"45844d9b-e989-5e5d-a827-7b4283304cb1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e7c65a75-31c8-5eb0-998d-e7836997cbca","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Money is best defined as","options":["any commodity that has intrinsic value","any financial asset that earns interest","anything that is generally acceptable as a means of payment for goods and services and in settlement of debts","only the currency and coins issued by the central bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"What makes something money is general acceptability: if people widely accept it in exchange for goods and services and to settle debts, it functions as money, whether it is cowries, coins, paper currency or bank deposits. Option D is too narrow, because bank deposits are also money. Option A describes commodity money only, and option B is wrong because many interest-earning assets, such as treasury bills, are near money rather than money."}},{"itemId":"16bfffd9-7d20-57f3-8cb8-5f2dfb76ab36","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5889074f-49c7-59fb-81e2-ca4c6a7091fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which pair is usually classified as the primary functions of money?","options":["Medium of exchange and store of value","Store of value and standard of deferred payment","Standard of deferred payment and measure of value","Medium of exchange and measure of value"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Medium of exchange and measure of value (unit of account) are called primary functions because they are the basic jobs money must perform in any economy. Option B lists the secondary functions, which build on the primary ones. Options A and C each mix one primary function with one secondary function, so neither is the correct pair."}},{"itemId":"419becd8-511d-53dc-a1fa-5e5a875a0739","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd3657c4-edf4-5213-b3f2-598280f441fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The direct exchange of goods and services for other goods and services without the use of money is called","options":["credit sale","cheque clearing","hire purchase","barter"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Barter is the oldest form of trade, in which goods are swapped directly for other goods, such as yams for fish, with no money involved. Options C and A are ways of buying goods with money paid later or in instalments. Option B is the process by which banks settle cheques drawn on one another."}},{"itemId":"f51a115b-90fc-5370-8275-679a312d1559","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5ca208a-62af-5cb5-8cb9-5419d35d81c7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Money supply refers to","options":["the total stock of money held by the public in an economy at a particular point in time","the amount of money the government spends in a year","the total amount of currency printed by the central bank since it was established","the total amount of money that households wish to hold"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money supply is a stock measured at a point in time, made up of currency in the hands of the public and bank deposits. Option C is wrong because much of the currency ever printed has been withdrawn or destroyed, and currency is only one part of the money supply. Option B describes government expenditure, which is a flow over a period, and option D describes the demand for money rather than its supply."}},{"itemId":"7ee733cf-6978-5b22-8e35-65959232f5a6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a951e81f-baf3-576c-a6c7-2031e4b0fa89","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Narrow money (M1) consists of","options":["demand deposits plus treasury bills held by banks","currency held by the public plus demand deposits with commercial banks","cash held in bank vaults plus banks' reserves with the central bank","currency held by the public plus savings and time deposits"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Narrow money covers the most liquid forms of money that are used directly for payments: currency in the hands of the public and demand deposits that can be spent by cheque or transfer. Option D wrongly includes savings and time deposits, which are added only in broad money. Option A includes treasury bills, which are near money, and option C lists bank reserves, which are not money held by the public."}},{"itemId":"1c739ff1-46b8-5bcb-af98-1771c88a8e0a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"efe2b046-2247-5af6-9b06-43b9f7bc09bb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In an economy, currency held by the public is N600 billion, demand deposits are N1,400 billion and savings and time deposits are N1,100 billion. What is the narrow money supply (M1)?","options":["N3,100 billion","N1,700 billion","N2,000 billion","N1,400 billion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"M1 is currency with the public plus demand deposits, so N600 billion + N1,400 billion = N2,000 billion. Option A also adds savings and time deposits, which gives broad money (M2), not M1. Option D counts demand deposits alone, and option B wrongly adds currency to savings and time deposits."}},{"itemId":"a80e2b62-673a-51e5-b52d-4d9a5a548de8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5b3d35f-a641-59df-a5fe-0d130f73e7aa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A central bank is best defined as","options":["the commercial bank with the largest branch network in a country","a privately owned bank that accepts deposits from and lends to the general public","the apex monetary authority of a country, with the sole right to issue currency and responsibility for regulating the banking system","a development bank that provides long-term loans to industry"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A central bank sits at the top of the financial system: it has the monopoly of currency issue, controls money and credit, and supervises other banks, as the CBN does in Nigeria. Option A describes a large commercial bank, which is still just one of the banks the central bank regulates. Option D is a development finance institution, and option B describes ordinary commercial banking, which a central bank does not do."}},{"itemId":"00fa578c-f4f7-55d4-9607-2b2c6a29c888","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb79186c-92a1-5681-9c14-0ad0fc715132","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which institution is the apex monetary authority in Nigeria?","options":["The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","The Central Bank of Nigeria","The Federal Ministry of Finance","The Securities and Exchange Commission"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Central Bank of Nigeria is the country's central bank and apex monetary authority, responsible for issuing the naira, conducting monetary policy and regulating banks. Option A insures deposits and helps resolve failed banks, and option D regulates the capital market. Option C manages the government's fiscal affairs, while monetary policy is the responsibility of the CBN."}},{"itemId":"58f0d065-e73f-5d30-a3c7-1002b7f98ef8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0147e538-51e8-5b9a-8b90-87cea4ec4c6e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigeria, the cash reserve ratio (CRR) is","options":["the ratio of a bank's capital to its risk-weighted assets","the minimum percentage of a bank's deposit liabilities that it must hold in liquid assets such as cash and treasury bills","the minimum percentage of a bank's deposits that it must keep as reserves with the Central Bank of Nigeria","the interest rate at which the CBN lends to banks overnight"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The cash reserve ratio requires each bank to keep a set percentage of its deposits with the CBN, which reduces the funds available for lending and is used as a monetary policy tool. Option B is the liquidity ratio, which covers a wider range of liquid assets held by the bank. Option D describes the standing lending facility rate, and option A is the capital adequacy ratio."}},{"itemId":"7ef1efa2-43b3-5328-8aac-7dd96ead66da","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25eeedbe-77c7-563c-b9d3-00e92dcb4b42","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the Central Bank of Nigeria?","options":["Acting as banker to other banks","Issuing legal tender currency","Acting as banker to the government","Accepting deposits from and granting loans to members of the public"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN does not deal directly with individual members of the public; ordinary deposit-taking and lending are left to commercial and other banks. Issuing legal tender (option B), acting as banker to banks (option A) and acting as banker to the government (option C) are core functions of the CBN, together with managing external reserves and conducting monetary policy."}},{"itemId":"a0e6aa90-a4e3-5dc8-9a08-94c7f41e7992","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"29cdf4cd-53e4-5d7e-9b2d-6e2c6fcd5196","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0035-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the CBN provides emergency funds to a solvent bank facing a temporary liquidity shortage, it is acting as","options":["lender of last resort","bank of issue","manager of external reserves","banker to the government"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"As lender of last resort, the central bank stands ready to lend to sound banks that run short of cash, preventing a temporary problem from becoming a wider banking crisis. Option B refers to issuing currency, option D refers to managing government accounts and borrowing, and option C refers to managing the country's foreign exchange holdings."}},{"itemId":"37d25d14-8139-5329-8e5b-04d2670c2bfa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"055a20c8-6c2e-5d97-9b1a-d20ccdc4edde","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Banking, in its basic sense, is the business of","options":["issuing currency and regulating the money supply","buying and selling shares on the stock exchange for clients","insuring lives and property against loss","accepting deposits from the public and lending or investing those funds"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"At its core, banking means taking deposits from people and organisations and using those funds to make loans and investments, earning a margin between the interest charged and the interest paid. Option A describes central banking, option C describes insurance, and option B describes stockbroking."}},{"itemId":"2c667681-7f99-586c-8af6-8cd62fe65613","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bb32ebf0-fd74-5736-9099-9a9a0b806570","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a barter economy, 'double coincidence of wants' means that","options":["each party to an exchange must have what the other wants and want what the other has at the same time","two people must want the same good at the same time","both parties must agree on a price expressed in money","goods must always be exchanged in equal quantities"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Double coincidence of wants means both sides of a barter deal must match: you must want what I have and I must want what you have, at the same time. Option B describes two people wanting the same good, which is not the matching problem barter faces. Option D is wrong because barter rates can be unequal, and option C is wrong because barter involves no money."}},{"itemId":"c64494c9-367e-5acc-8c6c-60f02888fe48","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"858df491-caed-5b2d-818b-08d25f8639ca","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0037-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A yam farmer wants shoes, but the only shoemaker nearby wants fish, not yams. Under barter, the trade cannot take place mainly because of","options":["the absence of a standard of deferred payment","the difficulty of storing value","the indivisibility of goods","the lack of a double coincidence of wants"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The shoemaker has what the farmer wants, but the farmer does not have what the shoemaker wants, so there is no double coincidence of wants and no direct exchange can happen. Option C would apply if the goods could not be split into the right quantities, and option B concerns goods losing value over time. Option A concerns payments to be made in the future, which is not the issue here."}},{"itemId":"ef95fb71-14a9-50ab-8e4b-8c21cc754a67","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b247c973-006e-58b3-bd97-2760ff780c44","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Fiat money is best described as","options":["paper money that can be converted into gold on demand","money created only by commercial banks in the form of deposits","money whose value as money equals the value of the metal it contains","money that is accepted because the government has declared it legal tender, though it has no intrinsic value and is not backed by a commodity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Fiat money gets its value from government decree and public confidence rather than from what it is made of; the naira today is an example. Option C describes full-bodied commodity money such as gold coins, and option A describes representative or convertible paper money. Option B describes bank deposit money, which is created by banks rather than declared legal tender by the government."}},{"itemId":"16193f23-3301-5234-8ddf-79a652678da6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b1894eb4-4f7d-54de-8f62-99ffe2db1595","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Legal tender money is also described as fiat money because","options":["it is issued by commercial banks rather than the government","it must be accepted in payment of debts by order of the government","it is fully backed by gold held by the central bank","it is accepted only on the basis of personal trust between the parties"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Fiat means 'let it be done', and legal tender is money that the law, on the government's order, says must be accepted in settlement of debts. Option C is wrong because modern legal tender is not backed by gold. Option D describes money accepted voluntarily on trust, which people may refuse, and option A is wrong because legal tender currency is issued by the central monetary authority, not commercial banks."}},{"itemId":"5815dc85-f46e-5be0-9482-5234958057a8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c6398f6a-9ee9-5bfa-a158-41ba27517b7b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Fiduciary money derives its acceptability mainly from","options":["trust or confidence in the issuer rather than from any intrinsic value","its convertibility into gold on demand","the value of the metal it contains","the scarcity of the commodity from which it is made"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The word fiduciary comes from the Latin for trust: fiduciary money is accepted because people have confidence that the issuer will honour it, not because the material it is made of is valuable. Option C describes full-bodied commodity money, and option B describes representative or convertible money backed by gold. Option D also points to commodity value, which fiduciary money does not rely on."}},{"itemId":"f2f45b0a-d90a-5472-972f-15eae8d4465b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a6072b4-e117-5997-ba2a-0d2c6dc85cce","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Money that the law of a country declares must be accepted in settlement of debts, such as the naira currency issued by the Central Bank of Nigeria, is called:","options":["Commodity money","Legal tender money","Near money","Customary money"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Legal tender is money that the law says must be accepted in settlement of debts, so a creditor cannot lawfully refuse it; in Nigeria the naira currency issued by the CBN has this status. Option D is the opposite case: money accepted by custom or habit without legal backing. Option C covers assets like time deposits and Treasury bills that can be turned into money but are not themselves legal tender, while option A is money valued for the material it is made of."}},{"itemId":"9d2da9e6-8c29-5e67-b2dd-cff78f292c98","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1fafde7-1c52-5dda-86db-882a510d0d36","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A trader borrows N500,000 today and agrees to repay N550,000 in two years' time. Which function of money makes it possible to contract this debt now and settle it in money later?","options":["Transfer of value","Store of value","Standard of deferred payment","Medium of exchange"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money acts as a standard of deferred payment when debts are agreed today and repaid in money later, as with loans, bonds and hire purchase; it is one of the secondary functions of money, together with store of value and transfer of value. Option B is about holding wealth in money to spend in the future, not about settling a debt. Option D describes money being used to buy and sell goods on the spot, and option A refers to moving purchasing power from one person or place to another."}},{"itemId":"317d5cfe-80d1-5cb3-9c80-a70bd4c07fa8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"499d81cb-866b-5602-a8f6-78e95ec86d99","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The two essential (principal) functions of a commercial bank are:","options":["Issuing currency and acting as banker to the government","Accepting deposits from the public and granting loans and advances","Acting as executor or trustee and keeping customers' valuables in safe custody","Buying and selling shares on the stock exchange and underwriting new issues"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Accepting deposits and lending them out as loans, overdrafts and discounted bills are the core of banking; through these two activities banks earn interest income and create credit. Option A lists functions of the central bank, not a commercial bank. Option C lists ancillary services a bank may offer, but they are extras rather than its essential business, and option D describes the work of stockbrokers and issuing houses in the capital market."}},{"itemId":"9ed43630-e05d-5e8c-b6b4-bb333b74e30b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"71b01e00-ea42-5140-85f4-e68d79a06b49","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Deposits held on current account, which the bank must pay whenever the customer asks, for example by cheque and without prior notice, are called:","options":["Savings deposits","Special deposits","Demand deposits","Time deposits"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Demand deposits, also called sight deposits, are current account balances that the bank must pay out on demand, usually through cheques or transfers, which is why they are counted as money in M1. Option D refers to deposits locked in for an agreed period, and option A refers to interest-earning savings balances whose withdrawals are usually limited. Option B is a monetary policy tool under which banks are made to lodge extra funds with the central bank, not a type of customer deposit."}},{"itemId":"57e99715-c347-5ce8-82b2-37055261e61b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bd7b945e-4c5b-5fae-8dfc-ee36e283f5d2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three main types of deposit accounts that commercial banks open for their customers?","options":["Loan account","Current account","Fixed (time) deposit account","Savings account"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Banks mainly accept deposits on current account, savings account and fixed or time deposit account, which differ in how easily money can be withdrawn and how much interest is paid. A loan account is the opposite of a deposit account: it records money the bank has lent, so the customer owes the bank. Options B, D and C are all genuine deposit accounts, so none of them is the exception."}},{"itemId":"a6f1705e-9257-5c77-afa6-c26a8160ffa3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5577c743-a7fa-513b-9740-7347fb19ed71","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0045-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Among the three main types of bank deposit, which one normally earns the depositor the highest rate of interest?","options":["All three earn the same rate of interest","Current account deposit","Savings deposit","Fixed (time) deposit"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A fixed or time deposit earns the highest interest because the customer agrees to leave the money with the bank for a set period, so the bank can lend it out with confidence. Option C earns interest too, but usually at a lower rate because withdrawals are allowed, while option B earns little or nothing since it is meant for frequent withdrawals. Option A is wrong because banks pay more for funds they can keep longer."}},{"itemId":"427a86c5-5b82-5e45-a6c4-2de72114915d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"33f80e56-e31b-5f12-9e91-5770f99d966f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Deposits that are repayable only after the expiry of an agreed fixed period are called:","options":["Savings deposits","Call deposits","Demand deposits","Time deposits"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Time deposits, also called fixed or term deposits, are placed with a bank for an agreed period such as six months or one year and repaid when that period ends; withdrawing early usually means losing some or all of the interest. Option C is the opposite, since demand deposits are payable whenever the customer asks. Option A allows withdrawals at any time, though usually limited in number, and option B has no fixed maturity and can be ended by either party giving notice."}},{"itemId":"2a9177f6-3db4-5744-b00f-de64c166add6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9d07c606-a2b4-5b9b-adb7-2ee91b0b5e60","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an agency function performed by a commercial bank for its customers?","options":["Keeping customers' valuables in safe custody","Collecting dividends and interest on the customer's behalf","Granting overdrafts to current account holders","Accepting deposits on savings account"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"In its agency functions the bank acts as its customer's agent, doing things on the customer's behalf such as collecting dividends, interest and cheques, paying bills and insurance premiums, buying and selling securities and transferring funds. Options D and C are the bank's principal functions of accepting deposits and lending. Option A is a separate safe-keeping service, where the bank holds the customer's property rather than acting for the customer."}},{"itemId":"f0aa2c69-cc0f-5dce-adfc-3028236299ad","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f9fba0c-dd54-5baa-ab32-7e7f57bd0939","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which type of deposit account traditionally pays the depositor little or no interest because it is designed for frequent withdrawals by cheque?","options":["Savings account","Current account","Fixed deposit account","Call deposit account"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"A current account exists for convenience: the holder can pay in and withdraw as often as needed, mainly by cheque or transfer, so banks have traditionally paid little or no interest on it, though interest may be paid on balances above a set minimum. Options A and C are interest-bearing accounts, and the fixed deposit pays the most because the money is tied up for a set period. Option D is also an interest-earning deposit, one that can be withdrawn at short notice."}},{"itemId":"4ce091d4-4e68-58f4-bda8-19d689e23527","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"61f53e73-d001-58eb-b73d-f622418fa5b5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An arrangement under which a current account holder is allowed to withdraw more than the balance in the account, up to an agreed limit, is called:","options":["A term loan","A standing order","An overdraft","Bills discounting"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"An overdraft lets a current account customer draw beyond the credit balance up to an agreed limit, and interest is charged only on the amount actually overdrawn; businesses often use it to smooth out working capital needs. Option A is a fixed sum lent for a set period and repaid by instalments, while option D means the bank buys a bill before it matures for less than its face value. Option B is simply an instruction to the bank to make regular payments from the account."}},{"itemId":"864e86df-4662-58c9-95d3-b95f6cf68268","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ac33fd19-9434-5f04-85fa-c0a2cb786a94","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An institution whose main business is accepting deposits from the public and using those funds to give loans and advances is a:","options":["Stock exchange","Bank","Pension fund administrator","Insurance company"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A bank is defined by the twin activities of taking deposits and lending them out, which makes it a financial intermediary between savers and borrowers. Option D collects premiums in return for covering risks, not deposits. Option A is a market where shares and bonds are traded, and option C invests contributors' pension savings rather than accepting deposits and granting loans."}},{"itemId":"890a9594-8256-5c33-aa53-5b06fb7cda9e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"79b66351-34e7-5f13-ada1-0b37b6ddade6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A commercial bank accepts deposits and lends at a margin in order to earn profit. Its main source of income is therefore:","options":["Premiums paid by depositors for insuring their deposits","Interest earned on the cash reserves it must keep with the CBN","The issue of new currency and coins","The difference between the interest it charges on loans and the interest it pays on deposits"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A commercial bank is a profit-seeking firm that takes in funds from depositors at a lower rate and lends them at a higher rate, and this interest margin or spread is its main income. Option C is wrong because only the central bank issues currency. Option A is wrong because deposit insurance premiums are paid by the banks to the deposit insurer, not received by them, and option B is wrong because cash reserves kept with the CBN earn little or no interest."}},{"itemId":"d90acad5-1438-5440-9a85-78ab26681de2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ebee451b-fef7-5795-9aa4-e32dd3f5f804","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigeria, the minimum percentage of its deposit liabilities that a deposit money bank must hold in specified liquid assets, such as cash, balances with the CBN and Treasury bills, is known as the:","options":["Liquidity ratio","Loan-to-deposit ratio","Capital adequacy ratio","Cash reserve ratio"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The liquidity ratio requires a bank to keep a minimum share of its deposits in assets that can quickly be turned into cash, such as cash in hand, balances with the CBN and Treasury bills, so that it can meet depositors' withdrawals. Option D is narrower: it is the part of deposits that must be kept as cash reserves with the CBN itself. Option C relates the bank's capital to its risk-weighted assets, and option B compares the bank's loans with its deposits."}},{"itemId":"1a7ef2ae-9579-5380-8d59-4c33844b0752","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d3759b78-fbef-571b-862c-364ad1473d91","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The percentage of its deposits that a bank is legally required to keep as cash reserves with the Central Bank of Nigeria is called the:","options":["Monetary policy rate","Cash reserve ratio","Capital adequacy ratio","Liquidity ratio"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"The cash reserve ratio, also called the legal or required reserve ratio, is the share of deposits that banks must hold as cash with the central bank, and these reserves cannot be lent out. Option D is broader, because it counts a range of liquid assets such as Treasury bills as well as cash. Option A is an interest rate that signals the CBN's policy stance, and option C measures a bank's capital against its risk-weighted assets."}},{"itemId":"62f650d0-4704-589a-834f-8bc728f19ab5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2d39acbc-74c8-5e0e-bc55-6b6693f5103a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0053-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the CBN raises the cash reserve ratio, the most likely effect on deposit money banks is that:","options":["Their capacity to grant loans and create credit rises","Their capacity to grant loans and create credit falls","Interest rates on their loans automatically fall","The amount of money in circulation automatically increases"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A higher cash reserve ratio means banks must lock up a larger share of each deposit with the CBN, leaving less to lend; it also lowers the money multiplier, which is 1 divided by the reserve ratio. Option A describes the effect of cutting the ratio, not raising it. Option D is wrong because tighter reserves reduce, not increase, the money in circulation, and option C is unlikely because scarcer loanable funds tend to push lending rates up, not down."}},{"itemId":"338224d3-6062-5671-9d1a-3fa9ab1f6c34","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0709b00-9d18-5583-b0d8-46d10f94f6c0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The buying and selling of government securities by the central bank in order to influence bank reserves and the money supply is known as:","options":["Selective credit control","Moral suasion","Open market operations","Special deposits"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Open market operations (OMO) involve the CBN buying or selling securities such as Treasury bills in the market; a sale drains reserves from banks and a purchase adds to them. Option B is persuasion of banks by the central bank without any market transaction. Option A directs credit to or away from particular sectors, and option D requires banks to lodge extra funds with the central bank rather than trading securities with them."}},{"itemId":"90125131-1c02-594c-938d-44affd3f51e4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"418bf042-261b-5051-82ee-128a7e6312a2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0054-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the CBN sells Treasury bills to banks and the public through open market operations, the likely effect is to:","options":["Increase the amount of currency issued by the CBN","Increase bank reserves and the money supply","Reduce bank reserves and the money supply","Leave the money supply unchanged because only paper changes hands"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Buyers pay the CBN for the securities out of their bank balances, so money is withdrawn from the banking system and banks have fewer reserves to lend; selling securities is therefore a tightening move. Option B is what happens when the CBN buys securities and pays money into the system. Option D ignores the fact that the money paid for the securities leaves circulation, and option A confuses OMO with currency issue."}},{"itemId":"7d69de21-422b-5df7-bbd9-94b04bcb1559","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a862a068-71ff-5ebf-9af4-fe330caa176c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In bank lending, the difference between the market value of the security offered by a borrower and the amount of the loan granted against it is called the:","options":["Equity of redemption","Interest spread","Guarantee","Margin"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The margin is the cushion a banker keeps by lending less than the security is worth, so that a fall in its value or the cost of selling it will not leave the bank exposed. Option A is a mortgagor's right to recover the mortgaged property once the debt is repaid. Option B is the gap between lending and deposit interest rates, and option C is a promise by a third party to pay if the borrower defaults, not a difference in values."}},{"itemId":"de69e523-0333-5e2e-a985-90457460881f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c0c5a53e-6e54-506f-b66c-4e70f10d561a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The apex institution that controls and regulates the banking and monetary system of a country and has the sole authority to issue its currency is the:","options":["Federal Ministry of Finance","Central bank","Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A central bank sits at the top of the banking system: it issues the currency, acts as banker to the government and to other banks, and conducts monetary policy, which in Nigeria is the role of the CBN. Option A manages government finances and fiscal matters but does not issue currency. Option D insures deposits and helps supervise banks, and option C regulates the capital market."}},{"itemId":"2dfb89a1-f783-5ff2-a62c-19fb6312f065","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e9e043b5-335f-5526-b030-ad0619865b43","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a qualitative (selective) instrument of monetary policy?","options":["Reserve requirements","Moral suasion","Open market operations","Discount (bank) rate"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Qualitative instruments aim at the direction or use of credit rather than its total volume, and moral suasion, the central bank's use of persuasion and appeals to banks, is a classic example along with selective credit control. Options C, A and D are quantitative instruments, because they work by changing the overall volume and cost of credit and bank reserves."}},{"itemId":"c07ed519-bffe-50fe-b333-09e7ea4c2f51","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"24fe2ddf-105a-5224-a163-9a1c99347afb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0057-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before the Structural Adjustment Programme of 1986, the CBN issued guidelines directing banks to lend stated proportions of their credit to agriculture and manufacturing at specified interest rates. This tool is known as:","options":["Open market operations","Special deposits","Moral suasion","Selective credit control"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Selective credit control uses administrative directives to channel credit into chosen sectors, in set amounts and at set rates, and it was widely used in Nigeria's regulated era before market-based tools took over. Option C also tries to influence lending, but only through persuasion and appeals, not binding guidelines. Options A and B are quantitative tools that change the total amount of bank reserves rather than steering credit to particular sectors."}},{"itemId":"b708e24f-4d52-5f6f-89c8-adaba8ae2343","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ce4b88b0-d1c5-56b0-a85d-dc60b37d4ccc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which group consists ONLY of quantitative instruments of monetary policy?","options":["Moral suasion, selective credit control and open market operations","Moral suasion and selective credit control","Open market operations, reserve requirements and the discount (bank) rate","Reserve requirements, moral suasion and the discount (bank) rate"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Quantitative instruments control the total volume and cost of money and credit: open market operations change bank reserves, reserve requirements fix how much of each deposit banks must hold back, and the bank rate sets the cost of borrowing from the central bank. Options A and D each slip in moral suasion, and option B is made up entirely of qualitative tools, which work through persuasion or by directing credit to particular sectors rather than changing its total amount."}},{"itemId":"c86c0c38-acbb-5cc6-9351-4cfb458f9024","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"626a2dfc-4585-5bbc-a0ee-d2f4f3056d84","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Federal Government of Nigeria raises short-term funds, for periods of up to one year, mainly by issuing which instrument through the CBN?","options":["Commercial papers","Treasury bills","Bankers' acceptances","FGN bonds"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury bills are short-term government IOUs issued at a discount for terms such as 91, 182 or 364 days, and the CBN sells them on the government's behalf to banks and other investors. Option D is a long-term government debt instrument. Options A and C are also money market instruments, but commercial papers are issued by companies and bankers' acceptances are bills accepted by banks on behalf of their customers, not government borrowing."}},{"itemId":"3e84172e-1fae-5e19-ae4b-e7504f06c4bb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"587cde52-be93-5c74-bfd8-02b29134285c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0059-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Temporary advances made directly by the CBN to the Federal Government to cover short-term shortfalls in revenue are known as:","options":["Ways and Means advances","Standing Lending Facility","Bankers' acceptances","Special deposits"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"As banker to the government, the CBN can lend to it directly through Ways and Means advances when revenue falls temporarily short of spending, and the law limits how much may be advanced. Option B is the window through which the CBN lends overnight to banks, not to government. Option D refers to funds banks are made to lodge with the central bank, and option C is a bill accepted by a bank on behalf of its customer."}},{"itemId":"412682ac-b148-541f-a5e9-e6ee33094980","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5f2076b2-83ab-58d0-8005-b100b444d8e3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The rate at which the central bank lends to commercial banks by rediscounting their eligible bills is traditionally called the:","options":["Interbank rate","Savings deposit rate","Bank rate","Prime lending rate"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The bank rate, also called the discount or rediscount rate, is what the central bank charges when it provides funds to banks against eligible bills; raising it makes bank borrowing dearer and tends to push up lending rates generally. Option D is the rate a commercial bank charges its best customers. Option A is the rate banks charge one another for short-term loans, and option B is what banks pay depositors on savings accounts."}},{"itemId":"6c88065b-05ca-55da-9552-bb9b57f47e48","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b7558004-9d37-5ccd-9b59-065c61337e20","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0060-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigeria, the CBN's Minimum Rediscount Rate (MRR) was replaced as the main signalling rate of monetary policy by the:","options":["Prime Lending Rate","Treasury bill rate","Standing Deposit Facility rate","Monetary Policy Rate (MPR)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The MPR replaced the MRR as the CBN's anchor rate, and it is set by the Monetary Policy Committee to signal whether policy is being tightened or eased. Option C is the lower edge of the corridor around the MPR, the rate paid on banks' overnight deposits with the CBN. Option A is set by individual banks for their best customers, and option B is the yield on government Treasury bills, which is determined at auction."}},{"itemId":"ad7c18c5-d2fe-5e4e-bc18-36a87a8b3b9e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18e019fd-c603-5b02-aedb-9ac6806949a5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a feature of a central bank but NOT of a commercial bank?","options":["It accepts deposits from and grants loans to members of the public","Its main objective is to earn profit","It has the monopoly of issuing the country's currency","Many of them can operate in the same country"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Only the central bank may issue the national currency; in Nigeria this sole right belongs to the CBN. Options A and B describe commercial banks, which deal directly with the public and are run mainly for profit, while the central bank exists to regulate the monetary and banking system. Option D also fits commercial banks, since a country normally has only one central bank."}},{"itemId":"88b0a6c9-1bec-5407-964d-4f95c8fffc6d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"00d5218c-276b-51e9-8573-664be5ca6a54","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0061-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly contrasts the Central Bank of Nigeria with a commercial bank?","options":["Commercial banks issue the naira, while the CBN only accepts deposits","The CBN works under the control of commercial banks, which are the apex of the money market","The CBN deals directly with the general public, while commercial banks deal only with the government","The CBN controls the availability of credit in the economy, while commercial banks create credit"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Commercial banks create credit by lending out deposits, while the CBN regulates how much credit can be created through tools such as reserve requirements and open market operations. Option C reverses the roles, because commercial banks serve the public and the CBN deals mainly with government and banks. Option A is wrong because only the CBN issues the naira, and option B is wrong because the CBN, not the commercial banks, is the apex institution."}},{"itemId":"63625740-b607-55ed-a25d-48e4473a4795","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d356091b-5ac4-5c0f-aba6-b87cdad1c836","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A barter economy can be defined as:","options":["An economy where shoes are used as means of exchange","An economy where goods and services are exchanged for each other directly","An economy where money is not in use","Only (b) and (c)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"A barter economy is one in which goods and services are exchanged directly for one another, which happens because money is not in use, so B and C together give the definition. Option A is wrong because if shoes were generally used as a means of exchange they would be serving as commodity money, not barter. B on its own is true but incomplete, which is why D is the best answer."}},{"itemId":"b513cad1-5b7f-5ab4-931b-758050da9254","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d31c8add-b86c-5382-bb02-7ffbaadda3fc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A basic feature of a barter economy is:","options":["A producer produces almost all of his needs himself","The level of surplus is limited","A producer directly exchanges his products for others' products","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"In a barter economy each producer makes almost everything he needs himself, so production and surplus are limited, and whatever he lacks he gets by directly swapping his own goods for someone else's. Options A, B and C each describe one of these features, so picking any single one leaves out the others; D is the best answer."}},{"itemId":"dcf63540-a48c-55dc-9df9-3eb9f5c7df1b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25476266-4130-5783-9804-b3a9304fc8e2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement about relative prices in a barter economy is correct?","options":["A barter economy needs only one price for each good, just like a money economy","A barter economy needs fewer relative prices than a money economy","A barter economy needs a very large number of relative prices, because every good must be valued in terms of every other good","Relative prices do not exist in a barter economy because goods are swapped directly"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Without a common unit of account, each good must be priced against every other good, so with 1,000 goods there are almost 500,000 exchange ratios to know, and gathering this information wastes time and resources. Options A and B describe the opposite situation, since it is money that reduces the task to one price per good. Option D is wrong because every direct swap still involves an exchange ratio, which is a relative price."}},{"itemId":"9f443440-8c24-5604-9881-b6a26f602560","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a469d981-12b8-5837-a499-c881c89d99d2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0065","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"That a carpenter who has a table to sell and needs a pair of trousers must necessarily locate a tailor who not only has a pair of trousers to sell but also needs a table creates what is known as:","options":["Asymmetric problem","Asymptotic problem","Problem of double-coincidence of wants","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Barter works only if each trader wants exactly what the other has to offer at the same time, and this requirement is called the double coincidence of wants; the carpenter must find a tailor who both has trousers and wants a table. Options A and B are technical-sounding terms that have nothing to do with this exchange problem, and D is wrong because C names it correctly."}},{"itemId":"22ef3976-26c2-5e41-abb0-22618504252d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f7005723-3c18-5ce9-8954-b30076b449c6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0066","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Among the many problems or limitations of barter is the fact that:","options":["Tremendous time is wasted hunting for who needs what one has and has what one needs","Much cost is involved in trying to gather information as to who needs what","Divisibility of goods to the proportion needed can sometimes be impossible","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Barter wastes time searching for a trading partner with matching wants, costs a lot in gathering information about who needs what and at what rate, and runs into trouble when goods such as a cow cannot be divided into the small quantities needed. Each of options A, B and C is a genuine limitation, so D is the best answer."}},{"itemId":"904deea4-144c-58d1-a40c-54ba801ccb69","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a581e5d-7584-5371-a494-e5a839669f06","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0067","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Attempts to reduce the cost of transactions under barter create two fresh additional costs, these are:","options":["Opportunity cost or waiting cost, and storage cost","Storage cost and advertisement cost","Opportunity cost and marketing cost","None of the above"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"To avoid searching for a partner every time, a producer may stockpile his output until the surplus is large enough to trade, and this creates two new costs: the waiting or opportunity cost of satisfaction given up while he waits, and the cost of storing the goods. Options B and C each pair one of these with a cost, advertising or marketing, that is not what arises here. D is wrong because A names both costs correctly."}},{"itemId":"9d36ac2e-8df5-5127-8e9d-ce9961aaae4d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"41d7e314-5618-5979-b543-81d5b0279525","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0068","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A major advantage of a money-economy is:","options":["It gives the holder pleasure and a sense of well-being","It acts as a store of value as against the use of perishable commodities in a barter system","It is divisible to small units and makes purchase of small unit items possible","Only (b) and (c) above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money stores value far better than perishable barter goods, and it can be divided into small units so that even tiny purchases are possible; both are real economic advantages over barter. Option A is about personal feelings rather than an advantage of a money economy over barter. Because B and C are both correct, D is the best answer."}},{"itemId":"c649d42e-1389-5273-a474-4a1df72835c8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"923e225e-29dd-50b4-ac1e-ccfdd0fa849a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0069","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best explains why money came to replace barter?","options":["The shortcomings of barter, such as the need for a double coincidence of wants, made a generally acceptable medium of exchange necessary","Barter goods were excellent stores of value, so money was needed only as a unit of account","Barter made it too easy to find trading partners, so money was introduced to slow trade down","Specialisation and division of labour reduced the amount of exchange, so barter was no longer needed"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The costs of barter, including the search for someone with matching wants, the huge number of relative prices and the perishability and indivisibility of goods, made it necessary to have one object that everyone would readily accept in exchange. Option D gets it backwards, because specialisation increased the volume of exchange and made barter even more costly. Option B is wrong because many barter goods perish quickly, and option C contradicts the main weakness of barter, which is how hard it is to find a trading partner."}},{"itemId":"748cb6cd-a944-5e46-8e47-37d42ae2e9ce","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d37ce91a-4e66-598f-9d4a-ad5229b2c9cf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0070","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is not a function of money:","options":["Medium of exchange","Medium of settling disputes","Unit of Account","Store of Value"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money's functions are economic: it serves as a medium of exchange, a unit of account, a store of value and a standard of deferred payment. Settling disputes is a job for courts and arbitrators, so B is not a function of money. Options A, C and D are all genuine functions."}},{"itemId":"9122d4c1-544c-5974-85d3-f0ac32de5841","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a141f394-c1c9-531e-b902-698818b9e649","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0072","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Token money can be defined as:","options":["Money that takes the form of cowries","Money that has no commodity value at all","Money whose value derives entirely from being generally acceptable","Only (b) and (c) are correct"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Token money has no commodity value of its own; it is accepted only because people generally agree to take it in exchange, as with paper money and bank deposits. Option A is wrong because cowries are an example of commodity money. Since B and C together make up the definition, D is the best answer."}},{"itemId":"aae87e05-6931-5864-8d51-03d2ea3a41cc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fb4ded3-605a-5a9e-be20-e3a345227d6e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0074","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Near money or quasi-money are:","options":["Financial assets that can easily be converted into money","Financial assets like savings and time deposits","None of the above","Both (a) and (b) are correct"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Near money, or quasi-money, consists of financial assets that are not generally used for payment but can be turned into cash quickly with little or no loss; time and savings deposits are the main examples, alongside Treasury bills and bills of exchange. Option A gives the general description and option B gives examples, so both are correct and D is the best answer. C is wrong because both statements are true."}},{"itemId":"4d3ed069-c5cb-585d-bf58-562c3cad46b1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5ebd6882-97b9-5588-be4f-c3a77c8f4589","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0075","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The difference between demand deposits on the one hand and time and savings deposits on the other is that:","options":["Demand deposits can be withdrawn at sight (on demand), whereas time deposits are tied to a fixed period and savings deposits carry withdrawal conditions","Demand deposits are held by the elites in the society whereas time and savings deposits are held by the ordinary people","Demand deposits can only be held in the account of development banks while time and savings deposits can be held in the account of merchant banks","None of the above"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Demand deposits sit on current account and are payable at sight, by cheque or on request, which is why they count as money in M1. Time deposits can only be withdrawn when the agreed period ends and savings deposits come with withdrawal limits, so A is the best statement of the difference. Option B is at most a generalisation about who tends to use each account, C is wrong because these deposits are held with deposit money banks generally, and D is wrong because A states the difference."}},{"itemId":"f8389211-6cfe-59df-8adf-ffe6cca1391f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a945f177-1c46-5902-9b57-a4bb412b9378","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0076","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The monetary aggregate known as M1 is made up of:","options":["Cash and demand deposits and savings deposit","Cash and Savings deposits and time deposits","Cash and demand deposits alone","Cash and demand deposits and savings deposits and time deposit"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Narrow money, M1, is made up of currency in the hands of the public plus demand deposits, the two forms of money that can be used for payment immediately. Options A and D add savings or time deposits, which are quasi-money and are counted only in broad money, M2. Option B leaves out demand deposits altogether."}},{"itemId":"4b1c0f3a-e20d-581d-aa84-f1eb4fa54061","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0d392676-faf4-54a2-83e1-77d897800ada","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0077","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Time and Savings deposits as well as some other liquid financial assets are called:","options":["Quasi-money or near money","Quasi-money or far money","Make-belief money or near money","None of the above"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Time and savings deposits, together with other liquid financial assets, are called quasi-money or near money because they can be turned into cash quickly but are not themselves generally used for payment. Option B uses the made-up term 'far money', and option C uses the made-up term 'make-belief money'. D is wrong because A gives the correct names."}},{"itemId":"c94aa01f-52a0-5b9e-8204-f43426915cb6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bdffa256-1f40-51c2-ad1a-2a92797b376f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0078","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The monetary aggregate M2 is a summation of M1 and ------------","options":["Quick-money","Quasi-money","High-powered money","All of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Broad money, M2, is narrow money (M1) plus quasi-money, that is, the time and savings deposits held with banks. Option A is not a recognised component of the money supply, and option C, high-powered money, is not the item added to M1 to obtain M2, so D cannot be correct either."}},{"itemId":"e548683e-cfab-5460-b5bc-af6fa838c0c1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"79c259a3-4809-517f-9703-4cad1f273ace","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0079","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Use the table below to answer. Monetary Aggregates in Nigeria 2007-2011 (in billions of naira). Columns: Year | Cash or Currency outside Banks | Demand Deposits | Savings Deposits | Time Deposits. 2007 | 500 | 1000 | 780 | 680; 2008 | 750 | 1550 | 950 | 800; 2009 | 1000 | 2000 | 1450 | 1250; 2010 | 1125 | 2500 | 1800 | 1600; 2011 | 1125 | 2500 | 1800 | 1600. From the table, the monetary aggregate M1 for the years 2007 to 2009 is:","options":["N2280bill ; N2300bill ; N4250bill","N1500bill ; N2300bill ; N3000bill","N1780bill ; N2350bill ; N3450bill","None of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"M1 is currency outside banks plus demand deposits: 500 + 1,000 = N1,500 billion for 2007, 750 + 1,550 = N2,300 billion for 2008 and 1,000 + 2,000 = N3,000 billion for 2009. Option A mixes M1 with savings or time deposits, and option C adds demand deposits to savings or time deposits instead of to currency. D is wrong because B matches the table."}},{"itemId":"baadf59c-90d3-5b33-b4af-ee6fac907577","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8ed6f6cd-5921-571c-877d-5211f7aace94","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0080","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Use the table below to answer. Monetary Aggregates in Nigeria 2007-2011 (in billions of naira). Columns: Year | Cash or Currency outside Banks | Demand Deposits | Savings Deposits | Time Deposits. 2007 | 500 | 1000 | 780 | 680; 2008 | 750 | 1550 | 950 | 800; 2009 | 1000 | 2000 | 1450 | 1250; 2010 | 1125 | 2500 | 1800 | 1600; 2011 | 1125 | 2500 | 1800 | 1600. From the table, quasi-money (near money) for the years 2008 to 2011 is:","options":["N2280bill ; N3250bill ; N3400bill ; N4300bill","N2300bill ; N3450bill ; N3900bill ; N4300bill","N1750bill ; N2700bill ; N3400bill ; N3400bill","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Quasi-money is savings deposits plus time deposits: 950 + 800 = N1,750 billion for 2008, 1,450 + 1,250 = N2,700 billion for 2009, and 1,800 + 1,600 = N3,400 billion for both 2010 and 2011. Options A and B give figures that do not match these sums, and D is wrong because C matches the table."}},{"itemId":"72e9a1bf-d808-59d1-a30a-a8432378208b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2dcb62f5-d331-538c-b9b9-696102c2d5c8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0081","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The table shows monetary aggregates in Nigeria, 2007-2011 (in billions of naira). Columns: Year | Cash or Currency outside Banks | Demand Deposits | Savings Deposits | Time Deposits. 2007 | 500 | 1000 | 780 | 680; 2008 | 750 | 1550 | 950 | 800; 2009 | 1000 | 2000 | 1450 | 1250; 2010 | 1125 | 2500 | 1800 | 1600; 2011 | 1125 | 2500 | 1800 | 1600. What are the values of the monetary aggregate M2 for the years 2010 and 2011?","options":["N7,025 billion; N7,025 billion","N3,000 billion; N3,400 billion","N3,900 billion; N4,300 billion","N3,400 billion; N4,300 billion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"M2 (broad money) is M1 plus quasi-money, that is cash outside banks + demand deposits + savings deposits + time deposits. For 2010 that is 1125 + 2500 + 1800 + 1600 = N7,025 billion, and because the 2011 figures are identical, 2011 also gives N7,025 billion. The figures in options B, C and D are far too small, since M1 alone (cash plus demand deposits) is already N3,625 billion in each of those years."}},{"itemId":"893805d4-4c0d-5efe-a9cd-8644812c74a4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e1a959d-5ad0-511a-810e-76b11554d013","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0082","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A decline in the share of currency (cash) in the total money stock is a reflection of the fact that:","options":["The public have grown more confident in the banking industry","There is financial distress in the economy","The structure of the financial system is getting more sophisticated","Only (a) and (c) are correct"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"When people hold a smaller share of their money as cash, it usually means they trust banks enough to keep more of it as deposits, and that the payment system has become more sophisticated, with cheques, cards, transfers and ATMs reducing the need for cash. Both A and C describe this, so D is the best answer. Option B points the wrong way: financial distress makes people lose confidence in banks and hold more cash, not less."}},{"itemId":"360227f2-4ae0-5989-baf7-82a6e16ced98","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0534416d-a69a-5582-8b06-1c195ed397c4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0083","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following statements about the components of a country's money stock is correct?","options":["Only currency notes and coins are money; bank deposits are never part of the money stock","Each component is regarded as money to the extent that it performs some of the functions of money","A deposit counts as money only if it pays interest to its holder","Savings and time deposits are excluded from money at every level of definition"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money is defined by what it does, so each part of the money stock counts as money to the extent that it performs some of the functions of money. Currency and demand deposits serve as a medium of exchange, while savings and time deposits are a good store of value, which is why they are included in broad money (M2). Options A and D are wrong because bank deposits are part of the money stock, and option C is wrong because demand deposits usually earn no interest yet are still money."}},{"itemId":"3cd83f6d-ab46-5967-aeee-df3550520cf8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"edf004d8-8f40-50d8-98f6-6e07871f4816","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0084","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In terms of immediate liquidity and general acceptance as a means of payment, which components of the money stock are the most qualified to be called money?","options":["Savings and time deposits (quasi-money)","Currency and demand deposits (M1)","Deposits held with non-bank financial institutions","Time deposits alone, because they earn the highest interest"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Currency and demand deposits together make up narrow money (M1), and they are the most liquid parts of the money stock because they can be spent at once and are generally accepted in payment. Savings and time deposits (option A) are quasi-money: a good store of value, but they usually have to be converted into cash or demand deposits before they can be spent, and option D confuses earning interest with liquidity. Deposits with non-bank financial institutions (option C) are counted only in the even broader M3 measure."}},{"itemId":"1a36dd69-bc5f-5ab0-898d-008686836dd0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d373b60d-9280-5009-a366-421d86211abf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0085","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The value of money is best measured by:","options":["The amount of metal contained in its coins","Its purchasing power, that is, the quantity of goods and services a given amount of money can buy","The rate of interest that banks pay on deposits","The total quantity of money in circulation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money is not wanted for itself but for what it can buy, so its value lies in its purchasing power. The more goods and services a given amount of money can obtain, the greater its value; when the same amount buys less, its value has fallen. Option D confuses the quantity of money with its value, and option A describes the commodity value of old metallic coins rather than the value of modern money."}},{"itemId":"e2a2a62f-7dd0-5174-9752-593244774c51","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fcd0a1b6-8cd9-525d-a82e-36f2b7fa993e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0086","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"That the value of money falls when the prices of goods rise implies that:","options":["There is a direct relationship between the value of money and the prices of goods","There is an inverse relationship between the value of money and the prices of goods","Both (a) and (b) are correct under certain circumstances","None of the above is correct"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The value of money and the general price level move in opposite directions: when prices rise, each naira buys fewer goods, so the value of money falls, and when prices fall its value rises. That is an inverse relationship, so B is correct. Option A would mean value and prices rise together, which contradicts the statement in the question, and C cannot be right because the relationship cannot be both direct and inverse at once."}},{"itemId":"4bef0cf5-d354-553f-bf92-3bc5fa8d7089","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6fa65dcb-e2d3-548c-8a83-ee5be09d844d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0087","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the assumptions behind the basic theory of credit creation by banks?","options":["That there is a mandatory reserve proportion the bank has to keep from each deposit","That only a fraction of each deposit is needed by depositors in the immediate future","That banks must keep the whole of every deposit as reserves and lend none of it out","That there must be some initial primary deposit in the form of cash"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The basic credit creation process assumes that banks receive an initial primary deposit in cash, that they must keep a legally required fraction of each deposit as reserves, and that depositors need only part of their deposits in the near future, leaving the rest free to lend. Keeping the whole of every deposit as reserves is the opposite of what the theory assumes, because banks could then lend nothing and no credit would be created. The other three options all describe genuine assumptions of the theory."}},{"itemId":"111606a3-f2a3-5e53-8a69-d4a6778e8671","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e0813588-bb73-50c8-a0da-af4f2cf49480","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0088","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The credit creation ability of the banking system is limited by:","options":["The amount of the initial deposit","The level of the reserve ratio","The existence of leakages in the system","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Three things limit how much credit the banking system can create: the size of the initial cash deposit, the cash reserve ratio, and leakages from the system. Total deposits created equal the initial deposit times the money multiplier (1/r), so a smaller deposit or a higher reserve ratio means less credit, and cash withdrawn and kept outside the banks breaks the deposit-lending chain. Since A, B and C are all genuine limits, D is the answer."}},{"itemId":"764d4e9a-35b6-5f6e-8f0b-c34deb0fabe3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d8c8f193-acd8-54e4-bdb9-27402af1a51e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0089","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In any financial system there are three basic types of institutions. Which of these is NOT included among them?","options":["Bank Financial Institutions","Non-bank Financial Institutions","Reformatory Institutions","Regulatory and Supervisory Institutions"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A financial system is made up of bank financial institutions such as commercial and merchant banks, non-bank financial institutions such as insurance companies and pension funds, and regulatory and supervisory institutions such as the CBN and the NDIC. Reformatory institutions are correctional institutions and play no part in the financial system, so C is the odd one out while A, B and D are all genuine categories."}},{"itemId":"cb3c7444-cac8-5c80-bebf-258b429789bf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e891d355-34f9-5f23-9418-cae4f74ca57e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0090","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT an example of a non-bank financial institution?","options":["Pension funds","Insurance companies","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","Finance houses"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The NDIC is a regulatory and supervisory body: it insures bank deposits and supervises banks alongside the CBN, instead of mobilising and lending funds as a financial intermediary does. Pension funds (A), insurance companies (B) and finance houses (D) all pool funds from the public and invest or lend them, which is why they are classed as non-bank financial institutions."}},{"itemId":"d3892261-55cb-5785-badf-a5d25faa5b8d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"07a561d7-58be-565e-b6e1-86fc162a9c34","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0091","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The body that regulates the activities of insurance companies in Nigeria is known as:","options":["CBN","NAICOM","SEC","NDIC"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The National Insurance Commission (NAICOM) is the body that regulates and supervises insurance companies in Nigeria. The CBN (A) regulates banks and other financial institutions under its control, the SEC (C) regulates the capital market, and the NDIC (D) insures bank deposits and helps supervise banks."}},{"itemId":"e0499c4a-0eb2-59ef-91e5-fe39665f2bcf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"122d8aea-0d43-53ac-b8ba-61af2d21950b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0092","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The development bank UDBN (Urban Development Bank of Nigeria) was set up mainly to provide funds for:","options":["Building houses","Building networks of bridges","Providing infrastructural facilities in cities such as public toilets","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The UDBN was created to finance urban infrastructure that conventional banks would not fund, such as motor parks, markets, drainage, street lighting, waste disposal and public conveniences like public toilets. Building houses (A) is the work of housing finance institutions such as the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria. Option B is too narrow and is not what the UDBN was known for; its mandate was the general infrastructure that makes cities work."}},{"itemId":"b52a7077-620c-5cea-b297-365d531a842a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b7ed91f2-6fb2-5b14-9b2d-d65e5d489c21","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0093","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the basic role of financial intermediaries in the savings-investment process?","options":["Giving investors information on who has spare funds, while leaving them to raise the funds directly from the owners","Mobilising funds from surplus units (savers) and channelling them to deficit units (investors)","Regulating and supervising banks and insurance companies","Issuing the national currency and controlling the money supply"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial intermediaries pool funds from savers and lend or invest them with those who need funds, which lowers the risk and cost of funds and allows longer-term use. Option A describes the slow direct approach that intermediaries replace, because they actually collect and lend the funds themselves. Options D and C describe the work of the central bank and the regulatory bodies, not of intermediaries."}},{"itemId":"be804a88-5a3c-5a6f-8e4c-82ba5c1bf38b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b58c8415-d13c-5360-aa6d-f9dd8f709a0a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0094","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Among the several limitations of trying to source funds directly from the ultimate owners is that:","options":["The cost of funds would be high","The period for which the funds can be used would be short","The risk on releasing funds would be high","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Raising funds directly from fund owners is slow and limiting. The single owner bears all the risk alone, so the risk is high and he charges a high price for his money, and he will usually release it only for a short period, which makes long-term investment difficult. Since A, B and C are all genuine limitations, D is the answer."}},{"itemId":"565bbd05-e934-5c0b-bee5-bfa2d387636f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5099ab31-f48d-55fd-aa01-b7327bcb0edb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0095","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT a basic advantage of having financial institutions as intermediaries in an economy?","options":["Investors can now access funds in fairly bulky sums","The risk on funds is now higher as bulky sums can be accessed","The cost of funds is reduced","The term for fund usage is now longer"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial intermediaries pool funds from many savers, so the risk carried by each fund owner is lower, not higher. Because risk is spread, the cost of funds falls (C), investors can obtain large sums (A), and funds can be made available for longer periods (D). Option B is therefore not an advantage; it reverses what intermediation actually does."}},{"itemId":"5c77e5c0-49b1-5f4e-b78a-c5c6a1c30ece","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"327ee39c-cc65-58ba-ad29-54f13faf96e3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0096","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT included among the factors that make for successful financial intermediation?","options":["Trust and confidence of the public in the financial institutions","Multilingual ability (ability to speak several languages) of the staff of the financial institutions","Convenience of accessing the financial institutions by the people","Price charged by the financial institutions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Successful financial intermediation depends on public trust and confidence (A), the convenience and ease of dealing with the institutions (C), and the price they charge, such as interest and fees (D). Staff who speak several languages may help customer service, but that is not one of the basic factors that decide whether intermediation succeeds."}},{"itemId":"23113ef4-eefc-5a82-a161-54e34d0a39dd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2c5a7fd1-5622-591f-87e3-9119e5bf8a71","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0097","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Deposit money banks have three major goals. These are:","options":["Longevity, profitability and solvency","Liquidity, profitability and solvency","Endurability, liquidity and profitability","None of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"A deposit money bank must earn profits for its owners, stay liquid enough to meet depositors' withdrawals, and remain solvent so that it can meet all its obligations in the long run. Balancing liquidity, profitability and solvency is the central task of bank management. Longevity (A) and endurability (C) are not recognised banking goals, although a bank that stays liquid, profitable and solvent will naturally survive."}},{"itemId":"ad55bfde-935f-51ff-a152-dc524f98c811","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc5d0952-e8ba-5682-ab6e-d1866104a3ab","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0098","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best describes the relationship between liquidity and profitability in a deposit money bank?","options":["The more liquid a bank is, the more profitable it becomes, because cash attracts more depositors","A bank becomes most profitable by holding all of its deposits as cash in its vaults","The more liquid a bank is, the less profitable it tends to be, because idle liquid funds earn little or no income","Liquidity and profitability are unrelated, so a bank can pursue each goal separately"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Liquid assets such as cash earn little or no income, while loans and investments earn interest but tie up funds. So the more liquid a bank keeps itself, the less it can earn, and the more it lends, the less liquid it becomes; managing this trade-off is a core part of banking. Option A reverses the trade-off, option D ignores it, and option B would leave the bank earning nothing on its deposits."}},{"itemId":"e90c9119-a5a4-5ca8-be81-2f161db3308b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ab6f8046-405f-5510-b1c0-313b62b2df3b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0099","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When we talk of deposit money banks, we refer to:","options":["Savings banks and merchant banks","Development banks and commercial banks","Merchant banks and commercial banks","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Deposit money banks are the commercial banks and the merchant banks, the two types of bank that hold the most significant position in the financial system. Option A wrongly includes savings banks, and option B wrongly includes development banks, which are specialised institutions set up mainly to provide long-term finance."}},{"itemId":"8ef0a47c-3d5b-5a2a-b5ba-fb47524f8e80","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"32c6b496-bb25-5b08-9466-3d4a610d5063","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0100","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first commercial bank in Nigeria came in _______ and was known as ________.","options":["1892, Barclays Bank","1952, Industrial and Commercial Bank","1892, African Banking Corporation","1882, Standard Bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Commercial banking in Nigeria began in 1892, when the African Banking Corporation opened in Lagos. Its business was taken over in 1894 by the Bank of British West Africa, which later became Standard Bank and is today First Bank of Nigeria. Barclays Bank (A) came later, the Industrial and Commercial Bank (B) was an indigenous bank of 1929 rather than 1952, and Standard Bank (D) was a later name, not the first bank."}},{"itemId":"b2c99ef4-8ef5-5255-b92b-1316417b0717","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"30c699c7-1cc7-5210-8550-1f43ed74d3da","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0101","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The factors that made for the rapid collapse of indigenous commercial banks before the Banking Ordinance of 1952 included:","options":["Weak capital base","Untrained and inexperienced managers","Fraud","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Before the 1952 Banking Ordinance there were no banking rules or bank examiners, and most of the many indigenous banks set up in the 1940s boom collapsed quickly. They had weak capital bases that could not absorb shocks (A), untrained and inexperienced managers who could not analyse balance sheets or forecast bad debts (B), and widespread fraud (C). All three were causes, so D is correct."}},{"itemId":"f87f91db-8302-5a96-a346-49e9a293b3b3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"027b3119-b46f-5462-969a-d2bc8e36c480","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0102","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before 1952, only the indigenous commercial banks set up by government or with strong government backing survived the wave of bank failures. Which of these was NOT one of such banks?","options":["WEMA Bank","African Continental Bank","Afribank","National Bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Afribank began as the International Bank for West Africa, a foreign-owned bank that came into Nigeria later, so it was not one of the indigenous banks that survived because of government backing. National Bank (D) was backed by the Western Regional government, African Continental Bank (B) by the Eastern Regional government, and WEMA Bank (A) grew out of Agbonmagbe Bank, which the Western Regional government supported."}},{"itemId":"a1d5a27b-aa94-5e1e-9659-f2e546d1e7dd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"08050aa1-e98b-5e25-a740-0b5c1e2ddcd1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0103","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"After the Banking Ordinance of 1952, many more banks collapsed because:","options":["They could not meet the minimum capital requirement set by the Ordinance","There was no supervisory and regulatory agency to give purposeful direction and leadership","They lacked government patronage","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Many indigenous banks failed after the 1952 Ordinance because they could not meet its new minimum capital requirement (A). There was also no central bank yet to supervise banks and give direction (B), since the CBN only began operations in 1959, and banks without government patronage (C) lacked the support that kept the government-backed banks alive. All three explain the failures, so D is correct."}},{"itemId":"0def7cce-4643-5cdb-ab5d-6ebe4572226a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ebf10de-b245-5fe5-b68d-eac16d1ce5c0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0104","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of a commercial bank?","options":["Accepting deposits from members of the public","Granting its customers loans and advances","Writing wills for its customers","Safe-keeping of wills, jewellery and other important documents for its customers"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Commercial banks accept deposits (A), grant loans and advances (B), and keep wills, jewellery and important documents in safe custody for their customers (D). Writing a will is a legal task for the customer and a lawyer; a bank may keep the finished will safely, but drafting it is not a commercial banking function."}},{"itemId":"c5bb73e7-726b-5ee9-b832-fb82545934b5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fe52f7fc-8025-536d-af19-d366e10d54b3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0105","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a way in which commercial banks provide credit to their customers?","options":["Acting as lender of last resort to other banks","Safe-keeping customers' valuables and documents","Discounting bills of exchange","Issuing the national currency"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"When a bank discounts a bill of exchange, it pays the holder the face value less a discount before the bill matures, which is a form of short-term credit. Safe-keeping of valuables (option B) is a service, not credit, while issuing the currency (option D) and acting as lender of last resort (option A) are functions of the Central Bank of Nigeria, not of commercial banks."}},{"itemId":"a6f6e5b3-42bb-517f-9121-475038eca9a3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da02c551-b284-5e6a-b7c6-4c5e6ae76a2b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0106","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT an asset to a commercial bank?","options":["Cash","Deposits","Loans and advances","Its account balances with other banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Deposits are what a bank owes its customers, so they appear on the liabilities side of its balance sheet. Cash (A), loans and advances (C) and balances held with other banks (D) are things the bank owns or is owed, so they are assets. It is tempting to treat deposits as assets because the bank holds the money, but the bank must repay them on demand or at maturity."}},{"itemId":"3719981c-835f-5782-a7ec-89d65c6699c5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"94f48bfc-65d2-5628-8710-f8b1673b9e86","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0107","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT a function of merchant banks?","options":["Equipment leasing","Project financing","Provision of safe-keeping for customers' jewellery and wills","Provision of investment and advisory services"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Merchant banks deal mainly with corporate and institutional clients, offering services such as equipment leasing (A), project financing (B), and investment and advisory services (D). Safe-keeping of customers' jewellery and wills is a typical retail service of commercial banks, not a merchant banking function."}},{"itemId":"7260e4f9-3d8d-58e2-97f9-022eb8c1311d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4da876a1-bd55-54e6-ab95-511250bcd191","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0108","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the universal banking model, which the CBN replaced with a new banking licence regime in 2010, a universal bank was a bank that:","options":["Could only accept demand deposits and grant short-term loans","Could carry out both commercial and merchant banking business and also engage in services such as insurance and stockbroking","Was owned jointly by the Federal government and the CBN to provide long-term development finance","Operated only on non-interest principles"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Universal banking allowed one banking licence to cover commercial banking, merchant banking and other financial services such as insurance and stockbroking. Option A describes a narrow traditional commercial bank, option C describes a development bank, and option D describes a non-interest bank."}},{"itemId":"c451aac9-f399-5119-86bf-2ed834b8d67a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"68ec871a-41ce-5180-9899-2340ec53289f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0109","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first Nigerian merchant bank, NAL Merchant Bank, which came on board in 1969, was a merger of two companies that had existed since 1960 and were already performing merchant banking functions. These were:","options":["Nigerian Acceptances Limited and Peoples Co Limited","Nigerian Acceptances Limited and Philip Hill (Nigeria) Limited","Nigerian Acceptances Limited and United African Company","None of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Merchant banking in Nigeria began in 1960 with Nigerian Acceptances Limited and Philip Hill (Nigeria) Limited. In 1969 the two merged to form NAL Merchant Bank, the first Nigerian merchant bank. Neither Peoples Co Limited (A) nor the United African Company (C), a trading company, was part of this merger."}},{"itemId":"01c2370d-ff77-54a0-9ace-1d5e4c670c62","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4dec9b01-6c8d-50b6-8e5a-3212db9d0492","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0110","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT one of the reasons for setting up development banks?","options":["To fill the gap left by the DMBs in medium-term and long-term financing","To fill the gap in funding socially desirable projects (such as housing) which are not commercially attractive","To provide government officials with an opportunity to raise funds for projects that will be useful to them after retirement","To accelerate the development of sectors such as agriculture and industry that have heavy multiplier effects in the long run but whose short-run returns may not attract investors"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Development banks were set up to fill gaps left by deposit money banks: medium- and long-term finance (A), socially desirable but commercially unattractive projects such as housing (B), and sectors like agriculture and industry whose long-run benefits are large but whose short-run returns are low (D). Raising money for officials' private retirement projects would be an abuse of office, not a reason for a development bank."}},{"itemId":"8227c0ef-73bf-5a88-aac8-5767381d4ed0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a16d2bb-3f61-59b7-a286-d0218fda92a1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0111","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Development banks in Nigeria have generally been established by:","options":["The CBN and the Federal government","The CBN and the DMBs","The Federal government and the DMBs","None of the above"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Nigeria's development banks were generally set up and owned jointly by the Federal government and the CBN, because their purpose is national development rather than profit. Deposit money banks (B and C) are privately owned, profit-seeking banks; the very reason development banks exist is that DMBs would not provide the long-term finance these sectors needed."}},{"itemId":"a65a82dd-301f-5282-9546-cfafb77403b9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5caa9bf0-7d99-512c-b685-811000a63d38","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0112","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT a development bank in Nigeria?","options":["Nigerian Industrial Development Bank","Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry","African Continental Bank","Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The African Continental Bank was a commercial bank with strong Eastern Regional government backing, not a development bank. The NIDB (A), the NBCI (B) and the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (D) were all development finance institutions, set up to provide long-term funds for industry, commerce and housing."}},{"itemId":"eca46d02-f388-5924-8459-5b6a69b600c8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c7479dd2-c232-5b1b-94c2-c4b08c5f786b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0113","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why were some Nigerian development banks merged in the early 2000s?","options":["To convert them into deposit money banks that take demand deposits from the public","Because long-term development finance was no longer needed in Nigeria","To form bigger, more resilient institutions after the banking crises of the 1990s had weakened them","To privatise them fully and remove all government ownership"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The crises in Nigerian banking in the 1990s left several development banks weak, so in the early 2000s some were merged into bigger, more resilient institutions, such as the Bank of Industry. The merged banks remained government-owned development banks, so option A (conversion into deposit money banks) and option D (full privatisation) are wrong. Option B is wrong because the merger was meant to strengthen long-term development finance, not end it."}},{"itemId":"eb159201-f6b0-5873-b7a6-713b88d236ba","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e4c1193d-c527-5298-a7b5-8eca43f133fb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0114","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Bank of Industry (BOI), formed in 2001, is an amalgam of:","options":["The NIDB, the NBCI and the FMBN","The NBCI, the UDBN and NERFUND","The NIDB, the NBCI and NERFUND","The NACB, the NIDB and the NBCI"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Bank of Industry was formed in 2001 by bringing together the Nigerian Industrial Development Bank, the Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry and the National Economic Reconstruction Fund (NERFUND). The FMBN (A) is a housing finance institution, the UDBN (B) financed urban infrastructure, and the NACB (D) went into the new agricultural development bank instead."}},{"itemId":"dce9c766-fd62-5de5-a987-bc15b793a6bd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"36803ce5-641e-59f7-a324-76bdfd484058","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0116","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is a function of the NIDB?","options":["To identify and promote projects whose value added to industry was high","To provide long-term loans to medium- and large-scale industrial enterprises","To mobilise investible funds as well as skills from both domestic and foreign sources","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Nigerian Industrial Development Bank was set up to identify and promote projects with high value added to industry (A), to provide long-term loans and equity finance mainly to medium- and large-scale industrial enterprises (B), and to mobilise investible funds and skills both within Nigeria and from abroad (C). Since all three are genuine NIDB functions, D is the answer."}},{"itemId":"5bcd901d-8b56-548f-8671-c6a9ba6f6eaf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"de6d8119-3f0c-5098-8d47-0e6760f01bcf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0117","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry (NBCI) was set up mainly to:","options":["Provide long-term mortgage finance for housing","Help Nigerians obtain long-term funds to buy shares in enterprises affected by the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decree of 1972","Grant loans to farmers and cooperative societies","Finance urban infrastructure such as motor parks, markets and drainage"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Nigerian Enterprises Promotion (indigenisation) Decree of 1972 required foreign interests in many firms to be sold to Nigerians, but most Nigerians lacked the money to buy the shares. The NBCI was created to give indigenous investors access to long-term funds for this purpose. Option D describes the Urban Development Bank, option A describes housing finance institutions such as the FMBN, and option C describes the agricultural bank (NACB)."}},{"itemId":"172028e8-e8c7-568b-8aaf-a08dda7f4985","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5ae7e9e6-9754-53cf-bcf0-e82c7fe5814c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0118","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The NBCI provided equity capital provided that the promoters of a project were themselves providing at least ____ of the required funds, and its loans were repayable over about ____ years.","options":["10%; 10 to 14 years","15%; 15 to 20 years","30%; 12 to 15 years","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NBCI would take up equity in a project only if the promoters themselves provided at least 30% of the required funds, and its loans were usually repayable over about 12 to 15 years. These long repayment periods reflect its role as a development bank providing long-term finance. The figures in options A and B do not match these terms."}},{"itemId":"525b7be5-60c3-5b02-b6f6-a7f5d35e3b11","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6d0867e4-5ffc-57cd-989f-f44bec289388","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0119","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What prompted the government to set up the Nigerian Agricultural and Cooperative Bank (NACB) was:","options":["A desire to make the country self-sufficient in food production","A desire to provide a considerable proportion of the country's needed raw materials","A desire to enable the country to earn more foreign exchange through exports of agricultural produce","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NACB was set up because government wanted to achieve self-sufficiency in food production (A), increase the domestic supply of raw materials for industry (B), and earn more foreign exchange from exports of agricultural produce (C). Food self-sufficiency is the best-known of these aims, but it was not the only one, so D is the best answer."}},{"itemId":"b305e111-a280-5ad8-86c4-99ecc00e0a85","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fe1e5281-681f-5efc-9924-cd1940590777","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0120","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Urban Development Bank of Nigeria (UDBN) came on board the Nigerian financial scene in the:","options":["1980s","1990s","2000s","None of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Urban Development Bank of Nigeria was established by Decree 51 of 1992, so it came on the scene in the 1990s. It was created to finance urban infrastructure that conventional banks would not fund. Options A and C give the wrong decade."}},{"itemId":"61f73f90-5524-5a6d-b40f-c91280aff453","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bb44075b-1190-5095-b3fa-230330d2fc17","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0121","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT one of the purposes the government specified when it set up the Bank of Industry (BOI) from the merger of the NIDB, the NBCI and NERFUND?","options":["To act as lender of last resort to the deposit money banks","To help in the revitalization of the nation's ailing industries through the pumping of fresh funds and ideas","To help modernize, expand and diversify existing enterprises that are not yet moribund","To help improve the productive capacity of the economy through the empowerment of small and medium-scale enterprises (SMEs)"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Acting as lender of last resort to deposit money banks is a function of the Central Bank of Nigeria, not of a development finance institution like the BOI. The government set up the BOI to revive ailing industries with fresh funds and ideas (B), to help existing enterprises modernize, expand and diversify (C), and to boost the economy's productive capacity by empowering SMEs (D). So B, C and D are all genuine BOI purposes."}},{"itemId":"a567d4a4-df98-5b1f-b401-e93173cad387","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"21a55a09-428f-525c-bdfe-4f1838a9748c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0123","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The need for the Peoples Bank of Nigeria (PBN) arose because the category of people to whom loans were to be granted under the PBN arrangement","options":["Could not afford collateral security","Could not afford the high interest rates charged by the DMBs","Both (a) and (b) are correct","None of the above is correct"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The PBN was created for the rural poor and the urban poor, who were shut out of ordinary bank credit for two reasons: they had no collateral security to pledge, and they could not bear the high interest rates charged by deposit money banks. That is why the PBN lent without collateral and without interest. Options A and B are each only half of the reason, so C is the best answer."}},{"itemId":"52017563-d77b-5a11-87ce-e3415fadb2eb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b513e912-2678-574e-b055-d4dd6dd97969","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0124","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Community banks in Nigeria performed basically the same functions as commercial banks. The main differences between a community bank and a commercial bank were that","options":["A community bank was limited to providing financial services to the community where it was located","A community bank could not deal in foreign exchange transactions without express approval","A community bank was not admitted into the clearing house system","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Community banks took deposits and granted credit just like commercial banks, but with three restrictions. Their operations were confined to the community where they were located, they could not deal in foreign exchange without express approval, and they were not admitted into the clearing house. Since A, B and C are each true, D is the correct choice."}},{"itemId":"e962cfc3-ef73-5844-a45b-d3594d46e0ef","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1392cf81-e48f-5657-97f4-4485da4d9715","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0125","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the community banking system, which body was given the immediate supervision of community banks and was meant to keep a fund from which they could borrow in times of crisis?","options":["Bank of Industry (BOI)","National Board for Community Banks (NBCB)","Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN)","Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The National Board for Community Banks was set up to monitor, inspect and supervise community banks, and it was to maintain a fund, built from government contributions, gifts and donations, that the banks could draw on in difficult times. The BOI (A) is a development bank for industry, the FMBN (C) deals with housing finance, and the SEC (D) regulates the capital market, so none of them supervised community banks."}},{"itemId":"53ee7ece-cdf1-58f2-a591-11da25f442c4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"002888b7-6182-58b0-8a9d-f4cc09c420a8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0126","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Community Banks Decree of 1992, the minimum paid-up capital of a community bank was _________. Before community banks were converted into microfinance banks, this minimum had been raised to __________.","options":["N250,000; N25,000,000","N500,000; N50,000,000","N250,000; N5,000,000","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Community banks started with a modest minimum paid-up capital of N250,000, in line with their role of serving small savers and borrowers in local communities. As weak capital became a problem, the CBN later raised the minimum to N5 million. Option A has the right starting figure but a later figure that is far too high, and option B gets both figures wrong."}},{"itemId":"e66a27a8-df4a-5bc3-ae06-d58a5aae86dd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"20fe7a68-a165-5168-8238-9b092f8c9dcc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0127","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In December of which year did the CBN launch the policy under which community banks were to transform, and into what type of institution?","options":["2005; Microfinance banks","2008; Bank of Industry","2007; Bureaux de Change","None of the above"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The CBN launched its Microfinance Policy, Regulatory and Supervisory Framework in December 2005, and under it existing community banks were to convert into microfinance banks once they met the new capital and other requirements. Bureaux de change (C) deal only in buying and selling foreign currency, and the Bank of Industry (B) is a development bank formed from earlier mergers, so neither is what community banks became."}},{"itemId":"ef3f82b3-f78f-588c-ae10-7a3dfcbc72ae","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"65759a12-36d1-5f06-9fbd-9ac01c36d653","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0128","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT an element of the 2005 Microfinance Policy Framework?","options":["That state governments and local governments set aside not less than 1.0 percent of their annual budgets for microfinancing","That the federal government makes available 25 percent of its recurrent expenditure for microfinancing","That a Microfinance Development Fund be established","That a deposit insurance scheme be set up for microfinance banks to protect depositors"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Nothing in the framework asked the Federal Government to commit 25 percent of its recurrent expenditure to microfinance. The framework did ask state and local governments to set aside at least 1 percent of their annual budgets for on-lending through microfinance banks (A), and it provided for a Microfinance Development Fund (C) and deposit insurance for microfinance banks (D)."}},{"itemId":"69da8371-1dcb-59ea-8cca-ef9ea09f05e8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3042b438-9ef9-57d3-a5f1-123018750f87","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0129","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the 2005 microfinance policy framework, microfinance banks were to be of two types. These are","options":["Unit microfinance bank and State microfinance bank","Regional microfinance bank and State microfinance bank","Provincial microfinance bank and Unit microfinance bank","None of the above is correct"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The 2005 framework created two categories: the unit microfinance bank, with a lower minimum capital, and the state microfinance bank, with a higher one. Regional (B) and provincial (C) microfinance banks were not categories under that framework, and D is wrong because A is correct."}},{"itemId":"dcfffa35-0358-5326-a3cc-daaa64876e23","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8583dfcf-a817-5611-9ad2-88766c72f831","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0130","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The advantages of bringing microfinance institutions under the supervisory purview of the CBN include that","options":["It enhances monetary stability","It expands the financial infrastructure to meet the financial needs of micro-enterprises","It promotes linkages between universal banks or DMBs, the development banks and the microfinance institutions","All of the above are correct"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Bringing microfinance institutions under CBN supervision supports monetary stability, because more of the financial activity in the economy comes within the CBN's oversight. It also expands the financial infrastructure serving micro, small and medium enterprises and links universal banks, development banks and microfinance banks together. Each of A, B and C is a real advantage, so D is the best answer."}},{"itemId":"9766c269-4028-564f-8df2-f1ceb2c37cfd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"92351083-71e0-576e-a73a-6dcc12861ab7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0131","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the 2005 microfinance policy, which of the following was NOT one of the government's expectations of microfinance banks?","options":["To provide diversified, affordable and dependable financial services to the active poor in a competitive manner","To mobilize savings for intermediation and create employment opportunities","To finance the large-scale industrial projects of multinational companies","To facilitate payment services such as salaries, gratuities and pensions for the various tiers of government"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Microfinance banks are meant to serve the economically active poor and micro and small enterprises, not to fund big industrial projects for multinationals, which need large long-term finance from commercial and development banks. Providing affordable services to the active poor (A), mobilizing savings and creating jobs (B), and handling payments such as salaries and pensions for governments (D) were all expected of them."}},{"itemId":"d913c685-8d2c-59af-99ab-d3277d8c4b45","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b6a3f905-650a-549c-a8fb-1057a6abf4a9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0133","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) was established by the Central Bank Act of ______.","options":["1954","1958","1960","1961"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN was established by the Central Bank of Nigeria Act of 1958 and began operations on 1 July 1959. Option C, 1960, is the year of Nigeria's independence, not of the CBN Act, and neither 1954 nor 1961 is linked to its creation."}},{"itemId":"a33e8de2-0b86-55fc-ac05-a06681189995","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"834cba91-8f0f-56c1-8ff0-814f75ea67fe","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0134","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT a function expected of the CBN?","options":["Issuance of currency notes and coins or legal tender to the banking system","Granting of loans and advances to individuals and households","Managing the country's external reserves","Being the lender of last resort to the other banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A central bank is banker to the government and to the banks, not to individuals and households, so granting personal loans is the business of deposit money banks and microfinance banks. Issuing currency (A), managing external reserves (C) and acting as lender of last resort to banks (D) are all traditional functions of the CBN."}},{"itemId":"297a3c55-43af-559f-bc3b-6c85a32cbdfe","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2be76e34-7de4-539e-9ab8-09357ed1e990","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0135","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Prior to the establishment of the CBN, the function of issuance of legal tender in Nigeria was performed by the","options":["Currency Board of Nigeria","West African Monetary Union","West African Currency Board","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Before the CBN took over, Nigeria's currency was issued by the West African Currency Board, set up by the colonial government in 1912 to issue currency for the British West African colonies of Nigeria, Ghana, Sierra Leone and The Gambia. There was no 'Currency Board of Nigeria' (A), and the West African Monetary Union (B) was never the body that issued Nigeria's money."}},{"itemId":"f5c4f41e-37c7-5d6f-add1-a046b7fd1747","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4336c01d-b891-541f-923b-c0fda5a8e97c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0136","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these was NOT one of the commissions or missions that examined the setting up of a central bank in Nigeria before 1958?","options":["Fisher's Commission 1953","Johnson's Commission 1954","The World Bank Commission 1955","The Loynes Commission 1957"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Fisher Commission (1953), the World Bank Mission (1955) and the Loynes Commission (1957) all looked into whether Nigeria should have a central bank, and the Loynes Report led directly to the CBN Act of 1958. There was no 'Johnson's Commission' in this process. Fisher and the World Bank Mission did not favour an immediate central bank, but they were still part of the process, so A and C are not the answer."}},{"itemId":"570c9c19-e990-5d5c-81a3-7baf3852dd58","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"137c394f-1d39-5763-96b8-2dd59c4fe497","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0137","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the years before the CBN Act of 1958, the Nigerian government became persuaded of the need for a central bank for which of the following purposes?","options":["To provide leadership and direction for the banks and other financial institutions","To better harness the financial resources of the economy","To create institutions, instruments, and markets that will ensure the growth and development of the financial system","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Nigerian leaders wanted a central bank that would lead and direct the banks and other financial institutions, harness the economy's financial resources, and create the institutions, instruments and markets the financial system needed to grow. Each of A, B and C was one of these reasons, so D is correct."}},{"itemId":"9750e555-309e-5d06-8058-46ad9fbaf580","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"36e139c8-9374-556c-9e72-838ad0ca1f0c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0140","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"With the deregulation of the economy, exporters and other customers were allowed to keep foreign exchange in domiciliary accounts with banks in Nigeria. These domiciliary accounts are of two types, namely","options":["Import domiciliary account and export domiciliary account","Export domiciliary account and special domiciliary account","Export domiciliary account and ordinary domiciliary account","None of the above is correct"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Domiciliary accounts let customers hold foreign currency with Nigerian banks, and they come in two types: the export domiciliary account, which receives export proceeds, and the ordinary domiciliary account, which receives other foreign currency inflows. Options A and B pair the export account with categories that do not exist (import and special), so they are wrong."}},{"itemId":"28e71a97-2bdc-5972-9fc2-df278e9d5feb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1890c37e-bdb6-5d2c-9283-9590306c3b2c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0141","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the developmental functions of the Central Bank of Nigeria?","options":["Accepting savings deposits from members of the public","Insuring the deposits of customers of failed banks","Providing the financial infrastructure for the take-off of the money and capital markets","Registering companies and business names"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Beyond its traditional functions, the CBN has a developmental role, and one part of it is providing financial infrastructure: it introduced money market instruments such as treasury bills and encouraged the setting up of the Lagos Stock Exchange in 1961. Taking savings deposits from the public (A) is done by deposit money banks, insuring deposits (B) is the job of the NDIC, and registering companies (D) belongs to the Corporate Affairs Commission."}},{"itemId":"79025b8b-fe6d-5260-a145-72fc80819b3f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4bca7820-0b11-5255-8490-a8d38d70ca50","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0142","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT one of the schemes/funds established by the CBN or inspired by the CBN?","options":["The Agricultural Credit Guarantee Scheme","National Economic Reconstruction Fund","National Economic Rejuvenation Fund","Small and Medium Industries Equity Investment Scheme"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN set up or inspired the Agricultural Credit Guarantee Scheme (A), the National Economic Reconstruction Fund or NERFUND (B), and the Small and Medium Industries Equity Investment Scheme (D) to channel credit to important sectors. There has never been a 'National Economic Rejuvenation Fund'; the name is a twist on NERFUND, whose R stands for Reconstruction."}},{"itemId":"0653bec3-9886-5a4b-a5ac-d83c82faef3f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"331d4604-73ed-5e0a-8f4e-dca949f39a36","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0143","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was one of the reasons for setting up the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) in 1988?","options":["To take over the issue of currency notes and coins from the CBN","To act as banker to the Federal Government in place of the CBN","To complement the supervisory work of the CBN as the number of banks grew after financial liberalization","To fix the lending rates charged by all deposit money banks"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Liberalization in the late 1980s led to a sharp rise in the number of banks, which made supervision much harder for the CBN alone, so the NDIC was created partly to share that oversight work. Issuing currency (A) and acting as banker to the government (B) remain CBN functions, and the NDIC does not fix banks' lending rates (D)."}},{"itemId":"555ba434-af0e-588e-ad8c-7325811314d7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"431b455f-08bd-5139-a341-28ab71a85eae","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0144","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT one of the purposes for setting up the NDIC?","options":["To protect depositors, especially small depositors, in times of bank failures","To prevent the government from losing its deposits with the banks","To prevent banks from failing by coming to their aid as soon as they start to experience difficulties","In case some banks do fail, to reduce the economic dislocations and disruptions that such failure might cause"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NDIC was set up to protect depositors, especially small ones (A), to help prevent banks from failing by assisting them early (C), and to reduce the disruption caused when banks do fail (D). Protecting the government's own deposits was not one of its purposes, so B is the odd one out."}},{"itemId":"f7754242-c58f-5a80-b141-355d5772a909","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"83584b1b-f66f-5faa-8103-dd5fff427456","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0145","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement about the deposit insurance scheme run by the NDIC is correct?","options":["It guarantees that no insured bank in Nigeria will ever fail","It protects only the shareholders of insured banks","It protects depositors and also helps prevent a massive loss of confidence in the banking system","It removes the need for the CBN to supervise banks"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Deposit insurance pays depositors up to a set limit when a bank fails, and because people know their money is protected, they are less likely to panic and run on the banks. It cannot guarantee that no bank will fail (A), it is designed for depositors rather than shareholders (B), and the CBN still supervises banks alongside the NDIC (D)."}},{"itemId":"8c8f3283-67c2-527e-ade1-1b105734188e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"835689ee-6db9-5e6b-afae-437e4f676da1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0147","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best describes the working relationship between the NDIC and the CBN?","options":["The NDIC supervises the CBN and can revoke its licence","They work closely together, sometimes examining banks jointly and sharing reports of their examinations","The CBN has no role in supervising banks that are insured by the NDIC","The NDIC issues currency on behalf of the CBN"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NDIC and the CBN share responsibility for supervising banks, so they carry out joint examinations, set up joint committees for problem banks, and exchange examination reports and other information. The NDIC does not supervise the CBN (A), the CBN remains a main supervisor of insured banks (C), and only the CBN issues currency (D)."}},{"itemId":"245093b7-fafc-5773-b8d3-fa0419969859","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d64ee36e-dbe1-5129-9e91-dc60643ff796","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0148","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the flat-rate deposit insurance scheme introduced by the NDIC Decree of 1988, each insured bank was expected to pay _______ of its total deposit liabilities as annual premium to the NDIC.","options":["5%","2%","15/16 of one percent","10/16 of one percent"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"When deposit insurance started, every insured bank paid the same annual premium of 15/16 of one percent of its total deposit liabilities. Banks complained that this was too high and that a uniform rate ignored differences in risk. Rates of 5 percent (A) or 2 percent (B) would be far heavier, and 10/16 of one percent (D) is not the rate that was set."}},{"itemId":"51870cae-25c0-5fb0-9961-c2b3059828a6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9bfc0ee0-ad22-5f45-afd4-75fc97843bb8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0149","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In case of failure of a deposit money bank, each depositor used to be entitled to a maximum of ________ out of his deposit in the first instance. This figure was later raised to _______ by the year 2010.","options":["N100,000; N750,000","N150,000; N3,000,000","N50,000; N500,000","None of the above is correct"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"When the scheme began, the NDIC paid each depositor of a failed bank up to N50,000 in the first instance, with more paid later on a pro-rata basis as the bank's assets were sold. After complaints that this was too small, the cover was raised in stages and reached N500,000 by 2010; it has been raised again since then. The pairs in A and B do not match these figures."}},{"itemId":"754f4876-a1ef-5276-8d8b-27ce8a77da01","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1465ec2e-76ec-51d8-8366-bdff7beedfde","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0150","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the decree setting up the NDIC, a bank is regarded as sick (distressed) and may seek NDIC assistance when _______","options":["It cannot meet its obligations to its depositors/creditors as and when due","It has accumulated losses that have eroded or are threatening to erode shareholders' funds","Both (a) and (b) are correct","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"A bank is treated as sick if it cannot meet its obligations to depositors and creditors as they fall due, or if accumulated losses have eroded, or threaten to erode, its shareholders' funds. Either condition qualifies a bank for NDIC assistance, so C is correct; choosing only A or only B leaves one of them out."}},{"itemId":"72f1548f-5131-5021-b90c-772f99030f09","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"33880b74-dda4-5bcb-ac72-15cdff275d11","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0151","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The NDIC in its supervisory oversight is concerned with three principal things about deposit money banks, which are","options":["Capital adequacy, loan collateralization and liquidity ratio","Cash ratio, loan concentration and capital adequacy","Loan collateralization, liquidity ratio and capital adequacy","Capital adequacy, loan concentration and liquidity ratio"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"In its supervision, the NDIC pays most attention to capital adequacy (the cushion that absorbs losses), loan concentration (how much risk is tied to a few borrowers) and the liquidity ratio (the ability to meet daily obligations). Options A and C include loan collateralization and option B includes the cash ratio, which are not among the three principal concerns."}},{"itemId":"76b20450-6225-53e4-8974-847477da43c8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"95e03f07-c637-5f6b-be6a-fc125d956c65","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0152","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"That the capital adequacy ratio is 1:10 means","options":["That N1 of loans can only be made when there is N10 of capital","That N1 of capital can only support a maximum of N10 of loans","Both (a) and (b) are correct","None of the above is correct"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"A capital adequacy ratio of 1:10 means capital must be at least one-tenth of loans, so every N1 of capital can support no more than N10 of loans. Option A turns the ratio upside down by requiring N10 of capital for every N1 of loans, so C, which accepts both, is also wrong."}},{"itemId":"f1a5862e-2944-5a4c-864a-19d60be70fd8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"04505a91-9b42-57c5-96b0-28fc0014075e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0153","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT one of the distress resolution options that the NDIC has applied to distressed banks?","options":["Imposition of holding actions","Change of management","NDIC's assumption of control and management of a distressed bank","Making the CBN and the Federal Ministry of Finance buy equity in the distressed bank"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"To deal with distressed banks, the NDIC has used holding actions such as restrictions on lending and deposit advertising (A), changes of management through interim boards (B), and taking over the control and management of a sick bank itself (C), with liquidation as the last resort. Making the CBN and the Federal Ministry of Finance buy equity in a distressed bank is not one of its resolution options, so D is the answer."}},{"itemId":"b9aaf71c-05cd-5709-bf8a-d2fd333d7311","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa2dbd8e-e80b-5cf6-889c-cb738467fc7b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0154","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a criticism of the NDIC's existence and operations?","options":["That a premium of 15/16 of one percent of a bank's deposit liabilities is too large","That the lack of NDIC offices in every state of the federation is not good","That the maximum payment of N50,000 in the first instance, in case of liquidation, is too small","That banks should be made to pay premium based on the kind of risk each bank subjects its own deposit liabilities to, rather than a uniform premium"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Critics argued that the 15/16 of one percent premium was too heavy (A), that the N50,000 payout was too small (C), and that premiums should be risk-based instead of uniform (D). The lack of NDIC offices in every state was not one of the criticisms raised, so B is the answer."}},{"itemId":"045b174b-4f79-5644-b6da-79d4234625eb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"52660bfa-9b04-5aae-8505-d9f662539276","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0155","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Markets that mobilize funds from the surplus units of society (savers and lenders) to the deficit units (borrowers and investors) are called","options":["Commodity markets","Financial markets","Labour markets","Product markets"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial markets channel funds from those who have more than they currently need, the surplus units, to those who need funds to borrow and invest, the deficit units. Commodity markets (A) and product markets (D) deal in goods, and labour markets (C) deal in workers' services, not in the transfer of funds."}},{"itemId":"173b9c06-6950-53a2-ac30-ceb9816fa4c9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"79404a99-9349-5c76-8ce9-a554d0fe07cb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0156","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT a function of the financial system?","options":["Mobilization of savings and provision of liquidity","Storage of wealth and provision of payment systems","Provision of credit and protection against risk","None of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The financial system mobilizes savings, provides liquidity, stores wealth, provides payment systems, provides credit and offers protection against risk. Every item in A, B and C is therefore a genuine function, so none of them is the odd one out and D is correct."}},{"itemId":"2fb22d47-50b1-5303-b5bc-fa6e264703da","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38ca4d31-0893-5c99-9cba-8f47064e894f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0157","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The money market can be defined as","options":["The market where money is traded","The market where goods and services are exchanged without the use of money","The market for buying and selling short-term financial instruments or investible funds","None of the above is correct"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The money market is the market for buying and selling short-term financial instruments or investible funds, such as treasury bills and commercial papers. Option A sounds close but is too vague, since long-term funds are traded in the capital market instead, and B describes barter, not a money market."}},{"itemId":"11d8fc7b-d6c9-5b22-86a7-a8f1c8da2a63","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd32d1f8-ec18-51ac-9263-7884e89ac555","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0158","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The need for a money market arises out of which of the following needs?","options":["Government's need for accommodation finance to bridge the gap between its expenditure needs and its expected revenues","Firms' need for working capital purposes","Traders' need to finance their trade, sometimes on an overnight basis","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"A money market is needed because government must bridge the gap between its spending and the timing of its revenue (A), firms need short-term working capital (B), and traders need funds, sometimes overnight, to finance trade (C). All three create demand for short-term funds, so D is correct."}},{"itemId":"ccd616a0-c4aa-5a1f-b93a-f2842de64a80","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"21421442-9db5-50a7-a256-becb7660ca29","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0159","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following have operated in the Nigerian money market?","options":["The Central Bank of Nigeria and commercial, merchant and universal banks","Discount houses and specialized banks","Federal and state governments","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Many institutions have taken part in the Nigerian money market: the CBN, which brings government securities such as treasury bills to the market, and the commercial, merchant and universal banks (A), discount houses and specialized banks such as community banks (B), and federal and state governments raising short-term funds (C). Since each group is an operator, D is the correct answer."}},{"itemId":"d7f412aa-cb50-5539-9900-6d0a3705bddd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"06576868-df22-5ff8-b2f7-3cb5f8632cb6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0160","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these are NOT money market instruments?","options":["Treasury bills and treasury certificates","Commercial papers and certificates of deposit","Debentures and equities","Call-money fund and Bankers' Unit Fund"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Debentures and equities are long-term securities traded in the capital market, so they are not money market instruments. Treasury bills and certificates (A), commercial papers and certificates of deposit (B), and the call-money fund and Bankers' Unit Fund (D) have all been money market instruments in Nigeria."}},{"itemId":"2d3e1c2b-2ac0-5ac2-9e93-18e736ae21f8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"85d05cd3-d33c-56d0-8613-5c8d277d95d5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0161","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which payment device looks like a credit card but carries a memory chip that stores value and keeps a record of the transactions made with it, and has been described as a 'cashless wallet'?","options":["Travellers' cheque","Smart card","Bank draft","Cheque guarantee card"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A smart card is a plastic card with an embedded memory chip that stores value and an up-to-date record of every transaction made with it, which is why it is described as a cashless wallet. Its chip security also helps to reduce fraud and allows secure off-line payments. Options A and C are paper payment instruments, and option D only assures a payee that a cheque up to a stated limit will be honoured, so none of them stores value on a chip."}},{"itemId":"332a5e82-f97b-59ba-aac9-ccfb43d87d08","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"98cbb550-6e42-5f80-9ae5-2e1389877d17","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0162","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The money market has a lot of benefits; these include","options":["Reducing the amount of idle barren cash that people hold","Providing the Central Bank with a ready instrument for gauging the scarcity or relative availability of funds in the economy","Both (a) and (b) are correct","None of the above is correct"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"By giving individuals and banks a safe outlet for short-term surplus funds, the money market reduces the amount of idle, barren cash they hold and lets them earn some income on it. Movements in money market interest rates also give the Central Bank a ready gauge of how scarce or plentiful funds are in the economy. Since both A and B are genuine benefits, C is the right choice and D is wrong."}},{"itemId":"8f70d8e3-ecc8-55f3-a8ce-ee88d4777d06","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"44183d59-c0cd-53dc-a065-1cf03e584f5d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0163","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is not one of the major challenges faced by the Nigerian money market?","options":["Dominance of the market by government securities","Lack of market space for location of the market in some cities of Nigeria","Lack of sufficient awareness on the part of the public of the existence and benefits of a money market","Lack of adequate and effective institutional framework for ensuring probity, integrity and accountability in the market"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The money market is not a building or physical location; it is a network of banks, discount houses, the CBN and other dealers who trade short-term instruments by telephone and electronic means, so lack of market space is not one of its problems. Its real challenges include dominance by government securities (A), low public awareness of the market and its benefits (C), and a weak institutional framework for probity and accountability (D)."}},{"itemId":"dfd81def-bd9c-5f36-beb7-08c365b7bf50","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aeb0a52b-faa0-53bd-aa91-017844ea8989","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0164","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best describes the structure of the Nigerian money market?","options":["It is perfectly competitive, with many small operators and no dominant players","It has an oligopolistic structure, being dominated (apart from government) by a few large banks","It is a pure monopoly in which the CBN is the only buyer and seller of instruments","It is dominated by individual households trading treasury bills directly"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Although many types of operators take part in the Nigerian money market, it has an oligopolistic structure: apart from government, activity has long been dominated by the banks, and among them by a few big banks. Option A is wrong because having many participants does not remove the dominance of a few large players, and C is wrong because the CBN is an important participant, not the only one. D is wrong because low participation by the general public is itself one of the market's weaknesses."}},{"itemId":"86daa1e3-45b2-566e-ba25-d870eb16a35b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53f0be86-af78-5cfa-b1a5-a6421998d5a0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0165","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The capital market can be defined as","options":["The market for purchasing capital goods otherwise known as equipment and machinery","The market for raising medium- to long-term funds for investment purposes","Both (a) and (b) are correct","None of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The capital market is the financial market where medium- to long-term funds are mobilised and traded through instruments such as shares, bonds and government stocks. It is not a market for physical machinery and equipment, so option A confuses financial capital with capital goods. Because A is wrong, option C cannot be correct either."}},{"itemId":"2f46e850-7938-5009-91cf-946fcd61942d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"91070d1b-60b5-5962-8ae9-4b81f1796ff9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0168","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Two principal institutions in the Nigerian capital market are","options":["The CBN and the Nigerian Stock Exchange (NSE)","The NDIC and the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)","The NSE and the SEC","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The Securities and Exchange Commission is the apex regulator of the capital market, while the stock exchange provides the market where securities are listed, bought and sold, so the NSE and the SEC are its two principal institutions. The NSE was demutualised in 2021 and now operates as the Nigerian Exchange (NGX). Option A is wrong because the CBN's main role is in banking and the money market, and option B is wrong because the NDIC insures bank deposits rather than running the capital market."}},{"itemId":"6269e3b7-cda0-555d-9397-addf9e8f548f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7919aa2-546c-5534-977b-bfd4317959bd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0172","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which body has overall oversight of (is the apex regulator of) the Nigerian capital market?","options":["Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)","National Insurance Commission (NAICOM)","Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)","National Pension Commission (PenCom)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The Securities and Exchange Commission is the apex regulator of the Nigerian capital market: it registers securities and market operators, monitors the stock exchange and protects investors. The NDIC (A) insures deposits in banks, NAICOM (B) regulates insurance companies, and PenCom (D) regulates pension fund administrators and custodians, so none of them oversees the capital market."}},{"itemId":"e2af2854-6340-58fc-a95e-be7342821de2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f0f350cf-a545-5245-832f-23a187f25613","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0175","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is not a non-bank financial institution?","options":["Insurance companies","Pension funds","Debt Management Office","Primary Mortgage Institutions"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The Debt Management Office is a government agency that manages Nigeria's public debt; it does not mobilise funds from savers and lend them out, so it is not a financial intermediary at all. Insurance companies (A), pension funds (B) and primary mortgage institutions (D) all pool funds from the public and channel them into investment without operating as deposit money banks, which is why they are classed as non-bank financial institutions."}},{"itemId":"6793a592-1f11-541f-b558-a660d94010ef","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f9718518-94e7-519f-ab29-2e2d49de4220","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0176","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"There are two major types of insurance. These are","options":["Life insurance and animal insurance","Life insurance and non-life insurance","Life insurance and property insurance","None of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Insurance business is broadly divided into life insurance, which covers risks to human life such as death, disability or old age, and non-life (general) insurance, which covers other risks. Option C is tempting, but property insurance is only one branch of non-life insurance, which also includes liability insurance, so B is the complete answer. 'Animal insurance' in A is not a major class of insurance."}},{"itemId":"35c13ae7-e6d1-598e-8490-d4a8aaa3385e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"31e10d81-ce3e-59e2-9cc1-683322a6b30e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0177","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under a life insurance policy, the policy holder is mainly protected against financial loss to himself or his family arising from","options":["Fire or theft affecting his property","Loss of goods at sea through marine mishaps","Death, disability or old age","Legal claims against him for negligence"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Life insurance covers risks to the insured person's life, so it protects him or his dependants against financial loss from death, disability or old age, usually in return for regular premiums. Options A and B describe property insurance and option D describes liability insurance, and both of these fall under non-life (general) insurance."}},{"itemId":"431a0770-b782-583f-a8ac-496d927705bc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"be709728-d7d3-5d41-9c4b-b22e228d0adf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0178","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The National Provident Fund (NPF) was established in _______ and was later reformed into the Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund (NSITF) in _______ by the NSITF Decree No. 73","options":["1960; 1997","1961; 1993","1963; 2000","1959; 1988"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The National Provident Fund was set up in 1961 as a compulsory savings and social security scheme for private sector workers. Because of poor record-keeping and non-remittance of contributions by employers, it was replaced in 1993, under Decree No. 73, by the Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund, which took over its assets and liabilities. None of the other pairs of dates, such as 1959 and 1988 in D, matches these two events."}},{"itemId":"2530fd1f-2420-5274-9665-948bad3cd7ee","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"96f6b0fb-fc0b-5a1e-96e1-703b5ab3f0a6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0179","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When it was set up in 1993, the Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund's areas of operation included","options":["Retirement pension and survivors' pension","Retirement grant and survivors' grant","Invalidity pension and invalidity grant","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The NSITF scheme set up in 1993 was a social insurance scheme covering seven benefit areas: retirement pension, retirement grant, invalidity pension, invalidity grant, survivors' pension, survivors' grant and funeral grant. Options A, B and C each list only part of this coverage, so D is the best answer."}},{"itemId":"619c55e2-2591-56a5-b097-e087d9607978","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1e0eff1-3fea-5461-b007-74d4a61eef86","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0181","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Pension Reform Act of 2004 created a body that is supposed to take oversight of all pension institutions. This body is known as the","options":["Nigerian Pensions Board","National Pensions Board","National Pension Commission","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The Pension Reform Act of 2004 established the National Pension Commission (PenCom) to regulate and supervise pension schemes and to license and supervise pension fund administrators and custodians. Options A and B sound similar but are not the name of any body created by the Act, and D is wrong because a specific regulator was created."}},{"itemId":"20c3910c-e157-5a4c-a568-9a1459e1bd29","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2cacdc45-5c28-5b16-8b8c-c8d578be9405","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0182","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Apart from the National Pension Commission, which two types of pension institutions were created under the Pension Reform Act of 2004?","options":["Pension Fund Managers and Pension Fund Custodians","Pension Fund Consultants and Pension Fund Administrators","Pension Fund Custodians and Pension Fund Administrators","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The 2004 Act created Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs), which open and manage each employee's Retirement Savings Account and invest the contributions, and Pension Fund Custodians (PFCs), which hold the pension fund assets in safe keeping. Option A is close, but 'pension fund managers' is not the name the Act gives to these institutions, and the 'pension fund consultants' in B were not created by the Act."}},{"itemId":"e5711ca4-6a51-52a4-ad4b-04d5ff2a6f35","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"584552f0-85ef-5fbe-bcb3-6200b5f37b2e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0184","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When primary mortgage institutions (PMIs) were first set up under the Mortgage Institutions Decree No. 53 of 1989, they were answerable to (regulated and supervised by)","options":["The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN)","The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)","The Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN)","(a) and (c) above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Under the 1989 decree, the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria became the apex mortgage institution and was given regulatory and supervisory oversight of the PMIs, which provided housing finance at the retail level. The CBN (A) only took over their regulation and supervision in 1997, so A and D do not describe the original arrangement. The NDIC (B) is a deposit insurer, not the regulator that licensed the PMIs."}},{"itemId":"6a8a3dfe-d14c-564f-888d-24bbca7aafd2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5802a312-6768-540d-aeab-acd3df198fe0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0185","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is not one of the broad aims of monetary policy?","options":["Balance of payments equilibrium","High growth rate of output","High growth rate of population","Stable prices"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Monetary policy is aimed at stable prices, a high rate of growth of output, full employment and balance of payments equilibrium. A high growth rate of population is not something the monetary authorities try to achieve, so it is not a policy aim. Options A, B and D are all standard objectives of monetary policy."}},{"itemId":"37a23c91-93b5-5161-87be-fcb27570498c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"007d2710-3694-5709-865b-4dc62388e535","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0186","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Monetary policy, like fiscal policy, belongs to the group of policies called","options":["Management policies","Strategic economic policies","Economic stabilization policies","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Monetary and fiscal policies are known as economic stabilization policies because the authorities use them to bring a misbehaving economy, for example one facing high inflation or unemployment, back to a path of steady growth. 'Management policies' (A) and 'strategic economic policies' (B) are not the standard name for this group, and since C is correct, D is wrong."}},{"itemId":"7f0424dd-044f-5165-a9c9-df7983c5f443","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a88e0a7e-0663-580c-99ca-8d8cfc837b02","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0187","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Monetary policy is best defined as the policy that","options":["Uses taxation, government expenditure and the public debt to achieve macroeconomic objectives","Fixes the prices of goods and services sold in the markets","Regulates the listing and trading of shares on the stock exchange","Employs monetary instruments to influence the quantity, availability and cost of money and credit in an economy"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Monetary policy is the deliberate action of the monetary authorities, led by the CBN, to influence the quantity, availability and cost of money and credit, using instruments such as the reserve requirement, open market operations and the monetary policy rate. Option A is the definition of fiscal policy, not monetary policy. Options B and C describe price control and capital market regulation, which are different activities."}},{"itemId":"95e65720-f56e-5518-82cf-979e0dbc7dd5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27274e47-7d1f-5b5f-b984-8dccdb9b04a6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0189","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"As an objective of monetary policy, full employment means that","options":["There is a total absence of unemployment, both voluntary and involuntary","Every adult in the country is employed by the government","The central bank fixes the number of jobs in each sector of the economy","All those able and willing to work can find jobs, although some frictional and seasonal unemployment remains"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Full employment does not mean zero unemployment; it means that everyone who is able and willing to work can secure a job. Some frictional unemployment (people moving between jobs) and seasonal unemployment will always exist, so option A sets an impossible standard. Options B and C describe direct government control of jobs, which is not what the objective means."}},{"itemId":"1a4b2963-3329-5857-b954-370ddea7def5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"10606075-9f5d-5571-863f-77d70d063253","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0190","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"As an objective of monetary policy, 'stable prices' is best understood to mean that","options":["Prices of all goods and services remain exactly the same from year to year","The central bank fixes the price of every commodity by law","The exchange rate of the naira never changes","The general price level avoids wide fluctuations, changing only at a gradual rate"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Price stability means avoiding wide swings in the general price level, whether sharp rises (inflation) or continuous falls (deflation), so that prices change only gradually. Prices cannot realistically be frozen from year to year, so option A is wrong. Option B describes price control, and C refers to exchange rate stability, which is a separate goal linked to the balance of payments."}},{"itemId":"20d881f6-f858-5d0e-b16a-cc81250c4b55","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"743a5fa6-fd0f-576b-a9ab-2308b88c4fbc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0191","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is an instrument of monetary policy?","options":["Reserve requirement","Open Market Operations","Monetary Policy Rate","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN controls money and credit through several instruments: the reserve requirement (the share of deposits banks must hold as reserves), open market operations (buying and selling government securities to add or withdraw liquidity) and the monetary policy rate, which anchors other interest rates. Since A, B and C are all monetary policy instruments, D is correct."}},{"itemId":"61f0b5ca-13d1-54b0-9836-434d2d72d7cf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99dc7600-1fff-523d-87a6-55d92ec561fb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0197","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Value of money can be described as the worth of money or its _______","options":["Stock of money","Face value of money","Velocity of money","Purchasing power of money"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The value of money is what a unit of money can buy in goods and services, that is, its purchasing power; when prices rise, the value of money falls. The face value in B is just the amount printed on a banknote or coin, which stays the same even when its purchasing power changes. The stock of money (A) is the total amount of money in the economy, and velocity (C) is how fast money changes hands."}},{"itemId":"6ce46d98-d044-51f6-a9ab-99a438909479","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5ce83ea4-7cbe-5e74-a351-087a6332ca4b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0202","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The two types of monetary policy are:","options":["Expansionary and fiscal","Fiscal and contractionary","Descriptive and inferential","Expansionary and contractionary"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Monetary policy is either expansionary (easy), where the central bank increases money supply and lowers the cost of credit to stimulate activity, or contractionary (tight), where it reduces money supply and raises interest rates to curb inflation. Options A and B wrongly pair one type of monetary policy with fiscal policy, which is the government's use of taxation and spending and is a separate policy altogether. Option C, descriptive and inferential, are branches of statistics, not types of monetary policy."}},{"itemId":"bc2a4357-fda7-504c-b56f-e9151f3e8644","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cccae86e-ff84-579a-9c42-e325cb6d3950","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0206","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Who propounded the view that \"money is what money does\"?","options":["Hicks","Keynes","Fisher","Friedman"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Hicks argued that money is defined by its functions: anything that is used as money is money. This is why demand deposits, savings deposits and time deposits are counted in the money stock to the extent that they perform the functions of money. Option C, Fisher, is linked with the equation of exchange (MV = PT), while Keynes (B) and Friedman (D) are known for their theories of the demand for money, not this functional definition."}},{"itemId":"f9239cc4-dc45-51d5-b549-cd0ee2c3ebe4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"65f412ff-cfc7-5f36-9282-8a19f31a2ea5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0207","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a feature of good money?","options":["Acceptability","Homogeneity","Stability","None of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Acceptability, homogeneity and stability are all qualities that good money must have, so none of options A to C is the odd one out. Acceptability means people readily take it in payment, homogeneity means every unit of the same denomination is identical (N1 in Abuja is the same as N1 in Ibadan), and stability means its value or purchasing power does not swing widely over time. An item lacking any of these would perform poorly as money."}},{"itemId":"5d298fa4-1839-5332-9ef2-6b7461e67244","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7cd2747f-7b59-5a09-8416-a83d9d0f5619","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0208","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is not among the types of money?","options":["Durable money","Commodity money","Token money","Fiat money"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Durability is a quality that good money should possess, not a type of money, so option A is the odd one out. Commodity money (B) has value in itself as a good, such as gold or cowries, token money (C) has little or no commodity value and is accepted because people generally take it in exchange, and fiat money (D) has value because government declares it legal tender."}},{"itemId":"98786bf2-0208-526e-83f8-a7c31888c4cd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"70ceeb1e-fba6-5e4e-852f-eac2ba5150d5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0209","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"________ refers to the supply or quantity of money available in an economy","options":["Price index","Value of money","Velocity of money","Stock of money"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The stock of money, also called money supply, is the total quantity of money available in an economy at a point in time, such as currency held by the public plus deposits with banks (measured as M1, M2 and so on). Option B, the value of money, is its purchasing power, and option C, velocity, is the number of times a unit of money changes hands in a period. Option A, a price index, measures the average level of prices, not the quantity of money."}},{"itemId":"0a0a26af-6ad5-5c34-a9c3-5524b0885124","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c8d17e9e-7a0b-5414-95b4-6a792be9deb0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0210","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The value of money can be measured by the use of what?","options":["Paper index","Price index","Value index","Stock index"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The value of money is its purchasing power, and it moves inversely with the general price level. Because the general price level is measured with a price index (a weighted average of prices), the value of money is found as the reciprocal of the index; for example, if the index rises from 100 to 250, money is worth only 40 percent of its earlier value. Option C, a value index, sounds similar but tracks changes in the total value of goods (price times quantity), and option D, a stock index, tracks share prices on the stock exchange."}},{"itemId":"a5227f72-02cc-5def-8995-c89e0dd559e9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4af6077f-e412-5ab1-8cc1-a934569d41df","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0227","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"________ is a monetary measure used in controlling inflation.","options":["Surplus budgeting","Encouragement of household saving","Selective credit control","Increase in savings"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Selective credit control is a monetary tool: the central bank issues guidelines on which sectors banks may lend to, how much and at what rates, so it can restrict credit to non-priority or speculative uses and ease inflationary pressure. Option A, surplus budgeting, is a fiscal measure in which government spends less than it earns. Options B and D, encouraging or increasing savings, are general or fiscal measures rather than central bank credit instruments."}},{"itemId":"777c4a18-7fe9-550e-9df3-334a9db2f2e4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ddc89971-ddfa-555f-9ff0-d2a07e52cac1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0236","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the full meaning of FDI?","options":["Federal Development Initiative","Federation Developmental Investment","Foreign Development Initiative","Foreign Direct Investment"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"FDI stands for Foreign Direct Investment, which is investment by foreign firms or individuals that gives them a lasting ownership and control stake in businesses in another country, such as building a factory or buying a controlling share of a local company. It differs from foreign portfolio investment, where the investor only buys shares or bonds without control. Options A, B and C are not real expansions of the abbreviation."}},{"itemId":"ac745ffd-5f9a-5d75-8c1f-4b90f3d2f5fe","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"61eb8b19-7071-51c6-975a-e7686dcec7a3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0237","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"______economy does not involve the use of money","options":["Mixed Economy","Barter Economy","Traditional Economy","None of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"In a barter economy, goods and services are exchanged directly for other goods and services without any medium of exchange, which is why it suffers from the problem of double coincidence of wants. Option A, a mixed economy, combines private and public ownership and uses money, and option C, a traditional economy, is organised around custom and tradition rather than being defined by the absence of money. Option D is wrong because B fits exactly."}},{"itemId":"e724ad2a-6893-5285-baee-fae7ae7e78f9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6506e27f-5bcb-5153-8e80-d50f6a27ff9e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0238","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a non-market based instrument","options":["OMO","Rediscount rate","Legal reserve requirement","Moral suasion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Moral suasion is a qualitative, direct tool: the central bank uses persuasion and friendly appeal to get banks to act in a desired way, for example to lend more to small businesses, without working through market prices. OMO (A), the rediscount rate (B) and the legal reserve requirement (C) are quantitative instruments that work through bank reserves and the cost of funds, which is why they are grouped as market-based or indirect tools."}},{"itemId":"4b343763-70a1-53ed-a2cc-acd2aa202d49","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93ffcce5-a2ff-5f7e-9f92-c2fdac5da501","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0242","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The colonial government promulgated Nigeria's first Banking Ordinance, based on the Paton Report, in","options":["1950","1951","1952","1953"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Nigeria's first banking law was the Banking Ordinance of 1952, passed after the Paton Report looked into banking practice in the colony. It ended the era of free, unregulated banking by introducing licensing and minimum paid-up capital rules that many weak indigenous banks could not meet. Options A, B and D are wrong because no banking ordinance was passed in 1950, 1951 or 1953; the next major ordinance came in 1958, when the 1952 law was replaced."}},{"itemId":"1d76d879-5168-5cea-9042-ac808547de5b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"12b19a10-e6f4-5144-aa5a-3bf32807540a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0244","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Without money, the ________ would be inoperative.","options":["Price mechanism","Resource allocation system","Both A and B","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"In a market economy prices are quoted in money, and those money prices signal producers what to make and consumers who gets what, so the price mechanism cannot work without money. Because land, labour and capital are shared out among their uses through that same price mechanism, the allocation system in option B also depends on money. Options A and B are each only half of the answer, and D is wrong because both systems clearly depend on money."}},{"itemId":"000f749d-399b-5b71-ae6c-cf8b95119037","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"162dfad4-899e-5fbf-81b7-d5293d46d22e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0246","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The study of money, especially coins and paper currency, is called","options":["Numeric","Numerical","Numismatics","Numerixes"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Numismatics is the study and collection of money in its physical forms, such as coins, tokens and paper currency, including their history and design. Options A and B are ordinary words relating to numbers, not to money, and option D is not a real term."}},{"itemId":"c5012d97-8e12-5a53-8104-98bf26ce2bb3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0b62e87e-5124-5b75-b925-1f2854f3de60","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0247","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The total money created by the banking system is ________ related to the reserve requirement.","options":["Directly","Proportionally","Inversely","Positively"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Total money created equals the initial deposit multiplied by the money multiplier, and the multiplier is 1 divided by the reserve ratio. A higher reserve requirement therefore means a smaller multiplier and less money created, while a lower requirement means more, so the relationship is inverse. Options A, B and D all suggest that money created rises when the reserve requirement rises, which is the opposite of what happens."}},{"itemId":"a2dadbfa-6266-5016-bb10-0a12fdb9fc8c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"30577af4-60c0-52ae-bddd-41e299f80fc5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0249","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"________ is the type of money whose intrinsic value is equal to its face value.","options":["Subsidiary money","Representative money","Standard money","Metallic money"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Standard money, also called full-bodied money, is money whose value as a commodity (for example, the gold in a gold coin) is equal to the value stamped on it. Option A is wrong because subsidiary coins are small change whose face value is higher than the metal they contain. Option B is paper that represents a claim on a commodity but has little value of its own, and option D is too broad, since metallic money can be full-bodied or token."}},{"itemId":"23c95afa-413d-51fe-b53d-3295caa85b1f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f2bfc0cf-1f74-54b5-8bfc-772ff427b4ae","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0250","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The type of money that has been declared legal tender by the government but is not backed by any physical commodity is","options":["Token money","Fiat money","Commodity money","Metallic money"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Fiat money is currency that has value only because the government declares it legal tender, and it cannot be exchanged for gold or any other commodity; the naira is an example. Option A is a broader term for money whose face value is above its commodity value, and it includes convertible paper that is backed by a commodity. Options C and D are wrong because commodity and metallic money draw their value from the material they are made of."}},{"itemId":"feba4ea6-883b-57dc-9dca-ce73f60691ca","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"24ad6611-0442-5f6f-a25a-5afd7d97b84c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0253","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The following are functions of money except","options":["Unit of account","Standard of deferred payment","Measure of value","Creation of employment"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The functions of money are medium of exchange, unit of account (measure of value), store of value and standard of deferred payment. Creating employment is an economic goal that money may help to support indirectly, but it is not one of the functions of money itself. Options A, B and C are all genuine functions of money."}},{"itemId":"032c7eb9-e1fc-5066-9feb-11e8ac71b043","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2a85a864-48e9-5387-95af-5d8e24cbbf6e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0256","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the total money (deposits) created by the banking system is N240 million from an initial deposit of N15 million, the reserve requirement ratio is","options":["16%","8%","6.25%","12.5%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Total money created equals the initial deposit divided by the reserve ratio, so the reserve ratio equals the initial deposit divided by the total: N15 million / N240 million = 0.0625, or 6.25%. Option A comes from confusing the money multiplier (240 / 15 = 16) with the ratio itself. Options B and D would give totals of N187.5 million and N120 million, not N240 million."}},{"itemId":"d6c52670-8cfb-5c0c-92b6-cb3c761ec1ee","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9d70c980-4ab5-5a41-8445-dd7eeb3dca48","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0257","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If a reserve requirement of 6.25% is increased by 100%, determine the total money (deposits) created by the banking system from an initial deposit of N15 million.","options":["N240 million","N180 million","N120 million","N480 million"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A 100% increase doubles the reserve requirement from 6.25% to 12.5%, so the money multiplier falls to 1 / 0.125 = 8, and N15 million x 8 = N120 million. Option A is the total at the old 6.25% ratio, and option D wrongly doubles the total instead of halving it. A higher reserve requirement always reduces the money the banks can create."}},{"itemId":"5da9afb3-8c9a-500b-a2e5-d5980394f88e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a8ffa6a2-f598-58ea-8985-c6c00f7bb593","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0258","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If an initial deposit of N15 million is decreased by 25%, what is the total money (deposits) created by the banking system with a reserve requirement of 6.25%?","options":["N240 million","N180 million","N120 million","N300 million"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A 25% fall leaves an initial deposit of N11.25 million, and with a 6.25% reserve ratio the multiplier is 1 / 0.0625 = 16, so total money created is N11.25 million x 16 = N180 million. Option A is the total from the original N15 million deposit, and option D wrongly treats the fall in the deposit as a rise. Option C would need a 12.5% reserve ratio on the full N15 million."}},{"itemId":"c12ca09a-7acf-56d8-a108-e1a722894b1c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b518ee95-e858-5266-af0a-1e26211429b9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0259","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If an initial deposit of N15 million is decreased by 25% and a reserve requirement of 6.25% is increased by 100%, determine the total money (deposits) created by the banking system.","options":["N240 million","N180 million","N120 million","N90 million"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The deposit falls to N15 million x 0.75 = N11.25 million and the reserve ratio doubles to 12.5%, so the multiplier is 1 / 0.125 = 8 and total money created is N11.25 million x 8 = N90 million. Option B applies only the smaller deposit and keeps the old 6.25% ratio, while option C applies only the higher ratio to the full N15 million. Both changes reduce credit creation, so the answer must be lower than either of those."}},{"itemId":"ccca9e5b-9631-5fbb-ab1c-f95708e881f3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"68f00571-9480-532b-baa8-162bff80b81e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0261","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"________ is more or less an appeal by the Central Bank to the commercial banks to behave in certain ways.","options":["Special deposits","Special directives","Moral suasion","Open market operations (OMO)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Moral suasion is the use of persuasion, friendly appeals and meetings by the Central Bank to get banks to act voluntarily in a desired way, for example to lend more to small businesses. Option B is wrong because directives are formal instructions the banks must obey, not appeals. Option A requires banks to lodge funds with the Central Bank, and option D is the buying and selling of government securities."}},{"itemId":"7c83b62a-a275-53a9-b61a-fc2e3e9ff8ea","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"833f359d-a77c-5ae9-aba5-066ae3d35651","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0262","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The buying and selling of securities by the Central Bank in order to control the volume of money in the economy is known as","options":["Moral suasion","Open market operations (OMO)","Discount rate","Cash reserve ratio"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Open market operations are the Central Bank's purchase or sale of government securities: selling them mops up money from the system, while buying them injects money. Option A works through persuasion rather than trading securities, option C is the interest rate the Central Bank charges when it lends to banks, and option D is the share of deposits banks must keep as cash reserves."}},{"itemId":"1b083f13-4ba5-5ae7-8a7b-d5109317ec12","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"51c89833-4fe5-5953-acf4-cdad74d144b8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0263","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The discount rate is also known as the","options":["Liquidity rate","Cash reserve ratio","Special deposits","Bank rate"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The discount rate, the rate at which the Central Bank lends to or rediscounts bills for commercial banks, is traditionally called the bank rate. Option B is the proportion of deposits banks must hold as reserves, and option C refers to funds banks are required to lodge with the Central Bank, so neither is an interest rate. Option A is not a standard name for this rate."}},{"itemId":"f56ae27d-d2db-509e-bc0c-6ba5d8605629","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5424915f-8a51-5d92-af4b-8c04b516c764","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0267","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The rate at which the Central Bank charges interest to commercial banks and other financial institutions for discounting bills of exchange or lending to them is known as the","options":["Bank/discount rate","Bank/money rate","Discount/cash rate","Cash/money rate"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"When the Central Bank acts as lender of last resort, the interest it charges for lending to banks or discounting their bills is called the bank rate or discount rate. Options B, C and D pair the term with names such as \"money rate\" or \"cash rate\", which are not used for this rate. Raising the bank rate makes borrowing from the Central Bank dearer, while lowering it makes borrowing cheaper."}},{"itemId":"25d9bd67-3a6a-5200-a868-a38488e49168","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"97d03032-4428-58ce-acd0-6d51f074c2d2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0268","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the CBN wants to pump money into the economy, the discount rate will be","options":["Increased","Reduced","Inflated","Deposited"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Lowering the discount rate makes it cheaper for banks to borrow from the CBN, so they can lend to the public at lower rates, which increases credit and the money supply. Option A is the opposite policy, used when the CBN wants to reduce the money in circulation. Options C and D do not describe any change that can be made to an interest rate."}},{"itemId":"1b8ff828-ab39-5c8b-bd85-8fa4c312db88","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f87b68c2-92a7-5ee0-98a6-cd8393769ac4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0270","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The three basic types of financial institutions in any financial system are","options":["Regulatory bodies, financial institutions and insurance companies.","Regulatory and supervisory institutions, banking financial institutions and insurance companies.","Regulatory and supervisory institutions, banking financial institutions and non-banking financial institutions.","All of the above."],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A financial system is made up of bank financial institutions (such as commercial and merchant banks), non-bank financial institutions (such as insurance companies, pension funds and finance houses), and the regulatory and supervisory institutions that oversee them (such as the CBN and NDIC). Options A and B are wrong because insurance companies are only one example of non-bank financial institutions, not a separate basic type. Option D cannot be right because A and B are incorrect."}},{"itemId":"20b99e56-574c-51d8-9e68-7e5ce210a15d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"73002d01-2545-57cd-bd55-380efdb96a65","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0271","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before the advent of the monetary economy, there was in existence a","options":["Fiscal system","Barter system","Speculative system","Scarce system"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Before money came into use, people exchanged goods and services directly for other goods and services, which is the barter system. Barter's problems, such as the need for a double coincidence of wants and the difficulty of storing value, led to the development of money. Option A refers to government taxing and spending, and options C and D are not systems of exchange."}},{"itemId":"41aa09ed-dec7-5604-8886-e277d28d47e7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e7a689a-b212-5bf5-a88b-3709d3497cdd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0273","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What general name is given to the institutions whose role is to move funds from surplus units to deficit units?","options":["Financial intermediaries","Insurance companies","Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN)","Merchant banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial intermediaries are the institutions that collect funds from savers (surplus units) and channel them to borrowers and investors (deficit units), and this is their defining role. Options B, C and D each name one particular institution or group, so none of them is the general name for the whole class. The question asks for the overall term, which is financial intermediaries."}},{"itemId":"d9a64bb8-b4d1-56df-afb6-8bd2f5f93451","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f0b83b2e-3014-5e03-8386-ab19e170b490","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0274","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Treasury bills, treasury certificates, commercial papers, certificates of deposit and bankers' acceptances are instruments traded in which market?","options":["Capital market","Stock market","Money market","Product market"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"All of these are short-term instruments, usually maturing within a year, and the market for short-term funds and instruments is the money market. Options A and B deal in long-term securities such as shares and bonds, and option D is a market for goods and services rather than financial instruments."}},{"itemId":"7b65f226-2911-5632-b69d-4189f7f510de","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"71f6968e-3e02-52b7-993b-6d6809252a35","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0275","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The full name of NEXIM is","options":["National Export and Import Bank","National Export and International Bank","Nigerian Export-Import Bank","Nigerian Emergency and Export/Import Management"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"NEXIM is the Nigerian Export-Import Bank, a development finance institution that provides credit, guarantees and insurance to support Nigerian exporters and the import of essential inputs. Options A and B wrongly begin with \"National\", and option B also replaces \"Import\" with \"International\". Option D is not the name of any bank."}},{"itemId":"1da267de-198d-50a7-9f91-e7a141baa0eb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aa7935d0-6866-57d4-b832-d9815650f53b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0276","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The full meaning of NSITF is","options":["Nigerian Social Insurance and Technical Fund","National Social Insurance and Technical Fund","Nigerian Societal Insurance and Trust Fund","Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"NSITF stands for the Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund, a non-bank financial institution set up in 1993 to take over the old National Provident Fund and provide social insurance for workers. Options A and B wrongly expand the \"T\" as \"Technical\", and option C wrongly uses \"Societal\" instead of \"Social\"."}},{"itemId":"05f29421-e817-5531-b3cf-e59533cc0c8c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a13e4e02-aec7-530c-a919-058b657c5f47","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0277","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigeria's early banking history, the operations of the African Banking Corporation in Lagos were taken over by which bank?","options":["Barclays Bank DCO","Bank of British West Africa","British and French Bank","Colonial Bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The African Banking Corporation was one of the first banks in colonial Lagos, but it ran into difficulties and its operations were taken over by the Bank of British West Africa, which later became Standard Bank and then First Bank of Nigeria. Option A grew out of the Colonial Bank (option D) and later became Union Bank, while option C later became the United Bank for Africa. None of these took over the African Banking Corporation."}},{"itemId":"50427daa-5233-5072-a0f3-10ef6da61e3b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0d9f4f19-9b42-51a3-aa2a-27ecebefe1e7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0279","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"All these are monetary policy measures except","options":["Open market operations","Moral suasion","Budgeting and taxation","Discount rate"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Budgeting and taxation are tools of fiscal policy, which is handled by the government through its revenue and spending decisions, not by the Central Bank. Open market operations (A), moral suasion (B) and the discount rate (D) are all instruments the CBN uses to influence money supply and credit."}},{"itemId":"5bef4e99-0697-53dd-acfa-ff8cc7419135","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd361573-e574-5e5f-9d01-c27fd45c446b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0280","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A suitable policy to restore equilibrium in an economy during an economic recession is","options":["Expansionary monetary policy","Contractionary monetary policy","Exchange policy","Financial policy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"In a recession spending and investment are too low, so the Central Bank eases monetary policy, for example by lowering rates or buying securities, to increase credit and encourage spending. Option B would reduce money and credit and deepen the downturn, so it is used against excess demand and inflation instead. Options C and D are vague labels rather than a specific policy stance for fighting a recession."}},{"itemId":"4e6a01e4-f515-57f1-93c8-b3b69c584075","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"002a118d-6424-516e-be55-f0dcbe107905","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0281","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given an initial deposit of N5,000 and a reserve ratio of 25%, what is the maximum total deposit that the banking system can create from it?","options":["N200,000","N20,000","N2,000","N50,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"With a 25% reserve ratio, each N1 of fresh reserves can support N1 / 0.25 = N4 of deposits, so N5,000 x 4 = N20,000 in total deposits (the original N5,000 plus N15,000 of new deposits created through lending). Option D (N50,000) would only follow from a 10% reserve ratio, and option A (N200,000) from a 2.5% ratio. Option C is smaller than what the deposit multiplier gives."}},{"itemId":"1943afb3-b09e-53dd-9de7-58c56db0972f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e5560b4f-ef21-5acc-bd18-db1c2e211ce0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0282","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If commercial banks keep a reserve ratio of 25%, what is the bank deposit multiplier?","options":["2.6","4","0.25","25%"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The deposit (credit) multiplier is the reciprocal of the reserve ratio: 1 / 0.25 = 4, so every N1 of new reserves can support up to N4 of deposits. Options C (0.25) and D (25%) simply restate the reserve ratio itself rather than the multiplier. Option A (2.6) does not follow from the formula at all."}},{"itemId":"fb8346e5-b259-51b8-91c6-cdcd92093cba","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c402d544-843f-5813-9c8e-5f35735447c6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0283","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The acquisition of capital assets to enhance productivity is called","options":["Capital appreciation","Capital formation","Capital structure","Capital rationing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Capital formation means adding to the stock of real capital assets such as machinery, buildings and equipment so that the economy can produce more, and banks support it by channelling savings into loans for investment. Option A, capital appreciation, is a rise in the value of an asset you already own. Option C, capital structure, is the mix of debt and equity a firm uses, and option D, capital rationing, is limiting investment because funds are scarce."}},{"itemId":"06bf5d70-3144-543e-b80c-b41d13f030b6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"97602290-6a7b-5284-bd6c-3e19c7e71258","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0284","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A persistent rise in the general price level of commodities over a period of time is","options":["Inflation","Deflation","Stagflation","None of the above"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Inflation is a persistent rise in the general level of prices over time, which means each naira buys less than before. Option B, deflation, is the opposite: a sustained fall in the general price level. Option C, stagflation, is a special situation where rising prices come together with stagnant output and high unemployment, so it is narrower than the general definition asked for here."}},{"itemId":"542b4735-4ab9-598e-bdfb-46c0db5be57a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0df12b2a-227c-5925-84ba-70072e050590","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0286","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Community banks in Nigeria came into existence as a result of the","options":["Community Banks Decree of 1946","Community Banks Decree No. 46 of 1946","Community Banks Decree No. 46 of 1992","Community Banks Decree No. 92 of 1992"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Community banks were given their legal framework by the Community Banks Decree No. 46 of 1992, to bring small-scale banking to rural communities and micro-entrepreneurs who were not served by the big banks. Options A and B wrongly date the law to 1946, long before community banking existed in Nigeria. Option D has the right year but the wrong decree number."}},{"itemId":"413fbb91-14d0-51e8-a28f-f04a0a49ed6f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"258f68fd-7d64-5370-927e-a0d6280735fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0287","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"_________ banks were designed mainly to take care of the urban poor and the rural masses in order to empower them, by making funds available to them in very small amounts.","options":["Specialized banks","Specific banks","Special banks","Specified banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Specialised banks are set up to serve a particular sector or group rather than the general public. In Nigeria, the People's Bank, community banks and microfinance banks make up the group of specialised banks aimed at the urban poor and rural dwellers, giving them access to very small loans. Options B, C and D are not recognised categories of banks; they are just similar-sounding words."}},{"itemId":"8a9807cc-b379-5a34-abc6-cdd69e055ee7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae475525-61c2-5328-9d5a-e2ae61659668","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0288","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank of Nigeria was established in the year ____ and started operation in the year ____","options":["1956; 1957","1956; 1956","1957; 1958","1958; 1959"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Central Bank of Nigeria was created by the CBN Act of 1958 and opened for business on 1 July 1959, so establishment and operations fall in different years. Option C is tempting because it also shows a one-year gap, but both of its dates are a year too early. Options A and B place the bank's creation in 1956, before the CBN Act was even passed."}},{"itemId":"2595faf2-ba4a-5d31-af78-d91746317060","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1498b6bb-cbbf-5e89-a197-c34bae4815d3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0289","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) was established in the year _____","options":["1968","1978","1988","1998"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NDIC was set up in 1988 by Decree No. 22 of that year to insure deposits and protect depositors in licensed banks, and it started operations in 1989. Options A and B are years before any deposit insurance agency existed in Nigeria. Option D, 1998, is a decade too late."}},{"itemId":"26e27695-358e-5ab0-a24b-db53d1a8a239","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7b1f0b89-4137-56bf-be07-2bf819012c7e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0291","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The market that deals in the buying and selling of short-term financial instruments is the","options":["Capital market","Short term market","Money market","Financial market"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The money market is the market for short-term funds and instruments, usually with maturities of one year or less, such as treasury bills, commercial papers and bankers' acceptances. Option A, the capital market, deals in long-term securities like shares and bonds. Option D, financial market, is the broad umbrella covering both, so it is less precise, and 'short term market' in option B is not the standard name."}},{"itemId":"ba989cdc-94a0-595b-98ce-c4addc5d9898","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"afaa0034-5fe1-525d-afc8-416cbcbf278a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0292","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The shortest standard maturity period of a Nigerian treasury bill is","options":["81 days","61 days","91 days","71 days"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury bills are short-term Federal Government securities sold at a discount, and their classic and shortest tenor is 91 days (longer issues run for 182 and 364 days). Options A, B and D (81, 61 and 71 days) are not standard treasury bill maturities."}},{"itemId":"d6c549d2-a903-5e52-a64f-29ba8b7e40a8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf1e5080-d2e3-53d2-96dc-fdd1004d509d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0293","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main limitation of a barter economy is _________","options":["Inadequate storage of value","Exchange for cows at the expense of wheat","Double coincidence of wants","All of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Barter needs a double coincidence of wants: you must find someone who has what you want and who also wants what you have, which makes trade slow and difficult. Option A, the difficulty of storing value, is also a drawback of barter, but it is not the main one. Option D cannot be right because option B is not a genuine limitation of barter."}},{"itemId":"5a2490fd-d6b1-5aff-beda-527427679aac","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0f53103c-ae31-58ac-a86f-414c027e1c27","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0294","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When, for each good, people know how many units of the object called money must be exchanged for it, money is performing its function as a ________","options":["Medium of exchange","Unit of account","Store of value","Standard of deferred payment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"As a unit of account, money is the common measure in which the value of every good is expressed as a price, so people know how many naira a good is worth and can compare values easily. Option A, medium of exchange, is about using money to make payments. Option C, store of value, is about holding money to spend later, and option D is about settling debts in the future."}},{"itemId":"f27fceaa-ab9f-5c43-be75-d53d96daa845","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"762b616f-1ab1-5daf-bd6e-f81e59aaf66d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0296","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"M2 equals","options":["Cash + quasi money","M1 + cash + quasi money","M1 + quasi money","M1 + bank deposits"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"M2, or broad money, is narrow money (M1, which is currency in circulation plus demand deposits) plus quasi money, that is, savings and time deposits. Option B double counts cash, since cash is already part of M1. Option A leaves out demand deposits, and option D is too vague because demand deposits at banks are already inside M1."}},{"itemId":"db99b583-406c-579e-94a2-7c225292792f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8585e3e2-faaf-5b11-b102-8dbeffc8cebc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0302","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The NDIC in its supervisory oversight of banks is concerned with one of the following","options":["fiscal policy","printing of money","monitoring the balance of payments of the Nigerian economy","liquidity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"In supervising insured banks, the NDIC focuses on their soundness, especially capital adequacy, loan concentration and liquidity, because a bank must be able to pay depositors as their claims fall due. Option A, fiscal policy, is handled by the Federal Government. Options B and C, issuing currency and watching the balance of payments, are responsibilities of the CBN, not the NDIC."}},{"itemId":"c0d525fa-bf2b-54cd-bdb5-dc3fb3457153","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b299a555-8f73-5114-9987-51ededad5c34","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0304","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Industrial and Commercial Bank, widely regarded as the first indigenous bank in Nigeria, was established in","options":["1894","1929","1930","1932"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Industrial and Commercial Bank was set up in Lagos in 1929 as the first bank owned by Nigerians, but it collapsed soon after. Option C, 1930, is the year it failed, not the year it was founded. Option A, 1894, belongs to the era of the foreign-owned Bank of British West Africa, and option D, 1932, does not match the founding date."}},{"itemId":"e64692da-5591-58a9-9da5-a1ae989f5e92","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c258ce27-3ff2-5b5b-9353-e39ee4863469","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0305","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The foreign bank that began operating in Nigeria as the Colonial Bank is now known as","options":["First Bank Plc","GTB Plc","Union Bank Plc","UBA Plc"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Colonial Bank came to Nigeria during the First World War period, later became Barclays Bank DCO and then Barclays Bank of Nigeria, and is known today as Union Bank of Nigeria Plc. Option A, First Bank, grew out of the Bank of British West Africa, the earlier foreign bank. Option D, UBA, grew out of the British and French Bank, and GTB in option B is a much newer bank."}},{"itemId":"3f7bc83a-5bd5-5752-ad07-6153bbf025d6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"466efa02-236f-5ad9-82de-632b6804be80","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0306","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"All these are money market instruments except","options":["treasury bills","bankers' acceptances","bonds","commercial papers"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Bonds are long-term debt securities, so they belong to the capital market rather than the money market. Treasury bills, bankers' acceptances and commercial papers (options A, B and D) are all short-term instruments, usually maturing within a year, which is exactly what the money market deals in."}},{"itemId":"bc44ce36-953c-540c-a355-ea8f15c448b4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f29dcdb2-79f5-59fd-923c-2d8b49f2378d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0307","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The existence of both unorganized and organized financial markets side by side in developing economies is known as","options":["financial liberalisation","financial stabilization","financial dualism","financial integration"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial dualism describes an economy where a formal, regulated financial sector (banks, insurance firms, the capital market) operates side by side with an informal one such as esusu, ajo and local moneylenders, as is common in Nigeria. Option D, financial integration, is the opposite idea of the sectors being merged into one linked system. Option A, financial liberalisation, means removing controls on interest rates and entry into banking."}},{"itemId":"d74a7480-987b-5c97-b0d8-b756043e043c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5f0bc536-58e4-5033-9e29-3b8e2069f91b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0308","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The apex institution of the Nigerian money market is the","options":["NDIC","CBN","SEC","NAICOM"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The Central Bank of Nigeria sits at the top of the money market: it regulates the market, issues treasury bills for the government and acts as lender of last resort to banks. Option A, the NDIC, insures deposits, option C, the SEC, regulates the capital market, and option D, NAICOM, regulates insurance companies."}},{"itemId":"56cf9cc4-7e24-5397-bb01-52dd8efaddb5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"64c672e4-a493-5c96-8e48-a0985d04be86","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0311","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Wema Bank Plc, which began as Agbonmagbe Bank, was established in","options":["1944","1945","1946","1947"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Wema Bank started life in 1945 as Agbonmagbe Bank, one of the indigenous banks that sprang up in the post-war banking boom, and it is still operating today under the Wema name. Options A, C and D are nearby years from the same period of indigenous banking, but they are not the year Agbonmagbe Bank was founded."}},{"itemId":"870fedb0-6202-5d65-ae4e-f74f74ae56a5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c2ad7a71-68bc-5be9-b954-90f563b8d224","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0312","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"________ is the only market segment where capital formation occurs.","options":["capital market","money market","secondary market","primary market"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The primary market is where new shares and bonds are sold for the first time, so the money raised goes straight to the issuing company or government to fund new investment, which is capital formation. Option C, the secondary market, only moves existing securities between investors, so no new capital is raised. Option A is partly true because the capital market contains the primary market, but it also includes secondary trading, so it is not the only segment where capital formation happens."}},{"itemId":"45e10e02-cf52-59ec-a9be-482b59706329","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b62529b-9366-5d6d-9888-48f5723c37cb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0313","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"One of these is NOT a distress resolution option of the NDIC","options":["imposition of holding actions","moral suasion","change of account","change of management"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"When a bank is in distress, the NDIC working with the CBN can use options such as moral suasion (dialogue to persuade the owners and managers to change their practices), imposition of holding actions and change of management, so 'change of account' is the one that is not a resolution option. Option B is tempting because moral suasion is best known as a CBN monetary policy tool, but it is also used in dealing with sick banks."}},{"itemId":"7bcb73db-95e8-510d-8c39-9cf04c1e0b7b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d86bd93-dd70-5c01-9d19-d8a791c75689","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0314","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The law establishing the Central Bank of Nigeria (the CBN Act) was passed in","options":["1960","1952","1958","1959"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Central Bank of Nigeria was established by the CBN Act of 1958 and then began operations on 1 July 1959. Option D, 1959, is tempting, but it is the year the bank started work, not the year the law creating it was passed. Options A and B are not linked to the CBN's creation; 1960, for example, is the year of independence."}},{"itemId":"7f850662-bd6f-5ccc-94f8-7224cfbb8ab9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a6f1d2e-2979-5b57-a919-015537890d24","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0317","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The system of exchange that requires a double coincidence of wants is known as","options":["the gold standard","the barter practice","the commodity money system","the gold exchange standard"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Barter is the direct exchange of goods for goods without money, so every trade needs a double coincidence of wants. Under a commodity money system (option C), one generally accepted item such as cowries serves as money, which removes that problem. The gold standard and gold exchange standard (options A and D) are money systems linked to gold, so they do not need a double coincidence either."}},{"itemId":"1eb50201-c62b-5b8e-ad77-70a274cdf065","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3f949e72-6d7c-5f3d-85a2-ea6466a6000e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0318","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The value of money varies","options":["inversely with the price level","directly with the volume of employment","directly with the price level","directly with the interest rate"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The value of money is what it can buy, so when the general price level rises each naira buys less and its value falls, and when prices fall its value rises. That inverse relationship is exactly why option C, which says the value moves in the same direction as prices, is wrong. Options B and D link the value of money to employment and interest rates, which do not define its purchasing power."}},{"itemId":"9562e9c7-47e9-5573-9664-6a22bb174316","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd62b0bd-e364-5386-a83e-a41ed79cf5e1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0320","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The most essential attribute of a good currency, without which it cannot function as money at all, is","options":["scarcity","homogeneity","acceptability","cognizability"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Acceptability is the most essential quality of money: if people will not take something in payment for goods and debts, it cannot work as money however scarce, uniform or recognisable it is. Scarcity, homogeneity and cognizability (options A, B and D) are also desirable qualities of good money, but they only matter once the item is generally accepted."}},{"itemId":"0afa8f57-c39a-56df-b161-fa0fb599fe17","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b4480c77-19da-54fc-9957-847a0ecaeafa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0321","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The value of money is generally measured by reference to the","options":["interest rate charged by banks","general price level","size of a country's gold stock","level of economic development"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The value of money is its purchasing power, that is, how much in goods and services a given amount of money can buy. It moves inversely with the general price level: when prices in general rise, each naira buys less, so the value of money falls. Option A, the interest rate, is the price of borrowing money rather than a measure of what money can buy, and Option C mattered only when currencies were tied to gold."}},{"itemId":"ae88bc9f-03a7-5342-b3ec-57c85b11c192","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fb30da1c-bbbb-51db-97eb-4ac5dca84a62","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0322","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Banks can create more money by","options":["increasing their cash ratio with the central bank","issuing more bank charges","accepting more deposits from customers","lending out more money from customers' deposits"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Banks create money by lending: when a bank lends out part of a deposit, the loan is spent and redeposited in the banking system, creating new deposits that can be lent again, so total deposits grow to a multiple of the original cash. Option A has it backwards, because a higher cash ratio leaves banks less to lend and reduces credit creation. Option C, accepting deposits, gives banks something to lend from, but by itself it only turns cash into deposits; new money appears when those deposits are lent out."}},{"itemId":"5ca8125c-92b2-5b6a-a29a-7e090fa74c44","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45f78029-ac91-5679-ac57-f3b3bbd29183","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0323","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"To perform its function as a store of wealth and standard for deferred payment, money must be","options":["portable","in attractive form","stable in value","spent more on capital goods than on consumer goods"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"To work as a store of wealth and a standard of deferred payment, money must keep its value over time, so that what is saved or owed today is worth about the same when it is spent or repaid later. Portability (Option A) matters for money as a medium of exchange, not for storing value, and an attractive form (Option B) has nothing to do with these functions. Option D describes spending patterns in the economy, not a quality of money."}},{"itemId":"3e44c476-6fee-5648-b90a-dcc3c9254ffa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"611a2e7b-00d1-5ee5-a83c-c0b45b7c5eaf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0324","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The best way to reduce the supply of money in the economy is to","options":["increase the liquidity ratio","increase the number of bank notes and cheques produced","decrease treasury bills","liberalise access to credit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Raising the liquidity ratio forces banks to hold a larger share of their deposits in cash and other liquid assets, leaving less to lend, so credit creation and the money supply shrink. Producing more currency and cheques (Option B) and liberalising access to credit (Option D) would expand the money supply, not reduce it. Option C is also wrong, because selling fewer treasury bills leaves more money in the hands of banks and the public."}},{"itemId":"fe11da1f-4cf7-5970-8f9d-5baa0c7d418b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"66352db8-ed7f-5c38-b04f-38e1c54dd41a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0325","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The lender of last resort in the banking system is the","options":["last resort bank","commercial bank","central bank","mortgage bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The central bank is the lender of last resort: when a basically sound bank faces a temporary cash shortage and cannot borrow elsewhere, the central bank provides emergency funds to stop the problem spreading. In Nigeria this role belongs to the Central Bank of Nigeria. Commercial and mortgage banks (Options B and D) are among the institutions that may need such help, and Option A is not a real type of bank."}},{"itemId":"e0129f53-b3be-5d9b-85ce-dac083692ec0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8e73d2ac-7eda-5f4e-9970-b08f373771e0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0326","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The most liquid asset of a bank is _______","options":["money at call","bank draft","money order","cash"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Cash is the most liquid asset a bank can hold, because it is already money and can meet withdrawals immediately with no delay or loss of value. Money at call (Option A) is very liquid, since it can be recalled at short notice, but it still has to be called back before it can be used, so it ranks just below cash. Bank drafts and money orders (Options B and C) are payment instruments rather than the liquid assets a bank keeps to meet its obligations."}},{"itemId":"96e0d5d9-55aa-5e0b-8d6b-c52c0ef6b9a6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a8fd653-289b-543e-9b39-263fb7bd0357","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0328","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The drawer of a cheque is the","options":["person who is to be paid","bank on which the cheque is drawn","person who writes out the cheque","bank official who certifies the payment"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"The drawer of a cheque is the person who writes and signs it, ordering his bank to pay a sum of money. The person who is to be paid (Option A) is the payee, and the bank on which the cheque is drawn (Option B) is the drawee. Option D does not describe any of the parties to a cheque."}},{"itemId":"29b897bc-4883-5f7e-8e08-1e03b1bfab0e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"db2be036-03e7-596a-b641-a4939bb14ecf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0329","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following statements is correct? The Central Bank","options":["prints the country's currency","handles the collection of debts owed to the government","lends money to businessmen","issues the country's currency"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Central Bank of Nigeria has the sole legal authority to issue the country's currency and coins and to control their circulation. Option A is the common trap: the CBN designs and issues the naira, but the actual printing and minting are contracted out to specialised printing companies. Lending to businessmen (Option C) is the work of commercial banks, and Option B is not how the CBN serves the government; it acts as the government's banker and manages its borrowing rather than collecting debts owed to it."}},{"itemId":"a2b0ac54-cf5b-5302-b784-ce905876c889","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"905f24ff-0a7b-5fd6-bed2-960ffab89187","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0340","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Money can simply be referred to as a","options":["medium of exchange","means for the settlement of debts","durable assets for doing business","standard of value"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"In its simplest form, money is defined as anything that is generally acceptable as a medium of exchange, that is, accepted by everyone in payment for goods and services. Settling debts (Option B) and serving as a standard of value (Option D) are genuine functions of money, but they belong to the fuller definition, while the simplest one rests on the medium of exchange role. Option C is wrong because money is not defined as a durable business asset."}},{"itemId":"20e22da2-33af-5efe-8f30-9f31d7cb4450","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"722081d8-d2e2-501b-91b6-3f58dbd2444b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0342","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Public debt management is one of the functions of","options":["Merchant Bank","Central Bank","Commercial Bank","Development Bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Managing the public debt is a central bank function that comes from its role as banker to the government. The Central Bank of Nigeria helps the government borrow by issuing treasury bills and bonds, keeps the register of government debt and handles repayments. Merchant, commercial and development banks (Options A, C and D) may buy government securities, but they do not manage the government's debt."}},{"itemId":"5e964390-4f6f-5587-8ef4-5c50f0c7091f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"665d0b4b-8579-50f6-8804-6aca8b7c099a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0345","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A form of money that has gone out of use is","options":["commodity money","bank money","paper money","foreign money"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Commodity money, such as cowries, salt or other goods that had value in themselves and were also used in exchange, has gone out of use in modern economies. Paper money (Option C) and bank money, that is, deposits paid out by cheque or transfer (Option B), are the main forms of money today. Foreign money (Option D) is still widely used, especially in international trade."}},{"itemId":"cab0acde-bcd6-57e0-9f98-bf4d0b80002a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3033a34b-61c4-5637-a8db-98baa370d78f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0347","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A market where new shares are traded is known as","options":["primary market","stock market","secondary market","money market"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The primary market is the part of the capital market where new shares are issued and sold for the first time, so the money raised goes to the issuing company. The secondary market (Option C) is for buying and selling shares that already exist. Stock market (Option B) is a general term for share trading as a whole, and the money market (Option D) deals in short-term instruments, not shares."}},{"itemId":"2fb7c272-8868-559d-b8b6-d71771dca65b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"163f9e78-c9b7-520a-9820-edf8bf96d060","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0351","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The former name of United Bank for Africa (UBA) was","options":["Agbonmagbe Bank","Barclays Bank","British and French Bank","Standard Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"United Bank for Africa was formed to take over the business of the British and French Bank, so that is its former name. Barclays Bank (Option B) is the forerunner of Union Bank of Nigeria, Agbonmagbe Bank (Option A) became Wema Bank, and Standard Bank of Nigeria (Option D) is a former name of First Bank of Nigeria."}},{"itemId":"715c95b5-317e-5d44-a396-f126d10c8b5f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd3d55cd-1e36-5286-9234-951727288ac8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0352","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The former name of Wema bank was","options":["British and French Bank","Bank of British West Africa","Barclays Bank","Agbonmagbe Bank"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Wema Bank began as Agbonmagbe Bank, an indigenous bank founded in the Western Region that was later renamed Wema Bank. The Bank of British West Africa (Option B) is the forerunner of First Bank of Nigeria, Barclays Bank (Option C) became Union Bank of Nigeria, and the British and French Bank (Option A) became United Bank for Africa."}},{"itemId":"2ff70728-61c8-5a51-9484-42701ba9a88b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4aea47e7-0e91-50ef-af51-9a4b8402b9c5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0354","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Reasons for the collapse of many early indigenous banks in Nigeria during the period of unregulated (free) banking include the following EXCEPT","options":["poor management","aggressive competition","lack of regulatory authority","none of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"All three of the listed factors were genuine reasons why so many early indigenous banks collapsed, so none of them is an exception and D is correct. Many of these banks were poorly managed and undercapitalised, they faced aggressive competition from larger and better-established expatriate banks, and there was no regulatory authority to set standards or supervise them before banking legislation came in. Choosing A, B or C would wrongly suggest that one of these was not a cause of the failures."}},{"itemId":"b0576d18-35ca-5a7b-a7b0-17a3b0d2c815","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d1f505d7-9b73-55a3-98f0-3d489e9f551d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0355","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The former name of Union Bank of Nigeria Plc was","options":["British and French Bank","Bank of British West Africa","Barclays Bank","Agbonmagbe Bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Union Bank of Nigeria was formerly Barclays Bank (Barclays Bank DCO), a British-owned bank that was later renamed Union Bank. The Bank of British West Africa (Option B) became First Bank of Nigeria, Agbonmagbe Bank (Option D) became Wema Bank, and the British and French Bank (Option A) became United Bank for Africa."}},{"itemId":"8e5b7cb2-90cf-54a3-b62b-c18992516036","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90cde852-afb7-586a-9bb4-2d979e1a8d0e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0356","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank of Nigeria performs the following functions except","options":["traditional function","regulatory function","developmental function","cold room function"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN's work is usually grouped into traditional central banking functions such as issuing currency, acting as banker to the government and to banks and running monetary policy; regulatory and supervisory functions over banks and other financial institutions; and developmental functions such as special funds and intervention schemes. A 'cold room function' is not a recognised central bank function at all, so D is the exception. Options A, B and C are all genuine groups of CBN functions."}},{"itemId":"0fb92007-1f24-5318-8ce5-2e15cef3cbf1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f0a9deff-8387-5ccd-bd1c-5d3259b34fb1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0357","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the NDIC Act of 2006, the annual insurance premium paid to the NDIC by an insured deposit money bank could not exceed ____ of 1% of its total deposit liabilities.","options":["14/16","15/16","15/20","14/20"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Under the NDIC Act of 2006, an insured deposit money bank paid an annual premium of not more than 15/16 of one per cent of its total deposit liabilities. These premiums build up the deposit insurance fund used to pay depositors when an insured bank fails. Options A, C and D are close-looking fractions, but none of them is the rate set by the law."}},{"itemId":"cbc9933a-9e72-5de3-a70a-5165e14ea59c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f14914c8-4ebd-5bb3-a575-48ba8855c657","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0358","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"_______ is a market for existing issues of securities or claims","options":["money market","capital market","primary market","secondary market"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The secondary market is where securities that have already been issued are bought and sold among investors; it makes long-term securities liquid because holders can sell them whenever they need cash. The primary market (Option C) is for new issues. The capital market (Option B) is the whole market for long-term funds, covering both the primary and the secondary market, and the money market (Option A) deals in short-term instruments."}},{"itemId":"3a928cbd-6589-5adc-9880-73df92d4a2a9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"282f1ce0-618e-57be-81ad-9ccb770df7e2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0360","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The capital market participants include the following except","options":["Finance houses","Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)","Nigerian Stock Exchange (NSE)","Underwriters"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Finance houses raise short-term placements and lend them out, so they operate mainly in the money market and are the exception here. The Securities and Exchange Commission (Option B) regulates the capital market, the Nigerian Stock Exchange (Option C) is where long-term securities are traded, and underwriters (Option D) guarantee the sale of new share and bond issues, so all three are capital market participants."}},{"itemId":"4d90598c-2b9e-5d02-bed9-2ea265a02eb9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"44ee849f-5c30-5894-a117-89bd87f32ab0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0362","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following can be regarded as a liability of a commercial bank?","options":["Advances","Deposits","Treasury bills","Overdrafts"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Deposits are money customers have placed with the bank, so the bank owes that money back to them on demand or at maturity, which makes deposits a liability. Options A and D (advances and overdrafts) are loans the bank has granted to customers, so they are assets, and Option C, treasury bills, are investments the bank holds, which are also assets."}},{"itemId":"22d8b52d-51f5-5514-8755-59c63021467b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"de61ad95-8548-5b5a-a1a4-3f7dce6b43b7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0363","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Lagos Clearing House is","options":["a commodity market","an insurance underwriting centre","another name for the Lagos Stock Exchange","a cheque sorting centre"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"A clearing house is where banks exchange the cheques drawn on one another and settle only the net amounts they owe each other, so the Lagos Clearing House is essentially a cheque sorting and settlement centre. Option C is wrong because a stock exchange is a market for buying and selling shares, not for clearing cheques, while Options A and B describe commodity trading and insurance, which have nothing to do with cheque clearing."}},{"itemId":"b469ccaf-e907-5ebe-bd8f-6155a096dc8d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5e6ed432-3045-537a-8349-c484b06be529","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0364","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"By 'trade by barter', we mean","options":["trade done by people in villages","exchange of goods for money","the trade of the Middle Ages","exchange of goods for goods"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Barter is the direct exchange of goods (or services) for other goods without using money, for example swapping yams for cloth. Option B describes a money transaction, which is the system that replaced barter, while Options A and C wrongly tie barter to a place or a period instead of to the method of exchange."}},{"itemId":"3380a76d-67b1-5a3a-987c-e8013472dd7c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"44faf7cf-3c44-593e-bfee-daba0aec718a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0365","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An increase in liquid reserve requirements by the Central Bank of Nigeria will result in","options":["a reduction in commercial banks' excess reserves","more commercial bank loans to members of the public","an increase in commercial banks' excess reserves","no change in commercial banks' excess reserves"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Excess reserves are the funds a bank holds above the minimum the CBN requires. When the CBN raises the reserve requirement, more of each bank's funds must be held as required reserves, so excess reserves fall and banks have less to lend. Options C and D are wrong because a higher requirement cannot raise or leave unchanged the spare funds banks have, and Option B is the opposite of what happens, since less spare cash means fewer loans."}},{"itemId":"e413d3d1-44a5-5e42-a0df-0d349c0a4675","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c5657eba-60c3-50b5-9a05-c76378bab3eb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0366","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank controls the activities of other banks by all but one of the following. Which one?","options":["The use of bank rate","The use of directives","Special deposits","Taxation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The central bank influences banks through monetary tools such as the bank rate (the rate at which it lends to banks), directives telling banks how to lend, and special deposits that lock up part of their funds. Taxation is a fiscal tool used by government through its tax authorities, not a device the central bank uses to control banks, so Option D is the exception."}},{"itemId":"af94712d-b85a-5fd1-9988-c368e216a0d5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd0ba35e-cc3d-53d3-a2f8-4555291fad34","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0367","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these alternatives is wrong? Central banks are","options":["bankers to the government","bankers to commercial banks","merchant banks","issuers of bank notes"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Central banks act as banker to the government, banker to the commercial banks and sole issuer of the national currency, so Options A, B and D are all true. A central bank is not a merchant bank; merchant banks are profit-seeking institutions that provide wholesale and corporate finance services, which is why Option C is the wrong statement."}},{"itemId":"871a51bd-39d2-5b45-8188-acf5e95f8b73","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c55e33a-8594-5825-9b16-18d985a7947e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0368","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these financial institutions cannot provide direct loans to individuals?","options":["The Industrial Bank","The Agricultural Bank","The Central Bank","The Co-operative Bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The Central Bank is banker to the government and to other banks, not to private individuals, so it does not grant loans directly to members of the public. The industrial, agricultural and co-operative banks in Options A, B and D are development and specialised institutions set up precisely to lend to firms, farmers and members in their sectors."}},{"itemId":"767b12bc-0e03-5005-b0d8-9ba920b1db86","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"22538469-1bb8-5dac-81e0-9a3441a30acd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0369","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Commercial banks can create money in which of the following ways?","options":["By issuing coins and paper money","By printing money on the instructions of the Central Bank","By borrowing money from banks abroad","None of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Commercial banks create money by granting loans and overdrafts that show up as new deposits in the banking system, and none of Options A to C describes this. Issuing coins and paper money (Option A) or printing money (Option B) is the job of the central bank alone, and borrowing from banks abroad (Option C) is simply taking on a debt, not creating new deposit money."}},{"itemId":"cbe32e6c-a598-5dd0-8232-838e9643a35f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e0eff07a-4241-5289-9de2-503fdfd919a3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0370","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The value of money depends primarily on","options":["the gold backing of the currency","government decree that it is legal tender","the general price level","none of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The value of money is its purchasing power, the quantity of goods and services a unit of money can buy, so it depends mainly on the general price level: when prices rise the value of money falls, and when prices fall it rises. Option A is wrong because modern currencies are not backed by gold, and Option B, legal tender status, makes money acceptable in payment but does not decide how much it can buy."}},{"itemId":"24cd9641-ce44-5746-b0f2-4322b43d804e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e51a8ce9-69d5-505e-9a0b-3cc1f2346d8c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0371","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A commercial bank is able to create money","options":["by printing it","by maintaining reserves","by creating demand deposits as it gives a new loan","by borrowing from the Central Bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"When a bank grants a loan it usually credits the borrower's account, creating a new demand deposit that did not exist before, and because demand deposits are part of the money supply, this is how commercial banks create money. Option A is wrong because only the central bank issues currency, Option B (keeping reserves) actually limits how much a bank can lend, and Option D only adds to the bank's reserves without by itself creating deposit money."}},{"itemId":"b05b6439-2e51-58cc-ac45-284dab8d4e63","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"75125239-0ba7-5e37-a01f-15611a6415fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0372","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Money becomes a very poor store of value in a period of","options":["deflation","harvest","recession","inflation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money stores value well only if its purchasing power holds over time. During inflation prices keep rising, so the same amount of naira buys less and less, which makes money a poor store of value. Option A, deflation, is the opposite case, because falling prices increase what money can buy, while a harvest or a recession (Options B and C) does not by itself erode money's purchasing power."}},{"itemId":"39a32c93-1082-5db6-a28b-f6467924dccb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93597ad0-ec93-51db-b111-849b602be930","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0373","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The function of money as a standard of deferred payments","options":["makes it possible for an individual to build up stores of many things for future use","makes it possible for payments to be postponed from the present to a future date","facilitates the exchange of goods","makes easy calculations possible"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"As a standard of deferred payment, money is the unit in which future payments and debts are stated, so goods can be bought now and paid for later, loans repaid and salaries agreed in advance. Option A describes the store of value function, Option C describes money as a medium of exchange, and Option D describes money as a unit of account."}},{"itemId":"e0424664-6d6e-59bf-99fc-299bdc05276a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d3334413-a164-5c57-8f48-4a0b051f947d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0374","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a major function of a Central Bank?","options":["Serving as government's bank","Serving as bankers' bank","Acceptance of deposits from the public","Responsibility for monetary policy"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A central bank's major functions include acting as banker to the government, banker to the banks and manager of monetary policy, which covers Options A, B and D. It does not normally take deposits from members of the public; that is the business of commercial and other deposit-taking banks, so Option C is the exception."}},{"itemId":"21811724-e54e-5384-afc6-1fa14e7da01c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bc07db75-c095-59e9-9c67-a206bb54c678","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0376","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following does NOT belong in respect of the qualities of money?","options":["Durability","Portability","Divisibility into the smallest units of value","Measure of value"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Durability, portability and divisibility are qualities good money must have, so that it lasts, is easy to carry and can be broken into small units for small purchases. Measure of value is not a quality but a function of money, its role as the yardstick for pricing goods and services, so Option D does not belong."}},{"itemId":"072957b8-dda1-5fc7-b7eb-9ee845ef8fd3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f396e78-5473-50af-9618-6390defbed89","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0377","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the Central Bank intends to increase the money supply through open market operations then it will","options":["sell securities in the open market","buy securities in the open market","issue more currency notes","print more demand deposits"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"In open market operations the CBN buys or sells government securities. When it buys securities it pays with new money that flows into banks' reserves, so the money supply rises; selling securities (Option A) does the opposite by mopping up money. Option C is not an open market operation at all, and Option D is wrong because demand deposits are created by banks through lending, not printed by the central bank."}},{"itemId":"75f63b26-3130-5b5f-b4c4-96b423801262","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd4435d5-95df-5818-967f-8b2f8a111b12","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0380","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Higher interest rates tend to lead to ________ consumer and business spending, while lower interest rates tend to lead to ________ spending by consumers and businesses.","options":["higher; lower","lower; lower","higher; higher","lower; higher"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Interest is the cost of borrowing, so when rates rise, loans for cars, houses or business expansion become dearer and households and firms cut back on spending. When rates fall, borrowing becomes cheaper and spending tends to rise. This is why the CBN raises its policy rate to cool spending and lowers it to stimulate the economy; Options A, B and C each get one or both directions wrong."}},{"itemId":"35414fef-9c52-52e7-836e-8782436ed237","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25f59218-063b-57d4-a111-4d0d9330d855","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0386","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The government controls the size of the money supply through ________ policies.","options":["revenue","monetary","inflation","fiscal"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Monetary policy is the control of the money supply, credit and interest rates, carried out in Nigeria by the CBN through tools such as open market operations, reserve requirements and the policy rate. Option D, fiscal policy, is about government taxation and spending rather than the money supply, while Options A and C are not recognised types of economic policy."}},{"itemId":"421cafc9-0976-5977-a8db-89319c1eb940","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c202ca60-cb09-5686-a38d-552e0672830e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0392","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Recapitalization policy will enable commercial banks to","options":["employ more qualified workers","open more branches","reduce interest rate","have a very strong capital base"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Recapitalisation requires banks to raise their minimum capital, giving them a stronger capital base to absorb losses, support bigger loans and protect depositors. Options A, B and C may happen as side effects in some banks, but they are not what the policy is designed to achieve."}},{"itemId":"12f7523e-7c1e-5812-b64d-49bfe5367115","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1db53715-98d1-5b3e-a5a6-c5ad6d8e6e04","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0395","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An issue of bank notes not backed by gold but by government securities is known as","options":["fiduciary issue","guaranteed money","seigniorage","legal tender"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"A fiduciary issue is currency issued on trust: it is backed not by gold but by government securities and the public's confidence in the issuing authority. Option D, legal tender, is money that must by law be accepted in settlement of debts, and Option C, seigniorage, is the profit a government earns from issuing money, so neither describes how the currency is backed."}},{"itemId":"6cd94659-ba40-5242-84dd-e658b5eb350d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5e19827e-c20e-573a-bb51-519827532677","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0396","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A rise in the market price of fixed interest securities is an indication that the","options":["supply of money has decreased","liquidity preference has increased","market rate of interest has risen","market rate of interest has fallen"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Fixed interest securities pay a set rate, so their prices move opposite to market interest rates: when market rates fall, existing securities paying the older, higher fixed rate become more attractive and their prices rise. Option C is the reverse case, and Options A and B would both push interest rates up and security prices down, because less money in circulation or a stronger wish to hold cash leads people to sell securities."}},{"itemId":"8572bad2-6f0f-5828-91cd-44112b55dcd0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"de31e227-19f1-5570-a862-f835cc2ef75e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0397","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The regulatory authority of the capital market in Nigeria is the","options":["Central Bank","Securities and Exchange Commission","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","Stock Exchange Market"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Securities and Exchange Commission is the apex regulator of the Nigerian capital market, registering securities, licensing market operators and protecting investors. Option A, the CBN, regulates banks and the money market, Option C, the NDIC, insures deposits and supervises banks alongside the CBN, and Option D, the stock exchange, is the market where securities are traded under SEC supervision."}},{"itemId":"7c3d2584-f2b0-5cea-8d28-f996f6f55925","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"040910df-3272-5291-9e7b-a1d223a87001","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0400","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Money performs a number of functions. One of these functions makes it possible for a person to provide for old age. Because of that particular function, money is regarded as a","options":["medium of exchange","measure of value","unit of account","store of value"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money's store of value function lets people save part of their income today and spend it in the future, for example in old age. Option A, medium of exchange, is about using money to buy and sell now, while Options B and C, measure of value and unit of account, are about pricing goods, not keeping wealth for later."}},{"itemId":"be0502b0-cb69-5c18-b05a-33f95d9cbe37","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93d45fee-b216-59a4-ace9-fef5ae755907","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0401","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The most important characteristic of money is","options":["portability","usefulness","acceptability","beauty"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Acceptability matters most because something can only work as money if people generally accept it in exchange for goods and services; if it is refused, its other qualities count for nothing. Portability (A) is helpful, but a portable item that nobody accepts still cannot serve as money. Usefulness (B) and beauty (D) are not required at all, since modern paper money has little use or beauty of its own."}},{"itemId":"8bab8e0c-d0df-5a96-89f6-7bc57256aad8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fc648844-549e-54cf-940b-930a227f22cc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0403","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"All of the following are tools for controlling persistent inflation in an economy except","options":["Surplus budget","Sale of treasury bills through Open Market Operations (OMO)","Decrease in the Monetary Policy Rate","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Cutting the Monetary Policy Rate eases monetary policy: it makes borrowing cheaper and puts more money into circulation, which would worsen inflation rather than control it. Selling treasury bills through OMO (B) mops up excess money from the banking system, and a surplus budget (A) cuts total spending, so both help to fight inflation. Option D is wrong because C is clearly not an anti-inflation tool."}},{"itemId":"051a0847-ad6c-5533-8b1f-b9f438896cff","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c61e1c3d-35f7-5e06-adfb-6c70adf135ed","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0407","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The barter system of exchange is noted for many drawbacks, which include the following except","options":["Lack of double coincidence of wants","Time wastage","Lack of a common measure of value","Direct exchange of goods for goods"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Direct exchange of goods for goods is simply what barter is - its defining feature - not a drawback of it. The lack of double coincidence of wants (A), the time wasted searching for a suitable trading partner (B) and the lack of a common measure of value (C) are all genuine weaknesses of barter that money later solved."}},{"itemId":"b9937bec-800d-5293-a566-67d628799b91","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c1b5cecb-c3eb-58b6-9787-6c033dce793d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0408","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following qualify as near (quasi) money and legal tender respectively?","options":["Treasury bills and naira notes","Currency notes and fixed deposits","Fixed deposits and silver notes","Gold coins and naira notes"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Treasury bills are near money because they are not used to make payments but can be turned into cash quickly with little loss, while naira currency issued by the CBN is legal tender that must be accepted in settlement of debts. Option B has the pair the wrong way round, since currency is the legal tender and fixed deposits are the near money. In C, fixed deposits are near money but the second item is not Nigeria's legal tender, and in D gold coins are not near money."}},{"itemId":"fdf68d12-461a-58b7-ac45-77c3ce0a1b06","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0994fe7c-2124-5aca-a088-0a505edf3c07","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0409","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following tools can be used to measure the value (purchasing power) of money?","options":["Regression method","Inflation method","Index number method","None of these"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The value of money is its purchasing power, and it is measured with price index numbers, which compare the general price level in one period with a base period. When the price index rises, each naira buys less, so the value of money has fallen. Regression (A) is a general statistical technique and an 'inflation method' (B) is not a recognised measuring tool, so D is also wrong."}},{"itemId":"2dbcfffe-e650-569b-b3e4-0ce45690f0e3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2838e95c-1486-5119-be6f-7b1c980895ef","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0413","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given an initial deposit of N500 and a reserve requirement ratio of 15%, what is the money (credit) multiplier?","options":["15","6.67","0.15","1.18"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The money multiplier is the inverse of the reserve ratio: k = 1/r = 1/0.15 = 6.67, so every naira of fresh deposit can support about 6.67 naira of total deposits. Option C (0.15) is just the reserve ratio itself, and D (1.18) wrongly inverts 0.85, the share of each deposit that is lent out. Option A treats 15% as 15 instead of inverting 0.15."}},{"itemId":"8cfe05c8-4b34-5add-9148-5d20b537b6bc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"97528d9b-446c-57db-892f-401c74da7d86","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0414","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given an initial deposit of N500 and a reserve requirement ratio of 15%, what is the total deposit created in the banking system, including the initial deposit?","options":["N75","N425","N3,333.33","N7,500"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Total deposits equal the initial deposit times the money multiplier: 500 x (1/0.15) = N3,333.33. Option A (N75) is only the reserve the first bank must keep, and B (N425) is the amount that first bank can lend out. Option D comes from multiplying 500 by 15 instead of dividing by 0.15."}},{"itemId":"0f45e0a2-a131-5deb-ab3b-78aa3ab89b1f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8ced3e33-9e20-5ea3-99bf-ed487902e4ec","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0415","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given an initial deposit of N500 and a reserve requirement ratio of 15%, how much is the excess reserve of the bank that receives the deposit?","options":["N75","N500","N425","N3,333.33"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The bank must keep 15% of N500, which is N75, as its required reserve; what is left, N500 - N75 = N425, is the excess reserve it is free to lend out. Option A is the required reserve, not the excess, and B is simply the original deposit. Option D is the total deposit the whole banking system can eventually create."}},{"itemId":"7016d365-d683-592d-b672-0935925227d8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0fe66873-debd-5d97-bb5f-6c95610182f0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0418","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the total supply of money in the economy is N280 million and the initial deposit is N35 million, what is the reserve requirement ratio?","options":["12.5%","8%","25%","87.5%"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Total money created equals the initial deposit divided by the reserve ratio, so r = 35/280 = 0.125, or 12.5%. Option B mixes up the ratio with the money multiplier, which is 280/35 = 8. Option D (87.5%) is the share of each deposit that would be lent out, and C is double the correct ratio."}},{"itemId":"c53a39b8-61cb-5f93-b64f-157d9beb0f19","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"06de826c-176e-508c-bb04-b46feaba999d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0420","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The total supply of money in an economy is N280 million, created from an initial deposit of N35 million. If the reserve requirement ratio increases by 100% and the initial deposit decreases by 25%, what is the total money supply created in the system?","options":["N140 million","N210 million","N105 million","N175 million"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The original ratio is 35/280 = 12.5%, so doubling it gives 25% and a multiplier of 4. The new deposit is 75% of N35 million = N26.25 million, so total money created = 26.25 x 4 = N105 million. Option A ignores the fall in the deposit, B ignores the rise in the ratio, and D wrongly raises the deposit by 25% instead of reducing it."}},{"itemId":"bddcb4b9-9cd6-53be-8a13-bcd2fa84fbbd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1c3e5cf9-d8dd-5bfb-a521-16ae217aef00","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0421","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is legal tender in Nigeria, that is, must be accepted by law in settlement of a debt?","options":["Postal order","Cheque","Naira notes issued by the CBN","Treasury bill"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Legal tender is money that the law says must be accepted in payment of a debt, and in Nigeria that status belongs to the naira currency issued by the CBN. A postal order (A) is only a payment instrument that has to be cashed or paid into an account, so nobody is legally bound to accept it. A cheque (B) is merely an order on a bank to pay, and a treasury bill (D) is a short-term government security, so neither is legal tender."}},{"itemId":"f6ed44d3-06fd-5bf9-9ecc-24c7f9c5d445","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a6ac00c-47b7-5fc4-9af2-eab9acecf723","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0423","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The total supply of money in an economy is N280 million, created from an initial deposit of N35 million. If the reserve requirement ratio increases by 100%, determine the total money created.","options":["N280 million","N140 million","N560 million","None of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The original reserve ratio is 35/280 = 12.5%, and a 100% increase doubles it to 25%, so the multiplier falls from 8 to 4. Total money created is therefore 35 x 4 = N140 million. Option A is the old total before the change, and C wrongly doubles the money instead of halving it, so D is also wrong."}},{"itemId":"4c57c0d9-4109-5fd7-baca-6e8f829e5555","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"30fcc3c3-f8e4-5e62-a79a-9bf13b106e10","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0424","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The total supply of money in an economy is N280 million, created from an initial deposit of N35 million. If the initial deposit increases by 25% (with the reserve ratio unchanged), determine the total money created.","options":["N210 million","N180 million","N120 million","N350 million"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The reserve ratio is 35/280 = 12.5%, which gives a multiplier of 8. A 25% rise makes the deposit 35 x 1.25 = N43.75 million, so total money created = 43.75 x 8 = N350 million. Option A (N210 million) is what you get if the deposit falls by 25% instead of rising, while B and C do not follow from the multiplier at all."}},{"itemId":"5e1b846d-7cfc-5eaa-8f07-b409241dbe16","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6964c29d-a0c6-5a5d-885a-e64b569a77dd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0425","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The apex regulatory institution of the Nigerian capital market is","options":["SEC","NDIC","CBN","NAICOM"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is the apex regulator of the Nigerian capital market: it registers capital market operators, oversees the issue and trading of securities and protects investors. The CBN (C) is the apex authority over banks and the money market, not the capital market. NDIC (B) insures bank deposits, and NAICOM (D) regulates the insurance industry."}},{"itemId":"a6db02b7-6cca-51cc-8034-35589a1edffd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"953732e6-4ba7-5498-9749-da757a08ec37","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0426","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation performs the following functions except","options":["To strengthen the supervisory role of the Central Bank of Nigeria","Insuring bank deposits against failure","To implement distress resolution mechanisms in the event of bank failure","To regulate money supply in the economy"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Regulating the money supply is a monetary policy job of the Central Bank of Nigeria, not of NDIC. NDIC insures bank deposits (B), helps to resolve distressed and failed banks (C), and examines insured banks alongside the CBN, which strengthens the CBN's supervisory role (A)."}},{"itemId":"12044777-e053-53a0-b073-bf829cb25021","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"64e27ad3-0bfc-5938-9670-d4fac341f43c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0427","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank of Nigeria performs all but one of the following functions. Which one does it NOT perform?","options":["Serves as banker to the Federal Government","Undertakes research on various facets of economic activity","Registers stock broking firms","Administers the country's foreign exchange reserves"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Registering stockbroking firms is the work of the Securities and Exchange Commission, which regulates capital market operators, not the CBN. Acting as banker to the Federal Government (A), carrying out economic research (B) and managing the country's foreign exchange reserves (D) are all regular CBN functions."}},{"itemId":"f82c1863-d024-5573-a2cd-b599f96340ed","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45638582-1bd9-5d15-9639-0f2dd5c79c4f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0428","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which one of the following assets cannot be regarded as quasi money?","options":["Postal orders","Deposits in a fixed deposit account","Treasury bills","Deposits in a current account"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Current account balances are demand deposits that can be withdrawn or transferred by cheque at once, so they are money itself (part of narrow money, M1), not quasi money. Quasi or near money means assets that are not generally used to make payments but can be turned into cash quickly with little loss. Fixed deposits (B) and treasury bills (C) fit that description, and postal orders (A) likewise have to be cashed before they can be spent."}},{"itemId":"da821ad8-0d60-5047-a035-89012c4d8bfc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"07f3fa8e-4947-5527-b88b-fd1bc63a6fc0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0430","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Unilag deposited an initial sum of N1,000 in a commercial bank. If the cash reserve ratio fixed by the CBN is 15%, what is the total amount of deposits that the entire banking system can create from it?","options":["N7,600","N6,667","N1,500","N15,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The money multiplier is 1/0.15 = 6.67, so the banking system can build total deposits of about 1,000 x 6.67 = N6,667 from the initial N1,000; of this, about N5,667 is new lending on top of the original deposit. Option D (N15,000) comes from multiplying by 15 instead of dividing by 0.15. Options A and C do not follow from the multiplier at all."}},{"itemId":"cd8fdb1f-133c-5bc1-8ea8-2e9311c91632","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"71204b5b-eb51-5343-9ef0-d6fac1cc7bb1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0431","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first banking ordinance in Nigeria was passed in the year","options":["1952","1958","1872","1912"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Banking Ordinance of 1952, based on the 1948 Paton Commission report, was Nigeria's first banking law, passed to check the wave of indigenous bank failures. Option B (1958) is the year that ordinance was replaced by a new Banking Ordinance and the CBN Act was passed. Option D (1912) is when the West African Currency Board was set up, and C (1872) is before modern banking even reached Nigeria."}},{"itemId":"f3ac20e6-7104-509a-85ec-bf74b0bf6a09","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4460c056-91f4-56d9-a969-a50e1d782298","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0432","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following describes how trade was actually carried out under the barter system of exchange?","options":["Traders could always easily find a partner with a double coincidence of wants","Wealth could easily be stored in a generally acceptable form","Borrowing and lending could easily be arranged in a common unit of value","Goods were exchanged directly for other goods"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Barter is the direct exchange of goods for goods, so that is how trade actually took place. Without money it was hard to find someone with a double coincidence of wants (A), and wealth was hard to store (B) because most goods spoil or lose value. Borrowing and lending (C) were also difficult because there was no common unit for measuring what was owed."}},{"itemId":"80263cbb-1c7d-52f7-b83e-8562de60e927","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e1335a59-9728-5669-b860-6b448661c59c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0435","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"To reduce the supply of money, the government (through the central bank) could:","options":["Reduce interest rates","Buy back government bonds","Sell government bonds","Encourage banks to lend"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Selling government bonds or other securities draws money out of the system, because buyers pay with cash and bank reserves, so the money supply falls. Buying back bonds (B) does the opposite and injects money into the economy. Reducing interest rates (A) and encouraging banks to lend (D) both expand credit and so increase the money supply."}},{"itemId":"cdf8aaa6-2929-5de2-b03b-55a9ac27947c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"42664edd-674f-5c10-89fe-d4a47ffa8a6b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0437","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Open Market Operations occur when the central bank","options":["Reduces spending","Buys and sells treasury bills","Increases taxation","Increases the exchange rate"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Open Market Operations are the buying and selling of government securities, mainly treasury bills, by the central bank to influence bank reserves and the money supply. The CBN sells them to mop up excess liquidity and buys them to inject money. Reducing spending (A) and increasing taxation (C) are fiscal actions of government, while D concerns exchange rate policy."}},{"itemId":"2890b12f-8df3-5d2c-acf4-2571378c83d5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"196816ac-a8d1-5352-ae93-2b4752e3e6a0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0439","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first legislative measure to tackle the problems associated with the free-for-all banking era was:","options":["The enactment of the Central Bank of Nigeria Act","The enactment of the Banking Ordinance in 1952","The setting up of the Loynes Commission of 1957","The setting up of the Fisher Commission"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Banking Ordinance of 1952 was the first law passed to end the era of free, unregulated banking, when many poorly capitalised indigenous banks were collapsing. The Fisher Commission (D) of 1953 and the Loynes Commission (C) of 1957 were enquiries into whether Nigeria should have a central bank, not laws. The CBN Act (A) came later, in 1958."}},{"itemId":"ef227cdb-09f5-537a-a354-39400f941ea9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5a7d736-0725-568a-9cfb-64546e4f8b73","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0440","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The West African Currency Board, established in 1912, was criticised on several grounds. Which of the following was NOT one of them?","options":["Hindering the growth and development of Nigeria's own financial system","Tying domestic money supply expansion to the balance of trade performance of the economy","Convertibility of the local currency into the British pound sterling","Lack of independence for Nigeria in carrying out its own domestic monetary policy"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Easy convertibility into sterling was actually seen as a benefit of the WACB system, because it gave the colonies' currency international recognition and confidence. The real criticisms were that it invested the earnings from Nigeria's trade abroad and so held back the local financial system (A), tied growth in the money supply to balance of trade performance (B), and left Nigeria without an independent monetary policy (D)."}},{"itemId":"b359f429-a68b-5a95-869b-dec4daad06ac","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"84d881f9-e1cf-5944-8df8-871ba0e269e6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0441","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In studying the evolution of commercial banking in Nigeria, the Structural Adjustment Programme adopted in Nigeria in the mid-1980s was responsible for all of the following within the Nigerian banking system from the mid-1980s to the early 1990s except:","options":["Interest rate deregulation","Proliferation in the number of banks operating in Nigeria","Increase in Federal Government deposits in commercial banks","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Structural Adjustment Programme liberalised banking: interest rates were deregulated and bank licensing was relaxed, so the number of banks grew rapidly. It did not raise Federal Government deposits in commercial banks; in fact, public sector deposits were moved out of the banks to the Central Bank of Nigeria during this period. Options A and B were genuine results of SAP, and D cannot be right because C is the exception."}},{"itemId":"e7bf8377-1754-56f8-8420-4a0b276b78c5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e2b88755-c0c6-5e9c-a7c3-6f14dae61456","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0442","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is not a money market instrument?","options":["Certificate of Deposit","Treasury certificate","Mortgage","Call money"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"A mortgage is a long-term loan secured on property, so it belongs to the capital (long-term) market, not the money market. Certificates of deposit (A), treasury certificates (B) and call money (D) are all short-term instruments traded in the Nigerian money market."}},{"itemId":"d7d7de3f-8269-5dd7-956b-b5811eea3954","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ce12cd0-f2f3-5a67-b0c3-dde28c779dcf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0446","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"All these are \"traditional\" (quantitative) tools of monetary policy except","options":["Bank rate","Open market operations","Cash reserve requirements","Moral suasion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Moral suasion is a qualitative tool: the central bank uses persuasion and friendly appeals to get banks to behave in a desired way, instead of directly changing the cost or quantity of money. The bank rate (A), open market operations (B) and cash reserve requirements (C) are the traditional quantitative tools, because each works directly on the cost of funds, the monetary base or the money multiplier."}},{"itemId":"9eca476d-4b24-558f-a161-9204a03fec40","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"71c53fe6-37d1-5b6f-8022-a7b663410f3a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0447","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is made legal tender through government decree?","options":["Fiat money","Near money","Quasi money","All of the above"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Fiat money has no intrinsic value and is not backed by any commodity; it is money because the government declares it legal tender, and 'fiat' itself means 'let it be done'. Near money (B) and quasi money (C), such as time deposits or treasury bills, have no legal tender status and are not generally accepted in payment, which also rules out D."}},{"itemId":"d435ff79-5254-5fdb-b69b-53f046b21b25","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff37235c-25ca-5400-aadc-d89aab970672","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0448","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"One quality of money which makes it possible to be easily carried about is:","options":["Scarcity","Durability","Portability","Accessibility"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Portability means money must be easy to carry about, with high value relative to its weight and size, which is why paper money and coins replaced bulky commodities like livestock. Scarcity (A) is about keeping money relatively limited so that it holds its value, and durability (B) is about money lasting long without wearing out, so neither describes ease of carrying. Accessibility (D) is not one of the recognised qualities of money."}},{"itemId":"45d97388-2f22-5bad-b769-45df5aef8a2c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b29674b-d91d-5b02-bbca-d4e743124a77","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0449","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The total amount of money that the banking system can create from an initial deposit is determined by the","options":["Money multiplier","Deflation","Accelerator","All of the above"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Total money created equals the initial deposit multiplied by the money (credit) multiplier, which is the inverse of the cash reserve ratio (k = 1/r). Deflation (B) is a general fall in prices, and the accelerator (C) links investment to changes in output, so neither determines how much money banks can create, which also rules out D."}},{"itemId":"2cb6cf96-1ea7-56f6-b214-9518ce457d7b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2cd6b564-3474-504e-b686-0644e9941afb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0450","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The most important characteristic of money which is not shared by other quasi-money assets is:","options":["Durability","Scarcity","General acceptability","Stability"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money is generally acceptable as a medium of exchange, largely because it is legal tender, and this is exactly what near money or quasi-money assets lack. Time deposits, treasury bills and bills of exchange can be durable (A), relatively scarce (B) and fairly stable in value (D), but they must first be converted into money before they can be used to pay for goods and services."}},{"itemId":"4f4c3793-2948-5d49-be1b-4a3b7fdc8157","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"31a620c0-39c7-5549-baca-cc555c9c5cd0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0452","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a recognised feature (characteristic) of good money?","options":["Understandability","Uniformity","Uncontrollability","All of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Uniformity (also called homogeneity) means every unit of the same denomination must be identical and worth exactly the same wherever it is used, so one N100 is as good as any other N100. Understandability (A) is not a feature of money, and uncontrollability (C) is the opposite of what is needed, since the supply of money must be controlled to keep its value. That also rules out D."}},{"itemId":"9fe47aa2-6cd4-5569-8a64-d769e9eb62ad","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"558f3392-d195-57c6-9297-7977400645d2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0453","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Some of the reasons for the mass failure of indigenous banks in Nigeria between 1945 and 1952 include all of the following except","options":["Operational inefficiency","Discriminating attitude of the colonial masters","Lack of competent staff","Foreign merchants patronage and encouragement"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The indigenous banks of the post-war banking boom failed mainly because of poor management and operational inefficiency, inadequate capital, a lack of trained and competent staff, and a discriminating colonial environment. Patronage and encouragement from foreign merchants was not a cause of failure; the indigenous banks actually struggled against competition from the well-established foreign banks. So A, B and C were genuine causes, while D is the exception."}},{"itemId":"ea877cb8-3e9b-5d10-a07b-037c172e3aa2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0a262057-a0e0-5a0b-8f0e-7fd6b9d750e2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0454","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The West African Currency Board was mandated to","options":["Accept deposits from Nigerians","Issue and distribute currency in the British West African colonies","Print the naira notes","None of these"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The West African Currency Board, set up in 1912, issued and distributed a sterling-backed currency for the British West African colonies, including Nigeria. It was not a bank and did not take deposits (A), and the naira (C) came much later, issued by the Central Bank of Nigeria. Since B correctly states its mandate, D is wrong."}},{"itemId":"3971cd9e-5534-562c-9375-028af2c5bf5a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a23134bb-8f82-5705-9bbb-eac4f45446bc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0455","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these banks was formerly known as \"Agbonmagbe Bank\" in Nigeria?","options":["First Bank of Nigeria","Access Bank of Nigeria","Savannah Bank Nigeria","Wema Bank Nigeria"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Agbonmagbe Bank was one of the indigenous banks set up in the Western Region during the post-war banking boom, and it later became Wema Bank, which still operates today. First Bank (A) grew out of the Bank of British West Africa, a foreign bank, while Access Bank (B) and Savannah Bank (C) have different histories."}},{"itemId":"16f913ca-a41b-54a7-979d-083f1f92041a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d1779fe7-110c-5f92-b447-ee361c03b7e3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0456","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first foreign-owned bank was set up in Nigeria in the year","options":["1930","1945","1892","1952"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Banking in Nigeria began in 1892, when the African Banking Corporation, a foreign bank, opened in Lagos to serve colonial trade; its business was taken over by the Bank of British West Africa (later First Bank) in 1894. The years 1930 (A), 1945 (B) and 1952 (D) all come long after foreign banking had already been established in Nigeria."}},{"itemId":"8577f01f-a11c-51ac-b8fc-4d24675ac6ee","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2caf0e51-c508-588a-81f6-6ee0d86a1b74","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0457","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these will discourage credit creation?","options":["Leakages in the economy","The central bank's contractionary monetary policy","All of the above","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Leakages, such as cash withdrawn and kept outside the banking system, stop deposits from flowing round the banks, so less credit can be created. A contractionary monetary policy, for example raising the reserve requirement or selling securities through open market operations, also cuts the reserves banks can lend. Since both A and B discourage credit creation, C is the best answer and D is wrong."}},{"itemId":"ca595cfb-0155-56f3-b753-f4cc0ea2cc2d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0c12e98-a717-53e5-81c4-091e895239ea","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0458","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The relationship between the reserve requirement and the total credit created is","options":["Direct","Proportionally direct","Inverse","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Total credit created equals the initial deposit times 1/r, where r is the reserve requirement, so a higher reserve requirement means less credit and a lower one means more. That is an inverse relationship, which rules out A and B; D is wrong because C correctly describes the link."}},{"itemId":"86e288a5-2af3-52da-a73a-b5918efa8bd0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"77caaf40-48ea-5e60-b6db-9da72bbd7f48","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0459","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The relationship between the reserve requirement and the deposit multiplier is","options":["Direct","Proportionally direct","Inverse","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The deposit multiplier is k = 1/r, so when the reserve requirement rises the multiplier falls; a 10% requirement gives a multiplier of 10, while 20% gives only 5. The two move in opposite directions, so the relationship is inverse, not direct (A) or proportionally direct (B)."}},{"itemId":"92ff3443-34e2-584b-ba67-e03da8b709e3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3139a2a4-e0e8-5981-b186-8fb48cb7a36c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0460","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The monetary authority imposes a minimum reserve requirement of 10% and the initial deposit of your bank is N10 million. What is the total amount of money that can be created in the banking system from this deposit?","options":["N80 million","N100 million","N120 million","N140 million"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The money multiplier is the inverse of the reserve ratio: k = 1/0.10 = 10. Total money created = N10 million x 10 = N100 million. N80 million (A) and the larger figures in C and D do not follow from the 1/r formula."}},{"itemId":"0b21ace6-2fa1-5ae6-b2a7-c5e2a26355b2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"78f45176-5a0b-5175-98f6-3a078e8b0ac7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0461","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The monetary authority imposes a minimum reserve requirement of 10% and the initial deposit of your bank is N10 million. If the reserve requirement is increased by 50%, what is the new level of money created?","options":["N56.37 million","N66.67 million","N86.97 million","None of the above"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A 50% increase raises the reserve requirement from 10% to 15% (10% x 1.5), not to 60%. The new multiplier is 1/0.15 = 6.67, so money created = N10 million / 0.15 = about N66.67 million. Options A and C do not match this calculation, and D is wrong because B is correct."}},{"itemId":"83d09afe-323b-5b9f-8f88-fc45b3137502","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18c46c41-07d5-56b0-bb9c-7b4548c1e2e8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0462","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The monetary authority imposes a minimum reserve requirement of 10% and the initial deposit of your bank is N10 million. If the reserve requirement is instead reduced by 50%, what is the new level of money created?","options":["N560 million","N200 million","N150 million","N160 million"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Reducing 10% by half gives a new reserve requirement of 5%, so the multiplier becomes 1/0.05 = 20. Money created = N10 million x 20 = N200 million. N150 million (C) and N160 million (D) understate the effect of the lower reserve ratio, and N560 million (A) is far too large."}},{"itemId":"15a027a3-a904-5d18-836f-4929273d9fee","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1de6670f-2801-52ff-bb5b-9493e6d7f163","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0463","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How can you describe the deposit multiplier quantitatively?","options":["It is half of the cash reserve ratio","It is double the cash reserve ratio","It is the inverse of the cash reserve ratio","None of the above"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The deposit (money) multiplier is k = 1/r, the inverse or reciprocal of the cash reserve ratio, so a 25% ratio gives k = 1/0.25 = 4. Halving (A) or doubling (B) the ratio just gives another ratio, not the multiplier, and D is wrong because C is correct."}},{"itemId":"c625efdb-a6f8-560c-a1a1-a53629afda7c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8d969d81-1e45-5022-9fbe-090ae5161031","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0467","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic an item must have to serve well as money?","options":["Divisibility","Abundance, so that it is as freely available as sand","Durability","Homogeneity"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money must be relatively scarce; if it were as plentiful as sand, nobody would give up valuable goods for it, so abundance (option B) is the opposite of what money needs. Divisibility (A), durability (C) and homogeneity (D) are all genuine characteristics of money, along with portability, stability and acceptability."}},{"itemId":"3ad3d864-6982-5ebe-a0df-5e4ab81f76e9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aab21885-a566-518d-9149-3153150b401c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0467-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The characteristic of money which requires that N1 in Abuja must be exactly the same as N1 in Ibadan is","options":["Portability","Divisibility","Scarcity","Homogeneity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Homogeneity (uniformity) means every unit of money of the same denomination must be exactly alike in size, quality and value wherever it is used. Portability (A) is about being easy to carry, divisibility (B) is about breaking into smaller units for small transactions, and scarcity (C) is about keeping supply limited so that money keeps its value."}},{"itemId":"741eb2b2-ead2-56e3-97a6-f304f7ee8127","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"780e1ec8-9095-5143-b475-e360a9988270","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0467-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The quality of money that allows transactions of any size, no matter how small, to be carried out without hindrance is","options":["Durability","Stability","Divisibility","Acceptability"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Divisibility means money can be broken into small units, so even tiny purchases can be paid for exactly; this was a real problem under barter, where a cow cannot be split to buy a small item. Durability (A) is about money lasting a long time, stability (B) is about money keeping a steady value, and acceptability (D) is about people being willing to take it in payment."}},{"itemId":"c4ce85c1-2596-5257-9e6a-7b55af94055c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b670305b-04c4-5a7d-83f4-5f40d066aaf8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0468","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of money?","options":["Medium of exchange","Unit of account","Earning interest for its holder","Standard of deferred payment"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The four functions of money are medium of exchange (A), unit of account (B), store of value and standard of deferred payment (D). Earning interest, option C, is not a function of money; it is a feature of near money such as time and savings deposits, which people hold for income and safety."}},{"itemId":"57e8faff-42bc-5ccb-823b-084442da66db","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58110fda-5cd8-5b17-9ca8-87a58fbd4427","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0468-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the prices of all goods and services in Nigeria are expressed in naira, money is performing its function as a","options":["Unit of account","Medium of exchange","Store of value","Standard of deferred payment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"As a unit of account, money is the common measure in which all prices are stated, which makes comparing values and gathering price information easy. Medium of exchange (B) is about actually using money to buy and sell, store of value (C) is about holding wealth for the future, and standard of deferred payment (D) is about settling debts at a future date."}},{"itemId":"e75773a5-43ae-5388-8bd6-edce8fba55b1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"39b5b077-eb9e-5230-9de3-5ec2753c23d3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0468-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bond contract states that interest and the principal will be paid in naira at specified future dates. Here money is serving mainly as a","options":["Unit of account","Standard of deferred payment","Medium of exchange","Store of value"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money acts as a standard of deferred payment when contracts and debts to be settled in the future are stated and paid in money, as with bond interest and principal. Unit of account (A) is closely related, since the future sums are measured in naira, but the main idea here is payment at a later date. Medium of exchange (C) is about present purchases, and store of value (D) is about holding wealth rather than settling debts."}},{"itemId":"05a5166e-9a1b-501b-bf8e-b0bf77d890c5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bae0891f-5a23-5a03-b98e-320abc41d3fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0469","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a limitation of a barter economy?","options":["The existence of one common unit in which all prices are quoted","The need for a double coincidence of wants","The need to know a very large number of relative prices","The indivisibility of many goods"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"A common unit for quoting all prices (option A) is a benefit of a money economy, so it cannot be a limitation of barter; its absence is actually one of barter's problems. The double coincidence of wants (B), the huge number of relative prices (C) and the indivisibility of goods (D) are all genuine limitations of barter, along with the lack of a good store of value."}},{"itemId":"845d278e-3650-53d2-bd8c-e4fa99e8e70b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6ba9b3f1-c021-5918-9442-8e26b5e0d31a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0469-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a barter economy with 1,000 commodities, roughly how many relative prices would traders need to know?","options":["About 1,000","About 1,000,000","About 500,000","About 2,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Each pair of goods needs its own rate of exchange, so the number of relative prices is n(n - 1)/2 = 1,000 x 999 / 2 = 499,500, or about 500,000. Option A would be true only if every good were priced in one common unit, as in a money economy, option B counts every pair twice, and option D has no basis in the formula."}},{"itemId":"2dbbef48-fdb0-54c0-a7be-95ac81721500","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f91554e-ff15-535d-abd4-f0ad91502a0d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0469-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under barter, stockpiling one's output while waiting to find a suitable trading partner creates which two additional costs?","options":["Interest cost and tax cost","Waiting cost and storage cost","Printing cost and minting cost","Transport cost and advertising cost"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Storing output to cut search costs means the producer gives up immediate satisfaction while waiting (waiting cost) and must pay to keep the goods safe (storage cost). Interest and tax costs (A) and printing and minting costs (C) belong to a money economy, while transport and advertising costs (D) are not the specific costs that stockpiling creates."}},{"itemId":"a8eb3843-a123-5ba5-8722-741e1e11dc54","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"435f6d5f-10e4-5c93-8c2d-c9217b3fee28","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0470","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A yam farmer who wants palm oil must find someone who not only has palm oil to exchange but also wants yam. This requirement of barter is known as","options":["Division of labour","Indivisibility of goods","Lack of a store of value","Double coincidence of wants"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Double coincidence of wants means each party must have what the other wants and want what the other has at the same time; searching for such a partner wastes time and effort and is the biggest drawback of barter. Division of labour (A) is specialisation in production, indivisibility (B) is about goods that cannot be split into small units, and lack of a store of value (C) is about goods that cannot be kept for long."}},{"itemId":"25f802a4-6f7e-5ff2-b246-cbe125a3e9f5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b09178ec-b21e-578b-a569-5be9de38fe50","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0471","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which quality of money mainly enables a money economy to overcome the double coincidence of wants problem of barter?","options":["Its intrinsic value as a useful commodity","Its convertibility into gold on demand","Its general acceptability, so a seller can accept money and use it to buy from anyone else","Its ability to earn interest for the holder"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Because money is generally acceptable, a seller no longer needs to find someone who wants exactly what he has; he takes money and spends it with whoever has what he needs. Intrinsic value (A) and convertibility into gold (B) are not needed, since modern paper money has neither, and earning interest (D) is a feature of near money, not of money itself."}},{"itemId":"6196e7ac-ae91-5c38-89b4-047a29423a43","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b8c27a8-4fa4-5a6a-a269-fedbae15b0cb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0472","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following has historically served as commodity money?","options":["Treasury bills","Cheques","Cowrie shells","Demand deposits"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Cowrie shells, like salt, gold and silver, have value of their own and were widely used as money in the past, which makes them commodity money. Treasury bills (A) are near money, a cheque (B) is only an instrument for transferring a bank deposit, and demand deposits (D) are a modern form of token money with no commodity value."}},{"itemId":"a6ab36bb-2e4b-5b9d-87d7-72b0c0bcbe8e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a7ebf28-4c9d-5545-85b0-8c9cdacd5d03","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0472-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Commodity money is best described as money that","options":["Has an intrinsic value of its own apart from its use as money, such as gold or salt","Is paper money that can be converted into gold on demand","Has value only because the government declares it legal tender","Exists only as entries in bank ledgers"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Commodity money is a medium of exchange that would still be valuable as a commodity even if it stopped being used as money, as with gold used for ornaments or salt used as food. Option B describes representative (convertible) token money, C describes fiat money, and D describes bank deposits, none of which has intrinsic value."}},{"itemId":"9aed7247-025c-53f4-8828-f0b40c518f28","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c1a4beff-ecfd-5d8f-a04d-b41f8214308c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0473","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Paper money that can be exchanged for a commodity such as gold on demand is known as","options":["Inconvertible token money","Representative (convertible) token money","Near money","Commodity money"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Representative or convertible token money is a paper claim that promises to pay a commodity, usually gold, on demand, like the old goldsmiths' receipts that people came to accept in payment. Inconvertible token money (A) cannot be exchanged for gold, near money (C) refers to assets like time deposits and treasury bills, and commodity money (D) is the commodity itself, such as gold coins, not paper representing it."}},{"itemId":"c3dad34b-e64e-57b5-bdeb-83e8ad6f4669","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a030106b-2a92-5eec-87f8-5205dbdf4fb0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0473-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Paper money that is not backed by gold or any other commodity, and whose supply is limited only by law, is best described as","options":["Representative convertible token money","Commodity money with a design or symbol","Near money","Inconvertible token money (fiduciary issue)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Inconvertible token money cannot be exchanged for gold or any commodity; its scarcity, and so its value, is kept by law rather than by a commodity reserve, and today's naira is of this type. Representative money (A) is convertible on demand, commodity money with a design (B) is something like a gold coin, and near money (C) is not legal tender at all."}},{"itemId":"ac611df6-0112-50eb-8e78-099c3583d8f4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ac3506da-2fe8-5a19-9ee4-aaa28b6d1d18","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0474","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigeria, narrow money (M1) is defined as","options":["Currency plus time and savings deposits","Demand deposits plus savings deposits only","Currency plus demand, time and savings deposits","Currency in circulation with the public plus demand deposits"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Narrow money, M1, covers the most liquid forms of money: cash held by the public plus demand (current account) deposits that can be spent by cheque. Option C is broad money (M2), which adds time and savings deposits, while A and B wrongly mix quasi-money into the narrow measure."}},{"itemId":"f87a3e63-aa0a-5f2a-97a8-ff2918e0383a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e165e3bd-2e35-5d04-a58c-4e21ecea4bbb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0474-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the definition of Nigeria's money supply, quasi-money refers to","options":["Time and savings deposits held by the public with banks","Currency in circulation with the public","Demand deposits on current accounts","Cash held in bank vaults"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Quasi-money is the sum of time and savings deposits: they earn interest and are a good store of value, but they are not as readily accepted as a means of payment, and adding them to M1 gives broad money (M2). Currency (B) and demand deposits (C) together make up M1, and cash in bank vaults (D) is not counted as money held by the public."}},{"itemId":"ef95c147-7574-5d48-8b04-5b25006f1fee","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"448d2051-e3fe-5703-a45b-4a9847165d83","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0475","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In 2009 an economy's money supply data (N billion) were: currency 150, demand deposits 400, savings deposits 350, time deposits 100, deposits in non-bank financial institutions (NBFIs) 600. What was narrow money (M1) in 2009?","options":["N1,000 billion","N1,600 billion","N400 billion","N550 billion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"M1 = currency + demand deposits = 150 + 400 = N550 billion. N1,000 billion (A) is M2, which also adds savings and time deposits, N1,600 billion (B) is M3, which further adds NBFI deposits, and N400 billion (C) counts only demand deposits and leaves out currency."}},{"itemId":"c727a715-668b-5bc8-8408-e9a28e85ca95","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4494b335-196a-577b-a00e-f159ce52fcd7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0475-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In 2008 an economy's money supply data (N billion) were: currency 100, demand deposits 300, savings deposits 280, time deposits 80, deposits in non-bank financial institutions (NBFIs) 500. What was broad money (M2) in 2008?","options":["N400 billion","N760 billion","N1,260 billion","N660 billion"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"M2 = M1 + quasi-money = (100 + 300) + (280 + 80) = N760 billion. N400 billion (A) is only M1, N1,260 billion (C) is M3 because it includes NBFI deposits, and N660 billion (D) adds up the bank deposits but leaves out currency."}},{"itemId":"a954b4a6-0fd6-5ff3-b6db-4186eb7bd52b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fbc3e1b-745a-52b4-90c0-b1faada11a81","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0475-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In 2007 an economy's money supply data (N billion) were: currency 80, demand deposits 320, savings deposits 300, time deposits 50, deposits in non-bank financial institutions (NBFIs) 400. What was the total money supply defined as M3 in 2007?","options":["N750 billion","N1,150 billion","N400 billion","N1,070 billion"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"M3 = currency + demand deposits + time and savings deposits + deposits of NBFIs = 80 + 320 + 300 + 50 + 400 = N1,150 billion. N750 billion (A) is M2, N400 billion (C) is M1, and N1,070 billion (D) leaves out the N80 billion of currency."}},{"itemId":"f7395155-d7bd-5807-a44d-7536a84f22be","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c9fa04e-f9ce-5005-9f85-a4228a7239e6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0476","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bank X receives a primary deposit of N1,000 million and must keep 25% of it as reserves. How much can it lend out at the first stage of the credit creation process?","options":["N250 million","N750 million","N1,000 million","N4,000 million"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The bank keeps 25% of N1,000 million, which is N250 million, as reserves and can lend out the remaining 75%, which is N750 million. N250 million (A) is the reserve, not the loan; N1,000 million (C) ignores the reserve requirement; and N4,000 million (D) is the total deposits the whole banking system can eventually create (N1,000 million x 4), not the first-stage loan."}},{"itemId":"2d474e7f-3607-5f96-807c-d88f9f84e8f0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0c9ea57f-9e30-569a-88f8-2a526d1f0ea5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0476-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the credit creation process, the part of a bank's primary deposit left after it has met the required reserve and expected withdrawals, which it is free to lend out, is called","options":["Excess reserve","Required reserve","Primary deposit","Special deposit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Excess reserve is what remains of a primary deposit after the bank has set aside the legally required reserve and what it expects depositors to withdraw; lending it out starts the credit creation chain. The required reserve (B) is the fraction the law says must be kept, the primary deposit (C) is the original cash deposit that starts the process, and a special deposit (D) is a separate central bank control tool."}},{"itemId":"d39d5b99-2eca-5600-b4f8-fc573e1b0242","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b0c57a8-b2b2-5705-baf8-655eb9c4ef60","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0477","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following does NOT limit the ability of the banking system to create credit?","options":["Borrowers redepositing their loans in other banks","The size of the initial cash deposit","The required reserve ratio","Leakages of cash out of the banking system"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"When borrowers redeposit their loan proceeds in other banks, the deposits keep circulating through the system, which is exactly what makes credit creation possible. The size of the initial deposit (B), the reserve requirement (C) and leakages such as cash kept at home (D) are the three factors that limit how much credit the banks can create."}},{"itemId":"be85debe-b63b-596e-af7d-63167afa1aa0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"414cdec9-4a1a-55b0-a573-96f9b8a44cd2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0477-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"With an initial deposit of N1,000 million and a reserve ratio of 25%, the banking system can create N4,000 million. If the reserve ratio rises to 40%, the total money that can be created becomes","options":["N4,000 million","N2,500 million","N6,400 million","N1,600 million"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The new multiplier is 1/0.40 = 2.5, so total money created = N1,000 million x 2.5 = N2,500 million. A higher reserve ratio lowers the multiplier, so the total must fall, which rules out N4,000 million (A) and N6,400 million (C); N1,600 million (D) comes from wrongly multiplying N4,000 million by 0.40."}},{"itemId":"679dc813-2ae9-5b42-bc3c-0a309594fa12","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a146a418-3b8a-528d-8ef9-7a37164bbf56","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0478","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An economy is able to create N500 million of money from an initial deposit of N50 million. What is the money multiplier in this economy?","options":["0.1","9","25","10"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The money multiplier is total money created divided by the initial deposit: N500 million / N50 million = 10, which also means the reserve ratio is 1/10, or 10%. Option A (0.1) is that reserve ratio, not the multiplier, option B (9) comes from using only the newly created N450 million, and option C (25) has no basis in the figures."}},{"itemId":"b868dbfc-8bc4-5cbe-a999-296dca0ec354","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4e3a9717-f411-5d59-89c4-36ddc3219324","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0479","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An economy creates N500 million of money from an initial deposit of N50 million. If it increases its reserve requirement by 100%, what is the new reserve requirement?","options":["10%","100%","110%","20%"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The original multiplier is 500/50 = 10, so the reserve requirement is 1/10 = 10%, and increasing it by 100% doubles it to 20%. Option A is the old requirement, option B confuses the size of the increase with the new ratio, and option C wrongly adds 100 percentage points."}},{"itemId":"f845759c-c7e6-5b8a-8dce-4bf2d87399c7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a49d3b19-de3c-5711-98ec-8c634ac1950f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0479-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An economy creates N500 million of money from an initial deposit of N50 million. If its reserve requirement is increased by 100% while the initial deposit is increased by 40%, what is the total money that can be created?","options":["N700 million","N350 million","N250 million","N1,400 million"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The original reserve ratio is 50/500 = 10%, so doubling it gives 20% and a new multiplier of 1/0.20 = 5. The new deposit is N50 million x 1.4 = N70 million, so total money = N70 million x 5 = N350 million. N700 million (A) ignores the higher reserve requirement, N250 million (C) ignores the larger deposit, and N1,400 million (D) wrongly uses 20 as the multiplier instead of 5."}},{"itemId":"53b3a14d-8f2b-58df-9207-b5148efc6e08","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"92bdfac5-6841-5223-85e2-57dfc6d2f95e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0480","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial markets are best described as markets that","options":["Deal only in the shares of quoted companies","Mobilise funds from surplus units (savers) and channel them to deficit units (borrowers and investors)","Exist mainly for the central bank to print and issue currency","Are used for buying and selling goods and services for money"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial markets link savers who have more funds than they need with households, firms and governments that need funds, and in doing so they determine the quantity and cost of funds in the economy. Option A describes only one part of the capital market, option C describes a central bank function rather than a market, and option D describes ordinary goods markets."}},{"itemId":"fdcbc4d2-4fe8-53cf-beed-c57279b6fc35","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d829255-aec9-5bff-8871-d8a9cbd19d4e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0480-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes the money market from the capital market?","options":["The money market deals in long-term funds, while the capital market deals in short-term funds","The money market deals in short-term funds and instruments, while the capital market deals in long-term funds","The money market serves only the government, while the capital market serves only private firms","The money market deals in shares, while the capital market deals in treasury bills"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The money market is where short-term funds and instruments, such as treasury bills, certificates of deposit and call money, are traded, while the capital market provides long-term funds through shares, bonds and similar securities. Option A reverses the two, option C is wrong because both markets serve government and private users, and option D swaps the typical instruments of each market."}},{"itemId":"5fd30942-fe6c-58dc-9652-a24f91fc88f0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"067d6da9-fb24-5b78-a9e2-941d28f35690","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0480-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the functions of financial markets?","options":["Mobilisation of savings","Fixing the prices of goods and services in the economy","Provision of liquidity","Protection against risks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial markets deal in funds and financial assets; they do not fix the prices of goods and services, which are set in goods markets. Mobilising savings (A), providing liquidity by letting people turn financial assets into cash (C) and protecting against risks through instruments like insurance policies (D) are all recognised functions, along with storing wealth, providing credit, providing payment facilities and helping to carry out government policy."}},{"itemId":"97d72c1b-8690-5136-99b3-2e9dc584a7b2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d3b3b93f-1f67-5dd5-b6f1-11cd2d0ee9b2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0481","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The financial market is commonly divided into two major segments, the money market and the capital market. Which statement correctly distinguishes them?","options":["The money market deals in medium- to long-term funds such as shares and bonds, while the capital market deals in short-term instruments such as treasury bills.","The money market deals only in foreign currencies, while the capital market deals only in instruments denominated in naira.","The money market deals in short-term funds and instruments (up to one year), while the capital market deals in medium- to long-term funds such as shares and bonds.","The money market is open only to the Central Bank of Nigeria, while the capital market is open only to private individual investors."],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The dividing line between the two markets is the maturity of the funds traded. The money market handles short-term borrowing and lending of up to about one year, using instruments like treasury bills and commercial papers, while the capital market raises medium- and long-term funds through shares and bonds. Option A simply swaps the two markets around, and options B and D are wrong because neither market is defined by currency or restricted to one type of participant."}},{"itemId":"95660d34-37e8-5aa2-bc9c-8a10a33cff2b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a40ac60-1b27-59ac-96b9-bd196d83b1a0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0481-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a capital market instrument rather than a money market instrument?","options":["Treasury bills","Bankers' acceptances","Commercial papers","Ordinary shares (equities) of a quoted company"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Ordinary shares are long-term instruments with no fixed maturity, so they are traded in the capital market. Treasury bills (option A), commercial papers (option C) and bankers' acceptances (option B) are all short-term instruments that usually mature within a year, which makes them money market instruments."}},{"itemId":"ff05ed34-694b-5c25-a5f7-05e9e20e4c96","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8eca8670-edde-5b76-8292-4fe0dc806583","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0482","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best distinguishes a deposit money bank from a non-bank financial institution such as an insurance company or a pension fund?","options":["Only banks act as intermediaries between savers and borrowers; non-bank financial institutions do not channel funds to borrowers at all.","A bank accepts deposits withdrawable on demand by cheque, which serve as money and allow it to create credit; non-bank institutions do not.","Banks deal only in long-term funds, while non-bank financial institutions deal only in short-term funds repayable within one year.","Non-bank financial institutions are not regulated by any government agency, while banks are regulated by the Central Bank of Nigeria."],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"What sets banks apart is that their demand (current account) deposits can be spent by cheque or transfer, so they count as money and allow banks to create credit. Insurance companies, pension funds and finance houses mobilise funds through premiums, contributions or placements, which are not used as money. Option A is wrong because both groups are financial intermediaries, option D is wrong because bodies such as NAICOM and PenCom regulate non-bank institutions, and option C is wrong because banks lend mostly short-term."}},{"itemId":"faf9289c-22d8-5be1-88fa-4ed48e8d6c4c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"47a61c30-eb85-5e0f-963b-4c7c7f893a95","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0482-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What basic function do bank financial institutions and non-bank financial institutions have in common?","options":["Both issue the national currency on behalf of the Federal Government.","Both act as lenders of last resort to the banking system.","Both mobilise funds from surplus units (savers) and channel them to deficit units (borrowers and investors).","Both accept current account deposits that customers can withdraw by cheque."],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Banks and non-bank financial institutions are both financial intermediaries: they pool funds from those with surplus money and pass them on to those who need funds for investment or spending. Options A and B describe functions of the Central Bank of Nigeria, not of ordinary financial institutions. Option D is a feature of deposit money banks only, which is exactly what separates them from non-bank institutions."}},{"itemId":"48d40eef-ca1e-59b3-a142-b3edafebb660","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99bc3442-ad9c-50e5-8a86-23dac93a98c1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0483","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Direct financing means that a borrower raises funds directly from fund owners, without the help of financial institutions. Which of the following is NOT a weakness of direct financing?","options":["The fund owner is likely to charge a high price to compensate for his risk.","Much time and effort is needed to find a fund owner who is willing to lend.","Funds are usually released for only a short period, making long-term investment difficult.","The risk borne by each lender is low because funds are pooled from many savers."],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Pooling funds from many savers so that the risk per person is low is an advantage that financial intermediaries bring, not a feature of direct financing. Without intermediaries, a borrower must search for a willing lender (option B), pay a high price because that single lender bears all the risk (option A), and usually gets the money for only a short time (option C). These are exactly the weaknesses that financial institutions remove."}},{"itemId":"f4ed080c-e16c-5961-9a45-37cd46f7ba7c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"109643b0-dde4-5192-9d6a-81995bc9a9d5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0484","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why are savers generally more willing to entrust their funds to a financial institution than to lend directly to an individual borrower?","options":["The institution promises every saver a share of the profits made by each of its borrowers.","The institution has a reputation to protect, pays interest and lets savers withdraw their funds when needed.","The institution is barred by law from lending out any of the funds deposited with it.","The institution guarantees that savers will never pay any charges on their accounts."],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial institutions attract funds because savers trust an established institution more than a private individual, they earn interest on money that would otherwise lie idle, and they can usually get their money back on demand, for example from a demand deposit. Option C is wrong because lending out deposits is how institutions earn their income, and options A and D promise things financial institutions do not offer."}},{"itemId":"5ae5f8e1-f5de-5e5e-9fbd-78d384f61184","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc980c14-1bb4-50b7-b924-6b347da1a892","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0485","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How do financial intermediaries mainly promote the savings-investment process and capital formation in an economy?","options":["By printing new money and lending it directly to investors who need funds","By pooling funds from surplus units and channelling them into productive investment by deficit units","By taking ownership of every business that borrows money from them","By transferring funds from investors (deficit units) back to savers (surplus units)"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial intermediaries collect savings, including small sums that would otherwise lie idle, and use their expertise to lend them to firms and others with productive investment needs, which raises capital formation and growth. Option A is wrong because only the central bank issues currency, option D reverses the direction in which funds flow, and option C is wrong because lending does not make an intermediary the owner of the borrower's business."}},{"itemId":"ef1cea63-a3dc-58b6-84c4-baa1fa72bdf9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d65cd8bd-8ec1-5573-bab1-bebb9e0543cd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0486","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following are the three main factors that make for successful financial intermediation?","options":["Government ownership, foreign management and a large, impressive head office","Liquidity, profitability and solvency of the institution's balance sheet","Public trust and confidence, convenience of dealing with the institution, and the price of its services","High interest charges on loans, long processing times and few branches"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Intermediaries thrive when the public trusts that their money is safe and can be retrieved at will, when it is convenient and easy to do business with them, and when their prices, such as interest charges and fees, are reasonable. Option B lists the three goals a deposit money bank tries to balance, not the factors behind successful intermediation. Options A and D would not attract customers, and high charges and delays would actually drive them away."}},{"itemId":"7a4f3871-b114-5ad4-972e-91b5b7b12276","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"077bb0c6-13b8-5157-bf1e-78f474bc4be6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0486-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the public loses confidence in financial institutions and withdraws funds from them in large numbers, the process that follows is known as:","options":["Bank consolidation","Credit creation","Financial deepening","Financial disintermediation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial disintermediation happens when funds move out of financial institutions, for example when people stop trusting banks and keep their money at home. If it spreads, it can disrupt the payments system and weaken the whole economy. Credit creation (option B) is the expansion of deposits through lending, bank consolidation (option A) refers to mergers and recapitalisation, and financial deepening (option C) is the growth of the financial system, the opposite of what happens here."}},{"itemId":"196313cf-7228-5abe-8db9-1d44f9eff919","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3013c017-3b2a-5bff-9bc1-267d96c2e9d1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0487","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why is there said to be a conflict between liquidity and profitability for a deposit money bank?","options":["Loans earn interest but tie up cash, while idle cash keeps the bank liquid but earns no income.","Staying liquid requires a bank to lend out all its deposits as long-term loans to investors.","Liquid assets such as cash earn the highest returns, so a more liquid bank is always more profitable.","A bank's profit depends only on its number of branches, not on the kinds of assets it holds."],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Lending is what earns a bank most of its profit, but every naira lent out is no longer available to meet depositors' withdrawals, so more lending means less liquidity. On the other hand, cash kept to meet withdrawals earns nothing, so a very liquid bank sacrifices profit. Option C is wrong because cash earns no interest, option B reverses the relationship between lending and liquidity, and option D ignores the role of earning assets in producing income."}},{"itemId":"e69a9bba-9395-5aa8-a79c-39b315873511","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8dbba85-ee81-5a9a-be6f-47616525478f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0487-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the context of the goals of a deposit money bank, solvency means the bank's ability to:","options":["open new branches in every state of the federation","earn a high return for its shareholders in the current financial year","meet customers' day-to-day cash withdrawals immediately on demand","meet all its obligations in the long run, with its assets exceeding its liabilities"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"A solvent bank can meet all its obligations in the long run because the value of its assets is greater than its liabilities. Option C describes liquidity, which is about meeting immediate cash demands, and option B describes profitability. A bank needs both liquidity and profitability to remain solvent, while option A has nothing to do with solvency."}},{"itemId":"4d5c25f3-86cd-51b7-b0d3-07a8f8347822","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7de399b8-3951-58db-a434-2051776c27ab","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0488","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Bank of British West Africa (BBWA), which took over the business of the African Banking Corporation in colonial Nigeria, is the forerunner of which present-day bank?","options":["United Bank for Africa","Wema Bank","Union Bank of Nigeria","First Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Bank of British West Africa later became Standard Bank of Nigeria and was eventually renamed First Bank of Nigeria. Union Bank (option C) grew out of Barclays Bank DCO, while UBA (option A) grew out of the British and French Bank for Commerce and Industry. Wema Bank (option B) is an indigenous bank that began as Agbonmagbe Bank."}},{"itemId":"f71daa84-fabb-5702-86ff-ea908da0e124","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cb86947f-0830-52cc-8d57-f80d972d0c6c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0488-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following early indigenous banks survived the wave of bank failures that swept Nigeria before the Central Bank of Nigeria was established?","options":["Industrial and Commercial Bank","Nigerian Farmers and Commercial Bank","Nigerian Mercantile Bank","National Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The National Bank of Nigeria, founded in 1933, was one of the few indigenous banks to survive, helped by strong backing from the Western Regional Government. The Industrial and Commercial Bank (option A) and the Nigerian Mercantile Bank (option C) both collapsed in the 1930s, and the Nigerian Farmers and Commercial Bank (option B) failed during the crash of the early 1950s."}},{"itemId":"e833f72d-d6c8-5d11-bedc-9379e126dcad","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b8fbeb23-67d9-5567-8ec5-b48dbd726cb1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0489","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a cause of the collapse of many indigenous banks in Nigeria before the Central Bank of Nigeria was established?","options":["Absence of banking regulation and supervision, which allowed fraud to thrive","Weak capital bases that could not absorb losses or shocks","Untrained and inexperienced staff and poor management","Strict cash reserve requirements imposed on them by the Central Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Central Bank of Nigeria did not begin operations until 1959, so it could not have caused the bank failures of the 1930s to 1950s. The real causes were weak capital (option B), inexperienced and untrained staff who managed the banks poorly (option C), and the lack of any banking law or supervisory authority before 1952, which allowed fraud and reckless lending (option A)."}},{"itemId":"351dcf5d-ebca-5d65-9eef-4858078a435e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b563cd3f-6235-5494-a5fe-8431f9d3c8f7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0490","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of a commercial bank?","options":["Accepting deposits from members of the public","Providing safe custody for customers' valuables and documents","Granting loans and overdrafts and discounting bills for customers","Issuing the country's legal tender currency"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Issuing legal tender is reserved for the Central Bank of Nigeria, so it is not a commercial bank function. Accepting deposits (option A) and granting advances such as loans, overdrafts and bill discounting (option C) are the two principal functions of a commercial bank, while safe custody (option B) is one of its ancillary services."}},{"itemId":"c0294703-5de8-532e-9ea4-9560be04fc54","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a05a0806-baed-5256-9a36-70f6a1175250","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0490-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A trader holding an N1,000,000 trade bill that matures in three months takes it to his bank, which pays him N950,000 now and collects the full N1,000,000 at maturity. This service is known as:","options":["Equipment leasing","A standing order","Bill discounting","An overdraft"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"In bill discounting, the bank buys a bill before it matures for less than its face value and collects the full amount at maturity; here the N50,000 difference is the bank's earning. An overdraft (option D) lets a current account holder draw more than his balance, a standing order (option B) is an instruction to make regular payments, and equipment leasing (option A) involves renting out an asset."}},{"itemId":"1d001ba2-ee18-5418-8e24-9289100adc54","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"20dfeeaa-16e3-5b3c-84f4-2c5e0e50691a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0491","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In managing its portfolio, a commercial bank balances its assets against its liabilities. Which of the following appears on the LIABILITIES side of a commercial bank's balance sheet?","options":["Investments in treasury bills","Loans and advances to customers","Customers' deposits","Cash in the bank's vaults"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Customers' deposits are money the bank owes to its depositors, so they sit on the liabilities side together with shareholders' funds and borrowings from other banks or the central bank. Loans and advances (option B), cash (option D) and investments in treasury bills (option A) are things the bank owns or is owed, so they are assets."}},{"itemId":"6e3ede00-848e-5337-abb0-8acafea1a8ff","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ee328b7a-8790-548c-8437-6997203c1653","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0491-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of a commercial bank's assets is the most liquid but earns no income for the bank?","options":["Loans and advances","Cash","Investments in government stocks","Bank premises and equipment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Cash is perfectly liquid because it can be paid out immediately, but it earns no interest, which is why holding too much of it reduces profit. Loans and advances (option A) earn the most interest but are much less liquid, government stocks (option C) earn income but must be sold to raise cash, and premises (option D) are fixed assets that are not held for liquidity at all."}},{"itemId":"ad8fb6a9-8d00-5acf-8edc-066300ffc9ce","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a9a9234f-83aa-57d6-8083-90d0925d054a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0492","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which law, passed after the Paton enquiry into bank failures, was the first banking legislation in Nigeria?","options":["The Central Bank of Nigeria Act of 1958","The Banks and Other Financial Institutions Decree of 1991","The Banking Decree of 1969","The Banking Ordinance of 1952"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Following the 1948 Paton enquiry into the failures of indigenous banks, the colonial government passed the Banking Ordinance of 1952, which introduced licensing and minimum capital and reserve requirements for banks. The CBN Act of 1958 (option A) created the central bank, the Banking Decree of 1969 (option C) later replaced the earlier banking laws, and the 1991 decree (option B) came much later."}},{"itemId":"f443516f-13a9-5fe7-b3f5-6f4021cac848","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fb4c4bfa-423c-57bf-ab67-8c1304840f63","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0492-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which law, enacted in 2020, replaced the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act of 1991 as the main law regulating banks and other financial institutions in Nigeria?","options":["The Companies and Allied Matters Act 1990","The Central Bank of Nigeria Act 2007","The Banking Ordinance of 1958","The Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act (BOFIA) 2020"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"BOFIA 2020 repealed and replaced the 1991 law and is now the principal law on the licensing, regulation and supervision of banks and other financial institutions. The CBN Act 2007 (option B) deals with the central bank itself, the Companies and Allied Matters Act (option A) governs companies in general, and the 1958 banking ordinance (option C) was a colonial-era law replaced long ago."}},{"itemId":"f3ce6451-9e2b-559e-86e0-542ddd5b88b6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5898be9e-413b-53c5-b6e1-ee872f41b799","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0493","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Merchant banks are often described as 'wholesale bankers', while commercial banks are called 'retail bankers'. This is because merchant banks:","options":["mobilise funds mainly from firms and institutions in large amounts and tailor their services to businesses","sell goods at wholesale prices to traders, distributors and market women","collect most of their deposits from individuals and households through many branches","deal only in foreign currencies and never in naira deposits or loans"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Merchant banks deal mainly with corporate and institutional customers, taking large deposits and granting bulkier, often medium-term loans, which is why they are called wholesale bankers. Commercial banks, by contrast, collect funds from all strata of society, which is what option C describes. Option B confuses banking with trading in goods, and option D is wrong because merchant banks also deal in naira."}},{"itemId":"fc7d1c99-6d2a-5268-9a03-eb7cd1dbefb0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e18a655d-8dec-58c9-aa79-e43d83395b03","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0494","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A merchant bank buys a machine needed by a manufacturing firm and rents it to the firm in return for agreed periodic payments. This service is known as:","options":["Issuing house service","A bankers' acceptance","Equipment leasing","Bill discounting"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"In equipment leasing, the bank owns the asset and the firm uses it in exchange for rentals, which helps firms that cannot afford to buy expensive machinery outright. Bill discounting (option D) involves buying a bill for less than its face value, an issuing house service (option A) helps a company sell its shares to the public, and a bankers' acceptance (option B) is a bill that a bank has agreed to pay."}},{"itemId":"f63d41ac-669f-5cc9-9cf5-047faf640b6a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"100e535f-6eec-5514-b052-7c95e1330b8d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0494-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a merchant bank helps a company to sell its new shares and other securities to the public, it is acting as:","options":["A bureau de change","An issuing house","A clearing house","A discount house"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"An issuing house manages the offer of a company's shares or other securities to the investing public, which is one of the main services of merchant banks. A discount house (option D) deals in short-term government securities in the money market, a clearing house (option C) settles cheques and other claims between banks, and a bureau de change (option A) deals in foreign currency for small users."}},{"itemId":"422012e9-03e9-5381-a9bb-732d7743930a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d36805dd-57ab-5519-80c7-9f8e8be3231a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0495","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"NAL Merchant Bank, licensed in 1969 as Nigeria's first merchant bank, grew out of which company that had been carrying out merchant banking functions since 1960?","options":["Nigerian Acceptances Limited","Industrial and Commercial Bank","Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry","Bank of British West Africa"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Merchant banking in Nigeria began with firms such as Nigerian Acceptances Limited, set up in 1960, which financed trade and acted as an issuing house before becoming NAL Merchant Bank in 1969. The Industrial and Commercial Bank (option B) was an early indigenous commercial bank, the Bank of British West Africa (option D) was a colonial commercial bank that became First Bank, and the NBCI (option C) was a development bank set up in the 1970s."}},{"itemId":"16a6fec7-ca77-5fc1-9f59-8b8a7ec24669","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"23e1828d-2ec0-5eb9-b769-c816281caa1b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0496","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Nigerian banking reforms of 2004-2005, 'bank consolidation' mainly involved:","options":["banks merging with or acquiring one another and raising fresh capital to meet a much higher minimum capital requirement","the Central Bank of Nigeria taking over the ownership and day-to-day management of all commercial banks in the country","splitting the large banks into many smaller banks so as to increase competition in the banking industry","converting all commercial and merchant banks into microfinance banks serving rural communities"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Consolidation strengthens a banking system by creating fewer, bigger and better-capitalised banks, mainly through mergers, acquisitions and fresh capital raised from investors. In Nigeria it reduced the number of banks from 89 to 25 by the end of 2005. Options B, C and D describe nationalisation, break-up and conversion, none of which was the aim of consolidation."}},{"itemId":"843d38d3-ac2a-5a94-99f1-5a1ec01e4512","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c4bfcc06-79f2-5697-83f5-bdc734368d04","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0496-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the bank consolidation programme announced by the CBN in July 2004, every Nigerian bank was required to have minimum shareholders' funds of how much by 31 December 2005?","options":["N25 billion","N5 billion","N100 billion","N2 billion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The 2004 consolidation programme raised the minimum capital of banks from N2 billion to N25 billion, with a compliance deadline of 31 December 2005. Option D was the old requirement that the reform replaced, while options B and C were not the figure set in 2004. Banks met the new level mainly through mergers, acquisitions and public offers, and those that could not lost their licences."}},{"itemId":"fe307153-df43-5188-823d-6c2e91fa606f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fdf1969c-4a24-5763-8394-119244362f81","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0497","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A major reason for setting up development banks in Nigeria is that deposit money banks:","options":["prefer short-term lending because their deposits are mostly short-term, leaving a gap in long-term finance","are not allowed by law to grant any loans or overdrafts to business customers","lend mainly long-term to agriculture and housing, leaving no funds for trade","charge no interest on their loans and so cannot cover their operating costs"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Because most of their deposits can be withdrawn at short notice, deposit money banks concentrate on short- and medium-term lending, yet a developing economy needs long-term finance for industry, agriculture and infrastructure. Development banks are set up to fill this gap. Option C reverses the real situation, while options B and D are simply untrue of deposit money banks."}},{"itemId":"a3e744d2-7903-500f-aac6-610b91c65ee6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"db2d5e7e-4229-5e56-bd12-39d2275463d6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0497-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The government set up the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria because conventional banks found housing finance unattractive, even though housing is vital to the economy. Which rationale for development banks does this illustrate?","options":["Financing projects that are socially desirable but commercially unprofitable","Mobilising short-term funds for trading in the money market","Issuing currency on behalf of the Federal Government","Acting as lender of last resort to the deposit money banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Development banks are set up to finance projects that benefit society but promise little profit, and housing is a classic example, which is why the government created the FMBN. The other rationales include filling the long-term finance gap, channelling funds from international development banks to local investors, and promoting catalyst sectors like agriculture. Options D and C are central bank functions, and option B is not a reason for setting up development banks."}},{"itemId":"82a7a51d-2a3b-53d1-9f5e-52584ea41b36","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7b8ebc3c-950a-536f-b477-a5786d88432f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0498","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian Industrial Development Bank (NIDB) was set up mainly to:","options":["finance urban infrastructure such as motor parks, markets and drainage systems","give small interest-free loans without collateral to petty traders and the rural poor","provide long-term loans and equity finance to medium- and large-scale industrial enterprises","grant mortgage loans to individuals building owner-occupier houses"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NIDB's job was to identify and promote industrial projects with high value added, mobilise funds and skills from home and abroad, and provide long-term loans and equity to industrial firms, mostly medium and large ones. Option B describes the Peoples Bank of Nigeria, option A the Urban Development Bank of Nigeria, and option D the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria."}},{"itemId":"291f6975-56fb-570b-ab6f-b0fa4ee5f1d9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"82bfa1a6-54d0-5a11-a8cc-69aedcb71f2e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0499","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT one of the services the NIDB provided to promote medium- and large-scale industries in Nigeria?","options":["Mobilising funds from bodies like the World Bank for on-lending to Nigerian entrepreneurs","Accepting current account deposits from the general public and issuing cheque books","Taking up equity shares in large industrial enterprises","Providing long-term loans to medium and large industrial enterprises"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"As a development bank, the NIDB did not run current accounts for the public; that is a deposit money bank activity. Its services included long-term loans (option D), equity participation (option C), mobilising foreign funds from multilateral lenders such as the World Bank and the African Development Bank (option A), and managerial and technical help to firms."}},{"itemId":"f8e0d579-29c1-5819-991e-847c713383de","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"94d5da7d-8181-5c59-9a05-54c514eaf742","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0500","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How did the Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry (NBCI) mainly differ from the Nigerian Industrial Development Bank (NIDB)?","options":["NBCI focused on large-scale industries, while NIDB was set up to help small enterprises and Nigerians buy into businesses under indigenisation.","NBCI was a foreign-owned commercial bank taking public deposits, while NIDB was a government-owned microfinance bank.","NBCI focused on small and medium enterprises and on helping Nigerians buy into businesses under indigenisation, while NIDB focused on medium and large industries.","NBCI financed only agricultural projects in rural areas, while NIDB financed only housing projects in the cities."],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NBCI was created after the indigenisation decree of 1972 to give Nigerians long-term funds to acquire shares in businesses, with its focus on small and medium enterprises. The NIDB, on the other hand, concentrated on medium- and large-scale industrial projects. Option A swaps the two banks' roles, and options D and B misdescribe both institutions."}},{"itemId":"ce59a8b7-0364-55d8-bdf0-d12ab496f861","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b0bb4de-c8c0-59ef-9570-94418362e293","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0501","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT an objective for setting up the Nigerian Agricultural and Cooperative Bank (NACB)?","options":["Financing urban mass transit and motor parks in state capitals","Increasing the domestic supply of agricultural raw materials for industry","Increasing the production of export crops to earn foreign exchange","Achieving self-sufficiency in food production"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Financing urban transport facilities was the work of the Urban Development Bank of Nigeria, not the agricultural bank. The NACB was set up to provide finance for agriculture so that Nigeria could feed itself (option D), supply raw materials to its industries (option B), and earn foreign exchange from exported crops (option C)."}},{"itemId":"ca94641a-9ea9-5205-bf6c-68fb70b3ce14","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c6c271f3-e727-5222-aeee-6dc21c38d9c8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0503","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Urban Development Bank of Nigeria (UDBN), set up in 1992, was established mainly to:","options":["finance the building and rehabilitation of urban infrastructure such as markets, motor parks and mass transit","finance farmers and cooperative societies in rural areas","regulate and supervise community banks across the country","provide long-term loans for owner-occupier houses under the National Housing Fund"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The UDBN was created to fill the gap left by conventional banks in financing urban infrastructure such as markets, motor parks, mass transit, drainage and waste disposal, and to give technical help to state and local governments on such projects. Option B describes agricultural finance, option C was the role of the National Board for Community Banks, and option D describes the housing role of the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria."}},{"itemId":"ce0c5898-f3f3-5adc-a44e-9aa8954bb269","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"95478196-976d-5b49-b082-d6f47532f384","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0504","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the National Housing Fund (NHF) scheme was set up in 1992, which institution was given the responsibility of managing the Fund?","options":["The Central Bank of Nigeria","The Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria","The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","The Urban Development Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NHF pooled contributions from workers and investments from banks and insurance companies to provide long-term housing finance, and the FMBN was made its manager. This gave the FMBN a steady pool of funds, which it had previously lacked. The CBN (option A) and the NDIC (option C) are banking regulators, and the UDBN (option D) finances urban infrastructure rather than housing loans."}},{"itemId":"6c3604d0-3fff-5dd0-b411-7511d5cccc6c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"07eb4d9f-240a-5e4a-8a0f-2d6d5cf1f4cf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0504-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"After primary mortgage institutions (PMIs) were introduced in 1989, how was housing finance in Nigeria shared between the PMIs and the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN)?","options":["PMIs and FMBN were both stopped from granting any housing loans to the public.","PMIs lent to the public at the retail level, while FMBN acted as the apex body providing wholesale funds and oversight.","PMIs financed only urban roads and bridges, while FMBN financed only farm buildings.","FMBN lent directly to every home buyer, while PMIs only supervised and regulated the FMBN."],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Primary mortgage institutions mobilise savings and grant loans to individuals who want to buy, build or renovate homes, which is retail housing finance. The FMBN became the apex mortgage institution, lending to PMIs and, for a time, overseeing them. Option D reverses the roles, and options C and A misstate what both institutions do."}},{"itemId":"22cdc998-8bf5-54d2-841d-72a24d59d3f6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c4ee5b0a-8606-5812-b1d3-96d5b360d1eb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0505","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which development in the 1990s mainly led the Federal Government to merge some development banks in the early 2000s?","options":["The transfer of all development banking functions to the Nigerian Stock Exchange","The banks had become so profitable that government wanted to share out their profits","A court ruling that development banks could no longer lend to industry or agriculture","Widespread loan defaults and financial distress that left the institutions weak"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"During the banking distress of the 1990s, many borrowers of the development banks defaulted, partly because the rising naira cost of imported inputs crippled their businesses, and the banks in turn struggled to repay funds borrowed from international lenders. The government responded by merging the weak institutions into stronger ones, such as the Bank of Industry. Options B, C and A are not what happened."}},{"itemId":"b4b58ad4-0867-5f79-bfeb-1908011da00b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"950cab6d-e8a5-54f9-912e-69ed577c016a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0505-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian Agricultural Cooperative and Rural Development Bank (NACRDB), formed in 2000, was a merger of the Nigerian Agricultural and Cooperative Bank (NACB) with:","options":["the Peoples Bank of Nigeria and the Family Economic Advancement Programme (FEAP)","the Nigerian Industrial Development Bank and the Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry","the National Economic Reconstruction Fund and the Nigerian Export-Import Bank","the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria and the Urban Development Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NACRDB brought together three bodies serving rural and low-income borrowers: the NACB, the Peoples Bank of Nigeria and the FEAP. The NIDB and NBCI (option B) were merged with NERFUND to form the Bank of Industry, so options B and C mix up the two mergers. Option D names housing and urban development institutions that were not part of it."}},{"itemId":"899c92fc-3fd8-5355-8da3-951cc2fd2f32","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f71aa5b0-f967-5eb5-b30c-51082a22c9f1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0506","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes the main purpose of the Bank of Industry (BOI)?","options":["Providing long-term loans and equity finance to revive, expand and modernise industrial enterprises, including SMEs","Financing only agricultural cooperatives and individual farmers in rural areas","Issuing treasury bills and other short-term securities on behalf of the Federal Government","Regulating and supervising the deposit money banks on behalf of the Federal Government"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The BOI was set up to improve the productive capacity of the economy by giving industrial firms, especially small and medium enterprises, long-term funds in the form of loans and equity, and by reviving and modernising ailing but viable industries. Supervising banks (option D) and issuing treasury bills for the government (option C) are CBN functions, and agricultural finance (option B) was handled by the agricultural development bank."}},{"itemId":"e6db8220-62ba-5b53-a3cd-0861bb75cf63","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d6564604-55c9-543b-a9e4-2cccd7a8e6ee","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0507","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Specialised banks such as the Peoples Bank of Nigeria and community banks were set up mainly because:","options":["large corporations needed banks that could lend them bigger sums in foreign currency","the Central Bank of Nigeria wanted to stop deposit money banks from operating in cities","the government wanted banks that would invest mainly in shares on the stock exchange","the rural poor and small urban entrepreneurs could not get credit from conventional banks, lacking collateral and nearby branches"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"In the late 1980s many rural areas had no bank branches, and the poor and micro-entrepreneurs who did live near banks could not meet the collateral demands or afford the interest charged. The Peoples Bank and community banks were created to bring small loans and savings facilities to these groups. Options A, B and C describe goals that have nothing to do with these grassroots banks."}},{"itemId":"fe6d167f-2e6a-58cc-9f94-1f430ccbb40e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"660dd476-ef88-54d4-b254-5430a1448ee9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0508","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Peoples Bank of Nigeria granted small loans without collateral. How did it mainly try to ensure that its loans were repaid?","options":["By requiring each borrower to obtain a repayment guarantee from a commercial bank","By lending to members of recognised trade or business groups, so that group members pressured one another to repay","By holding the borrowers' land title documents until the loans were fully repaid","By deducting repayments directly from borrowers' salaries through their employers"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Peoples Bank lent to people who belonged to recognised trade or business groups, and if one member defaulted, others in the group could not get loans, so members monitored and pressured each other. This peer pressure took the place of collateral. Options C and A are forms of security that the bank's poor customers could not provide, and option D would not work for traders and artisans who earned no salaries."}},{"itemId":"ff8ce5e1-0fb0-51bb-9d2a-729f4e43d258","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5a7acb28-c2b9-57c9-a47b-d9407004a662","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0509","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a challenge faced by the Peoples Bank of Nigeria?","options":["A shortage of skilled managerial staff to run its many offices","The high cost of administering many small loans spread across the country","Heavy dependence on government funding for its operations","Excessive demand for its loans by large multinational companies"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Peoples Bank served the rural poor and petty traders, not large multinationals, so option D was never one of its problems. It did struggle with the cost of managing many tiny loans (option B), a lack of skilled managers (option A) and reliance on government funds (option C), as well as loan defaults and losses on deposits it had placed with failed banks."}},{"itemId":"b436279f-cad9-5d29-b958-b0a14309dcf7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"05829b0c-f817-568e-a12b-642f36e8a523","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0510","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a main difference between a community bank and a commercial bank in Nigeria?","options":["A community bank could accept deposits but was not allowed to grant credit.","A community bank dealt mainly in foreign exchange for importers and exporters.","A community bank was owned and managed directly by the Central Bank of Nigeria.","A community bank's operations were limited to the community in which it was located."],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"A community bank performed most of the same basic functions as a commercial bank, but it served only the community where it was located and could not deal in foreign exchange without CBN approval. Option A is wrong because community banks did grant credit to their customers, option C is wrong because they were owned by the community rather than the CBN, and option B reverses their restriction on foreign exchange business."}},{"itemId":"d8cc3e21-a9bd-515b-a540-ea8c72941165","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"437b5927-3c66-5d3e-96ba-43b2262ae7b6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0511","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a factor in the collapse of many community banks in Nigeria in the 1990s?","options":["An excessively large capital base that made them difficult to manage","Inflation and the rising cost of doing business","High rates of loan default by their customers","Loss of funds they had placed with distressed deposit money banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Community banks started with very small capital, so an oversized capital base was never their problem. Many failed because the deposit money banks holding their funds became distressed (option D), because customers hit by a harsh economy defaulted on their loans (option C), and because inflation and high operating costs squeezed them (option B)."}},{"itemId":"0f1f08cd-ed5d-5ac8-b777-321c3d2b5976","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3be3cb0b-9683-5914-ba7c-40734e186e1e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0512","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Microfinance Policy launched by the CBN in December 2005, what were existing community banks required to do?","options":["Become discount houses dealing in treasury bills in the money market","Convert to microfinance banks within 24 months after meeting the new capital and other requirements","Close down permanently and refund all their depositors' money","Merge with the Bank of Industry within 12 months to form a single development bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The 2005 policy required licensed community banks to transform into microfinance banks within 24 months, mainly by raising their shareholders' funds to the new minimum of N20 million. The policy also brought informal microfinance providers under CBN supervision and introduced measures such as deposit insurance for microfinance banks and a Microfinance Development Fund. Options D, A and C were not part of the policy."}},{"itemId":"3b5e41a6-3045-5e2a-afea-6b2800b31383","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"07dbcba2-a15f-5a00-82cb-2300ccefa76b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0513","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN's 2005 Microfinance Policy, microfinance banks were expected mainly to:","options":["underwrite large government bond issues in the capital market","provide diversified, affordable and dependable financial services to the economically active poor","finance the foreign trade of large multinational corporations","issue the national currency in rural areas on behalf of the CBN"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Microfinance banks were meant to serve the active poor and micro-entrepreneurs who lacked access to regular banks, by mobilising their savings, giving them small loans, creating jobs and raising household incomes. They were also to serve as channels for government micro-credit programmes. Options C and A describe large corporate and capital market business, and option D is a function reserved for the CBN."}},{"itemId":"bda1b170-ff4e-5532-8203-ac43128f610a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d100d921-3844-5f52-901e-f865231e3e1f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0514","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN's 2005 Microfinance Policy framework, how did a unit microfinance bank differ from a state microfinance bank?","options":["A unit MFB could only grant loans, while a state MFB could only accept deposits.","A unit MFB needed minimum capital of N20 million, while a state MFB needed N1 billion.","A unit MFB needed minimum capital of N1 billion, while a state MFB needed N20 million.","A unit MFB was owned by the Federal Government, while a state MFB was owned by a state government."],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The 2005 framework created two categories: the smaller unit MFB, with minimum paid-up capital of N20 million, and the larger state MFB, serving a whole state, which needed N1 billion. Option C reverses the figures, option D is wrong because the names refer to the scope of operation rather than government ownership, and option A is wrong because both types could take deposits and grant loans. These capital levels applied under the 2005 framework and have since been reviewed."}},{"itemId":"b41e492f-d7ff-5d7f-9712-58d16ff2abd1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4794738a-63f6-5630-966e-84acd512ccf4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0515","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a weakness of the West African Currency Board (WACB) that made the establishment of the Central Bank of Nigeria necessary?","options":["It invested Nigeria's reserves in local industries instead of in the financial markets of developed countries.","It lent too freely to Nigerian banks as a lender of last resort, which caused rapid inflation in the colony.","It gave Nigeria no independent monetary policy, since the local money supply simply moved with the country's sterling earnings.","It issued a separate, inconvertible currency for Nigeria that was not recognised outside the country."],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The WACB, based in London, issued currency for the British West African colonies fully backed by sterling, so the money supply in Nigeria rose and fell with export earnings and there was no room for an independent monetary policy. It also invested the colonies' reserves abroad and did not act as a lender of last resort, so option B is wrong and option A reverses what it did. Option D is wrong because the WACB currency was freely convertible into sterling and widely recognised."}},{"itemId":"7eb67c1b-1ab3-5694-b5f3-ca9e22aaacd5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"88e1f514-cc78-5890-98ec-ab219a53e650","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0516","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which report, submitted in 1957, recommended the establishment of the Central Bank of Nigeria and paved the way for the CBN Act of 1958?","options":["The Paton Report","The Fisher Report","The World Bank Mission Report","The Loynes Report"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"J.B. Loynes of the Bank of England was invited to advise on how a central bank for Nigeria should be set up, and his 1957 report led directly to the CBN Act of 1958. The Paton Report (option A) of 1948 dealt with bank failures and led to the 1952 Banking Ordinance. The Fisher Report (option B) advised against a central bank at that time, and the World Bank mission (option C) proposed only an interim state bank."}},{"itemId":"681feb48-0fbe-5d35-a4df-0d1fdb8a617c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d18b17b-d63c-54e5-b739-b7a67123263f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0516-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Fisher Report of 1953 did not support setting up a central bank in Nigeria at that time. What was its main reason?","options":["Nigeria already had a central bank operating in Lagos under the colonial government.","The West African Currency Board had already been abolished and replaced by a state bank.","Nigerian commercial banks were too large and powerful for any central bank to regulate.","The financial system was too underdeveloped for a central bank to control the money supply, and trained staff were lacking."],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Fisher argued that Nigeria's financial system, with no organised money or capital market, was too narrow for a central bank to carry out its main role of controlling the money supply, and that there were not enough trained people to run one. Option A is wrong because the CBN did not exist until 1959, option B is wrong because the WACB was still operating, and option C is wrong because the indigenous banks of the time were small and weak."}},{"itemId":"619deb38-215a-5024-af89-9dcc83bcad43","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c3a9bf0-b438-5968-b46e-72688673681e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0517","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a developmental (non-traditional) function of the Central Bank of Nigeria rather than a traditional central banking function?","options":["Issuing the national currency","Acting as banker to the government","Running special credit schemes such as the Agricultural Credit Guarantee Scheme Fund","Acting as lender of last resort to banks"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Traditional functions are those every central bank performs whatever the level of development, such as issuing currency (option A), banking for the government (option B) and acting as lender of last resort (option D). In a developing economy like Nigeria, the CBN also takes on developmental roles, including building financial markets and instruments, running special funds and credit schemes such as the ACGSF, and providing information and training."}},{"itemId":"644d289e-e5f8-5182-81d0-fff0f19456fd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b64d87cd-fb98-5b37-8fcb-b31c398f873d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0518","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly lists the three main developmental functions of the Central Bank of Nigeria?","options":["Accepting deposits from the public, granting loans to households, and selling insurance policies","Issuing currency, acting as banker to the government, and acting as lender of last resort to banks","Provision of financial infrastructure, provision of special funds and schemes, and provision of information and training","Collecting taxes, paying civil servants' salaries, and fixing the prices of goods and services"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Beyond its traditional roles, the CBN helps develop the financial system by creating markets and instruments (financial infrastructure), running special funds and credit schemes for sectors like agriculture and small industry, and providing economic information, research and training. Option B lists traditional central banking functions, option A describes commercial bank and insurance business, and option D lists jobs of government, not of a central bank."}},{"itemId":"1039bfa6-ad2f-538a-804b-0fef95015582","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"97dd1d63-ba46-59b9-a591-168852400a5c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0519","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Agricultural Credit Guarantee Scheme Fund (ACGSF), established in 1977, what does the Fund promise banks that lend to agriculture?","options":["To repay the full amount of every defaulted loan together with interest","To repay 25 percent of the net amount in default if a borrower fails to repay","To repay 75 percent of the net amount in default if a borrower fails to repay","To repay 50 percent of the net amount in default if a borrower fails to repay"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The ACGSF encourages banks to lend to farmers by guaranteeing 75 percent of the amount in default, net of any security realised. The lending bank still bears the remaining 25 percent, so options B and D understate the cover, while option A is wrong because the guarantee never covers the whole loss."}},{"itemId":"3b12798f-90dc-5c41-b377-84604bab7539","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c759ad20-de03-54e0-92f2-6e1e1ba15b34","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0519-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Small and Medium Industries Equity Investment Scheme (SMIEIS), promoted by the CBN through the Bankers' Committee, banks were required to set aside:","options":["25 percent of their share capital for the purchase of treasury bills","10 percent of their profit before tax for investment in small and medium industries","10 percent of their total deposits as interest-free loans to the CBN","1 percent of their total loans for the National Housing Fund"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Under SMIEIS, banks set aside 10 percent of their profit before tax to invest in small and medium enterprises, mainly by taking equity stakes, which spares these firms heavy interest payments. Options C, A and D describe requirements that were not part of the scheme. The CBN's role was to initiate the scheme and guide it through the Bankers' Committee."}},{"itemId":"62969f2a-7e52-5dd2-acd5-91de54982201","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ec8fa8cb-fd42-59ec-b9d6-f6f5ed730056","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0520","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"At the apex of the organisational structure of the Central Bank of Nigeria is the:","options":["Bankers' Committee, chaired by the head of the largest bank","Monetary Policy Committee, chaired by the Minister of Finance","National Assembly Committee on Banking","Board of Directors, chaired by the Governor"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN is governed by its Board of Directors, which is chaired by the Governor and includes the Deputy Governors and other directors; below it are the directorates and departments that run the bank's daily operations. Option B is wrong because the Monetary Policy Committee sets monetary policy and is chaired by the Governor, not the Minister of Finance. The Bankers' Committee (option A) is a forum where the CBN meets bank chief executives, and the National Assembly (option C) oversees the CBN from outside, so neither sits at the top of its internal structure."}},{"itemId":"8b979dd1-9692-5e82-86da-ae3831fdd6c6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"043993b7-6684-5d67-95e6-9677e8b892e4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0520-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN Act 2007, how many Deputy Governors does the Central Bank of Nigeria have?","options":["Four","Two","Six","Three"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN Act 2007 provides for a Governor and four Deputy Governors, all appointed by the President subject to confirmation by the Senate. The Deputy Governors sit on the Board and each oversees a group of directorates. Options B, D and C do not match the number set by the Act."}},{"itemId":"8e861462-ef65-57a0-9c8e-20fe277b9379","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"faf2d650-3266-506f-9542-644c7917fdef","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0521","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"As part of its developmental role of providing information and public enlightenment, which of the following does the Central Bank of Nigeria do?","options":["It publishes the listing rules that companies must meet before their shares can be quoted on the stock exchange","It publishes economic and financial information such as Statistical Bulletins, Annual Reports and CBN Briefs, and organises seminars and policy forums","It insures the deposits of members of the public in banks that fail and pays them compensation","It sets and conducts the professional examinations that qualify people as chartered bankers in Nigeria"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN keeps government, investors, researchers and the general public informed through regular publications such as its Statistical Bulletin, Annual Report, CBN Briefs and Economic and Financial Review, and it also runs seminars, surveys and policy forums on economic issues. Option D describes the work of the Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN), not the CBN. Option A relates to the stock exchange and the SEC, while option C is the job of the NDIC."}},{"itemId":"87114808-6669-5ea7-9cf1-950958f22bea","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cbf5c638-8c0a-5e15-83f4-2a5bfb1e75dd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0522","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How was the Central Bank of Nigeria involved in the Small and Medium Industries Equity Investment Scheme (SMIEIS)?","options":["It required commercial banks to open branches in rural areas so as to mobilise rural savings for local lending","It guaranteed banks against up to 75% of the net losses they suffered on loans granted to farmers","It initiated the scheme, under which banks, through the Bankers' Committee, set aside 10% of their profit before tax for equity investment in small and medium industries","It bought shares in small and medium industries directly with its own reserves, without involving the banks at all"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"SMIEIS was a CBN initiative adopted through the Bankers' Committee: each bank set aside 10% of its profit before tax to invest as equity in small and medium industries, giving such firms long-term funds without the burden of interest charges. Option B describes the Agricultural Credit Guarantee Scheme Fund and option A describes the Rural Banking Scheme, which are different CBN schemes. Option D is wrong because the money came from the banks' own profits, not from the CBN's reserves."}},{"itemId":"67e84424-d98d-5a76-8e6a-1afa5aa2946f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f08d92b2-73e7-5e1a-b119-4511894f7856","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0523","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Rediscounting and Refinancing Facility (RRF) introduced by the Central Bank of Nigeria was mainly meant to:","options":["provide Ways and Means advances to the Federal Government when revenue falls short","mop up excess liquidity from the banking system in order to bring down inflation","encourage banks to finance exports by allowing them to rediscount or refinance the export credit they granted","guarantee banks against losses on loans that they granted to farmers and agricultural firms"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The RRF was an export-promotion facility: banks that granted credit to exporters could rediscount or refinance it, which freed up their funds and encouraged more lending for exports. It was later handed over to the Nigerian Export-Import Bank (NEXIM). Option D describes the Agricultural Credit Guarantee Scheme Fund, option B is the aim of tools such as CBN certificates and open market operations, and option A is part of the CBN's role as banker to the government."}},{"itemId":"2322992a-7071-518f-9f7e-e070539f2d44","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d3476c90-e76f-594d-84d0-b98a08329999","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0523-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Foreign Input Facility (FIF) introduced by the Central Bank of Nigeria was designed to:","options":["provide long-term housing loans to low-income earners in the cities","insure the deposits of bank customers in case their bank fails","help domestic producers obtain foreign exchange to import the capital goods and raw materials they need","encourage commercial banks to open more branches in rural areas"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The FIF made foreign exchange available so that Nigerian producers could import the machinery and raw materials needed for production. Like the RRF, it was later moved to the Nigerian Export-Import Bank (NEXIM). Option B is the work of the NDIC, option A is the business of mortgage institutions, and option D describes the Rural Banking Scheme."}},{"itemId":"2d1cc3f2-0618-5b91-9038-a4d2490e9da6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a8eef241-bd79-5152-ae61-f18b7d552f58","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0524","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When it is said that the CBN provides 'financial infrastructure' as part of its developmental role, this mainly means that the CBN:","options":["issues the country's currency notes and coins and manages their distribution to the banks","helps to create and develop financial markets and instruments, such as treasury bills and the money market, and encouraged the setting up of the stock exchange","grants loans and overdrafts directly to individuals, households and small traders","guarantees banks' agricultural loans through the Agricultural Credit Guarantee Scheme Fund"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The basic infrastructure of a financial system is its markets and instruments. When central banking began in Nigeria there was no organised money or capital market, so the CBN introduced instruments such as treasury bills, treasury certificates and bankers' acceptances, later licensed discount houses, and encouraged the setting up of the Lagos Stock Exchange. Option A is a traditional central bank function, option D belongs to the CBN's special funds and schemes, and option C is wrong because the CBN does not lend to the general public."}},{"itemId":"3031d688-c4d1-5521-a46b-e264d57f72db","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"815c07fe-bdef-522a-8865-5fd977b762e3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0525","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a reason for setting up the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)?","options":["To protect depositors, especially small depositors, when a bank fails","To guarantee that the shareholders of a failed bank get back their full investment","To complement the CBN's supervision as the number of banks grew rapidly after liberalisation","To ensure the orderly exit of failing banks without causing panic in the banking system"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Deposit insurance protects depositors, not shareholders: the owners of a failed bank bear the loss of their capital, and the NDIC can even enforce the liability of shareholders and directors of failed banks. Options A, C and D are all genuine reasons for the NDIC: the boom in bank numbers after deregulation stretched the CBN's supervisory capacity, small depositors needed protection, and failing banks had to exit without triggering a loss of confidence in the whole system."}},{"itemId":"1aa37816-3d8d-5980-ad9d-e7188d6a6023","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dcc10b38-11e7-5f4f-8b29-7216640d5ab4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0525-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The establishment of the NDIC in 1988 was closely linked to which development in Nigerian banking?","options":["The introduction of the naira and kobo as Nigeria's decimal currency","The deregulation of banking under the Structural Adjustment Programme, which led to a rapid increase in the number of banks","The banking consolidation of 2004 to 2005, which required banks to raise their capital base to N25 billion","The replacement of the West African Currency Board by the Central Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"From 1986 the Structural Adjustment Programme liberalised the economy and lowered the barriers to entry into banking, so the number of banks rose sharply. This increased the supervisory burden on the CBN and the risk of bank failures, which led to the creation of the NDIC to help supervise banks and protect depositors. Option C came much later, while options D and A relate to the late 1950s and 1973 respectively."}},{"itemId":"c2c8915b-7cfd-541f-9b5f-ce69852814d3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b964f77b-a842-531e-a289-bc407dc9cefc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0526","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the NDIC's supervision of banks, 'off-site surveillance' refers to:","options":["inviting a bank's owners and managers for dialogue to persuade them to drop unhealthy practices","sending examiners to a bank's premises to inspect its books, records and operations","selling off a failed bank's assets and paying its insured depositors","analysing the periodic returns that banks submit to check whether they are meeting prudential requirements"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Off-site surveillance is done away from the bank: banks send in regular returns on their assets, liabilities, loans and other items, and the NDIC analyses them for signs of weakness in capital, liquidity or loan quality. Option B describes on-site examination, which the NDIC uses to confirm that the returns are accurate. Option A is moral suasion and option C is liquidation, which are distress resolution steps rather than routine surveillance."}},{"itemId":"4edc4587-9c8d-5a87-af42-0ed12e3eabce","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11047149-3402-5917-be2f-b5d45174ff21","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0527","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT usually part of the 'holding actions' that the CBN and NDIC impose on a distressed bank?","options":["Opening new branches and advertising aggressively for more deposits","Injecting fresh capital funds into the bank","Stopping the granting of fresh loans and advances","Embarking on aggressive recovery of debts owed to the bank"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Holding actions place a distressed bank under close supervision and restrictions so that it can restructure itself and stop its position from getting worse. Such a bank is typically told to stop advertising for deposits without approval, stop new lending and new capital projects, recover its debts aggressively and bring in fresh capital, so options B, C and D are all holding actions. Expanding through new branches and deposit campaigns, as in option A, is exactly what holding actions are meant to restrain."}},{"itemId":"b5b9d68c-acc4-58bc-a6b4-49ecc8b8c220","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fbac8332-86fb-5844-a923-a6133570ee62","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0528","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the NDIC assumes control and management of a distressed bank, the main purpose is to:","options":["run the bank, usually through an interim management board, and work out a restructuring plan, selling or liquidating it if a turnaround is too costly","pay all depositors their deposits in full immediately and close the bank down on the same day","hand the bank straight back to its existing directors and management, without any changes to how it is run","convert the bank permanently into a government-owned bank run by civil servants, with no plan to sell it"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Assuming control and management is a rescue step: the NDIC takes over the running of the sick bank, usually by appointing an interim management board, to find and carry out a suitable restructuring strategy. If the funds needed for a turnaround are too large, the bank may be put up for sale, and one that finds no buyer may be liquidated. Option B is wrong because closure is the last resort, and even then insured depositors are paid only up to the insured limit; options C and D are not what the takeover aims at."}},{"itemId":"e188e3a8-1903-5b04-920d-aefba4c6314f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c00b151-fd15-5f49-a969-c295858152ef","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0529","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the liquidation of a failed insured bank in Nigeria, the NDIC's role is mainly to:","options":["act as liquidator, paying insured depositors up to the insured limit and sharing the proceeds of the bank's assets among depositors and other creditors","refund the shareholders' capital in full first, before any depositor or other creditor of the bank is paid","take over the bank's shares and keep running it as a going concern, with the same staff, for as long as it can","transfer all the bank's remaining deposits to the CBN, to be kept as part of government revenue"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Liquidation ends a bank's life. Once the CBN revokes the licence, the NDIC acts as liquidator: it pays each insured depositor up to the insured limit, then realises the bank's assets and pays depositors and other creditors further sums on a pro-rata basis from the proceeds. Option B reverses the order, because shareholders are the last to receive anything, option C describes a rescue rather than liquidation, and option D is not how liquidation works."}},{"itemId":"5bebd029-09dd-5d16-8538-36a4f16ac3cd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cbf0c648-3a26-5dd2-9839-bf039973accf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0529-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement about liquidation as a distress resolution option for banks in Nigeria is correct?","options":["It is carried out by the NDIC alone, without any role for the CBN","It is the first step the NDIC takes as soon as a bank shows any sign of weakness","It guarantees every depositor full repayment of all deposits, whatever their size","It is usually the last option, used when rescue measures have failed or the bank cannot be saved"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Liquidation is the last resort: the authorities first try options such as moral suasion, holding actions and change of management, and they liquidate only when the bank cannot be saved. A useful side effect is that it warns other bank owners that they can lose their investment if they run their banks badly. Option A is wrong because the licence is revoked by the CBN before the NDIC acts as liquidator, and option C is wrong because insured cover has a limit, with any further payment depending on what the bank's assets fetch."}},{"itemId":"eb71797d-e39d-56ce-8abf-e32df578ec55","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6b3385ad-8af7-5ea8-873f-a40d3447f807","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0530","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A distress resolution option in which the CBN and NDIC invite the owners and management of a sick bank for dialogue, to persuade them to drop unhealthy practices and adopt sound banking, is known as:","options":["moral suasion","imposition of holding actions","liquidation","purchase and assumption"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Moral suasion relies on persuasion rather than force: the regulators meet the owners and managers of the troubled bank and urge them to change their ways and adopt healthy banking practices. Holding actions in option B go further by placing formal restrictions on the bank, such as stopping new loans. Liquidation in option C closes the bank, and purchase and assumption in option D involves a healthy bank taking over the failing bank's assets and deposits."}},{"itemId":"1427f481-9e60-5681-9e1b-92d17b8bd015","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8d4ffb33-0b2a-5d37-8071-8dbcd845892b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0530-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a 'purchase and assumption' transaction used to resolve a failing bank:","options":["regulators hold talks with the failing bank's owners and managers to persuade them to change their practices","the failing bank is placed under restrictions, such as not granting fresh loans, while it restructures itself","the failing bank is closed, insured depositors are paid off by the NDIC and the bank's assets are sold piecemeal","a healthy bank buys all or part of the failing bank's assets and takes over all or part of its liabilities, especially its deposits"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"As the name suggests, a healthy institution purchases assets of the failing bank and assumes its liabilities, so depositors move to a sound bank without the disruption of a closure and payoff. Option B describes holding actions, option A describes moral suasion and option C describes liquidation."}},{"itemId":"06278f30-dab9-59f3-a43c-209f674c6586","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd92a047-642f-59bb-9314-6cecaf51e310","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0531","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the early years of Nigeria's deposit insurance scheme, which of the following was a criticism raised by banks against the NDIC?","options":["The maximum amount paid to each depositor of a failed bank was far too generous and encouraged careless banking","The NDIC had taken over from the CBN the power to issue currency notes and coins","The flat annual premium of 15/16 of one per cent of deposit liabilities was too heavy for many banks and ignored differences in risk between banks","Membership of the scheme was voluntary, so too few banks joined and the insurance fund stayed small"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"When the scheme started, every insured bank paid the same annual premium of 15/16 of one per cent of its deposit liabilities. Banks complained that this was too heavy, since some earned profits below that figure, and many argued for a risk-based premium instead. Option A reverses the real complaint, which was that the N50,000 cover was too small, option D also reverses it, since critics wanted the scheme to be optional for big banks, and option B is false."}},{"itemId":"52448794-6c62-5bf8-8efc-43e409041f34","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e94c9644-4a12-5644-b770-410ffe3aef9b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0532","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Critics once argued that big, strong banks should be allowed to stay out of Nigeria's deposit insurance scheme. How did the NDIC respond?","options":["It argued that only big banks needed deposit insurance, so small banks should leave the scheme","It argued that big banks can also fail, and that their participation helps to keep premiums affordable for smaller, weaker banks","It agreed with the critics and exempted all big banks from paying any premium","It said that big banks were already insured directly by the CBN instead"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NDIC pointed to experience in other countries, where large as well as small banks had failed, so no bank could simply be assumed safe. It also argued that including the big banks spreads the cost of the scheme and effectively subsidises the premium for small and weak banks, which are its most likely beneficiaries. Options A, C and D do not reflect the NDIC's position; membership remained compulsory for licensed deposit money banks."}},{"itemId":"e42772bf-e7de-5a68-90a4-d704ff2d339d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0c7fadf1-9267-5d40-8ec1-8388b0e0f68c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0532-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Some critics asked the NDIC to exclude banks' investments in government securities from the items it insured. What was the NDIC's reply?","options":["Excluding them would make the insurance cover for each depositor unlimited","Such investments are assets of the banks, not deposit liabilities, so they were never part of the insured deposits","Government securities were the main items covered by deposit insurance, so they could not be excluded","The CBN would insure banks' government securities in place of the NDIC"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Deposit insurance covers what a bank owes its depositors, which is a liability. Treasury bills and other government securities that a bank buys are assets it holds, so the NDIC replied that they had never been included in the deposit liabilities on which cover and premiums are based. Options A, C and D all miss this difference between a bank's assets and its deposit liabilities."}},{"itemId":"2f9b9543-bd9f-5964-8461-bd84c60c1418","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b673c00e-a80d-5541-a616-6613127ca709","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0533","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an achievement commonly credited to the NDIC since it began operations?","options":["Taking over from the CBN the issue of currency notes and coins and the conduct of monetary policy","Completely eliminating bank failures in Nigeria from the time it began operations","Helping to resolve distressed banks in an orderly way, for example by arranging rescues, sales or mergers and paying insured depositors","Abolishing deposit insurance premiums, so that insured banks no longer pay anything for the cover"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NDIC's record includes helping some ailing banks back to health, arranging for others to be bought over or merged with healthier banks, and paying insured depositors when banks were closed, all of which softened the shock of bank failures. Option B overstates its record, since banks still failed, for instance during the distress of the 1990s. Option A is a CBN function and option D is false, because insured banks still pay premiums."}},{"itemId":"34760a76-e330-5ab1-a1b7-ef901e8a65e0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c5851c9-10c4-57d7-9181-46ba63a1f63e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0533-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the banking consolidation programme announced by the CBN in July 2004, deposit money banks were required to raise their minimum capital base to ______ by 31 December 2005.","options":["N2 billion","N100 billion","N5 billion","N25 billion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 2004 consolidation raised the minimum capital base of every deposit money bank from N2 billion to N25 billion, with a deadline of the end of 2005. Many banks met the target through mergers, acquisitions and public share offers, and the number of banks fell sharply, which left a smaller group of better-capitalised banks. Option A was the old minimum before the exercise, while options B and C were not the 2004 requirement."}},{"itemId":"9d8c55be-f1cd-55b6-819a-8dad2c09302c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0652fe5-36cd-5c7a-9edf-63e9c2010740","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0534","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The money market is best described as the market for:","options":["buying and selling foreign currencies at the official exchange rate","exchanging goods directly for other goods without the use of money","short-term funds and financial instruments, usually with maturities of up to one year","medium- to long-term securities such as shares, bonds and debentures"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The money market is where short-term funds are lent and borrowed, through instruments such as treasury bills, commercial papers and bankers' acceptances, with maturities ranging from overnight to about a year. It helps governments, banks and firms manage temporary cash shortages and surpluses. Option D describes the capital market, option A the foreign exchange market, and option B barter."}},{"itemId":"cab373e3-4f12-58d8-b179-396eb159d504","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c4397106-fb88-53ff-8659-704e9376e682","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0535","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes the capital market from the money market?","options":["Money market instruments are generally riskier and much harder to sell quickly than capital market securities","The capital market deals in short-term instruments such as treasury bills, while the money market deals in long-term securities such as shares and bonds","The capital market mainly provides overnight funds to banks, while the money market finances long-term projects such as factories","The capital market deals in medium- to long-term funds and securities such as shares and bonds, while the money market deals in short-term instruments such as treasury bills"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The main difference is the maturity of the funds: the capital market mobilises medium- to long-term funds for factories, infrastructure and other long-term projects through shares, bonds and debentures, while the money market handles short-term funds through instruments like treasury bills and commercial papers. Options B and C swap the two markets, and option A is wrong because money market instruments are short-term, highly liquid and generally low-risk."}},{"itemId":"ebb5eef1-51a8-5284-8999-18414bb4d42a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"52053187-3314-51f9-9bd7-eb6375f807fc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0535-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a capital market instrument rather than a money market instrument?","options":["Debenture","Commercial paper","Bankers' acceptance","Treasury bill"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A debenture is a long-term loan stock issued by a company, which makes it a capital market instrument, like ordinary shares and government bonds. Treasury bills, commercial papers and bankers' acceptances (options B, C and D) are all short-term instruments traded in the money market."}},{"itemId":"e6c34ffb-b1c8-516a-a613-faf17ac8ed3c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa09246f-fea6-5da2-afbf-9d5d5d8efb40","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0536","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of financial markets?","options":["Providing protection against risk through insurance and diversification","Mobilising savings from surplus units of the economy","Issuing the country's legal tender currency","Providing liquidity by allowing financial assets to be converted into cash"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Issuing legal tender is the exclusive function of the central bank, not of financial markets. Financial markets mobilise savings, provide a store of wealth, liquidity and credit, support the payments system, offer protection against risk and serve as a channel for government's monetary policy, so options A, B and D are all genuine functions."}},{"itemId":"3e2d36c5-7849-5a7a-a68c-7bbcf36db6aa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4d0a14cb-c5e5-5e3b-9b8e-85fd765cb1a2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0537","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following groups consists entirely of operators in the Nigerian money market?","options":["The stock exchange, the Securities and Exchange Commission and the Central Securities Clearing System","Stockbrokers, registrars and issuing houses","The CBN, stockbrokers and the stock exchange","The CBN, deposit money banks, the Federal Government and large corporations"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Money market operators are those who lend or borrow short-term funds: the CBN, which issues and trades government securities and conducts open market operations; deposit money banks managing their liquidity; governments raising short-term funds through treasury bills; and corporations investing surplus cash or issuing commercial papers. Options A and B list capital market operators and institutions, while option C mixes the CBN with capital market operators."}},{"itemId":"9b45e7bc-7227-53e7-bfb0-8f8ac76192e9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a46baaaf-c3ac-51eb-826c-4e658ed5c3b8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0538","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How does the existence of a money market help the CBN to carry out monetary policy?","options":["It enables the CBN to buy or sell short-term government securities in open market operations to inject or withdraw liquidity from the banking system","It removes the need for the CBN to set any reserve requirement or liquidity ratio for the banks","It allows the CBN to fix the prices of goods and services in the economy directly by decree","It allows the CBN to lend long-term funds directly to manufacturers for building new factories"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Open market operations work through the money market: by selling treasury bills or CBN bills the CBN mops up excess liquidity, and by buying them it injects funds, which in turn influences interest rates and credit. Money market interest rates also show the CBN whether funds are scarce or plentiful. Option C is not how monetary policy works, option D is long-term lending rather than monetary control, and option B is wrong because reserve requirements remain a separate tool."}},{"itemId":"54f4b47c-67ea-5c20-8fbf-c6250fc89ccb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a167e93-4011-5c27-96a1-d656ae3057bd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0539","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A short-term unsecured promissory note issued by a large, creditworthy company to raise funds, backed only by the issuer's reputation, is called a:","options":["treasury bill","bankers' acceptance","commercial paper","certificate of deposit"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Commercial papers are issued by well-known 'blue-chip' companies to raise short-term funds; they are usually sold at a discount and repaid at face value on maturity, and their only backing is the issuer's good name. A treasury bill (option A) is issued by the Federal Government, a bankers' acceptance (option B) is a bill accepted by a bank, and a certificate of deposit (option D) is issued by a bank against a time deposit."}},{"itemId":"564a26e9-6b99-5a39-afbc-0013967bb917","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4ef7b431-c1c3-5d3c-b9c7-d0219ea8ea9f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0539-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bill of exchange that is drawn on a bank and accepted by it, so that the bank undertakes to pay it at maturity, is known as:","options":["a commercial paper","call money","a bankers' acceptance","a treasury certificate"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"By writing 'accepted' on the bill, the bank takes on the obligation to pay at maturity, which makes the bill very safe and easy to discount for cash in the money market; bankers' acceptances are widely used in financing trade. A commercial paper (option A) carries only the issuing company's name, a treasury certificate (option D) is a government security, and call money (option B) is very short-term lending between banks."}},{"itemId":"b17ea637-c691-5e33-acf5-755a60c15d3f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6fa21207-603e-5670-9414-193a9a020882","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0539-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement about Nigerian Treasury Bills is correct?","options":["They are time deposits issued by commercial banks to their customers","They are long-term bonds issued by companies to finance the expansion of their factories","They are shares in the Central Bank of Nigeria sold to members of the public","They are short-term Federal Government securities sold at a discount and redeemed at face value on maturity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury bills are the main money market instrument for the Federal Government's short-term borrowing. Because they are sold below face value and repaid in full at maturity, the difference is the investor's return, and since the government backs them they carry almost no default risk. Option B describes corporate bonds or debentures, option A describes certificates of deposit or fixed deposits, and option C is false."}},{"itemId":"063931b6-e8b9-5110-b854-6258b5737a22","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8dc7f5f1-ae5d-596e-aab8-22450bd9ec00","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0540","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"SWIFT, the network widely used for electronic fund transfers between banks across the world, mainly provides:","options":["insurance cover for deposits that customers hold in foreign banks","a secure system for banks to send payment instructions and other financial messages to one another","a stock exchange on which the shares of international banks are traded","a plastic card that bank customers use to withdraw cash from ATMs"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"SWIFT stands for the Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication. It is a messaging network that lets banks exchange standardised, secure instructions for payments, foreign exchange and other transactions, which makes cross-border transfers fast and reliable. Options D, A and C describe a payment card, deposit insurance and a stock exchange, none of which is what SWIFT does."}},{"itemId":"c72221a9-051b-5ba9-8cdd-56776122c87a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45c52258-6673-5df2-b282-b6cb4af4b44a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0540-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A plastic card similar to a credit card but with an embedded microchip that stores information and keeps a record of the transactions carried out on it is called a:","options":["standing order","traveller's cheque","bank draft","smart card"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A smart card carries a memory chip that stores data and transaction records and can hold security features such as PINs or biometric details, which makes payments secure and helps reduce fraud. A bank draft (option C) is a cheque drawn by a bank on itself, a standing order (option A) is an instruction to a bank to make regular payments, and a traveller's cheque (option B) is a pre-printed cheque meant for travellers."}},{"itemId":"eee4b259-002b-59c1-b9ed-f935e306cc91","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c8758b95-cee5-52db-a0d8-36df0cb7f6ec","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0541","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a benefit of having a money market in an economy?","options":["It is the main channel of long-term funds for building factories, roads and other infrastructure","It allows banks, firms and individuals to earn income on short-term surplus funds instead of keeping idle cash, while staying liquid","It guarantees that no bank in the economy will ever face a shortage of liquidity or need help","It removes the need for the country to have a central bank or any monetary authority"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Through the money market, holders of temporary surplus cash can invest in safe short-term instruments such as treasury bills and earn a return, then get their money back quickly when needed. The market also gives the CBN a gauge of how scarce or plentiful funds are and a channel for monetary policy. Option A describes the capital market, while options C and D overstate what a money market can do."}},{"itemId":"5da98feb-d4b5-5bde-8caf-b7560cd6fdb4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8dd94d29-8750-5d18-8f98-0e6bc77bb107","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0542","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a challenge that has faced the Nigerian money market?","options":["Dominance of the market by government securities and a narrow range of instruments","Lack of any role for the CBN, which leaves the market completely unregulated","Excessive participation by individual small investors, who crowd out the banks","Too many varieties of instruments, which confuse investors and spread funds too thinly"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The Nigerian money market has long relied heavily on government securities such as treasury bills, so its fortunes are tied to government finances, and the range of instruments has been limited, leaving the market without much depth or breadth. Other challenges include low public awareness and dominance by a few big banks. Options C and D state the opposite of the real situation, and option B is wrong because the CBN regulates the market and is one of its main participants."}},{"itemId":"7b4607dc-45c7-5a8d-87df-384e83d7a4a3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"616894d9-f781-535f-9fc7-5fb1614fe2cd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0543","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The segment of the capital market in which already-issued securities are bought and sold among investors is called the:","options":["money market","interbank market","secondary market","primary market"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The capital market has two arms. The primary market is where new securities are issued and fresh capital flows to the issuing firm or government, while the secondary market, mainly the stock exchange, is where existing securities change hands between investors. By providing this resale outlet, the secondary market makes long-term securities liquid and more attractive. Option D deals only with new issues, and options A and B handle short-term funds."}},{"itemId":"b65debae-c2de-5640-a416-c165dbeb001d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6a0c5c4f-243c-5611-b767-c99794db9ef8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0549","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following roles confirms the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) as the apex regulator of the Nigerian capital market?","options":["Registering securities and capital market operators such as stockbrokers and issuing houses, supervising the exchange and protecting investors","Insuring the deposits of customers whose banks fail and paying them compensation up to a limit","Licensing and supervising insurance companies, insurance brokers and loss adjusters","Issuing the national currency, setting the Monetary Policy Rate and acting as banker to government"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The SEC regulates and develops the capital market: it registers securities offered to the public and the operators who deal in them, monitors the stock exchange to keep trading fair and orderly, and protects investors from fraud and misinformation. Option B is the work of the NDIC, option D belongs to the CBN, and option C is the job of the National Insurance Commission (NAICOM)."}},{"itemId":"514031f6-5db7-561d-8175-2e03dea506aa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7af117d6-c21a-5e7c-82e3-2a95e965ce25","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0552","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a major benefit of a capital market to a developing economy such as Nigeria?","options":["It removes the need for banks to lend to businesses, since firms no longer need loans","It provides banks with overnight funds to meet their daily cash and clearing needs","It guarantees investors against any fall in the prices of the shares they buy","It channels medium- to long-term funds to firms and governments for expansion and development projects"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Development is a long-term process that needs long-term finance, and the capital market supplies it by letting firms and governments raise funds through shares, bonds and loan stocks for factories, infrastructure and other projects. This helps domestic firms expand and supports industrialisation. Option B describes the money market, while options C and A are false, since share prices can fall and banks remain important lenders."}},{"itemId":"906f2eb4-9525-50ab-a535-a8284d95e591","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f72e895-1d74-5cc6-92fb-de41b6740db0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0554","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Non-bank financial institutions (NBFIs) are described as a 'heterogeneous' group. This means that:","options":["all NBFIs accept demand deposits and issue cheque books to customers in the same way as commercial banks","all NBFIs are owned and run by the Federal Government through the Federal Ministry of Finance","all NBFIs perform exactly the same function, so any one of them can replace another in the financial system","the group is made up of many different kinds of institutions, such as insurers, pension fund administrators and bureaux de change, each with its own functions"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Heterogeneous means made up of different parts. The NBFI category groups together very different institutions: insurers pool risks, pension fund administrators manage retirement savings, mortgage institutions provide housing finance, finance houses take placements and lend, and bureaux de change deal in foreign exchange. Option C is the opposite of heterogeneous, option B is false since most are privately owned, and option A is wrong because taking demand deposits and issuing cheques is what sets banks apart from NBFIs."}},{"itemId":"47160abb-55a3-5adf-a32c-0f7046609fd7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9786cef0-d5f8-505f-a6b8-0983397a2686","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0555","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes life insurance from non-life insurance?","options":["Life insurance covers financial loss from death, disability or old age and is usually long-term, while non-life insurance covers property and liability risks such as fire and theft and is usually short-term","Non-life insurance policies usually run for twenty years or more, while life policies must be renewed every year","Life insurance requires no premium payments at all, while non-life insurance requires regular premiums","Life insurance covers fire, theft and motor accidents, while non-life insurance covers death, disability and old age"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Life insurance (assurance) protects the insured or their dependants against the financial effects of death, disability or old age, and policies such as endowment assurance usually run for many years. Non-life or general insurance covers property and liabilities, for example fire, theft, marine and motor risks, and policies are usually renewed yearly. Options B and D swap the two types, and option C is wrong because both types are paid for with premiums."}},{"itemId":"39a367dd-b433-5073-a0ad-830431a747d8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3953233c-46ed-54be-a2a0-270465b30d9f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0555-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A life policy under which the sum assured is paid at the end of an agreed period if the insured person is still alive, or to the beneficiaries if the insured dies before then, is called:","options":["endowment assurance","marine insurance","fire insurance","liability insurance"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Endowment assurance combines protection with saving: the policyholder pays premiums for a fixed term and receives the sum assured at the end of it, or the beneficiaries are paid if death occurs earlier. Fire insurance (option C), marine insurance (option B) and liability insurance (option D) are all non-life policies that cover property or legal liabilities, not life."}},{"itemId":"e580f757-32e8-5bd8-b886-e3d85879c4d7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0e1a8cc6-f0d0-5668-bb82-9ded5d4e5021","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0556","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The National Provident Fund (NPF), set up in 1961 for private-sector workers, was replaced by the Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund (NSITF) in 1993. Which problem contributed to this?","options":["Workers complained that its benefits were too generous for the country to afford","The fund had accumulated far more money than it could find ways to invest","Poor performance, including poor record-keeping and the failure of many employers to remit contributions","It was managed by foreign insurance companies based outside Nigeria"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The NPF was meant to be a compulsory savings scheme protecting workers against retirement, invalidity and death, but its performance was poor: records were badly kept and many employers deducted contributions without paying them in. After a government review, it was converted into the NSITF in 1993. Options A, B and D do not describe the NPF's real problems."}},{"itemId":"ede32e3c-1e48-55b8-90b3-84c3860bbdcb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8f46b65c-9a59-5b5a-bab7-0eff43681e75","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0557","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a function of the National Pension Commission (PenCom)?","options":["Insuring the deposits of bank customers against bank failure","Licensing and supervising Pension Fund Administrators and Pension Fund Custodians","Regulating and supervising insurance companies","Registering securities offered to the public on the stock exchange"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"PenCom was created by the Pension Reform Act of 2004 to regulate and supervise pension matters in Nigeria. Its functions include formulating pension policy, licensing and supervising PFAs and PFCs, issuing regulations and guidelines, and keeping a national data bank on pensions. Option A belongs to the NDIC, option C to the National Insurance Commission (NAICOM) and option D to the SEC."}},{"itemId":"0c99bd5d-140f-57c2-8b5f-fbfdc1e3bf47","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c06b2c38-96b5-5a7f-a416-c784b4d4345a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0558","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under Nigeria's contributory pension scheme, what is the main difference between a Pension Fund Administrator (PFA) and a Pension Fund Custodian (PFC)?","options":["The PFA opens and manages workers' Retirement Savings Accounts and invests the contributions, while the PFC keeps the pension fund assets in safe custody","The PFA regulates and licenses all the PFCs operating in Nigeria","There is no real difference, as the two names refer to the same institution","The PFC opens workers' Retirement Savings Accounts and invests the contributions, while the PFA only keeps the assets in custody"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The scheme deliberately separates management from custody. The PFA administers each worker's Retirement Savings Account, invests the contributions in permitted assets and pays retirement benefits, while the PFC holds the actual pension fund assets on behalf of the PFA, which protects contributors' money. Option D swaps the two roles, option B wrongly gives the PFA the regulator's job, which belongs to PenCom, and option C ignores the separation altogether."}},{"itemId":"14087394-1fe6-5038-b0b2-25de1e9a7364","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"12fce5b4-3e7e-540e-b77e-10e136a72070","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0558-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under Nigeria's contributory pension scheme, an employee opens his or her Retirement Savings Account (RSA) with:","options":["a Pension Fund Custodian (PFC)","a Pension Fund Administrator (PFA) of his or her choice","the National Pension Commission (PenCom)","the Central Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Each employee chooses a PFA and opens an RSA with it; the contributions of both the employee and the employer are credited to that account, and the PFA manages and invests them. The PFC (option A) only holds the assets in custody, PenCom (option C) is the regulator, and the CBN (option D) plays no direct role in individual pension accounts."}},{"itemId":"43cfb734-43ee-5583-b860-9d9b30b96de5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d07b9fb7-acf1-51e1-809a-f2a39cdfbe95","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0559","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a defined contribution pension scheme, such as Nigeria's contributory pension scheme, what a worker receives at retirement depends mainly on:","options":["the profit that the employer makes in the year the worker retires","the total contributions paid into the worker's Retirement Savings Account and the investment returns earned on them","an amount the government decides to pay all retirees each year, regardless of contributions","a fixed formula based on final salary and years of service, which the employer guarantees"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"In a defined contribution scheme it is the contribution that is defined, not the benefit: the employee and employer pay set percentages into the RSA, and the final benefit depends on how much has built up and how well it was invested, so the employee bears the investment risk. Option D describes a defined benefit scheme, where the pension is fixed in advance by formula. Options A and C have nothing to do with how benefits are worked out under this scheme."}},{"itemId":"c02ccf63-f7bd-54ce-9cd1-049502178d57","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58e0b66e-3ab7-55e5-a556-8983bc295562","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0559-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A pension plan in which the retiree's entitlement is fixed in advance by a formula based on factors such as final salary, years of service and rank is called a:","options":["defined contribution scheme","defined benefit scheme","endowment assurance policy","Retirement Savings Account"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"In a defined benefit scheme the pension promised is set in advance by a formula, so the employer, not the employee, carries the investment risk of meeting that promise. In a defined contribution scheme (option A), only the contributions are fixed and the benefit depends on the fund's growth. A Retirement Savings Account (option D) is the individual account used in the contributory scheme, and an endowment assurance policy (option C) is a life insurance product."}},{"itemId":"a6db835f-fad8-57a7-8794-421e311f0261","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"297aa14d-90a5-5d0c-86db-85545a9bc78c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0560","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Primary Mortgage Institutions (PMIs) in Nigeria are best described as institutions that:","options":["mobilise savings and deposits from the public and grant loans to people to buy, build or improve homes","deal mainly in buying and selling foreign exchange for small users","provide wholesale refinancing to mortgage lenders and act as the apex mortgage institution","insure the deposits of bank customers in case their bank fails"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"PMIs provide housing finance at the retail level: they take savings and deposits from the public and lend to individuals for buying, building or renovating houses, then monitor that the loans are used for that purpose. Option C describes the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria, which refinances PMIs, option B describes bureaux de change, and option D is the work of the NDIC."}},{"itemId":"599aabf1-3d60-58c9-ae56-7d50cb1abc49","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"541b5d52-1d6a-524b-bd2f-df56d4888884","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0560-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Today, Primary Mortgage Institutions in Nigeria are licensed and supervised by the:","options":["National Pension Commission","National Insurance Commission","Securities and Exchange Commission","Central Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"PMIs are deposit-taking institutions, so they fall under the CBN, which licenses and supervises them along with banks, microfinance banks and finance companies. Supervision passed to the CBN in the late 1990s, after widespread distress among PMIs. The SEC (option C) regulates the capital market, NAICOM (option B) regulates insurance, and PenCom (option A) regulates pensions."}},{"itemId":"9e5370cf-0812-51bf-b47b-0e2fad54ef6e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ad3de010-1645-56ad-bbf1-6c4a2f399e61","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0561","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes a bureau de change from a discount house in the Nigerian financial system?","options":["Both are deposit money banks that accept current account deposits and issue cheque books to the public","A bureau de change deals mainly in short-term government securities, while a discount house buys and sells foreign currency for travellers","A bureau de change buys and sells foreign exchange mainly for small users, while a discount house deals mainly in short-term government securities in the money market","A bureau de change trades company shares on the stock exchange, while a discount house grants long-term loans to farmers"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A bureau de change is a non-bank financial institution set up to deal in foreign exchange, mainly for small users such as travellers, while a discount house stands between the CBN and the banks, buying, holding and selling short-term government securities such as treasury bills. Option B simply swaps the two roles. Option A is wrong because neither is a deposit money bank, and option D confuses them with capital market and development finance institutions."}},{"itemId":"0b23296f-8754-5972-9229-13c858ba290d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f5029d22-f5bd-51f2-a015-365df61b0336","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0563","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four major macroeconomic objectives pursued through monetary policy?","options":["Balance of payments equilibrium","Price stability (avoidance of inflation)","Maximising the profits of deposit money banks","Full employment"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The four major macroeconomic objectives of monetary policy are price stability, a high rate of economic growth, full employment and balance of payments equilibrium. Making banks more profitable is a goal of the banks' own owners and managers, not a national policy objective. Options B, D and A are all among the four objectives, so none of them can be the exception."}},{"itemId":"c746832b-c5f3-5986-a890-300d5c15a551","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"77a9aaf8-3f2a-58b5-862b-fc7335ac17f6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0564","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the CBN raises the cash reserve requirement for banks from 10% to 20%, what happens to the simple deposit (money) multiplier?","options":["It stays at 10, because only the discount rate affects credit creation","It rises from 10 to 20","It falls from 10 to 5","It rises from 5 to 10"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The simple deposit multiplier is 1 divided by the reserve ratio, so it is 1/0.10 = 10 at a 10% requirement and 1/0.20 = 5 at 20%. Raising the reserve requirement therefore halves the banks' ability to create credit and slows the growth of money supply. Options B and D get the direction wrong, since a higher reserve ratio always means a smaller multiplier, and option A is wrong because the reserve requirement works directly on the multiplier."}},{"itemId":"059aa09d-07ec-55dc-a525-3a9b0c1f8efe","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7cb5489-c920-57d9-85b2-aae6c847fd43","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0564-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the CBN lowers the discount rate at which it lends to banks, the intended effect is to:","options":["Withdraw money from circulation by selling government securities","Encourage banks to borrow more and lend at lower rates, expanding credit and money supply","Force banks to raise their lending rates, reducing credit and money supply","Increase the amount of cash banks must hold as required reserves"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The discount rate (also called the bank rate or minimum lending rate) is the price banks pay to borrow from the central bank. Cutting it makes borrowing cheaper, so banks can lower their own lending rates and lend more, which expands credit and money supply. Option C describes the effect of raising the discount rate, while options D and A describe the reserve requirement and an open market sale, which are different instruments."}},{"itemId":"e23bc805-07b0-5bd5-8abf-eb96b0641e8f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"48c3430b-c81e-5f41-b3b2-fe810d1e57fa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0567","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Apart from reserve requirements, the discount rate and open market operations, which of the following is also an instrument of monetary policy?","options":["Personal income tax","Selective credit control","Government spending on roads and bridges","Import and excise duties"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Selective credit control uses administrative guidelines to direct banks to lend specified amounts to particular sectors, sometimes at set interest rates, and it sits alongside special deposits and moral suasion as an additional monetary policy tool. Options A, C and D are taxation and government spending measures, which belong to fiscal policy rather than to the central bank's control of money and credit."}},{"itemId":"a8a266dc-304f-545d-86bd-bc5e84ff9150","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7149dfe4-0b38-5e23-9927-1232cf55d2a5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0567-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which monetary policy instrument requires banks to keep cash with the central bank over and above their normal required reserves, with that cash not counted towards the reserve requirement?","options":["Open market operations","The discount rate","Special deposits","Moral suasion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Special deposits are extra balances the central bank asks banks to lodge with it on top of their normal required reserves; because they are not counted as reserves, they cut the banks' capacity to create credit, much like a higher reserve ratio. Option D, moral suasion, relies on persuasion and friendly appeal rather than compulsory deposits. Options A and B work through buying or selling government securities and through the cost of borrowing from the central bank."}},{"itemId":"ad028725-ca4a-54b2-bb01-b2197a8ef51c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df8506fa-6439-5d39-88fc-53256ec06155","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0569","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best distinguishes a barter economy from a money economy?","options":["A barter economy uses only coins, while a money economy uses only paper money","In a barter economy goods are exchanged directly for other goods, while in a money economy goods and services are exchanged for money","A barter economy has no exchange of any kind, while a money economy has trade","In a barter economy goods are exchanged for money, while in a money economy goods are exchanged directly for other goods"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Barter is the direct exchange of commodity for commodity, so every trade needs someone who has what you want and wants what you have. In a money economy, money acts as the go-between: goods and services are sold for money, which is then used to buy other things. Option D reverses the two systems, option A is wrong because barter involves no money at all, and option C is wrong because barter is itself a form of trade."}},{"itemId":"541266fd-060a-5540-98c0-880ff8ea4829","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a2745e49-7808-53e1-b6bc-ee3a551ac028","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0570","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The money stock (money supply) of a modern economy such as Nigeria is made up of:","options":["The external (foreign exchange) reserves held by the Central Bank of Nigeria","The total market value of shares quoted on the stock exchange","Only the banknotes and coins issued by the Central Bank of Nigeria","Currency (banknotes and coins) held by the public plus bank deposits"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The money stock is the total of cash, meaning banknotes and coins in circulation with the public, and bank deposits, including demand deposits on current account and time and savings deposits. Option C is too narrow because bank deposits are also money, being widely used to settle debts by cheque and transfer. Options A and B are assets held by the CBN and by investors respectively, not part of the money stock."}},{"itemId":"94b52471-066a-5e0d-b531-03239119272b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"14424750-ea21-50af-90db-441f38e1f328","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0571","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The value of money refers to:","options":["Its purchasing power, that is, the quantity of goods and services a given amount of money can buy","The cost of printing and minting the banknotes and coins","The face value printed on a currency note","The total amount of money in circulation in the economy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money is wanted for what it can buy, so its value lies in the quantity of goods and services a given sum of money can obtain; the more it buys, the higher its value. This is why the value of money moves inversely with the general price level. Option C describes only the face value, which stays the same even when prices rise, option D describes the money supply rather than its value, and option B is a production cost, not a measure of what money will buy."}},{"itemId":"5588e1e4-73c3-555a-a7ec-7dabc71090bc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e49e9f00-fcae-5714-a849-624de27e8d86","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0571-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the general price index rises from 100 to 250, the value of money becomes what percentage of its former value?","options":["40%","150%","250%","60%"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The value of money is the inverse of the price level, so the new value is (100/250) x 100 = 40% of the old value. In other words, a naira now buys only 40% of what it bought before. Option D, 60%, is the size of the fall in value, not what remains, and options C and B wrongly treat the value of money as moving in the same direction as prices."}},{"itemId":"32bffd12-3c8b-5eec-b282-bed51c4e0c22","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a9ef579-6a81-5512-8d93-b0214155af40","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0572","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the most basic determinants of the success of financial intermediaries, whose loss can lead to financial disintermediation?","options":["Having branches located far away from customers","Charging high interest rates on loans and high handling fees","Public trust and confidence in the institutions","The number of shareholders who own the institutions"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial intermediaries thrive on the confidence of the public: once people believe their funds are safe and can be withdrawn at will, they deposit with them, and once that trust is lost, people withdraw and hoard their money instead. Other success factors are convenience, competitive prices and nearness to customers. Options B and A are the opposite of those factors, since high charges and long distances drive customers away, and option D has no direct bearing on whether the public will use the institutions."}},{"itemId":"654b73e7-332b-5a6b-b2bb-3856c2427446","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bff49001-b359-5bd5-823f-a738dc73304b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0573","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigerian banking law, the abbreviation BOFIA stands for:","options":["Board of Financial Institutions Administration","Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act","Bank Owners and Financial Investors Association","Banking and Offshore Finance Inspection Authority"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"BOFIA is the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act, the main law under which the CBN licenses, regulates and supervises banks and other financial institutions in Nigeria. It was first enacted in 1991 and was replaced by a new BOFIA in 2020. Options C, D and A are made-up names; BOFIA is a law, not an association, authority or board."}},{"itemId":"77e4ed93-74e8-5b71-ae23-bade17175e86","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6045f020-8164-5f12-9191-10677c9d6cc0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0574","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three major types of deposit accounts operated by deposit money banks?","options":["Current account","Fixed (time) deposit account","Savings account","Loan account"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"The three major deposit accounts are current accounts (demand deposits drawn on by cheque), savings accounts and fixed or time deposit accounts. A loan account records money the bank has lent to a customer, so it is an asset of the bank rather than a deposit owed to the customer. Options A, C and B are all deposit accounts and so cannot be the exception."}},{"itemId":"12da409c-bfd0-50d4-9c11-827224fb7f8e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"32e741e4-a2c4-52bc-b847-d990fcb922f1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0575","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a function of community banks in Nigeria, the forerunners of today's microfinance banks?","options":["Providing credit facilities to small businesses and members of the community","Providing auxiliary banking services to their customers","Issuing the national currency and acting as banker to the Federal Government","Accepting savings, time and target deposits"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Issuing legal tender currency and acting as banker to the Federal Government are functions of the Central Bank of Nigeria, not of community banks. Community banks were set up to take banking to the grassroots, so they accepted savings, time and target deposits, gave small loans and provided auxiliary services, which is why options D, A and B are genuine functions."}},{"itemId":"2d90d2b7-2bfb-5ab1-8671-972e2bf7e431","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cbda13ba-dc7f-5df3-b5bf-2f26943730f4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0577","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the supervision of Nigerian banks by the CBN and NDIC, moral suasion refers to:","options":["Selling treasury bills to banks to mop up excess liquidity","Requiring banks to lodge special deposits with the CBN above their normal reserves","Revoking the licence of a distressed bank and paying off its depositors","Using dialogue and persuasion to get the owners and managers of a bank to change unhealthy practices voluntarily"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Moral suasion is an informal tool: rather than issuing formal orders or sanctions, the authorities invite a bank's owners and management to discussions and persuade them to drop unhealthy ways of doing business. The same idea is used in monetary policy, where the CBN appeals to banks to lend in desired directions. Option C describes liquidation, while options B and A are special deposits and open market operations, which work through compulsory deposits or market transactions rather than persuasion."}},{"itemId":"a12897a3-7eaa-586d-8c7f-6fb07e0b39d5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"43e05c2d-1fd0-5b60-be6f-527dee0991eb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0578","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Broadly, distress resolution for a sick bank in Nigeria involves which two basic options?","options":["Open market operations or moral suasion","Rehabilitation of the bank or its liquidation","Converting the bank into a bureau de change or a discount house","Increasing the deposit insurance premium or reducing the cash reserve ratio"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"When a bank becomes distressed, the authorities first try to rehabilitate it, for example through holding actions, a change of management or fresh capital, so that it returns to health. If the bank cannot be saved, the last option is liquidation, where it is closed and its assets are realised to pay depositors and creditors. Option A lists monetary policy tools, and options C and D are not recognised ways of resolving bank distress."}},{"itemId":"0757973c-dcd9-5eae-b8c1-6855f28fab47","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"73136fa6-9ccc-5366-9f5f-310eef7f7f59","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0580","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes the capital market?","options":["A market for buying and selling medium- to long-term financial instruments such as shares and bonds","A market for short-term instruments such as treasury bills and call money","A market where foreign currencies are bought and sold by small users","The arrangement through which banks exchange cheques drawn on one another at the clearing house"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The capital market mobilises medium- to long-term funds, for example through shares, debentures and government bonds, to finance long-term investment. Option B describes the money market, which deals in funds of up to about one year. Option D describes the clearing system, and option C describes the work of bureaux de change in the foreign exchange market."}},{"itemId":"6b769224-201c-5cb9-b67d-0b14d5decb6a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"564c289d-7c9f-5513-8585-825520871be4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0581","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT usually an operator in the Nigerian money market?","options":["Discount houses","Stockbroking firms buying and selling company shares on the stock exchange","The Central Bank of Nigeria","Commercial and merchant banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Buying and selling company shares is a capital market activity, since shares are long-term securities with no maturity date. The CBN brings treasury bills to the market, discount houses sell short-term securities and discount other short-term instruments, and commercial and merchant banks lend out surplus funds or borrow to meet short-term liquidity needs, so options C, A and D are all money market operators."}},{"itemId":"7cf82a8d-bf68-502f-aee7-ffd800fd6507","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"62a89ad0-3610-5466-b479-53682c691da2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0583","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT one of the main purposes for introducing bureaux de change in Nigeria?","options":["To bring the parallel (black) market under official regulation","To eliminate the parallel market premium by merging the parallel and official exchange rates","To provide large foreign exchange allocations for big corporate importers","To cater for small users of foreign exchange"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Bureaux de change were introduced to serve small users of foreign exchange, such as travellers, not to meet the large needs of big corporate importers. Their other purposes were to bring the parallel market into the official net so that government could regulate it, and to close the gap between the parallel and official exchange rates. Options D, A and B therefore state genuine purposes."}},{"itemId":"10df844c-a2a5-5924-ba18-01d88baa1806","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a927cf60-f215-5980-aa1c-1f20846a974c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0584","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Monetary policy can best be defined as:","options":["The use of the central bank's control over the quantity, availability and cost of money and credit to achieve general economic objectives","The lending policy a commercial bank sets for its own customers","The rules that govern the design and security features of currency notes","The use of taxation, government expenditure and public debt to influence the economy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Monetary policy is the deliberate use, by the central bank, of its control over money supply, credit and interest rates to achieve goals such as price stability and economic growth; in Nigeria this is the job of the CBN. Option D is the definition of fiscal policy, which works through the government budget rather than money and credit. Option B is a single bank's internal credit policy, not a national policy, and option C has nothing to do with controlling money and credit."}},{"itemId":"b72e4a45-19e5-5d5d-886e-c30bd72ccc8a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"29c64170-8c24-556c-b947-1888bd8662ba","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0585","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A customer of your bank, Mr Emeka, has a standing order of N5,000.00 due on the last day of every month. On 1 March 1996 his account had an opening balance of N1,000.00 DR. A local cheque for N8,000.00 was paid into his account on 6 March. On 10 March he was issued a cheque book and his account was debited N100.00 for its cost. On 22 March he issued a cheque of N1,950.00 to Ben, and on 25 March he made a cash lodgement of N920.00. The bank charges 1/2% for standing order services and N50.00 for postage, and he has an overdraft limit of N1,000.00. Which of the following is correct for the standing order due at the end of March?","options":["The bank will pay the standing order, leaving a balance of N2,795.00 CR after the payment and charges","The bank will pay the standing order, leaving a balance of N870.00 CR after the payment and charges","The bank will not pay the standing order, because N5,000.00 is more than his N1,000.00 overdraft limit","The bank will pay the standing order, leaving a balance of N795.00 CR after the payment and charges"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Before the standing order, the account has N8,920.00 of credits against N3,050.00 of debits (the N1,000.00 opening debit, N100.00 for the cheque book and the N1,950.00 cheque), giving N5,870.00 CR. The standing order and charges total N5,000.00 + N25.00 (1/2% of N5,000.00) + N50.00 = N5,075.00, which the balance covers, leaving N795.00 CR. Option B forgets the commission and postage, option A treats the opening N1,000.00 debit as a credit, and option C is wrong because there are enough funds, so the overdraft is not even needed."}},{"itemId":"d3c7a572-c996-5d2f-acb1-6b6469a1bb43","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f3856148-21a3-5be6-beae-45513e46edea","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0586","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Tola, a customer of your bank, had a credit balance of N40,000.00 on his current account on 1 January 1996 and made the following withdrawals: 15 January N50,000.00; 20 February N30,000.00; 8 March N10,000.00. The bank charges commission on turnover (COT) at 15% per annum on total debit entries. What is the COT for the quarter January to March 1996?","options":["N1,125.00","N6,000.00","N13,500.00","N3,375.00"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"COT is charged on the total debit entries for the period: N50,000 + N30,000 + N10,000 = N90,000. Because the rate is 15% per annum and the period is one quarter, the rate for the quarter is 15% / 4 = 3.75%, so COT = 0.0375 x N90,000 = N3,375.00. Option C applies the full annual rate to a three-month period, option A uses only one month's rate, and option B wrongly applies the rate to the opening balance instead of the debits."}},{"itemId":"36650ba0-8a97-5744-a351-b59121ab7d90","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5b20ebc6-0f9d-5d06-a825-999142c5ac40","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0587","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank attracted a corporate fixed deposit of N1,000,000.00 for 3 months at 12% per annum on a non-compound (simple interest) basis. How much will the bank pay the depositor at the end of the 3 months?","options":["N1,030,000.00","N1,360,000.00","N1,120,000.00","N30,000.00"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Simple interest is P x R x T = N1,000,000 x 0.12 x 3/12 = N30,000, and at maturity the bank repays the principal plus interest, N1,030,000.00. Option C charges a full year's interest on a 3-month deposit, and option B treats the 3 months as 3 years. Option D is only the interest, not the total amount paid to the depositor."}},{"itemId":"86575d0a-481c-5929-98ef-dfa321e2c662","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7dcd3300-4671-56bf-b706-c5900abf2c8a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-0588","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Eze was granted a vehicle loan of N200,000.00 on 1 January 1996, repayable over 2 years in 4 equal semi-annual instalments, with interest at 10% per annum calculated on a simple (flat) basis on the original principal. The loan was credited to his current account, which already had a credit balance of N40,000.00, and each repayment of principal and interest is debited to that account. How much is debited to his current account on each half-yearly repayment date?","options":["N70,000.00","N60,000.00","N50,000.00","N55,000.00"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Total flat interest is P x R x T = N200,000 x 0.10 x 2 = N40,000, or N10,000 for each of the 4 half-years, while each principal instalment is N200,000 / 4 = N50,000. Each repayment date therefore carries N50,000 + N10,000 = N60,000, which takes the account from N240,000 CR down to nil after the fourth payment. Option C leaves out the interest, option A charges a full year's interest (N20,000) every half-year, and option D calculates interest only on the instalment rather than on the original principal."}},{"itemId":"54f759c3-4eff-5929-9650-fd9c22150408","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"94416369-034a-501c-9d44-257e07fa038b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T001-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A yam farmer who wants cloth must find a weaver who has the cloth he wants and is also willing to take yams in exchange. This problem of barter is known as","options":["double coincidence of wants","lack of divisibility","lack of a store of value","difficulty of deferred payment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Double coincidence of wants means each party must want exactly what the other is offering at the same time, which is what the farmer and the weaver need here. Option B is about goods that cannot be split into small units, option C is about keeping wealth for the future, and option D is about postponing payment, none of which is the problem described."}},{"itemId":"0ccd8c65-8b71-554c-a250-704d5ef7623b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e52bfb52-a21f-5274-9b8b-d6fa9d5cedd6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In its general definition, money is","options":["any commodity that has intrinsic value of its own","only coins made of gold or silver","any asset that earns interest for its holder","anything that is generally acceptable in payment for goods and services and in settlement of debt"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The general definition rests on acceptability: whatever people generally accept in exchange and in settling debts serves as money, which is why items like cowries and tobacco once worked as money. Option A describes commodity money, only one type of money, option B is far too narrow, and option C is wrong because cash itself earns no interest."}},{"itemId":"398f177b-bd55-575f-af35-6bdc5849b807","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2db55556-3ed8-5863-9834-bc534ccf09f6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T002-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The monetarist (Chicago) view associated with Milton Friedman defines money as","options":["only notes and coins that the law declares to be legal tender","anything that serves as a temporary abode of purchasing power","whatever the monetary authority declares to be money by law","only cheques drawn on deposit money banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Friedman saw money as a temporary abode of purchasing power, a broad view that takes in cheques, drafts, promissory notes and other bills as well as cash. Option A is the narrow currency school view, option C is the legal definition of money, and option D wrongly limits money to cheques alone."}},{"itemId":"d76def4d-d15d-54d3-bb71-ab8302bc301a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"29c7ba99-5861-505f-8414-7ccd0a35574d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T002-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which school of thought accepted the general definition of money but insisted that bank cheques should also be treated as money?","options":["The banking school","The currency school","The Cambridge school","The classical school"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The banking school argued that since most deposit money bank transactions are settled by cheque, cheques should count as money alongside cash. Option B, the currency school, restricted money to legal tender currency, while options C and D are best known for their views on the demand for money, not for this definitional debate."}},{"itemId":"883f1cc6-59e4-50a3-b293-3f46701c50bd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"78fda811-d994-518e-acb8-c817e68e19db","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T003-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes money from near money?","options":["Near money is legal tender, while money has no legal status","Money earns a yield for its holder, while near money earns no return","Money is legal tender and can be spent directly, while near money mainly stores value and must first be converted into money before it can be spent","Near money can be spent directly, while money must first be converted"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money is legal tender, generally acceptable and fully liquid, while near money such as treasury bills or time deposits serves as a store of value and has to be converted, with some delay or cost, before it can be spent. Option A reverses the legal position, option B is wrong because cash earns no yield while near money assets usually do, and option D reverses the liquidity point."}},{"itemId":"0a2bfd2d-8f97-5919-a5f7-f8c6b74ffb44","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e210900-575e-57dd-9bc5-140f5cb97d22","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a trader buys goods today and agrees to pay for them in money three months later, money is performing its function as","options":["a medium of exchange","a standard of deferred payment","a unit of account","a store of value"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"A standard of deferred payment allows goods and services to be enjoyed now and paid for in money terms later, which is what this credit sale relies on. Option A describes paying at the moment of exchange, option C is about measuring and comparing values, and option D is about holding wealth over time."}},{"itemId":"71d703a3-278d-5d8c-9142-9a02608b5ea8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b9d44cab-c4b3-5dd6-90ff-7923c80e6b30","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T004-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Money serves as a common measure in which the values of all goods and services are expressed, much as the metre measures distance. This is the function of money as","options":["a store of value","a standard of deferred payment","a transfer of value","a unit of account"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"As a unit of account (standard of value), money lets the worth of every good be stated in one unit, so prices can be compared and accounts kept easily. Option A is about keeping wealth, option B is about settling debts in future, and option C is about moving value from one person or place to another."}},{"itemId":"ba36c223-0637-5903-a784-9131585ebaf4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e92cacb7-1b14-50c2-a6d0-5f8b1e8c2c41","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigeria, narrow money (M1) consists mainly of","options":["M1 plus time and savings deposits","currency outside banks plus treasury bills and time deposits","only the notes and coins held in the vaults of the CBN","currency outside banks plus demand deposits with deposit money banks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Narrow money, M1, is made up of currency in circulation outside the banks plus demand (current account) deposits, the money people can spend straight away. Option A describes broad money, M2, option B wrongly adds near money assets, and option C leaves out both the public's cash and bank deposits."}},{"itemId":"c66b5d42-bbec-5153-8645-7bf67082b893","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5091cf3-40fa-59eb-862c-ba9e04db87fc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T006-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following determinants of money supply is set exogenously by the central bank?","options":["The minimum cash reserve ratio","The public's desire to hold cash rather than bank deposits","Changes in the level of business activity in the economy","Customers' demand for bank loans and overdrafts"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The cash reserve ratio is fixed by the central bank, so it is an exogenous determinant of money supply that the public cannot change. Options B, C and D all depend on the behaviour of households and firms, which makes them endogenous influences on money supply."}},{"itemId":"2e84adfb-04dd-515a-9ea0-09b7caab26b6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cc3a377e-eaaf-516d-97b6-d7a0aef3be7e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Assuming a legal cash reserve ratio of 20% and no cash leakage from the banking system, an initial cash deposit of N2,000 can expand into total bank deposits of","options":["N8,000","N10,000","N2,400","N20,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Total deposits created equal the initial deposit divided by the reserve ratio: N2,000 / 0.20 = N10,000. Option A is the total of new loans (N10,000 minus the original N2,000), option C simply adds 20% to the deposit, and option D is what a 10% reserve ratio would give."}},{"itemId":"f7bdd7df-5d46-5da5-8dd0-656ef421ad82","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"74e6aa3a-d3ca-57be-b4f5-ae8c4c72187c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T008-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a factor that limits the ability of banks to create credit?","options":["The size of the legal cash reserve ratio","An increase in the cash deposited with banks by the public","Lack of acceptable collateral security from borrowers","Fear of cash drain to other banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"More cash deposits give banks a bigger reserve base, so they increase, not limit, the credit banks can create. Options A, C and D are all limits: a higher reserve ratio cuts lending, banks will not lend without good security, and fear of losing cash to other banks makes them cautious."}},{"itemId":"a112703b-a3e0-51f8-90ab-71429147d038","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff36b6d2-feef-5c86-b31a-490d4b988a8f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T008-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the cash reserve ratio is 12.5%, what is the credit creation multiplier, measured as total deposits created divided by the original deposit?","options":["7","8","12.5","80"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The multiplier is 1 divided by the reserve ratio, so 1 / 0.125 = 8; an original deposit of N1,000 could support N8,000 of total deposits. Option A counts only the new loans created relative to the deposit, option C just repeats the percentage, and option D misplaces the decimal point."}},{"itemId":"1477f254-fb61-53e7-8349-3bddab47db31","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ebc73754-4e4c-5237-9cbf-584b15b3e8ee","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The word 'bank' is generally traced to the Italian word 'banco', which referred to","options":["a strong room in which gold was stored","a receipt issued by goldsmiths for gold deposited","the bench or table on which money changers carried on their business","a temple in which valuables were kept for safety"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Italian money changers did their business on a bench or table, the banco, and the places where they changed money, took deposits and made loans came to be called banks. Option A describes where goldsmiths kept gold, option B describes the goldsmith's receipt that became the bank note, and option D describes how valuables were kept before banking began."}},{"itemId":"784d5159-203a-5b96-9ef1-fbdb6f5b1f7c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58e7e5bc-4ed1-5dd0-9486-814e9bdce161","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T009-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before modern banking developed, where were gold and other valuables commonly kept for safety in places such as ancient Rome, Greece and Mesopotamia?","options":["In central banks","In the vaults of commercial banks","In temples and other places of worship","In stock exchanges"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"In the ancient world, temples and places of worship were trusted as safe places to keep precious metals and valuables, long before banks existed. Options A, B and D are modern institutions that came into being much later."}},{"itemId":"b377f282-8325-57ba-a322-93ff87064a47","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"12363348-cc79-5567-a4f6-afaeda956cf8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Goldsmiths are credited with bringing lending into banking mainly because they","options":["were the first to issue legal tender currency on behalf of the state","were required by law to lend a fixed share of their deposits to government","charged fees for keeping gold and refused to pay interest on deposits","noticed that depositors did not all withdraw their gold at the same time, so they lent out part of the idle balances at interest"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Goldsmiths found that there were always balances left with them because owners did not all come for their gold at once, so they began lending these balances out at interest, which is the start of bank lending. Option A is wrong because legal tender is issued by the state or central bank, option B has no basis, and option C describes their early safe-keeping practice, not lending; they later paid interest to attract more deposits."}},{"itemId":"4197ed12-2d4c-5e1b-9a47-dc186d9f195e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b126437f-d359-537e-a121-fd9375f9e814","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T010-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Goldsmiths' receipts for gold deposited with them were later accepted as a means of payment. These receipts were the forerunners of","options":["treasury bills","bank notes","letters of credit","share certificates"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"As people began to pay one another with goldsmiths' receipts, the receipts came to be called goldsmiths' notes, the forerunners of the bank note. Option A is a short-term government security, option C is a bank's undertaking used in foreign trade, and option D is evidence of ownership in a company."}},{"itemId":"46a64b4b-a020-52e0-9e4a-2d28b78009e0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58c0d541-c288-5e18-a469-d8d07d6ee5ec","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T011-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a company raises funds by selling its bonds straight to households in the financial market, with no bank standing in between, this is known as","options":["indirect finance","financial intermediation","direct finance","credit creation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"In direct finance, borrowers get funds directly from lenders by selling them financial instruments in the market. Options A and B describe the route where an intermediary such as a bank borrows from savers and then lends to borrowers, and option D refers to banks expanding deposits through lending."}},{"itemId":"facb4c7a-3bb2-5907-ad75-03145fb95f43","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9ecde3f2-15e8-5307-987a-2d065b20d7d9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an objective of the Nigerian financial system?","options":["To achieve greater mobilisation of savings and channel them efficiently","To finance government spending without regard to its effect on inflation","To ensure that no viable project is frustrated simply for lack of funds","To assist in transforming the rural community"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"One stated objective of the financial system is to give non-inflationary support to the economy, so financing spending with no regard to inflation runs against its goals. Options A, C and D are genuine objectives: mobilising and channelling savings, making sure viable projects get funded, and helping rural transformation."}},{"itemId":"41eda806-373f-54af-8d29-80608a7a016b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"66fdac18-58e5-5348-961e-6a3d2e4cffb8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An IOU differs from a promissory note mainly because an IOU","options":["is an informal acknowledgement of a debt and is not a negotiable instrument","must always be drawn on a bank","can be freely transferred to another person by endorsement and delivery","is an unconditional order addressed by one person to another to pay"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bills of Exchange and Cheques","explanation":"An IOU simply records that one person owes another; it cannot be assigned, sold or traded, while a promissory note is a negotiable instrument. Option B describes a cheque, option C describes negotiability, which is a feature of the promissory note, and option D is the definition of a bill of exchange."}},{"itemId":"26a4546d-a388-51bc-9a69-399d9714958c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"96aea130-dba1-528c-a105-87ae1c4b596d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following contributed to the development of the Nigerian financial system?","options":["A return of the economy to trade by barter","The removal of all regulation of banks at independence","The establishment of the Central Bank of Nigeria","The closure of all indigenous banks after independence"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Setting up the CBN was a major step in developing the financial system, because the CBN went on to build money and capital market institutions and regulate the banks; other factors include independence, the oil boom, regulation and deregulation, and the bank reforms. Options A, B and D never happened and would have held the system back rather than developed it."}},{"itemId":"7c38c841-8922-5248-bb22-4ef233f0c3a4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"111a0aa8-514d-578c-90ba-6a4732b6a46d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a developmental function of the CBN rather than a traditional central banking function?","options":["Issuing legal tender currency","Acting as banker to the government","Acting as lender of last resort to banks","Directing commercial banks to open branches in rural areas under the rural banking scheme"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Pushing banks into rural areas is a developmental role, aimed at spreading banking and growth to neglected parts of the economy, much like the CBN's help in setting up development finance institutions. Options A, B and C are traditional functions that central banks perform everywhere."}},{"itemId":"a615d265-31fe-5ee9-8a78-de79d2e4173c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f78deea-81e9-5129-9bcf-8a0455ea08c8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In his 1953 report on establishing a central bank in Nigeria, J. L. Fisher concluded that","options":["a full central bank should be set up at once to finance rapid development","all expatriate banks in Nigeria should be nationalised","the financial environment hardly existed in Nigeria for a central bank to be established","Nigeria should adopt the US dollar as its official currency"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Fisher felt that Nigeria lacked the financial environment for a central bank and proposed a slow, three-stage approach instead, a view widely criticised as conservative. Option A is the opposite of his conclusion, and options B and D were never part of his report."}},{"itemId":"d9dbad79-3c4e-5fe8-917d-fd943c344ea7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"40246116-0dd6-5afc-8295-fd9df2f4ab4b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T018-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Whose report favoured the establishment of a central bank in Nigeria and formed part of the draft presented to the House of Representatives in 1958?","options":["J. L. Fisher","The IBRD (World Bank) mission","Lord Emmott's committee","J. B. Loynes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Loynes reported in favour of a central bank for Nigeria, and his recommendations shaped the 1958 law that set up the CBN. Option A, Fisher, said the environment was not ready, option B also felt it was too early and suggested a State Bank instead, and option C dealt with setting up the West African Currency Board decades earlier."}},{"itemId":"d0791c7c-b558-5f8f-87c4-87a91f7daf70","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b41d7229-a4b1-512e-b3d3-1431a0f9aa31","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the rural banking scheme that the CBN introduced in 1977, commercial banks were","options":["required to close their rural branches and move to cities","barred from lending to farmers","converted into community banks","directed to open branches in rural areas"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The rural banking scheme, run in phases from 1977, directed commercial banks to open branches in rural areas to bring banking services to the rural population. Option A is the opposite, option B is wrong because the aim was to increase rural lending, and option C is wrong because community banks were a separate, later scheme."}},{"itemId":"b8012d8b-e79e-5212-9868-329a903d9e35","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2b93bd1c-033a-5a5b-afb8-3949f32b7a07","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T019-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which grassroots banking scheme, introduced in 1990, replaced the rural banking scheme and later evolved into microfinance banking?","options":["Merchant banking","Community banking","Universal banking","Non-interest banking"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Community banks were introduced in 1990 to serve the grassroots and were later converted into microfinance banks. Option A is wholesale banking for corporate clients, option C removed the line between commercial and merchant banking, and option D is banking based on non-interest principles; none of them was set up to replace rural banking."}},{"itemId":"690c094a-383f-56ab-9711-08e49ffb28ec","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8888bc5b-d173-5ac7-a6b7-fc64868fffeb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Bank of British West Africa, established in 1894, is the forerunner of which present-day Nigerian bank?","options":["Union Bank of Nigeria","United Bank for Africa","First Bank of Nigeria","Wema Bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Bank of British West Africa took over from the African Banking Corporation, enjoyed a near monopoly of banking for years, and later became First Bank of Nigeria. Option A grew out of Barclays Bank DCO, option B grew out of the British and French Bank, and option D grew out of the indigenous Agbonmagbe Bank."}},{"itemId":"c489bbe5-8740-580a-afb7-d23c25e1202e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"23f72669-82c6-5d3c-b03e-2d7650ac8ce6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T022-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Barclays Bank DCO, which came to Nigeria in the 1920s after a merger that included the Colonial Bank, later became","options":["First Bank of Nigeria","United Bank for Africa","Wema Bank","Union Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Colonial Bank joined other banks to form Barclays Bank DCO, which later became Union Bank of Nigeria. Option A came from the Bank of British West Africa, option B came from the British and French Bank, and option C came from the indigenous Agbonmagbe Bank."}},{"itemId":"a8af7b7d-3f70-5fe8-9043-6f0b1b5845ba","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7c3f2f48-1c6a-5280-905e-780b2d5ccf3a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T022-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which bank, established in 1892 and engaged in distributing British currency, marked the beginning of commercial banking in Nigeria?","options":["Bank of British West Africa","Colonial Bank","African Banking Corporation","National Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Commercial banking in Nigeria began with the African Banking Corporation in 1892. Option A came in 1894 and took over its business, option B arrived later, and option D was an indigenous bank founded in the 1930s."}},{"itemId":"c16c2f43-5d33-5834-9c39-e1a2f860c899","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ed2d1a2d-562e-5de0-ae11-4d921c329961","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nigeria operates a branch banking system. This means that","options":["each bank is allowed to operate only one office","banks may operate only in the state where they were registered","each branch of a bank is a separately owned company","a single bank runs many offices across the country, one of which serves as its head office"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Under branch banking, one bank operates a network of offices across the country under a single head office and a single legal identity. Option A describes unit banking, option B describes a restriction that does not apply to national banks in Nigeria, and option C is wrong because branches belong to the same bank."}},{"itemId":"999f0fff-f23a-5601-9186-78f206042e2f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4314b334-911b-5e09-ac4a-1e4d46b061f7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T023-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the indigenisation policy of the 1970s, the Federal Government first acquired what share of the three big expatriate banks, a stake that was raised to 60% in 1976?","options":["20%","51%","40%","100%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Under the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion (Indigenisation) Decree, government took a 40% interest in the three big expatriate banks, later First Bank, Union Bank and UBA, and raised it to 60% in 1976. Options A, B and D do not match the stake first taken."}},{"itemId":"721352fd-a892-5689-b0ca-9a896e90a520","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"02c10f53-0d57-5803-bc37-3babe8f47ceb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a cause of the collapse of most indigenous banks in Nigeria in the early 1950s?","options":["Undercapitalisation","Fraud and bad or inexperienced management","Holding too much capital","Indiscriminate branch expansion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The early indigenous banks failed because they had too little capital, not too much. Options A, B and D were real causes, along with crude accounting systems and the stricter requirements of the 1952 banking law."}},{"itemId":"675c0747-8571-5da2-9923-8e597d13daff","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a3469e59-0b68-5bfd-9d1a-8826e31b42b5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T024-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which three indigenous banks survived the banking crisis of the early 1950s?","options":["National Bank of Nigeria, African Continental Bank and Agbonmagbe Bank","Industrial and Commercial Bank, Nigerian Mercantile Bank and National Bank of Nigeria","Bank of British West Africa, Barclays Bank DCO and British and French Bank","African Continental Bank, Industrial and Commercial Bank and Bank of British West Africa"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Of the many indigenous banks formed during the boom, only National Bank of Nigeria, African Continental Bank and Agbonmagbe Bank (later Wema Bank) survived, with help from their regional governments. Options B and D include the Industrial and Commercial Bank, which had already failed, and options C and D include expatriate banks, which were not indigenous."}},{"itemId":"e771a386-f1c8-5b90-8b74-1e6172c1fa6c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f28cb216-f86f-5dfc-b64e-45f61146e237","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A lending banker refuses to finance a customer's gambling business even though the customer offers good security. Which principle of lending is the banker applying?","options":["Profitability","Suitability","Liquidity","Safety"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Suitability asks whether the purpose of an advance is acceptable; banks should not finance gambling, betting or other speculative activities or anything against government policy, however good the security. Option A concerns earning a return, option C concerns how quickly the funds come back, and option D is already covered here by the good security."}},{"itemId":"45ed677b-bed1-5dfe-97a7-7f69237cee67","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5a3861e0-7ad9-5e09-a1c4-cfdcbed08b2e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T025-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is often described as the real security for any bank advance?","options":["A legal mortgage over landed property","The character of the borrower","A guarantee from a third party","Shares quoted on the stock exchange"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A good lending proposal should stand on its own, and the borrower's integrity, honesty and reliability are what really ensure repayment. Options A, C and D are acceptable forms of collateral, but they only protect the bank if things go wrong."}},{"itemId":"97c3f1c0-cd1e-5438-87a0-9405269253d5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2b7c8efc-f0b7-5603-8c08-3b4a0f8c8be9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Assets in a bank's balance sheet are usually listed","options":["in alphabetical order","in the order in which they were acquired","in order of liquidity, starting with cash","in order of size, starting with the largest"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Banks list their assets in order of liquidity, from cash and short-term funds down to fixed assets, which shows how well they can meet depositors' demands. Options A, B and D are not how a bank's balance sheet is set out."}},{"itemId":"f855779d-16a0-51c4-8204-8339c0f883bd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f46d4d86-b173-5a69-9e45-a8554c5fb290","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T026-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which asset in a deposit money bank's balance sheet is its main risk asset and generates the bulk of its income?","options":["Cash and short-term funds","Treasury bills","Fixed assets","Loans and advances"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Loans and advances are the credit facilities granted to customers; they carry the most risk and earn most of a bank's income, which is why they attract higher interest and are usually secured by collateral. Option A earns little or nothing, option B is safe and liquid but earns modest returns, and option C covers premises and equipment used in running the bank."}},{"itemId":"c07aa826-91e1-523b-923e-0d671d0cc582","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"46a84bc0-e660-5676-9883-27d4eeebbb2d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nigeria's Statement of Accounting Standard (SAS) 10 on accounting by banks focused mainly on","options":["staff recruitment, training and promotion","branch location, opening hours and staffing","allocation of foreign exchange to importers","income recognition, loss recognition and balance sheet classification"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"SAS 10 aimed to make banks' financial statements uniform and reliable by setting rules on when income and losses are recognised and how balance sheet items are classified, partly because profits were thought to be overstated. Options A, B and C are management or policy matters, not accounting standards."}},{"itemId":"ac1b70de-7971-523f-a777-f5488b23f68f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1c9a4493-1e9f-5de5-b488-c79e2f599310","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is usually the largest liability in the balance sheet of a deposit money bank?","options":["Customers' deposits","Share capital","Statutory reserves","Provision for taxation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Deposits from personal and business customers and other banks are the largest liability of a deposit money bank, and much of them can be withdrawn on demand or at short notice. Options B, C and D are also on the liabilities side but are far smaller than deposits."}},{"itemId":"6af931a5-87af-5b93-b319-9e6afec93a0c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ade92a6e-2c90-570a-bdfb-17b406b0a61c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T028-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a liability of a deposit money bank?","options":["Proposed dividends to shareholders","Bank drafts issued but not yet paid","Loans and advances to customers","Provision for taxation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Loans and advances are money owed to the bank by its customers, so they are assets. Options A, B and D are amounts the bank owes to others, so they appear among its liabilities."}},{"itemId":"920b7391-03fe-5d20-81c2-b46bbdc56f20","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"be50dc80-9f52-510b-9480-e73c5d5b8a4b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a non-deposit fund of a deposit money bank?","options":["Current account balances","Savings account balances","Retained earnings","Fixed deposits"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Balance Sheet Structure and Interpreting Customers' Accounts","explanation":"Retained earnings are profits kept back in the business after dividends are paid, so they come from the bank's own operations rather than from customers' deposits. Options A, B and D are all forms of depositors' funds brought in by customers."}},{"itemId":"05ed1fbf-35a3-543d-91f5-00f57204edba","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba2b617d-e326-56ad-8daf-d4d7d5009900","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank buys an accepted bill of exchange from its customer before maturity for less than its face value, and later collects the full amount from the acceptor. This service is called","options":["debt factoring","bill discounting","loan syndication","direct credit"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"In bill discounting the bank pays the customer the face value less a discount and then collects the full value at maturity, the difference being its earnings. Option A is the purchase of a firm's book debts, option C is several banks lending together, and option D is crediting uncleared cheques to an account."}},{"itemId":"267b5d56-e6b0-5fa1-bddc-a15ee2d4c178","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf426c4c-f609-5c4d-a922-f6de9adb687d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T031-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Debt factoring by a bank is essentially","options":["the lending of money by a group of banks to one large borrower","the hiring of equipment to a customer for regular rentals","the purchase of a customer's book debts at an agreed discount, after which the bank collects from the debtors","the crediting of uncleared cheques to a customer's account"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Factoring is the sale of book debts: the bank pays the customer an agreed part of their value and then collects directly from the debtors. Option A describes loan syndication, option B describes equipment leasing, and option D describes direct credit."}},{"itemId":"235d1767-4dd0-5140-935d-2d0b20b4f971","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fdafaef-9de4-537f-ac24-c61debb55b49","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T031-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a lead bank brings together several banks to jointly finance a large project that no single bank could conveniently fund, this is known as","options":["equipment leasing","bill discounting","debt factoring","loan syndication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"In loan syndication a lead bank organises a group of banks to share a big loan, often long-term, for a large corporate or government project. Option A is hiring out assets for rentals, option B is buying bills before maturity, and option C is buying a firm's book debts."}},{"itemId":"34798599-1c94-50d0-aecd-98ea6d0c1b0c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eab9f844-792f-5532-b8f6-3f61f4d01dfd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a customer deposits a sealed envelope or locked box with a bank for safe custody, the bank usually","options":["opens and values the contents and pays interest on them","treats the contents as a deposit that it may lend out","credits the customer's current account with the value of the items","accepts it 'contents unknown' and gives the customer a receipt that must be surrendered when the item is withdrawn"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"For safe custody the bank keeps sealed items 'contents unknown' (or lists them, signed by both sides) and issues a receipt, which the customer hands back to collect the item. Option A is wrong because the bank neither opens sealed items nor pays interest, and options B and C are wrong because the items stay the customer's property and are not money deposited with the bank."}},{"itemId":"c77af115-1fbd-5c62-9cce-beb330ca3548","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cc9dc579-552f-528c-9170-a63350c34cf0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which reply to a status enquiry shows the strongest opinion of a customer's ability to meet the commitment named?","options":["Undoubted for your figures","Considered good for your figures","Should be good for your figures","We cannot speak for your figures"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"'Undoubted for your figures' is the standard phrase for considerable strength. Option B means satisfactory, option C shows some doubt, and option D is an unfavourable reply."}},{"itemId":"7d509527-d8bd-583c-bfc7-4b2b55f639d6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50cf4b6c-bd3a-5566-8efb-9fa323223b87","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T033-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"To protect themselves from liability when giving opinions on customers' creditworthiness, bankers usually","options":["disclose the customer's exact balance and borrowing so the enquirer has the full facts","reply in brief, general terms and in confidence, without disclosing details of the customer's account","guarantee the customer's debts to the enquirer","attach copies of the customer's statements to support the opinion"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank-Customer Relationship","explanation":"Banks answer status enquiries in general terms and in confidence, often in set phrases that other bankers understand, so they neither break the duty of secrecy nor take on liability. Options A and D would breach secrecy, and option C would make the bank liable for the customer's debts, which is exactly what bankers avoid."}},{"itemId":"4147a2d1-9ec5-5cae-b1d9-a3516eef9cbc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f03a8b2-a556-52ba-9374-838347d0390d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an international method of payment rather than a domestic one?","options":["Standing order","Direct debit","Intra-bank transfer","Documentary letter of credit"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A documentary letter of credit is used in foreign trade to guarantee that an overseas exporter is paid if he meets the terms of the credit. Options A, B and C are domestic methods used to move funds between accounts within the country."}},{"itemId":"3b45c7b1-4a80-5c64-8e77-66355c3e0a4b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ac1f52fe-bbfd-5a34-bb75-139e4a1ccc11","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T034-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"From the exporter's point of view, which method of receiving payment for exports carries the greatest risk?","options":["Open account","Documentary letter of credit","Documentary collection","Payment in advance"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Under open account the exporter sends the documents of title straight to the importer and simply trusts him to pay later, so the exporter loses control of the goods. Option B is the safest because the issuing bank guarantees payment, option C at least keeps the documents under bank control until payment or acceptance, and option D means the exporter is paid before he ships."}},{"itemId":"dc1b165a-2ea5-5350-bfc6-2bbe0a52cfb7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"75f58ae9-6020-54d3-a356-d3365c1b633d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a documentary letter of credit transaction, the bank that opens the credit in favour of the exporter is","options":["the exporter's bank, known as the advising bank","the central bank of the exporter's country","the importer's bank, known as the issuing bank","the shipping company that carries the goods"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"After the buyer and seller agree terms, the importer's bank (the issuing bank) opens the letter of credit in favour of the exporter. Option A is the bank that tells the exporter about the credit, not the one that opens it, option B plays no direct part, and option D carries the goods and issues the bill of lading but is not a bank."}},{"itemId":"63f6b761-bd4e-5929-ab35-1206ac5cdc81","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45c71c23-dc52-5f15-aaea-b0cb44a7df1f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T035-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under a documentary credit, the exporter keeps control of the goods because","options":["the goods remain in the exporter's warehouse until the importer collects them in person","the importer pays the shipping company directly before loading","the advising bank buys the goods from the exporter","the documents of title are released to the importer only against payment or acceptance of the bill of exchange"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"The documents of title, such as the bill of lading, are handed over only when the importer pays (documents against payment) or accepts the bill (documents against acceptance), so the exporter is protected. Options A and B do not describe how the system works, and option C is wrong because the advising bank deals in documents, not goods."}},{"itemId":"966e6016-40e7-5015-9b61-399052465dda","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"85ef7524-7786-528e-9466-f983bbe9d221","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Nigerian bank keeps a US dollar account with a correspondent bank in New York to receive export proceeds and settle foreign payments. From the Nigerian bank's point of view, this account is","options":["a vostro account","a suspense account","a domiciliary account","a nostro account"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Account Services for Exporters and Importers","explanation":"Nostro means 'our', so it is the Nigerian bank's own account held abroad in foreign currency. Option A ('your') is an account a foreign bank keeps with a Nigerian bank in naira, option B is an internal holding account, and option C is a foreign currency account a customer opens with a Nigerian bank."}},{"itemId":"c543503c-ff36-57c8-a1b3-14ca5c6a2518","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f1e6ae3-170e-522e-873d-ee797806abb4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T036-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main difference between a direct debit and a standing order is that","options":["a standing order is started by the beneficiary, while a direct debit is started by the customer","a standing order can be used only for international payments","a direct debit is started by the beneficiary (creditor) with the customer's authority, while a standing order is started by the customer","a direct debit needs no authority from the customer whose account is debited"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"With a direct debit, the creditor, such as an insurance company, prepares the claim and the customer signs an authority allowing the bank to debit his account; with a standing order, the customer himself tells the bank to make regular payments. Option A swaps the two, option B is wrong because standing orders are a domestic method, and option D is wrong because the customer's signed authority is essential."}},{"itemId":"ce7d46ed-d18c-5790-a3fd-2ada5affcad0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ad3dec75-6860-5f2a-9f88-4b5a75c898ed","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T036-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"As methods of payment in Nigeria, a bank cheque differs from a bank draft in that","options":["a bank draft is used only within the same clearing area, while a bank cheque can be used anywhere","a bank cheque is used within the same clearing area, while a bank draft is drawn by one branch on another branch or head office and can be used across clearing areas","a bank cheque is drawn by the customer on his own account, while a bank draft is drawn by the central bank","a bank draft may be freely dishonoured, while a bank cheque never can be"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Both are bank instruments bought by the customer, but a bank cheque has the narrower use, within one clearing area, while a draft is drawn by a branch on another branch or head office of the same bank for payments elsewhere. Option A swaps them, option C confuses a bank cheque with a personal cheque and wrongly involves the central bank, and option D is wrong because drafts, being bank instruments, are not normally dishonoured."}},{"itemId":"00772897-87f9-525d-83be-f1a589b93e83","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d50e815b-636e-503f-b1a1-c0329d474471","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which service is regarded as the traditional function that marks merchant banks out from other financial institutions?","options":["Accepting bills of exchange to finance the trade of others","Opening savings accounts for individuals","Issuing legal tender currency","Running large rural branch networks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Acceptance, putting the bank's name on bills of exchange so that others' trade can be financed, is the traditional and distinctive merchant banking function. Option B is retail banking, option C belongs to the central bank, and option D describes commercial banks, since merchant banks need few branches."}},{"itemId":"78c0b2a6-6d8b-50f9-ae36-d49db34222e0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a3bab3e-fa4f-5b10-815f-5f28b6b1f083","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T038-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A merchant bank that helps a company raise capital from the public through an offer for subscription and advises it on getting a stock exchange quotation is providing","options":["issuing house services","equipment leasing","acceptance credit","debt factoring"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"As an issuing house, a merchant bank guides companies through offers for subscription or sale, places their shares with the public and advises on stock exchange quotation. Option B is hiring out assets, option C is short-term trade finance through accepted bills, and option D is buying book debts."}},{"itemId":"4126f6bc-733c-5eeb-b05c-1a7bf223294c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3ee954e-1e94-5091-a61e-5d207c93e0a4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes merchant banks from commercial banks in Nigeria?","options":["Commercial banks are wholesale bankers, while merchant banks are retail bankers","Merchant banks rely on large rural branch networks, while commercial banks have few branches","Merchant banks issue currency, while commercial banks do not","Merchant banks are wholesale bankers dealing mainly with corporate and institutional clients, while commercial banks are retail bankers serving the general public"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Merchant banks deal in large sums for firms and institutions, mostly from a few urban offices, while commercial banks serve individuals and businesses of all sizes through wide branch networks. Option A reverses the roles, option B is wrong because it is commercial banks that have branches in rural areas, and option C is wrong because only the CBN issues currency."}},{"itemId":"44b82316-7104-5ef7-871c-5d32d851126b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a67aa47e-5277-5bd6-b28c-e630894e9585","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main justification for setting up development banks in developing countries is that they","options":["provide short-term salary advances to workers","provide long-term finance to priority sectors whose projects are too large or slow to mature to attract conventional banks","issue currency and control the money supply","compete with commercial banks for current account deposits"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Development finance institutions fill gaps that ordinary banks leave, giving long-term loans to real sector and infrastructure projects with long gestation periods or very large funding needs. Option A is a deposit money bank service, option C is a central bank function, and option D is not their purpose."}},{"itemId":"8069b83f-ebb2-5fa4-b35e-f2f3eac897bd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"efaca804-1a0f-5ca8-9c6e-9d6630866433","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Bank of Industry was formed through the merger of","options":["the Nigerian Agricultural and Co-operative Bank, the People's Bank and the Family Economic Advancement Programme","the Federal Mortgage Bank, the Nigeria Export-Import Bank and the Urban Development Bank","the Nigerian Industrial Development Bank, the Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry and the National Economic Reconstruction Fund","First Bank, Union Bank and United Bank for Africa"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Bank of Industry was created by merging NIDB, NBCI and NERFUND, to give financial support to large, medium and small industrial enterprises. Option A is the merger that produced the Bank of Agriculture, option B lists separate institutions that were not merged, and option D lists commercial banks."}},{"itemId":"8daa3a5c-b9e6-5542-b2ca-e344ec29cb80","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a486bdc1-38ce-52de-9cdc-6b2e1d9c06f5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T041-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The merger of the Nigerian Agricultural and Co-operative Bank, the People's Bank of Nigeria and the Family Economic Advancement Programme produced the institution now known as","options":["the Bank of Agriculture","the Bank of Industry","the Nigeria Export-Import Bank","the Infrastructure Bank"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"These three bodies were merged into the bank now called the Bank of Agriculture, whose main role is lending to farmers to boost food production. Option B came from NIDB, NBCI and NERFUND, option C was set up separately to finance exporters, and option D was formerly the Urban Development Bank."}},{"itemId":"5dc4810d-6ac6-505f-9796-7dd44aa4356c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5fa497da-9115-5d2c-8063-899257dea9ce","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT one of the problems that undermined development finance institutions in Nigeria?","options":["Politicisation of lending decisions","A very high proportion of non-performing loans","Lack of quality staff","Strict commercial management that was free of political influence"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The trouble with the DFIs was the reverse: non-commercial governance and political interference in lending. Options A, B and C were real problems, together with poor credit discipline, bureaucracy and corruption, which left most of their loans non-performing."}},{"itemId":"081fd830-91e1-532f-8ef4-a412d9522cc4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"59bfd9b1-395a-59a7-9b92-c15963b7c072","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The mortgage finance market set up under the Mortgage Institutions Decree of 1989 has a two-tier structure in which","options":["primary mortgage institutions are the apex and the Federal Mortgage Bank forms the second tier","the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria is the apex institution and primary mortgage institutions form the second tier","insurance companies are the apex and building societies form the second tier","the stock exchange is the apex and finance houses form the second tier"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The 1989 law made the Federal Mortgage Bank the apex mortgage institution, with a network of primary mortgage institutions below it that take deposits and lend to people buying or building houses. Option A turns the structure upside down, and options C and D name institutions outside the mortgage system."}},{"itemId":"11cd2c74-acfd-5c81-8637-44265b1cc856","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ec8edbf6-ea4f-52a8-bdfc-0e0dc4f0215e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T043-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which institution manages and administers Nigeria's National Housing Fund?","options":["The Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria","The Bank of Industry","The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","The Securities and Exchange Commission"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Federal Mortgage Bank manages the National Housing Fund, which pools workers' contributions to provide cheap long-term housing loans. Option B finances industry, option C insures bank deposits, and option D regulates the capital market."}},{"itemId":"e64af811-b763-5187-9718-5b69788a069f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9d8880ef-78f0-5752-8cad-d97167f0ea79","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a problem holding back mortgage financing in Nigeria?","options":["Difficulty in obtaining title documents to land","An abundant supply of cheap long-term funds for mortgage institutions","The low income of most Nigerians","Lack of public awareness of mortgage services"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Mortgage institutions actually suffer from a shortage of long-term funds, since they compete for short-term money in the open market. Options A, C and D are real problems, along with weak capital, a shortage of skilled staff and poverty."}},{"itemId":"e1f79569-9f1d-5bfa-aec2-9fe2689916a4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c3401e38-fdde-5e90-91c7-651f42b0caef","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which function of the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria was later transferred to the Central Bank of Nigeria, while the Federal Mortgage Bank kept its role in the National Housing Fund?","options":["Granting long-term housing loans","Management of the National Housing Fund","Issuing legal tender currency","Regulation and supervision of primary mortgage institutions"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The Federal Mortgage Bank's role as regulator and supervisor of mortgage institutions passed to the CBN, while it kept managing the National Housing Fund. Option A is still a lending role, option B is the function it kept, and option C has always belonged to the CBN."}},{"itemId":"1da1e2e9-5545-5468-8492-1f9631035b2f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f7cc912-be83-5d1b-9e2a-87c451388324","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The basic distinction between bank and non-bank financial intermediaries is that","options":["only non-bank intermediaries accept funds from the public","the liabilities of banks, such as demand deposits, can be spent as money, while the liabilities of non-bank intermediaries cannot","banks are unregulated, while non-bank intermediaries are regulated","non-bank intermediaries can create deposits through lending, while banks cannot"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Bank deposits can be used as a means of payment, for example by cheque, while claims on non-bank intermediaries such as insurance policies cannot; this is also why banks can create credit and non-banks really cannot. Option A is wrong because banks accept deposits too, option C is wrong because banks are closely regulated, and option D reverses the credit creation point."}},{"itemId":"71a92449-eeaf-5e5c-9650-e7c6c3fbd29b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"954fbd44-2d26-53c5-98b1-331b97649dad","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Insurance companies perform two major functions in the economy. These are","options":["risk transfer and financial intermediation","currency issue and credit control","bill acceptance and loan syndication","cheque clearing and foreign exchange allocation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Insurers pool the premiums of many people to cover the losses of a few (risk transfer) and invest the pooled funds in the economy (financial intermediation). Option B describes the central bank, option C describes merchant and commercial banking services, and option D describes the payment and foreign exchange systems."}},{"itemId":"26074dc8-f6c4-5644-aca7-1ceefe0fff5f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dac74390-a3f3-5ec8-8996-6894be17fac0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T047-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why do life insurance companies tend to invest in long-term assets, while non-life insurers keep most of their funds in short-term securities?","options":["Life insurers are not allowed to buy treasury bills","Non-life insurers collect much larger premiums than life insurers","Life insurance is regulated by the CBN, while non-life insurance is not","Life policies run for long periods, while non-life policies are usually renewed every year"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Insurers match their investments to the length of their liabilities: life policies can run for twenty years or more, so their funds go into long-term investments, while motor, fire and similar policies last about a year. Options A, B and C do not explain the difference and are not true."}},{"itemId":"492fabff-8685-537f-ab75-e5cac3272707","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5aa367e7-f200-5b9b-b205-cd4640fa0e17","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a typical service of a finance company in Nigeria?","options":["Local purchase order (LPO) financing","Issuing legal tender currency","Acting as lender of last resort to banks","Clearing cheques for deposit money banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Finance companies provide services such as LPO financing, equipment leasing, hire purchase, debt factoring and project financing to consumers and businesses. Options B and C are functions of the central bank, and option D is done through the clearing system run for the banks."}},{"itemId":"c17b6889-6255-5a09-b901-d9de68dcbcde","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c30ae177-7faa-546f-aafc-57cf0c67d946","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The 'liquidity versus profitability' problem of a commercial bank arises because","options":["holding more cash helps the bank meet withdrawals but reduces the income it could earn by lending or investing those funds","the most profitable loans are always the most liquid assets","banks are not allowed to hold any cash","liquidity and profitability always rise together"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Cash is the most liquid asset but earns nothing, while loans and investments earn income but cannot be turned into cash as quickly, so the bank must balance the two to stay both safe and profitable. Option B is wrong because profitable loans are among the least liquid assets, option C is wrong because banks must keep cash and liquid assets, and option D misses the conflict between the two."}},{"itemId":"e55eb16f-02e0-59cf-8a04-4d48fc6c9891","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b7421cd3-e8ba-50aa-bf55-217e0925a3c8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T050-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking, liquidity refers to","options":["the ability of a bank to earn high profits on its loans","the ability of a bank to meet its customers' demands for cash and its other obligations as they fall due","the excess of a bank's total assets over its total liabilities","the share of a bank's capital owned by foreign investors"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"A liquid bank can pay depositors on demand and meet its daily cash needs, which keeps public confidence and satisfies the CBN's liquidity ratio. Option A describes profitability, option C describes solvency, and option D is about ownership."}},{"itemId":"b758cdb9-b778-52b2-a85e-3f922ae4a99d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e801ee79-c64b-5b9d-8020-3b5e0254cf46","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a reason why a deposit money bank keeps an adequate level of liquidity?","options":["To meet customers' cash withdrawals, since current account holders can demand their money without notice","To maximise the interest it earns by lending out as much of its deposits as possible","To maintain public confidence in the bank","To meet the liquidity requirements prescribed by the Central Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Lending out as much as possible to earn interest is the profitability objective, and it actually pulls against liquidity because loans cannot be turned into cash quickly. Banks stay liquid to pay depositors on demand (A), to keep the public's confidence (C) and to satisfy the CBN's liquidity ratio (D), so all three of those are genuine reasons."}},{"itemId":"8c79ee23-b120-5daa-92aa-5b4de950345a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"62fb9174-84ff-5bd2-b295-37819ee8cb81","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T051-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking, the term 'liquidity' refers mainly to a bank's:","options":["Ability to earn enough profit to pay dividends to shareholders","Excess of total assets over total liabilities","Total amount of paid-up share capital","Ability to meet customers' demands for cash and its other obligations as they fall due"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Liquidity is about having cash, or assets that can quickly become cash without much loss, so the bank can pay depositors on demand. Option B describes solvency, and a bank can be solvent but still illiquid if its assets are tied up in long-term loans. Option A is profitability, and C is the bank's capital base, which is a different thing."}},{"itemId":"b6457726-1038-500b-b58f-dea33a2fd933","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cb5ded78-0ca9-54a1-ae0b-7cdba92de855","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the Central Bank of Nigeria wants to reduce the money supply through open market operations, it will:","options":["Sell government securities to banks and the non-bank public","Lower the rate at which it lends to banks","Reduce the cash reserve ratio banks must keep with it","Buy government securities from banks and the non-bank public"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"When the CBN sells securities, buyers pay with money drawn from bank deposits, so bank reserves fall and banks can create less credit, which shrinks the money supply. Buying securities (D) does the opposite and injects money. Options C and B are not open market operations at all, and both would make it easier for banks to lend, not harder."}},{"itemId":"6531b158-8288-5e3e-a2ea-a2aa57721b38","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f8cbd842-2bf0-531d-a7f2-d9640440e9ac","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T053-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the most likely effect when the Central Bank of Nigeria raises the legal (cash) reserve requirement for banks?","options":["Interest rates on loans tend to fall sharply","The CBN directly buys more treasury bills from banks","Banks are able to grant more loans and advances","Banks are left with less money to lend, so credit and money supply tend to contract"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"A reserve requirement is the share of deposits that banks must hold as cash or as balances with the CBN that earn no interest. Raising it ties up more of their deposits, so banks may have to cut loans and investments, and the money supply contracts. Option C is what happens when the ratio is lowered, A is the opposite of the usual effect because tighter credit tends to push lending rates up, and B describes an open market purchase, which is a separate tool."}},{"itemId":"6aedc449-5bf5-540b-8af6-15f18055dd59","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c67f345-eaef-5910-8b57-d1f9cb0b6759","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T053-c","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the Central Bank of Nigeria uses moral suasion as a monetary policy tool, it:","options":["Fixes the minimum margin of security required for bank advances","Buys and sells government securities in the open market","Persuades banks to follow policies it considers in the national interest, without any legal compulsion","Sets a legal maximum on the total advances each bank may grant"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Moral suasion is persuasion: the CBN appeals to bankers through meetings, letters and dialogue, and the tool has no legal backing behind it. Option D is a credit (loan) ceiling, B is open market operations, and A is a margin requirement. Those three are formal, enforceable controls, unlike moral suasion."}},{"itemId":"d4fd6bb0-e156-5d28-a09f-e57356f5b73e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fb972a65-178e-5be5-bd03-e3583a3135dd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the microfinance policy issued by the Central Bank of Nigeria in December 2005, what was to happen to existing community banks?","options":["They were to be taken over by the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation as its branches","They were to become discount houses trading in treasury bills","They were to convert into microfinance banks by meeting the new capital and other requirements","They were to become merchant banks dealing in wholesale finance"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Community banks had not closed the large gap in financial services for poor and low-income Nigerians, so the 2005 policy phased them out and required them to recapitalise and convert into microfinance banks. Merchant banking (D) and discount house business (B) are wholesale activities, nothing like grassroots banking. The NDIC (A) insures deposits and does not run banks as branches."}},{"itemId":"ac4c9759-8653-5ffd-9ae5-34a5cccd6f97","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fb61a65-5721-573d-8aa0-e71241e24498","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T055-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the goals of microfinance banks in Nigeria?","options":["Mobilising savings for intermediation","Creating employment and raising the productivity and living standards of low-income households","Dealing in foreign exchange and opening domiciliary accounts for large corporate customers","Providing affordable and dependable financial services to the economically active poor"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Microfinance banks are meant for grassroots finance. A unit or state microfinance bank is not allowed to offer more sophisticated services such as foreign exchange business, which is left to deposit money banks. Serving the active poor (D), mobilising savings (A) and creating jobs to lift household incomes (B) are all stated goals of microfinance banks."}},{"itemId":"7dd4de7e-5f46-5dc0-868f-802ed339fc34","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cd587d3a-51de-567e-bfca-0f7ee58e78db","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bureaux de change were licensed in Nigeria as a segment of the foreign exchange market mainly in order to:","options":["Grant medium- and long-term loans to exporters","Trade treasury bills and other short-term government securities","Supply foreign exchange in bulk to banks and the Central Bank of Nigeria","Retail foreign exchange to small users and reduce the pressure on the official market"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Bureaux de change give small buyers, such as travellers, easy and legal access to foreign currency, and this takes some demand off the official market. They were expected to get their foreign exchange from private suppliers, not to supply banks or the CBN (C). Lending to exporters (A) is bank business, and trading treasury bills (B) is what discount houses do."}},{"itemId":"8f9a255f-3066-5bd3-bb5e-866c3ec3d9d8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"40a8deb5-f073-54de-83d5-fff5431e1729","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T056-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the objectives for licensing bureaux de change in Nigeria?","options":["To accept savings deposits and grant overdrafts to small businesses","To improve the statistical coverage of foreign exchange flows for better economic management","To give small buyers free and convenient access to foreign exchange","To give legal recognition to small dealers in foreign exchange"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A bureau de change only buys and sells foreign currency, so it does not take deposits or grant loans. Legal recognition for small dealers (D), easy access for small buyers (C) and better data on foreign exchange flows (B) were all reasons for licensing them."}},{"itemId":"9b8184ed-32b2-51c5-a558-6cb38485b1f4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d3100929-6c22-50c0-bc9e-54894af1ca82","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main function of a discount house in the Nigerian money market is to:","options":["Buy and sell shares of quoted companies on the stock exchange","Accept current account deposits from the public and issue cheque books","Act as an intermediary between the Central Bank of Nigeria and banks by trading in and holding treasury bills, commercial bills and other short-term securities","Provide long-term loans for industrial projects"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Discount houses are money market specialists. They buy, hold and sell short-term instruments such as treasury bills, and they stand between the CBN and the banks, which helps the money market grow and work efficiently. Retail current accounts (B) are deposit money bank business, long-term industrial loans (D) belong to development banks, and share dealing (A) is a capital market activity."}},{"itemId":"8971217e-aac4-58ad-b871-b11887ae4231","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d614eed-8223-581d-ba34-ccb7580a5f47","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T057-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes how discount houses help banks in the money market?","options":["They issue legal tender currency notes on behalf of banks","They fix the official exchange rate of the naira","They give banks a place to invest surplus short-term funds and to get funds back quickly when they need liquidity","They insure bank deposits against loss when a bank fails"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"By taking banks' surplus short-term funds and investing them in instruments such as treasury bills, which can be turned back into cash quickly, discount houses help banks adjust their liquidity at a profit. Deposit insurance (D) is the NDIC's job, while managing the naira exchange rate (B) and issuing currency (A) are CBN functions."}},{"itemId":"f67c8f32-536f-5c6b-a1ae-ca639718c9bb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0620b3ec-b231-569f-8bfb-c12d6d241ed1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a unit trust from a discount house in Nigeria?","options":["Both are deposit money banks licensed to operate current accounts","A unit trust pools the funds of many small savers and invests them in a diversified portfolio of securities, while a discount house deals mainly in short-term money market instruments","A unit trust deals mainly in treasury bills for banks, while a discount house invests small savers' funds in shares","A unit trust lends mainly to farmers, while a discount house finances housing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A unit trust is a collective investment scheme: it sells units to small investors and spreads the pooled money across many securities, so each saver gets diversification. A discount house works in the short-term money market with instruments such as treasury bills. Option C swaps the two roles, and A and D describe neither institution."}},{"itemId":"92ff0088-ed31-5cca-8587-f8673785ff45","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4695bc04-cd25-5638-a9df-38da6653c5e6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A financial market can best be defined as:","options":["A market in which only the Central Bank of Nigeria buys and sells goods","The department of a bank that handles customers' complaints","A set of institutional arrangements that brings together surplus and deficit units so that funds can be channelled from savers to users","A physical building where only bank notes and coins are exchanged"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A financial market is about intermediating funds: it mobilises savings from surplus units and passes them to deficit units for productive use, creating financial assets in the process. It is a network of arrangements and does not need to be a single building (D), and many participants trade in it, not just the CBN (A). Option B has nothing to do with markets."}},{"itemId":"39cd99a6-6df9-5e78-b4fb-4ce082391d6f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fc92ac2e-b37b-5626-8eb9-a4800015452b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T063-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the difference between the primary market and the secondary market in the financial system?","options":["The primary market is for government securities only, while the secondary market is for company shares only","The primary market deals only in short-term funds, while the secondary market deals only in long-term funds","The primary market is for raising new funds through new issues, while the secondary market is for buying and selling existing securities","The primary market is controlled by commercial banks, while the secondary market is controlled by insurance companies"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Both the money market and the capital market have primary and secondary segments. New securities are sold and fresh funds raised in the primary market, and existing securities change hands in the secondary market. Option B confuses this with the money market versus capital market split, and A and D invent restrictions that do not exist."}},{"itemId":"9cae319c-d5c6-57d0-9a9d-23aaa709c3cf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cc9b48ee-13c9-5ac5-b1ad-2a2581c1b166","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a money market instrument in Nigeria?","options":["Treasury bills","Certificates of deposit","Ordinary shares of a quoted company","Treasury certificates"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Ordinary shares have no maturity date and are traded on the stock exchange, so they belong to the capital market. Treasury bills (A), treasury certificates (D) and certificates of deposit (B) are all short-term instruments that can quickly be turned into cash, which is what makes them money market instruments."}},{"itemId":"964b525b-9839-5240-9d16-96ae45ef311f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5fd7fd7d-4d19-542a-a9a0-039a2d4a94cc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T064-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Treasury bills in Nigeria are best described as:","options":["Shares issued by deposit money banks to raise capital","Long-term bonds issued by state governments for infrastructure","Bills of exchange drawn by exporters on foreign buyers","Short-term debt instruments issued by the Central Bank of Nigeria on behalf of the Federal Government"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"A treasury bill is a short-term government IOU. The CBN issues it on behalf of the Federal Government to meet the government's temporary financing needs, and it is the main money market instrument. Long-term state bonds (B) and bank shares (A) are capital market instruments, and a bill drawn by an exporter (C) is a trade bill, not a treasury bill."}},{"itemId":"2baf1885-089c-5f1f-ab53-83ab641f9947","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c6e0ab2c-1dbb-52df-beff-e621f5f544aa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T065","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The basic difference between the money market and the capital market is that:","options":["The money market deals in short-term funds and instruments, while the capital market deals in long-term funds and instruments","The money market is regulated by the Securities and Exchange Commission, while the capital market is regulated by the NDIC","The money market is for individuals only, while the capital market is for companies only","The money market deals only in foreign currency, while the capital market deals only in naira"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"The main distinction is the term and liquidity of the funds. The money market handles short-term, highly liquid claims used for liquidity adjustment, and the capital market channels savings into long-term investment. Both markets serve individuals, firms and governments, so C is wrong. Option B mixes up the regulators, since the CBN oversees the money market and SEC the capital market, and D confuses either market with the foreign exchange market."}},{"itemId":"8ffd9ac1-935b-50c6-8c08-de4a41230925","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e9c9801e-6693-5678-a72f-48d894302141","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T065-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manufacturing company needs funds to build a new factory that will be repaid over 15 years. The most appropriate market to raise these funds is the:","options":["Inter-bank call money market","Capital market","Money market","Foreign exchange market"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"A 15-year project needs long-term finance, and long-term funds, for example through shares or bonds, are raised in the capital market. The money market (C) and the inter-bank call market (A) deal in short-term funds that would have to be repaid long before the factory starts paying for itself. The foreign exchange market (D) is for buying and selling currencies."}},{"itemId":"4912d72c-18dd-5174-a73f-c20932251868","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1603ec95-16e6-59fe-8974-e68843ef44a9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T077","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking, a 'distressed' bank is best described as one that:","options":["Is owned by the government rather than by private investors","Has severe financial, operational and managerial weaknesses that make it unable to meet its obligations to customers, owners and others as they fall due","Has temporarily closed a branch for renovation","Has more customers than it can serve in its banking hall"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Distress means a bank is in serious trouble. Its capital may be eroded, many of its loans may have gone bad, it may be short of cash and badly managed, so it cannot pay depositors and other creditors as and when due. Closing a branch (C) or having crowded banking halls (D) says nothing about financial health, and ownership (A) does not define distress."}},{"itemId":"2ef08685-d920-5c42-8abe-78ac95627bb3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b30ae5b2-7518-54fe-8779-62d241b9e6ac","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T077-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The CAMEL rating system used by Nigerian regulators to judge the health of a bank does NOT include which of the following?","options":["Capital adequacy","Asset quality","Earnings strength","Collateral value of customers' property"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"CAMEL stands for Capital adequacy, Asset quality, Management competence, Earnings strength and Liquidity, the five things regulators check to decide whether a bank is sound or distressed. The C stands for capital, not collateral. Capital adequacy (A), asset quality (B) and earnings (C) are all part of the system."}},{"itemId":"116c8a69-2c1e-50ba-a789-5e1a9a1cb600","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7df54d94-0564-5ce8-adae-ccfbe617b1f2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T078","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an INTERNAL (bank-specific) cause of distress in Nigerian banks?","options":["Political instability in the country","Macroeconomic instability such as recession and high inflation","Weak management, with insider abuse and poor credit control","A sharp fall in world oil prices"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Internal causes come from inside the bank, such as incompetent management, too little capital, fraud and insider lending, and poor management of assets and liabilities. Recession and inflation (B), political instability (A) and oil price shocks (D) are external factors outside the bank's control. They can still push weak banks into distress."}},{"itemId":"cb95fce9-0d9d-5e44-91cb-b3a43c6c9472","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b8c99ec6-ae6b-5737-8312-c35bdc012220","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T078-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following measures would help to curb distress in Nigerian banks?","options":["Granting more unsecured loans to directors and other insiders","Allowing banks to operate with lower capital","Improving the quality of bank management and strengthening internal controls against fraud","Reducing supervision and examination of banks by the regulators"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Poor management and fraud were among the main causes of bank distress, so better management, stronger internal controls, sound credit policies and good liquidity management are the main remedies. Lower capital (B), more insider lending (A) and weaker supervision (D) are the kinds of conditions that cause distress, so they would make it worse."}},{"itemId":"e5e2a72b-58b1-5ce1-9609-46a800440e80","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7c390013-41ae-5799-8d21-8a551bb463fa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T079","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a typical early warning sign (symptom) that a bank is becoming distressed?","options":["A steady rise in customer deposits and profits","Approval of new branches by the Central Bank of Nigeria","Persistent adverse clearing positions and borrowing at rates well above the market rate","Prompt payment of all customers' withdrawals"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"A bank heading for distress often keeps ending up short at clearing, overdraws its account with the CBN and borrows desperately at high rates. Other warning signs include window dressing of accounts, frequent boardroom quarrels and customer complaints about unpaid obligations. Rising deposits and profits (A) and prompt payment of withdrawals (D) point to a healthy bank, and branch approval (B) is a normal business event."}},{"itemId":"83e7a5d5-b35b-5d47-a318-4c2de8ec65d4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38d37f59-7ed3-50b5-ae1d-27f51ca76c8a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T080","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a major reason for the banking consolidation exercise announced by the Central Bank of Nigeria in July 2004?","options":["Nigeria had too few banks to serve the economy","The NDIC had stopped insuring bank deposits","Many Nigerian banks were small, fragile and had a weak capital base compared with their expected role in the economy","Banks were holding too much capital and earning too little"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Before consolidation, Nigeria had 89 banks, many of them tiny and undercapitalised, and the system was too weak to finance big development projects. The CBN therefore required banks to build a much larger capital base, mainly through mergers and acquisitions. The problem was too many weak banks, not too few (A), and banks had too little capital, not too much (D). Deposit insurance (B) did not stop."}},{"itemId":"4df5fb4f-670a-54dd-95e5-08c5808b718e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"478dc077-6882-52c9-8454-ff14fc33f72c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T081","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an argument AGAINST bank consolidation?","options":["Consolidation removes weak banks from the system","Bigger banks can achieve cost and revenue efficiency gains","Larger banks have better opportunities to diversify their risks","Very large banks can become complex and costly to manage, and may take on more risk"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Opponents of consolidation argue that economies of scale have limits: very big banks are harder and more expensive to manage, and they may increase risk-taking through higher leverage and off-balance-sheet business. Efficiency gains (B), getting rid of weak banks (A) and better diversification (C) are the arguments supporters give in favour of consolidation."}},{"itemId":"5f145520-09c5-589a-96ef-3fece90f7407","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3951b3a0-a875-5004-88c4-073500bcb93d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T082","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking, consolidation is best defined as:","options":["A reduction in the number of banks, with the banks that remain becoming larger and more concentrated","The closure of all foreign-owned banks in a country","An increase in the number of small banks in the economy","The conversion of commercial banks into microfinance banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Consolidation shrinks the number of banks while making the survivors bigger, usually through mergers, acquisitions and higher capital requirements. That is why Nigeria's 2004-2005 exercise reduced the number of banks sharply. Option C is the opposite, and D and B describe different policies altogether."}},{"itemId":"26db9b00-7969-5385-8f73-04ebe5c6996e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99bff0eb-048f-58d8-a640-d5a5c7d4d3f1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T082-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN's 2004 consolidation guidelines, which of the following could a bank NOT use to meet the new minimum capital requirement?","options":["An outright acquisition or takeover of another bank","A mere group arrangement, in which banks stay separate but are linked through common ownership","A merger with one or more other banks","Raising fresh capital from investors"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN accepted mergers and outright acquisitions as the legal modes of consolidation, and banks could also raise new capital on their own. A loose group arrangement was rejected because it would not create a single, stronger bank. That is why banks with subsidiaries or common owners were encouraged to merge fully. So C, A and D were all acceptable."}},{"itemId":"46ba320a-9fb3-544c-8a56-5cc1bebf8fa3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e652c052-a59d-559f-aa57-cd6e064d2889","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T083","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"By the end of the bank consolidation exercise in December 2005, the number of deposit money banks in Nigeria had fallen from 89 to about:","options":["50","75","25","12"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Mergers and acquisitions cut the number of banks from 89 to 25 bigger banks. The banks that failed to meet the N25 billion capital requirement had their licences revoked, and the 25 later became 24 after one more merger. Options A, B and D do not match the outcome of the exercise."}},{"itemId":"7967eab8-8156-5e41-9a7d-2b4737e4672d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b8eae2f4-27d8-58c2-a0b0-777f9723c1a3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T084","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The clearing system in Nigerian banking can best be defined as:","options":["The writing off of bad debts by a bank at the end of the year","The process of paying cash over the counter to a customer who presents his own bank's cheque","The transfer of a customer's account from one branch to another of the same bank","The process by which banks exchange cheques drawn on one another and settle the net amounts owed, through a clearing house run by or under the Central Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Clearing deals with cheques that are not in-house. A cheque paid into one bank but drawn on another has to be presented to the paying bank, so banks exchange such instruments at the clearing house and settle only the net balances through their accounts with the CBN. Cash paid on the bank's own cheque (B) and moving an account between branches of the same bank (C) happen inside one bank, and writing off bad debts (A) is an accounting matter."}},{"itemId":"e718ddbb-0d92-567c-bfe1-d86a0678ba0c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"89659703-19fc-58eb-9e21-195b232de14a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T084-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"At the end of a clearing session, how are the net amounts owed between Nigerian banks settled?","options":["By customers paying the differences directly to the other banks","By the NDIC paying the balances on behalf of the banks","By debiting or crediting the banks' settlement accounts with the Central Bank of Nigeria","By each bank sending cash in vans to every other bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Each bank keeps an account with the CBN. After cheques are exchanged, only the net position is worked out, and the CBN debits the banks that owe and credits the banks that are owed. Moving cash between banks (D) is exactly what clearing avoids. The NDIC (B) insures deposits and has no settlement role, and customers (A) have no part in inter-bank settlement."}},{"itemId":"d46c8b75-6065-55ce-8015-643f5662c843","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6d23805e-90e4-507a-ada8-07651d50a448","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T085","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the manual cheque clearing system used in Nigeria before automation, cheques were cleared by:","options":["Each customer taking his cheque to the paying bank in person","Posting all cheques to the Central Bank of Nigeria's head office in Abuja for payment","Scanning the cheques and sending their images electronically to a central computer","Representatives of the banks meeting physically at the clearing house to exchange cheques and work out their claims against one another"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"In manual clearing, bank clerks met at the clearing house, swapped the cheques by hand and calculated who owed whom, and settlement was then made on their accounts with the CBN. The process was slow, and customers could wait many days for value. Electronic presentation of cheque data (C) came only with automated clearing, while B and A do not describe how clearing worked."}},{"itemId":"f01d7182-6088-5204-a1ff-a12fa7803e08","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5313712-e41d-5378-84e7-ce11adeb3f13","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T086","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a major benefit of introducing automated cheque clearing in Nigeria?","options":["It abolished the use of cheques in Nigeria","It removed the need for banks to keep accounts with the Central Bank of Nigeria","It allowed banks to overdraw their accounts with the CBN without limit","It shortened the clearing cycle and reduced the clearing float, so customers got value for cheques faster"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Automated clearing replaced the slow, rowdy manual exchange with electronic processing of cheque data. Cheques cleared in fewer days, the float fell, and fraud and errors were easier to control. Cheques are still used (A), and banks still settle through accounts with the CBN (B). The new settlement rules were in fact designed to stop banks from overdrawing those accounts (C)."}},{"itemId":"d80fa56a-ab15-5006-a6d6-bf111397cfa0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4e33a324-93e9-5746-98b4-689af45a061c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T086-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigeria Automated Clearing System (NACS), which handles electronic cheque clearing, is operated by:","options":["The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)","The Nigeria Inter-Bank Settlement System (NIBSS)","The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"NIBSS is the inter-bank payments infrastructure company owned by the CBN and the banks, and automated cheque clearing was moved to it and run through NACS. The NDIC (D) insures deposits, SEC (B) regulates the capital market, and the CIBN (A) is the professional body for bankers, so none of them runs cheque clearing."}},{"itemId":"4c5d3448-d373-5c65-bf79-d10b09a7d969","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"05deb85a-e742-5fbb-9139-429a6c5d9bff","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T087-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In practice, which objective has become the primary focus of the Central Bank of Nigeria's monetary policy?","options":["Price stability (low and stable inflation)","Fixing the share prices of quoted companies","Financing every budget deficit of the Federal Government","Maximising the profits of deposit money banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Like most central banks, the CBN treats price stability as its main monetary policy goal, because low and stable inflation is an important condition for macroeconomic stability and sustainable growth. Bank profits (D) are the banks' own concern. Automatically financing deficits (C) would undermine price stability, and share prices (B) are set by the market."}},{"itemId":"b075fce7-b0cd-50f8-9f7b-9258b2e17e14","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50499307-3f6e-5383-8d4c-fc8187e299ab","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T088","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main intermediation role of deposit money banks in the economy is to:","options":["Mobilise savings from surplus units and channel them as loans to deficit units for productive investment","Collect taxes on behalf of the government","Regulate the activities of other banks","Print currency notes for use by the public"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial intermediation means standing between savers and borrowers: banks take deposits from people and firms with spare funds and lend to those who need funds to invest. Printing currency (D) and regulating banks (C) are central bank functions, and tax collection (B) belongs to the tax authorities, even though banks may sometimes collect revenue as agents."}},{"itemId":"a7a3780c-f3d5-5e2c-bbd6-b0960f8719e0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"41cd5767-1a64-5b62-ae53-99601af060f0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T088-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Deposit money banks can finance long-term investments even though many savers deposit money only for short periods. This role is best described as:","options":["Credit rationing","Monetary policy formulation","Window dressing","Bridging different maturity (capital lock-up) periods between savers and borrowers"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Because a bank pools deposits from many customers, it can rely on a stable core of funds and lend for longer periods than any single depositor is willing to commit. This is often called maturity transformation. Credit rationing (A) means restricting loans, window dressing (C) means making accounts look better than they are, and monetary policy (B) is the CBN's job."}},{"itemId":"e8adb340-a051-5227-b408-bad4bbf8a93e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"de81e428-20a2-54b7-aa27-8e25fda7625a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T089","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Bank of Industry (BOI) in Nigeria was formed by merging which institutions?","options":["The Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria and the Urban Development Bank","The People's Bank of Nigeria, the Nigerian Agricultural and Co-operative Bank and the Family Economic Advancement Programme","First Bank, Union Bank and United Bank for Africa","The Nigerian Industrial Development Bank (NIDB), the Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry (NBCI) and the National Economic Reconstruction Fund (NERFUND)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The BOI brought together the three main industrial finance bodies, NIDB, NBCI and NERFUND, to provide medium- and long-term finance to industry. Option B lists the institutions that formed the Bank of Agriculture. Option A names housing and infrastructure finance bodies, and C names commercial banks."}},{"itemId":"a0becc9b-ebd7-57e0-9d82-aec002ea8c88","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8d0bee45-18d4-5545-8132-d49672327849","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T089-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a specialised (development) bank in Nigeria rather than a deposit money bank?","options":["Nigerian Export-Import Bank (NEXIM)","Access Bank","Zenith Bank","Guaranty Trust Bank"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"NEXIM is a government-owned specialised institution set up to finance and promote exports, alongside development banks such as the Bank of Industry, the Bank of Agriculture and the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria. Zenith (C), GTBank (D) and Access (B) are commercial deposit money banks."}},{"itemId":"e24c6ab4-2a5c-534e-a64b-fa14d2fe7634","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"59a99651-499e-54e9-85d5-32e96a8df50b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T090","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before the Pension Reform Act of 2004, the civil service pension scheme in Nigeria was mainly:","options":["A scheme in which each worker kept his own retirement savings account","A scheme run by deposit money banks that guaranteed interest on contributions","A fully funded contributory scheme managed by pension fund administrators","An unfunded defined benefit scheme, with pensions paid out of annual government revenue"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Under the old scheme the government promised retirees a benefit fixed by formula, but it set no fund aside and paid pensions from each year's budget. This led to huge arrears. Options C and A describe the contributory pension scheme that replaced it, with individual retirement savings accounts managed by PFAs, and B describes no Nigerian pension scheme."}},{"itemId":"4ec9a7ed-e6ba-52e5-9d24-c71746a8e736","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b4449043-c79a-568a-8b26-0520270a94fd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T091","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under Nigeria's contributory pension scheme, the main role of the National Pension Commission (PenCom) is to:","options":["Pay salaries to all federal civil servants","Fix the interest rates charged by deposit money banks","Hold workers' pension contributions in its own vaults","Regulate and supervise pension administration, including licensing and overseeing pension fund administrators and custodians"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"PenCom is the regulator of the pension industry. It licenses, monitors and sets rules for pension fund administrators and pension fund custodians so that retirees' money is safe. The funds themselves are held by custodians, not by PenCom (C). Paying salaries (A) is the employer's job, and influencing bank interest rates (B) falls to the CBN."}},{"itemId":"649f1c95-3922-5650-a1e2-caa5bee78e83","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"01a7a5b8-7cea-535a-baef-1442d4206936","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T092","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a feature of Nigeria's contributory pension scheme?","options":["Each worker has an individual retirement savings account (RSA)","Both the employer and the employee contribute to the scheme","Pensions are paid out of the government's annual budget, with no fund set aside","The funds are privately managed by pension fund administrators and kept by pension fund custodians"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The contributory scheme is fully funded: contributions build up in each worker's RSA (A) and are invested, so benefits are not paid from yearly budgets. Payment from the annual budget was the weakness of the old scheme. Joint contributions (B) and private management by PFAs with custody by PFCs (D) are core features of the new scheme."}},{"itemId":"ede85404-2017-5157-9362-c358a5c6d388","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0f910fe-d668-5024-935b-15ba2abd6c1f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T092-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Pension Reform Act 2014, the minimum monthly contributions to an employee's retirement savings account are:","options":["10% of monthly emoluments by the employer and 8% by the employee","7.5% by the employer and 7.5% by the employee","15% by the employer only","8% by the employer and 10% by the employee"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The 2014 Act raised the minimum total contribution to 18% of monthly emoluments, made up of 10% from the employer and 8% from the employee. Option B was the arrangement under the 2004 Act, which the 2014 Act replaced. Option D reverses the two shares, and C wrongly leaves the employee out."}},{"itemId":"f55ae8f6-f585-57b1-950b-eb9884e63a67","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c2a3fbd0-dd15-5ad6-be9d-9844d592ead9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T093","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main purpose of establishing the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) was to:","options":["Replace the Central Bank of Nigeria as the regulator of banks","Guarantee that every bank will make a profit each year","Provide long-term loans to industries","Protect depositors, especially small ones, against loss when a bank fails, and so strengthen public confidence in banks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Deposit insurance means that if an insured bank fails, its depositors are paid up to a set limit. This protects small savers and helps prevent panics and runs on banks. The NDIC does not guarantee bank profits (B). It works alongside the CBN and does not replace it (A), and long-term industrial lending (C) is the work of development banks."}},{"itemId":"8274254a-fd5b-52a5-b247-fe9f2d439135","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0a377858-b84a-58d9-a105-a94face7b554","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T094","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the review announced by the NDIC in 2024, the maximum deposit insurance cover per depositor of a failed deposit money bank was raised to:","options":["N50,000","N5,000,000","N200,000","N500,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"In 2024 the NDIC raised the maximum cover for each depositor of a deposit money bank to N5 million, so that most depositors would be fully protected if their bank failed. N500,000 (D) was the limit set in 2010 and has been overtaken. N50,000 (A) was the original cover when the NDIC started, and N200,000 (C) was an earlier intermediate limit."}},{"itemId":"fc0fc642-8c36-52b3-b170-7dc0df772ac2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a1ff4d6-a880-52a8-9bdd-0d606d6e09a8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T094-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the NDIC began operations in 1989, the maximum amount guaranteed to each depositor of a failed insured bank was:","options":["N25,000","N500,000","N5,000,000","N50,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"At the start of the scheme, each depositor of a failed insured bank was guaranteed up to N50,000. The limit has been raised several times since then as the value of money and the size of deposits grew. N500,000 (B) was introduced in 2010 and N5,000,000 (C) in 2024, while N25,000 (A) was never the NDIC limit."}},{"itemId":"c2507736-5787-5039-9e3d-a43de2fcedda","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e0287273-a425-5976-b5f3-48b5bb6adfbd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T095","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the NDIC pays an insured depositor of a failed bank, the depositor's claim against the failed bank to the extent of that payment:","options":["Remains with the depositor, who can claim the same amount again","Passes to the Central Bank of Nigeria","Passes to the NDIC (subrogation), so the NDIC can recover it from the bank's assets","Is cancelled, and nobody can recover it"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Subrogation means that once the NDIC has paid a depositor, it steps into that depositor's shoes for the amount paid and can claim it in the liquidation of the failed bank. The claim is not wiped out (D), it does not go to the CBN (B), and the depositor cannot be paid twice for the same amount (A)."}},{"itemId":"3a06512b-43e1-521d-a200-a0254e9a5163","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a4a5febd-2d3d-595d-942a-a2f56cf55637","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T095-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation?","options":["Insuring the deposit liabilities of licensed banks","Acting as liquidator of failed insured banks","Issuing legal tender currency notes and coins","Giving financial or technical assistance to insured banks in difficulty, in the interest of depositors"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Issuing currency is a core function of the Central Bank of Nigeria. The NDIC insures deposits (A), helps troubled insured banks to protect depositors (D), supervises banks alongside the CBN, and winds up failed banks as liquidator (B)."}},{"itemId":"c6d76cce-8c41-5706-9aea-44eb191ed661","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eec17adb-c45c-5733-a88d-7800a30d5a2f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T096","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial inclusion can best be defined as:","options":["Merging small banks into a few large banks","Ensuring that all sections of society, especially low-income and vulnerable groups, have access to appropriate and affordable financial products and services","Requiring every bank to open branches outside Nigeria","Including all banks in the stock exchange's list of quoted companies"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial inclusion is about access: people, including the poor and those in rural areas, should be able to use regulated services such as savings accounts, payments, credit and insurance at a fair cost. Listing banks on the exchange (D), opening foreign branches (C) and consolidation (A) are unrelated policies."}},{"itemId":"c1c2a904-8d98-5be9-a65e-bc09418423db","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a3a3eb7c-872a-5c28-83ea-ca75bff746a7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T096-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following would most directly help to bring more financially excluded Nigerians into the formal financial system?","options":["Requiring collateral for every small savings account","Closing bank branches in rural areas","Expanding agent banking, mobile money and simple low-cost accounts in rural and underserved areas","Raising the minimum opening balance for savings accounts"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"People are excluded mainly because of distance, cost and complicated requirements, so bringing cheap, simple services closer to them through agents, mobile channels and easy accounts increases inclusion. Higher opening balances (D), closing rural branches (B) and demanding collateral (A) would all push more people out of the system."}},{"itemId":"7ae770da-ee20-5a7e-aa3c-d04ddbb40579","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"49007fec-66b9-5d81-95b3-9b64718b9e95","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T097","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes financial innovation from globalisation?","options":["Financial innovation is the creation of new financial products, services or processes, while globalisation is the growing interconnection of countries through flows of goods, services, finance, people and ideas","Both terms mean the same thing: the merger of banks across borders","Financial innovation refers only to printing new currency notes, while globalisation refers only to foreign aid","Financial innovation is the removal of trade barriers between countries, while globalisation is the creation of new bank products"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Business of Banking and the Financial System","explanation":"Financial innovation is about new things in finance, such as mobile and internet banking, POS payments and new instruments. Globalisation is the wider process of countries becoming linked and interdependent across borders. Option D swaps the two definitions, B wrongly treats them as the same, and C gives definitions that are far too narrow."}},{"itemId":"81b55a2c-d7b9-506f-be93-514554db3a72","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"511beaeb-1d27-5254-8287-cbf4622eaa09","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T098","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a role of the Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)?","options":["Upholding professional ethics and codes of conduct in the banking industry","Granting banking licences to new banks","Promoting the general advancement of banking practice","Promoting banking education and awarding professional qualifications to bankers"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The CIBN is the professional body for bankers. It trains and examines bankers, awards professional qualifications, enforces ethical standards and promotes good banking practice (D, A and C). Licensing banks is a regulatory function of the Central Bank of Nigeria, not of a professional institute."}},{"itemId":"40c33023-f802-5f58-b598-dcd81dc0e08b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"63f63505-8af3-53d7-8b12-da576929935f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T098-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How does the CIBN mainly promote professionalism in the Nigerian banking sector?","options":["Through rigorous professional examinations and training, codes of conduct, and recognition of distinguished members","By insuring the deposits of bank customers","By setting the monetary policy rate","By buying and selling treasury bills for banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria (CIBN)","explanation":"The CIBN builds professionalism by training bankers and examining them for professional qualifications, setting and enforcing ethical codes, and honouring outstanding contributions to the profession. Deposit insurance (B) is the NDIC's role, the monetary policy rate (C) is set by the CBN, and trading treasury bills (D) is money market business."}},{"itemId":"e92422c2-fadd-5a86-9a86-c34353ca9733","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b723e0ff-2d9f-5a95-ad89-ef72e7d7a966","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T100","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Basel I Capital Accord of 1988, banks were required to hold capital of at least:","options":["25% of their total deposits","50% of their loans and advances","8% of their risk-weighted assets","2% of their total liabilities"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Basel I linked a bank's capital to the riskiness of its assets. Each asset is given a risk weight, for example zero for claims on government and 100% for most corporate loans, and capital must be at least 8% of the resulting risk-weighted total. The other ratios, A, B and D, are not the Basel standard."}},{"itemId":"eabc6ebd-bc50-506c-8cd7-47b49fe20f4d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ab4138fe-c537-5a4c-8996-428bcb39ffe4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T100-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the three pillars of the Basel II Accord?","options":["Market discipline through effective public disclosure","Abolition of bank supervision","Deposit insurance for all depositors","Fixed interest rates on all loans"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Liability and Asset Portfolio Management","explanation":"Basel II rests on three pillars: minimum capital requirements, supervisory review of a bank's capital adequacy and risk assessment, and market discipline through disclosure, so that investors and depositors can judge the bank. Deposit insurance (C) and fixed loan rates (D) are not Basel pillars. Basel II strengthens supervision rather than abolishing it (B)."}},{"itemId":"ec6cb83b-febf-5c07-b542-998d721a4e0d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5bce049a-a46e-520c-a500-3d73147a56d7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T101","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one way the Central Bank of Nigeria has supported manpower development in the banking industry?","options":["Requiring all bankers to train abroad","Supporting the Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria and the Financial Institutions Training Centre, and running training programmes for staff","Banning the employment of graduates in banks","Taking over the recruitment of staff for every commercial bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN has backed banking manpower development by leading the Bankers' Committee, supporting its training institutions, the CIBN and the FITC, running its own training school and guiding banks through its examination reports. There was no rule forcing bankers to train abroad (A), no ban on graduates (C), and the CBN does not recruit staff for commercial banks (D)."}},{"itemId":"d81f109f-a3e3-54d2-9998-53416b7ba93a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8dfb146b-fa30-5c80-a5d5-13aed9d4a46c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T102","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a major advantage of the universal banking system practised in Nigeria between 2001 and 2010?","options":["It stopped banks from granting medium- and long-term loans","It removed the dichotomy between commercial and merchant banking and created a level playing field for banks","It reduced competition among banks","It made every bank offer all financial services whether it wanted to or not"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Universal banking allowed a bank to choose its own mix of money market, capital market and retail or wholesale services, so the old split between commercial and merchant banks disappeared and competition increased. It gave banks the option, not an obligation, to offer every service (D). It actually let commercial banks lend for longer terms (A), and it increased rather than reduced competition (C)."}},{"itemId":"b5987c2b-1386-5f08-ba49-448ceaee86cb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38888d99-7a59-5303-bf13-624cc3e9ea95","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T103","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the main difference between non-interest (Islamic) banking and conventional banking?","options":["Conventional banks share profits and losses with depositors, while non-interest banks pay fixed interest","Non-interest banking avoids paying or charging interest (riba) and works on profit-and-loss sharing, while conventional banking is based on interest","Non-interest banks do not accept any deposits from customers","Non-interest banks can only lend to the government"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Islamic banking follows the ban on riba (interest), so returns come from sharing the profits, and possibly losses, of the ventures financed, while conventional banks pay and charge agreed interest rates. Non-interest banks do accept deposits (C), option A reverses the two systems, and they lend to businesses and individuals, not only to government (D)."}},{"itemId":"f22e870f-ee2b-5205-b3e7-14c1de729efe","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0f198831-60f4-5ea1-b84f-445b60e8faa1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T103-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a non-interest bank, customers who keep investment (time) deposits are rewarded by:","options":["A share of the profit earned by the bank, according to a profit-sharing ratio agreed in advance","A guaranteed bonus equal to the Central Bank's monetary policy rate","A fixed rate of interest agreed at the time of deposit","Nothing at all, since all deposits earn no return"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Investment deposits in a non-interest bank work like a partnership: the depositor gets an agreed share of the profit from how the funds are used, so the return is not fixed beforehand. A fixed or rate-linked return (C, B) would be interest, which is not allowed. Option D applies only to demand deposits, which are repayable on demand but earn no return."}},{"itemId":"f5e0ab3e-e6a7-53d9-a44b-2f0f4739a3c8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7ff7d0cc-da9a-5cb4-9cc1-459bde0945a2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T104","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a major factor behind the stress in the Nigerian banking sector in 2009?","options":["A sharp rise in the prices of shares on the stock market","Heavy concentration of bank lending in the capital market (margin loans) and the oil and gas sector, combined with weak risk management and corporate governance","Banks holding too much capital and too little risk","Too little lending to the capital market"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"After the stock market crash and the global financial crisis, banks that had lent heavily against shares and to oil and gas businesses found many loans going bad, and weak governance and risk management made things worse. Option C is the opposite of the real problem. Share prices fell rather than rose (A), and over-lending to the capital market, not too little lending (D), was part of the trouble."}},{"itemId":"2153f968-6745-521c-ac48-e45918d81311","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"012794dc-3399-56ce-8201-f5fef8b6d47d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T106","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a key finding of the special examination of five Nigerian banks by the CBN and NDIC in 2009?","options":["The banks were net lenders of large surplus funds to the inter-bank market","The banks had too much capital and very few bad loans","The banks had excessively high levels of non-performing loans and were illiquid, depending heavily on CBN support and inter-bank borrowing","The banks' liquidity ratios were far above the regulatory minimum"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The examination found very high non-performing loans caused by poor credit risk management, and the five banks were persistent borrowers from the CBN and the inter-bank market, which was clear evidence of illiquidity. Their liquidity ratios were below the minimum, not above it (D). Options B and A describe healthy banks, not these ones."}},{"itemId":"116609a6-8406-56ec-9cbb-6fec7cbac1b5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"00085e44-6b95-52dd-afbf-0e69435f6a3a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T107","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Electronic banking can best be described as:","options":["The use of ledger cards and manual files to record customers' accounts","A system in which only the Central Bank of Nigeria may carry out transactions","The delivery of banking services through electronic channels such as cards, ATMs, POS terminals, electronic funds transfer, telephone and internet banking","Banking in which all transactions must be done over the counter with cash"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Electronic banking uses technology so that customers can pay, transfer, withdraw and check balances without handling cash or visiting the banking hall, moving the economy towards cashless payments. Over-the-counter cash (D) and manual ledger records (A) are traditional methods it replaces. E-banking is open to ordinary bank customers, not just the CBN (B)."}},{"itemId":"16d38c98-40fd-5ddb-8652-1788eb13ab4c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7b007935-52e0-5c0d-a035-01fabb4bb2e9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T108","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a problem that limits the use of cash as an efficient means of payment in Nigeria?","options":["Cash leaves an electronic record of every transaction","Carrying large sums of cash exposes people and businesses to robbery","Large amounts of cash must be carried because of the falling value of the naira","Banks spend heavily on staff and counting machines to handle cash"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Cash leaves no electronic trail, which is one reason electronic payments are preferred, so this statement is false and is not a problem of cash. The need to carry bulky sums (C), the risk of robbery (B) and the high cost of handling cash (D) are all real problems that pushed banks towards cards and electronic payments."}},{"itemId":"b4d40986-c273-50ce-a817-42b0d64675e0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e27ed4b-3a86-52cb-86f7-23eb134b6fd1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T109","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT an electronic banking service?","options":["Withdrawing cash over the counter with a withdrawal slip signed in the banking hall","Internet banking","Telephone banking","Electronic funds transfer"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Withdrawing cash with a paper slip at the counter is a traditional, manual service. Internet banking (B), electronic funds transfer (D) and telephone banking (C) all use electronic channels to deliver banking services, together with smart cards, ATMs and POS terminals."}},{"itemId":"ec8c9025-f379-58ef-8990-7596cba14fea","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ad9757ff-5aa8-5e2a-a4db-0c7222f4bc07","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T110","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following services is NOT normally provided through an automated teller machine (ATM)?","options":["Approving a new loan application","Transferring funds between accounts","Checking the current balance on an account","Withdrawing cash"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"An ATM is a self-service machine that does a cashier's routine work: cash withdrawals (D), balance enquiries (C), transfers (B), mini-statements and, on some machines, cash deposits. Approving a loan needs a credit officer's appraisal of the borrower, so an ATM cannot do it."}},{"itemId":"ad65ecc4-fe41-52e3-bc83-640ace4863a7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0990aedc-5815-505e-a605-1c568866c808","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T110-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"To carry out most transactions at an ATM, a bank customer must use a card together with:","options":["A letter of introduction from a referee","A cheque book","A specimen signature card","A secret personal identification number (PIN)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The PIN shows that the person using the card is the genuine cardholder, so it replaces the signature used in the banking hall. A specimen signature card (C) is checked against paper instruments by bank staff, a cheque book (B) is not used at ATMs, and a referee's letter (A) is needed only when opening an account."}},{"itemId":"72a72992-eb6b-5dc0-9ec5-a15970e48252","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"456a47d0-b9fc-5646-8eb7-1544db230abb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T111","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under Nigerian banking law (the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act), a person who wants to carry on banking business in Nigeria must apply in writing for a licence to:","options":["The Managing Director of the NDIC","The Director-General of the Securities and Exchange Commission","The Governor of the Central Bank of Nigeria","The Registrar-General of the Corporate Affairs Commission"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Banking licences are granted by the CBN. The application goes to the CBN Governor together with a feasibility report, the draft memorandum and articles, details of shareholders and directors, and the fee, and the proposed bank must also deposit the minimum paid-up capital. The NDIC (A) insures deposits and SEC (B) regulates the capital market. The CAC (D) only incorporates the company, which is needed but is not a banking licence."}},{"itemId":"4ab23391-9e76-5876-9f53-a87d1fb65e23","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7aff5ca4-6640-5ce2-b20e-87add7ede6e8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T111-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act, a licensed bank in Nigeria may open or close a branch only:","options":["Whenever its board decides, without informing any regulator","With the approval of its customers at a general meeting","With the prior written consent of the Central Bank of Nigeria","After notifying the NDIC one week later"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The law requires the CBN's prior written consent before a bank opens or closes any branch, inside or outside Nigeria, so the regulator can control how the banking network expands and contracts. Customers (B) have no say in this, informing the NDIC after the event (D) is not enough, and a board cannot act alone (A)."}},{"itemId":"2579e5db-045f-5ca8-957c-6b8fee13bda1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"35602eaa-03f8-52e6-8b80-7a0e12d343f2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T112","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the banking licensing regime introduced by the Central Bank of Nigeria in 2010, the minimum capital for a commercial bank with international authorisation was:","options":["N25 billion","N2 billion","N10 billion","N50 billion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"The 2010 regime ended universal banking and created regional, national and international commercial banks with minimum capital of N10 billion, N25 billion and N50 billion respectively, plus merchant banks at N15 billion. N25 billion (A) was for national banks, N10 billion (C) was for regional banks, and N2 billion (B) was the requirement before the 2004 consolidation."}},{"itemId":"8e887a0d-c7e4-57e2-94ca-ca7d096df1ee","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"12ae3227-82af-52e5-9331-772895113e0c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T113","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a reason why banks are heavily regulated by the monetary authorities?","options":["To guarantee that every bank earns a profit every year","To prevent bank failures and crises that could damage the whole economy","To promote monetary stability by influencing the cost, volume and availability of credit","To protect depositors and maintain public confidence in the banking system"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Central Bank of Nigeria","explanation":"Regulation is meant to keep banks safe and sound, not to guarantee their profits, which depend on how well each bank is managed. Protecting depositors and confidence (D), supporting monetary stability (C) and preventing crises (B) are all genuine reasons, because banks hold the public's money and sit at the centre of the payments system."}},{"itemId":"d7850692-8fd6-5310-ab5c-feb9af0e3021","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"52780693-45e9-5670-98e1-04eb8a97230b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T114","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first banking legislation in Nigeria was enacted mainly because of:","options":["The introduction of the naira as Nigeria's currency","The need to set up the Nigerian Stock Exchange","The widespread failure of indigenous banks that sprang up during the unregulated free banking era","The consolidation of banks to a minimum capital of N25 billion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Before any banking law existed, anyone could set up a bank, and many poorly capitalised and badly managed indigenous banks opened and then collapsed, so depositors lost their savings. The government therefore introduced licensing and minimum capital rules. The stock exchange (B), the naira (A) and the 2004 consolidation (D) all came much later."}},{"itemId":"a602bec4-036c-50b6-a2cc-039985fcac35","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1bb48ac5-4d29-589c-8df2-d5907f3f9a9a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T114-b","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The report of which commission led to the enactment of Nigeria's first banking law?","options":["The Paton Commission","The Okigbo Financial System Review Committee","The Barback Committee","The Loynes Commission"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"G. D. Paton was asked to study banking in Nigeria and recommend controls, and his report led to the first Banking Ordinance. The Loynes report (D) dealt with setting up a central bank, the Barback Committee (C) recommended a stock exchange, and the Okigbo Committee (B) reviewed the financial system in the 1970s."}},{"itemId":"44a39f41-4178-594b-8216-6aff5faefcf3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b345ec2d-5602-50c8-9218-1f79d0d08389","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T115","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A central bank digital currency (CBDC), such as the eNaira, is best defined as:","options":["A bank deposit held in a commercial bank's savings account","An electronic form of central bank money that can be used for payments and as a store of value, and that is legal tender","A foreign currency held by the Central Bank of Nigeria as part of its reserves","A privately issued cryptocurrency created by computer mining"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"A CBDC is money issued directly by the central bank in digital form. The eNaira is simply the naira in electronic form, carrying the same value and legal tender status as cash. Cryptocurrencies (D) are not issued by any central bank, a savings account (A) is a claim on a commercial bank, and external reserves (C) are foreign assets, not a domestic digital currency."}},{"itemId":"eca9dc38-c214-51ba-a3d3-73e3e84cf148","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"81335eb9-72b2-56b6-9b06-7e79596de566","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T116","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a central bank digital currency (CBDC) from a cryptocurrency?","options":["A CBDC is issued and backed by the central bank and is legal tender, while a cryptocurrency is not issued by a central bank, is not legal tender and is usually very volatile","A CBDC has highly volatile value, while cryptocurrencies are fixed in value by law","A cryptocurrency is issued by the central bank, while a CBDC is created by private users","Both are legal tender and have equally stable values"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The eNaira is state-backed: it is issued by the CBN, it is legal tender and it is worth the same as the cash naira, so its value is stable. Cryptocurrencies are created on decentralised networks, are not legal tender and swing widely in value. Option C reverses who issues each, D wrongly gives both the same status, and B reverses their volatility."}},{"itemId":"d162459c-8cc4-5e25-a188-f426dc16ee23","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0a5a31ba-2ddc-590c-a471-786ed94d14e0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T117","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a benefit expected from the introduction of a central bank digital currency such as the eNaira?","options":["Reducing the cost of printing and handling naira notes","Paying holders a high rate of interest on their digital currency balances","Accelerating financial inclusion by bringing more people into the formal financial system","Improving the effectiveness of monetary policy through better payment data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The eNaira was designed as a non-interest-bearing currency, just like cash, so earning interest is not one of its benefits. Wider financial inclusion (C), lower currency printing and handling costs (A) and better data for monetary policy (D) are among the benefits expected, along with safer and cheaper payments."}},{"itemId":"dfb20359-5080-5e1b-b9c1-035fe89a84fe","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f16dd9c5-d3bc-5d13-a53e-e10be9b92ca8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:F209-T118","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a feature of the eNaira as designed by the Central Bank of Nigeria?","options":["Its value floats freely against the cash naira","It is legal tender with the same value as the cash naira and complements, rather than replaces, physical cash","It can only be used by people who have a bank account","It replaces all naira notes and coins completely"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The eNaira is the digital form of the naira. One eNaira equals one naira, it is legal tender, and it was introduced to work alongside cash, not to abolish it (D). Its value does not float against cash (A). The wallet system also allows people without bank accounts to open a basic wallet with their National Identity Number, so C is wrong."}},{"itemId":"5bde88f3-dbb4-5ab5-980d-b4a0051c6d6c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d437f830-875b-54ea-a998-991dd621ef9a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a characteristic of the expatriate (colonial) banks that operated in Nigeria before independence?","options":["They accepted deposits from Africans but were reluctant to lend to them","They were registered, headquartered and controlled from London","They mainly financed the trade of British and other foreign firms","They built wide branch networks in rural areas to give credit to peasant farmers"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Colonial banks concentrated their offices in the commercial centres where British trading interests were strong, so rural credit to peasant farmers was never their focus. Options A, B and C are all genuine features: the banks were London-controlled, served foreign trade and colonial government business, and took African deposits while refusing most African loan requests."}},{"itemId":"469ebc6f-c4d4-59ff-a9e4-6f5d9b20b49b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0dbbab5b-67c6-5424-ba6f-422a6fdc5040","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In 1940 a Lagos produce trader keeps his savings with a foreign-owned bank, but his request for a modest loan is refused while a British trading firm with similar business easily obtains credit. This experience best illustrates","options":["the credit ceilings introduced under the Structural Adjustment Programme","the conservative cash reserve rules imposed by the Central Bank of Nigeria","the minimum capital requirement of the 1952 Banking Ordinance","the discriminatory lending practices of the expatriate banks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Expatriate banks were happy to take Nigerian deposits but favoured expatriate firms when granting loans, and this discrimination was a major reason Nigerians set up their own banks. Option B is wrong because the CBN did not exist in 1940, and options A and C refer to later laws and policies that came long after this period."}},{"itemId":"2ed0100d-7e30-5c15-bb08-d6fbafb2c7ef","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c06d47ae-6912-5aee-b931-44d4b2364bfd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which pair of factors best explains the emergence of indigenous banks in Nigeria during the colonial period?","options":["The deregulation of interest rates and the liberalisation of bank licensing","The expansion of African businessmen and the discriminatory lending of expatriate banks","The 2004 consolidation exercise and the adoption of universal banking","The establishment of the CBN and the introduction of deposit insurance"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Indigenous banks arose because a growing class of African businessmen needed finance that the expatriate banks were unwilling to give them. Option D describes institutions that came only in 1958-1988, while options A and C describe reforms of the SAP era and the 2000s."}},{"itemId":"afc93d6f-9d56-5b1f-b28d-f854029cf858","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"364ff1c1-8ac6-54b1-84e7-6cbda7ea3bdf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Because the colonial banks in Nigeria were registered and headquartered abroad, they fell mainly under the regulatory jurisdiction of","options":["Lagos","Accra","London","Johannesburg"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The colonial banks were registered, headquartered and controlled from London, so they had little need for regulation by the host territory. Lagos (option A) had no banking law at all until 1952, and Accra and Johannesburg (options B and D) were not the home base of these banks."}},{"itemId":"38124ae6-59bf-593f-88ad-03a007ebb0ba","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"60bca1e9-331e-59bd-9e63-371a04d949cc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which group of banks dominated Nigerian banking in the years just before independence?","options":["Guaranty Trust Bank, Zenith Bank and Access Bank","Bank of British West Africa, Barclays Bank DCO and the British and French Bank","Nigerian Industrial Development Bank, Federal Mortgage Bank and NACB","Spring Bank, Unity Bank and Skye Bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Three expatriate banks, the Bank of British West Africa (now First Bank), Barclays DCO (now Union Bank) and the British and French Bank (now UBA), controlled most of the banking business before independence. Option A lists banks set up only after deregulation, option C lists development banks of the post-independence era, and option D lists banks formed during the 2004-2005 consolidation."}},{"itemId":"c6133f26-68f1-5715-ad46-94cb488a4d3d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4cdd2bb4-39f8-538c-83a4-ea382bfbe30b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The colonial-era banks such as the Bank of British West Africa, Barclays DCO and the British and French Bank are often grouped together as Nigeria's","options":["third-generation banks","first-generation banks","non-interest banks","community banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"First-generation banks are the earliest banks, set up in the colonial period mainly to serve the colonial administration and foreign trade; today they survive as First Bank, Union Bank and UBA. Option A refers to much later banks of the liberalisation era, while community banks (option D) and non-interest banks (option C) are special categories created long after independence."}},{"itemId":"d9a40332-e06b-50c2-8871-96d1ac971ba1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90bd6c85-9e8f-54af-b456-2a8c2bd1c109","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the West African Currency Board was set up in 1912, the Bank of British West Africa served in Nigeria as the Board's","options":["agent for the custody and distribution of its currency","supervisor and examiner of indigenous banks","lender of last resort to other banks","deposit insurer for small savers"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Bank of British West Africa was appointed agent of the Currency Board, holding and distributing the currency the Board issued. Lender of last resort (option C) and bank supervision (option B) are central bank roles that came with the CBN in 1959, and deposit insurance (option D) only began with the NDIC in 1988."}},{"itemId":"916038c5-1c35-5e8d-a51a-c2a0045472f4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9221abb0-90da-5a58-94b0-c242d437d1c1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the West African Currency Board system, the West African pound circulating in Nigeria was","options":["issued by commercial banks and backed by their loans to traders","issued at a fixed rate against sterling and backed 100 per cent by sterling reserves","issued by the Central Bank of Nigeria and backed by gold held in Lagos","issued at a floating rate and backed mainly by Nigerian government bonds"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"A currency board issues money only against full reserves of an anchor currency, so the West African pound was tied to sterling at a fixed rate with 100 per cent sterling backing. Option D is wrong because the rate was fixed, not floating, and option C is wrong because the CBN did not exist yet. Option A wrongly treats commercial banks as currency issuers."}},{"itemId":"ea408be4-1777-5be2-9e60-5f46f1941c87","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"14504e11-fa56-5c0b-891d-3712c83ad9ee","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nigerian nationalists in the 1950s argued that the West African Currency Board arrangement had to give way to a central bank. Their main objection was that the Board","options":["left Nigeria with no room for discretionary monetary management suited to its own needs","insured bank deposits and so encouraged risky behaviour","issued too much currency and caused runaway inflation","lent heavily to indigenous banks and encouraged reckless banking"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"A currency board simply exchanges local money for sterling, so it cannot run an independent monetary policy; this lack of local monetary control was the core complaint. Option C is wrong because full sterling backing actually restricted money supply, and options B and D describe roles the Board never performed."}},{"itemId":"ec7066df-e72f-5733-a9b4-a2994b61315f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99131eb7-951a-51dd-b916-f17ff11c2235","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The period before 1952, when anyone could set up a bank in Nigeria without a licence, minimum capital or any form of regulation, is known as the era of","options":["consolidation","regulated banking","deregulation","free (laissez-faire) banking"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Free or laissez-faire banking describes the pre-1952 situation in which there was no banking law, so banks could open and fail without official control. The regulated era (option B) began with the 1952 Banking Ordinance, deregulation (option C) came with SAP in 1986, and consolidation (option A) refers to the 2004-2005 recapitalisation."}},{"itemId":"8dd4febd-8f50-5de4-a058-2cfc29d216e8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f3b75a57-a5c2-5d3b-a11d-02ba91511614","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The decade from 1945 to 1955, when many indigenous banks were registered and then most of them collapsed, is described in Nigerian banking history as the period of","options":["banking consolidation","banking indigenisation","banking boom and banking doom","universal banking"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Between 1945 and 1955 there was a rush to register indigenous banks (the boom), followed by a wave of failures that left only a few survivors (the doom). Consolidation (option A) is the 2004-2005 recapitalisation, universal banking (option D) dates from 2001, and indigenisation (option B) refers to the 1970s ownership decrees."}},{"itemId":"bca59a0a-87c5-501f-8b9e-05b4ed270b24","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba7ddee3-81e0-5e14-a473-be655a16d65e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In 1953 a small indigenous bank in Lagos with very little paid-up capital is forced to close because it cannot satisfy the requirements of the new banking law within the grace period. This bank is a victim of","options":["the minimum paid-up capital and licensing requirements of the 1952 Banking Ordinance","the N25 billion recapitalisation directive of the CBN","the cash reserve requirement set by the Central Bank of Nigeria","the indigenisation provisions of the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decree"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 1952 Ordinance required every bank to hold a licence and a minimum paid-up capital, and many weak indigenous banks wound up because they could not comply. Option D came in 1972, option B in 2004, and option C is wrong because the CBN did not begin operations until 1959."}},{"itemId":"16491e8d-01f4-5191-baa6-46b350632da6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9805ac3e-e165-591a-ab05-58f968a33009","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a cause of the collapse of many indigenous banks during the boom of the late 1940s and early 1950s?","options":["Margin lending to stockbrokers during a stock market bubble","Removal of their chief executives by the CBN","Failure to meet the N25 billion minimum capital base","Undercapitalisation and indiscriminate branch expansion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The early indigenous banks failed largely through malpractice: too little capital, rapid and unplanned branch opening, poor management, fraud and crude accounting. Option A describes the 2009 crisis, option C the 2004-2005 consolidation, and option B the CBN intervention of 2009."}},{"itemId":"03dcea52-ae3b-53d4-84ae-1f106093ed00","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a67dd4de-51f2-5370-bf02-c6061e5664b4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The colonial enquiry into banking practice whose report formed the basis of Nigeria's first banking law was headed by","options":["G. D. Paton","Pius Okigbo","J. B. Loynes","Roy Pentelow Fenton"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"G. D. Paton led the colonial enquiry into banking in Nigeria, and the Paton Report led directly to the Banking Ordinance of 1952. J. B. Loynes (option C) reported in 1957 on setting up a central bank, Pius Okigbo (option B) chaired the 1976 financial system review, and Roy Fenton (option D) was the first CBN Governor."}},{"itemId":"cf45aba3-b691-5d3e-97dd-ded6693daaaf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b8d5872-d215-5c6c-ab32-4a9c5ee23d2b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main task of the Paton commission set up by the colonial government was to","options":["design a new Nigerian currency to replace the West African pound","look into banking business in Nigeria and recommend the form and extent of control to be introduced","merge the surviving indigenous banks into one national bank","set up a deposit insurance fund for depositors of failed banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Following the boom and crisis in banking, Paton was appointed to study banking business in Nigeria and advise how far the government should control it, which led to the 1952 Ordinance. Options A, C and D describe tasks never given to the commission; currency issue and deposit insurance came much later with the CBN and NDIC."}},{"itemId":"67061493-1483-5d18-83f0-ecde843aaaa8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd2fba1d-d71c-58a6-9688-41a1ba74f31b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a provision of the Banking Ordinance of 1952?","options":["The keeping of reserve funds and the examination of banks","Compulsory insurance of all bank deposits by a deposit insurance corporation","A minimum paid-up capital for banks","The requirement to obtain a licence before carrying on banking business"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Deposit insurance did not exist in 1952; it came only when the NDIC was established in 1988. Options A, C and D were all part of the 1952 Ordinance, which introduced licensing, minimum capital, reserve funds and bank examinations to end the free banking era."}},{"itemId":"b40b315b-89ca-5446-b4e9-c122c133fd50","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a4072ecf-fd4f-5ead-a17e-1d36e2038e19","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Banking Ordinance of 1952, how did the minimum paid-up capital for banks incorporated in Nigeria compare with that for banks incorporated outside Nigeria?","options":["It was higher for banks incorporated in Nigeria than for those incorporated abroad","Only banks incorporated abroad had to meet a minimum capital","It was the same for all banks wherever incorporated","It was lower for banks incorporated in Nigeria than for those incorporated abroad"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 1952 Ordinance set a much smaller minimum capital for locally incorporated (mostly indigenous) banks than for foreign-incorporated banks, which had far more capital. Option A reverses the relationship, option C ignores the two separate levels, and option B is wrong because local banks also had a minimum to meet."}},{"itemId":"00e41ddb-9a11-5e48-a584-79ad40e7c62d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"854def45-c4c7-5737-a3ed-4e3124f66028","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The most important long-term significance of the Banking Ordinance of 1952 was that it","options":["nationalised the three big expatriate banks","allowed banks to combine commercial, merchant and insurance business","transferred currency issue from the Currency Board to the Central Bank","ended free banking and established the principle of government regulation of banks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"As Nigeria's first banking law, the 1952 Ordinance brought the free-for-all era to an end and set the precedent that banks must be licensed and supervised. Option C happened only with the CBN in 1959, option A wrongly describes the partial government stakes taken in the 1970s, and option B describes universal banking, introduced in 2001."}},{"itemId":"2a0e15c5-9aa8-5ea2-9a52-b6c0ba0354f0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25ff8b52-be79-590a-9c31-507917c142a5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank of Nigeria was established by the CBN Act of 1958 but began full operations on","options":["1 April 1960","1 July 1959","1 October 1960","1 January 1958"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN Act was passed in 1958 and was fully implemented on 1 July 1959, when the Bank came into full operation. Option C is Nigeria's independence day, option D comes before the Act was even passed, and option A falls in 1960, when the CBN issued its first treasury bills."}},{"itemId":"43b3a5b9-9f21-5ddd-8364-dfb8fb7d1f36","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93ac193b-4451-54b6-b409-63a45837e4ac","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The 1957 report whose recommendations led the colonial government to agree to set up a central bank for Nigeria is associated with","options":["Charles Soludo","J. B. Loynes","G. D. Paton","Pius Okigbo"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"J. B. Loynes produced the 1957 recommendations that led to the CBN Act of 1958. Paton (option C) reported on commercial banking before the 1952 Ordinance, Soludo (option A) was the CBN Governor who announced the 2004 consolidation, and Okigbo (option D) chaired the 1976 financial system review committee."}},{"itemId":"29cce8e8-fea7-5e3f-8351-fac5c515bbe2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"84dc2e6a-1de7-58fa-a12a-cbb81d197837","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first Governor of the Central Bank of Nigeria, a British banker who served from 1958 to 1963, was","options":["G. D. Paton","Roy Pentelow Fenton","Aliyu Mai-Bornu","J. B. Loynes"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Roy Pentelow Fenton was the first CBN Governor, serving from July 1958 to July 1963. Aliyu Mai-Bornu (option C) succeeded him as the first Nigerian Governor, while Paton (option A) and Loynes (option D) wrote reports that led to the 1952 Ordinance and the CBN respectively."}},{"itemId":"1015d8d3-558e-54e5-944c-79b11e93fbc3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e5cce8ad-7d1c-5353-9f9f-c67b56a06991","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In 1960 the newly established Central Bank of Nigeria issued its first short-term instruments for government borrowing, which also helped to start a local money market. These instruments were","options":["certificates of deposit","development stocks","treasury bills","bankers' acceptances"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Treasury bills are short-term government debt instruments, and the CBN issued the first ones in 1960 to finance government and build the money market. Development stocks (option B) are long-term government securities, while bankers' acceptances (option D) and certificates of deposit (option A) are created by banks, not issued by the central bank for government borrowing."}},{"itemId":"93bcb73d-150b-57f2-a108-42233df59fb9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d33cef4-66e3-53f3-9c84-80ccc20d8271","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Lagos Bankers' Clearing House, set up under the CBN in 1961, was mainly designed to","options":["settle cheques drawn on one bank and paid into another bank","issue Nigerian currency notes and coins to the banks","buy non-performing loans from distressed banks","insure the deposits of customers of banks that fail"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"A clearing house is where banks exchange and settle the cheques drawn on each other, so the 1961 Lagos clearing house made cheque collection between banks efficient. Option B is the CBN's currency-issue function, option D is the role of the NDIC from 1988, and option C is the job of AMCON, created in 2010."}},{"itemId":"6f032f4f-e4ab-5a86-8b12-39cd394d1d45","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d0ec002d-68b1-5d32-81c0-ccea29efd8cb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The greatest significance of the establishment of the Central Bank of Nigeria in 1958-1959 was that it","options":["gave Nigeria control over its own currency and monetary policy","introduced deposit insurance for Nigerian depositors","ended foreign participation in Nigerian banking","raised the minimum capital of banks to N25 billion"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"With its own central bank, Nigeria could issue its own currency and run monetary policy for local needs instead of relying on the sterling-based Currency Board. Option C is wrong because expatriate banks continued to dominate for years afterwards, option B came with the NDIC in 1988, and option D came with the 2004 consolidation."}},{"itemId":"9cefd107-1d0a-5b16-899e-2deb3d0dc636","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b057f0f9-5e7d-5f37-a5fd-143ee3582724","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which banking law was more comprehensive than the earlier ordinances, added to the CBN's control techniques and extended monetary control to most banking institutions other than commercial banks?","options":["The Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decree of 1972","The Banking Decree of 1969","The NDIC Decree of 1988","The Banking Ordinance of 1952"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Banking Decree of 1969 replaced the colonial-era ordinances and greatly strengthened the CBN's grip on the banking system, including institutions other than commercial banks. The 1952 Ordinance (option D) was a basic first law, the 1972 Decree (option A) dealt with indigenous ownership, and the 1988 Decree (option C) created deposit insurance."}},{"itemId":"69fda5a2-146b-51f7-8d5e-2e646b89d23e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bce980b2-22f3-5286-b336-5e06a22c5770","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Banking Decree of 1969, the minimum paid-up capital requirement for commercial banks was set at","options":["N25,000 for indigenous banks and N200,000 for expatriate banks","N1.5 million for indigenous banks and N600,000 for expatriate banks","N600,000 for indigenous banks and N1.5 million for expatriate banks","N25 billion for all banks regardless of ownership"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 1969 Decree raised minimum paid-up capital to N600,000 for indigenous banks and N1.5 million for expatriate banks, keeping a higher requirement for foreign banks. Option B reverses the two figures, option A gives figures far below the 1969 levels, and option D is the 2004 consolidation requirement."}},{"itemId":"0781dcfd-88e6-56e3-8b85-500c086d57a5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"66b8fe41-5a87-518f-956c-faf7c17798bd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Banking Decree of 1969 and all its amendments were repealed and replaced by","options":["the Banking Ordinance of 1958","the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decree of 1977","the Asset Management Corporation of Nigeria Act of 2010","the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act (Decree) No. 25 of 1991"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"During the SAP era, BOFIA No. 25 of 1991, together with the CBN Decree No. 24 of 1991, repealed the 1969 Banking Decree to cover the many new banks and other financial institutions. Option A came before 1969, option B concerned Nigerian ownership of enterprises, and option C set up a bad-loan agency in 2010."}},{"itemId":"0c8e1abd-e959-5e3f-8724-49471bda26e3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0161c3f-2137-58c3-9b47-9c70241cd7b3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A government policy of transferring the ownership and control of business enterprises, including banks, from foreigners to Nigerian nationals is called","options":["deregulation","indigenisation","privatisation","consolidation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Indigenisation means increasing Nigerian ownership and control of enterprises, and in banking it was carried out through the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decrees of the 1970s. Deregulation (option A) removes government controls, consolidation (option D) reduces the number of banks through mergers, and privatisation (option C) moves government-owned businesses into private hands."}},{"itemId":"009a9741-d2d4-5336-a31f-4a2a4c7003e4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a86dcde3-87c6-5628-8a88-5155be36f504","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the indigenisation programme that began with the Nigerian Enterprises Promotion Decree of 1972, the Federal Government first acquired an equity interest of","options":["60 per cent in the Central Bank of Nigeria","40 per cent in the three largest foreign banks","10 per cent in all indigenous banks","100 per cent in all foreign banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"In the first stage of indigenisation, the Federal Government took a 40 per cent stake in the three big expatriate banks, First Bank, Union Bank and UBA. Option D is wrong because the banks were never fully nationalised, option C confuses foreign and indigenous banks, and option A makes no sense because the CBN is wholly government-owned."}},{"itemId":"8948167d-f160-5137-b447-99361ee099e8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b88f2168-6891-5e7c-8fcc-ce8d339bf06a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Following the 1976-1977 amendments to the indigenisation laws, banking was placed in the category of businesses that had to be","options":["at least 60 per cent Nigerian-owned","wholly owned by state governments","wholly owned by the Central Bank of Nigeria","at least 40 per cent foreign-owned"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The amended indigenisation rules raised required Nigerian ownership of banks to 60 per cent, building on the earlier 40 per cent stake. Option D reverses the aim of the policy, while options B and C are wrong because private Nigerians and foreign partners kept shareholdings."}},{"itemId":"af4ee327-1d9c-5c23-8b25-67bafffa6bc9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90a52b21-0c38-5779-ad6a-7a0e4b36b82f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was an outcome of the indigenisation of banking in Nigeria in the 1970s?","options":["Foreign banks gained full ownership of all Nigerian banks","Banks were barred from lending to Nigerian businessmen","Nigerians moved into top management positions and directorships in the formerly expatriate banks","The West African Currency Board resumed issuing currency in Nigeria"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Indigenisation gave government and private Nigerians large stakes in the old expatriate banks, opening senior management and board positions to Nigerians. Option A is the opposite of what happened, option D is wrong because the Board had been replaced by the CBN in 1959, and option B contradicts the policy, which pushed banks to lend more to Nigerians."}},{"itemId":"2ccacc39-1f26-58a5-b3d5-066645310539","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c40471d4-f9a3-525d-9901-1d455436a3c5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the 1970s a Nigerian entrepreneur needs a 10-year loan to build a textile factory. Among the specialised institutions set up by government, the one created to give long-term finance for industrial projects was the","options":["Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation","Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria","Nigerian Industrial Development Bank","Nigerian Agricultural and Cooperative Bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Nigerian Industrial Development Bank was a development bank meant to provide medium- and long-term finance for industrial projects such as factories. The NACB (option D) financed agriculture and cooperatives, the Federal Mortgage Bank (option B) financed housing, and the NDIC (option A) insures deposits and does not lend to businesses."}},{"itemId":"1c4ceb5b-acf8-572b-809b-15b8bc780c8f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"39b74eb9-9ce6-5a94-99be-393722ec0b90","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly matches a Nigerian development finance institution with its main purpose?","options":["Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry - issuing the national currency","Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria - financing industrial machinery","Nigerian Agricultural and Cooperative Bank - financing agriculture and rural cooperatives","Nigerian Industrial Development Bank - insuring bank deposits"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The NACB was set up to finance agricultural development and rural cooperatives. Option B is wrong because the Federal Mortgage Bank provides long-term housing finance, option D is wrong because the NIDB financed industry, not deposit insurance, and option A is wrong because only the CBN issues currency."}},{"itemId":"dac36988-e3da-5411-89e7-c92f2b4fafb8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"509e98c5-9a84-5d37-ba41-b28a309298fe","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian Bank for Commerce and Industry was set up during the indigenisation era chiefly to","options":["lend to Nigerian businessmen, including for buying shares in foreign firms","supervise and license the community banks in Nigeria","manage Nigeria's external reserves on behalf of government","act as banker and fiscal agent to the Federal Government"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The NBCI was created to lend to indigenous businessmen, especially to help Nigerians acquire equity in foreign-owned firms as the indigenisation decrees required. Options C and D are central bank functions performed by the CBN, and option B wrongly gives it a supervisory role."}},{"itemId":"6a87380b-c954-5c5b-9647-468fade0fdf6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0d1c12db-daea-5c7f-a622-20871f77eb97","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Nigerian banking system of the 1970s and 1980s, merchant banks differed from commercial banks mainly in that merchant banks","options":["were owned exclusively by state governments and their agencies","issued currency notes on behalf of the Central Bank of Nigeria","were the only banks allowed to open current accounts for individuals","focused on wholesale, medium- to long-term corporate finance rather than retail deposits"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Merchant banks dealt mainly with large corporate customers, offering wholesale funds, medium- to long-term credit and services such as issuing houses and acceptances, while commercial banks took retail deposits and gave mostly short-term loans. Option C describes commercial banks, option B is a CBN function, and option A is wrong because merchant banks were often joint ventures with foreign and private investors."}},{"itemId":"c2e3909a-e0f9-59e4-9186-244d18098d0e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b8ce89fa-e701-500a-88b2-98ea997a2f12","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the usual division of Nigerian banking history, the period from 1986 to the mid-1990s is called the","options":["deregulation era of the Structural Adjustment Programme","free banking era of the colonial period","regulated banking era after independence","consolidation era of the Soludo reforms"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"From 1986 the Structural Adjustment Programme liberalised interest rates, licensing and foreign exchange, so the period is known as the deregulation era. Free banking (option B) was the pre-1952 period, regulated banking (option C) covers roughly 1960-1985, and consolidation (option D) refers to July 2004 to December 2005."}},{"itemId":"b93c0164-4135-5b87-8d9f-5d1dba1192da","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1eb17491-b40d-5b61-8cce-3dc1b24e3fc1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a banking-sector reform of the Structural Adjustment Programme era in Nigeria?","options":["Liberalisation of bank licensing","Deregulation of interest rates","Liberalisation of the foreign exchange market","Raising the minimum capital base of banks to N25 billion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The N25 billion capital base was a 2004 consolidation measure, nearly two decades after SAP began. Options A, B and C were all SAP-era reforms: interest rates were freed, it became easier to obtain a bank licence, and the foreign exchange market was liberalised through SFEM and later IFEM."}},{"itemId":"22a810c3-4303-57c6-ae90-219cd7c2a6a8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3666962a-b1c4-5cc7-aba1-ecef26570b9c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The foreign exchange market introduced in Nigeria in 1986 under SAP, in which the naira exchange rate was determined largely by market forces, was the","options":["Lagos Bankers' Clearing House","West African Currency Board","Second-tier Foreign Exchange Market (SFEM)","Expanded Discount Window (EDW)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"SFEM was the market-based foreign exchange window introduced under SAP and later developed into the Inter-Bank Foreign Exchange Market. The Expanded Discount Window (option D) was a CBN liquidity facility of 2009, the clearing house (option A) settles cheques, and the Currency Board (option B) was a colonial currency issuer."}},{"itemId":"2a4f9041-561f-52c7-84b5-faed9d923e55","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4340200b-8161-501b-8d6b-67a9c608023b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A situation in which a bank suffers severe financial, operational and managerial weakness that makes it unable to meet its obligations to customers, owners and the economy as and when due is known as","options":["bank distress","indigenisation","financial deregulation","bank consolidation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Bank distress describes a bank so weakened financially and managerially that it cannot meet its obligations as they fall due. Consolidation (option D) is the merging of banks into fewer and bigger ones, deregulation (option C) is the removal of controls, and indigenisation (option B) concerns ownership by Nigerians."}},{"itemId":"903d6027-c68d-5ef6-a50d-275bb1cdebdb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"500ce20e-248a-5791-9c30-40b2f62ff85f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nigerian regulators use the CAMEL rating system to determine whether a bank is distressed. Which of the following is NOT one of the CAMEL parameters?","options":["Asset quality","Capital adequacy","Liquidity","Market share"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"CAMEL stands for capital adequacy, asset quality, management competence, earnings strength and liquidity, so market share is not one of the parameters. Options A, B and C are all genuine CAMEL components."}},{"itemId":"283c69a4-a1e5-5110-ac74-14a47f42206e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"60d57f31-2410-568f-b1ff-c642755551ea","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a symptom that a bank is becoming distressed?","options":["Persistent adverse clearing position and overdraft with the CBN","Desperate borrowing at interest rates above the market rate","Being a regular net placer of surplus funds in the interbank market","Frequent boardroom squabbles among directors"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"A bank that regularly lends its surplus funds to other banks is liquid, which is a sign of health rather than distress. Options A, B and D are classic warning signs of distress, along with window dressing of accounts and customer complaints about unpaid obligations."}},{"itemId":"ccd938cb-fa45-52cf-99b8-652749205adb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"067d67c5-b3ae-5649-9494-583e5e9bd495","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The causes of bank distress in Nigeria are usually grouped into internal and external factors. Which of the following is an EXTERNAL cause?","options":["Fraudulent and corrupt practices of owners and managers","Poor asset and liability management","Weak management","Macroeconomic instability such as high inflation and recession"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"External causes arise from the environment outside the bank, such as recession, inflation and currency depreciation, which make borrowers unable to repay. Options A, B and C are internal causes because they come from the bank's own owners, managers and policies."}},{"itemId":"68869923-40ac-54bf-947d-d3149a08e80f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c13307ce-faa7-50e5-b129-56bdce6d08f4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation, established in 1988, had its origin in a 1983 CBN committee recommendation for the setting up of a","options":["depositors protection fund","bankers' clearing house","currency board","national development bank"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 1983 committee recommended a depositors protection fund, and this led to the NDIC, created by Decree No. 22 of 1988 to insure deposits and help resolve failing banks. A development bank (option D) lends for projects, a clearing house (option B) settles cheques, and a currency board (option C) issues currency."}},{"itemId":"fad35310-8749-5dee-867b-239767f7054e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7c52d220-b3ae-5e80-bae9-be8da0990462","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"After a bank fails in the mid-1990s, a special tribunal is used both to recover the unpaid loans owed to the failed bank and to try its former directors for banking malpractice. This reflects the purpose of the","options":["Failed Banks (Recovery of Debts) and Financial Malpractices in Banks Decree of 1994","Central Bank of Nigeria Act of 1958 establishing the CBN","Nigerian Enterprises Promotion (Indigenisation) Decree of 1972","Banking Ordinance of 1952 based on the Paton Report"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The Failed Banks Decree of 1994 provided for speedy recovery of debts owed to failed banks and speedy trial of bank directors and officials accused of financial malpractice. The 1952 Ordinance (option D) introduced licensing, the 1972 Decree (option C) dealt with indigenous ownership, and the 1958 Act (option B) set up the CBN."}},{"itemId":"50f10758-b017-51ee-a68b-895dc42fe684","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1b13d4af-5358-5360-86c4-4c9970ab0185","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In January 1998, during the distress of the 1990s, the Nigerian authorities liquidated the largest single batch of banks of that decade. How many banks were involved?","options":["26","5","14","89"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"In January 1998, 26 terminally distressed banks (a mix of commercial and merchant banks) lost their licences for failing to recapitalise. Option C, 14, is the number of banks liquidated after the 2004-2005 consolidation, option B, 5, is the number of banks whose chief executives were removed in 2009, and option D, 89, is the number of banks before consolidation."}},{"itemId":"4f238d99-ae33-5265-8de9-4243994e2970","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"36c4de86-0da2-5300-9996-05e14a47e7d7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The banking consolidation programme that raised the minimum capital base of Nigerian banks was announced at a special meeting of the Bankers' Committee on 6 July 2004 by CBN Governor","options":["Charles Soludo","Roy Pentelow Fenton","Sanusi Lamido Sanusi","Aliyu Mai-Bornu"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Professor Charles Soludo announced the consolidation reform in July 2004 in his address on consolidating the banking industry to meet development challenges. Sanusi Lamido Sanusi (option C) led the 2009 reforms, while Fenton (option B) and Mai-Bornu (option D) were the first two CBN Governors."}},{"itemId":"092387f5-c7f8-5e68-8bb1-f5e30ce79f0c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"542f3204-de07-5d40-89a0-c411c8443b14","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The reduction in the number of banks and other deposit-taking institutions, with a simultaneous increase in the size and concentration of the remaining entities, is known as","options":["universal banking","indigenisation","banking consolidation","banking distress"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Consolidation means fewer but bigger and more concentrated banks, achieved mainly through mergers and acquisitions. Distress (option D) is a weak bank failing to meet obligations, indigenisation (option B) concerns ownership by Nigerians, and universal banking (option A) concerns the range of business one bank may carry on."}},{"itemId":"97f21b2f-f7b2-5097-b376-53839f0abc8a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a025aa6d-0a75-5900-bf1d-b5c648a3bcaf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"During the 2004-2005 consolidation, a holding company that owned two small banks proposed to keep them as separate banks within a group and count their combined capital towards the N25 billion. Under the CBN guidelines this was","options":["acceptable, provided both banks were listed on the stock exchange","not acceptable, because banks with subsidiaries were to be liquidated","not acceptable, because only mergers and outright acquisitions were allowed","acceptable, because group arrangements were the preferred mode"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN guidelines allowed only mergers and outright acquisitions or takeovers, and stated that a mere group arrangement could not be used to meet the N25 billion; banks with common ownership were encouraged to merge. Options A and D wrongly treat a group arrangement as valid, and option B invents a liquidation rule that did not exist."}},{"itemId":"55804237-f9b4-5913-891a-f3a874c746bc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ed52e663-1b77-53d3-a42d-32a0597fbfdc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For the purpose of the 2004 consolidation guidelines, a bank's 'capital base' meant","options":["total loans and advances outstanding","paid-up capital and reserves unimpaired by losses","total deposits held by the bank","the market value of the bank's branches"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN defined capital base as paid-up capital plus reserves not impaired by losses, which is the owners' cushion against losses. Deposits (option C) are liabilities to customers, loans (option A) are assets financed largely by deposits, and branch values (option D) are only part of fixed assets."}},{"itemId":"b3f9d007-cbe5-5fd2-886c-54060808f9a7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0545376-7d5e-56a5-875c-49f004c1af75","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT among the incentives the CBN offered to banks that met the N25 billion capital base in the 2004-2005 consolidation?","options":["Permission to take public sector deposits","Prospects of managing part of Nigeria's external reserves","Exemption from CBN supervision and examination","Authorisation to deal in foreign exchange"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The CBN never offered to stop supervising consolidated banks; bigger banks still faced full regulation. Options A, B and D were genuine incentives announced to encourage banks to consolidate early."}},{"itemId":"a9e5345a-c1bd-5854-b180-30c1924335d1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2cf555e7-f5cf-52a4-abb6-fa9b438c95bb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"At the end of the 2004-2005 consolidation exercise, the number of banks in Nigeria fell","options":["from 25 to 14","from 119 to 89","from 40 to 24","from 89 to 25"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 89 banks that existed in 2004 emerged as 25 banking groups by the end of 2005 through mergers and acquisitions, while 14 banks that could not meet the requirement were liquidated. Option A confuses the number of survivors with the number liquidated, and options B and C give figures that do not describe the outcome of the exercise."}},{"itemId":"2840bf28-0fd1-5c85-b25f-2cc070a6f76b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5658004b-8757-5fb0-9f32-3e1eaf90f6f8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"During the 2004-2005 consolidation, the National Bank of Nigeria, one of the few indigenous banks that survived the colonial bank failures, merged with","options":["Wema Bank","Zenith Bank","United Bank for Africa","First Bank of Nigeria"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"In the consolidation, the National Bank of Nigeria combined with Wema Bank, itself the former Agbonmagbe Bank. UBA (option C) merged with Standard Trust Bank, First Bank (option D) merged with MBC International Bank, and Zenith Bank (option B) met the requirement on its own."}},{"itemId":"a0029633-cf24-5e45-9537-ff5600e2ab91","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9afc52a3-4214-5655-9faa-023d1c09ef64","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In 2009 the special examination that exposed serious problems in five Nigerian banks was carried out by a joint team of officials from","options":["the NDIC and the Corporate Affairs Commission","the Federal Ministry of Finance and the Nigerian Stock Exchange","the CBN and the Securities and Exchange Commission","the CBN and the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 2009 special examination was a joint exercise by CBN and NDIC examiners, the two bodies that share responsibility for bank supervision. The SEC (option C) and the Nigerian Stock Exchange (option B) regulate the capital market, and the Corporate Affairs Commission (option A) registers companies."}},{"itemId":"30397dac-d467-5201-8d25-94c4c0f705d4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0c6bcf52-58c8-5dad-8914-4f0694715fc3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In August 2009 the CBN removed the chief executives of five banks found to be in grave condition. Which of the following banks was NOT one of the five?","options":["Intercontinental Bank","Finbank","Oceanic Bank","Zenith Bank"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The five banks whose chief executives were removed in August 2009 were Afribank, Finbank, Intercontinental Bank, Oceanic Bank and Union Bank. Zenith Bank was certified fit after the examinations, while options A, B and C were among the five."}},{"itemId":"2a3c055a-fde3-5b19-b058-b6be1a52f667","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cecddf98-0c55-51f1-94cd-d6fc6a0b8a7c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before the 2009 intervention, some troubled Nigerian banks kept borrowing from the CBN's Expanded Discount Window against commercial paper and bankers' acceptances and were almost permanently unable to repay. This pattern was clear evidence that those banks were","options":["net placers of funds","illiquid","overcapitalised","excessively profitable"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"A bank that cannot meet maturing obligations without constant support from the CBN or the interbank market is illiquid, and the five banks' heavy reliance on the Expanded Discount Window showed this. Option C is wrong because the banks were in fact undercapitalised, option A is the opposite of their position, and option D conflicts with their huge bad loans."}},{"itemId":"40ecfd2b-ebd9-5c84-ac51-24bf6eb8d832","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6255141a-b034-5c37-b3d7-fda4d2f0697d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was a major cause of the problems found in Nigerian banks in 2009?","options":["Inability of most banks to raise their capital from N2 billion to N25 billion","Heavy exposure to margin loans and oil and gas, with weak risk management and governance","Excessive government ownership of banks under the indigenisation decrees","The abolition of the West African Currency Board and its sterling backing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 2009 examination found very high non-performing loans driven by margin lending, concentration in oil and gas, and weak risk management and governance. Option A describes the 2004-2005 consolidation challenge, option D refers to colonial history, and option C relates to the 1970s, not 2009."}},{"itemId":"41697f38-7ff6-5e45-82fc-929583b868c2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"887dfd9f-d53e-529f-a8a6-0e478571da1d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the second stage of the 2009 intervention, the CBN removed the managing directors and executive directors of three more banks found to be unhealthy. These banks were","options":["First Bank, UBA and Stanbic IBTC","Zenith Bank, Guaranty Trust Bank and Access Bank","Bank PHB, Equatorial Trust Bank and Spring Bank","Fidelity Bank, FCMB and Ecobank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"After auditing the remaining banks, the CBN found Bank PHB, Equatorial Trust Bank and Spring Bank unhealthy and sacked their top management. The banks in options A, B and D were among those the CBN certified fit or sound after the examinations."}},{"itemId":"f7b678c7-ad1a-529e-9850-53d89696edd0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc14fd7f-f6c7-5b11-aca9-629ed49e26b9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In 2010 a new government corporation was created to buy non-performing loans from troubled Nigerian banks, take over the collateral and help recapitalise the banks. This body is the","options":["Asset Management Corporation of Nigeria (AMCON)","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)","West African Currency Board (WACB)","Nigerian Industrial Development Bank (NIDB)"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"AMCON was established by the AMCON Act of 2010 to acquire bad loans from banks and manage their recovery, cleaning up bank balance sheets after the 2009 crisis. The NDIC (option B) insures deposits and liquidates failed banks, the NIDB (option D) was an industrial development bank, and the WACB (option C) was a colonial currency issuer."}},{"itemId":"bb8cdf57-d466-53e8-88cf-2b6501429f17","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ed8c431-630c-5523-be2d-c1ce24cdd2f9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A banking model, adopted in Nigeria in 2001, under which one bank licence allowed a bank to carry on commercial banking together with merchant (investment) banking and other financial services is called","options":["correspondent banking","branch banking","universal banking","unit banking"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"Universal banking lets a single institution combine commercial banking with investment banking and other financial business. Unit banking (option D) means a bank operates from a single office, branch banking (option B) means one bank runs many branches, and correspondent banking (option A) is one bank acting as agent for another."}},{"itemId":"1241ce29-1c73-55be-83aa-702b0727ad9c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d7f6824-f884-5840-96ee-6c591f35512c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When the CBN reviewed the universal banking model in 2010, banks were required to","options":["become wholly government-owned development banks funded by the CBN","divest non-core businesses or adopt a holding company structure, under commercial, merchant or specialised licences","combine insurance, banking and stockbroking under one universal licence","convert all their branches into microfinance banks owned by local communities"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 2010 reform ended the universal banking licence: banks had to divest non-banking subsidiaries or adopt a holding company structure, and licences were split into commercial, merchant and specialised categories. Options A and D were never required, and option C describes the very universal banking practice the CBN was ending."}},{"itemId":"5d0c86ed-599d-552a-874f-fcf87abc57f5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"db101e86-e266-57c6-b050-091334e15207","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the commercial banking licensing regulations introduced by the CBN in 2010, the minimum capital for commercial banks depended on their scope of operation. Which set of figures is correct?","options":["Regional N2 billion, national N10 billion, international N25 billion","Regional N5 billion, national N15 billion, international N25 billion","Regional N10 billion, national N25 billion, international N50 billion","Regional N25 billion, national N50 billion, international N100 billion"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"From 2010 a commercial bank needed N10 billion to operate regionally, N25 billion nationally and N50 billion internationally, replacing the single N25 billion minimum of 2004. Options A, B and D mix up these figures with other levels such as the old N2 billion minimum and the N15 billion merchant bank requirement."}},{"itemId":"92c5a7e8-e991-5b7b-97fc-f32bf975f073","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2c4aa887-a56a-5201-bd74-70b0f17c18f7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:HIS-062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How did the Banking Ordinance of 1952 differ from the Banking Decree of 1969?","options":["The 1952 Ordinance introduced universal banking, while the 1969 Decree abolished it","The 1952 Ordinance created the CBN, while the 1969 Decree created the NDIC","The 1952 Ordinance raised capital to N25 billion, while the 1969 Decree reduced it","The 1952 Ordinance ended free banking under colonial rule, while the 1969 Decree gave the CBN a much wider range of controls over the banking system"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Historical Development of Banking in Nigeria","explanation":"The 1952 Ordinance was Nigeria's first banking law, introducing licensing and minimum capital, while the 1969 Decree was a more comprehensive post-independence law that greatly widened the CBN's powers. Option B is wrong because the CBN came from the 1958 Act and the NDIC from a 1988 Decree, option A describes reforms of 2001 and 2010, and option C describes the 2004 consolidation."}},{"itemId":"5a67273d-bc62-5989-9cd7-14817c54294f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f7ab9ba4-3eef-5f58-8c87-c245f4487c33","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking, the extension of a credit facility to an intending borrower on the understanding that the principal and the interest on it will be repaid at an agreed future date is called","options":["Borrowing","Financial disintermediation","Bank lending","Deposit mobilisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Bank lending is the bank's act of extending credit to a borrower, who must repay the principal plus interest at an agreed future date. Option A describes the same transaction from the customer's side, the ability to obtain credit, not the bank's act of granting it. Options D and B concern gathering deposits and bypassing banks, not granting credit."}},{"itemId":"2eccfc17-e453-508e-8e50-09789195b0d9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e6ae4bb1-f9bb-599a-a881-f9e269db907b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The ability of a willing and creditworthy customer to obtain a credit facility from a bank or other loan provider, with a view to repaying the sum at an agreed future date, is best described as","options":["Borrowing","Discounting","Lending","Guaranteeing"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Borrowing is the customer's side of a credit transaction: a willing, creditworthy person obtains funds and undertakes to repay them later. Option C is the bank's act of extending the credit, while B is buying a bill before maturity and D is promising to answer for another person's debt."}},{"itemId":"a8a4abd7-45a2-568a-9cb8-957c05d79f03","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"daa535fa-8434-5518-9da6-9755ebf9151c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Lending bankers usually charge a higher rate of interest on a facility mainly in order to","options":["raise the bank's cash reserve ratio with the CBN","reduce the amount of security the borrower must provide","guarantee that depositors can withdraw at any time","compensate the bank for the risk that the borrower may default"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Every loan carries a risk that the principal or interest will not be repaid, so the interest charged includes compensation for that risk of default. Option A is a CBN reserve requirement and has nothing to do with the rate charged to a borrower. Option B is wrong because a higher rate does not replace security, and C is about the bank's liquidity, not loan pricing."}},{"itemId":"3d1328b6-bfcf-5e4b-b480-a167cc88de05","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4ad1cc0c-61bd-5032-86cc-191824b35228","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In bank lending, interest is best described as","options":["the difference between the market value of a security and the loan granted","the value of the asset pledged by the borrower as security for the loan","the portion of the loan that is repaid at the end of each period","the return paid by the borrower to the provider of funds, usually stated as a percentage of the principal"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Interest is the reward the borrower pays the lender for the use of the funds, normally quoted as a percentage of the principal per month or per annum. Option B describes security or collateral, C describes a principal instalment, and A describes the margin on a security."}},{"itemId":"2632a2cf-0480-51e8-a24e-b6256d7be015","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f2aebb2-3331-5d4b-834e-56ef20eb858a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A loan or advance granted by a bank is described as a risk asset because","options":["it is held as cash in the bank's vault","it must be backed by treasury bills","the recovery of all the credit extended to the customer is uncertain","it carries no interest until it is repaid"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Loans are called risk assets because the bank cannot be sure of recovering every naira lent, so the possibility of loss always exists. Option A describes cash, the most liquid asset, which is not a loan. Options B and D are not features of loans: advances need not be backed by treasury bills, and they earn interest while outstanding."}},{"itemId":"c41b1874-1970-506d-80c1-d9738865f626","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1c824d44-b504-555e-8302-7b299347af7c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the five Cs of bank lending?","options":["Character","Currency","Capital","Condition"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The five Cs of bank lending are character, capacity, capital, collateral and condition, so currency is the odd one out. Options A, C and D are all genuine members of the five Cs."}},{"itemId":"3196ca88-6686-5927-837e-d55e6fe2e00e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f5820765-7b5f-5b51-96e0-095d50dba47b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the five Cs of bank lending?","options":["Clearing","Capacity","Commission","Consortium"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Capacity is one of the five Cs, alongside character, capital, collateral and condition. Option A is the process of settling cheques between banks, C is a charge for a banking service and D is a group of lenders sharing a large loan, none of which is one of the five Cs."}},{"itemId":"d84dbc2d-8a1e-5b3b-92c1-082aa1c71f03","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"baa56893-d023-5fa7-8093-01a7a09cf8f1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Among the five Cs of lending, the one regarded as the first and the best security a borrower can offer a bank is","options":["Capital","Collateral","Character","Condition"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A borrower's character, meaning his integrity, reliability and past record with money, is regarded as the first and best security because an honest borrower repays willingly. Option B is the tempting answer, but collateral is treated as the last and least consideration, only a fallback. Options A and D concern the owner's stake and the state of the economy."}},{"itemId":"5b246754-3130-5ae9-9ba7-57d8b25d616b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d9cea6e7-f645-5e47-b46b-8284a7c13bb8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the five Cs of lending is described as the last and least consideration, a residual matter that is not crucial to the final lending decision?","options":["Collateral","Character","Capital","Capacity"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Collateral is only a cushion the bank falls back on if the borrower defaults, so it is the last and least factor in the decision to lend. Option B is the opposite, being the first and best security, while D and C are examined before the bank looks at the security."}},{"itemId":"c50acab4-1fe7-55c7-b868-948f655ba874","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3cada3f-1d60-52c3-8509-923b320c6c75","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"During an economic recession, a Lagos bank decides to cut back new loans to hotels and event centres even for customers with good records. The C of lending the bank is applying here is","options":["Condition","Collateral","Capital","Character"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Condition refers to the state of the economy and the business environment, such as a recession or high inflation, which can affect a borrower's ability to repay. Option D concerns the borrower's personal integrity, which is not in doubt here. Options C and B relate to the owner's stake and the security offered, not to the economic climate."}},{"itemId":"e888ca13-62a7-5a3d-b398-573eac28deb5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2ae212e0-8a9f-5e10-bfd4-0a38408ddd40","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before approving a N50 million loan for a cassava processing plant, a bank insists that the promoters must first put a substantial sum of their own money into the project. The bank is assessing the borrower's","options":["Condition","Capital","Character","Collateral"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Capital is the owners' own contribution to the project, and banks want it to be substantial so that the borrower has a real stake in success. Option C is about integrity and track record, A is about the economic environment, and D is about the asset pledged as a fallback."}},{"itemId":"e2c0cf90-f5c0-5544-812e-212f1fa61dc8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1e4a0684-695a-5625-a946-f3e729923686","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Lending bankers insist that a borrower contributes a reasonable proportion of a project's finance mainly because","options":["the borrower's contribution replaces the need for a status enquiry","the contribution makes the bank a shareholder in the borrower's business","a borrower with little of his own money at stake may not be committed, leaving the risk of failure mostly on the bank","the bank is not allowed to finance more than one stage of a project"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"When the bank finances almost everything, the borrower has little to lose and may not work hard to make the project succeed, so the risk falls mainly on the bank; the higher his own investment, the greater his determination. Option A is wrong because capital does not replace checking character, and B is wrong because a lender does not become an owner. Option D invents a rule that does not exist."}},{"itemId":"1f87b78c-10a4-5651-bb77-b9d57a20dfa2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"de887496-e548-5330-a9be-c5c5caa42f42","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank may sometimes grant an advance without demanding collateral. This is most likely where the borrower","options":["is a minor whose parents own the business","is highly creditworthy with a proven good track record","refuses to disclose the purpose of the loan","intends to use the funds for sports betting"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Because collateral is only a fallback, a bank can lend unsecured to a highly creditworthy customer with a good record. Option A is wrong because a minor cannot validly contract with a bank, D is a speculative purpose banks avoid, and C fails the basic check on purpose, so none of these would attract even a secured loan."}},{"itemId":"911898d0-c479-58f7-804a-c4e94bb2a8be","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2d9a7396-3233-5185-9651-8667a2b1d000","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The process by which a lending banker obtains background information on a prospective borrower, to find out whether he is prudent or prodigal with borrowed funds, is known as","options":["Land registry search","Status enquiry","Loan syndication","Account reconciliation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A status enquiry is the banker's investigation of a customer's background, integrity and conduct with money, often through references and reports from other banks. Option A is a search to verify title to land offered as security, C is the sharing of a large loan among several lenders, and D is matching account records."}},{"itemId":"da8096f2-046c-5936-a644-f5aa69b52d8e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"722bbcf6-6a3d-5b2c-9433-8e66f5a46ebf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the objectives banks seek to achieve when granting credit facilities?","options":["Speculation","Suitability","Liquidity","Profitability"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The objectives of bank lending are growth, profitability, safety, liquidity and suitability; speculation is something banks avoid when lending. Options D, C and B are genuine objectives of bank lending."}},{"itemId":"7eff2496-f7cf-5d5e-b32a-7b9831476788","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"de85274b-69fe-5d3a-8766-8fd71741a72b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The ability of a bank's loans, advances and investments to yield good returns to its shareholders is the lending objective of","options":["Profitability","Liquidity","Safety","Growth"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Profitability is about loans and investments earning good returns so that shareholders' funds are worthwhile. Option B is about having cash available for withdrawals, C is about protecting depositors' funds, and D is about loans contributing to the progress of the bank and the economy."}},{"itemId":"9566cb1d-d17c-550f-a2ae-fcfa535b3e70","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"978e5a64-beac-5f5c-9a86-dea24763714e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The ease with which a bank's assets can be quickly converted into cash to meet depositors' withdrawals and the requirements of the CBN describes the lending objective of","options":["Safety","Suitability","Liquidity","Profitability"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Liquidity is the availability of money, or the ease of turning assets into cash to meet withdrawals, new loan requests and CBN requirements. Option D is about earning returns, B is about loans fitting the bank's policy and regulations, and A is about protecting depositors' funds rather than having cash on hand."}},{"itemId":"883016b8-21b6-58b3-9d08-a253e30eb718","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"728bed2c-60ab-5654-8086-d267a7b771bf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The lending objective which requires that a bank should not commit all its loans to long-term projects, and that its lending must comply with the rules of the regulatory authorities, is","options":["Liquidity","Growth","Profitability","Suitability"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Suitability means loans must fit the bank's policy, the length of repayment period it can bear and the regulators' rules. Option B relates to the contribution of lending to progress, C to returns, and A to cash availability; liquidity is affected by long-term lending, but the objective stated here, covering bank policy and regulation, is suitability."}},{"itemId":"94025a1a-ea13-54d1-80a7-60a068c7642a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c06ede63-d4f6-5584-b6c9-230fef3ae245","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The lending objective concerned with safeguarding depositors' funds so that depositors can withdraw their money on demand without delay is","options":["Suitability","Growth","Profitability","Safety"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Safety is about protecting depositors' funds and the loans granted, so that the public keeps confidence in the bank. Option C focuses on returns to shareholders, B on the contribution of loans to progress, and A on matching loans to the bank's policy and regulations."}},{"itemId":"cbccaab3-9ea0-5a33-9265-9d87559e4c01","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"20e1dd90-94c3-5590-ab36-b20845061e84","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If a bank granted almost all of its deposits as loans and kept very little cash, the lending objective it would most directly fail to meet is","options":["Liquidity","Growth","Suitability","Profitability"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Lending out nearly all deposits leaves the bank unable to meet depositors' withdrawals and the CBN's liquidity requirements, which is a failure of liquidity. Option D would actually tend to rise in the short run because more funds earn interest. Options B and C are not the objective most directly hurt by running out of cash."}},{"itemId":"98550e00-ec6b-5158-9e5a-0ba4d3511d33","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"91d3c6df-088c-5b34-a813-800b5c04798a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank that kept all its deposits in the form of physical cash, granting no loans at all, would mainly sacrifice","options":["Safety","Profitability","Marketability","Liquidity"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Cash earns nothing, so a bank that holds all deposits as cash gives up the interest it would earn on loans and becomes unprofitable. Option D would be at its highest in this case, not sacrificed. Option A is not the main loss, and C is a quality of a security rather than a lending objective."}},{"itemId":"5dbd03e7-8b53-56a5-b813-1bb94aad4afa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fe9bb074-5d4f-5ebd-b7a4-7b5b828a758b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the classic three-principle view of bank lending (safety, suitability and profitability), which principle is described as being of paramount importance to the bank?","options":["Suitability","Safety","Profitability","Liquidity"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Safety is treated as paramount because the funds lent are mainly depositors' money, so the bank must be sure of the borrower's integrity and of where the repayment will come from. Options A and C are the other two principles but are not described as paramount, and D is not one of the three principles in this formulation."}},{"itemId":"3a7ec44c-dc6e-5509-8c46-bcfbb9573888","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a992ee4-93a1-50b4-8f9f-874142c49a0b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A customer with a strong repayment record asks his bank for a loan to stake on football betting. The bank declines because the purpose conflicts with sound lending policy. The lending principle applied is","options":["Capital","Liquidity","Profitability","Suitability"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Suitability requires that the purpose of an advance be acceptable and not conflict with government policy, and bankers are not expected to finance gambling, betting or other speculative activities. Option C could even favour the loan if the rate were high, B concerns the bank's cash position, and A is the borrower's own contribution."}},{"itemId":"39afeaf2-74fc-5bb6-a537-a3e45647f7f9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"24c55e55-9d71-56c3-8d5d-9d74b571535f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a lending officer asks a loan applicant 'What is the source of repayment?', the canon of lending being examined is","options":["Amount","Purpose","Repayment","Duration"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Asking how and from what funds the loan will be paid back tests the repayment plan, which must be convincing before a bank lends. Option A asks how much is needed, D asks for how long, and B asks what the money is for."}},{"itemId":"26c26527-de86-5a8d-b98f-1babb2eac878","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cd72fa5e-cad3-5e61-819e-3e7cb0d8387c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank that lends a customer less than his project genuinely needs runs the risk that","options":["the customer will repay the loan too early","the bank's liquidity ratio will become too high","the project may be abandoned uncompleted, so that repayment does not come","the security will automatically be released to the customer"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Underestimating a borrower's needs can force awkward 'topping up' decisions later or leave the project abandoned, in which case the loan may not be repaid. Option A is not a danger of lending too little, B confuses one small loan with the bank's overall ratios, and D has no basis because security is released only when the loan is repaid."}},{"itemId":"a5e63f25-343f-53dc-a93a-f4bcca67032d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8297f6d2-6120-5612-945f-bd91d8eb4fef","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Adekunle Plastics Ltd wants a short-term facility repayable within months to pay for a new factory building. A lending banker would be concerned mainly because","options":["the firm would be borrowing short and investing long in fixed assets that are not quickly saleable","a factory building is a current asset of the business","short-term facilities do not attract any interest","fixed assets can never be used as security for a bank loan"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Using short-term borrowing to buy fixed assets makes the firm 'borrow short and invest long'; since fixed assets cannot be sold quickly at a good price, the firm's financial position becomes riskier. Option D is false because land and buildings are common securities, C is false because short-term facilities do carry interest, and B is wrong because a building is a fixed asset."}},{"itemId":"9e180caf-a3ed-536a-b1bf-8ccd1682c631","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fe7bd3d-4d91-53ea-bdd1-c349c29cf6f8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nigerian commercial banks have traditionally preferred to grant short-term working capital finance rather than medium or long-term loans mainly because","options":["most of their deposits are short-term in nature","the CBN forbids them from lending for more than one year","short-term loans never require any security","long-term loans cannot earn any interest"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Since bank deposits are commonly short-term and withdrawable, banks prefer short-term working capital lending and often refer medium-term requests to merchant banks and development institutions such as the Bank of Industry. Option B overstates the position because there is no outright ban, C is false because short-term loans may be secured, and D is false because long-term loans do earn interest."}},{"itemId":"3ae978d2-3878-55cd-be38-088ac2a720c4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d2d9622-85b8-55fe-be39-600632fc5d91","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"To judge whether a company's proposed repayment plan is feasible, a lending banker would normally ask the company for","options":["its certificate of occupancy and a survey plan only","a list of its staff and their dates of employment","its share register and the minutes of its last annual general meeting","a cash budget, a projected profit and loss account and a projected balance sheet"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A business repays from future profits and cash flows, so the banker needs forward-looking statements such as a cash budget, projected profit and loss account and projected balance sheet. Options C and B show ownership and staffing, not ability to repay. Option A relates to land offered as security, which is a fallback rather than proof of repayment capacity."}},{"itemId":"b89cada0-bd93-5595-8835-9bc4c714c0bb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"845ac3fe-ec42-576e-ba72-80cc7d48806e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In loan appraisal, examining a firm's balance sheets and profit and loss accounts for about three years to see whether it can remain a going concern if its plans suffer a setback tests the","options":["duration of the loan","strength of the business","amount required","purpose of the loan"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Strength of the business asks whether the firm is financially strong enough to keep going if things go wrong, which is judged by analysing several years of accounts. Option D looks at what the money is for, A at how long it is needed and C at how much is needed."}},{"itemId":"602283ab-0dd9-51da-acfc-ea49eb881fc5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"62feaca6-8528-508e-8ed1-8757ff1182ab","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following loan purposes would a lending banker in Nigeria be LEAST willing to finance?","options":["An activity that is illegal under Nigerian law","Purchase of raw materials by a bakery","Stocking a registered provisions shop","Working capital for a licensed pharmacy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The purpose of a loan must be legal and within the CBN's regulatory lending framework, because the law punishes parties to illegal contracts. Options D, B and C are ordinary working capital needs of legitimate businesses, the kind of short-term lending banks favour."}},{"itemId":"2bcd6e62-9d4f-531e-b4df-e76580ad760f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c30c950f-d372-53a3-aecf-cbbabc344f4d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the basic questions a lending officer should ask on receiving a loan request?","options":["For how long is the money needed?","What security can the customer offer?","How many branches does the customer's bank have?","How much does the customer require?"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The canons of lending ask how much, why, for how long, where repayment will come from, how strong the business is, what security is offered and what the customer is like. The number of branches a bank has is irrelevant to the borrower's creditworthiness. Options D, A and B are genuine canon questions."}},{"itemId":"a19afa66-895b-545a-8b37-801424750015","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7533cb9e-821b-50b1-8485-9b2c4aa7f158","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An asset that a bank accepts from a borrower for a loan facility, to protect the bank should the borrower fail to honour his obligation, is called","options":["Security","Margin","Interest","Moratorium"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Security, or collateral, is the asset a lender holds as protection in case the borrower defaults, much like insurance against the unexpected. Option B is the excess of the security's value over the loan, C is the price of the loan, and D is a period during which no repayment is required."}},{"itemId":"ea4a2c4b-ed82-5f29-bd25-2e6fb5f5a55e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3ca4da51-b74b-5776-b742-558738669613","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An enforceable legal claim on collateral that has been pledged to obtain a loan, giving the lender the right to repossess the property if the borrower fails to repay, is known as a","options":["security interest","equity of redemption","letter of offer","moratorium"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A security interest is the lender's enforceable legal claim over pledged assets, allowing repossession on default. Option B is the borrower's right to recover mortgaged property on paying off the debt, D is a repayment holiday, and C is the document setting out the terms of a facility."}},{"itemId":"db601642-cf9b-5e65-9f72-4a79450563ea","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"54194018-2d3f-5e8d-930c-49d39725476d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under Nigeria's Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act, a manager or other officer of a bank may grant an advance, loan or credit facility to a person only if","options":["the facility has first been approved by the NDIC","it is authorised in accordance with the rules and regulations of the bank","the borrower is related to a director of the bank","the borrower has kept an account with the bank for at least five years"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The Act forbids bank officers from granting any advance, loan or credit facility unless it is authorised under the bank's own rules and regulations, including its rules on security. Option D invents a time requirement, A wrongly gives the deposit insurer an approval role, and C describes insider lending, which raises concern rather than providing authority."}},{"itemId":"3d3c02ea-2d33-5525-804c-6e52b0154d20","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c7370e89-ff36-5b98-be42-324202461a64","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Where security is required for an advance, sound lending practice is that the bank should","options":["allow the borrower to keep the title documents until the final instalment","take security only for loans to companies and never for individuals","disburse the funds first and take security only if the borrower defaults","obtain and perfect the security before disbursing the funds"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A banker must ensure that security is properly taken and perfected before any money is released, otherwise there may be nothing enforceable to fall back on. Option C reverses the correct order, A leaves the bank without control of the title, and B is false because individuals routinely provide securities such as land, shares and life policies."}},{"itemId":"8fd4c02b-e7b8-5832-9aed-be5a840d7de1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"28bed821-de1f-50e0-8f38-25ab098b2276","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"One reason banks demand security for credit facilities is that a customer who has deposited his property or title documents with the bank is more likely to","options":["avoid providing financial statements to the bank","withdraw his deposits from the bank quickly","demand a lower rate of interest than other borrowers","use the funds judiciously for the purpose for which they were granted"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Having his property at stake makes a borrower careful to use the money well on the agreed project, which is one of the reasons for demanding security. Options C, B and A are not results of giving security and do not explain why banks ask for it."}},{"itemId":"377f2e87-2c5b-5b2e-8649-1e1b73fba35c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6fc04351-9b81-5deb-ae38-aa0683f42146","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a quality of a good banking security?","options":["High maintenance cost","Undoubted title","Marketability","Stability of value"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A good security should be cheap to take and hold, so a high maintenance cost is a drawback rather than a quality. Options C, D and B are all genuine qualities: a ready market, a stable price and certain ownership."}},{"itemId":"109185f0-bbd9-58a2-b9b9-4f970ed81a3d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a9fd4e3b-c303-52c9-b0f6-4b53110cb5fe","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Bello offers her shop as security, but a search shows it is already charged to another bank for an earlier loan. The quality of good security that the shop lacks is","options":["Stability of value","Marketability","Non-encumbrance","Income generation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Non-encumbrance means the asset is free from prior charges and third-party claims; if it is already charged to another lender, the new bank would have only a secondary claim. Options B, A and D may all be satisfied by the shop, since it can be saleable, stable and rent-yielding while still carrying a prior charge."}},{"itemId":"a38894db-83aa-5270-bf23-e35e4d3c39d9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"427cd1ca-3282-5338-8738-51df3ee4838b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bankers are generally reluctant to accept luxury goods as security, compared with necessities, mainly because luxury goods","options":["can never be insured against loss","are always encumbered by prior charges","have prices that fluctuate widely with changes in fashion and taste","cannot legally be owned by individuals in Nigeria"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A good security should be stable in value; luxury goods swing widely in price as fashions and tastes change, whereas necessities are more stable in the short run. Options D, A and B are untrue generalisations about luxury goods."}},{"itemId":"aeabfe75-77d5-5489-9af7-eb5ef6f1c463","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e79cfab1-64bf-5fc3-9dd4-261c5a59253c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The quality of a good security which requires that it has a ready, broad market so the bank can sell it to recover the loan is","options":["Non-encumbrance","Marketability","Legal acceptability","Adequacy"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Marketability means the security can easily find buyers, because the bank takes it not to keep it but to sell it if the borrower defaults. Option D is about the value covering the loan, C is about the asset being lawful to own, and A is about it being free from prior charges."}},{"itemId":"b3a0b350-c6fd-58a0-a264-a12b79c573c0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"61ff499c-d25f-574f-98ab-51995c0d1398","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The requirement that the value of a security must be enough to cover the full amount of the loan, with a reasonable margin over it, is the quality of","options":["Liquidity","Marketability","Adequacy","Proper documentation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Adequacy means the security's value fully covers the bank's exposure and leaves a margin, so that a fall in value or costs of sale still leave the loan covered. Option A is about how quickly the asset turns into cash, B about having a ready market, and D about properly executed deeds and agreements."}},{"itemId":"4a18aa37-da7d-5c47-821b-b7f096ab1157","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"053f90a3-2ed2-522e-9c47-6917257be6b0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Two plots are offered to a bank as security: a vacant plot, and a block of flats let out to tenants who pay rent throughout the loan period. Other things being equal, the bank prefers the block of flats because of the security quality of","options":["Undoubted title","Non-encumbrance","Income generation","Legal acceptability"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"An asset that generates earnings while the loan is outstanding is a better security than one that does not, which is why rented flats are preferred. Options B, D and A could be equally satisfied by both plots, so they do not explain the preference."}},{"itemId":"b9478211-8b4f-5d73-804f-3195eb8eeaae","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"383d829a-2635-5661-920f-c516d68283ae","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Ojo offers his late father's house as security, but the property has not been transferred into his name and other family members dispute his claim to it. The quality of good security most clearly missing is","options":["Undoubted title (ownership)","Stability of value","Income generation","Marketability"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A good security must belong to the borrower beyond doubt, because a defective or disputed title can leave the bank unable to enforce or sell it. Options B, D and C relate to price, ease of sale and earnings, none of which cures a disputed ownership."}},{"itemId":"3f64acba-c880-5246-abd3-2486fd0ddb30","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57f4261d-f72f-5d5a-9a6e-e9b933534829","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank should not accept as security an asset that is illegal to own or possess mainly because","options":["the bank would face difficulty disposing of it and could face legal consequences","illegal assets cannot be valued by an estate surveyor","illegal assets always fall in value quickly","the borrower would be unable to pay interest on the loan"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Legal acceptability is a quality of good security: an asset that is unlawful to possess cannot be sold openly when the borrower defaults, and holding it may expose the bank itself to legal action. Options C, B and D do not state the real problem, which is unenforceability and legal risk."}},{"itemId":"6c605f2c-16e8-54b9-bf4a-b5b6872630c8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c4f0aeee-b80e-58f8-b894-37000bdde566","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a personal security?","options":["A pledge of goods in a warehouse","A guarantee given by a third party","A legal mortgage over land","A fixed charge over a machine"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Personal security is a promise by a person, such as a guarantee, rather than a charge over a specific tangible asset. Options C, A and D are non-personal securities because each gives the bank rights over identifiable property."}},{"itemId":"8a30c463-c0de-5bff-8e44-cb90b78ecc76","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8fc4a3b9-a344-5e16-9caf-3b8e96d711e4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Non-personal (tangible) security is generally regarded as safer for a bank than personal security because","options":["it is always more liquid than a cash deposit","a tangible property can be sold in the market to recover the unpaid amount if the borrower defaults","it removes the need to assess the borrower's character","it never needs to be valued before the loan is granted"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"With tangible property the bank can realise the asset and recover what is owed, whereas a personal guarantee depends on the guarantor's willingness and means to pay. Option D is false because tangible securities must be valued, C is false because character remains the first consideration, and A is false because nothing is more liquid than cash."}},{"itemId":"28e3fe60-0adc-56f6-bcc7-17f75a5d7d2f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a95dc126-8a4f-5815-b661-bf520fcead78","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Compared with freehold land, leasehold land offered as security has the disadvantage that","options":["its value falls as the unexpired years of the lease reduce","it lasts indefinitely and cannot be sold","it can never be mortgaged to a bank","it gives the holder absolute title to the land"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A leasehold title runs only until the lease expires, after which possession reverts to the freeholder, so its value shrinks as the remaining term gets shorter. Option C is false since leaseholds are commonly mortgaged, while B and D describe freehold rather than leasehold."}},{"itemId":"979e57a0-6d50-593a-8a29-31d0b297695b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df726946-552b-55fa-9faf-9a78ee34f99a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A form of security created by contract which gives a lender an interest in a borrower's property until a given sum of money is repaid, usually created by a written agreement registered at the land registry, is a","options":["Hypothecation","Lien","Guarantee","Mortgage"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A mortgage gives the lender an interest in property, usually land and buildings, as security for repayment, and it is created by a written agreement registered with the land authority. Option B is a right to retain goods already in the creditor's possession, A is a charge over goods that stay with the borrower, and C is a promise by a third party."}},{"itemId":"6b731447-60a7-50f2-a96a-2f627796b750","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bd61742b-af10-528d-b69b-1005cec16575","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Adewale deposits the title deeds of his house with his bank as security, but the legal estate is not transferred to the bank and he continues to live in the house. This arrangement is","options":["a legal mortgage","a pledge","a continuing guarantee","an equitable mortgage"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"In an equitable mortgage the lender holds the title deeds while the property remains with the mortgagor, and no legal estate passes to the lender. Option A would require a deed transferring legal title to the bank, B applies to movable goods delivered to the lender, and C is a promise by a third party."}},{"itemId":"4080b274-fc8d-575f-b9f0-c5a843eb08f2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"913e4e04-55ca-5437-b9d0-031b757d41ef","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The essential feature of a legal mortgage is that it","options":["gives the lender possession of movable goods only","vests a legal estate in the mortgagee and must be created by a mortgage deed","is an oral promise by a third party to pay the debt","leaves the legal estate with the borrower and is created by depositing title deeds"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A legal mortgage transfers legal title in the property to the lender, together with a right of possession, and must be executed under seal as a mortgage deed. Option D describes an equitable mortgage, A describes a pledge and C describes a guarantee, which in any case is normally in writing."}},{"itemId":"cbc309b4-0503-58b9-9157-644553f6bb4a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd111f63-c770-5c1a-963e-12e1261b9765","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a mortgage transaction where a bank lends to a customer on the security of his house, the customer and the bank are respectively called the","options":["mortgagee and the mortgagor","mortgagor and the mortgagee","guarantor and the principal debtor","pledgee and the pledgor"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The borrower who gives the mortgage over his property is the mortgagor, and the lender who receives it is the mortgagee. Option A reverses the two terms, C names parties to a guarantee, and D uses pledge terms and also reverses them, since the borrower would be the pledgor."}},{"itemId":"0dcd8195-db46-573c-9590-386012901127","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9860429b-8aeb-5b26-91fa-ea5c9e05d9da","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before taking a legal mortgage over a plot of land offered as security, a Nigerian bank conducts a search at the land registry (ministry of lands) mainly to","options":["register the borrower as a customer of the bank","determine the rate of interest to charge on the loan","verify the authenticity of the title document and the borrower's ownership","obtain the borrower's statement of affairs"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The search confirms that the deed or certificate of occupancy is genuine and that the land belongs to the customer, after which a physical inspection checks that the property matches the description. Options B, A and D are not done at the land registry and have nothing to do with confirming title."}},{"itemId":"8e07dd29-75e2-57a2-ba02-be6547885696","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"62c1b1d5-fc42-519b-abd4-4e2474ed0a6a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A promise by one person to answer for the present or future debt of another person, made to the person to whom that other is or will become liable, is a","options":["Hypothecation","Guarantee","Lien","Pledge"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A guarantee is a promise by a guarantor (surety) to answer for the debt of the principal debtor to the creditor. Options C, D and A are all rights over goods or property, not promises by a third person to pay another's debt."}},{"itemId":"6590e65d-4cd7-5ef1-a330-c5588e363588","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fe7371ad-1e17-5214-bcbf-207129a21d97","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a bank guarantee securing a customer's loan, the borrower is referred to as the","options":["creditor","surety","guarantor","principal debtor"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The three parties to a guarantee are the guarantor (or surety), the creditor (the bank) and the principal debtor (the borrower). Options C and B are two names for the same person who gives the promise, and A is the bank."}},{"itemId":"235ea54b-7ddf-5792-a635-23cadc26f1fd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"79276eef-5c5e-52d6-b8c6-a148682fe558","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chief Okoro guarantees his son's overdraft, which rises and falls daily, and the guarantee is to cover not only the present debit balance but any future balance on the account. This is a","options":["legal mortgage","continuing guarantee","letter of hypothecation","specific guarantee"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A continuing guarantee covers a series of transactions, such as a fluctuating overdraft, including balances arising in future. Option D covers a single debt only, such as one term loan, while A and C are charges over property and goods rather than guarantees."}},{"itemId":"84bc45a6-27a8-5b29-a8ff-9f90fe880e1e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ef980511-d44d-55f1-b7d0-d807d5f78bdc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main weakness of a guarantee as a banking security is that","options":["it is very difficult and costly to take","it requires transfer of the legal estate in land","its value depends wholly on the financial standing of the guarantor","it can only be used for loans to limited companies"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A guarantee is only as good as the guarantor's ability and willingness to pay, and enforcing it may require costly legal action. Option A is the opposite of the truth, since guarantees are easy to take by simply signing forms. Option D is false because individuals' debts are often guaranteed, and B describes a legal mortgage."}},{"itemId":"4075f91f-30f8-57cb-954e-32a658451a34","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"04abbaac-a75f-5954-9ca0-5a9c14286c83","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A banker's lien differs from an ordinary (particular) lien in that","options":["the banker may sell the goods after giving proper notice, while an ordinary lienee may only retain them","the banker may only hold goods that the customer has hypothecated","the banker must transfer ownership of the goods to himself first","an ordinary lien gives the creditor ownership of the goods"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A lien is a right to hold the borrower's goods until the debt is paid; under an ordinary lien the creditor can only keep possession, but a banker's lien allows sale after proper notice. Option C is wrong because the customer remains the owner, B confuses lien with hypothecation, and D is wrong because no lien gives ownership."}},{"itemId":"1709052c-72cd-5f1a-b910-a35d99fd17c2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"251775a1-025d-5ce8-84d3-a48c47605acb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A trader borrows from his bank against bags of rice stored in a warehouse. The bank puts its own padlock on the warehouse door and keeps the key, though the rice is never moved. This arrangement is","options":["a pledge, with constructive delivery of the goods","a hypothecation, since the goods were not moved","a specific guarantee of the trader's debt","an equitable mortgage of the warehouse"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A pledge is a bailment of goods as security, and delivery can be constructive, for example when the bank puts its lock on the store or takes the key, so possession passes even though the goods stay where they are. Option B is wrong because in hypothecation possession stays with the borrower, while D and C concern land and third-party promises."}},{"itemId":"ddb5f2b8-8156-5733-8c8e-fc4bd6fed06a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1bfbef46-0b6c-50a5-804e-eaa1c0f6cd31","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A charge over goods under which both ownership and possession remain with the borrower, while an equitable charge is created in favour of the bank, is known as","options":["Pledge","Lien","Hypothecation","Legal mortgage"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Under hypothecation the borrower keeps the goods and remains the owner, and the bank has an equitable charge plus a right to call for possession when needed. Options A and B both involve the bank holding the goods, and D transfers legal title in land to the lender."}},{"itemId":"3f5afb88-82d9-5484-a86f-21867c46aac1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b7f3db8b-d7f5-5635-8970-6ed1bbaf1915","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Advances against hypothecated goods are considered riskier for a bank than advances against pledged goods because under hypothecation","options":["the goods must be sold before the loan is granted","the bank becomes the legal owner of the goods","no written document is ever signed","the goods remain in the borrower's possession"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"In a pledge the bank holds the goods, but in hypothecation the borrower keeps them, so he could sell or remove them; this is why the bank must check the stock regularly and ask for periodic reports. Option B is false for both arrangements, C is false because a letter or deed of hypothecation is signed, and A makes no sense."}},{"itemId":"565b7326-33dd-5618-a90b-aaf0d3d929d3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1a61842e-4d71-566e-8d34-aa67da55db95","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under which of the following does the bank obtain possession of the borrower's goods while the borrower remains their owner?","options":["Pledge","Personal guarantee","Hypothecation","Floating charge"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A pledge involves delivering the goods, actually or constructively, to the bank as bailee, while ownership stays with the borrower. Option C leaves both ownership and possession with the borrower, B involves no goods at all, and D lets the company keep and trade its assets until crystallisation."}},{"itemId":"9eedee95-00b8-5b9b-9015-d009750118f0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a2015912-d964-5d52-9a58-d6d95860e892","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank takes a charge over all of a manufacturing company's stock-in-trade and receivables, but the company is free to buy and sell these items in the normal course of business until it defaults. This is a","options":["pledge","floating charge","legal mortgage","fixed charge"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A floating charge covers a class of changing assets, such as stock and receivables, which the company may deal with until the charge crystallises, usually on default or liquidation. Option D attaches to specific identified assets that cannot be disposed of without the lender's consent, C concerns land, and A requires the goods to be delivered to the bank."}},{"itemId":"cdab8e55-542f-5a23-8790-92aa9adceac0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c440646b-dc35-5043-afb9-c134bb3a5a6d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a life assurance policy is offered as security, the bank completes a deed of assignment and must then","options":["give notice of the assignment to the insurance company","obtain a certificate of occupancy for the policy","transfer the policy at the stock exchange","register the policy at the land registry"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Taking a life policy involves checking the insurer's soundness, the beneficiary clause, the surrender value and premium payments, completing a deed of assignment, and giving notice to the insurer, who acknowledges it. Option C applies to shares, while D and B apply to land."}},{"itemId":"f8e3ea72-a2fd-598d-9d72-040b6a6bac6c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cdd404ff-c5cb-5e5b-8817-aa653e4c01e7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a customer offers quoted shares as security for a loan, the document he signs giving the bank rights over the shares, including the right of disposal on default, is a","options":["memorandum of deposit","certificate of occupancy","letter of hypothecation","deed of assignment of a life policy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"With stocks and shares, the customer signs a memorandum of deposit which gives the bank rights over the shares, including the right to sell them if he defaults. Option B is a title document for land, C is used for goods kept by the borrower, and D is used for life policies."}},{"itemId":"a8a5f5d4-4d28-5730-b245-ec19bc8d986b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e322817c-76f0-5654-828d-9cb4102c2e70","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-065","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A customer wishes to use his savings account balance as cash security for a loan. Which of the following would he NOT be required to do?","options":["Issue a letter of authority to transfer money from the account","Deposit the certificate of occupancy of his house","Surrender his savings passbook to the bank","Sign a blank withdrawal form without date and amount"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"To pledge a savings balance, the customer gives a letter of authority, surrenders the passbook and signs an undated blank withdrawal form, so the bank can apply the money to the loan. A certificate of occupancy is a land title document and is not part of a cash security. Options A, C and D are all genuine steps."}},{"itemId":"d80f6dc1-c3b4-504a-90c3-5abad775339c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"28841944-9c33-5596-bd68-3ae691adf37b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-066","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following documents would a bank require from a corporate loan applicant but NOT from an individual applicant?","options":["Acceptable security","Evidence of a current account with the bank","An application for the loan","Certificate of incorporation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A certificate of incorporation proves that a company legally exists, so it is required only from corporate customers, along with the memorandum and articles of association and audited accounts. Options C, B and A are required from both individual and corporate applicants."}},{"itemId":"88023f5a-c69f-50c0-8acb-15e4d0446f8f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"37ad5f3d-ae03-5d89-af3a-cbebd7f58816","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-067","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A term loan agreement states that the borrowing company must not create any further charge over its fixed or current assets while the loan is outstanding. This condition is known as","options":["prohibition of further charges","rendering of financial returns","provision of insurance cover","restriction of benefits to shareholders"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Prohibiting further charges stops other lenders from taking security over the borrower's assets, which protects the lending bank's position. Option D limits dividends and directors' drawings, B requires regular financial statements, and C requires the mortgaged property to be insured."}},{"itemId":"18730edb-19cb-597b-9299-003235a79967","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"34640d84-3ac6-59de-9a1e-de574043ec33","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-068","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a usual condition attached to a bank loan agreement?","options":["Dividends may be restricted until loan repayments are met","The bank must receive a share of the borrower's annual profits","The mortgaged property must be insured and kept in good repair","Major changes in management must be approved by the bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Usual loan conditions include prohibition of further charges, approval of management changes, regular financial returns, restriction of benefits to shareholders and insurance cover; a lender earns interest, not a share of profits. Options D, C and A are genuine loan conditions."}},{"itemId":"ccdb1722-29cd-5333-8354-8ad6895c2044","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a28513c-52b6-570c-845a-643e5d008d72","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-069","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"After a loan has been approved and disbursed, which of the following is a method a bank uses to monitor it?","options":["Conducting a fresh status enquiry before the application","Asking the customer to open a current account","Physical inspection of the project being financed","Issuing a letter of offer to the customer"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Monitoring after disbursement includes regularly demanding the firm's records, physically inspecting the project and watching how the loan account is conducted. Options A, B and D all belong to the stages before the loan is granted, not to monitoring."}},{"itemId":"c1beeb68-a764-56fc-9dc9-69add10e2898","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a0b10853-899b-506f-b440-fedb52aeec67","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-070","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The practice where bank lending officers grant loans to their relations or to businesses related to them without applying the basic principles of lending is called","options":["Credit rationing","Financial intermediation","Connected lending","Loan syndication"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Connected lending, also called insider lending, is lending to persons or businesses linked to bank staff or owners without proper appraisal, and it is a common cause of bad debts. Option D is several banks jointly financing a large loan, A is limiting the amount of credit granted, and B is the general role of banks in channelling funds."}},{"itemId":"61eba016-ff6f-56a4-af9e-b38a58349d0a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0b401275-f6ad-51a7-9607-a57bf24284cc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-071","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank lends N30 million against a building valued at N40 million, but when the borrower defaults the building sells for only N22 million because the valuation did not reflect current market prices. The cause of bad debt illustrated is","options":["changes in economic policies","connected lending","natural hazards","lending on overvalued securities"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Securities that are not valued at current market prices may be overvalued, so on realisation they fail to cover the loan. Option C refers to events such as flood or drought, B to loans to insiders and relations, and A to government policy changes, none of which is described here."}},{"itemId":"026a645e-36e3-51af-9f40-b29845581826","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25e6a6cb-040c-5c20-835a-2a9960bbec76","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-072","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Natural hazards such as drought and flood are a cause of bad debts that tends to affect which category of bank loans more than others?","options":["Overdrafts to salary earners","Loans secured on cash deposits","Agricultural loans","Loans to importers of computers"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Drought, floods and similar acts of God destroy crops and livestock, so they hit agricultural loans harder than other categories. Options A, B and D are far less exposed to weather, and a loan backed by a cash deposit is largely protected whatever happens."}},{"itemId":"02cb5c10-87ad-50bd-8fa6-fe344ab2e36f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2bee0278-470a-5ebe-931c-2c067659b642","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-073","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In loan recovery, amicable methods such as restructuring or rescheduling repayments are usually preferred to court action because they","options":["release all the securities to the borrower","preserve the banking relationship and avoid costly litigation","transfer the loan automatically to the CBN","cancel the borrower's obligation to pay interest"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Amicable recovery, through engagement, restructuring, rescheduling or negotiated settlement, keeps the relationship intact and avoids expensive and slow litigation. Option D is wrong because interest generally remains payable, C has no basis, and A would leave the bank unprotected."}},{"itemId":"5e4eacca-f57e-5452-99b2-72066bd117b8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"178a017e-ca59-5460-9e6a-322399909244","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-074","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a method by which a bank realises its security when a borrower defaults?","options":["Crediting the loan account with a new loan","Exercising the power of sale under a mortgage","Issuing a fresh letter of offer to the borrower","Conducting a status enquiry on the borrower"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Realisation of security includes exercising the power of sale under a mortgage, appointing a receiver under a debenture, or enforcing a pledge or guarantee, following proper legal procedure. Options C and D belong to the appraisal and approval stage, and A merely adds to the debt without recovering anything."}},{"itemId":"28cb2d1e-f932-5237-bbc0-93afb1952637","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bee33ff7-81c5-53c0-83d0-a6bdae1efed8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-075","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Reporting a defaulting borrower to a licensed credit bureau helps a bank in loan recovery mainly because","options":["it cancels the borrower's debt to the bank","it replaces the need for any security on future loans","it affects the borrower's future access to credit from other lenders","it transfers ownership of the borrower's assets to the bureau"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Once a default is reported to a credit bureau, other lenders can see it, so the borrower's future access to credit suffers, which encourages repayment. Options A, D and B are not effects of credit bureau reporting; the debt remains owed and the bureau acquires no assets."}},{"itemId":"3ac6358e-3592-5e82-a7cc-34dc623d4d62","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8869a798-6424-5c2e-805f-61324f21a081","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-076","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a way in which a commercial bank extends credit to its customers?","options":["Loan","Standing order","Discounting of bills of exchange","Overdraft"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Banks lend through loans, overdrafts and the discounting of bills of exchange. A standing order is an agency service in which the bank makes periodic payments on the customer's instruction, not a form of credit. Options A, D and C are all credit facilities."}},{"itemId":"5cd45ce6-c652-57a6-805d-fafd9cd32009","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd5d9e29-7b44-5c8a-aac6-5307b606526e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-077","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes a loan from an overdraft?","options":["On a loan, interest is charged on the whole amount granted; on an overdraft, interest is charged only on the amount actually overdrawn","A loan is given only to current account holders, while an overdraft can be given to anybody","A loan is normally short-term, while an overdraft is normally long-term","On a loan, interest is charged only on the amount used; on an overdraft, interest is charged on the full limit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"A loan is a lump sum placed at the borrower's disposal, so interest runs on the full amount, whereas overdraft interest is charged only on the debit balance actually used. Option D reverses this, B reverses who can obtain each facility, since an overdraft is for current account holders, and C reverses the usual terms."}},{"itemId":"e1e58331-a959-5ed3-9a03-196736beab47","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"baa4f96b-0296-5f04-b4d3-940a4420584b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-078","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A key difference between a loan and an overdraft is that","options":["an overdraft requires a separate overdraft account, while a loan runs on the current account","a loan requires no documentation, while an overdraft requires a great deal","an overdraft is a lump sum credited to a savings account","a separate loan account is opened for a loan, while an overdraft runs on the customer's current account"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"When a loan is granted the bank opens a separate loan account in the borrower's name, whereas an overdraft is simply the current account being allowed to go into debit up to a limit. Option A reverses the accounts, B reverses the documentation burden, since loans need more documents, and C misdescribes an overdraft."}},{"itemId":"ed94d504-8dfe-5d08-9253-ad7100cd2851","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"639f473e-34a2-5baf-994f-7ca737f073a5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-079","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the banker-customer relationship, a customer who has been granted an overdraft is generally obliged to","options":["repay only if the bank first obtains a court judgment","repay only when he chooses to close the account","repay only at the end of five years","repay the overdraft on demand when the bank recalls it"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Overdrafts are repayable on demand: the bank may recall the overdraft when it judges this the best course, and the customer is then expected to pay immediately. Options C, A and B wrongly suggest a long fixed term or conditions that would delay repayment."}},{"itemId":"59c49f52-7870-5d6e-95f1-4d9ad020c0a6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a2173de6-7f89-5f53-9ecd-97c12bde984f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-080","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a bank grants a loan to a customer under normal loan-account practice, the correct entries are to","options":["credit a new loan account and debit the customer's current account","debit a new loan account and credit the customer's current account","debit the customer's current account and credit the bank's cash account","debit the customer's savings account and credit the bank's capital account"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"On granting a loan, a separate loan account is opened and debited with the amount lent, while the customer's current account is credited so he can draw on the funds. Repayments later work the other way, debiting the current account and crediting the loan account. Option A shows the repayment direction, and D and C use the wrong accounts."}},{"itemId":"d27b48e2-c811-58c7-8f25-a98fefdc1da4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b16d2326-d739-5b63-ac4b-597c2150808f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-081","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An arrangement that allows a borrower to retire a loan gradually through periodic payments over its life, rather than paying it all at once at maturity, is known as","options":["Loan amortisation","Bullet repayment","Loan syndication","Loan crystallisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Amortisation spreads repayment of a loan over its life, protecting both lender and borrower against the borrower failing to provide for one large final payment. Option B is the opposite, a single lump-sum repayment at maturity. Option C is several lenders sharing one loan, and D is not a repayment method."}},{"itemId":"ba641f50-4bf5-5699-8e80-8d98a6b90837","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0ad07bc5-9e45-501f-bc72-fc9a86a6078f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-082","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Where a loan is taken to purchase a specific asset such as a delivery truck, the amortisation schedule is best designed so that","options":["the entire principal falls due on the first day of the loan","repayments continue long after the asset has been scrapped","repayments match the productive life of the asset and are paid from the cash flows the asset generates","the borrower pays interest only and never repays the principal"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"When a loan finances a specific asset, the repayment schedule should be geared to the asset's productive life, with payments made from the cash flows the asset produces. Option A defeats the purpose of amortisation, B would leave the borrower paying for an asset that no longer earns, and D is not amortisation at all."}},{"itemId":"c004bf7a-230f-531f-9710-0e0fd14efba7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3f600e37-93f0-5e56-a066-810ae8bd65a9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-083","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Adeyemi borrows N500,000 from her bank for 3 years at 18% per annum flat (simple) interest, calculated on the original principal for the whole period. What is the total interest she pays?","options":["N135,000","N770,000","N90,000","N270,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Flat interest is principal x rate x time = 500,000 x 0.18 x 3 = N270,000. Option C is only one year's interest, B is the total amount repayable (principal plus interest), and A is the result of using only half the period."}},{"itemId":"2a864546-037c-5357-8111-f63cffcf1954","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1899f7e1-be10-5dca-bfea-2bff3ec440ac","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-084","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A trader borrows N1,200,000 for 12 months at 15% per annum flat interest charged on the original principal. Principal and interest are repaid in 12 equal monthly instalments. What is each monthly instalment?","options":["N15,000","N280,000","N100,000","N115,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Flat interest for the year is 1,200,000 x 0.15 x 1 = N180,000, so total repayable is N1,380,000, and 1,380,000 / 12 = N115,000 a month. Option C repays principal only, A is the monthly interest only, and B wrongly adds the whole year's interest to one month's principal."}},{"itemId":"72652e16-04b3-56b1-b0b8-12e72912b424","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"51b0b76a-4a4f-54ce-9412-c7ccbd275abc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-085","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank grants Mr Uche a loan of N800,000 repayable in 4 equal half-yearly principal instalments over 2 years, with interest at 12% per annum flat on the original principal paid separately each half-year. Immediately after the third principal instalment is paid, what is the balance of principal on his loan account?","options":["N200,000","N400,000","N600,000","N56,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Each principal instalment is 800,000 / 4 = N200,000, so after three instalments 800,000 - 600,000 = N200,000 remains; interest is paid separately and does not reduce the principal. Option C is the amount already repaid, B is the balance after only two instalments, and D wrongly deducts the three half-yearly interest payments of N48,000 each from the remaining principal."}},{"itemId":"eefc209d-6d8b-5fbd-9dd4-1fb601161500","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a14598d2-b1aa-5b1b-8622-b344dc599ceb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-086","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bisi has a credit balance of N150,000 in her current account when her bank grants her a loan of N600,000, which is credited to that account. The loan is repayable in 3 equal annual principal instalments, with interest at 10% per annum flat on the original principal; each year's principal and interest are debited to her current account. Assuming no other transactions, what is her current account balance immediately after the first year's repayment?","options":["N550,000 credit","N370,000 credit","N490,000 credit","N550,000 debit"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The account stands at 150,000 + 600,000 = N750,000 after the loan is credited; the first debit is principal 600,000 / 3 = N200,000 plus flat interest 600,000 x 0.10 = N60,000, so 750,000 - 260,000 = N490,000 credit. Option A ignores the interest debit, B wrongly deducts the interest for all three years at once, and D reverses the sign of the balance."}},{"itemId":"4d29a0ee-0dde-55c1-91fd-7e3393bcea8e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8ac2e6bf-dffa-5b82-85e5-9a4b7b850f35","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-087","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank lends N1,000,000 at 10% per annum, to be fully amortised by 3 equal annual payments made at the end of each year (annuity method, payment = loan / PVIFA, where PVIFA = [1 - (1.10)^-3] / 0.10). To the nearest kobo, what is each annual payment?","options":["N433,333.33","N366,666.67","N333,333.33","N402,114.80"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"PVIFA at 10% for 3 years is 2.48685, so the payment is 1,000,000 / 2.48685 = N402,114.80. Option A is the flat-interest figure (1,300,000 / 3), C repays principal only, and B adds just one year's interest to the principal before dividing by 3."}},{"itemId":"8080e152-773b-5a21-8ea6-50dd56e03d35","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3bc5289e-391f-5c52-84d6-83fbb82f9a12","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-088","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A N1,000,000 loan at 10% per annum is amortised by 3 equal end-of-year payments of N402,114.80 each, with interest each year charged on the opening balance. How much of the FIRST payment goes to reduce the principal?","options":["N100,000.00","N333,333.33","N302,114.80","N402,114.80"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Interest for year 1 is 10% of the opening balance of N1,000,000, which is N100,000, so the principal reduction is 402,114.80 - 100,000 = N302,114.80. Option D treats the whole payment as principal, B assumes equal principal portions, which happens with equal-principal loans rather than annuities, and A is the interest portion."}},{"itemId":"ce413658-7826-5a4a-93b0-856dee61e669","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99874fae-487d-5528-8256-bf774ddec52e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-089","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kola's current account was overdrawn by N400,000 for 73 days. The bank charges overdraft interest at 20% per annum on the amount overdrawn, using an actual/365 day-count (interest = amount x rate x days / 365). How much interest is charged?","options":["N80,000.00","N6,666.67","N16,222.22","N16,000.00"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Interest = 400,000 x 0.20 x 73 / 365 = N16,000. Option A is a full year's interest, C wrongly uses a 360-day year, and B is one month's interest (400,000 x 0.20 / 12), not 73 days."}},{"itemId":"95faf275-d261-5bba-a12c-fbb48eb38096","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3f961213-70ed-5986-b20f-6b4ca9da5af9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-090","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Femi Stores Ltd has an overdraft limit of N1,000,000 but actually overdraws only N600,000, which stays unchanged for 30 days. Interest is charged at 24% per annum on the amount actually overdrawn, actual/365 day-count. To the nearest kobo, what is the interest for the 30 days?","options":["N144,000.00","N11,835.62","N12,000.00","N19,726.03"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Overdraft interest is charged on the amount actually overdrawn, so it is 600,000 x 0.24 x 30 / 365 = N11,835.62. Option D wrongly charges interest on the full N1,000,000 limit, C uses a 360-day year, and A is a full year's interest on N600,000."}},{"itemId":"3bb6d67b-eb27-52ac-b339-928ac7851651","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"059ac803-f958-5903-bd98-71e158d057ca","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-091","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank grants a loan of N12 million against a house valued at N20 million. The loan-to-value (LTV) ratio is calculated as loan / value of security x 100. What is the LTV ratio?","options":["40%","166.67%","12%","60%"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"LTV = 12,000,000 / 20,000,000 x 100 = 60%. Option A is the margin as a percentage of the value (the part of the value not lent), B inverts the ratio (value / loan), and C wrongly treats the loan amount in millions as the percentage."}},{"itemId":"ff18681a-ecee-5886-93dd-86605a025ade","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ce4008d6-584c-50d9-9225-75f501a8e67b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-092","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank's policy allows a maximum loan-to-value ratio of 70% (maximum loan = 70% of the value of the security). A customer offers a plot of land valued at N15 million. What margin, in naira, will the bank keep between the security's value and the maximum loan?","options":["N1,050,000","N10,500,000","N4,500,000","N15,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"The maximum loan is 0.70 x 15,000,000 = N10,500,000, so the margin, the excess of the security's value over the loan, is 15,000,000 - 10,500,000 = N4,500,000. Option B is the maximum loan itself, D is the full value of the land, and A misplaces a decimal in the loan figure."}},{"itemId":"fc997045-299b-56f2-a340-6ac9ed4f0a3a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"40ceceb2-d3ef-57fd-8c03-990f5ced39c6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-093","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Tunde takes a loan of N300,000 for 9 months at 16% per annum flat (simple) interest on the original principal. Using interest = principal x annual rate x (months / 12), how much interest does he pay?","options":["N48,000","N432,000","N4,000","N36,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Interest = 300,000 x 0.16 x 9/12 = N36,000. Option A is a full year's interest, C is just one month's interest, and B wrongly multiplies the annual rate by 9 months instead of 9/12 of a year."}},{"itemId":"45bb732e-b284-5e17-9352-2ede03b344a6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d373651-4189-5d56-a8d1-d91ae800cd0b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-094","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A cooperative society borrows N2,000,000 for 2 years at 14% per annum flat interest on the original principal. Principal and total interest are repaid in 8 equal quarterly instalments. What is each quarterly instalment?","options":["N250,000","N285,000","N320,000","N390,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Flat interest is 2,000,000 x 0.14 x 2 = N560,000, so the total repayable is N2,560,000, and 2,560,000 / 8 = N320,000 per quarter. Option A is principal only, B includes only one year's interest, and D wrongly spreads the two years' interest over just 4 quarters."}},{"itemId":"583c8e11-a9d2-5a1e-b998-6f75b477232d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f6f012f3-f1d9-5e19-b6fc-670284c87247","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-095","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A customer's current account was overdrawn by N250,000 for 20 days and then by N450,000 for the next 10 days. The bank charges 18% per annum on the daily amount actually overdrawn, actual/365 day-count. To the nearest kobo, what is the total overdraft interest for the 30 days?","options":["N3,698.63","N4,750.00","N6,657.53","N4,684.93"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Interest is worked out on each balance for the days it was outstanding: 250,000 x 0.18 x 20/365 = N2,465.75 plus 450,000 x 0.18 x 10/365 = N2,219.18, giving N4,684.93. Option C charges the higher balance for all 30 days, B uses a 360-day year, and A charges the lower balance for all 30 days."}},{"itemId":"69e7c497-8762-5248-9149-f7814c7a9d65","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4bb83ae9-f4d5-5eca-b8ae-9b52bc90f832","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:LEN-096","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank lends N1,800,000 against quoted shares with a market value of N2,500,000. Expressing the margin (market value of security minus loan) as a percentage of the market value of the security, what is the margin?","options":["72%","138.89%","38.89%","28%"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Bank Lending","explanation":"Margin = 2,500,000 - 1,800,000 = N700,000, and 700,000 / 2,500,000 x 100 = 28%. Option A is the loan-to-value ratio rather than the margin, C wrongly divides the margin by the loan instead of the security's value, and B is value divided by loan."}},{"itemId":"183cb3fb-599f-5563-a7d9-d90573c4e5ea","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cba2429a-58ad-54d2-af03-b91e45b7fb61","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of the money market?","options":["Its instruments are generally low-risk and issued by creditworthy borrowers","Its instruments are highly liquid and can be turned into cash quickly","Its instruments usually have maturities of five years or more","Its deals are usually large, wholesale transactions"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The money market deals in short-term instruments, generally maturing within one year, so maturities of five years or more belong to the capital market. Options B, A and D are genuine features of the money market: high liquidity, low risk and large wholesale deals."}},{"itemId":"762621ba-f22f-5f25-a309-54b285d1fce8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58545761-1201-5127-99a1-69f7254c23b4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The money market is often described as a 'wholesale' and 'professional' market. This mainly means that","options":["deals are usually large and dominated by institutional investors with specialised knowledge","instruments are sold only to retail savers in small units","only stockbrokers licensed by the stock exchange may buy or sell in it","all transactions must take place on a single physical trading floor"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Money market deals usually involve large sums with substantial minimum amounts, and the market is dominated by banks and other institutions that understand it. Option C describes dealing members of the stock exchange, while option B is the opposite of wholesale. Option D is wrong because money market dealing is not tied to one trading floor."}},{"itemId":"f3f34c02-e335-5d59-8620-d10992489232","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d958741d-5e42-5130-ba32-5e65d0583484","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The most basic distinction between the money market and the capital market lies in","options":["whether the market is located in Lagos or in Abuja","whether the buyers are individuals or companies","the term and liquidity of the instruments traded, short-term and highly liquid in the money market","whether the instruments are issued in naira or in foreign currency"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The money market trades short-term, highly liquid instruments for adjusting liquidity, while the capital market deals in long-term funds for investment. Option D is wrong because both markets mainly deal in naira instruments. Options B and A are wrong because individuals and companies take part in both markets, and location does not define either one."}},{"itemId":"8fba76af-528d-569a-82ba-ad588a930448","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a73e2758-0d41-5d8f-9624-85d663a3e759","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly compares the money market and the capital market?","options":["The money market deals in equities, while the capital market deals in Treasury bills","The money market finances factories and roads, while the capital market finances day-to-day cash needs","The money market is regulated by the SEC, while the capital market is regulated by the CBN","The money market mainly helps units adjust their short-term liquidity, while the capital market channels savings into long-term investment"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The money market exists mainly so that banks, firms and government can even out short-term cash surpluses and shortages, while the capital market turns savings into long-term funds for expansion and development. Option B reverses the two roles, and option A swaps their instruments. Option C swaps the regulators: the CBN oversees the money market and the SEC the capital market."}},{"itemId":"236dfa82-a69c-595d-8642-ba473349b611","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"84477a25-9c31-589c-9e44-4464411cfa89","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"While the Central Bank of Nigeria is the apex regulator of the Nigerian money market, the apex regulator of the Nigerian capital market is the","options":["Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)","Debt Management Office (DMO)","Federal Ministry of Finance","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The SEC is the apex regulatory body of the capital market, approving and registering securities and supervising capital market operators. Option D, the NDIC, insures bank deposits and helps supervise banks. Option B, the DMO, manages government debt but does not regulate the market. Option C makes fiscal policy rather than regulating the capital market."}},{"itemId":"f51d4ed5-6d3b-5bd5-97a7-eae3dda23ba3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4d65a962-c989-56d9-904a-02404a5e495e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which group of institutions dominates the Nigerian money market, in the same way that non-bank financial institutions dominate the capital market?","options":["Deposit money banks","Stockbroking firms","Development finance institutions","Insurance companies"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Deposit money banks are the main lenders and borrowers of short-term funds, using the money market to manage their reserves and invest surplus cash. Options D and C take part but are more prominent as long-term investors and lenders in the capital market. Option B, stockbroking firms, are capital market operators who deal in shares."}},{"itemId":"ad333ac2-e1cd-5747-9dcb-a80f840b1238","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f78246eb-d73d-5e46-bbe6-88c74dbb8c6d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A merchant bank buys newly issued 182-day Treasury bills at the CBN's auction, and two weeks later sells some of them to a pension fund. The two transactions took place, in turn, in the","options":["informal market and then the formal market","primary market and then the secondary market","capital market and then the money market","secondary market and then the primary market"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The primary market is where new securities are issued to raise fresh funds, as at a Treasury bill auction, while the secondary market is where existing securities change hands. Option D reverses the order. Option A is wrong because Treasury bills belong to the formal money market, and option C is wrong because both deals involve the same short-term instrument."}},{"itemId":"ae402fb4-d40c-5fb3-a2f3-a5f90ca2ec01","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bc719117-d885-5efe-9547-377acfa2b9c2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Traders at a Lagos market each contribute N2,000 every day into a rotating 'ajo' scheme, and one member collects the whole pool in turn. In the structure of the Nigerian money market, this arrangement belongs to the","options":["formal (organised) money market","discount market","interbank market","informal money market"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Esusu, ajo and similar traditional savings and credit schemes make up the informal money market, which is less regulated and less organised. Options C and B are parts of the formal market where banks and dealers trade with each other. Option A is the CBN-supervised segment, which an ajo scheme is not part of."}},{"itemId":"5feac6d2-c8aa-538d-aec4-8bb4fffcefe8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ceba4ee5-bd14-5021-85d4-f109ed188473","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a component of the formal (organised) segment of the Nigerian money market?","options":["Esusu contribution clubs","The interbank market","Standing facilities operated by the CBN","Central Bank open market operations"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Esusu clubs are traditional savings arrangements and belong to the informal money market. Options B, D and C are regulated activities supervised or run by the CBN, so they belong to the formal, organised segment."}},{"itemId":"e4c91472-b418-5410-9d21-9db8849ea5ec","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5467940e-adde-5ad9-b4c3-e5063ebe760f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Nigerian interbank market, banks","options":["deposit customers' foreign currency with the Debt Management Office","buy goods on credit from one another using trade bills","borrow long-term funds from the public by issuing shares","lend to and borrow from each other to manage their daily liquidity, at rates set by supply and demand"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The interbank market lets a bank with spare cash lend it, often overnight, to a bank that is short, and the rate moves with the supply of and demand for funds. Option C describes raising capital in the capital market. Options A and B do not describe interbank lending at all."}},{"itemId":"88b14db0-d596-5a02-90c8-2008af6b8e17","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c5cc0b8-0c8a-5993-bf01-68ce8f62b8fe","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the prerequisites for developing a money market?","options":["A continuous and sufficient flow of assets of varying maturities","Availability of short-term funds whose owners are willing to invest them","A requirement that all instruments be listed on the stock exchange","A lender of last resort that guarantees the liquidity of the assets traded"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Money market instruments are mainly traded among banks and dealers, so listing on the stock exchange is not a condition for a money market. Options B, A and D are genuine prerequisites, along with intermediaries who pool funds and invest them in short-term bills."}},{"itemId":"17254a3d-d388-5f9e-8752-9ed28220311f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"76ebd4bb-47c3-5e54-8048-dd425f25d141","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For a money market to work well, there must be an institution that stands ready to guarantee the liquidity of the assets traded by providing funds to banks when they cannot raise cash elsewhere. This institution is called the","options":["lender of last resort","fiscal agent","clearing house","issuing house"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"A central bank acting as lender of last resort supports the liquidity of money market assets, because banks know they can get funds from it in an emergency. Option C settles cheques and payments between banks, and option D arranges new share issues in the capital market. Option B describes a central bank acting for government in its financial affairs, which is not the same as backing market liquidity."}},{"itemId":"9bf966ac-7497-5fdf-80ed-7a05194a4c81","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f61f02e8-d234-54e1-846a-efca258b0648","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before the Central Bank of Nigeria was established, banks operating in Nigeria that had surplus short-term funds usually","options":["deposited them with Nigerian discount houses","lent them to the Nigerian government through Treasury bills","sent them abroad for investment in the London money market","invested them in CBN bills"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Before the CBN existed, Nigeria had no money market, so surplus funds were repatriated overseas for investment in the London money market. Options B, A and D were not yet available: Treasury bills came after the CBN, and discount houses and CBN bills came decades later."}},{"itemId":"f4b7c36c-dc79-5f5f-bb29-97ac8d0c2ff3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8abea534-77ef-5e1d-ab25-45c1f8cf3584","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"One reason for setting up the Nigerian money market was to 'Nigerianise the credit base'. This meant","options":["converting all foreign currency deposits into naira","restricting bank loans to Nigerian government agencies only","keeping funds in Nigeria to meet local credit needs instead of letting them flow to overseas markets","replacing all foreign bank staff with Nigerians"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Nigerianising the credit base meant giving local investment outlets to funds that would otherwise go abroad, so that they could meet the working capital needs of Nigerian businesses. Option D describes staff indigenisation, not credit. Options B and A are not part of the objective."}},{"itemId":"ded6dd88-70dd-533a-8dd1-bb0f98c9cc06","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3345f38-fbe5-585c-bd44-998e6d4e2834","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Government revenue in Nigeria often comes in unevenly during the year while spending goes on all the time. The money market helps government deal with this mainly by","options":["letting it fix the interest rates that banks pay depositors","letting it issue long-term development stocks to cover monthly salaries","letting it issue Treasury bills to bridge short-term gaps between spending and revenue","letting it sell shares in ministries to the public"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury bills let government borrow for short periods and repay when revenue comes in, evening out seasonal swings in its cash flow. Option D is not possible, since ministries do not issue shares. Option B uses a long-term instrument for a short-term need, and option A is not a money market function."}},{"itemId":"ea7f87a9-24e6-52e3-a058-b4437a8a0aa3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"edb2c8e2-985b-547d-a33d-b36910f11ef9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a factor that helped the growth of the Nigerian money market?","options":["The listing of Treasury bills on foreign stock exchanges","Laws allowing new and varied money market instruments to be created","The establishment and enthusiasm of the Central Bank of Nigeria","Government's policy of Nigerianising the credit base"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury bills are domestic instruments issued through the CBN, and listing them abroad did not drive the market's growth. Options C, D and B are recognised growth factors, along with the teleprinter service and government's own financing needs."}},{"itemId":"77ff7285-44a8-5c94-a3ff-65c6f6b515b1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a396d51e-1887-529e-a878-eb996902cd50","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The introduction of the teleprinter service is listed as one of the factors that helped the Nigerian money market grow, because it","options":["replaced Treasury bills with electronic money","speeded up transfers of funds between CBN branches and its head office in Lagos","allowed customers to withdraw cash from automated teller machines","allowed the stock exchange to trade shares online"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The teleprinter carried typed messages quickly, so bank transfers between CBN branches and the Lagos head office moved faster and funds could be put to use sooner. Options C and D describe much later electronic banking and trading technology. Option A is wrong because Treasury bills remained the main money market instrument."}},{"itemId":"f3d81ed6-f8b7-57a6-b48a-cab02d3858ad","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25d5bea7-659d-5704-95e1-3d9d7b9e43b6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How does the money market give the CBN a ready gauge of whether funds are scarce or plentiful in the economy?","options":["Through the number of new companies listed on the stock exchange","Through the number of bank branches opened each year","Through the volume of goods imported each month","Through movements in money market interest rates"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"When funds are scarce, money market rates rise, and when there is excess liquidity they fall, so the CBN can read conditions from these rates. Option A reflects capital market activity, while options C and B are not direct measures of short-term funds."}},{"itemId":"30b77ce1-962b-51c4-a333-b155c2cb299e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7f186aea-d0e0-5593-a78b-bc218f751ec1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The function of the money market by which it sets benchmark short-term interest rates that reflect market conditions and serve as reference rates for other financial instruments is called","options":["capital formation","price discovery","risk transfer","financial intermediation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Price discovery means the market itself sets the price of short-term funds, giving benchmark rates for other instruments. Option D refers to channelling funds from surplus to deficit units, and option C refers to shifting risk to others. Option A is the building up of long-term productive assets, mainly through the capital market."}},{"itemId":"c94db54c-6dc7-56f3-82ed-c27b7982be2d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f763da3-45e7-5ff3-a5e8-1192ddef07dd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a Nigerian bank's cash falls below the level it needs, its 'first line of defence' in the money market is usually to","options":["sell its head office building","issue new shares on the stock exchange","discount its holdings of Treasury bills","draw on call money or borrow in the interbank market"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Call money and interbank funds are the quickest ways for a bank to raise cash, so they are its first line of defence. Option C, discounting bills such as Treasury bills, is the second line of defence, used when call money is not enough. Options A and B are long-term capital decisions, not money market liquidity tools."}},{"itemId":"913310ce-11d6-5915-bba1-51aed94babd3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"35821fe8-b78d-561f-9657-6f90c15e35ed","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bank A has already used up the call money and interbank funds it can raise, but is still short of cash at the end of the day. Its 'second line of defence' in the money market would be to","options":["raise its own interest rates on loans","lend more money to other banks overnight","discount bills it holds, such as Treasury bills, for cash","buy more commercial papers from companies"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Discounting bills such as Treasury bills for cash is the second line of defence when call money and interbank funds are not enough. Option B would make the shortage worse, since lending uses up cash. Options A and D do not produce cash quickly, and buying commercial papers also uses cash."}},{"itemId":"ccb9bb2a-a140-580c-a5c7-aceb53694a26","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d24ede80-b334-5d2b-8289-771786401250","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The CBN sells Treasury bills to banks through open market operations when it believes there is too much money in the banking system. The direct effect of this sale is to","options":["increase banks' reserves and so inject liquidity into the system","reduce banks' reserves and so withdraw liquidity from the system","increase the amount of currency in circulation","raise the capital base of the banks"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"When banks pay for the bills, money moves from their reserves to the CBN, so liquidity is withdrawn and the money supply tends to fall. Option A is what happens when the CBN buys securities. Option D is wrong because buying bills does not change a bank's share capital, and option C is the opposite of the intended effect."}},{"itemId":"beb8f369-51e7-59c6-ba87-1427a3db8ceb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"852b64b1-6bdd-54d2-8c5a-4acdd17de401","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a standard maturity of Nigerian Treasury bills?","options":["91 days","270 days","182 days","364 days"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Nigerian Treasury bills are issued in maturities of 91, 182 and 364 days. 270 days is the upper end of the usual range for commercial papers and bankers' acceptances, not a Treasury bill maturity. Options A, C and D are all standard Treasury bill maturities."}},{"itemId":"edd34592-4803-5804-af0f-77e05e9ddcb7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2cf8ee72-5152-533e-ad8f-3784dd19c20f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An investor who buys a Treasury bill and holds it to maturity earns a return that comes from","options":["dividends declared by the Federal Government","the difference between the face value received at maturity and the discounted price paid","coupon interest paid to him every quarter","a share of the profits of the Central Bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury bills are sold at a discount and redeemed at face value, so the holder's return is the gap between the two amounts. Option C describes a coupon bond, not a discount instrument. Options A and D are wrong because government securities do not pay dividends or profit shares."}},{"itemId":"8b321022-9266-51c3-a266-68dea6cbcd16","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7ae2ec91-8642-5519-8264-040c31aab96a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In which year were Treasury bills first issued in Nigeria by the Central Bank on behalf of the Federal Government?","options":["1975","1960","1958","1968"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury bills were first issued in 1960, under the Treasury Bills Act of 1959, soon after the CBN began operations. Option C is the year the CBN Act was passed, option D is when Treasury certificates were introduced, and option A is when certificates of deposit were introduced."}},{"itemId":"672c9ffe-fdc8-59bd-99c6-a6bcaa258d9a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b6810d87-be0f-52de-97a8-cdf06a06dc3e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Treasury certificates were introduced in Nigeria in 1968, mainly to","options":["give commercial banks a way to lend to merchant banks","replace Treasury bills, which were abolished that year","finance the building of the National Theatre","widen the range of money market instruments and help finance the civil war and post-war reconstruction"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury certificates were introduced in 1968 to add to the money market instruments available and to raise funds for the civil war and reconstruction. Option B is wrong because Treasury bills continued alongside them. Option A describes certificates of deposit, introduced in 1975, and option C has nothing to do with Treasury certificates."}},{"itemId":"e38fbfc3-3ae2-561a-a490-e11cb245b2b0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"69f06491-686a-51df-890a-1b88dc607bdc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How did Nigerian Treasury certificates differ from Treasury bills?","options":["They had longer maturities of one or two years and so offered higher returns","They had shorter maturities of 14 to 30 days","They were long-term equity instruments traded only on the stock exchange","They were issued by companies rather than by government"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury certificates were Federal Government instruments like Treasury bills, but with maturities of one or two years and higher returns. Option D is wrong because they were government securities. Option B gets the maturity backwards, and option C wrongly makes them shares."}},{"itemId":"6bd28e3b-7952-51e2-ada5-57be79392b6b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd85cc16-80b8-5a35-8e75-da60778e736a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nigerian Treasury bills are sold to investors mainly through","options":["an auction in which authorised dealers such as banks submit tenders on behalf of themselves and their customers","direct over-the-counter sales at Federal Ministry of Finance offices","a lottery among holders of savings accounts","a public offer of shares managed by an issuing house"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Treasury bills are issued on a discount basis through an auction-based system in which authorised dealers are invited to tender, and members of the public bid through these dealers. Option D describes how new shares are sold in the capital market. Options B and C do not describe how Treasury bills are issued."}},{"itemId":"fb1df67e-9726-5c7f-bc8a-fa6c2a1b5f5e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0bf2a288-2bd6-5d4e-8ecd-98681dfd0da1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For a Nigerian deposit money bank, Treasury bills and Treasury certificates are regarded as","options":["part of the bank's share capital","long-term risk assets similar to mortgages","fixed assets used in running the bank","primary sources of liquidity, because they can be turned into cash quickly"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Money market instruments such as Treasury bills and certificates count as primary liquidity, while loans and advances provide only secondary liquidity. Option C refers to buildings and equipment, and option A refers to funds contributed by shareholders. Option B is wrong because these government instruments are short-term and low-risk."}},{"itemId":"87896317-c214-5ac2-86ae-eb487c084daf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"74de6cec-384d-5a44-bbb6-d85f9a844704","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A large, highly rated Nigerian food company needs N2 billion for 120 days to buy raw materials. It wants to borrow directly from pension funds and other investors, at a lower cost than a bank loan and without pledging any assets. The most suitable instrument is","options":["commercial paper","a Treasury certificate","an eligible development stock","a certificate of deposit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Commercial paper is an unsecured short-term note that creditworthy companies sell at a discount directly to investors, usually more cheaply than a bank loan. Options B and C are government securities that a company cannot issue. Option D is issued by banks to evidence deposits, not by companies."}},{"itemId":"55d8b21e-7e1d-5fd2-9219-1097ff116462","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ac221547-423f-5352-985b-00ac4305ee3e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the key difference between commercial paper and a commercial (trade) bill?","options":["Commercial paper is secured on the issuer's assets, while a commercial bill is unsecured","A commercial bill is issued by the Federal Government, while commercial paper is issued by banks","Commercial paper has no maturity date, while a commercial bill matures in five years","A commercial bill arises from an actual sale of goods on credit, while commercial paper is not tied to any particular trade transaction"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Commercial bills come from business transactions, such as a buyer who cannot pay cash immediately for goods, while commercial papers are raised for general short-term funding and are not tied to a trade deal. Option A is wrong because commercial paper is unsecured. Options B and C misstate who issues these instruments and how long they last."}},{"itemId":"6c568327-fc8e-58c8-9fef-a50f854fcd3b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2ea7076b-8878-5788-8bdc-175c40cfd82b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bankers' acceptance usually carries a lower credit risk than a commercial paper of the same maturity because","options":["a bank has accepted it and so undertakes to pay it at maturity","it is secured by a mortgage on the holder's house","it is issued by the Federal Government","it has no maturity date"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Once a bank writes 'Accepted' on a bill, the bank itself is bound to pay at maturity, so the holder relies on the bank's credit rather than only the trader's. Commercial paper is backed only by the issuing company's reputation. Option C describes Treasury bills, and options D and B are not features of bankers' acceptances."}},{"itemId":"98e22029-7476-5f46-8f26-05c1cbae6070","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff3dc021-f345-54d4-a54c-37665d2826f3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bankers' acceptances are used mainly in","options":["paying monthly salaries of civil servants","raising equity capital for new companies","financing international trade, where an importer's draft is accepted by a bank","financing long-term government infrastructure"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"A bankers' acceptance is a time draft drawn on and accepted by a bank, and it is used mainly to finance imports and exports because the seller can rely on the bank's promise to pay. Option D uses long-term bonds, and option B uses shares in the capital market. Option A is a payments matter, not trade finance."}},{"itemId":"494710d0-c8e8-5bf0-8577-28e95118ceda","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18beec0a-5862-5de4-b70b-e6ff432adb6c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank formally accepts a time draft drawn on it, turning it into a bankers' acceptance, by","options":["endorsing the back of the draft in favour of the CBN","stamping 'Paid' on the draft before it matures","writing 'Accepted' with an authorised signature across the face of the draft","crossing the draft with two parallel lines"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The drawee bank accepts by writing the word 'Accepted' on the face of the draft and signing it, which commits it to pay at maturity. Option A describes an endorsement, which transfers a bill, and option D describes crossing, which applies to cheques. Option B would mean the draft had already been settled."}},{"itemId":"d275e8b3-e913-5999-873a-e630133df696","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f671d38b-da67-5569-a448-da024e38719d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The holder of a negotiable certificate of deposit who needs cash before the certificate matures can","options":["only wait until maturity, since it can never be transferred","demand that the CBN convert it into Treasury certificates","sell it to another investor in the secondary market","exchange it for shares in the issuing bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"A negotiable CD can be traded, so the investor can sell it before maturity to another buyer. Option A describes a non-negotiable CD, which is held to maturity. Options B and D are not features of CDs."}},{"itemId":"f53d71aa-c5fa-508a-8049-e04f9904075a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2c996dc7-f01f-5c58-9cf1-55990820ee3a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When certificates of deposit were introduced in Nigeria in 1975, they were designed mainly as","options":["a way for the Federal Government to finance the civil war","an instrument for marketing boards to finance export produce","a share certificate issued by newly listed companies","an interbank instrument to channel commercial banks' surplus funds to merchant banks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Certificates of deposit were introduced in 1975 as an interbank debt instrument, with merchant banks as the main issuers absorbing commercial banks' surplus cash during the oil boom. Option A relates to Treasury certificates of 1968, and option B relates to the produce bills scheme. Option C confuses a CD with a share certificate."}},{"itemId":"1ad104b5-ff7d-5455-9e94-24510828f963","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"51f523a4-b7c9-57a1-8d33-63892cc61ffd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Very short-term loans, usually overnight or for a few days, lent among banks and repayable on demand or at very short notice, are known as","options":["call money","eligible development stocks","Treasury certificates","commercial bills"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Call money is lent for very short periods and can be called back at short notice, which makes it ideal for meeting immediate liquidity needs. Option B refers to government stocks close to maturity, option C to one- or two-year government securities, and option D to trade bills of about 90 days."}},{"itemId":"26e63148-9731-5c48-8328-36c9b1d58074","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"626d50db-2f62-50a5-be37-9751be5d3783","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Call Money Fund scheme introduced in 1962, banks lent their surplus balances to the fund for investment in Treasury bills. The scheme was abolished in 1974 mainly because","options":["the government's financial position had improved","banks were no longer allowed to keep balances with the CBN","the CBN had been shut down","Treasury bills had been abolished"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The Call Money Fund scheme was abolished in 1974 because the government's financial position had improved, so it no longer needed the scheme to fund its borrowing. Options C, D and B are wrong because the CBN, Treasury bills and banks' balances with the CBN all continued after 1974."}},{"itemId":"bfff7985-7901-5c15-a528-ea7e518d6522","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9da32363-2a91-5505-96af-52c1d5b48ff0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A transaction in which a bank sells government securities to another party and at the same time agrees to buy them back at a set price on a set date is called","options":["a bankers' acceptance","a rights issue","a repurchase agreement (repo)","an offer for sale"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"A repo is in effect a short-term loan secured by the securities sold, with the difference between the sale and buy-back prices as the interest. Option A is a bank-accepted bill of exchange. Options D and B are ways of issuing shares in the capital market."}},{"itemId":"f64d462a-2118-5bf5-a0aa-faf7d5b5cb57","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"79db89d4-c739-55e7-950a-1e2f978d7192","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How do CBN bills differ from Treasury bills in purpose?","options":["CBN bills finance government budget deficits, while Treasury bills are used only to control inflation","CBN bills are issued by deposit money banks, while Treasury bills are issued by companies","CBN bills are long-term bonds, while Treasury bills are equities","CBN bills are issued by the CBN for monetary policy, to mop up excess liquidity, while Treasury bills raise short-term funds for the Federal Government"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"CBN bills are the Central Bank's own instrument, used to withdraw excess liquidity from the banking system, though they look much like Treasury bills. Treasury bills are mainly a way for the Federal Government to borrow short-term. Option A reverses the purposes, and options C and B misstate what the instruments are and who issues them."}},{"itemId":"51bf5ded-297f-5eb2-ac25-5621e435317b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"234e7c81-2aec-51e7-bfaf-6415dca5186e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Government development loan stocks are normally long-term, but those with less than three years left to maturity are counted as liquid assets of commercial banks when computing their liquidity ratio. Such stocks are called","options":["Treasury certificates","stabilisation securities","National Savings Certificates","eligible development stocks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Development stocks close to maturity, with under three years to run, are called eligible development stocks and are accepted as bank liquid assets. Option B refers to securities the CBN directed banks to take up to reduce money in circulation. Option A refers to one- or two-year Treasury instruments, and option C to a savings instrument."}},{"itemId":"a9a7c045-371f-55f2-aaf7-71845524b485","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"daaf7fe3-965d-5494-8bcb-ab9748a07ff2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The money market arrangement of the 1970s under which banks pooled their surplus funds with the CBN for investment, mainly in Federal Government stocks, was the","options":["Bankers' Unit Fund","Produce Bills Finance Scheme","Standing Deposit Facility","Call Money Fund"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The Bankers' Unit Fund pooled banks' idle funds with the CBN, which invested them in interest-yielding assets, mainly government stocks. Option B financed marketing boards' exports, and option D was the 1962 scheme for investing banks' surplus balances in Treasury bills. Option C is a modern CBN overnight deposit window."}},{"itemId":"0599189f-7b85-5590-90ae-77dd9f9c4617","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50d2e65a-4fea-5a76-894a-ef533823b1b0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Produce Bills Finance Scheme, which operated in Nigeria in the 1960s,","options":["commercial banks lent their surplus balances to merchant banks through certificates","a group of financial houses discounted marketing boards' export produce bills","farmers deposited their harvests with the CBN as security for loans","the Federal Government issued long-term stocks to buy cocoa farms"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The Produce Bills Finance Scheme let a group of financial houses discount the bills that marketing boards drew to finance export produce, and it was abolished in 1968. Options C and D are invented arrangements. Option A describes certificates of deposit, introduced in 1975."}},{"itemId":"3a486653-c82c-5154-bfe4-bd63930eccee","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a2c7fabb-3f90-5324-af3f-f94b62412fd5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Adeyemi places N5 million with her bank with no fixed maturity date. Either she or the bank can end the placement by giving seven days' notice, and interest is paid at an agreed rate. This placement is a","options":["certificate of deposit","call deposit","current account","fixed deposit"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"A call deposit has no set maturity and can be ended by either party on the agreed notice period, such as 24 hours or 7 days. Option D is placed for a fixed period, and option A is a certificate issued for a specified term. Option C is a cheque account that is repayable on demand without notice."}},{"itemId":"b0359da6-0433-5db6-af89-371cc30b08df","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1fa1ea80-f60e-510f-8abb-01f1b083647a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When discount houses operated in Nigeria, their principal role in the money market was to","options":["regulate stockbrokers and approve new share issues","insure the deposits of commercial bank customers","accept current account deposits and issue cheque books to the public","act as intermediaries between the CBN and licensed banks, trading in and holding Treasury bills and other short-term securities"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Discount houses traded in and held Treasury bills, commercial bills and other short-term securities, linking the CBN and the banks and helping the money market grow. Option C describes deposit money banks, and option A describes the SEC and the stock exchange. Option B is the role of the NDIC."}},{"itemId":"ad166d1a-7fe0-5fa8-b037-e4dbd5979b06","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d45f35d7-2863-51be-b7b9-e47e16864c52","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The first discount house in Nigeria, which began operating in 1992 under CBN guidelines, was","options":["Consolidated Discount Limited","First Securities Discount House (FSDH)","Express Discount Limited","Kakawa Discount House"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"First Securities Discount House, set up in 1992, was Nigeria's first discount house and later became FSDH Merchant Bank. Options D, A and C were also discount houses, but they came after FSDH."}},{"itemId":"526750cb-70e7-59fd-9b8a-3d0ca47208bd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"39e5baa6-973a-5022-b008-d6d56ce8c2b4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"'Information asymmetry' is listed as a problem of the Nigerian money market. This means that","options":["instruments carry no information about their maturity","participants do not have equal access to market information, which weakens the efficiency of pricing","every participant has exactly the same information at all times","the CBN publishes too much information about interest rates"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Information asymmetry means some participants know more than others about market conditions, so prices may not reflect true supply and demand. Option C describes the opposite, equal information. Options D and A are not what the term means."}},{"itemId":"e8c33b51-0eab-525d-b65d-6cbc1d9a54dc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"06d30860-9987-5d6a-b813-fdb1607f626e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN's standing facilities, the Standing Lending Facility (SLF) allows","options":["a bank short of funds to borrow overnight from the CBN, usually against eligible securities","companies to borrow directly from the CBN for five years","the Federal Government to borrow from commercial banks without interest","a bank with surplus funds to place them overnight with the CBN"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The Standing Lending Facility lets banks obtain overnight liquidity from the CBN when they are short of funds. Option D describes the Standing Deposit Facility, the matching window for banks with surplus cash. Options B and C are not CBN standing facilities."}},{"itemId":"014a4804-534f-54ef-b9f7-40061977fd8e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2333ce00-9e37-56c5-8fe9-cc71194cb00a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A pension fund buys 91-day Federal Government Treasury bills with a face value of N10,000,000 at a discount rate of 12% per annum. Using the bank discount method on a 365-day year (Discount = Face value x rate x days/365), what price does the fund pay?","options":["N9,696,666.67","N299,178.08","N9,700,821.92","N8,800,000.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The discount is N10,000,000 x 0.12 x 91/365 = N299,178.08, so the price is N10,000,000 - N299,178.08 = N9,700,821.92. Option A uses a 360-day year instead of 365, and option D charges a full year's discount. Option B is the discount itself, not the price paid."}},{"itemId":"e0717b30-fc6f-5552-ba18-1525167ee6ef","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8fdf0459-9a65-5bcb-af77-4828caf1736f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the bank discount method on a 365-day year, what is the discount on a 182-day Treasury bill with a face value of N5,000,000 issued at a discount rate of 10% per annum?","options":["N252,777.78","N249,315.07","N4,750,684.93","N500,000.00"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The discount is N5,000,000 x 0.10 x 182/365 = N249,315.07. Option A uses a 360-day year, and option D ignores the 182-day term and charges a full year. Option C is the price paid, N5,000,000 minus the discount, rather than the discount."}},{"itemId":"4fb2df52-ce5b-5ec3-a7a0-448441979efe","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3fe823fd-d71d-5ad6-9b51-30ac0f7f34bd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the bank discount method on a 365-day year, what is the discount on a 364-day Treasury bill with a face value of N1,000,000 at a discount rate of 8% per annum?","options":["N80,000.00","N79,780.82","N80,888.89","N920,219.18"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The discount is N1,000,000 x 0.08 x 364/365 = N79,780.82, slightly less than a full year's 8% because the bill runs 364 days, not 365. Option A treats the bill as a full 365-day year, and option C uses a 360-day year. Option D is the price paid, not the discount."}},{"itemId":"b0484ed4-8354-5c36-8ec5-16d76681c20f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"934abe0a-aaca-5b17-9f92-86a42ad72b88","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An investor buys a 91-day Treasury bill with a face value of N1,000,000 for N975,000 and holds it to maturity. On a 365-day year, what is the investor's simple annual yield, calculated as (Face value - Price)/Price x 365/91?","options":["10.14%","10.28%","2.56%","10.03%"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The gain is N25,000 on an outlay of N975,000, so the yield is 25,000/975,000 x 365/91 = 10.28% per annum. Option D divides by the face value instead of the price paid, which gives the discount rate rather than the yield. Option C is the 91-day return without annualising, and option A uses a 360-day year."}},{"itemId":"c8b28466-f68a-5fb0-b96d-17649b29f019","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cc41a898-30a4-5dc4-93f3-5dd704fcfc40","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bank X lends N50,000,000 to Bank Y overnight (1 day) in the interbank call money market at 15% per annum. Using simple interest on a 365-day year, how much interest does Bank Y pay?","options":["N20,833.33","N205,479.45","N7,500,000.00","N20,547.95"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Interest = N50,000,000 x 0.15 x 1/365 = N20,547.95. Option A uses a 360-day year, and option C charges a whole year's interest for a one-day loan. Option B misplaces the decimal point, giving ten times the correct figure."}},{"itemId":"5cc3b792-bc8c-5e38-ba9a-fba8bbfb650b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8b958519-1e0d-5f08-bd13-c9ad5f31a08c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank lends N20,000,000 at call for 7 days at 10% per annum. Using simple interest on a 365-day year, what interest does it earn?","options":["N2,000,000.00","N5,479.45","N38,356.16","N38,888.89"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"Interest = N20,000,000 x 0.10 x 7/365 = N38,356.16. Option D uses a 360-day year, and option A is a full year's interest. Option B is the interest for only one day rather than seven."}},{"itemId":"c3dfcdfb-4654-5de5-a878-dd8d6a3ab548","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"153c1a8f-9637-5f21-b25f-10298d49e053","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bank P sells Treasury bills to Bank Q for N9,950,000 under a repurchase agreement and agrees to buy them back for N10,000,000 after 14 days. Using simple interest on a 365-day year, with the interest measured on the amount received (N9,950,000), what is the implied annual repo rate?","options":["13.04%","0.50%","13.10%","12.92%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The interest is N10,000,000 - N9,950,000 = N50,000, so the rate is 50,000/9,950,000 x 365/14 = 13.10% per annum. Option A measures the interest on the repurchase price of N10,000,000 instead of the amount received. Option B is the 14-day return without annualising, and option D uses a 360-day year."}},{"itemId":"2dd657c4-c8df-5c81-866f-28d2e38acb0b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"31dd2303-af25-582e-ac3a-d7b99b242835","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MMK-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company issues 90-day commercial paper with a face value of N2,000,000 at a discount rate of 14% per annum. Using the discount method on a 365-day year, how much cash does the company receive?","options":["N69,041.10","N1,930,000.00","N1,720,000.00","N1,930,958.90"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Money Market","explanation":"The discount is N2,000,000 x 0.14 x 90/365 = N69,041.10, so the company receives N2,000,000 - N69,041.10 = N1,930,958.90. Option B uses a 360-day year, giving a N70,000 discount, and option C takes a full year's discount. Option A is the discount, not the cash received."}},{"itemId":"cb43cf76-9130-5e78-b706-403d5bce72d9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c5bdf019-688a-5bae-87be-24199a50a3db","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Despite its many problems, barter is said to have some advantages. Which of the following is one of them?","options":["It is free from currency devaluation, over-issue of money and inflation","It provides a common unit for measuring the value of all goods","It makes it easy to arrange payments for a future date","It makes it easy to store wealth for many years"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Because no currency is used under barter, there is no money to be over-issued or devalued, so the economy is free from monetary manipulation and inflation of that kind. Options B, C and D describe things barter lacks: a common measure of value, a means of storing value and a standard for deferred payments are all classic limitations of barter."}},{"itemId":"78331b27-bc3b-50d9-bc52-8e815d0abf9a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"af2716f5-9098-55ad-8b23-51f77fd07ea4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Two countries agree that one will supply crude oil and the other will supply machinery of equal value in return, with no money changing hands. This modern form of barter is called","options":["foreign exchange trading","debasement","bill discounting","countertrade"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"International countertrade is a modern form of barter in which countries exchange goods directly instead of paying in money. Option A involves buying and selling currencies, which is the opposite of avoiding money. Option B is reducing the metal content of coins, and option C is a bank buying a bill of exchange before it matures."}},{"itemId":"ad8eed48-be00-549e-810c-636779925d7c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e1c076f5-a625-52f1-87cd-a0f27ed5d9ce","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a community that uses grain as money, an unusually large harvest suddenly floods the market with grain and its value as money falls sharply. This illustrates which limitation of commodity money?","options":["Lack of double coincidence of wants","Indivisibility","Debasement","Supply fluctuations"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The supply of commodity money depends on natural events such as harvests, so a bumper harvest or a disaster can change its quantity and cause unpredictable inflation or deflation. Option B is about goods that cannot be split into smaller units, and option A is a problem of barter, not of commodity money. Option C is a deliberate reduction in the metal content of coins by rulers."}},{"itemId":"ec235883-76e9-522c-a0e4-7741216eac5b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a256c69f-7a4f-5339-9364-e6963a82586e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Two cows offered as payment are of the same breed, but one is healthy and fat while the other is old and thin, so traders argue over what each is worth. This illustrates which limitation of commodity money?","options":["Variability in quality","Excessive portability","Supply fluctuations","Difficulty in making large transactions"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Commodity money often varies in size, quality and condition, so units of the same type do not have the same value and pricing becomes difficult; this is the opposite of the uniformity good money needs. Option C concerns changes in the total quantity available, not differences between units. Option D is not the issue here, and option B is wrong because cattle are hard, not easy, to carry."}},{"itemId":"183b27c6-4ffd-5822-9a56-a736a5de5ad9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4d0c5c68-776a-593c-95b4-d4bf96013c31","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a reason why gold and silver came to be preferred as money?","options":["They are naturally scarce, which helps them keep a stable value","They are durable and do not rust or corrode","They decay quickly and must be used soon after they are obtained","They have high value relative to their weight, making them portable"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Precious metals were preferred precisely because they do not decay, so option C describes the opposite of their real quality. Options A, B and D are genuine reasons, alongside divisibility, uniformity, intrinsic value and universal acceptability."}},{"itemId":"6cd6d743-9844-5022-9f68-6ad7f4f70f67","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"638d7644-5a24-54a2-ade3-3cb95753c800","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The earliest standardised coins, associated with the ancient kingdom of Lydia in Asia Minor, were made of electrum, which is","options":["pure refined gold stamped with the king's seal","an alloy of iron and nickel","a mixture of copper and tin, also called bronze","a naturally occurring alloy of gold and silver"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Electrum is a natural mixture of gold and silver, and it was the metal of the early Lydian coins that began the history of standardised coinage. Option C describes bronze, which the Romans used in their early aes money. Option A is wrong because electrum is not pure gold, and option B is not electrum at all."}},{"itemId":"8acff5a3-56e1-5c9f-b681-7c6fcc722609","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f66c68e-bc1e-58d7-8047-0a2f163fb0c7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The practice by which a ruler reduces the gold or silver content of coins, for example by mixing in copper, while keeping their face value unchanged, is known as","options":["debasement","devaluation","convertibility","demonetisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Debasement means lowering the precious metal content of coins but keeping the same face value, which let rulers mint more coins from the same metal and earn a profit. Option B refers to an official cut in a currency's exchange value against other currencies. Option D means withdrawing a form of money from use as legal tender, and option C is the right to exchange paper money for gold or silver."}},{"itemId":"74064430-c07c-5b13-ab18-4030fadbf526","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"808968d9-bc68-5483-bbe7-f5edb5faaacc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a result of rulers debasing their coins?","options":["Loss of public trust in the currency","Rejection of debased coins in favour of full-value or foreign coins","A rise in the purchasing power of each coin","Inflation, as people raised prices to reflect the lower metal content"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Debasement made each coin worth less in real terms, so its purchasing power fell rather than rose. Options A, B and D are all genuine consequences: prices went up, trust was eroded, and people refused debased coins in favour of better ones."}},{"itemId":"b999243c-ddf8-5f3e-acf7-6c185b0ca4c7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d0a220d-a043-5b5c-a85a-33aa7ec76586","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A ruler holds 120 kg of silver. Full-value coins contain 20 g of silver each, but he decides to debase the coins so that each contains only 15 g while keeping the same face value. How many MORE coins can he mint from the same silver?","options":["8,000 coins","2,000 coins","6,000 coins","1,500 coins"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"At 20 g per coin, 120,000 g of silver makes 6,000 coins; at 15 g per coin it makes 8,000 coins, so debasement yields 2,000 extra coins of the same face value. Option A is the total number of debased coins, not the increase, and option C is the number of full-value coins. Option D wrongly takes 25% of the original 6,000 coins instead of dividing by the new weight."}},{"itemId":"2099b9c8-7ab9-578e-ae1d-45c38d1e3cf7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c6992554-d813-5dc8-8271-f4781be2f39c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the development of banking in England, the receipts that goldsmiths issued for gold deposited with them later","options":["had to be destroyed as soon as the gold was deposited","circulated as a means of payment and became known as goldsmiths' notes, the forerunners of bank notes","could be used only by the person who made the deposit","were treated by law as shares in the goldsmith's business"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"People found it convenient to pass the goldsmiths' receipts to others in payment instead of withdrawing the gold, so each receipt came to be called a goldsmith's note, the forerunner of the modern banknote. Option A is wrong because the receipts were evidence of the deposit, and option C contradicts the fact that they changed hands. Option D confuses a deposit receipt with ownership of the business."}},{"itemId":"22178503-9a86-5000-a7eb-6d302321b2bb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"361495f6-7ab7-5b0a-a226-81031a328c1c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bankers noticed that not all depositors demanded their gold at the same time, so they began issuing notes worth more than the metal actually held in their vaults. This practice marked the beginning of","options":["fractional reserve banking","debasement of coinage","the barter system","the gold standard"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Fractional reserve banking means keeping only part of deposits as reserves and lending or issuing paper claims against the rest, relying on the fact that depositors do not all withdraw at once. Option C is direct exchange without money. Option D fixes the value of a currency to gold, and option B reduces the metal content of coins."}},{"itemId":"04c80edd-89cc-51dc-95da-6166fd7e7532","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"79fb563c-3537-5068-9a45-7abe88738455","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why did the earliest bank notes have to be convertible into gold or silver coins on demand?","options":["Banks wanted to prevent the notes from circulating as money","People were willing to accept them only because they believed the notes could be exchanged for gold or silver whenever they wished","Paper was more valuable than gold at that time","The law required every note to be destroyed once it was converted"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"A paper note had no value of its own, so early acceptance rested on the promise that it could be converted into gold or silver coins on demand; such a note was a substitute for money rather than money itself. Option D has no basis, and option C is plainly false. Option A is wrong because convertibility was what made such paper acceptable in circulation."}},{"itemId":"42d3f011-6627-5f02-8d08-dbb2f7c43c29","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"163619c2-330f-5ec0-9e4a-3a92359fd2dd","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following was NOT a feature of the gold standard?","options":["Governments could expand the money supply freely without regard to their gold reserves","Currencies were defined in terms of a fixed weight of gold","Exchange rates between countries were fixed by the gold value of their currencies","Paper money could be exchanged for gold at banks or the treasury"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Under the gold standard the money supply was limited by gold reserves, which is exactly why governments found it hard to respond to crises with monetary policy. Options B, C and D are genuine features: currencies were defined by gold weight, paper money was convertible into gold, and exchange rates were fixed through gold values."}},{"itemId":"93886c33-0b4a-5aca-99ba-c1e436cafdbc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d49fd596-3022-5200-9f04-60bc2efd52b7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The word 'fiat' in fiat money comes from Latin and means","options":["backed by gold","made of paper","by agreement of the people","let it be done"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Fiat means 'let it be done', reflecting that fiat money has value because the government declares it legal tender, not because it is backed by a commodity. Option A describes representative money, which is the opposite of fiat money. Options B and C are not the meaning of the word, although paper currency can be fiat money."}},{"itemId":"7871d164-8e4a-5e58-ab29-8f07a8b14ad9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b79e38ce-997f-593f-a9b9-8d17c478c155","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The international link between currencies and gold effectively ended in 1971 when","options":["the Bank of England was established","Britain first adopted the gold standard","the United States suspended the convertibility of the dollar into gold","the first paper banknotes were issued in Europe"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"In 1971 the US stopped exchanging dollars for gold, which ended the Bretton Woods gold link and completed the move of the world to fiat currencies. Options A, B and D are events from much earlier in the history of money and banking, and none of them ended the gold standard."}},{"itemId":"f0c8cdb0-3c1e-509c-a6be-91163018528b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0d2ace73-1b86-5478-84ce-b62e92278faa","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Early issuers of paper money added watermarks, intricate designs, special paper and several official signatures to their notes. These features were introduced mainly to deal with the problem of","options":["overissuance","counterfeiting","debasement","double coincidence of wants"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Paper is easier to copy than metal, so security features such as watermarks, special paper and signatures were added to stop counterfeiting. Option A was a separate problem of printing too much paper money, which needed monetary discipline rather than better designs. Option C relates to the metal content of coins, and option D is a barter problem."}},{"itemId":"b5b64f79-bf3a-5fa6-89c5-7780c9d16987","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a26d7bc-8b5d-5f55-bc46-d21a0ea302cb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following shows the correct order in which money developed?","options":["Barter, commodity money, metallic money (coins), paper money, electronic money","Metallic money (coins), barter, commodity money, electronic money, paper money","Commodity money, barter, paper money, metallic money (coins), electronic money","Barter, paper money, commodity money, electronic money, metallic money (coins)"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money evolved from direct barter, to commodities with their own value, to standardised metal coins, then to paper money and finally to electronic and digital money, each stage solving problems of the one before. Options B, C and D all place at least one stage out of its proper order, for example putting paper money before coins or barter after money had appeared."}},{"itemId":"321a88c9-17db-56bd-a776-852b70987300","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a4eec3e7-bfd9-573d-82dd-d0e5ea1f26e8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Services such as M-Pesa in Kenya, which let people send, receive and store money on their mobile phones without a traditional bank account, have mainly","options":["returned those economies to the barter system","ended the use of the national currency as a unit of account","promoted financial inclusion by reaching previously unbanked people","restored the gold standard in developing countries"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Mobile money uses the phone as a platform for transfers and storage of value, bringing financial services to people who never had bank accounts, which is the essence of financial inclusion. Option A is wrong because mobile money is still money, not direct exchange of goods. Options B and D are false, since balances are still held in and priced in the national currency."}},{"itemId":"25541d13-4392-560f-8286-2b1cd80979b6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"072c8c3e-22e1-5a4b-80b6-87b286fcb8b1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes a cryptocurrency such as Bitcoin?","options":["Legal tender issued by a central bank and backed by gold reserves","A form of commodity money with intrinsic value as a metal","A decentralised digital currency built on blockchain technology and not controlled by any government or central bank","A paper certificate that can be exchanged for silver on demand"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Cryptocurrencies are digital, use blockchain and cryptography, and allow peer-to-peer payments with no central authority in control. Option A describes neither fiat money nor crypto correctly, and option D describes representative money. Option B is wrong because a cryptocurrency has no physical or metallic form."}},{"itemId":"3d9533c7-d27c-5c9f-8ce6-bd143df954ef","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"67bbbbf7-6ba0-514d-9492-2dca0a82208e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Pierce and Shaw distinguished an abstract from a tangible (concrete) definition of money. Under the abstract definition, money is","options":["whatever the law of the land declares to be legal tender","a unit of account, the common denominator in which the exchange value of all goods and services is expressed","a medium of exchange whose ownership is capable of changing hands","anything that serves as a temporary abode of purchasing power"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The abstract definition sees money as a measure of exchange value, the unit in which all prices are expressed. Option C is Pierce and Shaw's tangible or concrete definition. Option A is the legal definition of money, and option D is Milton Friedman's view."}},{"itemId":"3168e3fb-5724-5fa3-a10c-4db0a6176893","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3a03514c-152f-529b-aa0d-92b5c82b7831","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which school of thought on the definition of money holds that 'cheque is money and money is cheque', because most bank transactions are carried out with cheques?","options":["The currency school","The banking school","The barter school","The monetarist (Chicago) school"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"The banking school accepts the general definition of money but objects to leaving out bank cheques, since the bulk of deposit money bank transactions are settled by cheque. Option A limits money to legal tender currency such as naira and kobo. Option D, associated with Milton Friedman, defines money as a temporary abode of purchasing power, and option C is not a school of thought on money."}},{"itemId":"adfb2fbc-70a4-5532-a85a-6d44e7a45331","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"998c0b47-da6d-52d8-9597-c50243a8993e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Milton Friedman, often described as the leading monetarist, defined money as","options":["the table on which money changers carried on their trade","legal tender currency notes and coins only","a commodity with intrinsic value equal to its face value","anything that serves as a temporary abode of purchasing power"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Friedman's monetarist or Chicago view treats money as a temporary abode of purchasing power, which is broad enough to include cheques, drafts, a promissory note and other bills. Option B is the narrower currency school view. Option C describes standard or full-bodied commodity money, and option A refers to the origin of the word 'bank'."}},{"itemId":"8c11313a-67d2-5c20-ab12-4a1fcdaa6778","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38ad5c11-7018-5ae0-82e1-e56c2eddbbe6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Emeka, who works in Lagos, sends N30,000 every month to his parents in Kano, who use it to buy food there. The function of money mainly illustrated here is","options":["standard of deferred payment","store of value","transfer of value","unit of account"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Because money is generally acceptable and its ownership can pass from one person to another, it allows value to be moved from person to person and from one place to another, as Emeka does. Option D is about expressing prices in a common unit, and option A is about settling debts at a future date. Option B is about holding purchasing power over time rather than moving it."}},{"itemId":"23d9d15a-bb45-5129-93cb-1b5dcbd1bc09","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"96db80f7-5d02-5406-b86c-dfd76821014b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes money held as cash from near-money assets such as treasury bills?","options":["Cash is legal tender but earns no yield, while near-money assets earn a return but must first be converted, with some delay or cost, before they can be spent","Near-money assets are legal tender, while cash is not","Cash cannot be spent directly, while near-money assets are accepted everywhere as payment","Cash earns interest, while near-money assets earn nothing"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Money is legal tender, fully liquid and generally acceptable, but cash kept idle yields nothing; near money gives a return yet has to be turned into money first, which takes some time or cost. Option D reverses the yield difference. Options B and C reverse the points on legal status and acceptability."}},{"itemId":"fd110873-ae3d-5141-843e-f6ddeb6e7baa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5fd0fdb2-f04f-5e6c-ab90-cdfefc058575","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:MONW-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The characteristic of good money which requires that every unit of the currency can be identified quickly as genuine money without any technical assistance, so that no time is wasted inspecting it, is","options":["scarcity","durability","divisibility","recognisability"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Development of Money","explanation":"Recognisability means each unit of money can be easily and quickly recognised, which speeds up transactions between buyers and sellers. Option C is about dividing money into smaller units for small purchases, option A is about keeping supply limited so money keeps its value, and option B is about money lasting through repeated use."}},{"itemId":"3b152c0f-01c6-5de0-9cac-85f8ca054af5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"160eb703-2ed5-540e-a0bc-2846fb11051e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial institutions that are members of a clearing house, maintain accounts with the Central Bank and can participate directly in the clearing and settlement of cheques and other payment instruments are known as","options":["clearing banks","microfinance banks","development banks","merchant banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Clearing banks are the banks that belong to the clearing house and settle inter-bank claims through their accounts with the Central Bank. Option D and option C describe banks defined by wholesale or long-term development financing, not by clearing-house membership, and option B refers to small community-focused lenders."}},{"itemId":"30b16852-b7b5-5352-9fd3-f881fd4576e3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53cba21e-bb92-5e31-bdcf-3e3ed2cf77c7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a key characteristic of a clearing bank in Nigeria?","options":["It is subject to strict CBN supervision","It is a member of the clearing house","It maintains a settlement account with the CBN","It is restricted to a single office in one locality"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"A single office in one locality is the feature of a unit bank, not of a clearing bank, which typically runs a wide branch network. Options C, B and A are genuine features of clearing banks: settlement accounts at the CBN, clearing-house membership and strict regulatory supervision."}},{"itemId":"94891677-71a3-5c86-8fc1-20b4438c79b9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50aace31-00b8-5a02-b6bc-30654c699ef5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Clearing banks maintain settlement accounts with the Central Bank of Nigeria mainly in order to","options":["settle inter-bank fund transfers and net clearing positions","keep customers' valuables in safe custody","receive deposit insurance premiums from customers","issue currency notes on behalf of the CBN"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The settlement account at the CBN is where each clearing bank's net position after clearing is debited or credited, so inter-bank obligations are settled there. Option B is a service banks give customers, option D is the CBN's own function, and option C wrongly describes deposit insurance, which is handled by the NDIC."}},{"itemId":"f25d56ab-9c7d-516a-a914-63a06658abb2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ed1740cd-f883-5606-834c-c33230f91d6d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The organizational structure of a clearing bank is commonly examined at three levels. These are the","options":["front office, middle office and back office levels","strategic, tactical and clerical levels","governance, management and operational levels","head office, branch and agency levels"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The three-tier view is governance (the board), management (executive management) and operational (departments and units). Option A is only the way the operational level is divided into front, middle and back office, and option D describes the branch network rather than the whole structure."}},{"itemId":"f506a04f-db83-5f2a-88ec-4fe5418ee10e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7fb21336-d53f-5f75-a0d8-17c81d33ebd8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The organ that sits at the apex of a clearing bank's structure and is responsible for its overall strategic direction and governance is the","options":["Internal Audit Department","Board of Directors","Executive Management Committee","Annual General Meeting of depositors"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The Board of Directors is at the top of the bank's organizational structure and sets strategy and governance. Option C is the management level that implements the board's policies, option A is an operational control unit, and option D is not an organ of a bank at all, since depositors are creditors rather than owners."}},{"itemId":"26574167-5818-5081-afab-4abe9ab273eb","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2d379c9b-8ed9-5c37-9ef9-02c7a1aec7f6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the board of directors of a clearing bank?","options":["Approving annual budgets and financial statements","Approving the risk management framework","Appointing and overseeing senior management","Processing customers' account-opening forms"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Processing account-opening forms is routine transaction work done by the operations department in the back office. Options B, C and A are all board functions: approving the risk framework, appointing and overseeing senior management, and approving budgets and financial statements."}},{"itemId":"1bfc0cf9-6474-579e-a416-a9e8d0d54ff5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"584bd0f3-a077-5768-8c6c-545aa31e16c0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the typical board composition of a Nigerian clearing bank, the Board Chairman is","options":["a non-executive director","the Chief Financial Officer","the Managing Director/Chief Executive Officer","the most senior Executive Director"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The chairman leads the board but is non-executive, keeping the oversight role separate from the day-to-day management led by the MD/CEO. Option C is wrong because the MD/CEO is the top executive and sits on the board as a separate member, while options B and D are executive roles."}},{"itemId":"ba8b4b2b-a52a-58c9-a0d4-eb64ba4c6541","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"db123f8c-85a7-5c53-b9ea-5ac1423a2e19","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The board of Zenmark Bank Plc wants a committee to oversee financial reporting, internal controls and the work of the external auditors. This is the","options":["IT Governance Committee","Audit Committee","Remuneration Committee","Credit Committee"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The Audit Committee oversees financial reporting, internal controls and the external audit process. Option D deals with approving large loans and credit policy, option C with executive pay, and option A with technology strategy and cybersecurity."}},{"itemId":"59a5a84b-f957-56f5-8276-aeea4efdf289","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cae07a01-212e-51bb-90d9-ff2f120bffec","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A N25 billion facility requested by a cement manufacturer exceeds the approval limit of executive management and is sent to a board committee that reviews and approves large credit facilities and sets credit policies. The committee is the","options":["Credit Committee","Risk Management Committee","Governance and Nomination Committee","Audit Committee"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The board Credit Committee reviews and approves large credit facilities and sets the bank's credit policy. Option B is tempting because credit risk is a risk, but that committee monitors all categories of risk rather than approving individual loans; options D and C handle audit and board appointments."}},{"itemId":"279d8025-34e5-5c03-b0e2-a3b3ca8b1de1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"711dd1bd-faf8-50de-a8f6-46e1bd60a5e4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Remuneration Committee of a bank's board is mainly responsible for","options":["overseeing cybersecurity and digital transformation","monitoring all categories of risk facing the bank","determining compensation packages for executive management","handling board appointments and succession planning"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The Remuneration Committee decides the pay and compensation packages of executive management. Option D belongs to the Governance and Nomination Committee, option B to the Risk Management Committee, and option A to the IT Governance Committee."}},{"itemId":"fccd1f65-bc61-581c-a663-5d502480c8e9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"51c52377-95ae-565a-ab6e-996ea81e8aa4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The chairman of a bank is due to retire next year, and a board committee is asked to identify a successor and review board membership. This task falls to the","options":["Audit Committee","Governance and Nomination Committee","Remuneration Committee","Credit Committee"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Board appointments, succession planning and governance matters are handled by the Governance and Nomination Committee. Option C is tempting because it deals with directors' pay, but it does not choose who sits on the board; options A and D deal with audit and lending."}},{"itemId":"4ecfde88-a4ee-5322-8cb2-d0487d1692c9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c5d4de85-f1be-5add-b402-afbe1252b582","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The board committee of a clearing bank that oversees technology strategy, cybersecurity and digital transformation is the","options":["Risk Management Committee","Executive Committee","IT Governance Committee","Audit Committee"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The IT Governance Committee is the board committee that oversees technology strategy, cybersecurity and digital transformation. Option A is tempting since cyber risk is a risk, but that committee monitors risk across all categories; option D covers financial reporting and controls, and option B is a management body, not one of the listed board committees."}},{"itemId":"d3408710-452e-557d-a4f8-4b0eba48c373","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"82b9a321-af1e-578c-9173-319ae64f42bb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best distinguishes the board of directors from executive management in a clearing bank?","options":["The board sets strategy and oversees; executive management runs the bank day to day","The board is appointed by the CBN; executive management is elected by depositors","The board handles cheque clearing; executive management handles shareholders","The board runs the branches; executive management approves the annual accounts"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The board provides strategic direction and oversight, while executive management handles the day-to-day running of the bank and implements board policies. Option D reverses the roles, and options C and B are wrong because clearing is an operations task and neither the CBN nor depositors appoint these organs."}},{"itemId":"b3e69fc2-5bfc-5f64-af78-158c5962959d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"59e1db01-f490-554c-8a4e-e80be60f93ac","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The highest-ranking executive officer of a clearing bank, who implements board decisions, leads the management team and represents the bank externally, is the","options":["Deputy Managing Director","Managing Director/Chief Executive Officer","Company Secretary","Board Chairman"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The MD/CEO is the top executive officer, responsible for the overall management of the bank and for carrying out the board's decisions. Option D is tempting, but the chairman is non-executive and leads the board, not management; option A assists the MD/CEO and option C gives legal and governance support."}},{"itemId":"be363dc7-366d-59e6-b071-54cc11999f67","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a8982624-238c-55f4-8aa2-cf5de922e514","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"While the MD/CEO of a bank is on a two-week trip abroad, the executive who normally assists the MD/CEO and acts in his absence will be the","options":["Board Chairman","Chief Operations Officer","Deputy Managing Director","Chief Compliance Officer"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The Deputy Managing Director assists the MD/CEO and acts in his absence, often while overseeing specific business units. Option A is tempting, but the chairman is non-executive and does not step into the executive role, while options B and D head operations and compliance respectively."}},{"itemId":"14e1176d-8d99-59a2-9cdc-1200b48af566","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"16e239f3-ddfc-50bb-a39e-6642042ebf7e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Foreign exchange operations, the investment portfolio and money market activities of a clearing bank are overseen by the","options":["Executive Director, Treasury and Financial Markets","Executive Director, Retail Banking","Executive Director, Commercial Banking","Chief Compliance Officer"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Treasury and Financial Markets handles foreign exchange, the investment portfolio, treasury operations and money market dealings. Option B manages branches and consumer lending, option C oversees corporate, commercial and SME banking, and option D is a control role for regulatory compliance."}},{"itemId":"0b3a684c-94ad-5930-a2f6-b2e5bcd15a02","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c30bd763-d53d-50e4-a6d9-1ab441655f24","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which executive of a clearing bank is responsible for financial planning, accounting, financial reporting and investor relations?","options":["Chief Risk Officer","Chief Operations Officer","Company Secretary/Legal Adviser","Chief Financial Officer"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The Chief Financial Officer manages financial planning, accounting, reporting and relations with investors. Option A oversees credit, market and operational risk, option B oversees service delivery and process efficiency, and option C provides legal counsel and handles regulatory filings."}},{"itemId":"d48904df-a8cb-5b94-a3e9-8f24d23a32bd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e0756e9d-95c3-5688-866f-e1a811bb82fb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kemi is the officer who heads her bank's anti-money laundering and know-your-customer programmes and makes sure the bank obeys regulatory requirements. Her title is most likely","options":["Chief Technology Officer","Chief Risk Officer","Chief Financial Officer","Chief Compliance Officer"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Ensuring regulatory compliance, AML and KYC programmes is the role of the Chief Compliance Officer. Option B is the closest distractor, but the CRO oversees enterprise, credit, market and operational risk rather than AML/KYC; options A and C handle technology and finance."}},{"itemId":"f5b5d069-941e-5cc7-ba15-4172b58bb38d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"36c18f5f-6125-5a99-9651-3e6406b78126","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a clearing bank, the Chief Risk Officer (CRO) is responsible for","options":["branch network operations and consumer lending","enterprise risk management, including credit, market and operational risk","the core banking system and digital banking platforms","board appointments and succession planning"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The CRO oversees enterprise-wide risk management, covering credit, market and operational risk. Option C is the job of the CTO/CIO, option D belongs to a board committee, and option A is the Retail Banking executive's portfolio."}},{"itemId":"4083582c-c37e-5fd9-bdf3-6defc3813817","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b202ca93-c070-53b6-926d-2c3a9bb6e132","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a clearing bank's operational structure, front office operations are best described as","options":["units that process transactions and settle payments","units that recruit staff and manage facilities","revenue-generating units that deal directly with customers","units that check risk and enforce internal control"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Front office divisions such as retail, corporate and treasury are the revenue-generating units that win and serve customers. Option D describes the middle office (support and control), option A the back office (processing and settlement), and option B the support functions."}},{"itemId":"981d361f-7e63-5290-97f4-bbd3bd7f3422","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"64088e91-3325-5c22-b67b-8e978c678ff9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a front office (revenue-generating) division of a clearing bank?","options":["Corporate Banking Division","Internal Audit Department","Private Banking/Wealth Management","Treasury Division"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Internal Audit is a middle office (support and control) department that independently reviews controls; it does not generate revenue. Options A, D and C are all front office divisions that earn income from corporate clients, markets and high net-worth individuals."}},{"itemId":"26b90ee6-f085-571a-9895-87256ac41064","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"54c79056-22e2-598b-892e-7b3378c518e1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank's Risk Management, Compliance, Internal Audit and Credit Administration departments are grouped together as","options":["back office (processing and settlement)","board committees","front office (revenue generating)","middle office (support and control)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"These departments form the middle office, whose job is support and control: analysing risk, enforcing compliance, auditing and administering credit. Option C covers customer-facing earning divisions, option A covers transaction processing, and option B is wrong because these are departments, not committees of the board."}},{"itemId":"a3afc807-b59f-5699-8a0e-bf39f21f0d06","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"390e48f0-9ebe-591f-84be-375574a38ad5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Operations Department, the Payment and Settlement Department and the Reconciliation Unit of a clearing bank belong to the","options":["back office","front office","middle office","governance level"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"These units carry out processing and settlement, which is the work of the back office. Option B is the revenue-generating side, option C is the control side, and option D refers to the board."}},{"itemId":"988053af-6944-5510-a5cc-7f613e1132aa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8203d1e0-18c3-5229-a5d1-15d070d04789","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A unit in a bank spends its day matching entries on its nostro and vostro accounts and clearing items stuck in suspense accounts. This unit is the","options":["Reconciliation Unit","Treasury Division","Credit Administration","Trade Services"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Account reconciliation, nostro/vostro account management and suspense account resolution are the work of the Reconciliation Unit in the back office. Option B is tempting because treasury trades foreign currency, but it deals rather than reconciles; options C and D handle loan documentation and letters of credit."}},{"itemId":"fd16c630-dce6-5625-b5d7-668a264c091b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf10df55-a5de-5b2f-8ae1-89438590ec51","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An importer of generators in Kano asks her bank to process a letter of credit and handle the related trade documents. In a clearing bank's structure this work is done by","options":["Trade Services in the back office","Marketing and Corporate Communications","the Internal Audit Department","the Remuneration Committee"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Letter of credit processing, bills for collection and trade documentation are handled by Trade Services, a back office unit. Option C reviews controls, option D sets executive pay, and option B manages the brand and public relations."}},{"itemId":"b214e438-27f1-573a-853e-5af3ddb8f206","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1dc08fc9-6fea-5ab2-9e60-35f041d7361a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Adeyemi, a high net-worth businessman, wants investment advisory and estate planning from his bank. He will be served by the bank's","options":["Reconciliation Unit","Commercial Banking Division","Private Banking/Wealth Management division","SME Banking Division"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Private Banking/Wealth Management serves high net-worth individuals with investment advice and estate planning. Option D serves small businesses and micro-finance, option B serves medium-sized enterprises, and option A is a back office unit that never deals with such clients."}},{"itemId":"56d72994-bbb1-5e77-acee-7445324ab0f2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7164c16f-38c9-5eb5-9dcb-fbea3ca2ff38","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mergers and acquisitions advisory, capital raising and underwriting are services of a bank's","options":["Retail Banking Division","Operations Department","Treasury Division","Investment Banking Division"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Investment Banking provides M&A advice, capital raising and underwriting of securities. Option C is tempting since treasury deals in securities, but it focuses on foreign exchange, money market and asset-liability management; option A serves individuals and option B processes transactions."}},{"itemId":"3092cd55-4101-5de2-8d93-3dcc87d43c50","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e5432d4-7cc6-5d8b-81b9-451f0f99827b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Credit appraisal, loan documentation, collateral management and credit monitoring are the functions of","options":["Credit Administration","the Corporate Banking Division","the Legal Department","the Customer Service Department"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Credit Administration, a middle office unit, appraises credit, prepares loan documents, manages collateral and monitors loans. Option B markets and books loans to large corporates but does not control them, option C reviews contracts and recovers debts, and option D handles enquiries and complaints."}},{"itemId":"5fb7ab9c-3c91-50cd-a55f-8485fc9d5f9b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a5e4a19-c7bb-58c9-8db4-e1075cc0dadb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a support function in a clearing bank?","options":["Human Resources Department","Payment and Settlement Department","Procurement/General Services","Marketing and Corporate Communications"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The Payment and Settlement Department, which runs clearing-house operations and inter-bank settlements, is a back office processing unit. Options A, C and D are support functions that serve the whole bank through staffing, vendor and facilities management, and brand management."}},{"itemId":"786927dc-a10d-50aa-a692-6ffb52b7df1e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"200d17e7-9849-54c8-999c-9c544a70a27c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Arranged from the top down, the hierarchical branch network of a typical Nigerian clearing bank is","options":["head office, regional/zonal office, area office, branch","regional office, head office, branch, area office","branch, area office, regional office, head office","head office, branch, area office, regional office"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The head office sits at the top, regional/zonal offices oversee clusters of branches within zones, area/divisional offices supervise smaller clusters within a region, and branches serve customers. Option C reverses the order (bottom up), and options B and D jumble the levels."}},{"itemId":"dbc9a8c4-8edc-5e55-9730-7c0eb6ab5617","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f6a2ec4d-08c4-57dc-8fb9-359c223e2487","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"To reach traders in a village in Ebonyi State with no bank branch, a bank appoints a local shop owner to offer basic deposit and withdrawal services on its behalf. This arrangement is","options":["agency banking","unit banking","correspondent banking","chain banking"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Using third-party agents to provide basic banking services in underserved areas is agency banking, which extends reach beyond traditional branches. Option C is a relationship between two banks, option D is control of several banks by the same persons, and option B is an independent one-office bank."}},{"itemId":"4c809cbb-f6ef-5412-8327-62754a2fc7e8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"738bf0c1-1187-5491-8f42-1a4dd01b9560","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a clearing bank's branch network, a regional or zonal office","options":["houses the executive management and central departments","oversees clusters of branches in a geographical zone and reports to head office","is an individual outlet serving customers directly","is a third-party outlet in an underserved area"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"A regional/zonal office supervises a group of branches within a zone, such as a Lagos or Northern zone, and its regional manager reports to head office. Option A describes the head office, option D agency banking, and option C a branch."}},{"itemId":"7c0d5107-8fa1-5e4a-89a6-a0817869ad7e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"790d8112-edcc-51d9-95a5-26de7c22509d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In organisational structure, line authority in a bank refers to","options":["the right of specialists to advise managers without power to command","authority given to outside consultants and auditors","direct authority passed down the chain of command over people who carry out the bank's main business","authority held only by the board's committees"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Line authority is the direct, superior-subordinate authority that runs down the chain of command, for example from the MD/CEO through regional managers to branch managers, over the people doing the bank's core business. Option A describes staff authority, which is advisory, while options D and B wrongly confine it to committees or outsiders."}},{"itemId":"c83b75d7-af89-5f11-9ef9-a918cabdc865","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"34de7abf-9cc4-5041-9694-3041c5065814","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Legal Department of Union Star Bank reviews a branch's loan contract and advises the branch manager, but cannot order the branch to grant or refuse the loan. The Legal Department here is acting as a","options":["board committee","line function","settlement bank","staff function"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Staff departments such as Legal, Human Resources and Research support and advise line managers but do not command them. Option B is wrong because a line function holds direct authority over the bank's main operations, while options A and C are unrelated organs."}},{"itemId":"6798d9a3-129c-5c51-85aa-f13e680f94da","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1e01dc43-3869-50e6-9ccf-b6da1ed778af","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following officers holds a LINE position in a clearing bank?","options":["A branch manager","The head of the legal advisory unit","An economic research analyst at head office","A human resources training officer"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"A branch manager sits in the direct chain of command running the bank's core business of taking deposits and lending, so the post is a line position. Options B, D and C are staff positions that give specialist advice and services to line managers."}},{"itemId":"f153da53-e25e-5e84-afda-78d15673438e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4979d1cc-7be5-5697-b788-c94537dd1e84","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The officer who has overall responsibility for running a bank branch, including business development, customer relations and approving loans within delegated limits, is the","options":["company secretary","branch manager","teller","regional internal auditor"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The branch manager heads the branch, is accountable to the area or regional office, and grants advances within the discretionary limits head office allows. Option C handles cash receipts and payments at the counter, option D reviews controls independently, and option A is a head office governance role."}},{"itemId":"bb926be6-5756-57aa-a7ac-4c28c2f52840","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd649cfb-d010-540a-8512-4b6646f5e6dc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a centralised bank, decision-making authority is","options":["held by third-party agents in rural areas","shared equally among all banks in the clearing house","spread to branch managers, who decide most matters locally","concentrated at the head office, with branches referring major decisions upward"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Centralisation means authority is concentrated at the top, so branches refer major matters, such as large loans, to head office. Option C describes decentralisation, and options A and B do not describe internal authority within one bank."}},{"itemId":"6b289f7a-58f3-50ca-86f9-3e1679ac72ee","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"80cddb39-b3d3-56fa-a382-c9b87adc02d5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A branch manager in Ibadan must refer every loan request, however small, to head office in Lagos, and customers complain about weeks of delay. This problem shows a weakness of","options":["the unit banking system","agency banking","excessive decentralisation of authority","excessive centralisation of authority"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"When all lending decisions sit at head office, branch managers lack authority to respond quickly and delays follow, which is a known drawback of centralised branch banking. Option C would give branches more local discretion and cut delays, while options A and B do not involve a head office controlling branches."}},{"itemId":"7fdb29ff-ed44-51e7-ada4-a8ee7652f541","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb0d1a6c-67eb-5fed-ae30-df173447840f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an advantage of decentralising authority to branch managers?","options":["Elimination of the need for internal audit","Removal of the need for a clearing house","Tighter uniform control by head office","Faster decisions that respond to local customers' needs"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Decentralisation lets branch managers decide promptly and tailor decisions to local conditions. Option C is the strength of centralisation, not decentralisation, and options A and B are false because audit and clearing are still needed whatever the structure."}},{"itemId":"fdc32634-790f-5ae0-b567-2c990644b71f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f3a48dcd-1ddb-5867-9383-802b4170b93d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A trend now seen in the organisation of clearing banks is the consolidation of back-office work into centralised processing centres and shared service models. The main aim is","options":["ending membership of the clearing house","replacing the board with branch managers","efficiency","creating more board committees"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Consolidating back-office operations into central processing centres and shared services is done for efficiency, so processing is not duplicated in every branch. Options D, B and A are not the purpose of this trend."}},{"itemId":"47769678-d716-5c1b-a78a-b898dce669b1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"19931974-7713-5b3e-a28d-7f05d8ad0c8b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A banking system in which each bank operates as a separate entity, usually from a single office, with its own independent management and its operations restricted to one locality, is","options":["group banking","chain banking","branch banking","unit banking"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Unit banks are independent, single-office banks confined to a particular locality. Option C uses a network of branches under one bank, while options A and B involve two or more banks under common control."}},{"itemId":"828dcce8-08ab-5b70-987f-16cfc2d27bb0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"12c2965b-ceff-5e32-8258-d94c2b5b82ac","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a merit of unit banking?","options":["It helps to prevent monopoly banking","It can easily grant very large loans","It attracts funds from many distant regions","It spreads risks across the whole country"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Because there are many small independent banks, unit banking helps to prevent monopoly in banking. Option B is a demerit in reverse, since unit banks have limited resources, while options D and C are merits of branch banking."}},{"itemId":"98532881-6373-509b-bc44-8338093e421b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"83a6acae-342d-5fda-8977-c48dbc15b698","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a demerit of unit banking?","options":["Close personal relationship with local customers","Difficulty in meeting a sudden rush of withdrawals","Dependence on correspondent banks for remittance of funds","Inability to grant large loans"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"A close personal relationship with local customers is a merit of unit banking, as it helps the bank mobilise funds. Options B, D and C are genuine demerits that come from the small size and single location of unit banks."}},{"itemId":"63be2114-c1e4-5f4a-9fcb-5e467de29247","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38f9b592-5b6b-5239-ac76-e0edabcf5c47","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nigeria's commercial banks, such as those operating hundreds of outlets across the 36 states and the FCT under one head office, practise","options":["unit banking","chain banking","branch banking","mixed banking"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"A single bank operating through a nationwide network of branches is branch banking, the system Nigeria practises. Option A is the single-office system popular in the USA, option B involves several separate banks controlled by the same persons, and option D refers to banks granting long-term industrial finance."}},{"itemId":"c934a41c-b61e-5fa6-9afe-bc8f6b1172e4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"42a53743-d239-5ff2-8cbc-6432de19e1a5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bank X suffers loan losses in its branches in a flood-hit state, but profits from its branches in other states cover the losses. This illustrates which merit of branch banking?","options":["Freedom from holding company control","Quick local decision-making","Geographical spreading of risk","Prevention of monopoly banking"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Under branch banking, risks are spread geographically, so losses in one area can be offset by profits made in others. Option D and option B are merits claimed for unit banking, and option A is unrelated to the scenario."}},{"itemId":"fc8fc328-40d1-5aa1-bd19-af5068ddaf96","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"44f293dd-9d36-5a10-ac50-c7b2b87b9026","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under branch banking, a branch does not need to keep large cash reserves for emergencies mainly because","options":["the CBN guarantees every branch's withdrawals","customers must give notice before every withdrawal","cash can be quickly mobilised from sister branches","branches are forbidden to pay out cash"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"A branch can draw cash quickly from sister branches of the same bank, so it can operate without holding large idle reserves. Options A, D and B are not features of branch banking and are simply false."}},{"itemId":"02734d57-4889-5719-9ca1-fdde563b83e8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8f1b06dc-c767-5721-8931-b53dd7cb9155","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a demerit of branch banking?","options":["It makes efficient central bank control impossible","It restricts savings facilities to one locality","It prevents risks from being spread geographically","It can create oligopolistic and bureaucratic conditions in banking"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Branch banking concentrates banking in a few large banks whose branches are controlled by head office, which can produce oligopoly and bureaucracy. Option C and option A contradict its merits of spreading risk and allowing efficient central bank control, and option B describes unit banking."}},{"itemId":"6dcc0750-263b-5da1-94b6-29434c933faa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6b19b904-88eb-5237-9bca-1d3b19b7bda4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Multiple-office banking in which two or more banks, each keeping its separate name, are controlled by a holding company is","options":["chain banking","group banking","branch banking","correspondent banking"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Group banking places two or more operating banks under a holding (parent) company, while each bank keeps its own identity. Option A is the closest, but in chain banking control is held by individuals through shares, not by a holding company; options C and D involve a single bank with branches or a deposit relationship between banks."}},{"itemId":"30849447-5148-5d89-90d8-be8f3fd6975a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c4fb5f6-a2f3-55c1-b66d-4f932f312940","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under group banking, the banks controlled by the holding (parent) company are called","options":["operating banks","unit banks","respondent banks","settlement banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The banks under the holding company in group banking are called operating banks, and each keeps its separate name. Option C is the small bank in a correspondent relationship, option D is a clearing term for banks that settle on behalf of others, and option B is an independent single-office bank."}},{"itemId":"fa0df329-336b-5f37-9a63-17d8fb7394b4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f992ce1-b417-564f-ba6d-ff9e08d968db","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a demerit of group banking?","options":["It may lead to monopoly","The holding company may divert surplus reserves to its non-banking interests","It allows operating banks to pool resources to finance large borrowings","The failure of one operating bank can affect others in the group"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Pooling resources to finance large borrowings is a merit of group banking. Options D, B and A are genuine demerits: contagion from a failing operating bank, diversion of funds by the holding company, and the risk of monopoly."}},{"itemId":"87a8f54c-e586-5c5f-a872-ea8dbdf50fab","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45fd3603-dfa3-58c4-ae26-d43fa6436894","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chief Okonkwo and his two brothers personally own controlling shares in three separate banks and exercise control through their proxies, without any holding company. This is","options":["branch banking","unit banking","chain banking","group banking"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Chain banking is control of two or more banks by the same individual or group of individuals through stock ownership, not through a holding company. Option D requires a holding company, option A is one bank with many branches, and option B is one independent bank."}},{"itemId":"117400b8-0791-5dd8-bbb8-bd374c478ec8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4ee6d67c-cac4-514e-b0ba-8e695e916ddb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main difference between group banking and chain banking is that","options":["group banks share one name, while chain banks keep separate names","group banking involves one bank, while chain banking involves many banks","group banking is found only in England, while chain banking is found only in Nigeria","group banking uses a holding company, while chain banking is control by individuals through share ownership"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Both involve control of two or more banks, but group banking works through a holding company, while chain banking is control by one person or group of persons through owning stock. Option A is wrong because operating banks in a group keep separate names, option B is wrong because both involve several banks, and option C is false since both are common in the USA."}},{"itemId":"6fcffa43-c8d9-551f-a6a8-f35ae1ec0ee7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"08698256-2a18-5b1e-a849-9a318c9103fe","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In correspondent banking, the small bank in a small community that keeps deposits with a large city bank is called the","options":["operating bank","respondent bank","correspondent bank","settlement bank"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The small bank holding deposits with the city bank is the respondent bank, while the large city bank that holds those deposits is the correspondent bank. Option C reverses the two, option A is a group banking term, and option D is a clearing term."}},{"itemId":"0fd6cd51-6827-5822-a703-984f01280701","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"33dcf8eb-8a02-5259-9cb3-4663d9e11118","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Correspondent banking is best described as a mechanism that","options":["places two or more banks under one holding company","replaces the central bank as lender of last resort","lets a bank lend long-term to start-up industries","knits unit banks together through deposit relationships with larger banks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Correspondent banking links independent unit banks into a network by having them keep deposits with larger city banks, so every bank is ultimately connected. Option A is group banking, option C is mixed banking, and option B is not a function of any commercial bank."}},{"itemId":"1f153972-923e-5fe3-aa51-db6303b1554e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1c9a0009-f6cf-5a65-9d95-1e0c51df43cf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The process of obtaining values for non-in-house cheques among deposit money banks, usually carried out at the clearing house of the CBN or its designated branches, is called","options":["agency banking","the clearing system","loan syndication","reconciliation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Clearing is the process by which banks exchange cheques drawn on one another and obtain value for them, traditionally at the CBN's clearing house. Option A is the use of third-party agents, option C is joint lending by banks, and option D is matching account entries."}},{"itemId":"17e55b12-2841-5933-aebb-54042ad10cb0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d941bc41-7863-5f95-8ca0-2abfcc9c23dc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the cheque clearing process of a clearing bank, which step comes immediately after branches collect cheques deposited by customers?","options":["Sorting the cheques by paying bank into on-us and other-bank items","Settlement of net positions at the CBN","Holding the clearing session at the clearing house","Notifying customers of returned items"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"After collection, cheques are sorted by paying bank so that on-us cheques are handled internally and those on other banks go to clearing. Options B, D and C all come later: the clearing session, settlement, and customer notification are the final stages."}},{"itemId":"bebfbab2-1d8a-57b4-a580-31aec5b1eeb8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd6fd526-42d5-58da-880a-7114cae00ef7","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Tunde pays into his account at Bank A a cheque drawn by his customer on Bank B. During sorting, the cheque will be treated as","options":["an item for the Reconciliation Unit to hold in suspense","an item to be presented through the clearing house","an on-us cheque settled internally","a bill for collection handled by Trade Services"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"A cheque drawn on another bank is not an on-us item, so it is presented to the clearing house for exchange with the paying bank. Option C applies only when the paying bank is the same as the collecting bank, while options D and A describe unrelated units."}},{"itemId":"1ab60eb4-b223-52eb-9788-01322c75e789","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b5a3d3c9-0de8-596a-898b-b2c881d41474","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"After a clearing session, the clearing banks settle with one another by","options":["paying each cheque separately in cash at each other's branches","settling net positions through their settlement accounts at the CBN","having the NDIC pay the net debtor banks","sending each other's customers bank drafts"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Clearing is multilateral: after the session each bank's net position is worked out and settled through its settlement account at the CBN. Option A ignores netting, option D is not how inter-bank settlement works, and option C confuses the deposit insurer with the settlement system."}},{"itemId":"fb839de7-2ad6-5a67-aa2a-d84bed7c569d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6e683452-a0e3-5165-a48d-e601f6a77202","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the clearing process, 'return items' are","options":["unpaid cheques sent back through the clearing system with reasons","cheques drawn on the collecting bank itself","cheques that the collecting bank has refused to accept for deposit","funds sent by the CBN to settle net positions"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Return items are cheques the paying bank declines to pay, which go back through clearing with the reason stated, after which the customer is told. Option B describes on-us cheques, option D describes settlement, and option C never enters clearing at all."}},{"itemId":"c0a82f4b-dba5-529a-afed-30d064a29835","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a03f8efe-c4d8-5da1-a984-431ba984b103","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Magnetic Ink Character Recognition (MICR) technology was introduced into Nigeria's payments system mainly to","options":["allow banks to keep accounts outside Nigeria","speed up machine processing of cheques and shorten clearing time","insure deposits in failed banks","replace cheques entirely with cash"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"MICR codes printed on cheques let machines read and sort them, facilitating document processing, shortening the clearing period and boosting public confidence. Options D, C and A are not aims of MICR; deposit insurance, for instance, is the NDIC's work."}},{"itemId":"e9fb6473-2ec9-5cf5-948b-2d253603bdea","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa75731f-3f88-59b0-a82a-78de5b381cea","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the core objectives of the Nigerian Automated Clearing System (NACS)?","options":["To significantly reduce the clearing float","To encourage the development of other electronic payment products","To abolish the use of cheques in Nigeria","To maintain and enforce standards for clearing procedures"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"NACS was meant to process cheques more efficiently, not to abolish them. Options A, D and B are genuine NACS objectives, together with cost-effective processing of clearing items based on electronic data presentation."}},{"itemId":"5f1205f4-a022-5bf6-a0e6-05dbc640dd58","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"99e95bb2-8e75-5619-978a-5b97be6b9bcc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Nigerian Automated Clearing System, an indirect participating bank differs from a direct participating bank in that it","options":["does not need any account with the CBN","manages the NACS infrastructure","takes part in clearing through a direct participating bank","transmits clearing data straight to the Zonal Clearing House"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"An indirect participating bank clears through a direct participating bank instead of sending data to the clearing house itself. Option D describes a direct participant, option B is the role of the Zonal Clearing House, and option A is wrong because both types kept settlement accounts with the CBN."}},{"itemId":"6e956850-4e37-55d0-9969-760e5f193171","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"83a128d2-41fb-500e-9bb7-612c6f291ddf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the settlement framework the CBN introduced in April 2004, a bank that clears and settles cheques on behalf of other banks is called a","options":["unit bank","respondent bank","operating bank","settlement bank"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"A settlement bank meets the conditions for direct participation and clears cheques on behalf of others, with non-settlement banks posting through it under an agency arrangement. Option B is a correspondent banking term, option A is a single-office bank, and option C is a group banking term."}},{"itemId":"59a766e7-e5d0-5aa6-a746-524344d56a90","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0874d66a-3215-5c91-ad85-b6838370ba11","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-065","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the April 2004 settlement framework, non-settlement banks were required to","options":["deposit clearing collateral directly with the CBN","stop clearing cheques altogether","keep only operations accounts with the CBN and clear through an agency arrangement with settlement banks","keep settlement accounts with the CBN and clear for other banks"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Non-settlement banks kept only operations accounts at the CBN and had their clearing balances posted to a settlement bank's account under an agency agreement. Option D describes settlement banks, option A is wrong because only settlement banks deposited collateral directly with the CBN, and option B is simply false."}},{"itemId":"6a68c476-b9ea-5b71-8958-3dee6eb833b6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"259dc3d0-872d-5fee-9b6f-1914652728d3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-066","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which electronic payment system in Nigeria lets a customer transfer funds to an account in another bank instantly, and is run by the Nigeria Inter-Bank Settlement System (NIBSS)?","options":["RTGS (Real-Time Gross Settlement)","MICR (Magnetic Ink Character Recognition)","NEFT (NIBSS Electronic Funds Transfer)","NIP (NIBSS Instant Payment)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"NIP is the NIBSS instant electronic funds transfer system. Option A is the real-time system for individual large-value payments operated by the CBN, option C processes transfers in batches rather than instantly, and option B is a cheque-reading technology, not a transfer system."}},{"itemId":"053e9e28-4920-536a-a8ad-7b6022543cfa","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2c875548-6f6d-55b4-8ed0-20d4841e469c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-067","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The key difference between RTGS and NEFT as used by Nigerian clearing banks is that","options":["RTGS is run by agents, while NEFT is run by the NDIC","RTGS settles individual large-value payments in real time, while NEFT processes transfers in batches","RTGS is for cheques only, while NEFT is for cash only","RTGS processes transfers in batches, while NEFT settles each payment in real time"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Real-Time Gross Settlement handles each large-value payment individually and immediately, while NEFT is a batch-processing transfer system. Option D swaps the two, and options C and A describe neither system correctly."}},{"itemId":"7eacc7f2-1408-5d5d-9e34-3694ce7696d9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d61c150f-0efa-5371-9170-f2f1a427e364","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-068","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The cheque truncation system introduced in Nigeria in 2012 is","options":["a rule limiting the value of cheques customers can write","the manual exchange of cheques by bank representatives at the clearing house","a system that stops banks from issuing cheque books","electronic image-based cheque clearing that reduces the physical movement of cheques"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"Under cheque truncation, the physical cheque stops at the collecting bank and its electronic image and data are used for clearing, so cheques no longer have to be moved around. Option B describes the old manual clearing it replaced, while options C and A are not what truncation means."}},{"itemId":"4738acc5-4c05-5917-befb-7fe5386c328f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57906522-1625-5263-a1f1-0ff0a2026ef9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:ORG-069","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the regulatory framework for Nigerian clearing banks, the body that insures deposits and handles the resolution of failed banks is the","options":["Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN)","Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC)","Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)","Nigeria Inter-Bank Settlement System (NIBSS)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Organizational Structure of Clearing Banks","explanation":"The NDIC insures deposits, takes part in bank supervision and handles failed bank resolution. Option A is the primary regulator that issues licences and prudential guidelines, option D runs inter-bank payment and clearing infrastructure, and option B registers companies."}},{"itemId":"6c88104f-57b0-59d3-92cb-645d7577359a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fc57a851-f07b-5e93-971e-e4d55bcbe310","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An instruction given by a customer to his bank to make regular payments of a fixed amount, at weekly, monthly or quarterly intervals, from his account to a named third party is a:","options":["direct debit","standing order","bank draft","banker's payment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A standing order is the customer's own instruction to his bank to pay a stated amount to a named beneficiary at regular intervals. Option A is a direct debit, which is started by the beneficiary rather than the customer. Option C is a single bank instrument for one payment, and option D is used by banks to pay each other."}},{"itemId":"0307eb98-4b26-578a-a24c-d53130d5c83e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"80fe8f9b-84fb-5bff-afef-6ed5268e97f6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Okafor instructs her bank in Surulere to pay N45,000 to her landlord's account on the first day of every month until further notice. This arrangement is a:","options":["direct debit","credit transfer by bank giro","bill for collection","standing order"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Mrs Okafor, the payer, gave her own bank a continuing instruction to pay a fixed sum at fixed dates, which is exactly a standing order. Option A would be wrong because in a direct debit the landlord, not Mrs Okafor, would send the claims to the bank. Option B is a one-off transfer made by filling credit slips, and option C relates to collecting bills of exchange."}},{"itemId":"f1d16e76-c067-51f3-9cb1-84d7ad7f71d1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"91b73536-702c-5141-bfd9-67d1e430aa75","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Standing orders are regularly used to make all the following payments EXCEPT:","options":["insurance premiums","a one-off purchase of a used car from a stranger","hire purchase instalments","school fees"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Standing orders suit recurring payments of a regular amount, such as premiums, rents, subscriptions, school fees and hire purchase or loan instalments. A one-off payment, option B, does not recur, so it would be made by cheque, draft or transfer instead. Options A, D and C are all typical standing order payments."}},{"itemId":"84126d58-ce2a-5cff-b2da-19c68ae5fc19","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c73fefdc-0e3c-5d4e-8954-793874f7e58b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"On the due date of a standing order, the customer's account has insufficient funds and no overdraft arrangement. Which statement is correct?","options":["The bank must pay and charge penal interest on the debit balance","The bank must pay and recover the amount later from the customer","The bank must refer the payee to the customer's employer","The bank is not obliged to pay and incurs no liability for not paying"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A bank carries out a standing order only out of available funds or an agreed credit facility, so when funds are insufficient it may refuse and is not liable for the non-payment. Options B and A wrongly suggest the bank must grant an unarranged overdraft. Option C has no basis in banking practice."}},{"itemId":"d60cd497-ccd7-53f3-8203-2cf7171a3079","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5a1db944-4663-52ff-a8a7-a2cd46975a1e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A banker's obligation to carry out a standing order stops in each of the following situations EXCEPT where:","options":["the balance is insufficient and there is no credit arrangement","the beneficiary writes directly to the bank asking it to cancel the order","a garnishee order stops payment out of the account","the customer countermands the payment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A standing order is the customer's mandate, so only the customer (or the law, through a garnishee order) can stop it, while lack of funds also excuses the bank. A request from the payee alone, option B, must be disregarded unless the customer confirms it. Options D, C and A are genuine cases where the bank's duty to pay ends."}},{"itemId":"7ee8b2f1-854d-5e0a-b5e7-9df6ebfb0472","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0b7c99c-fa11-5109-9e23-189f5e08b3ff","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Bello's standing order pays N20,000 monthly to his cooperative society. The society's secretary telephones Mr Bello's bank asking why this month's payment was late. The bank should:","options":["decline to explain and advise the society to contact Mr Bello","pay the society twice next month to make up","give the secretary details of Mr Bello's account balance","cancel the standing order to avoid further complaints"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The bank owes its customer a duty of secrecy, so it should not discuss the reason for the delay with the payee but should refer the payee to the customer. Option C would breach the duty of confidentiality, option D would ignore the customer's mandate, and option B is not authorised by the customer."}},{"itemId":"9c5b395f-1d98-5c54-8607-639ceca1cb84","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"396ff5ea-f41c-5512-8ebb-ac6cc94e385f","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If the beneficiary of a standing order refuses to accept the payments, the paying bank should:","options":["return the standing order card to the beneficiary","stop the standing order immediately without telling the customer","transfer the money to the Central Bank of Nigeria","continue making each payment as it falls due and notify the customer of each refusal"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The bank acts on its customer's mandate, so it keeps paying as instructed while informing the customer of every refusal, leaving the customer to decide whether to cancel. Option B would act without the customer's authority, and options C and A have no basis in standing order practice."}},{"itemId":"7365618d-4ac3-53d0-8e81-42de22cee8d1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90afaf56-f984-5f95-8ca5-fdd00dca1c37","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a duty of the CUSTOMER in a standing order arrangement?","options":["To send periodic statements of account to the bank","To charge commission for the service rendered","To notify the bank promptly when the standing order is to be discontinued","To handle the order with diligence and care on behalf of the bank"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The customer must give accurate details, keep enough funds in the account, pay the charges and tell the bank promptly when the order should stop. Option A and option D are duties of the banker, and option B is a right of the banker."}},{"itemId":"9606b820-6a26-5348-af2a-d4365a7ee30d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25cb96b4-23b9-5777-8a29-5d6cd5ad4547","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an advantage of a standing order to the CUSTOMER?","options":["The onus of making payment on the due dates rests with the banker","It widens the range of services the bank offers","The payee can vary the amount without the customer's consent","The bank earns commission income from the service"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"With a standing order the customer need not remember each due date, because the bank pays automatically and also provides a record of payment. Options D and B are benefits to the bank, not the customer, and option C is false because only the customer can change a standing order."}},{"itemId":"ec29e01b-48e3-56c0-b268-4a21fbecc276","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7bae0c66-79b8-5557-a806-c1d652fafca1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank needs all of the following information before it sets up a standing order EXCEPT:","options":["the beneficiary's monthly income","the name and account number of the beneficiary","the customer's signature in line with the account mandate","the amount and the intervals of payment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"To set up a standing order the bank needs the customer's signed application, the beneficiary's name, bank and account number, the amount and the payment intervals. The beneficiary's income, option A, is irrelevant to the payment. Options B, D and C are all required particulars."}},{"itemId":"7f26718b-1198-5ccf-8ba2-ae44562cffe4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d16d54af-d26d-5bef-a436-c6822a7be364","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A method of payment in which the BENEFICIARY (creditor) initiates the process, sending claims to the bank under an authority signed by the paying customer, is called a:","options":["direct debit","credit transfer","standing order","telegraphic transfer"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Under a direct debit the creditor, with the debtor's signed authority, sends debit claims to the bank, which then debits the debtor's account. Option C is started by the paying customer himself. Options B and D are payments pushed out by the payer, not pulled by the creditor."}},{"itemId":"1b6eb8a3-0c75-52cd-867f-6467e165a97d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae9b65c7-20eb-5405-979a-56ca1edfa135","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The key difference between a standing order and a direct debit is that:","options":["a standing order is used only abroad, while a direct debit is used only locally","a standing order is initiated by the payer, while a direct debit is initiated by the beneficiary","a standing order needs no signature, while a direct debit needs two","a standing order is paid in cash, while a direct debit is paid by draft"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"In a standing order the payer instructs his own bank to push regular payments out, but in a direct debit the creditor pulls payments from the payer's account under an authority the payer signed. For that reason a direct debit can also handle varying amounts. Options A, C and D describe differences that do not exist."}},{"itemId":"3e07032e-23fd-5108-b2f0-652bc0b3c0ed","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f9232fae-2e3f-5064-8ef7-f3f26fb0b928","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An insurance company sends signed debit claims to Union Bank from time to time, under an authority endorsed by its policyholder Mr Musa, so that premiums can be taken from his account. This method of payment is a:","options":["documentary collection","banker's payment","direct debit","standing order"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The creditor (the insurance company) initiates each claim using the debtor's endorsed authority, which is the mark of a direct debit; insurance companies and finance houses commonly use it. Option D would be started by Mr Musa himself. Option B is used between banks, and option A belongs to international trade."}},{"itemId":"811c67bd-8c46-51a2-ac26-b75161bc4b01","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d0682b1d-7655-5a91-9379-1b780632b100","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-014","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A gym in Lekki charges a different amount each month depending on the classes a member attends. Which payment method best suits collecting these monthly fees from members' accounts?","options":["Standing order","Direct debit","Bank cheque","Bank draft"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A direct debit lets the originator collect variable amounts from the customer's account under the customer's authority, so it fits a fee that changes monthly. Option A pays a fixed amount fixed by the customer, so it would need amending every month. Options C and D are single instruments for one payment each."}},{"itemId":"2f5c9ec1-64cf-5632-bc45-b1839c00d4f1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"089b60de-97df-5dea-86d5-449529236261","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A cheque drawn by a branch of a bank on another branch or on the head office of the same bank, signed by the bank's own authorised signatories, is a:","options":["bank draft","dividend warrant","traveller's cheque","personal cheque"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A bank draft is a bank's own instrument drawn by one of its branches on another branch or its head office, so the bank itself is liable on it. Option D is drawn by a customer on his own account, option B is a cheque drawn by a company to pay shareholders, and option C is a prepaid instrument for travel abroad."}},{"itemId":"e9d32253-7835-5a0b-9d6a-bfde1f41ae76","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58a9750b-8484-55ea-9073-a99e7a77f8ef","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Many suppliers insist on being paid by bank draft rather than by personal cheque mainly because:","options":["a draft does not need to pass through any bank","a draft is a bank's own instrument and is not normally dishonoured on presentation","a draft carries interest from the date of issue","a draft can only be drawn for small amounts"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Because the bank itself is the drawer of a draft, the risk of it bouncing for lack of funds is removed, which is why creditors prefer it to a personal cheque. Option C is false since drafts earn no interest. Option D is wrong because drafts are used for large payments, and option A is wrong because a draft is paid through the banking system."}},{"itemId":"281a4a23-9f9c-50da-9907-ae14348f3fc8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"02612d0b-4f88-5059-9dee-88bd57592273","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-017","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a bank issues a draft, the issuing branch sends the paying branch an advice giving the particulars of the draft. The purpose of this advice is to:","options":["notify the Central Bank of Nigeria of the transaction","inform the beneficiary's employer of the payment","allow the paying branch to confirm that the draft presented is genuine","cancel the customer's cheque book"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The advice, signed by the same signatories as the draft, lets the paying branch check the draft against its details before paying, which guards against forged or altered drafts. Options B, A and D are not functions of a draft advice."}},{"itemId":"b1ec32a8-5049-50a1-ac3e-322ffd4de511","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8894f18-b1bc-5f11-9f5c-911ae6c7c2cc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In banking practice, a bank cheque differs from a bank draft in that a bank cheque:","options":["can be dishonoured for lack of funds, while a draft cannot","is drawn by the customer on his own account, while a draft is drawn by the bank","is used only for international payments, while a draft is used only locally","is used within the same clearing area, while a draft is used where other clearing areas are involved"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Both are bank instruments, but a bank cheque is cleared within the same clearing locality, so it has a narrower use than a draft, which can be used across clearing areas and even internationally. Option B is wrong because a bank cheque is also issued by the bank, option A is wrong because both are backed by the bank, and option C reverses the scope."}},{"itemId":"8b8004e0-12a9-5bfc-8bad-2aec443096a3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c8b7ef9-0796-596c-8034-ae93500bc1a3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is the simplest and most common method of paying a debt within the domestic economy, though creditors may avoid it because of fraud and dishonour?","options":["Bill negotiation","A documentary letter of credit","A Nostro account transfer","A personal cheque"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A personal cheque is the simplest domestic payment method and is used for goods, services, fees and premiums, but because it can bounce or be forged many creditors prefer drafts or standing orders. Options B, C and A are mainly international payment and settlement methods."}},{"itemId":"d090c8e2-6ac5-5481-93fd-38300ffb9aed","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"002975ff-e544-5193-a51a-7870a4e44982","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An instrument used by banks to pay EACH OTHER, for example for cash supplied by one bank to another or for the proceeds of a cheque sent for special clearing, is a:","options":["standing order","dividend warrant","banker's payment","direct debit"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A banker's payment is the instrument a bank issues in favour of another bank to settle obligations between them, such as cash exchange or special clearing proceeds. Options A and D are customer payment methods, and option B is a company's cheque to its shareholders."}},{"itemId":"667ed16d-8553-517b-b0a6-85b3e142da3a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f88798ab-b2eb-5ede-8ac3-913e2f0c53c0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"After heavy cash withdrawals, Bank X asks Bank Y to supply it with N50 million in cash. Bank X settles by issuing an instrument in favour of Bank Y, and the amount passes through clearing. The instrument issued is a:","options":["banker's payment","standing order","bank draft in favour of a customer","letter of credit"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"When one bank pays another for cash supplied, it issues a banker's payment, after which Bank X is debited and Bank Y credited through clearing. Option C is drawn for a customer's beneficiary, option B is a customer's periodic instruction, and option D is used in international trade."}},{"itemId":"46ef9090-ce4f-5153-ab9a-514170850df7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"468580cb-fbcb-5ce9-be17-bfde87b3b431","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chidi writes to his bank to move N200,000 from his savings account to his current account at the same bank so that a cheque he has already issued can be paid. This is an example of:","options":["a banker's payment","a direct debit","a telegraphic transfer","an intra-bank transfer"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Moving funds between accounts of the same customer in the same bank is an intra-bank transfer, often done by letter of instruction. Option C sends funds to another place, option A is a payment between banks, and option B is initiated by a creditor."}},{"itemId":"8777fe6e-e4b4-5f84-a0dc-b238d669a7da","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"533b81d4-5820-5c02-bc7e-57e2ee6cd324","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main advantage of a telegraphic transfer over a mail transfer is that a telegraphic transfer:","options":["can only be used within the same branch","moves the payment instruction much faster, by cable, telex or electronic message","never involves a debit to the customer's account","does not need the beneficiary's account details"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"In both methods the customer gives written instructions and the bank debits his account, but a mail transfer sends the instruction by post while a telegraphic transfer sends it by telegraph or telex, so it is much faster. Option C is false because the remitter is always debited, option A is false because these transfers go to other places, and option D is false because beneficiary details are required."}},{"itemId":"98fdb8bb-ba22-5e1d-8981-97de8d3b85cd","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5985583e-6cc7-5fb0-ba6e-2e8e6b297ac3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"To remit funds by mail or telegraphic transfer, a customer gives the bank written instructions stating all the following EXCEPT:","options":["the name of the beneficiary","the beneficiary's bank and account number","the amount to be transferred","the beneficiary's date of birth"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A transfer instruction must clearly state the beneficiary's name, bank, account number and the amount, after which the bank debits the customer and pays the beneficiary. The beneficiary's date of birth, option D, is not part of the instruction. Options A, B and C are required particulars."}},{"itemId":"5ab03e42-6357-5b59-829d-fd0d115b8b7f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b569e20-0c53-5456-80ec-8605fcc85a4e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A system in which a payer completes credit slips (giro slips) at his own bank so that funds are credited directly to payees' accounts at the same or other banks is known as:","options":["direct debit","credit transfer (bank giro)","cheque cashing facility","bill negotiation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"In a credit transfer the payer pushes funds to the payees by handing in credit slips, with a single cheque or cash, at his bank, which passes the credits to the payees' banks; it is useful for paying several bills or salaries at once. Option A is pulled by the creditor, option D is the purchase of a bill by a bank, and option C lets a customer cash cheques at other branches."}},{"itemId":"d8fb9831-3725-5d3b-9168-5c47557ca8fc","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e903f457-0beb-544b-9d12-efc64566aeda","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"From the point of view of an exporter, which method of receiving payment for goods shipped abroad is the RISKIEST?","options":["Payment in advance","Documentary collection on D/P terms","Documentary letter of credit","Open account"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Under open account the exporter sends the documents of title straight to the importer, losing control of the goods and relying entirely on the importer's honesty to remit later. Option C gives a bank's guarantee of payment, option B keeps the documents under the bank's control until payment, and option A means the exporter is paid before shipping."}},{"itemId":"def8682c-577a-5eaf-a29a-005516f7d600","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2d019aa7-9a05-56aa-8bd9-24c67e8e3a88","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-027","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In bill collection, when only the bill of exchange is sent to the bank for collection WITHOUT commercial documents such as the bill of lading, it is called a:","options":["clean collection","documentary credit","bill negotiation","documentary collection"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A clean collection involves only the financial document, the bill, while a documentary collection also includes commercial documents like the bill of lading and insurance certificate. Option D is therefore the opposite. Option B is a letter of credit, and option C is the bank buying the bill rather than merely collecting it."}},{"itemId":"4c15bc2a-9eff-5dcb-ad17-539161fc0a6b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5664eed1-532e-5deb-9b40-efda4030e333","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-028","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A documentary collection is a bill sent for collection together with:","options":["a guarantee of payment from the importer's bank","the importer's tax clearance certificate only","commercial documents such as the bill of lading and insurance certificate","cash deposited by the exporter"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"In a documentary collection the bill travels with the shipping and commercial documents, and the bank releases them to the importer only against payment or acceptance. Option A describes a letter of credit, which collections do not provide. Options D and B are not features of a collection."}},{"itemId":"ea296a0d-d032-56c1-810d-a37f003e49db","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2ed11c38-5090-53bf-9d80-19848dc0517c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-029","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which method of payment for exports gives the exporter a bank's undertaking of payment, provided he complies with the terms, and is described as the surest for exporters?","options":["Documentary collection","Documentary letter of credit","Clean collection","Open account"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A documentary letter of credit is a bank's undertaking to pay the exporter once he presents documents that comply with its terms, so the exporter does not rely on the importer's honesty. Options D, C and A all leave the exporter exposed to the risk that the importer will not pay."}},{"itemId":"6031ccd3-dfde-5923-a75e-c547f495b813","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"20418abf-2813-56e4-87a2-09be4ff682d5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-030","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a documentary letter of credit transaction, the ISSUING bank is:","options":["the shipping company that issues the bill of lading","the exporter's bank, which tells the exporter that the credit has been opened","the importer's bank, which opens the credit in favour of the exporter","the central bank of the exporter's country"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The issuing bank is the importer's bank, which opens the letter of credit and undertakes to pay the exporter against compliant documents. Option B describes the advising bank. Option D is not a party to the credit, and option A is a carrier, not a bank."}},{"itemId":"8ea34fea-da01-5052-8f43-b922ee9f2457","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf472d62-45a3-57ac-9f55-6eb48f171f18","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-031","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a letter of credit transaction, the bank in the exporter's country that informs the exporter that a credit has been opened in his favour is the:","options":["collecting bank in the importer's country","Central Bank of Nigeria","issuing bank","advising bank"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The advising bank is usually the exporter's bank, and it passes the details of the credit to the exporter. Option C is the importer's bank, which opens the credit. Options A and B do not perform this role in a letter of credit."}},{"itemId":"a054e2c6-132e-5e95-8c77-034e585fa4d5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e68ffc3f-b477-5caa-9b5c-c509a622051e","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-032","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In documentary trade payments, the difference between D/P and D/A terms is that:","options":["D/P involves no bill of exchange, while D/A involves two","D/P means the exporter pays, while D/A means the bank pays","under D/P documents are released against payment, while under D/A they are released against acceptance of a bill","D/P is used only for exports by air, while D/A is used only for sea freight"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"D/P stands for documents against payment, so the importer pays before getting the documents of title, while D/A stands for documents against acceptance, so the importer gets them by accepting a usance bill to pay later. Options D, B and A misstate what the terms mean."}},{"itemId":"fe6eca98-2962-56db-92ba-0b96001428ff","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6e9672e5-77e2-5658-a627-bb5bb5f9c9b2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-033","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ade Cocoa Ltd has drawn a bill on its buyer in Germany but needs cash now. It endorses and delivers the bill to its bank, which pays the face value less a discount for commission and interest and collects the full amount at maturity. This is:","options":["open account payment","clean collection","documentary credit","payment by bill negotiation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"In bill negotiation the exporter transfers the bill to his bank by endorsement and delivery, and the bank pays him at once, less a discount, then collects the full value at maturity. Option B would merely collect the bill for the exporter without paying him in advance. Options A and C are different arrangements altogether."}},{"itemId":"9fe17351-1306-555b-8721-382f78762b73","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f3b48c4-c439-5c07-bb72-52988c59cb16","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-034","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An exporter in Kano negotiates a bill of exchange with a face value of N800,000 to his bank. The bank deducts 2.5% of the face value for commission and interest. How much does the exporter receive now?","options":["N20,000","N800,000","N780,000","N820,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The bank pays the face value less its discount: 2.5% of N800,000 is N20,000, so the exporter receives N800,000 - N20,000 = N780,000. Option B ignores the discount, option A is only the discount itself, and option D wrongly adds the discount."}},{"itemId":"098772ce-6ebb-5194-8963-085ee154edc5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f6157038-a4d2-58b1-8b9b-5a5c6dda07c0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-035","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An account maintained by a Nigerian bank with a correspondent bank abroad, used to receive export proceeds and make foreign currency payments, is from the Nigerian bank's point of view a:","options":["domiciliary account","Nostro account","suspense account","Vostro account"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Nostro is Latin for 'our', so it is 'our account with you', held by a Nigerian bank abroad in foreign currency. Option D is the reverse, a foreign bank's account held in Nigeria. Option A is a customer's foreign currency account at a local bank, and option C is an internal holding account."}},{"itemId":"5f3e7afd-35bd-542b-80ee-9156dd3829d6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"73bbdbd0-ebca-5cc3-8f18-d00ede5bc2a1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-036","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A London bank keeps a naira account with Zenith Bank in Lagos, through which it settles its obligations in Nigeria. From Zenith Bank's point of view, this account is a:","options":["Vostro account","escrow account","fixed deposit account","Nostro account"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Vostro means 'your', so to Zenith Bank this is 'your account with us', a foreign bank's local currency account held in Nigeria. Option D would be an account Zenith Bank itself holds abroad. Options C and B are unrelated account types."}},{"itemId":"131b0123-5225-5a92-b6da-c6e977630998","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0627f68d-4fd3-5080-80fa-f0d42e0d294c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-037","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Latin words 'nostro' and 'vostro' used in correspondent banking mean respectively:","options":["'our' and 'your'","'foreign' and 'local'","'your' and 'our'","'credit' and 'debit'"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Nostro means 'our' (our account held with you abroad) and vostro means 'your' (your account held with us). Option C reverses the meanings, and options B and D are not translations of the words."}},{"itemId":"ab80f756-0f74-5b26-813a-18c634c54658","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"721c2ded-8dbf-5a36-8ad8-045e26b7c2f4","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-038","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"SWIFT, used for international interbank payments, stands for:","options":["Society of World International Finance and Trade","System for Worldwide Interbank Fund Transfers","Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication","Secure Worldwide Interbank Foreign Transactions"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"SWIFT is the Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication, a member-owned cooperative based in Belgium that carries secure financial messages between banks. Options B, A and D are invented expansions that sound similar."}},{"itemId":"98a6946d-d44c-57ab-9873-07cbfd0e8296","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5086a0b-13ec-5fc0-b341-f4e711099b9a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-039","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a Nigerian bank sends a payment to a bank abroad through SWIFT, what actually travels through the SWIFT network?","options":["Secure payment instructions, while the funds are settled through the banks' correspondent accounts","Gold reserves of the Central Bank of Nigeria","The customer's original cheque book","Physical cash sealed in electronic vaults"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"SWIFT is a store-and-forward messaging system, so instructions to transfer funds, not the funds themselves, move through it; settlement happens on accounts such as Nostro and Vostro accounts. Options D, B and C wrongly suggest that money or documents physically move."}},{"itemId":"ee9087ec-df67-5f61-80ed-f8079bbea997","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bb539e4f-a786-5f1b-be49-8d9218aad7bc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-040","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The use of electronically recorded pre-authorised debits and credits to move funds between accounts, replacing paper cheques, is known as:","options":["bill discounting","electronic funds transfer (EFT)","safe custody","mail transfer"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Electronic funds transfer moves electronic records rather than paper, which computers process faster and more accurately than people handle cheques. Option D uses posted paper instructions, option A is the purchase of a bill before maturity, and option C is the keeping of valuables."}},{"itemId":"f0d13093-838f-5198-8efb-53228bb77203","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"41beb445-13e3-59db-967c-f441b6666744","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-041","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Lagos company sends its bank one electronic file each month so that the salaries of its 300 staff are paid straight into their personal accounts, instead of writing 300 cheques. This is an example of:","options":["a direct deposit (payroll) programme under electronic funds transfer","a cheque cashing facility","a banker's payment","a documentary collection"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"In a direct deposit programme an organisation sends its payroll electronically for crediting to employees' accounts, which cuts paper processing for the banks. Option D is an export payment method, option C is used between banks, and option B lets a customer cash cheques at other branches."}},{"itemId":"65a56f6f-5e01-572b-9327-700dcb6d859e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3c94f6b-0276-5a9a-a2d2-87ddcb7759e9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-042","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A debit card differs from a credit card in that a debit card:","options":["takes payment directly from the cardholder's own bank account at the time of use","can only be used outside Nigeria","gives the holder a revolving credit line repaid later with interest","requires no PIN or authorisation at all"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A debit card draws on money already in the holder's account, while a credit card lets the holder spend on credit up to a limit and repay later, often with interest on unpaid balances. Option C describes a credit card, and options B and D are false."}},{"itemId":"3a7a62c0-4698-5ff0-bc55-3cc46aaf067b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b30e20df-7418-5da5-b5f4-fdc793e07ce9","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-043","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A charge card differs from a credit card mainly because the holder of a charge card must:","options":["have the amount debited from his savings account at once","pay a minimum amount and carry the rest forward with interest","load value onto the card before using it","settle the full balance at the end of each billing period"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A charge card gives short credit within the month, but the whole statement balance must be paid when billed, with no revolving balance. Option B describes a credit card. Option A describes a debit card, and option C describes a prepaid or stored-value card."}},{"itemId":"7a764bcf-d914-5564-9211-fc097eefbd83","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa536f7c-61f2-5c07-8859-ce35641a0431","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-044","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An automated teller machine (ATM) can typically perform all the following services EXCEPT:","options":["transfer of funds between accounts","opening a fixed deposit account and issuing the deposit receipt","checking the account balance","withdrawal of cash"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"An ATM is a self-service machine that does a cashier's commonest jobs: cash withdrawal, balance enquiry, statement requests, cash deposits and transfers between accounts. Opening a fixed deposit with its receipt, option B, is not one of these functions. Options D, C and A are standard ATM services."}},{"itemId":"66c05964-df51-5e2b-86b8-242a478e3bdf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"87ab2e71-61db-5ef0-93c0-1042eff7114b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-045","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"To use an ATM, a customer inserts a card with a magnetic stripe or chip and must also enter a secret code to show he is the legitimate cardholder. This code is the:","options":["sort code","account mandate","personal identification number (PIN)","bank verification number (BVN)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The PIN is the secret number known only to the cardholder and keyed in to authenticate him at an ATM or POS terminal. Option D is a biometric identity number for bank customers, option A identifies a bank branch in clearing, and option B is the signing authority on an account."}},{"itemId":"7f6dab97-ddc7-570e-9998-ed981bac3937","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eeb77717-603d-514e-805d-a0791e5b252b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-046","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"All the following are advantages of automated teller machines to bank customers EXCEPT:","options":["they give access to cash outside banking hours","they reduce long queues in banking halls","they save customers time for their own business","they allow overdrafts to be granted without any bank approval"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"ATMs offer a 24-hour service, decongest banking halls and save customers time. They do not let anyone borrow beyond what the bank has approved, so option D is not an advantage. Options B, A and C are genuine advantages."}},{"itemId":"0977df8e-c07d-5603-8466-4800a747e469","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d77ba20c-aa9b-5650-9a33-168571d933ae","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-047","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Eze pays for groceries at a Shoprite till by inserting her debit card into a bank terminal and entering her PIN; her account is debited and the store's account credited electronically. The terminal is a:","options":["point of sale (POS) terminal","cheque sorter","telex machine","automated teller machine"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A POS terminal at a merchant's location lets a buyer pay with a card, transferring funds electronically from buyer to seller at the point of purchase. Option D dispenses cash and serves account holders, option B processes paper cheques in clearing, and option C sends telegraphic messages."}},{"itemId":"edef4369-ab5a-5577-b24c-cf21ede97983","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1e57c4a3-9c17-5179-b7e9-59177fa04ae8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-048","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Nigerian mobile banking, USSD services (for example dialling a short code such as *XYZ#) are popular mainly because they:","options":["can only be used inside a bank branch","require a smartphone with a banking app","are used only by banks to settle with the CBN","work on basic mobile phones without needing internet data"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"USSD (Unstructured Supplementary Service Data) runs over the ordinary mobile network, so a customer with any phone and no data can check balances, transfer funds or buy airtime. Option B describes app-based mobile banking, option A is false since USSD is used anywhere, and option C describes interbank settlement."}},{"itemId":"b97dec01-4f98-5433-aa30-8d3fbf6bdfd6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da08ea87-84e8-51d6-a0cb-31862167cb92","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-049","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following electronic banking channels lets a customer use a bank's website to check balances, view transaction history and transfer funds from home or office?","options":["Internet banking","Status enquiry","Safe custody","Bill discounting"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Internet banking gives customers secure access through the bank's website, 24 hours a day, for enquiries, transfers and bill payments. Options C, D and B are traditional bank services that have nothing to do with web access."}},{"itemId":"26dbe281-feff-5582-86f1-44962483add9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eb68e459-7bed-5bda-92fa-e040bb2bf4f1","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-050","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"NIP, the system through which most Nigerian interbank transfers are credited to the beneficiary within seconds, stands for:","options":["Naira Instant Posting","Nigerian Integrated Payroll","NIBSS Instant Payment","National Interbank Payment"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"NIP is NIBSS Instant Payment, a real-time, account-to-account transfer service run by the Nigeria Inter-Bank Settlement System. Options D, A and B are invented expansions."}},{"itemId":"ee32aab6-534d-552b-89d3-e90f99c1d790","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c6f35de-c677-5f67-b9c2-7e2314d08d6b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-051","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Comparing NIP and NEFT, the two NIBSS interbank transfer services in Nigeria:","options":["NIP is used only for international transfers, while NEFT is local","Both are run by SWIFT in Belgium","NIP credits the beneficiary in real time, while NEFT processes transfers in batches with a longer delay","NEFT is real time, while NIP takes several days"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"NIP (NIBSS Instant Payment) credits the beneficiary within seconds, while NEFT (NIBSS Electronic Funds Transfer) is an older batch-based service where value takes longer to arrive. Option D reverses the two, option A is wrong because both are local, and option B is wrong because both are run by NIBSS, not SWIFT."}},{"itemId":"0051860e-8996-50fe-8d47-86f8bd8f64a7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c36ce598-4b84-5cda-9f25-d7c61498d2bb","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-052","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Real-Time Gross Settlement (RTGS) system, such as the one operated by the Central Bank of Nigeria, is one in which:","options":["only cheques below a set amount are cleared","each payment is settled individually and immediately across banks' accounts, mainly for large-value transfers","payments are added up and settled once a month on a net basis","payments are made in physical cash between bank vaults"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"In RTGS, 'gross' means each transfer is settled on its own rather than netted, and 'real time' means it is settled at once, making it suitable for large-value, time-critical payments between banks. Option C describes deferred net settlement, and options A and D do not describe RTGS."}},{"itemId":"a8695a26-63de-5bc2-a876-a95a126b4c5b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27bfb264-10c3-5dc8-9f29-d2ae4c22427d","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-053","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank of Nigeria's cashless policy, aimed at reducing the amount of physical cash in circulation, was first piloted in which state, starting on 1 January 2012?","options":["Federal Capital Territory, Abuja","Kano State","Lagos State","Rivers State"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The cashless policy began with a pilot in Lagos State from 1 January 2012 before it was extended to other states. Options A, D and B joined later phases, not the first pilot."}},{"itemId":"b4bd2d6c-a402-57d2-90e3-6b6f3dcc9df8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"db1b7072-b376-52f9-a4cd-4e58082582da","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-054","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The problems that made Nigerian banks look for alternatives to cash as the main means of settlement include all the following EXCEPT:","options":["carrying cash exposes people to robbery and loss of life","large sums must be carried for transactions as the currency depreciates","banks spend heavily on staff and note-counting machines","cash is accepted everywhere instantly with no handling cost"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Cash has real costs: large volumes to carry, the risk of armed robbery and the expense of counting and handling physical cash, which pushed banks towards cards and electronic payments. Option D is not a problem at all. Options B, A and C are genuine factors."}},{"itemId":"5b017147-2df6-588f-849a-c3173aa8542e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9913f7da-f345-55ee-badf-43f207bdcf1c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-055","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The e-Naira, Nigeria's central bank digital currency, was officially launched by the Central Bank of Nigeria in which month and year?","options":["October 2021","January 2012","March 2023","August 2019"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The CBN launched the e-Naira on 25 October 2021, after issuing its guidelines earlier that year, making it a digital form of the naira issued by the central bank. Option B is when the cashless policy pilot began in Lagos. Options D and C are not the launch date."}},{"itemId":"04bd58ef-b2d5-58ef-b049-4e7febbdbcd6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"48c074fa-b0dd-5ecb-a16d-5c4dbfea1187","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-056","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An electronic form of central bank money that people can use to make digital payments and which can also act as a store of value is a:","options":["traveller's cheque","stored-value smart card issued by a private bank","cryptocurrency","central bank digital currency (CBDC)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"A CBDC, such as Nigeria's e-Naira, is a digital liability of the central bank itself, so it is legal tender like physical cash. Option C is issued privately on a blockchain and is not legal tender, option B holds commercial bank money, and option A is a paper travel instrument."}},{"itemId":"18d1ab53-a71e-5229-a561-29094585cda6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5cb6f68d-6806-50ee-a9e4-eda3a05177d8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-057","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes the e-Naira from a cryptocurrency such as Bitcoin?","options":["The e-Naira has no legal backing, while a cryptocurrency is backed by law","Both are issued and guaranteed by the Central Bank of Nigeria","The e-Naira is highly volatile, while a cryptocurrency has a stable value","The e-Naira is issued by the central bank and is legal tender, while a cryptocurrency is privately created and is not legal tender"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The e-Naira is state-backed, issued by the CBN, equal in value to the cash naira and legal tender, while cryptocurrencies are not issued by any central bank, are not legal tender and are very volatile. Options C and A reverse these differences, and option B is false for cryptocurrency."}},{"itemId":"76891aef-c443-5d62-86ac-4fa228472d48","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"485bacc7-124d-5ffc-8218-2714c29d07f8","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-058","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN's design, the relationship between the e-Naira and the cash naira is that the e-Naira:","options":["trades at a floating rate against the cash naira","complements the cash naira and has the same value, one e-Naira to one naira","can only be used for international payments","replaces all physical naira cash completely"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The e-Naira is a digital form of the same currency, legal tender at par with cash, intended to complement rather than replace physical cash. Option D contradicts this, option A is wrong because the value is the same, and option C is wrong because it is meant for domestic payments."}},{"itemId":"f7f39987-a3ab-52b6-b0a1-84b22b5b5e0a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"60d94418-a876-542c-ab71-bacdaf9eac04","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-059","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Expected benefits of the e-Naira include all the following EXCEPT:","options":["lower cost of printing naira currency","making the naira more volatile so that traders can speculate","greater financial inclusion of unbanked adults","more effective monetary policy from better payment data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The e-Naira was expected to deepen financial inclusion, reduce note-printing costs, improve data for monetary policy and make payments safer. Greater volatility, option B, would be a drawback, and stable value is in fact a feature of a CBDC. Options C, A and D are listed benefits."}},{"itemId":"b7cd4e1b-2be2-5778-89ef-f534367d1656","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df26574c-9777-56fb-b033-04871df58218","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-060","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Adebayo has a monthly standing order of N12,000. Her bank charges 1/2% of the amount for the standing order service plus N100 postage per remittance. What is the total debit to her account for one month's standing order?","options":["N12,600","N12,100","N12,160","N12,060"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The service charge is 0.5% of N12,000 = N60, so the total debit is N12,000 + N60 + N100 = N12,160. Option B leaves out the commission, option D leaves out the postage, and option A wrongly uses 5% instead of 1/2% and also leaves out the postage."}},{"itemId":"f0b2cff2-5785-5407-87c6-110e99330243","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57fb9783-9843-560f-9d30-e3ee9148856b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-061","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr Obi's account opened the month at N2,500 CR. He paid in a cheque of N15,000 and cash of N1,800, issued a cheque for N6,200, and was charged N200 for a cheque book. His standing order of N10,000 falls due at month end, with a 1% service charge and N50 postage. What is his balance after the standing order is paid?","options":["N2,750 CR","N2,900 CR","N12,900 CR","N2,850 CR"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Before the standing order the balance is 2,500 + 15,000 + 1,800 - 6,200 - 200 = N12,900 CR, and the standing order with charges is 10,000 + 100 + 50 = N10,150, leaving N2,750 CR. Option B deducts only the N10,000 standing order without its charges, option C is the balance before the standing order, and option D leaves out the 1% service charge."}},{"itemId":"83f99a90-c26e-5432-a9c6-9baf9b70dc13","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"82dff2fd-b02c-5607-9aa6-c9ea2e0bf378","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-062","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before a standing order of N5,000 falls due, Miss Dike's account shows N3,000 CR. The bank charges 1/2% for the service and N50 postage, and she has an approved overdraft limit of N1,500. What should the bank do?","options":["Pay only N3,000 and send the rest next month","Not pay, because paying would make the balance N2,075 DR, beyond the N1,500 limit","Pay, because the balance after payment would be N2,000 DR, within the limit","Pay, because any overdraft is allowed on standing orders"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The full debit would be 5,000 + 25 + 50 = N5,075, but she has only N3,000 plus a N1,500 limit, that is N4,500 available, so the account would go N2,075 DR, beyond her limit; the bank need not pay. Option C ignores the charges and miscalculates, option D wrongly assumes unlimited borrowing, and option A is a part payment the customer did not authorise."}},{"itemId":"f9b8c205-d5fc-5105-b58b-d299891564c5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1c002f8b-8f22-52a5-9ad4-556abace31c6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-063","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A standing order of N25,000 is paid every month for a full year, and the bank charges 1/2% of each remittance as commission. What is the total commission the customer pays for the year?","options":["N15,000","N125","N1,500","N12,500"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"Each month's commission is 0.5% of N25,000 = N125, and over 12 months this is N125 x 12 = N1,500. Option B is only one month's commission, option A uses 5% instead of 1/2%, and option D wrongly takes 50% of one month's payment."}},{"itemId":"28062fcb-cee3-5ef6-8c2d-d50793d34b41","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"be078e30-3dbb-5354-ba64-f80fe471d2bf","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:PAY-064","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Before his N4,500 standing order falls due, Mr Lawal's account shows N4,000 CR. The bank charges 1/2% for the service and N50 postage, and he has an approved overdraft limit of N1,000. What happens?","options":["The bank pays, and the account ends at N500 DR","The bank refuses, because the account would go into debit","The bank pays, and the account ends at N572.50 DR, within the limit","The bank pays, and the account ends at N572.50 CR"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Methods of Payment through the Banking System","explanation":"The total debit is 4,500 + 22.50 + 50 = N4,572.50, so the balance becomes 4,000 - 4,572.50 = N572.50 DR, which is within his agreed N1,000 overdraft, so the bank will pay. Option B ignores the approved credit arrangement, option A leaves out the charges, and option D gets the right figure but the wrong side of the account."}},{"itemId":"b7e16596-732f-5c54-982d-ccea8725760a","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f2db9ff2-de12-58a5-8f1c-695638ef2390","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-001","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Twenty traders at Oshodi market each contribute N5,000 every week, and each week one member collects the whole pool in turn. No licence is held and no regulator supervises the arrangement. This is part of the","options":["formal financial sector","capital market","informal financial sector","microfinance banking sector"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Rotating savings groups such as Esusu, Ajo or Isusu operate without a licence or formal regulation, which is exactly what defines the informal financial sector. The formal sector (A) and microfinance banks (D) are licensed and supervised, while the capital market (B) deals in long-term securities such as shares and bonds."}},{"itemId":"02334d00-6b43-59e0-b559-805771d035a6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd2fee42-2db9-586f-8f21-448ba20c5575","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-002","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A pension fund administrator in Lagos wants approval to manage workers' contributions under the contributory pension scheme. Which body regulates and supervises it?","options":["The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)","The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)","The National Pension Commission (PenCom)","The National Insurance Commission (NAICOM)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"PenCom oversees pension fund administrators and the contributory pension scheme. NAICOM (D) regulates insurance companies, SEC (B) regulates the capital market, and the NDIC (A) insures deposits in banks, so none of them licenses pension fund administrators."}},{"itemId":"6f37ef5f-e1f2-5844-a701-bd3c90ec6bf9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0cc09648-2006-55db-a080-01c10568bad0","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-003","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN's banking licence regime, a Nigerian commercial bank that wants to operate across all states of the federation and also run branches or subsidiaries outside Nigeria needs a licence with","options":["international authorisation","regional authorisation","non-interest (regional) authorisation","national authorisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Only a commercial bank with international authorisation may combine nationwide operations with offshore operations. A national licence (D) covers all of Nigeria but not offshore operations, and a regional licence (B) is limited to a specified group of states. Option C is a type of non-interest banking licence with an even narrower scope."}},{"itemId":"0b682918-f07b-598b-9b6f-4e5e77d12ee3","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"66693b1f-5f24-5640-8c35-add64570d265","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-004","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the bank recapitalisation programme announced by the CBN in March 2024 (with a deadline of 31 March 2026), what is the new minimum capital for a commercial bank with international authorisation?","options":["N25 billion","N200 billion","N50 billion","N500 billion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The 2024 programme raised the minimum capital for internationally authorised commercial banks to N500 billion, to be met within 24 months. N200 billion (B) is the figure for national authorisation, N50 billion (C) applies to regional commercial banks and merchant banks, and N25 billion (A) was the minimum set in the 2004-2005 consolidation."}},{"itemId":"11823c72-6ec0-5630-998b-295dd6cc1b47","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"69ea9f82-7238-5f61-ab27-3aaadd58c430","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-005","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Musa asks a non-interest bank to finance a N4 million delivery van. The bank buys the van, then sells it to Musa for N4.6 million, telling him the N600,000 mark-up openly, and he pays in monthly instalments. This non-interest contract is","options":["Murabaha","Ijarah","Mudarabah","Riba"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Murabaha is a cost-plus sale: the bank buys the asset and resells it to the customer at a disclosed profit margin, usually paid in instalments, so no interest is charged. Mudarabah (C) is a profit-sharing partnership, Ijarah (B) is leasing in which the bank keeps ownership and charges rent, and riba (D) is the interest that non-interest banking forbids."}},{"itemId":"0864d382-b915-5d02-9cd8-1ad4145c9677","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fb32ad6-ac78-5648-b43e-f3ea070f6eff","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-006","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The traditional function that first set merchant banks apart from other financial institutions is","options":["accepting small savings deposits from the general public","insuring the deposits of failed banks","issuing legal tender currency","accepting bills of exchange to finance the trade of others"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Acceptance business, where the merchant bank adds its name to a bill of exchange so that a trader can obtain short-term finance, is the classic merchant banking function. Option A is retail commercial banking, C belongs to the central bank, and B is the work of the NDIC."}},{"itemId":"f2c0c345-910f-560b-8765-17ea49ef609c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b94ff45d-0228-5910-a88b-37392ff51c51","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-007","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Delta Foods Plc has agreed to raise long-term funds through a share issue in four months' time, but it needs money now to start building a new plant. A merchant bank gives it a short-term loan to be repaid from the issue proceeds. This facility is a","options":["negotiable certificate of deposit","debt factoring arrangement","acceptance credit","bridging loan"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"A bridging loan is temporary finance that 'bridges' the gap until a company completes its permanent funding arrangements, which is exactly Delta Foods' situation. A negotiable certificate of deposit (A) is an interbank deposit instrument, an acceptance credit (C) finances trade through accepted bills, and factoring (B) means selling receivables for immediate cash."}},{"itemId":"90a11c04-ce53-5b6f-8249-e72592e09218","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5a2132e9-3d45-55ae-b944-acf335294d19","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-008","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Interbank debt instruments, with maturities of about 3 to 36 months, that are designed mainly to channel commercial banks' surplus funds into merchant banks are called","options":["unit trust certificates","bankers' acceptances","negotiable certificates of deposit (NCDs)","Treasury certificates"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"NCDs are issued by banks in exchange for fixed deposits and can be traded, which makes them a convenient way for commercial banks to place surplus funds with merchant banks. Treasury certificates (D) are government debt, bankers' acceptances (B) are trade bills accepted by banks, and unit trust certificates (A) show ownership of units in an investment fund."}},{"itemId":"900a15d5-027f-569a-91f6-194dd9620bf8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c443a80-6b8e-55bd-aca1-2b53dc9b4a94","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-009","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following correctly states a traditional difference between merchant banks and commercial banks in Nigeria?","options":["Merchant banks took mostly demand deposits, while commercial banks took only fixed deposits","Merchant banks mainly served individuals, while commercial banks mainly served large firms","Merchant banks were not members of the clearing house, while commercial banks took part in the clearing system","Merchant banks had large rural branch networks, while commercial banks were found only in cities"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Because merchant banks did not run cheque-based retail accounts for the public, they were traditionally not clearing house members, unlike commercial banks. Options D, B and A reverse the real differences: merchant banks were urban-based with few branches, served firms and institutions, and held mostly fixed deposits."}},{"itemId":"edf0d124-738d-51bd-a335-f14e25a9767d","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b4a24ce3-72f5-58a2-8e0e-2e2cbb8a7d14","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-010","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A World Bank review in 2000 traced the decline of Nigeria's federal development finance institutions to several causes. Which of the following was NOT one of them?","options":["Inappropriate funding vehicles","Excessive capital and too many highly qualified staff","Politicisation of lending","A poor credit-discipline environment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The review blamed non-commercial governance, politicised lending, poor credit discipline, lack of quality staff and inappropriate funding vehicles, which left the DFIs with heavy losses and mostly non-performing loans. Option B states the opposite of what happened, since the DFIs suffered from a shortage of quality staff and were short of funds. Options C, D and A were genuine causes."}},{"itemId":"c66b3784-e485-5b95-be3b-fa36abd8b864","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f1d63d17-2bbd-516b-bf5d-68edff97f47b","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-011","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A Nigerian cashew exporter worries that its buyer in Europe may fail to pay for a shipment because of commercial or political problems. Which development finance institution was set up to provide export credit insurance and guarantees for such risks?","options":["The Bank of Agriculture (BOA)","The Bank of Industry (BOI)","The Nigerian Export-Import Bank (NEXIM)","The Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"NEXIM is Nigeria's export credit agency, and it provides export credit guarantees and insurance against commercial and political risks of non-payment by foreign buyers. The BOI (B) finances industry, the BOA (A) finances farmers and rural enterprises, and the FMBN (D) deals in housing finance."}},{"itemId":"f3028963-ee03-5193-9597-3737cd5884f5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7bca09a7-9d94-5899-9a8f-251a575634a6","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-012","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Funmi, who owns a small bakery in Ibadan, wants a loan from the Development Bank of Nigeria (DBN), which began operations in 2017. How will she normally obtain DBN funds?","options":["Through the CBN discount window","Through a participating financial institution such as a commercial or microfinance bank, which on-lends DBN funds to her","By applying directly at a DBN retail branch, which lends to individual MSMEs","By buying DBN shares on the stock exchange"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The DBN is a wholesale development bank: it does not lend directly to small businesses but channels funds through participating financial institutions, which assess the borrower and bear the credit risk. Option C wrongly describes the DBN as a retail lender, A is a facility for banks, not businesses, and D has nothing to do with borrowing."}},{"itemId":"dc711def-0182-5eea-91a9-c945995b0e8b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8db818eb-7c3e-5b5c-b512-b72621a3e063","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-013","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the National Housing Fund (NHF) Act of 1992, a contributor pays 2.5% of monthly basic salary into the Fund. A public-sector worker in Abuja earns a basic salary of N180,000 a month. How much does she contribute to the NHF in a full year?","options":["N108,000","N4,500","N45,000","N54,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Her monthly contribution is 2.5% of N180,000 = N4,500, and over 12 months this comes to N4,500 x 12 = N54,000. Option B is only the monthly figure, C uses ten months instead of twelve, and A doubles the rate to 5%."}},{"itemId":"2fcfabac-cc97-56ba-85eb-b92896ebc5b5","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2f1ad79a-e9b6-59a6-ba36-152b5e687cc3","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-015","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When discount houses operated in the Nigerian money market, their main role was to","options":["act as intermediaries between the CBN and licensed banks by trading in and holding Treasury Bills, commercial bills and other short-term securities","buy and sell foreign currency notes for travellers and small users","lend long-term funds to manufacturers for plant and machinery","manage unit trust funds on behalf of small savers"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Discount houses dealt in short-term government and commercial bills and stood between the CBN and the banks in open market operations, which helped the money market grow. Option B describes bureaux de change, C describes development banks such as the Bank of Industry, and D describes unit trust managers."}},{"itemId":"645327a2-4259-5910-972d-1de9b775d71e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"70645979-4259-5c59-914e-d9f6d0bee517","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-016","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bureaux de change were introduced in Nigeria in 1989 to widen the foreign exchange market and serve small users. Under the original guidelines, a licensed bureau de change could deal only in","options":["the official auction of foreign exchange by the CBN","letters of credit for importers of capital goods","domiciliary accounts and foreign currency loans","foreign bank notes and coins, and the purchase of travellers' cheques"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Bureaux de change were set up to retail foreign exchange to small buyers, so they were limited to foreign cash and coins and cashing travellers' cheques. Options B and C are services offered by banks, which hold full foreign exchange authorisation, and A was the official market from which bureaux were originally expected to stay apart, buying their foreign exchange from private suppliers."}},{"itemId":"5e0650cf-ad1a-507d-89e0-22cba3399508","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9e41c076-c8a0-55af-8b20-f18d20f6c747","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-018","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank takes thousands of savings deposits that customers can withdraw at short notice, and uses part of them to grant a ten-year loan to a cement factory. Which aspect of financial intermediation does this show?","options":["Risk diversification","Maturity transformation","Moral suasion","Disintermediation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Turning short-term deposits into long-term loans is maturity transformation, one of the main reasons banks intermediate better than direct lending between savers and borrowers. Risk diversification (A) means spreading loans across many borrowers, which the scenario does not show, moral suasion (C) is a central bank tool, and disintermediation (D) is when savers and borrowers bypass banks altogether."}},{"itemId":"abfd7075-e651-5cb2-b9af-776f88f6a5f4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e610007-5dd0-5d83-b574-97ecca704bbc","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-019","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank pays 12% per annum on a savings deposit, while the inflation rate is 21%. Using the approximation that the real interest rate is the nominal rate minus the inflation rate, what is the saver's approximate real rate of return?","options":["-9%","+9%","+33%","-21%"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The real rate is about 12% - 21% = -9%, which means the saver's money loses purchasing power even though it earns interest, and this is why high inflation discourages saving. Option B gets the sign wrong, C wrongly adds the two rates, and D ignores the interest earned."}},{"itemId":"0de6e4d3-f189-541f-9dc6-d86838385eda","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b454c799-35de-5556-ad0e-3aa2a3ccdf0a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-020","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Chidinma keeps N800,000 in a savings account that pays simple interest of 6% per annum. Ignoring taxes and charges, how much interest will she earn after 9 months?","options":["N3,600","N36,000","N48,000","N54,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Simple interest = principal x rate x time = N800,000 x 0.06 x 9/12 = N36,000. Option C uses a full year, D multiplies by 9% instead of 6% for three-quarters of a year, and A misplaces the decimal point."}},{"itemId":"8ed92a92-76b1-595d-8bb7-2d8fa21d2bab","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"02baa078-7ccf-56bd-bbb8-bc572d629f2a","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-021","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In May 2024 the NDIC raised its maximum deposit insurance cover for each depositor of a deposit money bank to N5 million. Suppose a depositor has N7.2 million in a deposit money bank that later fails and is liquidated. Under that cover, how will she be paid?","options":["N7.2 million in full from the NDIC at once","N500,000 as insured deposit, with N6.7 million claimed from liquidation dividends","N5 million as insured deposit, with the remaining N2.2 million claimed from liquidation dividends as assets are realised","N2.2 million only, being the excess over the insured limit"],"correctOption":2,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The NDIC pays the insured amount up to the N5 million limit, and the uninsured balance of N7.2 million - N5 million = N2.2 million becomes a claim paid from liquidation dividends, if and when the failed bank's assets are realised. Option A ignores the limit, B uses the old N500,000 cover that applied before May 2024, and D confuses the uninsured excess with the insured payment."}},{"itemId":"f57737dc-89b8-555f-a958-48a22ef5be6c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c0de49e4-b47c-59e9-8af5-dbab94530173","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-022","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the flat-rate deposit insurance scheme of the NDIC Decree of 1988, an insured bank paid an annual premium of 15/16 of one per cent of its total deposit liabilities. If a bank's total deposit liabilities were N64 billion, what was its annual premium?","options":["N9.6 billion","N600 million","N640 million","N60 million"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Fifteen-sixteenths of one per cent is 0.9375%, so the premium is N64 billion x 0.009375 = N600 million. Option C uses a full 1%, D misplaces the decimal point, and A wrongly treats the rate as 15%."}},{"itemId":"d8870580-125c-5869-9193-87fec8f4f084","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cedc9cf7-87d7-5157-b039-234b861b1fd5","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-023","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the NDIC Act, when the NDIC pays an insured depositor of a failed bank, the depositor's rights against the failed bank pass to the NDIC to the extent of the amount paid. This legal principle is known as","options":["subrogation","lien","set-off","novation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Subrogation means stepping into another person's shoes: having paid the depositor, the NDIC takes over the depositor's claim against the failed bank for that amount and recovers it from the liquidation. Set-off (C) is combining debts owed both ways, a lien (B) is a right to retain property until a debt is paid, and novation (D) replaces an old contract with a new one by agreement."}},{"itemId":"a4575656-6e50-5f16-9e89-f1c5cb0cdf26","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a9d6251d-820b-5544-ab9a-3ff94fcec979","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-024","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In August 2011, after the licences of Afribank, Spring Bank and Bank PHB were revoked, the CBN and NDIC set up Mainstreet Bank, Enterprise Bank and Keystone Bank to take over their assets and liabilities temporarily, keeping services running until buyers could be found. These new banks were","options":["microfinance banks","development finance institutions","merchant banks","bridge banks"],"correctOption":3,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"A bridge bank is a temporary bank set up by the authorities to take over a failed bank's business so that depositors keep their services until the bank can be sold, and Nigeria used this option for the three banks in 2011. Options A, C and B are permanent categories of licensed institutions, not a failure-resolution tool."}},{"itemId":"2eb1807b-0c7c-5c28-b9e8-0f426be8a0f1","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1eecfab-3502-5c09-9c5d-0283a8ec8f2c","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-025","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A microfinance bank in Ibadan plans to start buying and selling US dollars for its customers. Under the CBN's rules for microfinance banks, this plan is","options":["allowed, because microfinance banks may carry out any service that commercial banks offer","not allowed, because foreign exchange transactions are a prohibited activity for microfinance banks","allowed, provided the dollars are sold only to rural customers","allowed, provided the NDIC insures the foreign currency deposits"],"correctOption":1,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"Microfinance banks are not permitted to deal in foreign exchange, a sophisticated service reserved for banks with the proper licence. Option C invents a rural exception, A is wrong because microfinance banks have a much narrower scope than commercial banks, and D is wrong because deposit insurance has nothing to do with permission to deal in foreign exchange."}},{"itemId":"81f8ded8-d3f5-58de-988f-d5eb142e364e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"292e2c0e-342a-5238-8369-194754cc33c2","sourceKey":"bank:FIN-CM209:STRW-026","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the CBN's 2010 regulation on the scope of banking activities, a commercial bank may NOT, without CBN authorisation,","options":["acquire real estate other than as business premises for its own use","grant overdrafts and loans to its customers","accept savings and fixed deposits from the public","provide payment services such as electronic funds transfers"],"correctOption":0,"section":"The Nigerian Banking Structure, Savings and Investment","explanation":"The 2010 regulation bars banks from acquiring real estate or immovable property except premises for their own use, unless the CBN authorises it, so that depositors' funds are not tied up in property speculation. Options C, B and D are core commercial banking functions: accepting deposits, giving credit and providing payment services."}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'elements-banking: cbt_question published payload and identity match source');
set local role authenticated;
select ok((select coalesce(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_object(
  'itemId', item_id, 'courseId', course_id, 'kind', kind,
  'questionId', question_id, 'sourceKey', source_key,
  'parentItemId', parent_item_id, 'revision', revision,
  'payload', payload) order by source_key collate "C"), '[]'::jsonb)
  from public.read_published_managed_content('58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed')
  where kind = 'theory_question') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"dbe954f3-3729-5b02-be2c-8358c1436612","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:1","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bank receives a fresh cash deposit of ₦4,000,000. The cash reserve ratio is 20%. Find (a) the excess reserve of the first bank, (b) the money multiplier, (c) the total deposits the banking system can create, and (d) the total if the reserve ratio is increased by 25%."}},{"itemId":"93b3314f-d916-555a-8263-c86999e9cc3f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:10","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ngozi guaranteed Ade's overdraft and gave notice ending her guarantee when the balance was ₦950,000 Dr. The bank did not stop the account. Ade then paid in ₦400,000 and ₦250,000 and withdrew ₦600,000. Using Clayton's Rule, find (a) Ngozi's remaining liability and (b) the total overdraft."}},{"itemId":"9437fe54-c183-5fea-93dc-a733022fb320","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:11","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An exporter negotiates a ₦6,000,000 export bill with his bank 2 months before maturity. The bank charges interest at 15% per annum and a commission of 0.25% of face value. How much does he receive?"}},{"itemId":"18d4be41-705b-541c-a2ea-ecd36219fe1f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:12","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Ada borrows ₦900,000 for 18 months at 12% per annum flat interest on the original principal, repaying principal and interest in 18 equal monthly instalments. Find the total interest and each instalment."}},{"itemId":"f637abb5-ecf7-5f70-8423-69c4aab9d5c0","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:13","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Oshodi Ventures Ltd asks for an overdraft. Current assets ₦9,000,000 (including stock ₦4,000,000); current liabilities ₦6,000,000; long-term loan ₦5,000,000; shareholders' funds ₦15,000,000; profit before interest and tax ₦3,000,000; loan interest ₦500,000. Compute the current ratio, quick ratio, working capital, gearing and interest cover, and comment."}},{"itemId":"f20cc198-5ebe-5c43-b2da-99b9171910d2","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:14","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Agege Bank has deposit liabilities of ₦300bn and qualifying liquid assets of ₦100bn. The minimum liquidity ratio is 30%. (a) What is its liquidity ratio? (b) What is the largest amount of liquid assets it can switch into loans and still comply? (c) What happens to its liquidity and profitability after the switch?"}},{"itemId":"b628bd1f-2f6b-5a85-9381-1c61cfc36600","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:15","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Discuss the role of the Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria in promoting professionalism in the Nigerian banking sector."}},{"itemId":"e3c86c07-6a9b-5074-9b35-8a144da2c912","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:2","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An economy's banks create total deposits of ₦500 million from an initial deposit of ₦50 million. (a) Find the money multiplier and the reserve ratio. (b) The reserve requirement is then raised by 100% and the initial deposit by 40%. Find the new total deposits."}},{"itemId":"b51b3be2-8fb2-5fba-b04b-741e9d970e39","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:3","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the 2004 consolidation, a bank had a capital base of ₦2 billion. (a) How much extra capital did it need, how many times did the requirement rise, and by what percentage? (b) By what percentage did the number of banks fall when 89 became 25?"}},{"itemId":"04394be6-b73b-50c1-b2b9-761e7cb01a71","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Lekki Bank has deposit liabilities of ₦800 billion. The CRR is 45% and the liquidity ratio 30%. (a) How much must it keep with the CBN as cash reserve? (b) What minimum liquid assets must it hold? (c) If the CBN cuts the CRR to 40%, how much is released for lending?"}},{"itemId":"b7457b9f-2007-595e-b06a-17c072779a6e","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:5","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A deposit money bank had total deposit liabilities of ₦48 billion. (a) What annual premium did it pay at 15/16 of 1%? (b) It later fails. A depositor holds ₦9 million. Using the ₦5 million cover, how is she paid?"}},{"itemId":"79757ac4-9d38-5934-b193-781b9f2373f8","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:6","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A ₦10,000,000 91-day Treasury bill is issued at a 10% discount rate. Find the price and the investor's simple yield (365-day year)."}},{"itemId":"cf183a1a-405b-5437-b01b-151c2507c180","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:7","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Distinguish a settlement bank from a non-settlement bank under the CBN's April 2004 framework, and list the steps in clearing a cheque."}},{"itemId":"b18a4814-169e-5a94-bd65-42e2358ab6f6","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:8","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A bill of exchange for ₦3,650,000 dated 5 May is payable 90 days after date. The holder discounts it with her bank on 28 June at 10% per annum (365-day year). Find the due date and the proceeds."}},{"itemId":"2bb50407-c66f-50dc-bf77-5c088c5cd6e4","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"theory_question","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-theory:9","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs Bello's account opened the month at ₦2,000 DR. She paid in a cheque of ₦9,500, issued a cheque of ₦4,800 and was charged ₦150 for a cheque book. Her standing order of ₦6,000 falls due at month end; the bank charges ½% commission and ₦50 postage, and her overdraft limit is ₦2,000. Will the bank pay?"}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'elements-banking: theory_question published payload and identity match source');
select ok((select coalesce(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_object(
  'itemId', item_id, 'courseId', course_id, 'kind', kind,
  'questionId', question_id, 'sourceKey', source_key,
  'parentItemId', parent_item_id, 'revision', revision,
  'payload', payload) order by source_key collate "C"), '[]'::jsonb)
  from public.read_published_managed_content('58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed')
  where kind = 'model_answer') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"172eacd8-50f3-5bd7-961c-df8da2dd0ff7","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:1","parentItemId":"dbe954f3-3729-5b02-be2c-8358c1436612","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"(a) Required reserve = 0.20 × ₦4,000,000 = ₦800,000; excess reserve = ₦4,000,000 − ₦800,000 = ₦3,200,000.\n(b) k = 1 ÷ 0.20 = 5.\n(c) Total = ₦4,000,000 × 5 = ₦20,000,000 (new deposits ₦16,000,000).\n(d) New r = 20% × 1.25 = 25%; total = ₦4,000,000 ÷ 0.25 = ₦16,000,000."}},{"itemId":"af3b4982-d256-51f6-920c-5ec56b555c25","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:10","parentItemId":"93b3314f-d916-555a-8263-c86999e9cc3f","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"1. Under Clayton's Rule the credits clear the oldest debit first: 950,000 − 400,000 − 250,000 = ₦300,000. This is all Ngozi still owes under her guarantee.\n2. The ₦600,000 withdrawal is new borrowing after the guarantee ended; she is not liable for it.\n3. Total overdraft = 300,000 + 600,000 = ₦900,000 Dr."}},{"itemId":"51edd875-6303-593d-9fbe-a269490bee06","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:11","parentItemId":"9437fe54-c183-5fea-93dc-a733022fb320","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Interest = 6,000,000 x 0.15 x 2/12 = ₦150,000. Commission = 6,000,000 x 0.0025 = ₦15,000. Amount credited = 6,000,000 - 150,000 - 15,000 = ₦5,835,000."}},{"itemId":"15f264a4-b3ce-552d-b4bd-f8e3ccc0c89b","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:12","parentItemId":"18d4be41-705b-541c-a2ea-ecd36219fe1f","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Interest = 900,000 x 0.12 x 18/12 = ₦162,000. Total repayable = 900,000 + 162,000 = ₦1,062,000. Monthly instalment = 1,062,000 / 18 = ₦59,000."}},{"itemId":"0b7a4630-5937-58a1-a521-4c8ff09bd0cf","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:13","parentItemId":"f637abb5-ecf7-5f70-8423-69c4aab9d5c0","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Current ratio = 9,000,000 ÷ 6,000,000 = 1.5 : 1.\n• Quick ratio = (9,000,000 − 4,000,000) ÷ 6,000,000 = 5,000,000 ÷ 6,000,000 = 0.83 : 1 (2 d.p.).\n• Working capital = 9,000,000 − 6,000,000 = ₦3,000,000.\n• Gearing = 5,000,000 ÷ (5,000,000 + 15,000,000) × 100 = 25%.\n• Interest cover = 3,000,000 ÷ 500,000 = 6 times.\n• Comment: liquidity is adequate on the current ratio, but the quick ratio below 1 shows reliance on selling stock to pay short-term debts. Gearing is low and interest is well covered, so the business can carry more debt; the banker should still check the cash flow forecast and value security on a break-up basis."}},{"itemId":"6ca0fedd-0a34-5e75-9b72-2b55a7bdab5c","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:14","parentItemId":"f20cc198-5ebe-5c43-b2da-99b9171910d2","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• (a) 100 ÷ 300 × 100 = 33.33% (2 d.p.).\n• (b) Required = 0.30 × 300 = ₦90bn. Switchable = 100 − 90 = ₦10bn.\n• (c) Its liquidity ratio falls to exactly 30% (less liquid), but the ₦10bn now earns loan interest, so it becomes more profitable, with more credit risk."}},{"itemId":"98196b70-c55a-5564-9970-fab28ff69d7f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:15","parentItemId":"b628bd1f-2f6b-5a85-9381-1c61cfc36600","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"Define the CIBN as the professional body for bankers, chartered in 1990 and now governed by the CIBN Act, 2007. State its objectives: banking education, professional ethics and tradition, advancement of banking practice and liaison with government. Then explain how it promotes professionalism: (1) rigorous examinations and training leading to qualifications such as ACIB; (2) high ethical standards, integrity and objectivity; (3) codes of conduct enforcing self-regulation, backed by the Investigating Panel, Disciplinary Tribunal and striking off; (4) recognition of merit through honorary awards. Add the legal backing: heads of technical departments must be members, and non-members holding themselves out as bankers commit an offence."}},{"itemId":"eab0e214-caf7-5bbb-8aa3-70efbb6fa155","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:2","parentItemId":"e3c86c07-6a9b-5074-9b35-8a144da2c912","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• (a) k = 500 / 50 = 10. Since k = 1 / r, r = 1 / 10 = 10%.\n• (b) New r = 10% × 2 = 20%. New initial deposit = ₦50m × 1.4 = ₦70m. New k = 1 / 0.20 = 5. Total deposits = ₦70m × 5 = ₦350 million. The doubled reserve ratio outweighs the larger deposit."}},{"itemId":"52fc979d-2b73-5a30-b041-862f78cd78b9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:3","parentItemId":"b51b3be2-8fb2-5fba-b04b-741e9d970e39","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"(a) Extra capital = ₦25 billion - ₦2 billion = ₦23 billion. Times = 25 ÷ 2 = 12.5 times. Percentage rise = (23 ÷ 2) × 100 = 1,150%.\n(b) Fall = 89 - 25 = 64 banks. Percentage = (64 ÷ 89) × 100 = 71.91, so about 71.9% (rounded to one decimal place)."}},{"itemId":"bd428785-2412-5646-a93c-51d9c99377ab","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:4","parentItemId":"04394be6-b73b-50c1-b2b9-761e7cb01a71","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"(a) 45% × ₦800bn = ₦360bn kept with the CBN.\n(b) 30% × ₦800bn = ₦240bn in liquid assets (cash, balances with the CBN, Treasury Bills).\n(c) New CRR: 40% × ₦800bn = ₦320bn. Released = ₦360bn − ₦320bn = ₦40bn. A lower CRR is expansionary: lending capacity and money supply rise."}},{"itemId":"c9da02ca-99ec-5a46-b081-f79252f5f5c9","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:5","parentItemId":"b7457b9f-2007-595e-b06a-17c072779a6e","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"(a) 48,000,000,000 × 15/16 × 1/100 = ₦450,000,000. (b) The NDIC pays the insured ₦5,000,000; the remaining 9,000,000 − 5,000,000 = ₦4,000,000 is claimed pro rata from liquidation dividends. Her claim passes to the NDIC for the ₦5m paid (subrogation)."}},{"itemId":"de9bcb00-7957-5508-845f-e25765590d77","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:6","parentItemId":"79757ac4-9d38-5934-b193-781b9f2373f8","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Discount = 10,000,000 x 0.10 x 91/365 = ₦249,315.07.\n• Price = 10,000,000 - 249,315.07 = ₦9,750,684.93.\n• Yield = 249,315.07 / 9,750,684.93 x 365/91 = 10.26% (2 d.p.)."}},{"itemId":"426e2daf-0657-5940-a5b0-42a445c6eb33","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:7","parentItemId":"cf183a1a-405b-5437-b01b-151c2507c180","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"A settlement bank clears and settles cheques on behalf of other banks, keeps a settlement account with the CBN and deposits clearing collateral directly with it. A non-settlement bank keeps only an operations account with the CBN (always in credit), and clears through an agency arrangement with a settlement bank, depositing at least ₦250 million collateral with that bank. Steps: collection, sorting (on-us or other-bank), presentation, clearing session, settlement of net positions through CBN settlement accounts, return of unpaid items with reasons, customer notification."}},{"itemId":"2a9a3647-98a2-583b-a89d-910a458f4f0f","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:8","parentItemId":"b18a4814-169e-5a94-bd65-42e2358ab6f6","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"1. 90 days after 5 May (excluding 5 May): May 26 days, June 30 (56), July 31 (87), August 3 (90): 3 August.\n2. Add 3 days of grace: due 6 August.\n3. Days from 28 June to 6 August: 2 (June) + 31 (July) + 6 (August) = 39 days.\n4. Discount = 3,650,000 x 0.10 x 39/365 = ₦39,000.\n5. Proceeds = 3,650,000 - 39,000 = ₦3,611,000."}},{"itemId":"3dedfe38-c67d-5fb0-ad7f-f59324deb709","courseId":"58f61fa3-cc23-5d99-bde7-5581043985ed","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:9","parentItemId":"2bb50407-c66f-50dc-bf77-5c088c5cd6e4","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"1. Balance before the order: −₦2,000 + ₦9,500 − ₦4,800 − ₦150 = ₦2,550 CR.\n2. Commission: 0.5% × ₦6,000 = ₦30. Charges: ₦30 + ₦50 = ₦80.\n3. Total debit: ₦6,000 + ₦80 = ₦6,080.\n4. Balance after: ₦2,550 − ₦6,080 = ₦3,530 DR.\n5. ₦3,530 DR is beyond the ₦2,000 limit, so the bank will not pay and incurs no liability for not paying."}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'elements-banking: model_answer published payload and identity match source');
