  'payload', payload) order by source_key collate "C"), '[]'::jsonb)
  from public.read_published_managed_content('4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119')
  where kind = 'model_answer') = $expected_content$[{"itemId":"38b0de96-307e-54ba-9931-80d8e447d02f","courseId":"4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:1","parentItemId":"73f00702-90bb-546d-a270-d21a617d25c1","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Introduction: technical meaning of law; the five blind men; Okunniga; theories overlap and are often implicit.\n• Positivism: Austin's definition and three elements; criticisms (wills, dictatorship, Lakanmi v A.G. (Western State) (1971), obedience without fear); Hart's primary and secondary rules. Relevance: supremacy of enacted law.\n• Pure theory: Kelsen's grundnorm and the Criminal Code chain; criticisms (formalism, locating the grundnorm, coups). Relevance: hierarchy of laws.\n• Natural law: Aquinas, Cicero, Grotius; criticisms (ought not is, multiple conscience). Relevance: Chapter IV rights, Niger Delta agitation.\n• Historical: Savigny's volksgeist; criticisms. Relevance: customary law and its three validity tests.\n• Others, one point and one criticism each: Ehrlich (checkpoint extortion), Pound (balancing), Bentham (airport searches), Holmes and Frank (extralegal factors).\n• Conclusion: each school sees one part of the elephant; Nigerian law draws on all; offer your own definition joining source, sanction and justice."}},{"itemId":"016acb76-d119-57ca-aeac-5bf32fdbc4f2","courseId":"4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:2","parentItemId":"056bbef2-4eac-53f6-86c2-5f26a4949416","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Introduction: law is the means, justice the end; Ross (correct application, not arbitrariness); Soyinka; explain the quote with winner and loser in court; list factors shaping one's view.\n• Perceptions: Aristotle (law and equality), equality and its limits (the mother's three children, legal aid), Ulpian and Aquinas (render each his due), Plato (fitted for: caste, Osu), Rawls (fairness), Bix and Lasswell.\n• Types: commutative and distributive (Aquinas); corrective and distributive (Aristotle) with Federal Character and palliatives; procedural (section 36); formal (Awolowo's treasonable felony trial, Sheddon v Goodrich) against substantive (Chinwendu v Mbamali, judicial activism, Dennis Lloyd, reasonable man test); Josiah v The State.\n• Conclusion: justice is relative, yet law must balance competing interests; a just law commands obedience; apartheid shows law and justice can part."}},{"itemId":"85b50280-9f15-5461-966c-4765154d9635","courseId":"4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:3","parentItemId":"52b0d680-0180-5709-bc2b-142dea0724a8","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Introduction: law can be classified in many ways; list the six classifications; thesis: useful tools that overlap in practice.\n• Public v private: define with examples; the state as 'higher' party; Nigeria has no separate courts (contrast France), only special procedures (fundamental rights, judicial review); Land Use Act shows land law moving into public law; tax is both.\n• Civil v criminal: define; standards of proof (ss.134-135 Evidence Act 2011); overlap: assault, false imprisonment, defamation; stealing a bicycle = stealing plus conversion.\n• Written v unwritten: technical meaning; case law is written down yet unwritten (Donoghue v Stevenson); s.36(12) and Aoko v Fagbemi.\n• Common law v equity: history, Earl of Oxford's case (1615), Lord Bacon, Judicature Acts 1875 fused administration, not rights.\n• Practical importance: choice of procedure and remedy; the dismissed injured employee (contract, labour, tort).\n• Conclusion: the classifications overlap and are the lawyer's servants, not his masters, but they remain essential for choosing the right court, procedure and remedy."}},{"itemId":"ac001134-4f1c-5a92-9cd7-645663fca301","courseId":"4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:4","parentItemId":"5fb8404d-f31e-5259-bfd9-fd3c3cd9fd53","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Introduction: define social control (Ross, Otite and Ogionwo); Pound's definition and \"paramount agent\"; Quinney's three elements; law's advantages and functions; list the seven techniques (Summers; Farrar and Dugdale).\n• Penal: prohibited conduct, penalty, procedure, punishment; police, Attorney-General, courts, prisons; Criminal and Penal Codes; alternatives with Aoko v Fagbemi and FCSC v Laoye.\n• Grievance-remedial: Summers' definition; remedies; logical basis; Smith v Selwyn; arbitration.\n• Constitutive: new legal person; Salomon v Salomon [1897]; incorporated trustees, universities.\n• Administrative-regulatory: standards, licensing, inspection; NAFDAC, NCC, school permits; preventive, unlike grievance-remedial.\n• Conclusion: techniques overlap and combine; note limits (dead-letter laws, weak enforcement, elite hijacking of welfare); law remains the most organised instrument of social control."}},{"itemId":"7e3bbb43-cc98-5eb9-81e0-86f7648b564a","courseId":"4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:5","parentItemId":"e7570f39-06f5-5958-b9eb-856041a2859e","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Introduction: adjudication requires the judge to find facts before applying law, by the adversarial or inquisitorial method.\n• Adversarial: parties control evidence; witness sequence; judge as umpire; balance of probability, modified by criminal rules (ss.35(2), 36(5); Woolmington v DPP; State v Keji Amusan) and res ipsa loquitur (Scott v London and St Katherine Docks). Five merits; five demerits (Whitehouse v Jordan; Ajani v Giwa).\n• Inquisitorial: adjudicator controls evidence (Ex parte Moore; Baba v NCATC). Four merits; three demerits (phantom coup trials).\n• Conclusion: state a reasoned preference, or argue for a blend as in divorce and custody cases."}},{"itemId":"031d4bcd-a8ac-5cdb-9c52-93457e4c7b91","courseId":"4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:6","parentItemId":"60048f78-076c-54fd-82f1-f119a83545ec","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Introduction: define legal reasoning; each pair is a tool of it.\n• (a) Principle (legal truth contradicted only by a clearer one; grounds rules, e.g. natural justice); standard (flexible measure such as the reasonable man; Pound), contrasted with a fixed rule.\n• (b) Rhetoric (Plato; Aristotle's forensic and deliberative; appeal to authority; acquired skill). Logic (its value; syllogism with example; three limitations; induction; Dorset Yacht; analogy).\n• (c) Practical reasoning (no strict deduction, uses analogy, weighs considerations, judged by society's values); Holmes; four justifying factors; Chinwendu v Mbamali.\n• Conclusion: logic and rhetoric are the technical side; justice, policy and common sense complete legal reasoning."}},{"itemId":"e9fe0018-be88-52a5-b6ff-951805f6c0e3","courseId":"4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:7","parentItemId":"b1d50cd4-755c-597c-8b21-8f82a6086dd2","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Introduction: s.6(6)(b) judicial power; statutes suffer ambiguity, vagueness, open texture; courts seek intention from the words.\n• Literal: Sussex Peerage; weaknesses shown by Awolowo v Shagari and Adegbenro v Akintola; Dias.\n• Golden: absurdity, no further; Adamolekun.\n• Mischief: Heydon's case four questions; limits.\n• Purposive: Nafiu Rabiu v State; Lord Simonds' warning in Magor.\n• Assessment: rules of practice; Fatayi-Williams CJN; Bennion; supplement with aids, presumptions (Barclays Bank v CBN) and maxims.\n• Conclusion: a combination of canons and aids, against the legislative purpose, best finds legislative intention."}},{"itemId":"b06199e6-789e-5856-b667-394bd4f7548d","courseId":"4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:8","parentItemId":"26d0c68c-8b07-5936-9b53-0696aaa660fa","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Introduction: define sources of Nigerian law and customary law (customs accepted by a community as binding); classify into ethnic customary law and Islamic law; note its flexibility (Lewis v Bankole). Thesis: courts enforce custom only if it passes three statutory tests.\n• Statutory basis: High Court Laws of the States and FCT (e.g. s 44 High Court Law, Oyo State 1978).\n• Repugnancy: no settled definition; Eshugbayi Eleko (barbarous custom); Re Effiong Okon Atta; Guri v Hadejia NA; limits: Dawodu v Danmole (inconsistency with English ideas is not repugnancy).\n• Public policy: Cole v Akinyele.\n• Incompatibility with written law: e.g. Legitimacy Act; s 36(12) 1999 Constitution and Aoko v Fagbemi (no unwritten customary crimes).\n• Conclusion: custom remains a primary, binding source within these limits; the tests protect justice without imposing English standards."}},{"itemId":"259ec35a-431f-5db2-a668-83603fe9759f","courseId":"4df568c2-4a68-52ab-80d6-380e5921d119","kind":"model_answer","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"guide-answer:9","parentItemId":"e508e863-80cf-546f-8bcd-1a2f8231d36a","revision":1,"payload":{"body":"• Introduction: define an essay; note the four types; thesis: good writing follows settled steps.\n• Pre-writing: choose a manageable topic with available materials (correct, complete, concise); preliminary reading; rethink with what, who, where, when, how, why; identify the basic question; formulate a restricted, organised thesis.\n• Test the idea: writing, credibility, friendly colleague, possibility.\n• Outline and materials: topic-specific, related, current and classic works (e.g. Obilade); read selectively, responsibly, critically.\n• Writing: at least four drafts (get it written; add detail; cut dead wood; review); chapters, sections, subsections.\n• General hints: brief engaging introduction, originality (no plagiarism), clarity, brevity, accuracy, organisation, paragraphs, mechanical accuracy.\n• Conclusion: choose reflective, looking-ahead or straightforward; following these steps produces an essay that impresses."}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'legal-methods-1: model_answer published payload and identity match source');
